a-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(484,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1143.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(2199.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D43A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1286,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D440\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D440\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(953.7,-719.9)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(466,289) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"2537\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-event-horizon-class-subtopic-link-event-horizon-a-0aed8b33\",\"infinite-shift-52e77e27\",\"light-capture-73b3b0b3\",\"newtonian-link-2ef093d0\"]'),
(2009346,'gravitational-lensing','astrophysical-general-relativity','Gravitational Lensing','<p>Gravitational Lensing is the phenomenon where a massive object (a \"Lens\") acts as a geometric magnifier, bending the path of light from a distant source (a \"Background\") as it travels through curved spacetime. In university-level astrophysics, lensing is the primary tool for mapping the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass-distribution\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass Distribution</strong></a> of the universe, detecting <strong>Dark Matter</strong>, and observing the most distant galaxies that would otherwise be too faint to see.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Lens Equation and Fermat\'s Principle</h3>\n                    <p>Lensing is described by the <strong>Lens Equation</strong>: \\( \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\boldsymbol{\\theta} - \\boldsymbol{\\alpha}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) \\). It relates the true position of the source \\( \\beta \\) to the observed position \\( \\theta \\) using the <strong>Deflection Angle</strong> \\( \\alpha \\). Lensing can also be understood via <strong>Fermat\'s Principle in General Relativity</strong>: light follows the path of stationary time delay. The total delay consists of a \"Geometric\" part (extra distance) and a \"Potential\" part (Shapiro delay).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Strong Lensing: Einstein Rings and Crosses</h3>\n                    <p>In cases of high mass and perfect alignment, <strong>Strong Lensing</strong> occurs. The light from a single source is split into multiple images or stretched into spectacular arcs and <strong>Einstein Rings</strong>. The <strong>Einstein Radius</strong> (\\( \\theta_E \\)) defines the characteristic scale of the lens; its size is a direct measure of the total mass (including dark matter) enclosed within the lensing region.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Weak Lensing: Cosmic Shear</h3>\n                    <p>Most lensing in the universe is <strong>Weak Lensing</strong>, where the images are slightly distorted rather than split. By statistically analyzing the \"Cosmic Shear\" of millions of distant galaxies, astrophysicists can map the large-scale <strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong> that surround galaxy clusters. This a critical probe of the <strong>Large-Scale Structure</strong> of the universe and the properties of <strong>Dark Energy</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Microlensing and Exoplanet Detection</h3>\n                    <p>When the \"Lens\" is a single star or planet, the effect is called <strong>Gravitational Microlensing</strong>. Instead of seeing multiple images, we observe a characteristic increase and decrease in the brightness of the background star as the lens passes by. This technique is uniquely sensitive to <strong>Exoplanets</strong> located far from their host stars and to <strong>MACHOs</strong> (Massive Compact Halo Objects) in the galactic halo.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Magnification and Caustics</h3>\n                    <p>Gravitational lenses act as <strong>Flux Magnifiers</strong>. The magnification is determined by the <strong>Jacobian</strong> of the lens mapping. Regions of infinite theoretical magnification are called <strong>Caustics</strong>. When a source crosses a caustic, its brightness can increase by thousands of times. This has allowed astronomers to see individual stars in galaxies billions of light-years away, a phenomenon known as \"Extreme Magnification Events.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Lensing as a Cosmological Probe</h3>\n                    <p>Lensing provides a way to measure the <strong>Hubble Constant</strong> (\\( H_0 \\)) independent of the distance ladder. By measuring the \"Time Delay\" between the arrival of signals in different images of a lensed quasar, astronomers can calculate the absolute size of the lens system. This <strong>Cosmography</strong> allows for a direct test of the expansion rate of the universe and the curvature of the <strong>FLRW Metric</strong>.</p>','Gravitational Lensing is the phenomenon where a massive object (a \"Lens\") acts as a geometric magnifier, bending the path of light from a distant source (a \"Background\") as it travels through curved spacetime. In university-level astrophysics, lensing is the primary tool for mapping the Mass Distribution of the universe, detecting Dark Matter, and observing the most distant galaxies that would otherwise be too faint to see. Lensing is described by the Lens Equation:.','Gravitational Lensing is the phenomenon where a massive object (a \"Lens\") acts as a geometric magnifier, bending the path of light from a distant source (a \"Background\") as it travels through curved spacetime. In university-level astrophysics, lensing is the primary tool for mapping the Mass Distribution of the universe, detecting Dark Matter, and observing the most distant galaxies that would otherwise be too faint to see. Lensing is described by the Lens Equation: <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13.301ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 5879 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-BI-1D737\" d=\"M59 -194H49Q31 -194 28 -182Q28 -178 107 139T192 473Q212 533 248 580T324 652T395 689T450 701H461Q514 701 551 688T605 652T630 607T637 561Q637 546 634 526T611 465T556 393Q572 382 590 347T608 262Q608 146 522 69T299 -8Q279 -8 261 -6T228 2T204 13T183 26T169 37T157 48L150 56L120 -64Q113 -90 104 -128Q93 -175 89 -184T73 -194H59ZM531 592Q531 651 463 651Q399 651 341 600T253 466Q250 458 217 327T182 185Q180 176 180 159Q180 108 212 76T301 44Q330 44 354 51T393 65T423 91T444 118T459 151T468 179T475 206Q490 264 491 296Q491 313 489 326T484 345L482 350Q481 350 477 348T464 344T444 340T413 335T372 333T334 334T301 340T274 355T265 380Q265 444 397 444Q425 444 445 441T476 436L485 433Q489 433 499 458Q509 482 520 527T531 592ZM424 390Q424 393 389 393Q383