a-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg> is the central subject of study, as its rate of change defines the Hubble Parameter and determines the ultimate fate of the universe.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.774ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1668 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q331 441 370 392Q386 422 416 422Q429 422 439 414T449 394Q449 381 412 234T374 68Q374 43 381 35T402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487Q506 153 506 144Q506 138 501 117T481 63T449 13Q436 0 417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM351 328Q351 334 346 350T323 385T277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q217 26 254 59T298 110Q300 114 325 217T351 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522 166 540T173 566T179 586T187 603T197 615T211 624T229 626Q247 625 254 615T261 596Q261 589 252 549T232 470L222 433Q222 431 272 431H323Q330 424 330 420Q330 398 317 385H210L174 240Q135 80 135 68Q135 26 162 26Q197 26 230 60T283 144Q285 150 288 151T303 153H307Q322 153 322 145Q322 142 319 133Q314 117 301 95T267 48T216 6T155 -11Q125 -11 98 4T59 56Q57 64 57 83V101L92 241Q127 382 128 383Q128 385 77 385H26Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(529,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(918,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1279,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"hubble-mapping-fc511c90\",\"redshift-link-1a6b3722\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-expansion-parameter-class-subtopic-link-expansion-parameter-a-b9ac2004\",\"energy-coupling-465b4023\"]'),
(2009349,'singularities','compact-objects','Spacetime Singularities','<p>In general relativity, a <strong>Singularity</strong> is a region of spacetime where the <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> becomes ill-defined and physical quantities like density and curvature grow to infinity. In university-level astrophysics, singularities are theoretical \"endpoints\" of gravitational collapse, representing the limits of Einstein\'s theory. They indicate the breakdown of smooth classical geometry and the necessity of a yet-to-be-discovered theory of <strong>Quantum Gravity</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Penrose-Hawking Singularity Theorems</h3>\n                    <p>The existence of singularities is not just a mathematical curiosity but a rigorous prediction of General Relativity. The <strong>Penrose-Hawking Singularity Theorems</strong> prove that for any physically reasonable distribution of matter, gravitational collapse will inevitably lead to a singularity. These theorems showed that singularities are a generic feature of the universe, appearing both at the birth of time (the <strong>Big Bang</strong>) and in the death of massive stars (<strong>Black Holes</strong>).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Curvature vs. Coordinate Singularities</h3>\n                    <p>Physicists distinguish between <strong>Coordinate Singularities</strong> (which can be removed by a change of variables, like the Schwarzschild radius) and <strong>Curvature Singularities</strong> (which are physical). A curvature singularity is identified by the divergence of <strong>Scalar Invariants</strong>, such as the <strong>Kretschmann Scalar</strong> (\\( R_{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} R^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} \\)). If these scalars go to infinity, the \"stretching\" and \"squeezing\" of space become infinite, tearing the fabric of the manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Schwarzschild Singularity: r=0</h3>\n                    <p>Inside a non-rotating black hole, the Schwarzschild metric predicts a singularity at the center (\\( r=0 \\)). Here, the entire mass of the star is compressed into a zero-volume point, resulting infinite <strong>Tidal Forces</strong>. Any object entering the black hole is eventually crushed to this point, its physical history terminating as it reaches the \"Edge of Time.\" The \\( r=0 \\) singularity is space-like, meaning it is an <strong>Event</strong> in the future of every infalling observer.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Ring Singularities in Kerr Black Holes</h3>\n                    <p>For rotating (Kerr) black holes, the singularity takes the form of a <strong>Ring</strong> rather than a point. This \"Ring Singularity\" is time-like, meaning an observer could theoretically avoid it and pass through the ring into a new region of spacetime (a <strong>White Hole</strong> or another universe). However, the internal stability of these regions is highly suspect, as they may be destroyed by <strong>Cauchy Horizons</strong> and extreme relativistic instabilities.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Cosmic Censorship Hypothesis</h3>\n                    <p>To preserve the predictability of the universe, Roger Penrose proposed the <strong>Cosmic Censorship Hypothesis</strong>. It suggests that all physical singularities must be hidden behind an <strong>Event Horizon</strong>, preventing them from influencing the external world. A \"Naked Singularity\" (one visible to an outside observer) would represent a total breakdown of <strong>Causality</strong>, as information could emerge from the singularity without following any deterministic laws.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Quantum Gravity: Taming the Infinite</h3>\n                    <p>The presence of singularities is widely seen as a sign that General Relativity is an <strong>Effective Field Theory</strong> that fails at extreme energy scales. Theories like <strong>String Theory</strong> and <strong>Loop Quantum Gravity</strong> attempt to resolve singularities by introducing a minimum length scale (the <strong>Planck Length</strong>). In these models, space is \"discrete\" or \"bumpy,\" preventing density from reaching infinity and potentially replacing the singularity with a highly energetic \"Quantum Bounce\" or a \"Fuzzball.\"</p>','In general relativity, a Singularity is a region of spacetime where the Metric Tensor becomes ill-defined and physical quantities like density and curvature grow to infinity. In university-level astrophysics, singularities are theoretical \"endpoints\" of gravitational collapse, representing the limits of Einstein\'s theory. They indicate the breakdown of smooth classical geometry and the necessity of a yet-to-be-discovered theory of Quantum Gravity.','In general relativity, a Singularity is a region of spacetime where the Metric Tensor becomes ill-defined and physical quantities like density and curvature grow to infinity. In university-level astrophysics, singularities are theoretical \"endpoints\" of gravitational collapse, representing the limits of Einstein\'s theory. They indicate the breakdown of smooth classical geometry and the necessity of a yet-to-be-discovered theory of Quantum Gravity.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.916ex\" height=\"2.23ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 4825 985.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D445\" d=\"M230 637Q203 637 198 638T193 649Q193 676 204 682Q206 683 378 683Q550 682 564 680Q620 672 658 652T712 606T733 563T739 529Q739 484 710 445T643 385T576 351T538 338L545 333Q612 295 612 223Q612 212 607 162T602 80V71Q602 53 603 43T614 25T640 16Q668 16 686 38T712 85Q717 99 720 102T735 105Q755 105 755 93Q755 75 731 36Q693 -21 641 -21H632Q571 -21 531 4T487 82Q487 109 502 166T517 239Q517 290 474 313Q459 320 449 321T378 323H309L277 193Q244 61 244 59Q244 55 245 54T252 50T269 48T302 46H333Q339 38 339 37T336 19Q332 6 326 0H311Q275 2 180 2Q146 2 117 2T71 2T50 1Q33 1 33 10Q33 12 36 24Q41 43 46 45Q50 46 61 46H67Q94 46 127 49Q141 52 146 61Q149 65 218 339T287 628Q287 635 230 637ZM630 554Q630 586 609 608T523 636Q521 636 500 636T462 637H440Q393 637 386 627Q385 624 352 494T319 361Q319 360 388 360Q466 361 492 367Q556 377 592 426Q608 449 619 486T630 554Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\" d=\"M74 431Q75 431 146 436T219 442Q231 442 231 434Q231 428 185 241L137 51H140L150 55Q161 59 177 67T214 86T261 119T312 165Q410 264 445 394Q458 442 496 442Q509 442 519 434T530 411Q530 390 516 352T469 262T388 162T267 70T106 5Q81 -2 71 -2Q66 -2 59 -1T51 1Q45 5 45 11Q45 13 88 188L132 364Q133 377 125 380T86 385H65Q59 391 59 393T61 412Q65 431 74 431Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70E\" d=\"M184 -11Q116 -11 74 34T31 147Q31 247 104 333T274 430Q275 431 414 431H552Q553 430 555 429T559 427T562 425T565 422T567 420T569 416T570 412T571 407T572 401Q572 357 507 357Q500 357 490 357T476 358H416L421 348Q439 310 439 263Q439 153 359 71T184 -11ZM361 278Q361 358 276 358Q152 358 115 184Q114 180 114 178Q106 141 106 117Q106 67 131 47T188 26Q242 26 287 73Q316 103 334 153T356 233T361 278Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(603,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1133,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1650,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(2412.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(603,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1133,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1650,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"physical-singularity-97cd8542\",\"causal-doom-fb28c39e\",\"resolution-limit-796fa53a\",\"rotating-core-48783b99\"]'),
(2009350,'black-hole','compact-objects','Black Holes','<p>A <strong>Black Hole</strong> is a region of spacetime where gravity is so strong that nothing—no particles or even electromagnetic radiation—can escape from it. In university-level astrophysics, black holes are recognized as the ultimate macroscopic manifestation of <strong>General Relativity</strong>. They are not merely \"dense objects\" but pure geometric entities where the roles of space and time are fundamentally reconfigured, serving as the primary laboratories for testing <strong>Strong-Field Gravity</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Anatomy of a Black Hole</h3>\n                    <p>Every black hole is composed of three primary components:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Singularity:</strong> The central point of infinite density where the laws of physics break down.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Event Horizon:</strong> The \"Surface\" or causal boundary from which escape is impossible.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Ergosphere:</strong> For rotating black holes, a region outside the horizon where space is \"dragged\" along with the hole\'s spin.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    This structure is determined entirely by the <strong>No-Hair Theorem</strong>, which states that a stationary black hole is uniquely defined by only three parameters: mass (\\( M \\)), charge (\\( Q \\)), and angular momentum (\\( J \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Stellar-Mass and Supermassive Varieties</h3>\n                    <p>Black holes are categorized by their scale. <strong>Stellar-Mass Black Holes</strong> (\\( 3-100 M_\\odot \\)) form from the <strong>Dynamical Collapse</strong> of massive stars. <strong>Supermassive Black Holes</strong> (\\( 10^6-10^{10} M_\\odot \\)) reside at the centers of most galaxies, including our own (Sgr A*). These giants act as the \"Gravitational Anchors\" of galaxies, driving the evolution of large-scale structure through <strong>AGN Feedback</strong> and relativistic jets.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Spacetime Dragging and Kerr Geometry</h3>\n                    <p>Most astrophysical black holes are expected to be rotating, described by the <strong>Kerr Metric</strong>. Rotation introduces the phenomenon of <strong>Frame-Dragging</strong> (the Lense-Thirring effect), where the very fabric of space is twisted around the hole. This creates the <strong>ISCO</strong> (Innermost Stable Circular Orbit), the closest point matter can orbit before falling in, dictating the brightness and size of the surrounding <strong>Accretion Disk</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Black Hole Thermodynamics</h3>\n                    <p>Black holes possess thermal properties, a discovery that linked GR to <strong>Quantum Field Theory</strong>. According to Stephen Hawking, black holes are not perfectly black but emit <strong>Hawking Radiation</strong> due to quantum fluctuations near the horizon. This implies that black holes have a temperature and an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> proportional to their surface area, leading to the <strong>Black Hole Information Paradox</strong>: what happens to the information of objects that fall in?</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Observational Evidence: EHT and LIGO</h3>\n                    <p>For decades, black holes were theoretical predictions. Today, they are observational realities. The <strong>Event Horizon Telescope (EHT)</strong> has captured direct images of the \"Shadow\" cast by the horizons of M87* and Sgr A*. Simultaneously, <strong>LIGO</strong> detects the gravitational waves from merging binary black holes, allowing us to \"hear\" the vibrations of their horizons and test the limits of Einstein\'s theory in the most extreme environments.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Information Paradox and Quantum Gravity</h3>\n                    <p>The \"Death\" of a black hole via Hawking radiation leads to the greatest puzzle in modern physics. If a black hole evaporates, the information it contained appears to be lost, violating the <strong>Unitarity</strong> of quantum mechanics. Resolving this paradox—through ideas like the <strong>Holographic Principle</strong> or \"Firewalls\"—is the current frontier of research, suggesting that black holes are the key to unifying <strong>General Relativity</strong> with quantum information theory.</p>','A Black Hole is a region of spacetime where gravity is so strong that nothing—no particles or even electromagnetic radiation—can escape from it. In university-level astrophysics, black holes are recognized as the ultimate macroscopic manifestation of General Relativity. They are not merely \"dense objects\" but pure geometric entities where the roles of space and time are fundamentally reconfigured, serving as the primary laboratories for testing Strong-Field Gravity.','A Black Hole is a region of spacetime where gravity is so strong that nothing—no particles or even electromagnetic radiation—can escape from it. In university-level astrophysics, black holes are recognized as the ultimate macroscopic manifestation of General Relativity. They are not merely \"dense objects\" but pure geometric entities where the roles of space and time are fundamentally reconfigured, serving as the primary laboratories for testing Strong-Field Gravity.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.378ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1051 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D440\" d=\"M289 629Q289 635 232 637Q208 637 201 638T194 648Q194 649 196 659Q197 662 198 666T199 671T201 676T203 679T207 681T212 683T220 683T232 684Q238 684 262 684T307 683Q386 683 398 683T414 678Q415 674 451 396L487 117L510 154Q534 190 574 254T662 394Q837 673 839 675Q840 676 842 678T846 681L852 683H948Q965 683 988 683T1017 684Q1051 684 1051 673Q1051 668 1048 656T1045 643Q1041 637 1008 637Q968 636 957 634T939 623Q936 618 867 340T797 59Q797 55 798 54T805 50T822 48T855 46H886Q892 37 892 35Q892 19 885 5Q880 0 869 0Q864 0 828 1T736 2Q675 2 644 2T609 1Q592 1 592 11Q592 13 594 25Q598 41 602 43T625 46Q652 46 685 49Q699 52 704 61Q706 65 742 207T813 490T848 631L654 322Q458 10 453 5Q451 4 449 3Q444 0 433 0Q418 0 415 7Q413 11 374 317L335 624L267 354Q200 88 200 79Q206 46 272 46H282Q288 41 289 37T286 19Q282 3 278 1Q274 0 267 0Q265 0 255 0T221 1T157 2Q127 2 95 1T58 0Q43 0 39 2T35 11Q35 13 38 25T43 40Q45 46 65 46Q135 46 154 86Q158 92 223 354T289 629Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D440\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D440\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-event-horizon-class-subtopic-link-event-horizon-a-0aed8b33\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-hawking-radiation-class-subtopic-link-quantum-emission-a-79daa0ea\",\"area-information-law-8c413639\",\"uniqueness-rule-15285633\"]'),
(2009351,'event-horizon','compact-objects','Event Horizon','<p>The Event Horizon is the fundamental causal boundary of a black hole, a 3D null hypersurface that separates the \"External Universe\" from a region from which no information or matter can escape. In university-level general relativity, the event horizon is characterized by the fact that the <strong>Escape Velocity</strong> exactly equals the speed of light (\\( c \\)). It acts as a <strong>One-Way Membrane</strong>, marking the definitive boundary of our observable reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Geometry of the Horizon</h3>\n                    <p>In the Schwarzschild metric, the event horizon is located at the <strong>Schwarzschild Radius</strong> \\( r = 2GM/c^2 \\). At this surface, the metric component \\( g_{00} \\) vanishes, and the role of the radial coordinate (\\( r \\)) and time coordinate (\\( t \\)) are swapped. Inside the horizon, the radial direction becomes \"Future-Directed\"—just as you cannot stop moving forward in time, an object inside a black hole cannot stop moving \"inward\" toward the singularity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Coordinate vs. Proper Time</h3>\n                    <p>A profound relativistic effect occurs at the event horizon. To an infinitely far-away observer (using <strong>Coordinate Time</strong>), an infalling object appears to slow down, redshift, and eventually \"freeze\" just outside the horizon, never quite crossing it. However, the infalling object (using <strong>Proper Time</strong>) crosses the horizon in a finite, often very short, amount of time and experiences nothing unusual at the boundary itself—a phenomenon known as the <strong>Equivalence Principle</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Gravitational Redshift to Infinity</h3>\n                    <p>The event horizon is a surface of <strong>Infinite Gravitational Redshift</strong>. Any photon emitted from the horizon reaches a distant observer with an energy of zero (infinite wavelength). This redshift is the physical reason why a black hole is \"black.\" It also leads to the <strong>Information Stretching</strong> effect, where the light from a falling astronaut is stretched across the entire surface of the hole from the perspective of an outside observer.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The No-Hair Theorem and Surface Gravity</h3>\n                    <p>The physical properties of the event horizon are remarkably simple, governed by the <strong>No-Hair Theorem</strong>. A horizon is smooth and characterized only by its <strong>Surface Gravity</strong> (\\( \\kappa \\)), which determines the strength of the local gravitational field. This simplicity allows astrophysicists to treat event horizons as \"membranes\" with a specific <strong>Electrical Resistivity</strong> and <strong>Viscosity</strong>, a framework known as the <strong>Membrane Paradigm</strong> used to model accretion and jets.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Quantum Effects: Hawking Radiation</h3>\n                    <p>While classically nothing escapes, <strong>Quantum Field Theory in Curved Spacetime</strong> proves that horizons emit radiation. Due to vacuum fluctuations, pairs of virtual particles are created near the horizon. If one falls in while the other escapes, the black hole appears to emit <strong>Hawking Radiation</strong>. This proves that the event horizon has a <strong>Temperature</strong> and is the source of the universe\'s most elusive form of energy loss.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Information Paradox and Firewalls</h3>\n                    <p>The \"surface\" of a black hole is the site of the most intense debate in modern physics. If information is stored on the horizon (the <strong>Holographic Principle</strong>), how is it retrieved? Some theories suggest that the horizon is actually a <strong>Firewall</strong>—a region of high-energy particles that incinerates any infalling observer. Resolving the nature of the event horizon is the key to reconciling <strong>General Relativity</strong> with the laws of <strong>Quantum Information</strong>.</p>','The Event Horizon is the fundamental causal boundary of a black hole, a 3D null hypersurface that separates the \"External Universe\" from a region from which no information or matter can escape. In university-level general relativity, the event horizon is characterized by the fact that the Escape Velocity exactly equals the speed of light. It acts as a One-Way Membrane, marking the definitive boundary of our observable reality.','The Event Horizon is the fundamental causal boundary of a black hole, a 3D null hypersurface that separates the \"External Universe\" from a region from which no information or matter can escape. In university-level general relativity, the event horizon is characterized by the fact that the Escape Velocity exactly equals the speed of light (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.98ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 433 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>). It acts as a One-Way Membrane, marking the definitive boundary of our observable reality.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.98ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 433 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"null-surface-c9f2ecc4\",\"infinite-dilation-d86bebb8\",\"geometric-pull-5c4dbf5b\",\"hawking-origin-c19bf748\"]'),
(2009352,'gravitational-waves','einstein-field-equations','Gravitational Waves','<p>Gravitational Waves are ripples in the fabric of spacetime caused by the acceleration of massive objects. Predicted by Albert Einstein 1916 and directly detected for the first time in 2015, these waves propagate at the speed of light (\\( c \\)) as 4D strain oscillations of the <strong>Metric Tensor</strong>. In university-level astrophysics, gravitational waves provide a new \"sense\" with which to observe the universe, allowing us to see events (like black hole mergers) that emit no light.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Linearized Gravity and Wave Solutions</h3>\n                    <p>In the weak-field limit, the Einstein <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/field-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Field Equations</strong></a> can be linearized into a form that resembles the electromagnetic wave equation: \\( \\square \\bar{h}_{\\mu\\nu} = -2\\kappa T_{\\mu\\nu} \\). The solution in a vacuum is a <strong>Plane Wave</strong> \\( h_{\\mu\\nu} \\) that represents a dynamic perturbation of the <strong>Minkowski Metric</strong>. This reveals that spacetime is not a static stage but a flexible medium capable of carrying energy and information across cosmic distances.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Quadrupole Formula</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike electromagnetism, where waves are produced by moving charges (dipole radiation), gravitational waves are produced by the <strong>Quadrupole Moment</strong> (\\( I_{ij} \\)) of the mass distribution. A perfectly spherical explosion or a rotating, symmetric object will not emit gravitational waves. Only systems with changing asymmetry, such as a <strong>Binary Star System</strong> or a lumpy spinning pulsar, can \"pump\" ripples into the spacetime manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Transverse and Quadrupolar Polarization</h3>\n                    <p>Gravitational waves possess two distinct polarizations, known as <strong>Plus (\\( + \\))</strong> and <strong>Cross (\\( \\times \\))</strong>. These waves are <strong>Transverse</strong>, meaning the oscillations occur perpendicular to the direction of travel. As a wave passes through a ring of particles, it causes them to stretch in one direction while squeezing in the other, a \"tidal\" effect that changes the <strong>Proper Distance</strong> between objects without any physical forces acting upon them.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Sources: Compact Binary Coalescence</h3>\n                    <p>The most powerful sources of gravitational waves are <strong>Compact Binary Coalescence (CBC)</strong> events. As two black holes or neutron stars orbit each other, they lose energy to gravitational radiation, causing them to spiral inward. This culminations in a \"Chirp\" signal—a rapid increase in frequency and amplitude—followed by a violent <strong>Merger</strong> and a final \"Ringdown\" as the new object settles into equilibrium.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The First Detection: GW150914</h3>\n                    <p>On September 14, 2015, the <strong>LIGO</strong> detectors recorded the first direct signal from a binary black hole merger. This event, known as <strong>GW150914</strong>, confirmed the existence of stellar-mass black holes and proved that gravitational waves are a physical reality. It marked the birth of <strong>Gravitational Wave Astronomy</strong>, providing the first direct test of general relativity in the strong-field, highly dynamical regime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Multi-Messenger Astronomy</h3>\n                    <p>The detection of <strong>Binary Neutron Star</strong> mergers (like GW170817) has unified gravitational wave science with electromagnetic observations. By observing the same event through both \"ripples\" and \"light,\" astronomers confirmed that gravitational waves travel at exactly \\( c \\) and identified these mergers as the primary sites for <strong>Nucleosynthesis</strong> (the origin of gold and platinum), linking GR to the chemical history of the universe.</p>','Gravitational Waves are ripples in the fabric of spacetime caused by the acceleration of massive objects. Predicted by Albert Einstein 1916 and directly detected for the first time in 2015, these waves propagate at the speed of light as 4D strain oscillations of the Metric Tensor. In university-level astrophysics, gravitational waves provide a new \"sense\" with which to observe the universe, allowing us to see events (like black hole mergers) that emit no light.','Gravitational Waves are ripples in the fabric of spacetime caused by the acceleration of massive objects. Predicted by Albert Einstein 1916 and directly detected for the first time in 2015, these waves propagate at the speed of light (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.98ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 433 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) as 4D strain oscillations of the Metric Tensor. In university-level astrophysics, gravitational waves provide a new \"sense\" with which to observe the universe, allowing us to see events (like black hole mergers) that emit no light.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.98ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 433 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"spacetime-wave-f694fe5a\",\"emission-rule-5b43384e\",\"energy-loss-8d543e0a\",\"signature-wave-90d750a6\"]'),
(2009353,'ligo','astrophysical-general-relativity','LIGO: Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory','<p><strong>LIGO</strong> (Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory) is the world\'s most sensitive measuring instrument, designed to detect the infinitesimal strains in spacetime caused by passing <strong>Gravitational Waves</strong>. It consists of two massive L-shaped interferometers located in Hanford, Washington, and Livingston, Louisiana. In university-level physics, LIGO represents the pinnacle of <strong>Precision Measurement</strong>, capable of detecting changes in distance smaller than one-thousandth the diameter of a proton.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Michelson Interferometer Design</h3>\n                    <p>LIGO operates on the principle of <strong>Laser Interferometry</strong>. A single laser beam is split into two 4-kilometer-long arms. After reflecting off mirrors, the beams are recombined at a detector. In the absence of a gravitational wave, the beams interfere destructively, and no light reaches the sensor. When a wave passes, it stretches one arm and squeezes the other, causing a <strong>Phase Shift</strong> that allows light to leak through, providing a direct measurement of the spacetime strain \\( h \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Measuring Strain (h)</h3>\n                    <p>The quantity LIGO measures is <strong>Strain</strong>, defined as the fractional change in length: \\( h = \\Delta L / L \\). For a typical black hole merger, \\( h \\) is on the order of \\( 10^{-21} \\). To achieve this sensitivity, LIGO must suppress all sources of <strong>Noise</strong>, including seismic vibrations, thermal fluctuations of the mirrors, and the \"Quantum Noise\" arising from the discrete nature of photons (the <strong>Standard Quantum Limit</strong>).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Seismic and Thermal Isolation</h3>\n                    <p>To detect subatomic movements, LIGO\'s mirrors are suspended by a series of <strong>Quadruple Pendulums</strong> that act as low-pass filters for seismic vibration. The entire 4km path is enclosed in one of the world\'s largest <strong>Ultra-High Vacuum</strong> systems to prevent air molecules from scattering the laser. Thisolation ensures that the \"noise floor\" is low enough to reveal the subtle \"Chirps\" originating from billions of light-years away.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Coincident Detection and Localization</h3>\n                    <p>Having two detectors is essential for <strong>Signal Verification</strong>. A true gravitational wave will appear in both detectors with a small time delay (up to 10 milliseconds) corresponding to the travel time at speed \\( c \\). This \"Coincident Detection\" allows physicists to rule out local noise and, by using <strong>Triangulation</strong> with other detectors like <strong>Virgo</strong> (Italy) and <strong>KAGRA</strong> (Japan), pinpoint the source\'s location in the sky.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Squeezed Light and Quantum Enhancement</h3>\n                    <p>Modern LIGO (Advanced LIGO Plus) uses <strong>Squeezed Light</strong> to bypass the uncertainty principle. By carefully manipulating the phase and amplitude uncertainty of the laser photons, engineers reduce the \"shot noise\" at high frequencies. This <strong>Quantum Enhancement</strong> allows the observatory to \"see\" further into the universe, increasing the volume of space it can probe and the frequency of detected merger events.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Future: Cosmic Explorer and LISA</h3>\n                    <p>LIGO has proved that the universe can be \"heard\" through gravity. Future ground-based detectors like <strong>Cosmic Explorer</strong> will have 40km arms, allowing us to see every stellar-mass black hole merger in the observable universe. Simultaneously, the space-based <strong>LISA</strong> (Laser Interferometer Space Antenna) will use a 2.5 million kilometer laser link to detect the low-frequency \"hum\" of supermassive black hole mergers, completing our map of the gravitational cosmos.</p>','LIGO (Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory) is the world\'s most sensitive measuring instrument, designed to detect the infinitesimal strains in spacetime caused by passing Gravitational Waves. It consists of two massive L-shaped interferometers located in Hanford, Washington, and Livingston, Louisiana. In university-level physics, LIGO represents the pinnacle of Precision Measurement, capable of detecting changes in distance smaller than one-thousandth the diameter of a proton.','LIGO (Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory) is the world\'s most sensitive measuring instrument, designed to detect the infinitesimal strains in spacetime caused by passing Gravitational Waves. It consists of two massive L-shaped interferometers located in Hanford, Washington, and Livingston, Louisiana. In university-level physics, LIGO represents the pinnacle of Precision Measurement, capable of detecting changes in distance smaller than one-thousandth the diameter of a proton.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.303ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 576 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-210E\" d=\"M137 683Q138 683 209 688T282 694Q294 694 294 685Q294 674 258 534Q220 386 220 383Q220 381 227 388Q288 442 357 442Q411 442 444 415T478 336Q478 285 440 178T402 50Q403 36 407 31T422 26Q450 26 474 56T513 138Q516 149 519 151T535 153Q555 153 555 145Q555 144 551 130Q535 71 500 33Q466 -10 419 -10H414Q367 -10 346 17T325 74Q325 90 361 192T398 345Q398 404 354 404H349Q266 404 205 306L198 293L164 158Q132 28 127 16Q114 -11 83 -11Q69 -11 59 -2T48 16Q48 30 121 320L195 616Q195 629 188 632T149 637H128Q122 643 122 645T124 664Q129 683 137 683Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"phase-detection-5c0f06f2\",\"signal-strength-96beb911\",\"precision-engineering-9653255f\",\"sky-mapping-bf3d811d\"]'),
(2009354,'nuclear-binding-energy','astrophysical-nuclear-physics','Nuclear Binding Energy','<p>Nuclear Binding Energy (\\( B \\)) is the energy required to completely disassemble an atomic nucleus into its constituent protons and neutrons. In university-level astrophysics, it is the fundamental measure of nuclear stability and the ultimate source of energy for all stars. Because the mass of a stable nucleus is always less than the sum of its parts—a phenomenon known as the <strong>Mass Defect</strong>—the \"lost\" mass is converted into the energy that powers the universe according to \\( E=mc^2 \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Mass Defect and Einstein\\s Relation</h3>\n                    <p>The origin of binding energy lies in the <strong>Mass-Energy Equivalence</strong>. When nucleons are brought together to form a nucleus, they enter a lower potential energy state due to the attractive <strong>Strong Nuclear Force</strong>. The difference in mass, \\( \\Delta m = [Z m_p + (A-Z) m_n] - M_{nucleus} \\), is released as energy. This binding energy is what keeps the nucleus together against the massive electrostatic repulsion of the protons, providing a stable \"well\" for subatomic matter.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Binding Energy per Nucleon (B/A)</h3>\n                    <p>A more revealing quantity is the <strong>Binding Energy per Nucleon</strong> (\\( B/A \\)). This value determines the relative stability of different isotopes. For light nuclei, \\( B/A \\) increases rapidly with mass number \\( A \\), making <strong>Nuclear Fusion</strong> an exothermic process. For very heavy nuclei, \\( B/A \\) decreases, making <strong>Nuclear Fission</strong> possible. This curve is the \"roadmap\" of cosmic chemistry, dictating which subatomic reactions release energy and which require an external power source.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Iron Peak and Stellar Fate</h3>\n                    <p>The curve of binding energy reaches its maximum at <strong>Iron-56</strong> (\\( ^{56}Fe \\)). Iron is the most tightly bound nucleus in nature; it represents the \"State of Maximum Stability.\" Because of the <strong>Iron Peak</strong>, stars can only produce energy through fusion until they reach iron. Beyond this point, any further fusion is endothermic (consumes energy), leading to the sudden loss of pressure support and the cataclysmic <strong>Core-Collapse Supernova</strong> that marks the death of massive stars.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Liquid Drop Model (SEMF)</h3>\n                    <p>To calculate binding energy theoretically, physicists use the <strong>Semi-Empirical Mass Formula (SEMF)</strong>, also known as the Liquid Drop Model. It models the nucleus as a drop of incompressible fluid and accounts for five distinct effects: \n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Volume Term:</strong> Proportional to the number of nucleons.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Surface Term:</strong> Corrects for nucleons at the boundary.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Coulomb Term:</strong> Accounts for proton-proton repulsion.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Asymmetry Term:</strong> Favors an equal number of protons and neutrons.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Pairing Term:</strong> Accounts for the stability of even-even nuclei.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    This model accurately predicts the \"Valley of Stability\" where most astrophysical isotopes reside.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Shell Model and Magic Numbers</h3>\n                    <p>While the liquid drop model treats the nucleus as a bulk fluid, the <strong>Nuclear Shell Model</strong> treats nucleons as occupying discrete energy levels, similar to electrons in an atom. When a shell is completely filled, the nucleus is exceptionally stable. These \"Magic Numbers\" (e.g., 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126) explain why certain elements like <strong>Helium-4</strong> and <strong>Oxygen-16</strong> are so abundant in the universe, providing the building blocks for planets and life.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Binding Energy in the Early Universe</h3>\n                    <p>During <strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN)</strong>, the binding energy of <strong>Deuterium</strong> was the primary bottleneck. Because deuterium is relatively weakly bound, it was easily destroyed by high-energy photons in the hot early universe. Synthesis of heavier elements could only begin once the universe cooled below the \"Deuterium Bottleneck\" temperature. This delay ensured that the primordial universe was composed primarily of hydrogen and helium, setting the initial chemical conditions for the first generation of stars.</p>','Nuclear Binding Energy is the energy required to completely disassemble an atomic nucleus into its constituent protons and neutrons. In university-level astrophysics, it is the fundamental measure of nuclear stability and the ultimate source of energy for all stars. Because the mass of a stable nucleus is always less than the sum of its parts—a phenomenon known as the Mass Defect—the \"lost\" mass is converted into the energy that powers the universe according to.','Nuclear Binding Energy (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.717ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 759 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\" d=\"M231 637Q204 637 199 638T194 649Q194 676 205 682Q206 683 335 683Q594 683 608 681Q671 671 713 636T756 544Q756 480 698 429T565 360L555 357Q619 348 660 311T702 219Q702 146 630 78T453 1Q446 0 242 0Q42 0 39 2Q35 5 35 10Q35 17 37 24Q42 43 47 45Q51 46 62 46H68Q95 46 128 49Q142 52 147 61Q150 65 219 339T288 628Q288 635 231 637ZM649 544Q649 574 634 600T585 634Q578 636 493 637Q473 637 451 637T416 636H403Q388 635 384 626Q382 622 352 506Q352 503 351 500L320 374H401Q482 374 494 376Q554 386 601 434T649 544ZM595 229Q595 273 572 302T512 336Q506 337 429 337Q311 337 310 336Q310 334 293 263T258 122L240 52Q240 48 252 48T333 46Q422 46 429 47Q491 54 543 105T595 229Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) is the energy required to completely disassemble an atomic nucleus into its constituent protons and neutrons. In university-level astrophysics, it is the fundamental measure of nuclear stability and the ultimate source of energy for all stars. Because the mass of a stable nucleus is always less than the sum of its parts—a phenomenon known as the Mass Defect—the \"lost\" mass is converted into the energy that powers the universe according to <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.699ex\" height=\"2.072ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 3845.1 915.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H757Q764 676 764 669Q764 664 751 557T737 447Q735 440 717 440H705Q698 445 698 453L701 476Q704 500 704 528Q704 558 697 578T678 609T643 625T596 632T532 634H485Q397 633 392 631Q388 629 386 622Q385 619 355 499T324 377Q347 376 372 376H398Q464 376 489 391T534 472Q538 488 540 490T557 493Q562 493 565 493T570 492T572 491T574 487T577 483L544 351Q511 218 508 216Q505 213 492 213Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\