 343 195 315T187 232V168V108Q187 78 188 68T191 55T200 49Q221 46 249 46H265V0H257L234 1Q210 2 183 2T145 3Q42 3 33 0H25V46H41Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"223C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1055.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-35\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(3396,0)\"><use data-c=\"A0\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(250,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"color-dynamics-58ac5023\",\"residual-range-d991f521\",\"trapped-charge-d89a31d6\",\"mass-source-a9043cc3\"]'),
(2009369,'parallax-baseline','astrophysical-parallax','Parallax Baseline','<p>In astrometry and surveying, the <strong>Baseline</strong> is the physical distance between two distinct points of observation used to determine the distance to a remote object via trigonometry. In the context of <strong>Stellar Parallax</strong>, the baseline is the most critical parameter for determining the precision and reach of the measurement. A longer baseline results in a larger apparent shift, allowing astronomers to resolve the distances to much further stars with higher accuracy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Astronomical Unit (1 AU) as a Baseline</h3>\n                    <p>The standard baseline for measuring stellar distances is the semi-major axis of the Earth\'s orbit around the Sun, defined as one <strong>Astronomical Unit (AU)</strong> (\\( \\sim 150 \\text{ million km} \\)). By observing a star from opposite sides of the orbit (a six-month interval), astronomers effectively use a total baseline of \\( 2 \\text{ AU} \\). However, by convention, the <strong>Parallax Angle</strong> (\\( p \\)) is calculated relative to a baseline of \\( 1 \\text{ AU} \\), forming the basis for the definition of the <strong>Parsec</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Inverse Relationship: Distance and Angle</h3>\n                    <p>The relationship between the baseline (\\( B \\)), the parallax angle (\\( p \\)), and the distance (\\( d \\)) is given by \\( d = B / \\tan p \\). Because stars are extremely distant, the angles involved are always smaller than one arcsecond. This necessitates the use of the <strong>Small Angle Approximation</strong>, where \\( d \\approx B / p \\). In this regime, the uncertainty in distance is directly proportional to the uncertainty in the angular measurement, proving that the maximum measurable distance is limited by the instrument\'s angular resolution for a fixed baseline.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Secular Parallax: The Solar Baseline</h3>\n                    <p>To measure distances to even more remote stars, astronomers utilize the <strong>Secular Parallax</strong>. This method uses the Sun\'s own motion through the local interstellar medium as a \"traveling\" baseline. The Sun moves at approximately \\( 20 \\text{ km/s} \\), creating a baseline that grows by about \\( 4 \\text{ AU} \\) every year. Over decades, this creates a much larger baseline than the Earth\'s orbit, allowing for the statistical measurement of distances to distant star clusters and the overall scale of the galactic neighborhood.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Moving-Cluster and Statistical Baselines</h3>\n                    <p>Advanced astrometry employs \"virtual\" baselines through the <strong>Moving-Cluster Method</strong>. By observing the common proper motions of a group of stars (like the Hyades), astronomers can derive their distance using the convergence point of their trajectories. This <strong>Statistical Baseline</strong> technique bypasses the need for simultaneous observations from different points, linking the group\'s internal velocity dispersion to the global geometric scale of the Milky Way.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Space-Based Baselines: Hipparcos and Gaia</h3>\n                    <p>The limitations of the Earth\'s atmosphere formerly constrained the effective baseline precision. Space-based observatories like <strong>Hipparcos</strong> and <strong>Gaia</strong> have revolutionized the field by measuring angles from above the atmospheric distortion. Gaia, in particular, utilizes the Earth-Sun L2 Lagrangian point to create a stable observation platform. Its ability to measure micro-arcsecond shifts effectively increases the \"useful\" depth of our 1 AU baseline, allowing for the creation of a 3D map of over a billion stars.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. VLBI and Earth-Diameter Baselines</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Radio Astronomy</strong>, the concept of the baseline is pushed to its extreme via <strong>Very Long Baseline Interferometry (VLBI)</strong>. By combining signals from radio telescopes on different continents, astronomers create a \"Virtual Telescope\" with a baseline equal to the diameter of the Earth. While primarily used for imaging active galactic nuclei and black hole horizons (like <strong>M87*</strong>), VLBI baselines also provide high-precision parallax measurements for water masers and pulsars, reaching into the distant reaches of the galaxy.</p>','In astrometry and surveying, the Baseline is the physical distance between two distinct points of observation used to determine the distance to a remote object via trigonometry. In the context of Stellar Parallax, the baseline is the most critical parameter for determining the precision and reach of the measurement. A longer baseline results in a larger apparent shift, allowing astronomers to resolve the distances to much further stars with higher accuracy.','In astrometry and surveying, the Baseline is the physical distance between two distinct points of observation used to determine the distance to a remote object via trigonometry. In the context of Stellar Parallax, the baseline is the most critical parameter for determining the precision and reach of the measurement. A longer baseline results in a larger apparent shift, allowing astronomers to resolve the distances to much further stars with higher accuracy.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"16.782ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 7417.8 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\" d=\"M55 166Q55 241 101 304T222 367Q260 367 296 349T362 304T421 252T484 208T554 189Q616 189 655 236T694 338Q694 350 698 358T708 367Q722 367 722 334Q722 260 677 197T562 134H554Q517 134 481 152T414 196T355 248T292 293T223 311Q179 311 145 286Q109 257 96 218T80 156T69 133Q55 133 55 166Z\"><