-TEX-N-6C\" transform=\"translate(1361,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6C\" transform=\"translate(1639,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"69\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-69\" transform=\"translate(1917,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"2D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2D\" transform=\"translate(2195,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"61\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-61\" transform=\"translate(2528,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"72\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-72\" transform=\"translate(3028,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"63\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-63\" transform=\"translate(3420,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"73\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-73\" transform=\"translate(3864,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"65\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-65\" transform=\"translate(4258,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"63\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-63\" transform=\"translate(4702,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6F\" transform=\"translate(5146,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6E\" transform=\"translate(5646,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"64\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-64\" transform=\"translate(6202,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"20\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-20\" transform=\"translate(6758,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" transform=\"translate(7008,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(7397,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"61\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-61\" transform=\"translate(8230,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"73\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-73\" transform=\"translate(8730,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" transform=\"translate(9124,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>, an order of magnitude improvement over ground-based observations.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.199ex\" height=\"1.717ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -759 971.9 759\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-V-2032\" d=\"M79 43Q73 43 52 49T30 61Q30 68 85 293T146 528Q161 560 198 560Q218 560 240 545T262 501Q262 496 260 486Q259 479 173 263T84 45T79 43Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(533,363) scale(0.707)\"><g data-c=\"2033\"><use data-c=\"2032\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-2032\"></use><use data-c=\"2032\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-2032\" transform=\"translate(275,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"resolution-jump-c2317343\",\"unified-map-0c2fe4ab\",\"state-vector-cf613c2f\",\"ladder-anchor-618d18ad\"]'),
(2009375,'gaia','astrophysical-parallax','The Gaia Mission','<p><strong>Gaia</strong> is an ambitious space mission by the European Space Agency (ESA) designed to create the most precise three-dimensional map of our galaxy. Launched in 2013, Gaia has reached a precision of <strong>Micro-arcseconds (\\( \\mu \\text{as} \\))</strong>, allowing it to measure <strong>Stellar Parallax</strong> for over a billion stars. In university-level astrophysics, Gaia represents the \"Final Frontier\" of astrometry, providing the data necessary to resolve the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> and chemical history of the entire Milky Way.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Micro-arcsecond Astrometry</h3>\n                    <p>The precision of Gaia is staggering: at \\( 10 \\mu \\text{as} \\), it can measure the width of a human hair from a distance of \\( 1,000 \\text{ km} \\). This sensitivity allows for accurate parallax measurements for stars up to the <strong>Galactic Center</strong> (\\( \\sim 8 \\text{ kpc} \\)) and beyond. Gaia utilizes two telescopes with a common focal plane, continuously scanning the sky as the spacecraft rotates, providing a self-calibrating <strong>All-Sky Solution</strong> of unprecedented accuracy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The 6-Dimensional Phase Space</h3>\n                    <p>Gaia goes beyond the standard 5 astrometric parameters by adding <strong>Radial Velocity</strong> (\\( v_r \\)) via an onboard spectrometer. This provides a complete <strong>6-Dimensional State Vector</strong> (\\( x, y, z, v_x, v_y, v_z \\)) for millions of stars. This \"Phase Space\" data allows astronomers to apply the laws of <strong>Analytical Mechanics</strong> to the entire galaxy, tracing the orbits of stars back in time to discover the origins of galactic structures.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Galactic Archaeology</h3>\n                    <p>By combining precision distances with chemical abundances (spectroscopy), Gaia enables the field of <strong>Galactic Archaeology</strong>. Astronomers can identify groups of stars that share the same chemical \"DNA\" and orbital history, revealing the past mergers and collisions that built the Milky Way. Gaia famously discovered the <strong>Gaia-Enceladus \"Sausage\"</strong>—the remains of a massive dwarf galaxy that collided with the Milky Way 10 billion years ago.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Fundamental Calibration of the Distance Ladder</h3>\n                    <p>Gaia provides the ultimate \"Zero Point\" for the <strong>Cosmic Distance Ladder</strong>. Its parallax measurements are so precise that they can resolve the subatomic variations in <strong>Cepheid Variable</strong> and RR Lyrae luminosities. This eliminates the primary source of uncertainty in measuring the <strong>Hubble Constant</strong> (\\( H_0 \\)), helping to resolve the \"Hubble Tension\" between local measurements and CMB-based cosmological models.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Testing General Relativity and Dark Matter</h3>\n                    <p>Gaia\'s precision is so high that it can detect the <strong>Gravitational Deflection</strong> of light by planets and even the Sun across the entire sky. This provides a constant, high-precision test of <strong>General Relativity</strong>. Furthermore, by measuring the precise acceleration of stars in the galactic disk, Gaia allows for a direct mapping of the <strong>Gravitational Potential</strong>, revealing the distribution of <strong>Dark Matter</strong> with far greater detail than ever before.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Gaia Data Releases (DR1, DR2, DR3)</h3>\n                    <p>The mission\'s impact is delivered through massive <strong>Data Releases</strong>, each containing billions of records. These releases have fundamentally altered almost every subfield of astronomy, from the study of <strong>Exoplanets</strong> (detecting orbital wobbles) to the physics of <strong>White Dwarfs</strong> and the mapping of the local <strong>Interstellar Medium</strong>. Gaia is not just a mission, but the primary reference library for the future of 21st-century astrophysics.</p>','Gaia is an ambitious space mission by the European Space Agency (ESA) designed to create the most precise three-dimensional map of our galaxy. Launched in 2013, Gaia has reached a precision of Micro-arcseconds, allowing it to measure Stellar Parallax for over a billion stars. In university-level astrophysics, Gaia represents the \"Final Frontier\" of astrometry, providing the data necessary to resolve the Total Dynamics and chemical history of the entire Milky Way.','Gaia is an ambitious space mission by the European Space Agency (ESA) designed to create the most precise three-dimensional map of our galaxy. Launched in 2013, Gaia has reached a precision of Micro-arcseconds (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.387ex\" height=\"1.502ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -448 1497 664\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-61\" d=\"M137 305T115 305T78 320T63 359Q63 394 97 421T218 448Q291 448 336 416T396 340Q401 326 401 309T402 194V124Q402 76 407 58T428 40Q443 40 