></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2191.5,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2960.5,1176.6) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(8473.3,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D440\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D440\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1003,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2299\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2299\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"chandrasekhar-mass-law-a4237e17\",\"relativistic-pressure-identity-7b977a9c\"]'),
(2009420,'neutron-degeneracy-pressure','degeneracy-pressure','Neutron Degeneracy Pressure','<p>Neutron Degeneracy Pressure is the outward pressure generated by the <strong>Pauli Exclusion Principle</strong> applied to neutrons. It is the fundamental force that supports <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutron-stars\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Neutron Stars</strong></a> against gravitational collapse. In university-level physics, it is the high-density counterpart to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electron-degeneracy-pressure\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electron Degeneracy Pressure</strong></a>, requiring much higher densities to become significant because the neutron\'s mass is approximately 1836 times greater than that of the electron.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/exclusion-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Exclusion Principle</strong></a> for Neutrons</h3>\n                    <p>Neutrons are <strong>Fermions</strong> with spin-1/2. According to quantum mechanics, two identical fermions cannot occupy the same set of quantum numbers. When matter is compressed to nuclear densities (\\( \\sim 10^{17} \\text{ kg/m}^3 \\)), the neutrons are forced into high-momentum states because the lower-energy states are already filled. This \"Quantum Crowding\" creates a kinetic pressure that is independent of thermal temperature of the star.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Scaling with Particle Mass</h3>\n                    <p>The magnitude of degeneracy pressure is inversely proportional to the mass of the fermion: \\( P_{deg} \\propto 1/m \\). Because neutrons are much heavier than electrons, they provide significantly less pressure for the same number density. This why a star must collapse much further—past the white dwarf stage—before neutron degeneracy can halt the pull of gravity. The resulting object is 1,000 times smaller and millions of times denser than a white dwarf.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Stiffening by the Strong Interaction</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike electrons, which only interact via the electromagnetic force, neutrons are subject to the <strong>Strong Nuclear Force</strong>. At the very high densities found in the center of a neutron star, the repulsive \"Hard Core\" of the nucleon-nucleon interaction provides an additional, even stronger source of pressure. This makes the real <strong>Equation of State</strong> for neutron stars \"stiffer\" than pure degeneracy models would suggest, allowing them to support higher masses.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Relativistic Limit and Softening</h3>\n                    <p>As neutrons are squeezed further, their <strong>Fermi Velocity</strong> approaches the speed of light. In this <strong>Relativistic Limit</strong>, the equation of state transitions from \\( P \\propto \\rho^{5/3} \\) to \\( P \\propto \\rho^{4/3} \\). This \"Softening\" of the pressure growth is a critical factor in the <strong>TOV Limit</strong>. It ensures that there is a maximum mass for any neutron-supported object, beyond which even the exclusion principle fails and a <strong>Black Hole</strong> is formed.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Role in Supernova Bounces</h3>\n                    <p>Neutron degeneracy pressure is the \"Trigger\" for the <strong>Bounce Shock</strong> in a core-collapse supernova. As the iron core of a massive star collapses, it hits the density where neutron degeneracy suddenly ignites. The collapse is halted abruptly, and the core \"bounces\" back like a spring. This bounce launches the shock wave that expels the rest of the star into space, seeding the galaxy with the products of <strong>r-process nucleosynthesis</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Uncertainty and Exotic Matter</h3>\n                    <p>The exact strength of neutron degeneracy pressure in the most massive neutron stars is one of the greatest mysteries in <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>. At extreme densities, neutrons might dissolve into a <strong>Quark-Gluon Plasma</strong> or form hyperons. These phase transitions \"soften\" the pressure, changing the star\'s radius and maximum mass. Measuring these effects via <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-waves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Waves</strong></a> is the only way to probe the limits of the exclusion principle in the strongest gravitational fields.</p>','Neutron Degeneracy Pressure is the outward pressure generated by the Pauli Exclusion Principle applied to neutrons. It is the fundamental force that supports Neutron Stars against gravitational collapse. In university-level physics, it is the high-density counterpart to Electron Degeneracy Pressure, requiring much higher densities to become significant because the neutron\'s mass is approximately 1836 times greater than that of the electron.','Neutron Degeneracy Pressure is the outward pressure generated by the Pauli Exclusion Principle applied to neutrons. It is the fundamental force that supports Neutron Stars against gravitational collapse. In university-level physics, it is the high-density counterpart to Electron Degeneracy Pressure, requiring much higher densities to become significant because the neutron\'s mass is approximately 1836 times greater than that of the electron.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13.334ex\" height=\"2.709ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -947.3 5893.4 1197.3\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\" d=\"M55 166Q55 241 101 304T222 367Q260 367 296 349T362 304T421 252T484 208T554 189Q616 189 655 236T694 338Q694 350 698 358T708 367Q722 367 722 334Q722 260 677 197T562 134H554Q517 134 481 152T414 196T355 248T292 293T223 311Q179 311 145 286Q109 257 96 218T80 156T69 133Q55 133 55 166Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273