 data-c=\"2248\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2248\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(5874.3,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,413) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-39\"></use><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(7664.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\"><use data-c=\"A0\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\"></use><use data-c=\"6B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6B\" transform=\"translate(250,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"67\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-67\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(1778,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2644,477.1) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"120-orders-of-error-85804735\",\"vacuum-mass-d88027e4\",\"theoretical-beauty-41cbb919\",\"mirror-math-abae5d63\"]'),
(2009436,'dark-energy-density','dark-energy-theory','Dark Energy Density (\\(\\Omega_\\Lambda\\))','<p><strong>Dark Energy Density</strong> (\\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\)) is the dimensionless parameter representing the fraction of the universe\'s total energy budget attributed to <strong>Dark Energy</strong>. In university-level cosmology, \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) is one of the \"Critical Parameters\" of the <strong>\\(\\Lambda\\)-CDM</strong> model. Current measurements from the <strong>Planck Mission</strong> indicate that dark energy accounts for approximately 68.3% of the cosmic energy density, making it the dominant factor in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/accelerated-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Accelerated Expansion</strong></a> and future of the manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Critical Density Basis</h3>\n                    <p>The total density of the universe \\( \\rho_{crit} \\) is the density required for space to be geometrically flat (\\( k=0 \\)). The dark energy density is defined relative to this value:\n                    \\[ \\Omega_\\Lambda = \\frac{\\rho_\\Lambda}{\\rho_{crit}} \\]\n                    Combined with the densities of <strong>Dark Matter</strong> (\\( \\Omega_c \\)) and <strong>Baryogenesis</strong> remnants (\\( \\Omega_b \\)), the sum determines the overall <strong>Topology</strong> of the . Observational evidence shows \\( \\sum \\Omega \\approx 1.0 \\), proving that our universe is \"Flat.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Non-Dilution of Density</h3>\n                    <p>A unique property of dark energy density (if modeled as a <strong>Cosmological Constant</strong>) is that it does not change as space expands. While matter density drops as \\( 1/a^3 \\), \\( \\rho_\\Lambda \\) remains constant. This means that as time passes, \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) grows while \\( \\Omega_m \\) shrinks. We live in the \"Lambda-dominated\" era, which began when the dark energy density finally exceeded the matter density about 6 billion years ago.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Measuring \\(\\Omega_\\Lambda\\) via CMB</h3>\n                    <p>The most precise measurement of the dark energy density comes from the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background</strong>. The position of the first acoustic peak in the CMB power spectrum is a \"Standard Ruler\" that measures the geometry of space. By combining CMB data with <strong>BAO</strong> and <strong>Type Ia Supernovae</strong>, astronomers break the <strong>Degeneracy</strong> between parameters, pinning down \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) to sub-percent precision.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Energy Density and the Hubble Parameter</h3>\n                    <p>The dark energy density dictates the late-time value of the <strong>Hubble Parameter</strong> (\\( H \\)).\n                    \\[ H^2 = H_0^2 [ \\Omega_m(1+z)^3 + \\Omega_\\Lambda ] \\]\n                    As \\( z \\to -1 \\) (the far future), the matter term vanishes, and the expansion rate becomes constant. This proves that \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) is the \"Cosmic Throttle,\" determining the speed at which the  will continue to stretch into eternity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Relation to the Cosmological Constant</h3>\n                    <p>In the standard model, \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) is directly proportional to Einstein\'s \\( \\Lambda \\): \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda = \\Lambda c^2 / 3H_0^2 \\). If dark energy is instead <strong>Quintessence</strong>, then \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) is a dynamic variable that traces the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of a scalar field. Discriminating between a fixed density and a varying density is the primary goal of the <strong>James Webb Space Telescope</strong> and the Vera Rubin Observatory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Budget of the Universe</h3>\n                    <p>The value of \\( \\Omega_\\Lambda \\) represents a fundamental shift in our <strong>Ontological</strong> view of the cosmos. It reveals that the \"Normal Matter\" we are made of is a mere 5% minority. The <strong>Dark Energy Density</strong> shows that the universe is primarily composed of a substance that does not emit light, cluster under gravity, or obey the standard <strong>Equations of State</strong> of atomic matter, leaving us to search for its origin <strong>\"New Physics.\"</strong></p>','Dark Energy Density is the dimensionless parameter representing the fraction of the universe\'s total energy budget attributed to Dark Energy. In university-level cosmology, is one of the \"Critical Parameters\" of the -CDM model. Current measurements from the Planck Mission indicate that dark energy accounts for approximately 68.3% of the cosmic energy density, making it the dominant factor in the Accelerated Expansion and future of the manifold.','Dark Energy Density (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.345ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.932ex\" height=\"1.938ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -704 1295.7 856.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A9\" d=\"M55 454Q55 503 75 546T127 617T197 665T272 695T337 704H352Q396 704 404 703Q527 687 596 615T666 454Q666 392 635 330T559 200T499 83V80H543Q589 81 600 83T617 93Q622 102 629 135T636 172L637 177H677V175L660 89Q645 3 644 2V0H552H488Q461 0 456 3T451 20Q451 89 499 235T548 455Q548 512 530 555T483 622T424 656T361 668Q332 668 303 658T243 626T193 560T174 456Q174 380 222 233T270 20Q270 7 263 0H77V2Q76 3 61 89L44 175V177H84L85 172Q85 171 88 155T96 119T104 93Q109 86 120 84T178 80H222V83Q206 132 162 199T87 329T55 454Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-39B\" d=\"M320 708Q326 716 340 716H348H355Q367 716 372 708Q374 706 423 547T523 226T575 62Q581 52 591 50T634 46H661V0H653Q644 3 532 3Q411 3 390 0H379V46H392Q464 46 464 65Q463 70 390 305T316 539L246 316Q177 95 177 84Q177 72 198 59T248 46H253V0H245Q230 3 130 3Q47 3 38 0