TEX-N-37\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(1000,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(1500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(3055.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"A0\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\"></use><use data-c=\"6B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6B\" transform=\"translate(250,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\" transform=\"translate(1611,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"73\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-73\" transform=\"translate(2111,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"separation-proof-894c4bc5\",\"gravity-gap-e81a65fa\",\"gas-drag-ee1ff6c9\",\"modified-fail-95646fd7\"]'),
(2009446,'planck-satellite','dark-matter-theory','The Planck Satellite and CDM','<p>The <strong>Planck Satellite</strong> was an ESA mission (2009–2013) that mapped the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB)</strong> with unprecedented precision. In university-level physics, Planck data is the \"Final Word\" on the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong>, providing the most accurate measurements of <strong>Dark Matter</strong> density (\\( \\Omega_c \\)). Its findings have cemented the <strong>\\(\\Lambda\\)-CDM</strong> model as the standard paradigm for understanding the  and the <strong>History of the Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Mapping the Acoustic Peaks</h3>\n                    <p>Planck measured the temperature fluctuations of the CMB down to angular scales of a few arcminutes. By analyzing the <strong>CMB Power Spectrum</strong>, specifically the ratio of the heights of the acoustic peaks, cosmologists can distinguish between baryonic matter and dark matter. The second and third peaks act as a \"Baryon Meter\" and a \"Matter Meter\"; Planck showed that there is five times more dark matter than atoms, a 10-sigma confirmation of <strong>Non-Baryonic Dark Matter</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Determining \\(\\Omega_c\\) and \\(\\Omega_b\\)</h3>\n                    <p>The Planck 2018 final results established the parameters of our universe:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Cold Dark Matter Density (\\( \\Omega_c h^2 \\)):</strong> \\( 0.120 \\pm 0.001 \\)</li>\n                            <li><strong>Baryon Density (\\( \\Omega_b h^2 \\)):</strong> \\( 0.0224 \\pm 0.0001 \\)</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    This precision allowed for the calculation of the total <strong>Cosmological Density</strong> (\\( \\Omega_m \\approx 0.31 \\)), proving that the universe is dominated by invisible matter that is \"Cold\" (moves at non-relativistic speeds).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. CMB Lensing: Mapping the Invisible</h3>\n                    <p>In addition to temperature, Planck measured <strong>CMB Lensing</strong>. As the CMB photons travel across the universe, they are slightly deflected by the gravitational pull of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a>. By mapping these tiny deflections, Planck created a 3D map of all the dark matter between us and the <strong>Surface of Last Scattering</strong>, providing an independent check on the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/accelerated-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Accelerated Expansion</strong></a> and growth of structure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Constraints on Dark Matter Candidates</h3>\n                    <p>Planck data placed strict limits on the properties of <strong>WIMPs</strong> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axions</strong></a>. By measuring the \"Optical Depth\" to <strong>Recombination</strong>, Planck showed that dark matter cannot annihilate too strongly in the early universe, as this would have altered the CMB pattern. These constraints guide <strong>LHC Searches</strong> and underground <strong>Direct Detection</strong> experiments, narrowing the \"Parameter Space\" for <strong>\"New Physics.\"</strong></p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Resolving the Flatness Problem</h3>\n                    <p>Planck confirmed that the universe is spatially flat to within 0.4% (\\( \\Omega_k \\approx 0 \\)). This <strong>CMB Flatness</strong> means that the total energy density must equal the <strong>Critical Density</strong>. Since matter only provides 31%, Planck indirectly provided the most robust evidence for <strong>Dark Energy</strong> (69%), completing the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> and resolving the <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong> of the 1990s.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Hubble Tension Legacy</h3>\n                    <p>A major legacy of the Planck mission is the <strong>Hubble Tension</strong>. Planck\'s measurement of the expansion rate (\\( H_0 \\approx 67.4 \\text{ km/s/Mpc} \\)) is in significant conflict with local measurements from <strong>Type Ia Supernovae</strong> (\\( H_0 \\approx 73 \\)). This discrepancy suggests that either the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cold-dark-matter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cold Dark Matter (CDM)</strong></a> model is slightly wrong or there is missing physics in the <strong>Early Universe</strong> light cone, driving the next generation of cosmological research.</p>','The Planck Satellite was an ESA mission (2009–2013) that mapped the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) with unprecedented precision. In university-level physics, Planck data is the \"Final Word\" on the Cosmic Budget, providing the most accurate measurements of Dark Matter density. Its findings have cemented the -CDM model as the standard paradigm for understanding the and the History of the Universe.','The Planck Satellite was an ESA mission (2009–2013) that mapped the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) with unprecedented precision. In university-level physics, Planck data is the \"Final Word\" on the Cosmic Budget, providing the most accurate measurements of Dark Matter density (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.514ex\" height=\"1.95ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -704 1111.2 861.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A9\" d=\"M55 454Q55 503 75 546T127 617T197 665T272 695T337 704H352Q396 704 404 703Q527 687 596 615T666 454Q666 392 635 330T559 200T499 83V80H543Q589 81 600 83T617 93Q622 102 629 135T636 172L637 177H677V175L660 89Q645 3 644 2V0H552H488Q461 0 456 3T451 20Q451 89 499 235T548 455Q548 512 530 555T483 622T424 656T361 668Q332 668 303 658T243 626T193 560T174 456Q174 380 222 233T270 20Q270 7 263 0H77V2Q76 3 61 89L44 175V177H84L85 172Q85 171 88 155T96 119T104 93Q109 86 120 84T178 80H222V83Q206 132 162 199T87 329T55 454Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(755,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>). Its findings have cemented the <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.57ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 694 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-39B\" d=\"M320 708Q326 716 340 716H348H355Q367 716 372 708Q374 706 423 547T523 226T575 62Q581 52 591 50T634 46H661V0H653Q644 3 532 3Q411 3 390 0H379V46H392Q464 46 464 65Q463 70 390 305T316 539L246 316Q177 95 177 84Q177 72 198 59T248 46H253V0H245Q230 3 130 3Q47 3 38 0H32V46H45Q112 51 127 91Q128 92 224 399T320 708Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"39B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-39B\"></use></g></g></g></svg>-CDM model as the standard paradigm for understanding the  and the History of the Universe.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.514ex\" height=\"1.95ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -704 1111.2 861.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A9\" d=\"M55 454Q55 503 75 546T127 617T197 665T272 695T337 704H352Q396 704 404 703Q527 687 596 615T666 454Q666 392 635 330T559 200T499 83V80H543Q589 81 600 83T617 93Q622 102 629 135T636 172L637 177H677V175L660 89Q645 3 644 2V0H552H488Q461 0 456 3T451 20Q451 89 499 235T548 455Q548 512 530 555T483 622T424 656T361 668Q332 668 303 658T243 626T193 560T174 456Q174 380 222 233T270 20Q270 7 263 0H77V2Q76 3 61 89L44 175V177H84L85 172Q85 171 88 155T96 119T104 93Q109 86 120 84T178 80H222V83Q206 132 162 199T87 329T55 454Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(755,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"matter-measure-d3ab39cc\",\"3d-shadow-58accdae\",\"statistical-map-fe815231\",\"speed-crisis-3ac48bce\"]'),
(2009447,'wimps','dark-matter-theory','WIMPs (Weakly Interacting Massive Particles)','<p><strong>Weakly Interacting Massive Particles (WIMPs)</strong> are the leading class of particle candidates for <strong>Dark Matter</strong>. In university-level physics, WIMPs are hypothesized heavy particles (\\( \\sim 10 \\text{ GeV} - 10 \\text{ TeV} \\)) that interact only through gravity and the weak nuclear force. Their popularity stems from the \"WIMP Miracle,\" where particles with these properties naturally provide the correct <strong>Cosmological Density</strong> required by the <strong>\\(\\Lambda\\)-CDM</strong> model.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The WIMP Miracle</h3>\n                    <p>The \"Miracle\" refers to a calculation in <strong>Early Universe</strong> thermodynamics. If dark matter is a thermal relic from the Big Bang, its current abundance is determined by its annihilation cross-section \\( \\langle \\sigma v \\rangle \\). For a particle interacting via the weak force, the predicted density (\\( \\Omega_{DM} h^2 \\approx 0.12 \\)) matches the value measured by the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong> almost perfectly. This suggests that dark matter may be a byproduct of the same subatomic physics that governs the <strong>Standard Model</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Supersymmetry and the LSP</h3>\n                    <p>The most concrete theoretical source for WIMPs is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/supersymmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Supersymmetry (SUSY)</strong></a>. In SUSY, every known particle has a \"Superpartner.\" The <strong>Lightest Supersymmetric Particle (LSP)</strong>, typically the <strong>Neutralino</strong>, is stable, neutral, and heavy—making it the ideal WIMP. This link provides a <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for dark matter, as it emerges from the internal symmetries of the  rather than being added as an arbitrary correction.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Direct Detection: The Underground Hunt</h3>\n                    <p>WIMPs are the primary target of <strong>Direct Detection</strong> experiments. Because they have a non-zero (though tiny) weak interaction, they should occasionally scatter off atomic nuclei. Ultra-sensitive detectors like LUX-ZEPLIN (LZ) and XENONnT use tonnes of liquid xenon buried deep underground to shield against cosmic rays, looking for the tiny \"Recoil\" of a nucleus hit by a WIMP traveling through the Earth.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Indirect Detection: Cosmic Annihilation</h3>\n                    <p>In high-density regions like the galactic center or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a>, WIMPs may collide and annihilate, producing <strong>Gamma Rays</strong>, neutrinos, or anti-matter. <strong>Indirect Detection</strong> efforts, such as the Fermi-LAT telescope, search for these \"Annihilation Signatures.\" While some \"Excesses\" have been reported in the galactic center, they remain controversial and could be attributed to <strong>Pulsars</strong> or other standard <strong>Astrophysical Dynamics</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. WIMPs at the LHC</h3>\n                    <p>If WIMPs exist at the TeV scale, they should be producible in high-energy collisions. In <strong>Collider Production</strong>, WIMPs would escape the detector without interacting, leaving a signature of \"Missing Transverse Momentum\" (\\( E_T^{miss} \\)). Despite a decade of searching at the <strong>Large Hadron Collider (LHC)</strong>, no definitive WIMP signal has been found, placing strict constraints on <strong>Supersymmetry</strong> and pushing the WIMP hypothesis into a <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The WIMP Forest and Decoupling</h3>\n                    <p>WIMPs are \"Cold\" dark matter, meaning they were moving at non-relativistic speeds when they decoupled from the primordial plasma. This allows them to cluster on all scales, forming a \"forest\" of small subhalos. This <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of clustering is essential for the formation of the first stars. If WIMPs are eventually ruled out, it would necessitate a shift toward <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axions</strong></a> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sterile-neutrinos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sterile Neutrinos</strong></a>, fundamentally changing our understanding of the subatomic .</p>','Weakly Interacting Massive Particles (WIMPs) are the leading class of particle candidates for Dark Matter. In university-level physics, WIMPs are hypothesized heavy particles that interact only through gravity and the weak nuclear force. Their popularity stems from the \"WIMP Miracle,\" where particles with these properties naturally provide the correct Cosmological Density required by the -CDM model.','Weakly Interacting Massive Particles (WIMPs) are the leading class of particle candidates for Dark Matter. In university-level physics, WIMPs are hypothesized heavy particles (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"19.623ex\" height=\"1.781ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 8673.2 787\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\" d=\"M55 166Q55 241 101 304T222 367Q260 367 296 349T362 304T421 252T484 208T554 189Q616 189 655 236T694 338Q694 350 698 358T708 367Q722 367 722 334Q722 260 677 197T562 134H554Q517 134 481 152T414 196T355 248T292 293T223 311Q179 311 145 286Q109 257 96 218T80 156T69 133Q55 133 55 166Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\