eld on galactic scales.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.022ex\" height=\"2.14ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864 6639.8 946\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\" d=\"M84 237T84 250T98 270H679Q694 262 694 250T679 230H98Q84 237 84 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-36\" d=\"M42 313Q42 476 123 571T303 666Q372 666 402 630T432 550Q432 525 418 510T379 495Q356 495 341 509T326 548Q326 592 373 601Q351 623 311 626Q240 626 194 566Q147 500 147 364L148 360Q153 366 156 373Q197 433 263 433H267Q313 433 348 414Q372 400 396 374T435 317Q456 268 456 210V192Q456 169 451 149Q440 90 387 34T253 -22Q225 -22 199 -14T143 16T92 75T56 172T42 313ZM257 397Q227 397 205 380T171 335T154 278T148 216Q148 133 160 97T198 39Q222 21 251 21Q302 21 329 59Q342 77 347 104T352 209Q352 289 347 316T329 361Q302 397 257 397Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-33\" d=\"M127 463Q100 463 85 480T69 524Q69 579 117 622T233 665Q268 665 277 664Q351 652 390 611T430 522Q430 470 396 421T302 350L299 348Q299 347 308 345T337 336T375 315Q457 262 457 175Q457 96 395 37T238 -22Q158 -22 100 21T42 130Q42 158 60 175T105 193Q133 193 151 175T169 130Q169 119 166 110T159 94T148 82T136 74T126 70T118 67L114 66Q165 21 238 21Q293 21 321 74Q338 107 338 175V195Q338 290 274 322Q259 328 213 329L171 330L168 332Q166 335 166 348Q166 366 174 366Q202 366 232 371Q266 376 294 413T322 525V533Q322 590 287 612Q265 626 240 626Q208 626 181 615T143 592T132 580H135Q138 579 143 578T153 573T165 566T175 555T183 540T186 520Q186 498 172 481T127 463Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\" d=\"\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-65\" d=\"M28 218Q28 273 48 318T98 391T163 433T229 448Q282 448 320 430T378 380T406 316T415 245Q415 238 408 231H126V216Q126 68 226 36Q246 30 270 30Q312 30 342 62Q359 79 369 104L379 128Q382 131 395 131H398Q415 131 415 121Q415 117 412 108Q393 53 349 21T250 -11Q155 -11 92 58T28 218ZM333 275Q322 403 238 411H236Q228 411 220 410T195 402T166 381T143 340T127 274V267H333V275Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-56\" d=\"M114 620Q113 621 110 624T107 627T103 630T98 632T91 634T80 635T67 636T48 637H19V683H28Q46 680 152 680Q273 680 294 683H305V637H284Q223 634 223 620Q223 618 313 372T404 126L490 358Q575 588 575 597Q575 616 554 626T508 637H503V683H512Q527 680 627 680Q718 680 724 683H730V637H723Q648 637 627 596Q627 595 515 291T401 -14Q396 -22 382 -22H374H367Q353 -22 348 -14Q346 -12 231 303Q114 617 114 620Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"36\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-36\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2208.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(3209.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(5195.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"A0\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\"></use><use data-c=\"65\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-65\" transform=\"translate(250,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"56\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-56\" transform=\"translate(694,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-zero-dipole-bb7ffbfd\",\"misalignment-17bca134\",\"axion-to-light-2753aaab\",\"quantum-fluid-37b4f3ea\"]'),
(2009449,'cold-dark-matter','dark-matter','Cold Dark Matter (CDM)','<p>Cold Dark Matter (CDM) is the standard model for dark matter, assuming it consists of particles that were moving at non-relativistic speeds (\\( v \\ll c \\)) at the time of <strong>Matter-Radiation Equality</strong>. In university-level cosmology, CDM is the \"Structural Engineer\" of the universe, providing the \"Bottom-Up\" hierarchy where small <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a> form first and merge to create the large-scale  web.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Temperature of Dark Matter</h3>\n                    <p>In physics, \"Cold,\" \"Warm,\" and \"Hot\" refer to the velocity of particles when they decoupled from the <strong>Early Universe</strong>.\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Hot (HDM):</strong> Particles move near \\( c \\) (e.g., neutrinos). They \"wash out\" small structures.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Cold (CDM):</strong> Particles move slowly (e.g., <strong>WIMPs</strong>, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axions</strong></a>). They allow for the formation of small clumps.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    Observation of the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB)</strong> and galaxy distribution confirms that dark matter must be Cold, as we see plenty of small galaxies.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Hierarchical Structure Formation</h3>\n                    <p>The defining feature of CDM is \"Hierarchical Growth.\" Because the particles are slow, they can stay trapped in tiny gravitational wells. These \"Seeds\" merge over 13.8 billion years to form larger and larger structures: stars \\(\\to\\) galaxies \\(\\to\\) clusters \\(\\to\\) filaments. This timeline is the <strong>History of the Universe</strong> as written in the distribution of matter, perfectly matched by CDM simulations like the Millennium Run.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The \\(\\Lambda\\)-CDM Paradigm</h3>\n                    <p>CDM is the \"C\" in the <strong>\\(\\Lambda\\)-CDM</strong> model. Combined with <strong>Dark Energy</strong> (\\( \\Lambda \\)), it provides a complete description of the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> and <strong>Expansion History</strong>. In this framework, CDM provides the \"Clumping\" while Dark Energy provides the \"Smoothing.\" The balance between these two is what determines the ultimate <strong>Fate of the Universe</strong> and the stability of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/galactic-rotation-curves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Galactic Rotation Curves</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Collisionless and Non-Baryonic</h3>\n                    <p>CDM is postulated to be <strong>Collisionless</strong>, meaning particles pass through each other with negligible electromagnetic or strong interaction. This proved by the <strong>Bullet Cluster</strong>, where the dark matter passed straight through a cluster merger. It must also be \"Non-Baryonic\" because <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bbn-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN)</strong></a> limits the number of normal atoms to only 5% of the <strong>Cosmological Density</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Small-Scale Crisis</h3>\n                    <p>Despite its success on large scales, CDM faces a <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong> on small scales. Simulations predict \"Cusps\" (sharp density peaks) and thousands of \"Subhalos\" (Missing Satellites Problem). Observations show \"Cores\" and fewer galaxies. Resolving this \"Small-Scale Crisis\" involves investigating whether dark matter is \"Warm\" or \"Self-Interacting,\" or if <strong>Astrophysical Dynamics</strong> (like supernova feedback) is more complex than assumed.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Is it a particle?</h3>\n                    <p>While CDM is modeled as a particle, it could be a property of <strong>Modified Gravity</strong> or <strong>Primordial Black Holes</strong>. However, because CDM perfectly predicts the \"Acoustic Peaks\" of the CMB measured by the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong>, any alternative must reproduce the same \"Cold\" clumping behavior. This makes CDM the most robust <strong>Ontological Commitment</strong> in modern cosmology, anchoring our map of the .</p>','Cold Dark Matter (CDM) is the standard model for dark matter, assuming it consists of particles that were moving at non-relativistic speeds at the time of Matter-Radiation Equality. In university-level cosmology, CDM is the \"Structural Engineer\" of the universe, providing the \"Bottom-Up\" hierarchy where small Dark Matter Halos form first and merge to create the large-scale web. In physics, \"Cold,\" \"Warm,\" and \"Hot\" refer to the velocity of particles when they decoupled from the Early Universe.','Cold Dark Matter (CDM) is the standard model for dark matter, assuming it consists of particles that were moving at non-relativistic speeds (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.152ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.596ex\" height=\"1.437ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -568 2473.6 635\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\" d=\"M173 380Q173 405 154 405Q130 405 104 376T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 294 29 316T53 368T97 419T160 441Q202 441 225 417T249 361Q249 344 246 335Q246 329 231 291T200 202T182 113Q182 86 187 69Q200 26 250 26Q287 26 319 60T369 139T398 222T409 277Q409 300 401 317T383 343T365 361T357 383Q357 405 376 424T417 443Q436 443 451 425T467 367Q467 340 455 284T418 159T347 40T241 -11Q177 -11 139 22Q102 54 102 117Q102 148 110 181T151 298Q173 362 173 380Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-226A\" d=\"M639 -48Q639 -54 634 -60T619 -67H618Q612 -67 536 -26Q430 33 329 88Q61 235 59 239Q56 243 