7Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 294 29 316T53 368T97 419T160 441Q202 441 225 417T249 361Q249 344 246 335Q246 329 231 291T200 202T182 113Q182 86 187 69Q200 26 250 26Q287 26 319 60T369 139T398 222T409 277Q409 300 401 317T383 343T365 361T357 383Q357 405 376 424T417 443Q436 443 451 425T467 367Q467 340 455 284T418 159T347 40T241 -11Q177 -11 139 22Q102 54 102 117Q102 148 110 181T151 298Q173 362 173 380Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-226A\" d=\"M639 -48Q639 -54 634 -60T619 -67H618Q612 -67 536 -26Q430 33 329 88Q61 235 59 239Q56 243 56 250T59 261Q62 266 336 415T615 567L619 568Q622 567 625 567Q639 562 639 548Q639 540 633 534Q632 532 374 391L117 250L374 109Q632 -32 633 -34Q639 -40 639 -48ZM944 -48Q944 -54 939 -60T924 -67H923Q917 -67 841 -26Q735 33 634 88Q366 235 364 239Q361 243 361 250T364 261Q367 266 641 415T920 567L924 568Q927 567 930 567Q944 562 944 548Q944 540 938 534Q937 532 679 391L422 250L679 109Q937 -32 938 -34Q944 -40 944 -48Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(762.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"226A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-226A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2040.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"cold-vs-hot-74b2a724\",\"hierarchy-dea15ce5\",\"ghost-matter-48330f34\",\"cmb-measure-7af4c3d2\"]'),
(2009450,'sterile-neutrinos','dark-matter-theory','Sterile Neutrinos','<p>Sterile Neutrinos are hypothetical right-handed neutrinos that do not interact via any of the fundamental forces of the <strong>Standard Model</strong> except gravity. In university-level particle physics, they are \"Sterile\" because they lack the weak-force charge (isospin) of active neutrinos. If they possess a mass in the keV range, they are prime candidates for <strong>Warm Dark Matter (WDM)</strong>, offering a potential solution to the <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong> of small-scale structure formation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Right-Handed Chirality and Mass</h3>\n                    <p>In the Standard Model, all observed neutrinos are left-handed. However, the discovery of <strong>Neutrino Oscillations</strong> proves that neutrinos have mass. The simplest way to generate this mass is through the <strong>Seesaw Mechanism</strong>, which requires the existence of heavy, right-handed \"Sterile\" partners. While the Seesaw often predicts ultra-heavy neutrinos, \"Sterile\" dark matter models focus on keV-scale masses, where the particle is stable enough to survive since the <strong>Big Bang</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Warm Dark Matter (WDM) and Structure</h3>\n                    <p>Sterile neutrinos are often classified as <strong>Warm Dark Matter</strong>. Unlike <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cold-dark-matter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cold Dark Matter (CDM)</strong></a>, which is stationary, keV neutrinos have a non-negligible \"Free-Streaming Length.\" This allows them to \"erase\" the smallest density fluctuations in the <strong>Early Universe</strong>. This property is highly attractive to cosmologists because it naturally resolves the \"Missing Satellites Problem\" and the \"Cusp-Core Problem\" observed in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Indirect Detection: The 3.5 keV Line</h3>\n                    <p>Sterile neutrinos are not perfectly stable; they can slowly decay into an active neutrino and a photon (\\( \\nu_s \\to \\nu_a + \\gamma \\)). In 2014, a mysterious X-ray emission line at <strong>3.5 keV</strong> was detected in galaxy clusters and the Andromeda galaxy. While still debated, this line is exactly what would be expected from the decay of a 7 keV sterile neutrino, providing a possible <strong>Indirect Detection</strong> of the dark matter particle.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Production via Active-Sterile Mixing</h3>\n                    <p>Sterile neutrinos can be produced in the early universe through \"Active-Sterile Mixing\" (the <strong>Dodelson-Widrow Mechanism</strong>). Active neutrinos in the primordial plasma occasionally \"flip\" into the sterile state due to their mass interference. The amount of mixing is constrained by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bbn-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN)</strong></a> and the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB)</strong>, as too much mixing would \"over-close\" the universe or alter the light-element abundances.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Laboratory Searches: KATRIN and TRISTAN</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike <strong>WIMPs</strong>, sterile neutrinos cannot be seen in <strong>Direct Detection</strong> tanks. Instead, physicists look for them in high-precision beta-decay experiments. The KATRIN experiment, and its upgrade TRISTAN, search for a \"kink\" in the energy spectrum of electrons from tritium decay. Such a kink would indicate the emission of a sterile neutrino, linking <strong>Analytical Mechanics</strong> of subatomic decay to the global <strong>Cosmological Density</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Is there room for sterile?</h3>\n                    <p>Sterile neutrinos sit at a crossroads in <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>. While they solve several small-scale problems, recent data from the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong> and dwarf galaxy surveys place tight limits on their free-streaming. If the 3.5 keV line is ruled out as an atomic transition from potassium or sulfur, the sterile neutrino remains the most viable \"Warm\" alternative to the <strong>\\(\\Lambda\\)-CDM</strong> paradigm, bridging the gap between neutrino mass and the  structure.</p>','Sterile Neutrinos are hypothetical right-handed neutrinos that do not interact via any of the fundamental forces of the Standard Model except gravity. In university-level particle physics, they are \"Sterile\" because they lack the weak-force charge (isospin) of active neutrinos. If they possess a mass in the keV range, they are prime candidates for Warm Dark Matter (WDM), offering a potential solution to the Theoretical Crisis of small-scale structure formation.','Sterile Neutrinos are hypothetical right-handed neutrinos that do not interact via any of the fundamental forces of the Standard Model except gravity. In university-level particle physics, they are \"Sterile\" because they lack the weak-force charge (isospin) of active neutrinos. If they possess a mass in the keV range, they are prime candidates for Warm Dark Matter (WDM), offering a potential solution to the Theoretical Crisis of small-scale structure formation.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"11.721ex\" height=\"1.808ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 5180.7 799\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\" d=\"M74 431Q75 431 146 436T219 442Q231 442 231 434Q231 428 185 241L137 51H140L150 55Q161 59 177 67T214 86T261 119T312 165Q410 264 445 394Q458 442 496 442Q509 442 519 434T530 411Q530 390 516 352T469 262T388 162T267 70T106 5Q81 -2 71 -2Q66 -2 59 -1T51 1Q45 5 45 11Q45 13 88 188L132 364Q133 377 125 380T86 385H65Q59 391 59 393T61 412Q65 431 74 431Z\"></path><path id=