Q385 55 378 6T357 -100Q341 -165 330 -190T303 -216Q286 -216 286 -188Q286 -138 340 32L346 51L347 69Q348 79 348 100Q348 257 291 317Q251 355 196 355Q148 355 108 329T51 260Q49 251 47 251Q45 249 31 249Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(527,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1186.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(2464.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(527,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3637.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4637.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"sterile-nature-a912a5aa\",\"free-streaming-16d08f84\",\"the-3-5-kev-mystery-9be4a1e1\",\"the-flip-de085c3a\"]'),
(2009451,'direct-detection','dark-matter-theory','Direct Detection of Dark Matter','<p><strong>Direct Detection</strong> is the experimental effort to observe the scattering of <strong>Dark Matter</strong> particles off atomic nuclei in a laboratory setting. In university-level physics, these experiments look for the tiny \"Nuclear Recoil\" energy produced when a <strong>WIMP</strong> or other candidate particle from the local <strong>Dark Matter Halo</strong> passes through the Earth and strikes a detector, providing the ultimate laboratory proof 4D dark matter existence.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Scattering Rate Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The number of scattering events expected in a detector depends on the particle mass (\\( m_\\chi \\)), the <strong>Cross-Section</strong> (\\( \\sigma \\)), and the local <strong>Density Profile</strong> of dark matter (\\( \\rho_0 \\approx 0.3 \\text{ GeV/cm}^3 \\)).\n                    \\[ R \\propto \\frac{\\rho_0}{m_\\chi} \\int \\frac{d\\sigma}{dq^2} v f(v) dv \\]\n                    Because the expected rate is extremely low (less than one event per tonne per year), detectors must be placed in deep underground mines (like SNOLAB or Gran Sasso) to shield against <strong>Cosmic Rays</strong> and radioactive background.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Detection Technologies: Xenon vs. Germanium</h3>\n                    <p>Modern direct detection uses two primary technologies:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Noble Liquid Time Projection Chambers (TPCs):</strong> Experiments like LUX-ZEPLIN (LZ) and XENONnT use large tanks of liquid xenon. They detect both scintillation light (S1) and ionized electrons (S2) to distinguish WIMP signals from background.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Cryogenic Bolometers:</strong> Experiments like SuperCDMS use germanium crystals cooled to millikelvin temperatures to detect the tiny \"Heat\" (phonons) of a single atomic recoil.</li>\n                        </ul></p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Spin-Dependent vs. Spin-Independent</h3>\n                    <p>WIMP interactions are categorized by their coupling to nuclear properties. \n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Spin-Independent (SI):</strong> The WIMP couples to the total mass of the nucleus (\\( \\propto A^2 \\)). This why heavy elements like Xenon (\\( A=131 \\)) are favored.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Spin-Dependent (SD):</strong> The WIMP couples only to the net nuclear spin. This probes the subatomic 4D structure of the <strong>Strong Interaction</strong> inside the nucleus.</li>\n                        </ul></p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Neutrino Floor</h3>\n                    <p>As detectors become more sensitive, they reach the <strong>Neutrino Floor</strong> (or Fog). At this level, coherent scattering of solar and atmospheric neutrinos off nuclei produces signals that look identical to dark matter. This represents a fundamental <strong>Epistemic Limit</strong> for direct detection: once reached, physicists must use directional information or annual modulation to distinguish a \"WIMP Wind\" from the constant \"Neutrino Rain.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Annual Modulation: The WIMP Wind</h3>\n                    <p>As the Earth orbits the Sun, its velocity relative to the <strong>Dark Matter Halo</strong> changes, creating a \"WIMP Wind\" that varies seasonally. The DAMA/LIBRA experiment has claimed to see such a modulation for decades. However, other experiments have failed to confirm this, leading to a <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong> over whether the signal is real or a seasonal environmental artifact.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Implications for Supersymmetry</h3>\n                    <p>The failure of direct detection experiments to find a WIMP so far has ruled out large portions of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/supersymmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Supersymmetry (SUSY)</strong></a> parameter space. Many \"Natural\" SUSY models predicted cross-sections that have already been excluded. This pushing researchers to explore lighter candidates like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axions</strong></a> or more complex <strong>Gauge Theories</strong>, shifting our <strong>Ontological</strong> search for the dark sector.</p>','Direct Detection is the experimental effort to observe the scattering of Dark Matter particles off atomic nuclei in a laboratory setting. In university-level physics, these experiments look for the tiny \"Nuclear Recoil\" energy produced when a WIMP or other candidate particle from the local Dark Matter Halo passes through the Earth and strikes a detector, providing the ultimate laboratory proof 4D dark matter existence. The number of scattering events expected in a detector depends on the particle mass, the Cross-Section, and the local Density Profile of dark matter.','Direct Detection is the experimental effort to observe the scattering of Dark Matter particles off atomic nuclei in a laboratory setting. In university-level physics, these experiments look for the tiny \"Nuclear Recoil\" energy produced when a WIMP or other candidate particle from the local Dark Matter Halo passes through the Earth and strikes a detector, providing the ultimate laboratory proof 4D dark matter existence. The number of scattering events expected in a detector depends on the particle mass (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.666ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.176ex\" height=\"1.666ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1403.6 736.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\" d=\"M21 287Q22 293 24 303T36 341T56 388T88 425T132 442T175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q303 442 384 442Q401 442 415 440T441 433T460 423T475 411T485 398T493 385T4