"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(543.7,289) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g><rect width=\"1667.3\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7120.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7605.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8155.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(8544.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9029.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9418.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9938.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-signal-26ecc44e\",\"the-mine-rule-97f806cc\",\"the-background-limit-1f8a3f46\",\"the-seasonal-shift-bd72d510\"]'),
(2009452,'indirect-detection','dark-matter-theory','Indirect Detection of Dark Matter','<p><strong>Indirect Detection</strong> is the search for the annihilation or decay products of <strong>Dark Matter</strong> in high-density cosmic environments. In university-level astrophysics, these experiments look for \"Excesses\" of <strong>Gamma Rays</strong>, neutrinos, or anti-matter (positrons, anti-protons) coming from regions like the galactic center, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a>, or dwarf spheroidal galaxies, providing a link between the  structure and subatomic <strong>WIMP</strong> physics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Annihilation Signal: Flux Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The expected flux of particles from dark matter annihilation is proportional to the square of the dark matter density (\\( \\rho^2 \\)), as it requires two particles to collide.\n                    \\[ \\frac{d\\Phi}{dE} = \\frac{\\langle \\sigma v \\rangle}{8\\pi m_\\chi^2} \\frac{dN}{dE} \\times J(\\psi) \\]\n                    The \"J-Factor\" encapsulates the <strong>Density Profile</strong> along the line of sight. By observing regions with high J-factors, such as the centers of galaxy clusters, astronomers maximize their chances of seeing the dark sector \"bleeding\" into visible light.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Gamma-Ray Excesses and Fermi-LAT</h3>\n                    <p>The Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope has detected a significant \"Galactic Center Excess\" (GCE) that matches the spectral shape expected from a \\( \\sim 40 \\text{ GeV} \\) WIMP. However, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the galactic center are complex; many researchers argue the excess is actually produced by a population of unresolved <strong>Pulsars</strong>. Resolving this \"GCE Mystery\" is a primary goal of <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>, as it sits on the edge of a definitive discovery.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Anti-Matter Probes: AMS-02</h3>\n                    <p>The Alpha Magnetic Spectrometer (AMS-02) on the International Space Station measures the flux of cosmic ray positrons and anti-protons. An excess of high-energy positrons has been observed, which could be a signature of <strong>Dark Matter</strong> annihilation. However, like the GCE, local <strong>Pulsars</strong> provide a viable alternative explanation, illustrating the difficulty of <strong>Indirect Detection</strong> in a \"messy\" baryonic universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Neutrinos from the Sun</h3>\n                    <p>WIMPs can become gravitationally trapped in the Sun or Earth. Over time, they sink to the center and annihilate, producing high-energy neutrinos that can escape. Neutrino telescopes like IceCube look for a \"Point Source\" of neutrinos from the solar core. Since the Sun is mostly hydrogen, this provides the most sensitive probe of <strong>Spin-Dependent</strong> dark matter interactions, independent of underground <strong>Direct Detection</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Dwarf Spheroidal Galaxies: The Clean Lab</h3>\n                    <p>Because dwarf spheroidal galaxies have very little gas or star formation, they are \"Astrophysically Clean.\" They have very high <strong>Mass-to-Light Ratios</strong>, meaning their <strong>Gamma Ray</strong> signal should be dominated by dark matter. The lack of detected signals from these dwarfs has placed the most stringent upper limits on the WIMP annihilation cross-section \\( \\langle \\sigma v \\rangle \\), challenging the \"WIMP Miracle\" predictions.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Cosmological Indirect Detection</h3>\n                    <p>Dark matter annihilation in the <strong>Early Universe</strong> would have injected energy into the primordial plasma, affecting the era of <strong>Recombination</strong>. This would leave a visible \"Spectral Distortion\" in the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB)</strong>. Data from the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong> shows no such distortion, proving that dark matter is highly stable and providing an absolute limit on the coupling of the dark sector to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-radiation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromagnetic Radiation</strong></a>.</p>','Indirect Detection is the search for the annihilation or decay products of Dark Matter in high-density cosmic environments. In university-level astrophysics, these experiments look for \"Excesses\" of Gamma Rays, neutrinos, or anti-matter (positrons, anti-protons) coming from regions like the galactic center, Dark Matter Halos, or dwarf spheroidal galaxies, providing a link between the structure and subatomic WIMP physics. The expected flux of particles from dark matter annihilation is proportional to the square of the dark matter density, as it requires two particles to collide.','Indirect Detection is the search for the annihilation or decay products of Dark Matter in high-density cosmic environments. In university-level astrophysics, these experiments look for \"Excesses\" of Gamma Rays, neutrinos, or anti-matter (positrons, anti-protons) coming from regions like the galactic center, Dark Matter Halos, or dwarf spheroidal galaxies, providing a link between the  structure and subatomic WIMP physics. The expected flux of particles from dark matter annihilation is proportional to the square of the dark matter density (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.157ex\" height=\"2.375ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 953.6 1049.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266