e data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2226,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2677,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1527.5,-686)\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><rect width=\"3266\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0,246.8)\"><use data-c=\"221A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-221A\"></use></g><rect width=\"3506\" height=\"60\" x=\"1020\" y=\"1636.8\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-scale-law-80c21ef5\",\"21-cm-probe-5de23c96\",\"milky-way-weight-803ff441\",\"energy-sum-08bdc3ff\"]'),
(2009456,'density-profile','dark-matter-theory','Density Profiles of Dark Matter','<p>A <strong>Density Profile</strong> (\\( \\rho(r) \\)) describes how the concentration of matter varies with the distance from the center of a system. In university-level cosmology, determining the density profile of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a> is the primary way to test different candidates for <strong>Dark Matter</strong>. It bridges the gap between the microscopic properties of particles (like <strong>WIMPs</strong> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axions</strong></a>) and the macroscopic 4D structure of galaxies.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The NFW Profile: The Standard Model</h3>\n                    <p>Large-scale N-body simulations of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cold-dark-matter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cold Dark Matter (CDM)</strong></a> consistently produce the <strong>Navarro-Frenk-White (NFW) Profile</strong>:\n                    \\[ \\rho(r) = \\frac{\\rho_s}{\\frac{r}{r_s}(1 + \\frac{r}{r_s})^2} \\]\n                    This profile features a \"Cusp\" at the center (\\( \\rho \\propto 1/r \\)) and a steeper decay at large radii (\\( \\rho \\propto 1/r^3 \\)). The NFW profile is a \"Universal\" result, appearing in everything from small subhalos to giant 1000-galaxy clusters.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Cusp-Core Problem</h3>\n                    <p>The \"Cusp\" predicted by CDM is one of the greatest <strong>Theoretical Crises</strong> in the field. Observations of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/galactic-rotation-curves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Galactic Rotation Curves</strong></a> in dwarf galaxies often show a \"Core\"—a flat central density profile (\\( \\rho \\approx \\text{const} \\)). This mismatch suggests that either dark matter is not purely collisionless (Self-Interacting Dark Matter) or that <strong>Astrophysical Dynamics</strong> (like gas bursts) \"puff out\" the center of the halo.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Einasto and Burkert Profiles</h3>\n                    <p>Physicists use alternative profiles to model specific systems:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Einasto Profile:</strong> A more flexible, non-power-law shape that often fits high-resolution simulations better than NFW.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Burkert Profile:</strong> A phenomenological core-profile used to represent galaxies where no cusp is seen, serving as the baseline for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mond\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND)</strong></a> comparisons.</li>\n                        </ul></p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Gravitational Lensing Inversion</h3>\n                    <p>Density profiles are mapped directly using <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-lensing\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Lensing</strong></a>. By observing the \"Arcs\" of light from background galaxies, astronomers can \"invert\" the distortion to find the total 4D mass distribution. This provides the most unbiased measure of the profile, proving that the <strong>Bullet Cluster</strong> and other mergers have profiles that are physically separated from the visible baryons.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Profiles in Self-Interacting Dark Matter (SIDM)</h3>\n                    <p>If dark matter particles can scatter off each other, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the halo center change. Frequent collisions transfer energy inward, \"flattening\" the NFW cusp into a core. Measuring the central density of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halos</strong></a> is therefore a direct probe of the <strong>Standard Model Conflict</strong>, potentially revealing a new subatomic force in the dark sector.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Cosmological Convergence</h3>\n                    <p>On the largest scales, the density profile of the entire universe is governed by the <strong>Scale Factor</strong>. The <strong>Cosmological Density</strong> of matter drops as \\( a^{-3} \\), while the <strong>Cosmological Constant</strong> remains uniform. The interaction between these different \"global profiles\" is what determines the <strong>Expansion History</strong> and the ultimate <strong>Big Freeze</strong> of the .</p>','A Density Profile describes how the concentration of matter varies with the distance from the center of a system. In university-level cosmology, determining the density profile of Dark Matter Halos is the primary way to test different candidates for Dark Matter. It bridges the gap between the microscopic properties of particles (like WIMPs or Axions) and the macroscopic 4D structure of galaxies.','A Density Profile (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.95ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1746 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295