(2009461,'hot-dark-matter','dark-matter','Hot Dark Matter (HDM)','<p>Hot Dark Matter (HDM) is a cosmological model assuming that dark matter consists of particles that were moving at relativistic speeds (\\( v \\approx c \\)) when they decoupled from the <strong>Early Universe</strong>. The primary candidate for HDM is light neutrinos. In university-level astrophysics, HDM is characterized by \"Top-Down\" structure formation, a process that is largely ruled out by observations of the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background</strong> and the early existence of galaxies.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Relativistic Decoupling</h3>\n                    <p>A particle is \"Hot\" if its mass is much smaller than the temperature at which it underwent <strong>Freeze-out</strong> (\\( m \\ll T_f \\)). Relativistic particles have very high kinetic energy, allowing them to travel long distances in a short time. This \"Free-Streaming\" prevents the particles from being trapped in small gravitational wells, smoothing out any density fluctuations on scales smaller than the <strong>Cosmological Horizon</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Free-Streaming and Silk Damping</h3>\n                    <p>The \"Free-Streaming Length\" (\\( \\lambda_{fs} \\)) is the distance an HDM particle travels before becoming non-relativistic. For light neutrinos, this length can be hundreds of megaparsecs. Any potential \"seed\" for a galaxy smaller than this length is simply washed away as the particles fly out of the clump. This process, similar to <strong>Silk Damping</strong> in baryons, makes it impossible for small 4D structures to form early in the <strong>History of the Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Top-Down Structure Formation</h3>\n                    <p>Because HDM erases small fluctuations, the first structures to form in an HDM-dominated universe would be massive, supercluster-sized \"Pancakes.\" These would later fragment into individual galaxies. This \"Top-Down\" model predicts that large-scale filaments should exist before galaxies. However, <strong>Hubble</strong> and <strong>JWST</strong> observations show that small galaxies existed very early (\\( z \\gt 10 \\)), providing a definitive <strong>Epistemic</strong> rejection of pure HDM models.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Neutrinos as HDM</h3>\n                    <p>Standard Model neutrinos are known to exist and have non-zero mass, making them a guaranteed component of the dark sector. However, the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong> data limits the total mass of all neutrino species to \\( \\sum m_\\nu \\lt 0.12 \\text{ eV} \\). This means neutrinos can only account for a tiny fraction of the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> (\\( \\Omega_\\nu \\sim 0.005 \\)), acting as a \"Hot\" perturbative correction to a primarily \"Cold\" universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Neutrino <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Constraint</h3>\n                    <p>Measuring the effect of HDM on the <strong>Large-Scale Structure</strong> is one of the few ways to measure the absolute mass scale of neutrinos. By looking at the \"suppression\" of power at small scales in galaxy surveys, cosmologists can weigh the neutrino. This links the macro-scale <strong>Expansion History</strong> of the  to the subatomic <strong>Analytical Mechanics</strong> of lepton mass generation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. HDM vs. CDM: The Structural Winner</h3>\n                    <p>The victory of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cold-dark-matter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cold Dark Matter (CDM)</strong></a> over HDM is one of the great triumphs of 20th-century cosmology. While HDM was an elegant idea, it failed to produce the \"lumpy\" universe we inhabit. Modern research into <strong>Warm Dark Matter</strong> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sterile-neutrinos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sterile Neutrinos</strong></a> attempts to find a middle ground, where particles are slow enough to form galaxies but fast enough to solve the \"Small-Scale Crisis\" of the <strong>NFW Profile</strong>.</p>','Hot Dark Matter (HDM) is a cosmological model assuming that dark matter consists of particles that were moving at relativistic speeds when they decoupled from the Early Universe. The primary candidate for HDM is light neutrinos. In university-level astrophysics, HDM is characterized by \"Top-Down\" structure formation, a process that is largely ruled out by observations of the Cosmic Microwave Background and the early existence of galaxies.','Hot Dark Matter (HDM) is a cosmological model assuming that dark matter consists of particles that were moving at relativistic speeds (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.094ex\" height=\"1.118ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -483 2251.6 494\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\" d=\"M173 380Q173 405 154 405Q130 405 104 376T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 294 29 316T53 368T97 419T160 441Q202 441 225 417T249 361Q249 344 246 335Q246 329 231 291T200 202T182 113Q182 86 187 69Q200 26 250 26Q287 26 319 60T369 139T398 222T409 277Q409 300 401 317T383 343T365 361T357 383Q357 405 376 424T417 443Q436 443 451 425T467 367Q467 340 455 284T418 159T347 40T241 -11Q177 -11 139 22Q102 54 102 117Q102 148 110 181T151 298Q173 362 173 380Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2248\" d=\"M55 319Q55 360 72 393T114 444T163 472T205 482Q207 482 213 482T223 483Q262 483 296 468T393 413L443 381Q502 346 553 346Q609 346 649 375T694 454Q694 465 698 474T708 483Q722 483 722 452Q722 386 675 338T555 289Q514 289 468 310T388 357T308 404T224 426Q164 426 125 393T83 318Q81 289 69 289Q55 289 55 319ZM55 85Q55 126 72 159T114 210T163 238T205 248Q207 248 213 248T223 249Q262 249 296 234T393 179L443 147Q502 112 553 112Q609 112 649 141T694 220Q694 249 708 249T722 217Q722 153 675 104T555 55Q514 55 468 76T388 123T308 170T224 192Q164 192 125 159T83 84Q80 55 69 55Q55 55 55 85Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(762.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2248\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2248\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1818.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) when they decoupled from the Early Universe. The primary candidate for HDM is light neutrinos. In university-level astrophysics, HDM is characterized by \"Top-Down\" structure formation, a process that is largely ruled out by observations of the Cosmic Microwave Background and the early existence of galaxies.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.094ex\" height=\"1.118ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -483 2251.6 494\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\" d=\"M173 380Q173 405 154 405Q130 405 104 376T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 294 29 316T53 368T97 419T160 441Q202 441 225 417T249 361Q249 344 246 335Q246 329 231 291T200 202T182 113Q182 86 187 69Q200 26 250 26Q287 26 319 60T369 139T398 222T409 277Q409 300 401 317T383 343T365 361T357 383Q357 405 376 424T417 443Q436 443 451 425T467 367Q467 340 455 284T418 159T347 40T241 -11Q177 -11 139 22Q102 54 102 117Q102 148 110 181T151 298Q173 362 173 380Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2248\" d=\"M55 319Q55 360 72 393T114 444T163 472T205 482Q207 482 213 482T223 483Q262 483 296 468T393 413L443 381Q502 346 553 346Q609 346 649 375T694 454Q694 465 698 474T708 483Q722 483 722 452Q722 386 675 338T555 289Q514 289 468 310T388 357T308 404T224 426Q164 426 125 393T83 318Q81 289 69 289Q55 289 55 319ZM55 85Q55 126 72 159T114 210T163 238T205 248Q207 248 213 248T223 249Q262 249 296 234T393 179L443 147Q502 112 553 112Q609 112 649 141T694 220Q694 249 708 249T722 217Q722 153 675 104T555 55Q514 55 468 76T388 123T308 170T224 192Q164 192 125 159T83 84Q80 55 69 55Q55 55 55 85Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(762.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2248\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2248\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1818.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"free-streaming-322fac8a\",\"big-to-small-b2cc523a\",\"the-1-rule-dea232c2\",\"power-suppression-0c3fb2a4\"]'),
(2009462,'mass-discrepancy','dark-matter','The Mass Discrepancy Problem','<p>The <strong>Mass Discrepancy Problem</strong> is the fundamental observation in <strong>Astrophysics</strong> that the gravitational mass required to explain the observed motions of stars and galaxies far exceeds the mass of the visible matter (baryons). In university-level physics, this problem is the historical origin of <strong>Dark Matter Theory</strong>, spanning over 90 years of research from the first cluster observations to modern <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-lensing\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Lensing</strong></a> maps of the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Zwicky and the Coma Cluster (1933)</h3>\n                    <p>The problem was first identified by Fritz Zwicky while studying the Coma galaxy cluster. He applied the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/virial-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Virial Theorem</strong></a> to the velocities of galaxies and found they were moving much too fast to be held together by the gravity of their visible stars. He called the missing component <em>Dunkle Materie</em> (Dark Matter). His calculation showed a discrepancy factor of nearly 100, marking the first time the <strong>Standard Model Conflict</strong> was quantified.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Galactic Rotation Anomaly</h3>\n                    <p>In the 1970s, the problem moved from clusters to individual galaxies. Observations of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/galactic-rotation-curves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Galactic Rotation Curves</strong></a> showed that rotation speeds remain high at large radii. According to the <strong>Newtonian Dynamics</strong> of visible matter, the speed should drop. This \"Local\" mass discrepancy proved that the invisible matter is not just in the intergalactic void, but forms a dense <strong>Dark Matter Halo</strong> around every galaxy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Lensing and the Weight of Space</h3>\n                    <p>Modern measurements use <strong>Weak Gravitational Lensing</strong> to resolve the mass discrepancy. By measuring how much a galaxy cluster distorts the images of background galaxies, astronomers \"weigh\" the total mass directly. These maps consistently show that visible gas and stars only account for \\( \\sim 15\\% \\) of the total pull, proving that the discrepancy is a geometric reality of the  curvature.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Baryon Fraction and BBN</h3>\n                    <p>The mass discrepancy is anchored by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bbn-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN)</strong></a>. BBN predicts the abundances of light elements (Helium, Deuterium) based on the density of atoms. These predictions are verified by the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong>, proving that the \"Missing <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>\" cannot be regular matter (like gas or dark stars) because that would have changed the <strong>Cosmic Abundance</strong> of subatomic elements in the first three minutes.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. MOND: Is it a \"Force Discrepancy\"?</h3>\n                    <p>A major <strong>Ontological</strong> question is whether the discrepancy is caused by \"Mass\" or a failure of \"Force\" laws. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mond\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND)</strong></a> argues that for very low accelerations, the gravitational force is stronger than \\( 1/r^2 \\). While MOND explains the discrepancy in galaxies well, it fails to explain the <strong>Bullet Cluster</strong>, where the gravity is centered in regions with zero visible mass, favoring the dark matter particle solution.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Impact on the History of the Universe</h3>\n                    <p>The mass discrepancy is not just a modern puzzle; it dictated the entire <strong>History of the Universe</strong>. Because dark matter provides 85% of the total matter density, it controlled the <strong>Expansion History</strong> and the timing of galaxy formation. Without the \"Extra Gravity\" of the mass discrepancy, the  would never have developed the high-density regions required for the birth of the first stars, linking the invisible dark sector to our own existence.</p>','The Mass Discrepancy Problem is the fundamental observation in Astrophysics that the gravitational mass required to explain the observed motions of stars and galaxies far exceeds the mass of the visible matter (baryons). In university-level physics, this problem is the historical origin of Dark Matter Theory, spanning over 90 years of research from the first cluster observations to modern Gravitational Lensing maps of the. The problem was first identified by Fritz Zwicky while studying the Coma galaxy cluster.','The Mass Discrepancy Problem is the fundamental observation in Astrophysics that the gravitational mass required to explain the observed motions of stars and galaxies far exceeds the mass of the visible matter (baryons). In university-level physics, this problem is the historical origin of Dark Matter Theory, spanning over 90 years of research from the first cluster observations to modern Gravitational Lensing maps of the . The problem was first identified by Fritz Zwicky while studying the Coma galaxy cluster.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.127ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.536ex\" height=\"1.824ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 2888.8 806\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\" d=\"M55 166Q55 241 101 304T222 367Q260 367 296 349T362 304T421 252T484 208T554 189Q616 189 655 236T694 338Q694 350 698 358T708 367Q722 367 722 334Q722 260 677 197T562 134H554Q517 134 481 152T414 196T355 248T292 293T223 311Q179 311 145 286Q109 257 96 218T80 156T69 133Q55 133 55 166Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-35\" d=\"M164 157Q164 133 148 117T109 101H102Q148 22 224 22Q294 22 326 82Q345 115 345 210Q345 313 318 349Q292 382 260 382H254Q176 382 136 314Q132 307 129 306T114 304Q97 304 95 310Q93 314 93 485V614Q93 664 98 664Q100 666 102 666Q103 666 123 658T178 642T253 634Q324 634 389 662Q397 666 402 666Q410 666 410 648V635Q328 538 205 538Q174 538 149 544L139 546V374Q158 388 169 396T205 412T256 420Q337 420 393 355T449 201Q449 109 385 44T229 -22Q148 -22 99 32T50 154Q50 178 61 192T84 210T107 214Q132 214 148 197T164 157Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-25\" d=\"M465 605Q428 605 394 614T340 632T319 641Q332 608 332 548Q332 458 293 403T202 347Q145 347 101 402T56 548Q56 637 101 693T202 750Q241 750 272 719Q359 642 464 642Q580 642 650 732Q662 748 668 749Q670 750 673 750Q682 750 688 743T693 726Q178 -47 170 -52Q166 -56 160 -56Q147 -56 142 -45Q137 -36 142 -27Q143 -24 363 304Q469 462 525 546T581 630Q528 605 465 605ZM207 385Q235 385 263 427T292 548Q292 617 267 664T200 712Q193 712 186 709T167 698T147 668T134 615Q132 595 132 548V527Q132 436 165 403Q183 385 203 385H207ZM500 146Q500 234 544 290T647 347Q699 347 737 292T776 146T737 0T646 -56Q590 -56 545 0T500 146ZM651 -18Q679 -18 707 24T736 146Q736 215 711 262T644 309Q637 309 630 306T611 295T591 265T578 212Q577 200 577 146V124Q577 -18 647 -18H651Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"223C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1055.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-35\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2055.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"25\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-25\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"zwicky-s-rule-8abd911d\",\"the-weight-ratio-08c66723\",\"atom-limit-0ea81752\",\"motion-proxy-74fe6e88\"]'),
(2009463,'fermi-energy','degeneracy-pressure','Fermi Energy (\\(E_F\\))','<p><strong>Fermi Energy</strong> (\\( E_F \\)) is the maximum energy level occupied by the particles of a <strong>Degenerate</strong> fermion system at absolute zero temperature. In university-level physics, \\( E_F \\) represents the \"Top of the <strong>Fermi Sea</strong>.\" It provides the quantitative scale for <strong>Degeneracy Pressure</strong>, determining the hardness of materials and the stability of stellar remnants like <strong>White Dwarfs</strong> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutron-stars\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Neutron Stars</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Zero-Temperature Limit</h3>\n                    <p>In a classical gas, if the temperature drops to zero, the particles stop moving. In a quantum gas, the <strong>Pauli Exclusion Principle</strong> forces particles to occupy the lowest available states. Even at \\( T=0 \\), the system has a non-zero energy because only two particles (spin up and spin down) can fit in each momentum state. The energy of the highest filled state is the <strong>Fermi Energy</strong>, proving that subatomic matter is never truly at rest.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Calculation from Density</h3>\n                    <p>For a non-relativistic gas of fermions with density \\( n \\), the Fermi energy is given by:\n                    \\[ E_F = \\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m} (3\\pi^2 n)^{2/3} \\]\n                    This relation shows that \\( E_F \\) depends only on the concentration of particles and their mass. In <strong>Metals</strong>, the Fermi energy is several electron-volts, which corresponds to a \"Fermi Temperature\" of tens of thousands of degrees, explaining why the properties of metals are dominated by quantum effects even at room temperature.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Fermi Velocity and Momentum</h3>\n                    <p>Associated with the Fermi energy are the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fermi-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fermi Momentum</strong></a> (\\( p_F \\)) and <strong>Fermi Velocity</strong> (\\( v_F \\)).\n                    \\[ v_F = \\sqrt{2E_F/m} \\]\n                    In extreme environments like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutron-stars\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Neutron Stars</strong></a>, the Fermi velocity can approach the <strong>Cosmic Speed Limit</strong> (\\( c \\)). This transition into the <strong>Relativistic</strong> regime changes the <strong>Equation of State</strong>, leading to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/chandrasekhar-limit\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Chandrasekhar Limit</strong></a> and the eventual formation of black holes.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Heat Capacity and the Fermi Surface</h3>\n                    <p>Only particles with energies near \\( E_F \\) (at the \"Fermi Surface\") can participate in thermal and electrical conduction. Because the vast majority of particles are \"locked\" in the <strong>Fermi Sea</strong> below \\( E_F \\), the <strong>Specific Heat</strong> of a degenerate gas is much lower than predicted by classical thermodynamics. This essential for understanding the <strong>Astrophysical Thermodynamics</strong> of cooling white dwarfs.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Fermi Energy in White Dwarfs</h3>\n                    <p>In a white dwarf, the Fermi energy of the electrons is high enough to resist the massive weight of the star. As the star is compressed, the density \\( n \\) increases, which raises \\( E_F \\). This increase internal energy provides the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-resistance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Resistance</strong></a> that balances gravity. The <strong>State Limit</strong> of the star is reached when the Fermi energy becomes comparable to the electron\'s rest mass energy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Real Scale</h3>\n                    <p>Fermi energy is one of the most successful predictions of <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong>. It bridges the gap between the subatomic <strong>Planck Constant</strong> and the macroscopic stiffness of matter. It identifies the  as a place where \"Nothingness\" (the vacuum) is regulated by a strict energy-accounting system, providing the <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for the volume and stability of all solid objects in the universe.</p>','Fermi Energy is the maximum energy level occupied by the particles of a Degenerate fermion system at absolute zero temperature. In university-level physics, represents the \"Top of the Fermi Sea.\" It provides the quantitative scale for Degeneracy Pressure, determining the hardness of materials and the stability of stellar remnants like White Dwarfs and Neutron Stars. In a classical gas, if the temperature drops to zero, the particles stop moving.','Fermi Energy (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.056ex\" height=\"1.878ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 1350.6 830\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H757Q764 676 764 669Q764 664 751 557T737 447Q735 440 717 440H705Q698 445 698 453L701 476Q704 500 704 528Q704 558 697 578T678 609T643 625T596 632T532 634H485Q397 633 392 631Q388 629 386 622Q385 619 355 499T324 377Q347 376 372 376H398Q464 376 489 391T534 472Q538 488 540 490T557 493Q562 493 565 493T570 492T572 491T574 487T577 483L544 351Q511 218 508 216Q505 213 492 213Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\" d=\"M48 1Q31 1 31 11Q31 