(2009465,'non-relativistic-degeneracy','degeneracy-pressure','Non-Relativistic Degeneracy','<p>Non-<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/relativistic-degeneracy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Relativistic Degeneracy</strong></a> occurs when the <strong>Fermi Velocity</strong> (\\( v_F \\)) of a degenerate gas is much less than the <strong>Cosmic Speed Limit</strong> (\\( c \\)). In university-level astrophysics, this is regime that describes low-mass <strong>White Dwarfs</strong> and the interiors of giant planets like Jupiter. It is characterized by a specific <strong>Power Law</strong> relating pressure to density (\\( P \\propto \\rho^{5/3} \\)), providing a stable equilibrium against gravity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The 5/3 Equation of State</h3>\n                    <p>In the non-relativistic limit, the kinetic energy of a particle is \\( E = p^2/2m \\). By integrating this over the <strong>Fermi Sea</strong>, we derive the pressure:\n                    \\[ P = \\frac{(3\\pi^2)^{2/3}\\hbar^2}{5m} n^{5/3} \\]\n                    This \\( 5/3 \\) exponent is a direct result of the 3D geometry of <strong>Phase Space</strong>. It ensures that as a star shrinks, its internal pressure rises faster than the gravitational pull (\\( P_{grav} \\propto R^{-4} \\)), leading to a stable, unique <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stability-threshold\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Stability Threshold</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Radius-<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Relationship</h3>\n                    <p>A counter-intuitive result of non-relativistic degeneracy is the <strong>Inverse Radius-<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Law</strong>: \\( R \\propto M^{-1/3} \\). This means that a more massive white dwarf is actually <em>smaller</em> and denser than a less massive one. As mass is added, the extra gravity squeezes the <strong>Fermi Sea</strong>, forcing the electrons into a smaller volume to generate the required balancing pressure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Giant Planets and Brown Dwarfs</h3>\n                    <p>Non-relativistic degeneracy is not limited to dead stars. The cores of <strong>Jupiter</strong> and <strong>Saturn</strong> are partially supported by electron degeneracy. <strong>Brown Dwarfs</strong>—objects too small for hydrogen fusion—are held up entirely by this pressure. This proves that <strong>Degeneracy Pressure</strong> is a universal feature of cold, high-density matter in the , linking planetology to <strong>Quantum Physics</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Stability and the Lane-Emden Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The structure of a non-relativistic degenerate object is modeled using the <strong>Lane-Emden Equation</strong> for a polytrope of index \\( n=1.5 \\). This mathematical framework shows that these objects are extremely stable. Unlike stars supported by thermal pressure (which can pulse or flicker), degenerate objects are \"Solid\" quantum bodies that maintain their shape over billions of years of <strong>History of the Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Transition to Relativistic Scales</h3>\n                    <p>As the mass of a degenerate object increases, the density rises, and the <strong>Fermi Energy</strong> (\\( E_F \\)) grows. When \\( E_F \\) becomes comparable to the particle\'s <strong>Rest Mass</strong> energy (\\( mc^2 \\)), the non-relativistic approximation fails. The velocity of the electrons begins to approach \\( c \\), and the star enters the <strong>Extremely Relativistic</strong> regime, where the \\( 5/3 \\) law \"softens\" toward \\( 4/3 \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Ideal Gas</h3>\n                    <p>The non-relativistic model assumes an \"Ideal\" degenerate gas where interactions between particles are negligible. In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutron-stars\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Neutron Stars</strong></a>, the <strong>Strong Interaction</strong> provides additional forces that modify this simple <strong>Power Law</strong>. However, for <strong>White Dwarfs</strong>, the non-relativistic model is one of the most accurate in all of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>, predicting the size of Earth-sized stars to within a few percent.</p>','Non-Relativistic Degeneracy occurs when the Fermi Velocity of a degenerate gas is much less than the Cosmic Speed Limit. In university-level astrophysics, this is regime that describes low-mass White Dwarfs and the interiors of giant planets like Jupiter. It is characterized by a specific Power Law relating pressure to density, providing a stable equilibrium against gravity.','Non-Relativistic Degeneracy occurs when the Fermi Velocity (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.483ex\" height=\"1.342ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -443 1097.6 593\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\" d=\"M173 380Q173 405 154 405Q130 405 104 376T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 294 29 316T53 368T97 419T160 441Q202 441 225 417T249 361Q249 344 246 335Q246 329 231 291T200 202T182 113Q182 86 187 69Q200 26 250 26Q287 26 319 60T369 139T398 222T409 277Q409 300 401 317T383 343T365 361T357 383Q357 405 376 424T417 443Q436 443 451 425T467 367Q467 340 455 284T418 159T347 40T241 -11Q177 -11 139 22Q102 54 102 117Q102 148 110 181T151 298Q173 362 173 380Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\" d=\"M48 1Q31 1 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H742Q749 676 749 669Q749 664 736 557T722 447Q720 440 702 440H690Q683 445 683 453Q683 454 686 477T689 530Q689 