ode=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(21099.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(21488.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(22210.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(23211,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D467\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(23676,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(422,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(24501.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"5D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-5D\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"expansion-speed-ecdcad3f\",\"hubble-time-4244e724\",\"hubble-tension-96da2442\",\"hubble-horizon-1fca1bd3\"]'),
(2009482,'keplerian-decline','galactic-rotation','The Keplerian Decline','<p>The <strong>Keplerian Decline</strong> is the expected decrease in orbital velocity of objects in a gravitationally bound system as their distance from the central mass increases. In university-level physics, it is the direct application of <strong>Kepler\'s Third Law</strong> and <strong>Newton\'s Universal Law</strong> to distributed systems. The observation that galaxies <em>lack</em> this decline is the primary evidence for the 4D existence of <strong>Dark Matter</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Physics of Central Force Orbits</h3>\n                    <p>In a system where mass is concentrated at the center (like the Solar System), the orbital velocity \\( v \\) is found by balancing the centripetal acceleration with the gravitational pull:\n                    \\[ \\frac{mv^2}{r} = \\frac{G M m}{r^2} \\implies v = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{r}} \\]\n                    This relation implies that \\( v \\propto r^{-1/2} \\). As you move from Earth (\\( 30 \\text{ km/s} \\)) to Pluto (\\( 4.7 \\text{ km/s} \\)), the speed drops precisely as predicted. This \"Decline\" is the signature of a mass-source that is finite and localized in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Expectation for Spiral Galaxies</h3>\n                    <p>In a spiral galaxy, most of the visible mass (stars and gas) is located in the central bulge and disk. Beyond the \"Optical Radius\" (\\( R_{opt} \\)), there is very little light. We would therefore expect the <strong>Rotation Velocity</strong> to follow a Keplerian decline in the outskirts. Measuring these velocities was the primary goal of 20th-century <strong>Stellar Dynamics</strong>, which eventually led to the <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong> of the \"Flat Curve.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Violation of the Decline</h3>\n                    <p>Observations by Vera Rubin and others in the 1970s using 21-cm radio lines showed that velocities remain constant (\\( v \\approx \\text{const} \\)) well beyond the visible stars. This \"Keplerian Violation\" proves that the <strong>Enclosed Mass</strong> \\( M(r) \\) must grow linearly with radius (\\( M(r) \\propto r \\)). Since we see no light to account for this mass, we must postulate a massive <strong>Dark Matter Halo</strong>, linking <strong>Newtonian Dynamics</strong> to <strong>Physical Cosmology</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Keplerian Decline in Accretion Disks</h3>\n                    <p>While galaxies do not show a Keplerian decline, the gas in an <strong>Accretion Disk</strong> around a <strong>Black Hole</strong> or protostar often does. Because these systems are dominated by a massive point source, the gas follows Kepler\'s laws. Measuring the specific \\( r^{-1/2} \\) decline in a disk is the primary way astronomers calculate the <strong>Initial Mass</strong> of subatomic and stellar-scale objects, providing a <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for their gravity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Alternatives: MOND and the Decline</h3>\n                    <p><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mond\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND)</strong></a> suggests that the Keplerian decline fails not because of mass, but because the law of gravity changes at low accelerations. MOND predicts a \"flat\" velocity \\( v^4 = G M a_0 \\). While MOND fits the data for galaxies, the lack of decline in clusters—and the <strong>Bullet Cluster</strong> offset—favors the \"Missing <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>\" (Dark Matter) explanation over \"Missing Physics,\" preserving the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> of Newton\'s laws.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Proof Darkness</h3>\n                    <p>The Keplerian decline is the \"Control\" experiment of the universe. It tells us how the  <em>should</em> behave if matter were only made of the <strong>Standard Model</strong> particles we see. The systematic failure of the decline across all spiral galaxies is the most robust <strong>Ontological</strong> evidence that we inhabit a <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> dominated by invisible dark energy and matter.</p>','The Keplerian Decline is the expected decrease in orbital velocity of objects in a gravitationally bound system as their distance from the central mass increases. In university-level physics, it is the direct application of Kepler\'s Third Law and Newton\'s Universal Law to distributed systems. The observation that galaxies lack this decline is the primary evidence for the 4D existence of Dark Matter.','The Keplerian Decline is the expected decrease in orbital velocity of objects in a gravitationally bound system as their distance from the central mass increases. In university-level physics, it is the direct application of Kepler\'s Third Law and Newton\'s Universal Law to distributed systems. The observation that galaxies lack this decline is the primary evidence for the 4D existence of Dark Matter.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.68ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"33.016ex\" height=\"5.566ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1717.3 14593.2 2460\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\" d=\"M21 287Q22 293 24 303T36 341T56 388T88 425T132 442T175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q303 442 384 442Q401 442 415 440T441 433T460 423T475 411T485 398T493 385T497 373T500 364T502 357L510 367Q573 442 659 442Q713 442 746 415T780 336Q780 285 742 178T704 50Q705 36 709 31T724 26Q752 26 776 56T815 138Q818 149 821 151T837 153Q857 153 857 145Q857 144 853 130Q845 101 831 73T785 17T716 -10Q669 -10 648 17T627 73Q627 92 663 193T700 345Q700 404 656 404H651Q565 404 506 303L499 291L466 157Q433 26 428 16Q415 -11 385 -11Q372 -11 3