(2009485,'hubble-constant','hubbles-law','The Hubble Constant (\\(H_0\\))','<p>The <strong>Hubble Constant</strong> (\\( H_0 \\)) is the current value of the <strong>Hubble Parameter</strong>, representing the instantaneous expansion rate of the universe today. In university-level cosmology, \\( H_0 \\) is the fundamental scale that determines the age, size, and <strong>Critical Density</strong> of the cosmos. Its measurement is the primary objective of the <strong>Cosmic Distance Ladder</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Units and Physical Meaning</h3>\n                    <p>\\( H_0 \\) is traditionally measured in kilometers per second per megaparsec (\\( \\text{km/s/Mpc} \\)). A value of \\( 70 \\text{ km/s/Mpc} \\) implies that for every million parsecs of distance, space is expanding by 70 kilometers every second. This \"Expansion Velocity\" is the 4D <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> of the <strong>Hubble-Lemaître Law</strong> (\\( v = H_0 d \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Determining the Hubble Time</h3>\n                    <p>The inverse of the Hubble constant, \\( t_H = 1/H_0 \\), defines the <strong>Hubble Time</strong>. For current measurements, this approximately 14.4 billion years. While not the exact age of the universe (which depends on the <strong>Density Parameter</strong>), it provides the 4D order of magnitude for the <strong>History of the Universe</strong> since the Big Bang.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Hubble Length and Horizon</h3>\n                    <p>The Hubble constant also sets the <strong>Hubble Length</strong> (\\( D_H = c/H_0 \\)). this is distance at which the recessional velocity equals the <strong>Velocity of Light</strong>. It defines the \"Causal Scale\" of the universe, dictating the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of what we can influence or observe in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Relation to Critical Density</h3>\n                    <p>The <strong>Critical Density</strong> of the universe is proportional to the square of the Hubble constant: \\( \\rho_c = 3H_0^2 / (8\\pi G) \\). This density determines the global <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/curvature-of-spacetime\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Curvature of Spacetime</strong></a>. Measuring \\( H_0 \\) is therefore equivalent to \"weighing\" the total energy-momentum of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/h0-tension\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The H0 Tension</strong></a>: The Modern Crisis</h3>\n                    <p>A major <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong> in physics is the 5-sigma discrepancy in \\( H_0 \\) measurements. \n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Local Measurements:</strong> (Supernovae) yield \\( \\sim 73 \\text{ km/s/Mpc} \\).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Global Measurements:</strong> (CMB) yield \\( \\sim 67 \\text{ km/s/Mpc} \\).</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    Resolving this tension is the primary motivator for <strong>\"New Physics\"</strong> research in the early .</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Precision Target</h3>\n                    <p>\\( H_0 \\) is the \"Speedometer\" of the universe. It is the most important <strong>Epistemic</strong> number in all of cosmology. By pinning down its value, we move from <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong> speculation to 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> about the fate of the cosmos, from the <strong>Big Bang</strong> to the eventual <strong>Big Freeze</strong>.</p>','The Hubble Constant is the current value of the Hubble Parameter, representing the instantaneous expansion rate of the universe today. In university-level cosmology, is the fundamental scale that determines the age, size, and Critical Density of the cosmos. Its measurement is the primary objective of the Cosmic Distance Ladder.','The Hubble Constant (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.375ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.868ex\" height=\"1.92ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1267.6 848.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43B\" d=\"M228 637Q194 637 192 641Q191 643 191 649Q191 673 202 682Q204 683 219 683Q260 681 355 681Q389 681 418 681T463 682T483 682Q499 682 499 672Q499 670 497 658Q492 641 487 638H485Q483 638 480 638T473 638T464 637T455 637Q416 636 405 634T387 623Q384 619 355 500Q348 474 340 442T328 395L324 380Q324 378 469 378H614L615 381Q615 384 646 504Q674 619 674 627T617 637Q594 637 587 639T580 648Q580 650 582 660Q586 677 588 679T604 682Q609 682 646 681T740 680Q802 680 835 681T871 682Q888 682 888 672Q888 645 876 638H874Q872 638 869 638T862 638T853 637T844 637Q805 636 794 634T776 623Q773 618 704 340T634 58Q634 51 638 51Q646 48 692 46H723Q729 38 729 37T726 19Q722 6 716 0H701Q664 2 567 2Q533 2 504 2T458 2T437 1Q420 1 420 10Q420 15 423 24Q428 43 433 45Q437 46 448 46H454Q481 46 514 49Q520 50 522 50T528 55T534 64T540 82T547 110T558 153Q565 181 569 198Q602 330 602 331T457 332H312L279 197Q245 63 245 58Q245 51 253 49T303 46H334Q340 38 340 37T337 19Q333 6 327 0H312Q275 2 178 2Q144 2 115 2T69 2T48 1Q31 1 31 10Q31 12 34 24Q39 43 44 45Q48 46 59 46H65Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q147 65 216 339T285 628Q285 635 228 637Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"