"0 -677 704 677\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D447\" d=\"M40 437Q21 437 21 445Q21 450 37 501T71 602L88 651Q93 669 101 677H569H659Q691 677 697 676T704 667Q704 661 687 553T668 444Q668 437 649 437Q640 437 637 437T631 442L629 445Q629 451 635 490T641 551Q641 586 628 604T573 629Q568 630 515 631Q469 631 457 630T439 622Q438 621 368 343T298 60Q298 48 386 46Q418 46 427 45T436 36Q436 31 433 22Q429 4 424 1L422 0Q419 0 415 0Q410 0 363 1T228 2Q99 2 64 0H49Q43 6 43 9T45 27Q49 40 55 46H83H94Q174 46 189 55Q190 56 191 56Q196 59 201 76T241 233Q258 301 269 344Q339 619 339 625Q339 630 310 630H279Q212 630 191 624Q146 614 121 583T67 467Q60 445 57 441T43 437H40Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D447\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D447\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"energy-source-f68c8f97\",\"particle-stream-bb06419f\"]'),
(2009505,'iron-peak','nucleosynthesis','Iron-56 and the Iron Peak','<p>The <strong>Iron Peak</strong> is a local maximum in the graph of elemental abundances, centered around <strong>Iron-56</strong> (\\( ^{56}Fe \\)) and its neighbors like nickel and cobalt. In university-level nuclear astrophysics, the Iron Peak represents the \"Thermodynamic Dead End\" of the universe, where the process of energy-releasing nuclear fusion terminates.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Maximum Binding Energy</h3>\n                  <p>Iron-56 has one of the highest <strong>Binding Energy</strong> per nucleon of any known isotope (approx. 8.8 MeV). This means the nucleons in an iron nucleus are more tightly \"Griped\" by the <strong>Strong Nuclear Force</strong> than in any other element. While fusing lighter elements (like hydrogen to helium) releases energy, fusing iron with other nuclei is <strong>Endothermic</strong>—it consumes energy. This creates a physical wall that stellar fusion cannot cross.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Silicon Burning and Core Collapse</h3>\n                  <p>In the final stages of a massive star\'s life, it undergoes <strong>Silicon Burning</strong>, which produces an iron-nickel core. Once the core is composed of iron, it can no longer generate the outward pressure needed to support the star against gravity. This leads to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/initial-singularity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Initial Singularity</strong></a> of the core-collapse process, resulting in a <strong>Supernova</strong> and the birth of a <strong>Neutron Star</strong> or <strong>Black Hole</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The Abundance Anomaly</h3>\n                  <p>The prevalence of iron in the cosmos is an \"Abundance Anomaly\" explained by the stability of its nucleus. Because stars spend millions of years synthesizing lighter elements only to get \"stuck\" at iron, the universe has accumulated a vast amount of it. This 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of nuclear stability is why iron is a major component of planetary cores, including the Earth\'s, dictating the <strong>Magnetohydrodynamics</strong> of our own world.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Post-Iron Synthesis: n-Capture</h3>\n                  <p>Any element heavier than the iron peak must be produced by <strong>Neutron Capture</strong> (the s-process and r-process) or <strong>Spallation</strong>. These processes require the massive energy inputs of supernovae or cosmic rays to \"Force\" nuclei past the iron barrier. The existence of gold and uranium proves that the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> has experienced violent, high-energy events that temporarily overrode thermodynamic equilibrium of the iron peak.</p>','The Iron Peak is a local maximum in the graph of elemental abundances, centered around Iron-56 and its neighbors like nickel and cobalt. In university-level nuclear astrophysics, the Iron Peak represents the \"Thermodynamic Dead End\" of the universe, where the process of energy-releasing nuclear fusion terminates. Iron-56 has one of the highest Binding Energy per nucleon of any known isotope (approx.','The Iron Peak is a local maximum in the graph of elemental abundances, centered around Iron-56 (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.536ex\" height=\"1.912ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 2005.1 844.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-35\" d=\"M164 157Q164 133 148 117T109 101H102Q148 22 224 22Q294 22 326 82Q345 115 345 210Q345 313 318 349Q292 382 260 382H254Q176 382 136 314Q132 307 129 306T114 304Q97 304 95 310Q93 314 93 485V614Q93 664 98 664Q100 666 102 666Q103 666 123 658T178 642T253 634Q324 634 389 662Q397 666 402 666Q410 666 410 648V635Q328 538 205 538Q174 538 149 544L139 546V374Q158 388 169 396T205 412T256 420Q33