29 117 631T101 634T58 637H25V683H36Q57 680 180 680Q315 680 324 683H335V637H313Q235 637 233 620Q232 618 232 462L233 307L379 449Q425 494 479 546Q518 584 524 591T531 607V608Q531 630 503 636Q501 636 498 636T493 637H489V683H499Q517 680 630 680Q704 680 716 683H722V637H708Q633 633 589 597Q584 592 495 506T406 419T515 254T631 80Q644 60 662 54T715 46H736V0H728Q719 3 615 3Q493 3 472 0H461V46H469Q515 46 515 72Q515 78 512 84L336 351Q332 348 278 296L232 251V156Q232 62 235 58Q243 47 302 46H335V0H324Q303 3 180 3Q45 3 36 0H25V46H58Q100 47 109 49T128 61V622Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1055.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(1555.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"A0\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(250,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"69\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-69\" transform=\"translate(1083,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6C\" transform=\"translate(1361,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6C\" transform=\"translate(1639,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"69\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-69\" transform=\"translate(1917,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6F\" transform=\"translate(2195,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"6E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6E\" transform=\"translate(2695,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"20\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-20\" transform=\"translate(3251,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"4B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-4B\" transform=\"translate(3501,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"low-binding-1299764a\",\"convective-marker-19ce1653\"]'),
(2009507,'spallation-physics','nucleosynthesis','Cosmic Ray Spallation','<p>Cosmic Ray Spallation is a non-thermal nucleosynthetic process where high-energy particles (cosmic rays) collide with atomic nuclei, causing them to fragment into lighter elements. In university-level astrophysics, spallation is the primary source of <strong>Lithium, Beryllium, and Boron</strong> in the universe, filling the gaps left by Big Bang and stellar nucleosynthesis.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Fragmentation Mechanism</h3>\n                  <p>When a relativistic proton or alpha particle from a <strong>Cosmic Ray</strong> strikes a heavier nucleus (like Carbon, Nitrogen, or Oxygen) in the interstellar medium, the energy of the collision exceeds the <strong>Binding Energy</strong> of the nucleons. The target nucleus is \"Shattered,\" releasing a shower of lighter fragments. This process is essentially \"Reverse Fusion,\" using kinetic energy from 4D <strong>Acceleration</strong> events to build lighter atoms from heavier ones.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Interstellar Nucleosynthesis</h3>\n                  <p>Unlike stellar fusion, which happens in dense, hot cores, spallation occurs in the cold, sparse <strong>Interstellar Medium (ISM)</strong>. This ensures that the newly created light elements are not immediately destroyed by further nuclear reactions. The 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> thus acts as a giant, slow-motion laboratory where cosmic rays continually enrich the chemical composition of galactic gas over billions of years.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Signatures of Cosmic Ray History</h3>\n                  <p>The abundances of spallation products provide a direct record of the <strong>Cosmic Ray Flux</strong> over the history of the galaxy. By measuring the ratios of different isotopes (e.g., \\( ^{10}B/^{11}B \\)), physicists can determine how long galactic matter has been exposed to high-energy radiation. This \"Cosmic Clock\" anchors <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the precise isotopes found in meteorites and starlight.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Laboratory Spallation</h3>\n                  <p>The same principles of spallation are used on Earth in <strong>Spallation Neutron Sources</strong>. By firing high-energy protons into a heavy metal target (like mercury or tungsten), researchers produce intense bursts of neutrons for <strong>Material Science</strong> and <strong>Quantum Physics</strong>. This allows for the study of the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>State Vector</strong></a> in solids, proving that the violent mechanics of cosmic rays can be harnessed for precision engineering.</p>','Cosmic Ray Spallation is a non-thermal nucleosynthetic process where high-energy particles (cosmic rays) collide with atomic nuclei, causing them to fragment into lighter elements. In university-level astrophysics, spallation is the primary source of Lithium, Beryllium, and Boron in the universe, filling the gaps left by Big Bang and stellar nucleosynthesis. When a relativistic proton or alpha particle from a Cosmic Ray strikes a heavier nucleus (like Carbon, Nitrogen, or Oxygen) in the interstellar medium, the energy of the collision exceeds the Binding Energy of the nucleons.','Cosmic Ray Spallation is a non-thermal nucleosynthetic process where high-energy particles (cosmic rays) collide with atomic nuclei, causing them to fragment into lighter elements. In university-level astrophysics, spallation is the primary source of Lithium, Beryllium, and Boron in the universe, filling the gaps left by Big Bang and stellar nucleosynthesis. When a relativistic proton or alpha particle from a Cosmic Ray strikes a heavier nucleus (like Carbon, Nitrogen, or Oxygen) in the interstellar medium, the energy of the collision exceeds the Binding Energy of the nucleons.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.141ex\" height=\"2.452ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 3598.2 1083.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\" d=\"M231 637Q204 637 199 638T194 649Q194 676 205 682Q206 683 335 683Q594 683 608 681Q671 671 713 636T756 544Q756 480 698 429T565 360L555 357Q619 348 660 311T702 219Q702 146 630 78T453 1Q446 0 242 0Q42 0 39 2Q35 5 35 10Q35 17 37 24Q42 43 47 45Q51 46 62 46H68Q95 46 128 49Q142 52 147 61Q150 65 219 339T288 628Q288 635 231 637ZM649 544Q649 574 634 600T585 634Q578 636 493 637Q473 637 451 637T416 636H403Q388 635 384 626Q382 622 352 506Q352 503 351 500L320 374H401Q482 374 494 376Q554 386 601 434T649 544ZM595 229Q595 273 572 302T512 336Q506 337 429 337Q311 337 310 336Q310 334 293 263T258 122L240 52Q240 48 252 48T333 46Q422 46 429 47Q491 54 543 105T595 229Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\" d=\"M423 750Q432 750 438 744T444 730Q444 725 271 248T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q68 -250 62 -245T56 -231Q56 -221 230 257T407 740Q411 750 423 750Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(33,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(790.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1549.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(533,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2839.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"impact-breakup-b7c2fa25\",\"non-stellar-source-707b33a4\"]'),
(2009508,'homogeneous-universe','physical-cosmology','The Homogeneous Universe','<p>A <strong>Homogeneous Universe</strong> is one where the distribution of matter and energy is uniform across all spatial locations at a fixed cosmic time. In university-level astrophysics, homogeneity is not an exact description of local space (which contains discrete stars and galaxies) but a 4D <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong> average that emerges on scales larger than the \"End of Greatness\" (approximately 300 million light-years).</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The End of Greatness</h3>\n                  <p>On small scales, the universe is highly non-homogeneous, characterized by the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the <strong>Cosmic Web</strong>. However, as we zoom out, the clusters and voids average out to a constant 4D <strong>Energy Density</strong> (\\( \\rho \\)). This transition scale marks the point where the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> becomes predictable via the 4D <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong>, anchoring 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in large-scale stability.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Translational Symmetry</h3>\n                  <p>Mathematically, homogeneity corresponds to <strong>Translational Invariance</strong> of the 3D spatial metric. This means that the 4D <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> depends only on cosmic time \\( t \\) and not on the spatial coordinates \\( x^i \\). This symmetry is the 4D <strong>Logical Basis</strong> for the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> model, allowing us to define a global time and a universal 4D <strong>Scale Factor</strong> that applies to all observers simultaneously.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Observational Tests</h3>\n                  <p>Homogeneity is tested using <strong>Galaxy Redshift Surveys</strong> (like SDSS) and the 4D <strong>Planck Mission</strong> data. By counting the number of galaxies in equal volumes at different parts of the sky, astronomers verify that the 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong> is filled uniformly. Any significant violation of homogeneity would imply that our local laws of physics might not be universal, challenging the 4D <strong>Epistemology</strong> of the entire field.</p>','A Homogeneous Universe is one where the distribution of matter and energy is uniform across all spatial locations at a fixed cosmic time. In university-level astrophysics, homogeneity is not an exact description of local space (which contains discrete stars and galaxies) but a 4D Statistical Mechanics average that emerges on scales larger than the \"End of Greatness\" (approximately 300 million light-years). On small scales, the universe is highly non-homogeneous, characterized by the 4D Causal Topology of the Cosmic Web.','A Homogeneous Universe is one where the distribution of matter and energy is uniform across all spatial locations at a fixed cosmic time. In university-level astrophysics, homogeneity is not an exact description of local space (which contains discrete stars and galaxies) but a 4D Statistical Mechanics average that emerges on scales larger than the \"End of Greatness\" (approximately 300 million light-years). On small scales, the universe is highly non-homogeneous, characterized by the 4D Causal Topology of the Cosmic Web.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.17ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 517 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"translational-symmetry-6053074f\",\"end-of-greatness-fc006735\",\"technical-relation-eb1365e6\"]'),
(2009509,'isotropic-universe','physical-cosmology','The Isotropic Universe','<p>An <strong>Isotropic Universe</strong> is one that appears the same in every direction to an observer. In university-level 4D <strong>Physical Cosmology</strong>, isotropy is the rotational symmetry of the 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong>. While an observer moving at high speed will see a dipole (a preferred direction due to 4D <strong>Doppler Shift</strong>), a <strong>Comoving Observer</strong> sees a perfectly uniform distribution of 4D <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background</strong> radiation.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Rotational Invariance</h3>\n                  <p>Isotropy implies that there are no \"Axes\" or preferred orientations in the . All 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> paths for light and matter are equivalent, regardless of their heading. This 4D <strong>Logical Basis</strong> simplifies the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/flrw-metric\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>FLRW Metric</strong></a> by ensuring that the 4D <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> components for the three spatial dimensions are identical, allowing for a single 4D <strong>Scale Factor</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Evidence from the CMB</h3>\n                  <p>The strongest evidence for isotropy comes from the 4D <strong>Planck Mission</strong>, which mapped the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background</strong>. The temperature of this radiation is 2.725 K in every direction, with variations only at the 4D <strong>Micro-Kelvin</strong> level. These 4D <strong>Fluctuations</strong> are the seeds of 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ontological-emergence-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ontological Emergence</strong></a>, but their statistical distribution is perfectly isotropic, anchoring 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the symmetry of the early universe.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Isotropy vs. Homogeneity</h3>\n                  <p>A universe can be isotropic at one point but not homogeneous (e.g., a spherically symmetric distribution like an explosion). However, if a universe isotropic at <em>every</em> point, it is mathematically proven to be homogeneous. This link is the 4D <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong> core of the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Principle</strong></a>, ensuring that the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> we observe is a true reflection of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p>','An Isotropic Universe is one that appears the same in every direction to an observer. In university-level 4D Physical Cosmology, isotropy is the rotational symmetry of the 4D Subatomic Vacuum. While an observer moving at high speed will see a dipole (a preferred direction due to 4D Doppler Shift), a Comoving Observer sees a perfectly uniform distribution of 4D Cosmic Microwave Background radiation.','An Isotropic Universe is one that appears the same in every direction to an observer. In university-level 4D Physical Cosmology, isotropy is the rotational symmetry of the 4D Subatomic Vacuum. While an observer moving at high speed will see a dipole (a preferred direction due to 4D Doppler Shift), a Comoving Observer sees a perfectly uniform distribution of 4D Cosmic Microwave Background radiation.','','[]','[]','[\"no-preferred-axis-9c22583a\",\"isotropy-marker-c6f8d62f\",\"technical-relation-f1fa03ed\"]'),
(2009510,'spatial-curvature-cosmology','physical-cosmology','Spatial Curvature and Global Geometry','<p><strong>Spatial Curvature</strong> (\\( k \\)) describes the 3D geometry of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> at a fixed cosmic time. In university-level cosmology, the curvature determines whether parallel light rays eventually meet, diverge, or stay parallel, and it dictates the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the universe\'s expansion and ultimate 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Three Geometric Possibilities</h3>\n                  <p>According to the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/flrw-metric\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>FLRW Metric</strong></a>, there are only three possible curvatures:\n                  <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Flat (\\( k=0 \\)):</strong> Euclidean geometry. Angles of a triangle sum to 180°. The universe is likely infinite.</li>\n                    <li><strong>Positive (\\( k=+1 \\)):</strong> Spherical geometry. The universe is finite but unbounded (like the surface of a 4D ball).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Negative (\\( k=-1 \\)):</strong> Hyperbolic geometry. Space \"Curves away\" from itself. The universe is infinite.</li>\n                  </ul></p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Curvature and the Density Parameter</h3>\n                  <p>The curvature is directly linked to the 4D <strong>Critical Density</strong> (\\( \\rho_c \\)) via the 4D <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong>. We define the curvature density parameter as \\( \\Omega_k = 1 - \\Omega_{total} \\). If the 4D <strong>Energy Density</strong> equals the critical density (\\( \\Omega=1 \\)), the 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong> is flat. This 4D <strong>Logical Basis</strong> proves that \"Geometry is Destiny,\" anchoring 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the mass-energy content of space.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The Flatness Problem</h3>\n                  <p>The universe currently appears to be extremely flat (\\( \\Omega_k \\approx 0 \\)). However, standard Big Bang 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> predicts that any initial curvature should grow over time. The fact that the  remains flat after 13.8 billion years is the 4D <strong>Flatness Problem</strong>, which is resolved by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-inflation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Inflation</strong></a>, which stretched the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> so much that any local curvature became undetectable.</p>','Spatial Curvature describes the 3D geometry of the 4D Block Universe at a fixed cosmic time. In university-level cosmology, the curvature determines whether parallel light rays eventually meet, diverge, or stay parallel, and it dictates the 4D Causal Topology of the universe\'s expansion and ultimate 4D Total Dynamics. According to the 4D FLRW Metric, there are only three possible curvatures: Flat : Euclidean geometry.','Spatial Curvature (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.179ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 521 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\" d=\"M121 647Q121 657 125 670T137 683Q138 683 209 688T282 694Q294 694 294 686Q294 679 244 477Q194 279 194 272Q213 282 223 291Q247 309 292 354T362 415Q402 442 438 442Q468 442 485 423T503 369Q503 344 496 327T477 302T456 291T438 288Q418 288 406 299T394 328Q394 353 410 369T442 390L458 393Q446 405 434 405H430Q398 402 367 380T294 316T228 255Q230 254 243 252T267 246T293 238T320 224T342 206T359 180T365 147Q365 130 360 106T354 66Q354 26 381 26Q429 26 459 145Q461 153 479 153H483Q499 153 499 144Q499 139 496 130Q455 -11 378 -11Q333 -11 305 15T277 90Q277 108 280 121T283 145Q283 167 269 183T234 206T200 217T182 220H180Q168 178 159 139T145 81T136 44T129 20T122 7T111 -2Q98 -11 83 -11Q66 -11 57 -1T48 16Q48 26 85 176T158 471L195 616Q196 629 188 632T149 637H144Q134 637 131 637T124 640T121 647Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) describes the 3D geometry of the 4D Block Universe at a fixed cosmic time. In university-level cosmology, the curvature determines whether parallel light rays eventually meet, diverge, or stay parallel, and it dictates the 4D Causal Topology of the universe\'s expansion and ultimate 4D Total Dynamics. According to the 4D FLRW Metric, there are only three possible curvatures:\n                  \n                    Flat (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.327ex\" height=\"1.756ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 2354.6 776\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\" d=\"M121 647Q121 657 125 670T137 683Q138 683 209 688T282 694Q294 694 294 686Q294 679 244 477Q194 279 194 272Q213 282 223 291Q247 309 292 354T362 415Q402 442 438 442Q468 442 485 423T503 369Q503 344 496 327T477 302T456 291T438 288Q418 288 406 299T394 328Q394 353 410 369T442 390L458 393Q446 405 434 405H430Q398 402 367 380T294 316T228 255Q230 254 243 252T267 246T293 238T320 224T342 206T359 180T365 147Q365 130 360 106T354 66Q354 26 381 26Q429 26 459 145Q461 153 479 153H483Q499 153 499 144Q499 139 496 130Q455 -11 378 -11Q333 -11 305 15T277 90Q277 108 280 121T283 145Q283 167 269 183T234 206T200 217T182 220H180Q168 178 159 139T145 81T136 44T129 20T122 7T111 -2Q98 -11 83 -11Q66 -11 57 -1T48 16Q48 26 85 176T158 471L195 616Q196 629 188 632T149 637H144Q134 637 131 637T124 640T121 647Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(798.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1854.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>): Euclidean geometry.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.179ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 521 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\" d=\"M121 647Q121 657 125 670T137 683Q138 683 209 688T282 694Q294 694 294 686Q294 679 244 477Q194 279 194 272Q213 282 223 291Q247 309 292 354T362 415Q402 442 438 442Q468 442 485 423T503 369Q503 344 496 327T477 302T456 291T438 288Q418 288 406 299T394 328Q394 353 410 369T442 390L458 393Q446 405 434 405H430Q398 402 367 380T294 316T228 255Q230 254 243 252T267 246T293 238T320 224T342 206T359 180T365 147Q365 130 360 106T354 66Q354 26 381 26Q429 26 459 145Q461 153 479 153H483Q499 153 499 144Q499 139 496 130Q455 -11 378 -11Q333 -11 305 15T277 90Q277 108 280 121T283 145Q283 167 269 183T234 206T200 217T182 220H180Q168 178 159 139T145 81T136 44T129 20T122 7T111 -2Q98 -11 83 -11Q66 -11 57 -1T48 16Q48 26 85 176T158 471L195 616Q196 629 188 632T149 637H144Q134 637 131 637T124 640T121 647Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"euclidean-space-e75403a1\",\"curvature-basis-ea041445\",\"technical-relation-505fe96d\"]'),
(2009511,'radiation-domination','physical-cosmology','The Radiation-Dominated Era','<p>The <strong>Radiation-Dominated Era</strong> was the first major epoch of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>, extending from the end of 4D <strong>Inflation</strong> to approximately 50,000 years after the Big Bang. During this period, the 4D <strong>Energy Density</strong> of the 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong> was primarily composed of relativistic particles (photons and neutrinos), whose 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> dictated a rapid expansion rate.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Scaling of Radiation Density</h3>\n                  <p>Because radiation possesses 4D <strong>Pressure</strong> (\\( P = \\rho c^2 / 3 \\)), its density dilutes faster than matter as space expands. While matter density falls as the volume (\\( a^{-3} \\)), radiation density falls as \\( a^{-4} \\) because the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a> also reduces the energy of each individual photon. This 4D <strong>Logical Basis</strong> explains why radiation, despite being dominant at the start, eventually gave way to 4D <strong>Matter Domination</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Expansion Dynamics (\\( a \\propto t^{1/2} \\))</h3>\n                  <p>In the radiation era, the 4D <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong> yield a scale factor expansion of \\( a(t) \\propto \\sqrt{t} \\). this is fastest standard (non-inflationary) 4D <strong>Expansion Rate</strong>. The high temperature and density of this epoch allowed for <strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis</strong>, where the first 4D <strong>Ontology</strong> of light elements (Hydrogen and Helium) was forged in the primordial 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Transition to Matter</h3>\n                  <p>As the universe cooled, the 4D <strong>Energy</strong> of radiation dropped below the rest-mass energy of matter. This point of <strong>Equality</strong> marks the end of the radiation era. This transition was critical for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ontological-emergence-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ontological Emergence</strong></a>, as the slower expansion rate of the 4D <strong>Matter Era</strong> allowed gravity to finally overcome the expansion and begin the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of galaxy formation.</p>','The Radiation-Dominated Era was the first major epoch of the 4D Block Universe, extending from the end of 4D Inflation to approximately 50,000 years after the Big Bang. During this period, the 4D Energy Density of the 4D Subatomic Vacuum was primarily composed of relativistic particles (photons and neutrinos), whose 4D Total Dynamics dictated a rapid expansion rate. Because radiation possesses 4D Pressure, its density dilutes faster than matter as space expands.','The Radiation-Dominated Era was the first major epoch of the 4D Block Universe, extending from the end of 4D Inflation to approximately 50,000 years after the Big Bang. During this period, the 4D Energy Density of the 4D Subatomic Vacuum was primarily composed of relativistic particles (photons and neutrinos), whose 4D Total Dynamics dictated a rapid expansion rate. Because radiation possesses 4D Pressure (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.116ex\" height=\"2.452ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 4471.1 1083.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\" d=\"M287 628Q287 635 230 637Q206 637 199 638T192 648Q192 649 194 659Q200 679 203 681T397 683Q587 682 600 680Q664 669 707 631T751 530Q751 453 685 389Q616 321 507 303Q500 302 402 301H307L277 182Q247 66 247 59Q247 55 248 54T255 50T272 48T305 46H336Q