,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D467\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(742.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2248\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2248\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1798.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-34\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(1000,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(1500,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"dynamic-shift-4093607b\",\"causal-imprint-495e51c8\",\"technical-relation-02c25a94\"]'),
(2009516,'cosmological-horizons','physical-cosmology','Cosmological Horizons and Causal Limits','<p><strong>Cosmological Horizons</strong> are the 4D <strong>Causal Boundaries</strong> of the observable universe. They define the maximum distance from which an observer could have received 4D <strong>Information</strong> since the beginning of time. In university-level relativity, these horizons are the 4D <strong>Logical Basis</strong> for the 4D <strong>Horizon Problem</strong> and provide the 4D <strong>Light Cone Boundary</strong> for 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Particle Horizon</h3>\n                  <p>The <strong>Particle Horizon</strong> (\\( d_p \\)) is the most fundamental limit. It is the distance light has traveled since the Big Bang, accounting for the 4D <strong>Space Expansion</strong>. It represents the \"Radius\" of the observable 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>. Any event beyond this horizon is 4D <strong>Spacelike</strong> and cannot have influenced us, anchoring 4D <strong>Ontology</strong> in a finite, light-limited volume.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. The Event Horizon</h3>\n                  <p>In an accelerating universe dominated by 4D <strong>Dark Energy</strong>, there is also a <strong>Cosmological Event Horizon</strong>. this is maximum distance from which light emitted <em>today</em> will ever reach us in the future. As 4D <strong>Expansion Rate</strong> increases, distant galaxies \"Cross\" this horizon and disappear from our 4D <strong>Epistemology</strong> forever, leading to a 4D <strong>Holistic</strong> isolation of the Local Group.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The Horizon Problem</h3>\n                  <p>The 4D <strong>Horizon Problem</strong> arises because the 4D <strong>Particle Horizon</strong> at the time of <strong>Recombination</strong> was very small. This means regions on opposite sides of the sky were never in 4D <strong>Causal</strong> contact, yet they have the exact same 4D <strong>CMB</strong> temperature. This conflict between 4D <strong>Locality</strong> and 4D <strong>Homogeneity</strong> is the primary 4D <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong> justification for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-inflation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Inflation</strong></a>.</p>','Cosmological Horizons are the 4D Causal Boundaries of the observable universe. They define the maximum distance from which an observer could have received 4D Information since the beginning of time. In university-level relativity, these horizons are the 4D Logical Basis for the 4D Horizon Problem and provide the 4D Light Cone Boundary for 4D Scientific Realism.','Cosmological Horizons are the 4D Causal Boundaries of the observable universe. They define the maximum distance from which an observer could have received 4D Information since the beginning of time. In university-level relativity, these horizons are the 4D Logical Basis for the 4D Horizon Problem and provide the 4D Light Cone Boundary for 4D Scientific Realism.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.65ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.169ex\" height=\"2.22ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 958.7 981.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\" d=\"M366 683Q367 683 438 688T511 694Q523 694 523 686Q523 679 450 384T375 83T374 68Q374 26 402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487H491Q506 153 506 145Q506 140 503 129Q490 79 473 48T445 8T417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157Q33 205 53 255T101 341Q148 398 195 420T280 442Q336 442 364 400Q369 394 369 396Q370 400 396 505T424 616Q424 629 417 632T378 637H357Q351 643 351 645T353 664Q358 683 366 683ZM352 326Q329 405 277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q233 26 290 98L298 109L352 326Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45D\" d=\"M23 287Q24 290 25 295T30 317T40 348T55 381T75 411T101 433T134 442Q209 442 230 378L240 387Q302 442 358 442Q423 442 460 395T497 281Q497 173 421 82T249 -10Q227 -10 210 -4Q199 1 187 11T168 28L161 36Q160 35 139 -51T118 -138Q118 -144 126 -145T163 -148H188Q194 -155 194 -157T191 -175Q188 -187 185 -190T172 -194Q170 -194 161 -194T127 -193T65 -192Q-5 -192 -24 -194H-32Q-39 -187 -39 -183Q-37 -156 -26 -148H-6Q28 -147 33 -136Q36 -130 94 103T155 350Q156 355 156 364Q156 405 131 405Q109 405 94 377T71 316T59 280Q57 278 43 278H29Q23 284 23 287ZM178 102Q200 26 252 26Q282 26 310 49T356 107Q374 141 392 215T411 325V331Q411 405 350 405Q339 405 328 402T306 393T286 380T269 365T254 350T243 336T235 326L232 322Q232 321 229 308T218 264T204 212Q178 106 178 102Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(553,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"observable-limit-29fe4b3a\",\"future-limit-8ea1f198\",\"technical-relation-73092db6\"]'),
(2009517,'planck-mission','physical-cosmology','The Planck Mission and Precision Cosmology','<p>The <strong>Planck Mission</strong> (ESA, 2009–2013) was the definitive satellite experiment for mapping the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB)</strong> with unprecedented 4D <strong>Spectral Resolution</strong>. In university-level cosmology, Planck results are the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> gold standard, providing the precise numerical values for the 4D <strong>Density Parameters</strong> that define our 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Mapping the Primordial Vacuum</h3>\n                  <p>Planck measured the 4D <strong>Isotropy</strong> of the early 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong> by detecting temperature fluctuations of millionths of a degree. These \"Anisotropies\" are the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the early universe, fossilized at the moment of <strong>Recombination</strong>. The statistical pattern of these fluctuations (the <strong>Power Spectrum</strong>) matches the predictions of 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-inflation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Inflation</strong></a> with incredible 4D 99.9% accuracy.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Determining the \"Cosmic Recipe\"</h3>\n                  <p>By fitting the 4D <strong>Lambda-CDM Model</strong> to the data, Planck determined the exact composition of the universe:\n                  <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Baryonic Matter:</strong> 4.9% (The \"stuff\" of stars and people).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Dark Matter:</strong> 26.8% (The invisible scaffolding).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Dark Energy:</strong> 68.3% (The driver of expansion).</li>\n                  </ul>\n                  These 4D <strong>Ontological Commitments</strong> confirm that our universe is spatially flat (\\( k=0 \\)), anchoring 4D <strong>Mathematical Realism</strong> in 4D <strong>Metric</strong> measurements.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The H0 Tension</h3>\n                  <p>While Planck provided the most precise value for the 4D <strong>Hubble Parameter</strong> (\\( H_0 \\approx 67.4 \\text{ km/s/Mpc} \\)), this value conflicts with local measurements using <strong>Type Ia Supernovae</strong>. This \"Tension\" is a 4D <strong>Epistemology</strong> crisis, potentially suggesting that our 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> model (Lambda-CDM) is missing a fundamental piece of 4D <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>, such as 4D <strong>Early Dark Energy</strong>.</p>','The Planck Mission (ESA, 2009–2013) was the definitive satellite experiment for mapping the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) with unprecedented 4D Spectral Resolution. In university-level cosmology, Planck results are the 4D Scientific Realism gold standard, providing the precise numerical values for the 4D Density Parameters that define our 4D Block Universe. Planck measured the 4D Isotropy of the early 4D Subatomic Vacuum by detecting temperature fluctuations of millionths of a degree.','The Planck Mission (ESA, 2009–2013) was the definitive satellite experiment for mapping the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) with unprecedented 4D Spectral Resolution. In university-level cosmology, Planck results are the 4D Scientific Realism gold standard, providing the precise numerical values for the 4D Density Parameters that define our 4D Block Universe. Planck measured the 4D Isotropy of the early 4D Subatomic Vacuum by detecting temperature fluctuations of millionths of a degree.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.327ex\" height=\"1.756ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 2354.6 776\" 