60T174 456Q174 380 222 233T270 20Q270 7 263 0H77V2Q76 3 61 89L44 175V177H84L85 172Q85 171 88 155T96 119T104 93Q109 86 120 84T178 80H222V83Q206 132 162 199T87 329T55 454Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\" d=\"M184 600Q184 624 203 642T247 661Q265 661 277 649T290 619Q290 596 270 577T226 557Q211 557 198 567T184 600ZM21 287Q21 295 30 318T54 369T98 420T158 442Q197 442 223 419T250 357Q250 340 236 301T196 196T154 83Q149 61 149 51Q149 26 166 26Q175 26 185 29T208 43T235 78T260 137Q263 149 265 151T282 153Q302 153 302 143Q302 135 293 112T268 61T223 11T161 -11Q129 -11 102 10T74 74Q74 91 79 106T122 220Q160 321 166 341T173 380Q173 404 156 404H154Q124 404 99 371T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(755,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"weighting-factors-38172778\",\"curvature-basis-ea041445\",\"technical-relation-55ff5d57\"]'),
(2009519,'expansion-rate-cosmic','physical-cosmology','The Expansion Rate and Hubble Flow','<p>The <strong>Expansion Rate</strong> of the universe—quantified by the <strong>Hubble Parameter</strong> (\\( H(t) \\))—is the speed at which the 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong> stretches. In university-level foundations, this not the \"Motion\" of galaxies <em>through</em> space, but the 4D <strong>Space Expansion</strong> of space itself. This 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> creates the <strong>Hubble Flow</strong>, the global recessional motion of all distant objects.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Time-Dependent Hubble Parameter</h3>\n                  <p>The 4D <strong>Expansion Rate</strong> is defined as the normalized rate of change of the 4D <strong>Scale Factor</strong>: \\( H = \\dot{a}/a \\). While commonly called the \"Hubble Constant\" (\\( H_0 \\)), it is only constant in space, not in time. The 4D <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong> show that the rate has slowed down during the 4D <strong>Matter Era</strong> and is now speeding up due to 4D <strong>Dark Energy</strong>, anchoring 4D <strong>Physical Causality</strong> in the 4D <strong>Vacuum Pressure</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Recessional Velocity</h3>\n                  <p>According to <strong>Hubble\'s Law</strong> (\\( v = Hd \\)), the recessional velocity of a galaxy is proportional to its distance. For very distant objects, this velocity can exceed the speed of light. This does not violate 4D <strong>Einstein Locality</strong> because no signal is traveling <em>through</em> the  \\( \\gt c \\); rather, the 4D <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> is adding new space between points, a 4D <strong>Structural</strong> expansion of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The Cosmological Force</h3>\n                  <p>The expansion rate can be viewed as a 4D <strong>Cosmic Force</strong> driving the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the universe. It acts as a \"Tension\" that stretches the 4D <strong>Wavelength</strong> of light (4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a>) and opposes 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/jeans-instability\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Jeans Instability</strong></a>. The balance between this 4D <strong>Total Information</strong> expansion and 4D <strong>Local</strong> gravity determines whether 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ontological-emergence-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ontological Emergence</strong></a> (stars/galaxies) can occur.</p>','The Expansion Rate of the universe—quantified by the Hubble Parameter —is the speed at which the 4D Subatomic Vacuum stretches. In university-level foundations, this not the \"Motion\" of galaxies through space, but the 4D Space Expansion of space itself. This 4D Total Dynamics creates the Hubble Flow, the global recessional motion of all distant objects.','The Expansion Rate of the universe—quantified by the Hubble Parameter (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.586ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 2027 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43B\" d=\"M228 637Q194 637 192 641Q191 643 191 649Q191 673 202 682Q204 683 219 683Q260 681 355 681Q389 681 418 681T463 682T483 682Q499 682 499 672Q499 670 497 658Q492 641 487 638H485Q483 638 480 638T473 638T464 637T455 637Q416 636 405 634T387 623Q384 619 355 500Q348 474 340 442T328 395L324 380Q324 378 469 378H614L615 381Q615 384 646 504Q674 619 674 627T617 637Q594 637 587 639T580 648Q580 650 582 660Q586 677 588 679T604 682Q609 682 646 681T740 680Q802 680 835 681T871 682Q888 682 888 672Q888 645 876 638H874Q872