(2009540,'galactic-rotation','astrophysics','Galactic Dynamics and Rotation Curves','<p>Galactic rotation curves describe the orbital velocity of stars and gas in a galaxy as a function of their distance from the center. In university-level astrophysics, these curves are the primary tool for probing the distribution of mass within galaxies and provide the strongest evidence for the existence of dark matter halos.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Keplerian Decline vs. Observed Flatness</h3>\n                    <p>According to Newtonian mechanics, if the mass of a galaxy were concentrated where the light is visible, the orbital velocity should follow a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/keplerian-decline\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Keplerian Decline</strong></a> (\\( v(r) \\propto r^{-1/2} \\)) beyond the central bulge. However, observations of spiral galaxies consistently show \"flat\" rotation curves, where the velocity remains constant (\\( v \\approx \\text{const} \\)) far into the outskirts. This implies that the total enclosed mass \\( M(r) \\) must increase linearly with radius, well beyond the visible disk.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>-to-Light (\\( M/L \\)) Ratio</h3>\n                    <p>To quantify the discrepancy between visible and total mass, astrophysicists use the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass-to-light-ratio\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass-to-Light Ratio</strong></a>. In the solar neighborhood, \\( M/L \\) is near unity (by definition, in solar units). In the outer regions of galaxies, the ratio can exceed 10 or even 100, indicating that the vast majority of the galaxy\'s mass is \"dark\" and does not contribute to its luminosity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Dark Matter Halos and NFW Profiles</h3>\n                    <p>The flat curves are explained by the presence of a massive, roughly spherical <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-matter-halos\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dark Matter Halo</strong></a>. Numerical simulations of structure formation suggest that these halos follow specific density distributions, such as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/nfw-profile\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Navarro-Frenk-White (NFW) Profile</strong></a>. Understanding these profiles is essential for modeling how galaxies form, collide, and evolve over cosmic time.</p>','Galactic rotation curves describe the orbital velocity of stars and gas in a galaxy as a function of their distance from the center. In university-level astrophysics, these curves are the primary tool for probing the distribution of mass within galaxies and provide the strongest evidence for the existence of dark matter halos. According to Newtonian mechanics, if the mass of a galaxy were concentrated where the light is visible, the orbital velocity should follow a Keplerian Decline beyond the central bulge.','Galactic rotation curves describe the orbital velocity of stars and gas in a galaxy as a function of their distance from the center. In university-level astrophysics, these curves are the primary tool for probing the distribution of mass within galaxies and provide the strongest evidence for the existence of dark matter halos. According to Newtonian mechanics, if the mass of a galaxy were concentrated where the light is visible, the orbital velocity should follow a Keplerian Decline (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"11.747ex\" height=\"2.587ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -893.3 5192.3 1143.3\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\" d=\"M173 380Q173 405 154 405Q130 405 104 376T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 294 29 316T53 368T97 419T160 441Q202 441 225 417T249 361Q249 344 246 335Q246 329 231 291T200 202T182 113Q182 86 187 69Q200 26 250 26Q287 26 319 60T369 139T398 222T