(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(485,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(874,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1325,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1991.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"221D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-221D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(3047.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(484,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1778,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-rotation-velocity-class-subtopic-link-rotation-velocity-a-922af76f\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-nfw-profile-class-subtopic-link-nfw-profile-a-c4f8e329\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-mass-to-light-ratio-class-subtopic-link-mass-to-light-ratio-a-472b036d\"]'),
(2009541,'dark-matter','astrophysics','Dark Matter and Structure Formation','<p>Dark matter is a non-baryonic form of matter that interacts via gravity but is invisible to the electromagnetic spectrum. It constitutes approximately 27% of the total energy density of the universe and acts as the \"gravitational scaffolding\" for the formation of Large-Scale Structure. In university-level astrophysics, dark matter is required to explain the dynamics of galaxies, the stability of clusters, and the detailed fluctuations of the CMB.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Galactic Dynamics and the NFW Profile</h3>\n                    <p>Observations of spiral galaxies consistently show <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/galactic-rotation-curves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Flat Rotation Curves</strong></a> (\\( v(r) \\approx \\text{const} \\)), contradicting the Keplerian decline (\\( v \\propto r^{-1/2} \\)) expected from visible matter. This necessitates a massive \"dark halo\" with a density profile \\( \\rho(r) \\propto r^{-2} \\). N-body simulations suggest that Cold Dark Matter (CDM) follows a universal <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/nfw-profile\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Navarro-Frenk-White (NFW) Profile</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\rho(r) = \\frac{\\rho_s}{\\frac{r}{r_s}(1 + \\frac{r}{r_s})^2} \\]\n                    This profile features a central \"cusp\" (\\( r^{-1} \\)) and a steeper fall-off (\\( r^{-3} \\)) in the outskirts, characterizing the distribution of mass in halos across several orders of magnitude.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Direct Evidence: The Bullet Cluster</h3>\n                    <p>The strongest evidence for dark matter as a physical substance (rather than a modification of gravity) comes from the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bullet-cluster\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bullet Cluster</strong></a> (1E 0657-558). During the collision of two galaxy clusters, the baryonic gas interacted electromagnetically and slowed down, while the dark matter—detected via <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-lensing\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Weak Gravitational Lensing</strong></a>—passed through nearly unimpeded. The resulting spatial offset between the X-ray emitting gas and the gravitational center of mass proves that the majority of the matter is collisionless and non-baryonic.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Boltzmann Equation and WIMP Freeze-out</h3>\n                    <p>In the early universe, dark matter was in thermal equilibrium. Its current \"relic abundance\" was determined when the expansion rate (\\( H \\)) exceeded the interaction rate, a process known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/freeze-out\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Freeze-out</strong></a>. The evolution of the number density \\( n \\) is governed by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/boltzmann-equation-cosmology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Boltzmann Equation</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\frac{dn}{dt} + 3Hn = -\\langle \\sigma v \\rangle (n^2 - n_{eq}^2) \\]\n                    A theoretical particle with a mass of \\( \\sim 100 \\text{ GeV} \\) and weak-scale interactions naturally yields the observed dark matter density, a coincidence known as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wimp-miracle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>WIMP Miracle</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Cold vs. Hot Dark Matter (CDM vs. HDM)</h3>\n                    <p>The \"temperature\" of dark matter refers to its velocity at the time of decoupling.\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Cold Dark Matter (CDM):</strong> Moves at non-relativistic speeds, allowing for \"Bottom-Up\" structure formation where small halos merge into larger ones. This matches the observed hierarchical clustering of galaxies.</li>\n                            <li><strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hot-dark-matter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hot Dark Matter (HDM)</strong></a>:</strong> Relativistic particles (like light neutrinos) that \"free-stream\" out of small fluctuations, leading to \"Top-Down\" formation. HDM is largely ruled out as the primary component because it fails to reproduce the observed abundance of small-scale structures.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </p>','Dark matter is a non-baryonic form of matter that interacts via gravity but is invisible to the electromagnetic spectrum. It constitutes approximately 27% of the total energy density of the universe and acts as the \"gravitational scaffolding\" for the formation of Large-Scale Structure. In university-level astrophysics, dark matter is required to explain the dynamics of galaxies, the stability of clusters, and the detailed fluctuations of the CMB.','Dark matter is a non-baryonic form of matter that interacts via gravity but is invisible to the electromagnetic spectrum. It constitutes approximately 27% of the total energy density of the universe and acts as the \"gravitational scaffolding\" for the formation of Large-Scale Structure. In university-level astrophysics, dark matter is required to explain the dynamics of galaxies, the stability of clusters, and the detailed fluctuations of the CMB.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.661ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"19.368ex\" height=\"5.19ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1118 8560.7 2294\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T30