EX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(484,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"812.1\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1052.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1441.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2163.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(3163.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(366.6,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(484,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"812.1\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(4215.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(422,289) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g><rect width=\"5241.2\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-dark-matter-halos-class-subtopic-link-halo-structure-a-bbbca9d5\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-freeze-out-class-subtopic-link-freeze-out-dynamics-a-d3da21db\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-mass-discrepancy-class-subtopic-link-mass-discrepancy-a-7d3c2ea6\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-cosmological-density-class-subtopic-link-cosmic-abundance-a-63b6834b\"]'),
(2009542,'hubbles-law','cosmic-distance-ladder','Expansion and the Hubble Constant','<p>Hubble\'s Law, or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hubble-lemaitre-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>the Hubble-Lemaître Law</strong></a>, describes the observation that galaxies are receding from Earth at speeds proportional to their distance. It is the observational foundation of the expanding universe and the primary method for determining the age and size of the observable cosmos.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Redshift and Recessional Velocity</h3>\n                    <p>The \"velocity\" in Hubble\'s Law is actually a measurement of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a> (\\( z \\)). As space expands, the wavelength of light traveling through it is stretched. For nearby galaxies, this appears as a Doppler-like shift (\\( v = cz \\)), but for distant objects, it is a direct measure of the expansion of the spacetime metric between the time of emission and detection.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The H0 Tension: A Crisis in Cosmology</h3>\n                    <p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hubble-constant\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hubble Constant</strong></a> (\\( H_0 \\)) represents the current expansion rate. Paradoxically, modern measurements yield two conflicting values: one from the \"Late Universe\" (using Supernovae) and another from the \"Early Universe\" (using the CMB). This <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/h0-tension\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>H0 Tension</strong></a> is one of the most significant problems in astrophysics, potentially suggesting the need for \"New Physics\" beyond the standard \\(\\Lambda\\)CDM model.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Hubble Flow and Peculiar Velocities</h3>\n                    <p>On large scales, galaxies follow the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hubble-flow\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hubble Flow</strong></a>—the general expansion of the universe. However, on smaller scales, the gravitational pull of neighboring galaxies creates <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/peculiar-velocities\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Peculiar Velocities</strong></a>. To measure \\( H_0 \\) accurately, astronomers must look deep enough into space that the regular Hubble expansion dominates over these local gravitational motions.</p>','Hubble\'s Law, or the Hubble-Lemaître Law, describes the observation that galaxies are receding from Earth at speeds proportional to their distance. It is the observational foundation of the expanding universe and the primary method for determining the age and size of the observable cosmos. The \"velocity\" in Hubble\'s Law is actually a measurement of Cosmological Redshift.','Hubble\'s Law, or the Hubble-Lemaître Law, describes the observation that galaxies are receding from Earth at speeds proportional to their distance. It is the observational foundation of the expanding universe and the primary method for determining the age and size of the observable cosmos. The \"velocity\" in Hubble\'s Law is actually a measurement of Cosmological Redshift (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.052ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 465 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\" d=\"M347 338Q337 338 294 349T231 360Q211 360 197 356T174 346T162 335T155 324L153 320Q150 317 138 317Q117 317 117 325Q117 330 120 339Q133 378 163 406T229 440Q241 442 246 442Q271 442 291 425T329 392T367 375Q389 375 411 408T434 441Q435 442 449 442H462Q468 436 468 434Q468 430 463 420T449 399T432 377T418 358L411 349Q368 298 275 214T160 106L148 94L163 93Q185 93 227 82T290 71Q328 71 360 90T402 140Q406 149 409 151T424 153Q443 153 443 143Q443 138 442 134Q425 72 376 31T278 -11Q252 -11 232 6T193 40T155 57Q111 57 76 -3Q70 -11 59 -11H54H41Q35 -5 35 -2Q35 13 93 84Q132 129 225 214T340 322Q352 338 347 338Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D467\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\"></use></g></g></g></svg>).','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.052ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 465 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\" d=\"M347 338Q337 338 294 349T231 360Q211 360 197 356T174 346T162 335T155 324L153 320Q150 317 138 317Q117 317 117 325Q117 330 120 339Q133 378 163 406T229 440Q241 442 246 442Q271 442 291 425T329 392T367 375Q389 375 411 408T434 441Q435 442 449 442H462Q468 436 468 434Q468 430 463 420T449 399T432 377T418 358L411 349Q368 298 275 214T160 106L148 94L163 93Q185 93 227 82T290 71Q328 71 360 90T402 140Q406 149 409 151T424 153Q443 153 443 143Q443 138 442 134Q425 72 376 31T278 -11Q252 -11 232 6T193 40T155 57Q111 57 76 -3Q70 -11 59 -11H54H41Q35 -5 35 -2Q35 13 93 84Q132 129 225 214T340 322Q352 338 347 338Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D467\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-cosmic-expansion-class-subtopic-link-cosmic-expansion-a-02784e94\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-redshift-definition-class-subtopic-link-redshift-definition-a-f7ba049b\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-hubble-parameter-class-subtopic-link-hubble-parameter-a-d95ea8e5\"]'),
(2009543,'physical-cosmology','astrophysics','Physical Cosmology and the Global Universe','<p>Physical Cosmology is the study of the universe as a whole—its origin, evolution, and ultimate fate—using the rigorous tools of general relativity, thermodynamics, and particle physics. In university-level astrophysics, cosmology transitions from qualitative descriptions to a precise mathematical model where the entire universe is treated as a unified dynamical system, governed by the distribution of mass-energy and the geometry of spacetime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Cosmological Principle</h3>\n                    <p>The foundation of physical cosmology is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Principle</strong></a>, which asserts that on sufficiently large scales (\\( \\gtrsim 100 \\text{ Mpc} \\)), the universe is both <strong>Homogeneous</strong> (looks the same at every point) and <strong>Isotropic</strong> (looks the same in every direction). This powerful assumption allows cosmologists to describe the universe using a single <strong>Scale Factor</strong> (\\( a(t) \\)) and reduces the complex Einstein field equations to a simpler set of ordinary differential equations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Robertson-Walker Metric</h3>\n                    <p>The geometry of a homogeneous and isotropic universe is described by the <strong>Friedmann-Lemaître-Robertson-Walker (FLRW) Metric</strong>. This metric defines the spacetime interval as:\n                    \\[ ds^2 = c^2dt^2 - a(t)^2 \\left[ \\frac{dr^2}{1-kr^2} + r^2(d\\theta^2 + \\sin^2\\theta d\\phi^2) \\right] \\]\n                    Where \\( k \\) represents the <strong>Spatial Curvature</strong> (\\( k=+1, 0, -1 \\)). The metric reveals that the \"distance\" between objects is not fixed but changes over time as the scale factor \\( a(t) \\) evolves, providing the geometric basis for the expansion of space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Friedmann Equations: Cosmic Dynamics</h3>\n                    <p>The evolution of the scale factor is determined by the <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong>, derived by applying the FLRW metric to Einstein\'s field equations. The first Friedmann equation relates the expansion rate (the <strong>Hubble Parameter</strong>, \\( H = \\dot{a}/a \\)) to the total energy density (\\( \\rho \\)) and curvature:\n                    \\[ H^2 = \\frac{8\\pi G}{3}\\rho - \\frac{kc^2}{a^2} \\]\n                    This equation proves that the geometry of the universe is a direct consequence of its material content, defining the boundary between an eternally expanding \"Open\" universe and a \"Closed\" universe that might eventually re-collapse.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Matter, Radiation, and Vacuum Domination</h3>\n                    <p>The history of the universe is divided into epochs based on which component dominates the energy density. Because different forms of energy scale differently with the volume of space:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Radiation (\\( \\rho \\propto a^{-4} \\)):</strong> Dominated the very early universe, driving rapid expansion.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Matter (\\( \\rho \\propto a^{-3} \\)):</strong> Dominated for billions of years, allowing for the growth of galaxies and large-scale structure.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Dark Energy (\\( \\rho = \\text{const} \\)):</strong> Dominates the current epoch, causing the expansion rate to accelerate.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    The transition between these eras marks the major milestones in cosmic evolution, such as <strong>Recombination</strong> and <strong>Equality</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Cosmic Redshift and the Horizon Problem</h3>\n                    <p>As light travels through expanding space, its wavelength is stretched—a phenomenon known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a> (\\( z \\)). The relation \\( 1+z = 1/a_{emit} \\) links the observed spectrum of a galaxy directly to the size of the universe when the light was emitted. This expansion also creates <strong>Cosmological Horizons</strong>: limits on how far we can see due to the finite speed of light. The <strong>Horizon Problem</strong>—the fact that distant regions of the CMB are at the same temperature despite being causally disconnected—led to theory of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-inflation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Inflation</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Density Parameters (\\( \\Omega \\))</h3>\n                    <p>To compare different cosmological models, physicists use dimensionless <strong>Density Parameters</strong> (\\( \\Omega_i = \\rho_i / \\rho_c \\)), where \\( \\rho_c \\) is the <strong>Critical Density</strong> required for a flat universe. Current measurements from the <strong>Planck Mission</strong> show that our universe is spatially flat (\\( \\Omega_{total} \\approx 1 \\)), composed of approximately 5% baryonic matter, 27% dark matter, and 68% dark energy. This \"Lambda-CDM\" model is the standard paradigm of modern cosmology, providing a consistent fit for all major astronomical observations.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Physical Cosmology and the Global Universe is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/homogeneous-universe\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Homogeneous Universe</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/isotropic-universe\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Isotropic Universe</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spatial-curvature-cosmology\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Spatial Curvature and Global Geometry</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/radiation-domination\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Radiation-Dominated Era</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/matter-domination\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Matter-Dominated Era</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dark-energy-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Dark Energy and the Accelerated Expansion</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/recombination-cosmic\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Recombination and the Last Scattering</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/equality-epoch\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Radiation-Matter Equality Epoch</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-horizons\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Cosmological Horizons and Causal Limits</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/planck-mission\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Planck Mission and Precision Cosmology</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-parameters\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Density Parameters ()</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/expansion-rate-cosmic\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Expansion Rate and Hubble Flow</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-force-acceleration\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Cosmic Force of Acceleration</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/flatness-threshold-critical\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Flatness Threshold and Critical Density</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/space-expansion-logic\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Logic of Space Expansion</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','Physical Cosmology is the study of the universe as a whole—its origin, evolution, and ultimate fate—using the rigorous tools of general relativity, thermodynamics, and particle physics. In university-level astrophysics, cosmology transitions from qualitative descriptions to a precise mathematical model where the entire universe is treated as a unified dynamical system, governed by the distribution of mass-energy and the geometry of spacetime. The foundation of physical cosmology is the Cosmological Principle, which asserts that on sufficiently large scales, the universe is both Homogeneous (looks the same at every point) and Isotropic (looks the same in every direction).','Physical Cosmology is the study of the universe as a whole—its origin, evolution, and ultimate fate—using the rigorous tools of general relativity, thermodynamics, and particle physics. In university-level astrophysics, cosmology transitions from qualitative descriptions to a precise mathematical model where the entire universe is treated as a unified dynamical system, governed by the distribution of mass-energy and the geometry of spacetime. The foundation of physical cosmology is the Cosmological Principle, which asserts that on sufficiently large scales (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.516ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.685ex\" height=\"2.172ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -732 4722.8 960\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2273\" d=\"M90 697Q83 704 83 712T88 726T99 732Q107 732 253 664T543 527T687 457Q694 452 694 442T687 427Q685 425 395 288L107 152H101Q83 154 83 172Q83 173 83 175Q82 182 98 191Q117 201 248 263Q326 300 378 324L626 442L360 568Q91 696 90 697ZM56 -194Q56 -107 106 -51T222 6Q260 6 296 -12T362 -56T420 -108T483 -153T554 -171Q616 -171 654 -128T694 -29Q696 6 708 6Q722 6 722 -26Q722 -102 676 -164T557 -227Q518 -227 481 -209T415 -165T358 -113T294 -69T223 -51Q163 -51 125 -93T83 -196Q81 -228 69 -228Q56 -228 56 -202V-194Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96