link:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(911,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(2427.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(507,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(620.5,-710)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2211\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-SO-2211\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(1222.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(911,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g><rect width=\"3428.6\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"standard-view-6eb5f687\",\"geometric-balance-a861efdc\",\"energy-split-98e2aa6f\",\"scattering-base-b98087c3\"]'),
(2009593,'reduced-mass','conservation-of-linear-momentum','Reduced Mass (\\(\\mu\\))','<p>The <strong>Reduced Mass</strong> (\\( \\mu \\)) is an effective mass used to simplify the <strong>Two-Body Problem</strong> into an equivalent one-body problem. In university-level mechanics, when two particles (such as a star and its planet) interact through a central force, their internal motion is mathematically identical to a single particle of mass \\( \\mu \\) moving around a fixed center. This concept is essential for accurately calculating the orbits of <strong>Binary Star Systems</strong> and the energy levels of the <strong>Hydrogen Atom</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Mathematical Definition</h3>\n                    <p>For two particles with masses \\( m_1 \\) and \\( m_2 \\), the reduced mass is defined as:\n                    \\[ \\mu = \\frac{m_1 m_2}{m_1 + m_2} = \\left( \\frac{1}{m_1} + \\frac{1}{m_2} \\right)^{-1} \\]\n                    This harmonic mean ensures that \\( \\mu \\) is always smaller than the smallest individual mass. As one mass becomes much larger than the other (\\( m_1 \\gg m_2 \\)), the reduced mass approaches the smaller mass (\\( \\mu \\to m_2 \\)), justifying the common approximation of a \"fixed\" central body.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Transformation to the CM Frame</h3>\n                    <p>The reduced mass arises naturally when expressing the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/kinetic-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Kinetic Energy</strong></a> of a system in the <strong>Center of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Frame</strong>. The internal kinetic energy is \\( K = \\frac{1}{2} \\mu \\dot{\\mathbf{r}}^2 \\), where \\( \\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}_1 - \\mathbf{r}_2 \\) is the relative separation vector. This allows the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> of the complex system to be written in terms of a single \"relative\" particle, separating the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservation-of-linear-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Linear Momentum</strong></a> of the CM from the internal orbital dynamics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Kepler Problem and Binary Stars</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Astrophysical Dynamics</strong>, the reduced mass is the key to <strong>Kepler\'s Third Law</strong>. For a binary star system, the orbital period \\( P \\) depends on the sum of the masses, but the individual trajectories are scaled by \\( \\mu \\). Without using reduced mass, astronomers would fail to account for the \"wobble\" of the primary star, an effect used today to detect <strong>Exoplanets</strong> through high-precision <strong>Radial Velocity</strong> measurements.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Reduced <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> in Quantum Mechanics</h3>\n                    <p>Reduced mass is critical for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a> of the hydrogen atom. Because the nucleus is not infinitely heavy, the electron and proton both orbit their common center of mass. Replacing the electron mass \\( m_e \\) with the reduced mass \\( \\mu \\) leads to a small but measurable shift in the <strong>Rydberg Constant</strong>. Measuring this shift is what allowed for the discovery of <strong>Deuterium</strong>, providing the first experimental proof isotopic mass effects.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Barycentric Wobble</h3>\n                    <p>The reduced mass captures the physical reality that every interaction is mutual. In a system like the Earth-Moon, the \"reduced\" perspective reveals that the Earth also travels in a small circle around the <strong>Barycenter</strong>. This <strong>Center of Mass</strong> motion is the basis for calculating tides and the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the Earth\'s rotation, linking the subatomic concept of reduced mass to the scale of planetary systems.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Implications for Scattering</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Particle Physics</strong>, the reduced mass determines the available energy in the \"Center of Momentum\" frame for collisions. It sets the scale for the <strong>Cross-Section</strong> of interactions, as the collision is only \"aware\" of the relative velocity scaled by \\( \\mu \\). This principle is used in the <strong>LHC</strong> to calculate the threshold for producing new heavy particles like the <strong>Higgs Boson</strong> or <strong>Quarks</strong>.</p>','The Reduced Mass is an effective mass used to simplify the Two-Body Problem into an equivalent one-body problem. In university-level mechanics, when two particles (such as a star and its planet) interact through a central force, their internal motion is mathematically identical to a single particle of mass moving around a fixed center. This concept is essential for accurately calculating the orbits of Binary Star Systems and the energy levels of the Hydrogen Atom.','The Reduced Mass (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.364ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 603 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T