391T234 425T327 443Q328 443 348 443T383 444Q434 444 442 438Q450 430 450 416Q446 392 424 383L376 382Q306 381 278 369Q230 349 208 294Q199 274 199 268Q199 267 291 267Q305 267 325 267T353 268Q383 268 394 263T406 241Q406 214 380 206Q375 205 279 205T183 203Q174 176 174 140Q174 87 208 65T292 43Q295 43 300 43T307 44Q337 49 372 69T415 89Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FF\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FF\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(444,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1271.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2327.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"7B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2827.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3377.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3822.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4608.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"7D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5330.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5830.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D750\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-BI-1D750\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"geometric-push-dbc2dde9\",\"classical-rule-52ece00c\",\"homogeneity-0c631165\",\"heisenberg-origin-7de13bcc\"]'),
(2009596,'symmetry-relation','conservation-of-linear-momentum','The Symmetry-Conservation Relation','<p>The <strong>Symmetry-Conservation Relation</strong> is the profound principle stating that for every continuous symmetry of a physical system, there exists a corresponding conserved quantity. In university-level physics, this formalized by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/noethers-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Noether\'s Theorem</strong></a>. For the case of linear momentum, the symmetry in question is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spatial-translation-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spatial Translation Symmetry</strong></a>, asserting that the fundamental laws of motion are invariant under a global shift in position.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Symmetry of the Action</h3>\n                    <p>A symmetry exists if a transformation (like shifting the whole universe by 1 meter) leaves the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/action-integral\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Action Integral</strong></a> unchanged (\\( \\delta S = 0 \\)). Mathematically, this implies that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> is independent of the absolute coordinates of the particles. This \"Indifference\" of nature to absolute location is the <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservation-of-linear-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Linear Momentum</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Momentum as the \"Noether Charge\"</h3>\n                    <p>In the language of field theory, linear momentum is the \"charge\" associated with the translation current. If the  is homogeneous, the total \"flow\" of momentum across any boundary must be zero unless an external force is applied. This elevates momentum from a simple mass-velocity product to a deep structural feature of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-interval\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spacetime Interval</strong></a> symmetry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Breaking the Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>Symmetry—and thus conservation—can be broken. If a system is placed in a potential \\( V(\\mathbf{x}) \\) that depends on position (like a gravity well), <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spatial-translation-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spatial Translation Symmetry</strong></a> is lost. The Lagrangian is no longer invariant under shifts. The resulting change in momentum over time (\\( \\dot{\\mathbf{p}} = -\\nabla V \\)) is what we physically perceive as a \"Force,\" proving that force is just the macroscopic signature of broken spatial symmetry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Global vs. Local Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>Baryogenesis research distinguishes between <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/global-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Global Symmetry</strong></a> (where the whole system is shifted) and <strong>Local Symmetry</strong> (where different points are shifted differently). The conservation of momentum arises from global translation symmetry. Local symmetries, by contrast, lead to the existence of <strong>Gauge Bosons</strong> (like the photon or gluon), showing that all the forces of the <strong>Standard Model</strong> are results of specific symmetry relations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Symmetry in Discrete Systems</h3>\n                    <p>In crystals or discrete lattices, full spatial translation symmetry is broken and replaced by <strong>Discrete Translation Symmetry</strong>. In these systems, momentum is only conserved \"modulo\" a reciprocal lattice vector. This leads to the concept of <strong>Crystal Momentum</strong> in solid-state physics, demonstrating that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservation-of-linear-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Linear Momentum</strong></a> is sensitive to the <strong>Topology</strong> of the space in which the particles move.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Cosmological Symmetry Breaking</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Physical Cosmology</strong>, the expansion of the universe (the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/flrw-metric\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>FLRW Metric</strong></a>) breaks the symmetry of time-translation but can preserve spatial homogeneity. This ensures that while <strong>Energy</strong> may not be conserved on cosmic scales, linear momentum remains a valid conserved quantity within comoving volumes, dictating the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of galaxy formation and large-scale structure evolution.</p>','The Symmetry-Conservation Relation is the profound principle stating that for every continuous symmetry of a physical system, there exists a corresponding conserved quantity. In university-level physics, this formalized by Noether\'s Theorem. For the case of linear momentum, the symmetry in question is Spatial Translation Symmetry, asserting that the fundamental laws of motion are invariant under a global shift in position.','The Symmetry-Conservation Relation is the profound principle stating that for every continuous symmetry of a physical system, there exists a corresponding conserved quantity. In university-level physics, this formalized by Noether\'s Theorem. For the case of linear momentum, the symmetry in question is Spatial Translation Symmetry, asserting that the fundamental laws of motion are invariant under a global shift in position.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.612ex\" height=\"1.808ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -717 2922.6 799\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FF\" d=\"M195 609Q195 656 227 686T302 717Q319 716 351 709T407 697T433 690Q451 682 451 662Q451 644 438 628T403 612Q382 612 348 641T288 671T249 657T235 628Q235 584 334 463Q401 379 401 292Q401 169 340 80T205 -10H198Q127 -10 83 36T36 153Q36 286 151 382Q191 413 252 434Q252 435 245 449T230 481T214 521T201 566T195 609ZM112 130Q112 83 136 55T204 27Q233 27 256 51T291 111T309 178T316 232Q316 267 309 298T295 344T269 400L259 396Q215 381 183 342T137 256T118 179T112 130Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554 544 579T526 616T501 641Q465 662 419 662Q362 662 313 616T263 510Q263 480 278 458T319 427Q323 425 389 408T456 390Q490 379 522 342T554 242Q554 216 546 186Q541 164 528 137T492 78T426 18T332 -20Q320 -22 298 -22Q199 -22 144 33L134 44L106 13Q83 -14 78 -18T65 -22Q52 -22 52 -14Q52 -11 110 221Q112 227 130 227H143Q149 221 149 216Q149 214 148 207T144 186T142 153Q144 114 160 87T203 47T255 29T308 24Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FF\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FF\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(444,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1366.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2422.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"symmetry-value-1d60b0b0\",\"rotational-symmetry-71c1696f\",\"broken-rule-06906acd\",\"energy-rule-fce84993\"]'),
(2009597,'variable-mass','conservation-of-linear-momentum','Systems with Variable Mass','<p>In university-level mechanics, a <strong>Variable <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> System</strong> is a collection of matter whose total mass (\\( M \\)) changes over time due to the ejection or accretion of material. Unlike standard Newtonian systems where \\( \\mathbf{F} = m\\mathbf{a} \\), variable mass systems must be analyzed using the more fundamental form of <strong>Newton\'s Second Law</strong>: \\( \\mathbf{F}_{ext} = d\\mathbf{p}/dt \\). This ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservation-of-linear-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Linear Momentum</strong></a> accounts for the momentum carried by the entering or leaving mass flux.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Generalized Equation of Motion</h3>\n                    <p>The motion of a body with changing mass is described by the equation:\n                    \\[ M \\frac{d\\mathbf{v}}{dt} = \\mathbf{F}_{ext} + \\mathbf{v}_{rel} \\frac{dM}{dt} \\]\n                    Here, \\( \\mathbf{v}_{rel} \\) is the velocity of the changing mass relative to the main body. The term \\( \\mathbf{v}_{rel} (dM/dt) \\) is known as the <strong>Thrust</strong> or reactive force. This equation reveals that acceleration can be achieved even in the absence of external forces (\\( F_{ext}=0 \\)) by simply \"throwing\" mass away at high speeds.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Momentum Flux and Accretion</h3>\n                    <p>For an accreting body (like a raindrop falling through mist), the mass increases (\\( dM/dt \\gt 0 \\)). The incoming mass carries its own momentum, which must be absorbed by the main body. This results in a \"drag\" force that opposes the motion. Analyzing this <strong>Momentum Flux</strong> is essential for modeling the growth of <strong>Planetary Systems</strong> and the accumulation of gas onto <strong>Black Hole</strong> accretion disks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Tsiolkovsky Limit</h3>\n                    <p>The most famous application of variable mass dynamics is the <strong>Tsiolkovsky Rocket Equation</strong>. It describes the \"impulse\" provided by the continuous ejection of propellant. Because the mass of the rocket decreases as it burns fuel, the same amount of thrust produces an ever-increasing acceleration. This non-linear feedback is the primary challenge of <strong>Space Dynamics</strong>, requiring multi-stage designs to achieve orbit.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Variable <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> in Fluid Dynamics</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Astrophysical Fluid Dynamics</strong>, variable mass is modeled using the <strong>Continuity Equation</strong> and the Navier-Stokes equations with source terms. A star losing mass through <strong>Stellar Winds</strong> is a variable mass system where the \"Thrust\" is distributed throughout the stellar envelope. The balance between the outward momentum of the wind and the inward pull of gravity dictates the star\'s final end-state as a <strong>Compact Object</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Relativistic Variable Mass</h3>\n                    <p>When mass is ejected at speeds near \\( c \\), the Newtonian momentum balance is replaced by the conservation of <strong>Four-Momentum</strong>. In <strong>Special Relativity</strong>, \"Mass\" is not a conserved scalar but part of the energy-momentum flow. The <strong>Relativistic Rocket Equation</strong> reveals that energy must be spent to accelerate not just the particles, but also the \"kinetic mass\" they possess, posing a fundamental <strong>Epistemic Limit</strong> on ultra-high-speed travel.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Conservation of Momentum in the System</h3>\n                    <p>The key to solving any variable mass problem is to define the \"System\" such that its total mass is constant. By including both the main body and the ejected/accreted particles in a single <strong>Center of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Frame</strong>, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservation-of-linear-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Linear Momentum</strong></a> remains absolute. This allows physicists to verify the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of complex interactions, from the microscopic scattering of ions to the macroscopic recoil of <strong>Relativistic Jets</strong>.</p>','In university-level mechanics, a Variable Mass System is a collection of matter whose total mass changes over time due to the ejection or accretion of material. Unlike standard Newtonian systems where, variable mass systems must be analyzed using the more fundamental form of Newton\'s Second Law:. This ensures that the Conservation of Linear Momentum accounts for the momentum carried by the entering or leaving mass flux.','In university-level mechanics, a Variable Mass System is a collection of matter whose total mass (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.378ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1051 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D440\" d=\"M289 629Q289 635 232 637Q208 637 201 638T194 648Q194 649 196 659Q197 662 198 666T199 671T201 676T203 679T207 681T212 683T220 683T232 684Q238 684 262 684T307 683Q386 683 398 683T414 678Q415 674 451 396L487 117L510 154Q534 190 574 254T662 394Q837 673 839 675Q840 676 842 678T846 681L852 683H948Q965 683 988 683T1017 684Q1051 684 1051 673Q1051 668 1048 656T1045 643Q1041 637 1008 637Q968 636 957 634T939 623Q936 618 867 340T797 59Q797 55 798 54T805 50T822 48T855 46H886Q892 37 892 35Q892 19 885 5Q880 0 869 0Q864 0 828 1T736 2Q675 2 644 2T