 data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(8188.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(650.8,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(9315.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(684,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(10402.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"phase-rotation-4e56d359\",\"noether-result-3281f99e\"]'),
(2009620,'differential-identities','noethers-theorem','Noether\'s Second Theorem: Differential Identities','<p><strong>Noether\'s Second Theorem</strong> deals with symmetries that are <strong>Local</strong> or <strong>Gauge Symmetries</strong>, where the transformation parameter depends on spacetime (\\( \\epsilon(x) \\)). Unlike the first theorem, which leads to conservation laws, the second theorem leads to <strong>Differential Identities</strong> (or off-shell identities)—mathematical redundancies that constrain the allowed form of the field equations.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Off-Shell vs. On-Shell</h3>\n                  <p>In university-level theoretical physics, a \"Conservation Law\" only holds for paths that satisfy the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/euler-lagrange-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Euler-Lagrange Equations</strong></a> (on-shell). A \"Differential Identity,\" however, holds for <em>any</em> configuration of the fields (off-shell). This redundancy is the hallmark of <strong>Gauge Symmetry</strong>, proving that some components of the  tensor are mathematical artifacts rather than physical degrees of freedom.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Bianchi Identities and General Relativity</h3>\n                  <p>A prime example of Noether\'s second theorem is found in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>. The invariance of the action under coordinate changes (<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/diffeomorphism-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Diffeomorphism Invariance</strong></a>) leads to the <strong>Contracted Bianchi Identity</strong>: \\( \\nabla_\\mu G^{\\mu\\nu} \\equiv 0 \\). This identity ensures that the <strong>Einstein Tensor</strong> is automatically conserved, providing the geometric consistency needed to match the conserved <strong>Stress-Energy Tensor</strong> of matter.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Gauss\'s Law as a Constraint</h3>\n                  <p>In electromagnetism, local gauge symmetry leads to the identity \\( \\partial_\\nu (\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}) \\equiv 0 \\). This implies that <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a> is not an independent dynamical equation but a constraint on the <strong>Charge-Current 4-Vector</strong>. It ensures that the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the field is rigid, preventing the existence of \"un-sourced\" longitudinal waves in the vacuum.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Reducing Degrees of Freedom</h3>\n                  <p>The existence of these identities is what allow physicists to \"Fix the Gauge.\" By using the mathematical freedom granted by the identities, we can simplify the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> (e.g., using the <strong>Lorenz Gauge</strong>). This process reveals that the fundamental forces are dictated by the <strong>Symmetry Group</strong> of the universe, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the formal constraints of the manifold.</p>','Noether\'s Second Theorem deals with symmetries that are Local or Gauge Symmetries, where the transformation parameter depends on spacetime. Unlike the first theorem, which leads to conservation laws, the second theorem leads to Differential Identities (or off-shell identities)—mathematical redundancies that constrain the allowed form of the field equations. In university-level theoretical physics, a \"Conservation Law\" only holds for paths that satisfy the Euler-Lagrange Equations (on-shell).','Noether\'s Second Theorem deals with symmetries that are Local or Gauge Symmetries, where the transformation parameter depends on spacetime (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.973ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1756 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D716\" d=\"M227 -11Q149 -11 95 41T40 174Q40 262 87 322Q121 367 173 396T287 430Q289 431 329 431H367Q382 426 382 411Q382 385 341 385H325H312Q191 385 154 277L150 265H327Q340 256 340 246Q340 228 320 219H138V217Q128 187 128 143Q128 77 160 52T231 26Q258 26 284 36T326 57T343 68Q350 68 354 58T358 39Q358 36 357 35Q354 31 337 21T289 0T227 -11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\" d=\"M52 289Q59 331 106 386T222 442Q257 442 286 424T329 379Q371 442 430 442Q467 442 494 420T522 361Q522 332 508 314T481 292T458 288Q439 288 427 299T415 328Q415 374 465 391Q454 404 425 404Q412 404 406 402Q368 386 350 336Q290 115 290 78Q290 50 306 38T341 26Q378 26 414 59T463 140Q466 150 469 151T485 153H489Q504 153 504 145Q504 144 502 134Q486 77 440 33T333 -11Q263 -11 227 52Q186 -10 133 -10H127Q78 -10 57 16T35 71Q35 103 54 123T99 143Q142 143 142 101Q142 81 130 66T107 46T94 41L91 40Q91 39 97 36T113 29T132 26Q168 26 194 71Q203 87 217 139T245 247T261 313Q266 340 266 352Q266 380 251 392T217 404Q177 404 142 372T93 290Q91 281 88 280T72 278H58Q52 284 52 289Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D716\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D716\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(406,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(795,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1367,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>). Unlike the first theorem, which leads to conservation laws, the second theorem leads to Differential Identities (or off-shell identities)—mathematical redundancies that constrain the allowed form of the field equations. In university-level theoretical physics, a \"Conservation Law\" only holds for paths that satisfy the Euler-Lagrange Equations (on-shell).','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.973ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1756 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D716\" d=\"M227 -11Q149 -11 95 41T40 174Q40 262 87 322Q121 367 173 396T287 430Q289 431 329 431H367Q382 426 382 411Q382 385 341 385H325H312Q191 385 154 277L150 265H327Q340 256 340 246Q340 228 320 219H138V217Q128 187 128 143Q128 77 160 52T231 26Q258 26 284 36T326 57T343 68Q350 68 354 58T358 39Q358 36 357 35Q354 31 337 21T289 0T227 -11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\" d=\"M52 289Q59 331 106 386T222 442Q257 442 286 424T329 379Q371 442 430 442Q467 442 494 420T522 361Q522 332 508 314T481 292T458 288Q439 288 427 299T415 328Q415 374 465 391Q454 404 425 404Q412 404 406 402Q368 386 350 336Q290 115 290 78Q290 50 306 38T341 26Q378 26 414 59T463 140Q466 150 469 151T485 153H489Q504 153 504 145Q504 144 502 134Q486 77 440 33T333 -11Q263 -11 227 52Q186 -10 133 -10H127Q78 -10 57 16T35 71Q35 103 54 123T99 143Q142 143 142 101Q142 81 130 66T107 46T94 41L91 40Q91 39 97 36T113 29T132 26Q168 26 194 71Q203 87 217 139T245 247T261 313Q266 340 266 352Q266 380 251 392T217 404Q177 404 142 372T93 290Q91 281 88 280T72 278H58Q52 284 52 289Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D716\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D716\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(406,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(795,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1367,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"geometric-necessity-310cc504\",\"gauge-freedom-bfc345aa\"]'),
(2009621,'cosmological-principle','spatial-translation-symmetry','Cosmological Principle','<p>The Cosmological Principle is a fundamental postulate in modern <strong>Physical Cosmology</strong> stating that, on sufficiently large scales, the universe is both <strong>Homogeneous</strong> and <strong>Isotropic</strong>. In university-level astrophysics, this principle implies that our location in the cosmos is in no way special; the large-scale structure and evolution of the universe appear identical to all observers, regardless of their position or the direction in which they look. This assumption allows for the derivation of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/flrw-metric\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>FLRW Metric</strong></a>, providing the mathematical foundation for the Big Bang model.</p><h3>1. Homogeneity vs. Isotropy</h3><p>The principle is composed of two distinct geometric requirements. <strong>Homogeneity</strong> asserts that the universe looks the same from every <em>location</em>, meaning there is no \"center\" or \"edge.\" <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/isotropy-of-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Isotropy</strong></a> asserts that the universe looks the same in every <em>direction</em>. While a space can be homogeneous but not isotropic (like a universe with a uniform magnetic field), or isotropic about only one point (like a centered explosion), the combination of both ensures a perfectly uniform manifold on scales exceeding 100 Megaparsecs.</p><h3>2. The Copernican Extension</h3><p>The Cosmological Principle is the ultimate extension of the Copernican idea that the Earth is not the center of the solar system. In its modern form, it suggests that the entire 3D spatial slice of the universe is a \"Transitive\" space where every point is geometrically equivalent. This allows physicists to define a global <strong>Cosmic Time</strong> and a single <strong>Scale Factor</strong> (\\( a(t) \\)) that describes the expansion of the entire cosmos, simplifying the complex <strong>Einstein Field Equations</strong> into the manageable <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong>.</p><h3>3. Scale-Dependent Validity</h3><p>A critical university-level insight is that the Cosmological Principle is only a <strong>Statistical Symmetry</strong>. On local scales—such as galaxies or clusters—the universe is highly non-homogeneous and anisotropic due to gravitational clumping. However, as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/measurement-focus\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Measurement Focus</strong></a> increases to encompass larger volumes of the manifold, the variations in density (the \"clumpiness\") average out to less than 1 part in 100,000, a fact verified by the extreme uniformity of the <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background</strong>.</p><h3>4. Observational Support and the Hubble Flow</h3><p>The primary evidence for the Cosmological Principle is the <strong>Hubble Flow</strong>: the observation that galaxies recede from us at speeds proportional to their distance. If the principle holds, every observer in every galaxy sees the same expansion pattern, proving that galaxies are not \"flying away\" through space, but that the <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> of the manifold itself is stretching uniformly. This anchors <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the belief that we are observing a universal process rather than a local accident.</p>','The Cosmological Principle is a fundamental postulate in modern Physical Cosmology stating that, on sufficiently large scales, the universe is both Homogeneous and Isotropic. In university-level astrophysics, this principle implies that our location in the cosmos is in no way special; the large-scale structure and evolution of the universe appear identical to all observers, regardless of their position or the direction in which they look. This assumption allows for the derivation of the FLRW Metric, providing the mathematical foundation for the Big Bang model.','The Cosmological Principle is a fundamental postulate in modern Physical Cosmology stating that, on sufficiently large scales, the universe is both Homogeneous and Isotropic. In university-level astrophysics, this principle implies that our location in the cosmos is in no way special; the large-scale structure and evolution of the universe appear identical to all observers, regardless of their position or the direction in which they look. This assumption allows for the derivation of the FLRW Metric, providing the mathematical foundation for the Big Bang model.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.774ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1668 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q331 441 370 392Q386 422 416 422Q429 422 439 414T449 394Q449 381 412 234T374 68Q374 43 381 35T402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487Q506 153 506 144Q506 138 501 117T481 63T449 13Q436 0 417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM351 328Q351 334 346 350T323 385T277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q217 26 254 59T298 110Q300 114 325 217T351 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522 166 540T173 566T179 586T187 603T197 615T211 624T229 626Q247 625 254 615T261 596Q261 589 252 549T232 470L222 433Q222 431 272 431H323Q330 424 330 420Q330 398 317 385H210L174 240Q135 80 135 68Q135 26 162 26Q197 26 230 60T283 144Q285 150 288 151T303 153H307Q322 153 322 145Q322 142 319 133Q314 117 301 95T267 48T216 6T155 -11Q125 -11 98 4T59 56Q57 64 57 83V101L92 241Q127 382 128 383Q128 385 77 385H26Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(529,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(918,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1279,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-flrw-metric-ea65da15\",\"homogeneity-constraint-6c9e76be\"]'),
(2009622,'isotropy-of-space','rotational-symmetry','Isotropy of Space','<p>Isotropy of Space is the fundamental geometric property stating that the universe has no \"preferred direction.\" In university-level physics, this homogeneity of the 4D <strong>Minkowski Vacuum</strong> under rotations implies that an experiment conducted in one orientation will yield the exact same results as the same experiment rotated in any direction.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Geometric Uniformity and SO(3)</h3>\n                    <p>Isotropy is mathematically expressed through the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/special-orthogonal-group-so3\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Special Orthogonal Group SO(3)</strong></a>. For a space to be isotropic, its <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> (\\( g_{\\mu\\nu} \\)) must be invariant under the application of rotation matrices. This uniformity ensures that the fundamental laws of motion do not depend on the absolute orientation of the observer, a requirement for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in a non-Euclidean manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Scalar Nature of the Lagrangian</h3>\n                    <p>In analytical mechanics, isotropy requires that the system\'s <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> (\\( \\mathcal{L} \\)) be a <strong>Lorentz Scalar</strong> under rotations. It can only depend on quantities that are invariant under \\( SO(3) \\), such as the magnitudes of position and velocity vectors (\\( r, v \\)) and their internal dot products (\\( \\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\)). If \\( \\mathcal{L} \\) were to depend on the absolute direction \\( \\theta \\), the universe would possess a <strong>Vector Anisotropy</strong>, leading to a violation of angular momentum conservation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Isotropy vs. Homogeneity</h3>\n                    <p>While often linked, <strong>Isotropy</strong> (direction invariance) and <strong>Homogeneity</strong> (position invariance) are distinct. A space can be isotropic at a point but not homogeneous (like a central force field) or homogeneous but not isotropic (like a uniform magnetic field). The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Principle</strong></a> asserts that on the largest scales, the universe is both, leading to the FLRW metric that governs the expansion of space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Testing the Vacuum</h3>\n                    <p>Isotropy has been verified to extreme precision. The <strong>Michelson-Morley Experiment</strong> and its modern laser-interferometer equivalents prove that the speed of light is identical in all directions. Any detected anisotropy would indicate a <strong>Coupling Constant</strong> to an external field or a breakdown of the standard 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> model, potentially revealing new physics at the Planck scale.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Quantum Isotropy and Selection Rules</h3>\n                    <p>In quantum mechanics, the isotropy of space is reflected in the <strong>Degeneracy</strong> of energy levels. For a perfectly isotropic atom, states with different magnetic quantum numbers (\\( m_l \\)) have the same energy. Breaking thisotropy with an external field (Zeeman Effect) splits these levels, proving that the underlying <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a> dynamics are strictly coupled to the rotational symmetry of the vacuum.</p>','Isotropy of Space is the fundamental geometric property stating that the universe has no \"preferred direction.\" In university-level physics, this homogeneity of the 4D Minkowski Vacuum under rotations implies that an experiment conducted in one orientation will yield the exact same results as the same experiment rotated in any direction. Isotropy is mathematically expressed through the Special Orthogonal Group SO(3). For a space to be isotropic, its Metric Tensor must be invariant under the application of rotation matrices.','Isotropy of Space is the fundamental geometric property stating that the universe has no \"preferred direction.\" In university-level physics, this homogeneity of the 4D Minkowski Vacuum under rotations implies that an experiment conducted in one orientation will yield the exact same results as the same experiment rotated in any direction. Isotropy is mathematically expressed through the Special Orthogonal Group SO(3). For a space to be isotropic, its Metric Tensor (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.08ex\" height=\"1.685ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1361.2 744.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D454\" d=\"M311 43Q296 30 267 15T206 0Q143 0 105 45T66 160Q66 265 143 353T314 442Q361 442 401 394L404 398Q406 401 409 404T418 412T431 419T447 422Q461 422 470 413T480 394Q480 379 423 152T363 -80Q345 -134 286 -169T151 -205Q10 -205 10 -137Q10 -111 28 -91T74 -71Q89 -71 102 -80T116 -111Q116 -121 114 -130T107 -144T99 -154T92 -162L90 -164H91Q101 -167 151 -167Q189 -167 211 -155Q234 -144 254 -122T282 -75Q288 -56 298 -13Q311 35 311 43ZM384 328L380 339Q377 350 375 354T369 368T359 382T346 393T328 402T306 405Q262 405 221 352Q191 313 171 233T151 117Q151 38 213 38Q269 38 323 108L331 118L384 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575