</use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(439,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(345,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(757,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(7148.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(588,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"continuous-symmetry-8031d56a\",\"algebraic-form-1b0d6d4c\",\"spinor-link-7e14db9d\"]'),
(2009625,'central-potential','rotational-symmetry','Central Potentials and Angular Independence','<p>A <strong>Central Potential</strong> (\\( V = V(r) \\)) is a scalar field whose value depends only on the distance from a fixed origin, not on the angular coordinates. In university-level mechanics, these potentials are the primary vehicles for studying systems with perfect <strong>Rotational Symmetry</strong>, such as planetary orbits and hydrogenic atoms.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Symmetry and Dimensional Reduction</h3>\n                    <p>The existence of a central potential implies that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/torque\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Torque</strong></a> on a particle is identically zero (\\( \\boldsymbol{\\tau} = \\mathbf{r} \\times -\\nabla V(r) = 0 \\)). According to the <strong>Conservation Law</strong>, the total angular momentum \\( \\mathbf{L} \\) is constant. This allows us to reduce a complex 3D problem to a 1D problem in terms of the radial coordinate \\( r \\), as the motion is strictly confined to a fixed <strong>Orbital Plane</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Effective Potential</h3>\n                    <p>In the radial equation of motion, the effect of rotational motion is captured by the <strong>Centrifugal Barrier</strong>. When combined with the actual potential \\( V(r) \\), we define the <strong>Effective Potential</strong>:\n                    \\[ V_{eff}(r) = V(r) + \\frac{L^2}{2mr^2} \\]\n                    This term represents the energy required to \"fight\" the angular momentum to move closer to the center, creating stable circular and elliptical orbits that prevent particles from falling into the origin.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Separation of Variables</h3>\n                    <p>In quantum mechanics, the rotational symmetry of a central potential allows the <strong>Wave Function</strong> to be separated into radial and angular parts: \\( \\psi(r, \\theta, \\phi) = R(r) Y_{lm}(\\theta, \\phi) \\). The angular parts are the <strong>Spherical Harmonics</strong>, which are the universal solutions for any isotropic system, while the radial part \\( R(r) \\) contains the specific physics of the interaction (e.g., gravity or Coulomb force).</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Degeneracy and Hidden Symmetries</h3>\n                    <p>Central potentials often exhibit high levels of <strong>Degeneracy</strong>; many different quantum states share the same energy. For the specific case of the \\( 1/r \\) potential (Kepler/Coulomb), an additional \"hidden\" symmetry leads to the conservation of the <strong>Laplace-Runge-Lenz Vector</strong>, resulting in closed orbits and the specific energy levels of the hydrogen atom.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Transition to Non-Central Forces</h3>\n                    <p>Most real-world systems only approximate a central potential. External mass distributions or internal spin-orbit couplings introduce angular dependence, breaking the symmetry and causing <strong>Precession</strong> of the orbits. Measuring these deviations (like the Precession of Mercury) provides critical tests for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> and the limits of classical <strong>Mathematical Realism</strong>.</p>','A Central Potential is a scalar field whose value depends only on the distance from a fixed origin, not on the angular coordinates. In university-level mechanics, these potentials are the primary vehicles for studying systems with perfect Rotational Symmetry, such as planetary orbits and hydrogenic atoms. The existence of a central potential implies that the Torque on a particle is identically zero.','A Central Potential (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"9.277ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 4100.6 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.