5E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(316.8,-2) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"2D9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2D9\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(479,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(7861.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(316.8,-2) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"2D9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2D9\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(479,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-degrees-of-freedom-class-subtopic-link-degrees-of-freedom-a-9057cc89\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-kinetic-metric-class-subtopic-link-metric-representation-a-5de8833f\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-line-element-class-subtopic-link-line-element-a-a1056c04\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-holonomic-constraints-class-subtopic-link-holonomic-constraint-a-69db1844\"]'),
(2009634,'torque','classical-mechanics','Torque','<p>Torque, also known as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/moment-of-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Moment of Force</strong></a>, is the rotational analog of force and serves as the primary generator for the time-evolution of a system\'s angular momentum. It is formally defined as the cross product of the position vector and the force vector: \\( \\boldsymbol{\\tau} = \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{F} \\). In university-level mechanics, torque is treated as an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axial-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axial Vector</strong></a> (or pseudovector), reflecting the inherent antisymmetry of rotational interactions.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Vector Nature: Axial vs. Polar Vectors</h3>\n                    <p>In classical mechanics, vectors are categorized by their transformation properties under <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/parity-inversion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Parity Inversion</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{r} \\to -\\mathbf{r} \\)). Polar vectors like position and force change sign under this transformation. However, torque, being the cross product of two polar vectors, remains invariant: \\( \\boldsymbol{\\tau}\' = (-\\mathbf{r}) \\times (-\\mathbf{F}) = \\boldsymbol{\\tau} \\). This axial nature demonstrates that torque represents a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/rotation-plane\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>rotation in a plane</strong></a> rather than a directed line segment, with its direction determined by the convention of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/right-hand-rule\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Right-Hand Rule</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generalized-torque\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Generalized Torque</strong></a> in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> Mechanics</h3>\n                    <p>In the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> framework, torque is defined through the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/principle-of-virtual-work\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of Virtual Work</strong></a>. For a system with generalized coordinates \\( q_j \\), the generalized force \\( Q_j \\) associated with angular displacement \\( \\theta \\) is the torque \\( \\tau \\):\n                    \\[ Q_\\theta = \\sum_i \\mathbf{F}_i \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{r}_i}{\\partial \\theta} = \\tau \\]\n                    This formulation is particularly powerful because it allows for the analysis of complex, constrained systems without needing to explicitly calculate workless constraint forces (like the tension in a rigid rod).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Torque in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-inertial-frames\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Non-Inertial Frames</strong></a> (Euler Torque)</h3>\n                    <p>When analyzing motion from a rotating reference frame, the effective torque includes <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fictitious-torques\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fictitious Torques</strong></a>. A critical term is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/euler-torque\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Euler Torque</strong></a>, which arises in systems with non-uniform angular velocity (\\( \\dot{\\boldsymbol{\\omega}} \\neq 0 \\)):\n                    \\[ \\boldsymbol{\\tau}_{Euler} = \\mathbf{r} \\times (-m\\dot{\\boldsymbol{\\omega}} \\times \\mathbf{r}) \\]\n                    This term is essential in rigid body dynamics, where it appears in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/euler-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Euler Equations of Motion</strong></a> to describe the precession and nutation of objects like spinning tops or planets.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Principle of Couples and Invariance</h3>\n                    <p>A <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/couple-mechanics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Couple</strong></a> consists of two parallel forces of equal magnitude but opposite direction acting along different lines. A unique property of a couple is that its total moment is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/origin-independence\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>independent of the choice of origin</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{d} \\times \\mathbf{F} \\]\n                    Where \\( \\mathbf{d} \\) is the separation vector between the forces. Because a couple produces a \"pure torque\" with zero net force (\\( \\sum \\mathbf{F} = 0 \\)), it is treated as a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/free-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Free Vector</strong></a> that can be applied at any point on a rigid body to produce the same rotational effect.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Conditions for Static Equilibrium</h3>\n                    <p>For a rigid body to be in absolute <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/static-equilibrium\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Static Equilibrium</strong></a>, two independent vector conditions must be satisfied: the vector sum of all external forces must be zero (\\( \\sum \\mathbf{F} = 0 \\)), and the vector sum of all external torques about <em>any</em> point must be zero (\\( \\sum \\boldsymbol{\\tau} = 0 \\)). In engineering, systems where these equations are insufficient to determine internal loads are termed <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/statically-indeterminate\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Statically Indeterminate</strong></a>, requiring the study of material elasticity to resolve the stresses.</p>','Torque, also known as the Moment of Force, is the rotational analog of force and serves as the primary generator for the time-evolution of a system\'s angular momentum. It is formally defined as the cross product of the position vector and the force vector:. In university-level mechanics, torque is treated as an Axial Vector (or pseudovector), reflecting the inherent antisymmetry of rotational interactions.','Torque, also known as the Moment of Force, is the rotational analog of force and serves as the primary generator for the time-evolution of a system\'s angular momentum. It is formally defined as the cross product of the position vector and the force vector: <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"9.873ex\" height=\"1.724ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 4364 762\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-BI-1D749\" d=\"M55 289H43Q23 289 23 303Q23 308 33 322Q116 434 199 443Q200 444 386 444Q571 444 577 442Q588 441 599 432T610 402Q610 359 572 340Q561 335 547 334T452 333H353V331Q352 330 342 261T320 119T306 40Q300 18 281 3Q257 -13 233 -13Q210 -13 196 0T181 35Q181 44 182 48Q183 53 229 187T279 331Q279 333 228 333H209Q163 333 136 328T88 301L77 290L55 289Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\" d=\"M405 293T374 293T324 312T305 361Q305 378 312 394Q315 397 315 399Q305 399 294 394T266 375T238 329T222 249Q221 241 221 149V62H308V0H298Q280 3 161 3Q47 3 38 0H29V62H98V210V303Q98 353 96 363T83 376Q69 380 42 380H29V442H32L118 446Q204 450 205 450H210V414L211 378Q247 449 315 449H321Q384 449 413 422T442 360Q442 332 424 313Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\" d=\"M630 29Q630 9 609 9Q604 9 587 25T493 118L389 222L284 117Q178 13 175 11Q171 9 168 9Q160 9 154 15T147 29Q147 36 161 51T255 146L359 250L255 354Q174 435 161 449T147 471Q147 480 153 485T168 490Q173 490 175 489Q178 487 284 383L389 278L493 382Q570 459 587 475T609 491Q630 491 630 471Q630 464 620 453T522 355L418 250L522 145Q606 61 618 48T630 29Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D405\" d=\"M425 0L228 3Q63 3 51 0H39V62H147V618H39V680H644V676Q647 670 659 552T675 428V424H613Q613 433 605 477Q599 511 589 535T562 574T530 599T488 612T44