g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1483.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"munder\" transform=\"translate(2539.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2211\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-LO-2211\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(600,-1084.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(4149.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D405\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D405\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(757,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5423,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(5923.3,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(507,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(386,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D703\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D703\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1567\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8008,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9063.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70F\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"vector-definition-b3f4b82f\",\"rotational-dynamics-743c8260\",\"generalized-torque-3a4ca4ac\",\"moment-of-a-couple-50577165\"]'),
(2009635,'rotational-symmetry','classical-mechanics','Rotational Symmetry (Isotropy)','<p><strong>Rotational Symmetry</strong>, also known as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/isotropy-of-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Isotropy of Space</strong></a>, is the fundamental principle stating that the laws of physics are invariant under rotations. In university-level mechanics, this symmetry is the mathematical origin of the <strong>Conservation of Angular Momentum</strong>. It implies that there is no \"preferred direction\" in the universe; the fundamental interactions between particles depend only on their relative orientations and distances, not on the absolute orientation of the entire system in space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Isotropy and the SO(3) Group</h3>\n                    <p>The concept of rotational symmetry is an expression of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/uniformity-of-directions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Uniformity of Directions</strong></a> in space. Mathematically, this described by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/special-orthogonal-group-so3\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Special Orthogonal Group SO(3)</strong></a>, the group of all rotations about the origin three-dimensional Euclidean space. If a physical system isolated, its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> (\\( \\mathcal{L} \\)) must be a scalar under these rotations, meaning it can only depend on rotationally invariant quantities like the magnitudes of vectors (\\( |\\mathbf{r}|, |\\mathbf{v}| \\)) and the dot products between them (\\( \\mathbf{r}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{r}_j \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> Invariance and Infinitesimal Rotations</h3>\n                    <p>A system possesses rotational symmetry if its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> remains unchanged when all position and velocity vectors are rotated by an infinitesimal angle \\( \\delta \\theta \\) about an axis \\( \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\). The change in a position vector under such a rotation is \\( \\delta \\mathbf{r} = \\delta \\theta (\\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\times \\mathbf{r}) \\). For the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> to be invariant (\\( \\delta \\mathcal{L} = 0 \\)), the following condition must hold:\n                    \\[ \\delta \\mathcal{L} = \\sum_i \\left( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}_i} \\cdot \\delta \\mathbf{r}_i + \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{\\mathbf{r}}_i} \\cdot \\delta \\dot{\\mathbf{r}}_i \\right) = 0 \\]\n                    Substituting the rotational variations and using the vector triple product identities, we find that this requirement is equivalent to the vanishing of the total external torque.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Deriving <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> Conservation</h3>\n                    <p>According to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/noethers-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Noether\'s Theorem</strong></a>, this rotational invariance leads directly to a conserved \"charge.\" Using the Euler-Lagrange equations, the variation of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> can be rewritten as the time derivative of a specific quantity:\n                    \\[ \\frac{d}{dt} \\sum_i (\\mathbf{r}_i \\times \\mathbf{p}_i) \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} = 0 \\]\n                    Since the axis \\( \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\) is arbitrary, the entire vector sum \\( \\mathbf{L}_{total} = \\sum \\mathbf{r}_i \\times \\mathbf{p}_i \\) must be a constant of the motion. This confirms that <strong>Angular Momentum Conservation</strong> is the physical manifestation of the isotropy of space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/central-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Central Forces</strong></a> and the Orbital Plane</h3>\n                    <p>A common example of rotational symmetry is a particle moving in a <strong>Central Potential</strong> (\\( V = V(r) \\)). Because the potential depends only on the distance from the origin, the system is invariant under rotations about any axis passing through that origin. Consequently, the angular momentum vector \\( \\mathbf{L} \\) is conserved in both magnitude and direction. This conservation forces the particle\'s trajectory to remain within a fixed plane perpendicular to \\( \\mathbf{L} \\), simplifying a three-dimensional problem into a two-dimensional one.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> as the Generator of Rotations</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> and Quantum mechanics, the relationship is reciprocal: angular momentum is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generator-of-rotations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Generator of Rotations</strong></a>. The operator that rotates a system\'s state by angle \\( \\theta \\) about an axis \\( \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\) is given by \\( U = e^{-i\\theta (\\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{J}})/\\hbar} \\). This reveals that the conserved quantity we measure (angular momentum) is the same mathematical object that \"rotates\" the universe, cementing the deep link between dynamics and the geometry of the rotation group.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Rotational Symmetry (Isotropy) is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/central-potential\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Central Potentials and Angular Independence</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/geometric-variation\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Geometric Variation under Rotation</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-abelian-algebra\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Non-Abelian Algebra of Rotations</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','Rotational Symmetry, also known as the Isotropy of Space, is the fundamental principle stating that the laws of physics are invariant under rotations. In university-level mechanics, this symmetry is the mathematical origin of the Conservation of Angular Momentum. It implies that there is no \"preferred direction\" in the universe; the fundamental interactions between particles depend only on their relative orientations and distances, not on the absolute orientation of the entire system in space.','Rotational Symmetry, also known as the Isotropy of Space, is the fundamental principle stating that the laws of physics are invariant under rotations. In university-level mechanics, this symmetry is the mathematical origin of the Conservation of Angular Momentum. It implies that there is no \"preferred direction\" in the universe; the fundamental interactions between particles depend only on their relative orientations and distances, not on the absolute orientation of the entire system in space.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.697ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"36.55ex\" height=\"5.977ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1449.5 16154.9 2641.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FF\" d=\"M195 609Q195 656 227 686T302 717Q319 716 351 709T407 697T433 690Q451 682 451 662Q451 644 438 628T403 612Q382 612 348 641T288 671T249 657T235 628Q235 584 334 463Q401 379 401 292Q401 169 340 80T205 -10H198Q127 -10 83 36T36 153Q36 286 151 382Q191 413 252 434Q252 435 245 449T230 481T214 521T201 566T195 609ZM112 130Q11