ef=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3325.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) is a second-order partial differential equation that describes the behavior of potential fields in regions free of sources. While commonly associated with electrostatics, it is equally vital in magnetostatics for finding the magnetic field in regions where no electric current is present. In university-level physics, solving Laplace\'s equation allows for the precise calculation of magnetic fields around permanent magnets and complex iron structures without the need for direct integration of current loops.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.654ex\" height=\"2.072ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 3825.1 915.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\" d=\"M312 622Q310 623 307 625T303 629T297 631T286 634T270 635T246 636T211 637H184V683H196Q220 680 361 680T526 683H538V637H511Q468 637 447 635T422 631T411 622V533L425 531Q525 519 595 466T665 342Q665 301 642 267T583 209T506 172T425 152L411 150V61Q417 55 421 53T447 48T511 46H538V0H526Q502 3 361 3T196 0H184V46H211Q231 46 245 46T