 transform=\"translate(6232.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D405\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D405\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7178.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7678.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(8198.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(507,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2032\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-2032\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-gauge-choice-84cbe50c\",\"energy-definition-3478b0a0\",\"potential-distinction-aa0351b2\",\"absolute-gradient-30e05d31\"]'),
(2009726,'divergent-fields','coulombs-law','Divergent Fields and Source Density','<p>In vector calculus and electromagnetism, a <strong>Divergent Field</strong> is one for which the net flux through a closed surface is non-zero, indicating the presence of sources or sinks. For the electric field, the property of being divergent is the defining physical consequence of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/coulombs-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Coulomb\'s Law</strong></a>, formalized in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a>. It states that electric field lines originate on positive charges and terminate on negative charges, reflecting the \"Point-Source\" nature of the electrostatic force.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Divergence Operator (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} \\))</h3>\n                    <p>The divergence of a field at a point measures the \"outgoingness\" of the field lines. For the Coulomb field of a point charge \\( q \\), the divergence is zero everywhere except at the location of the charge itself, where it is infinite. In university-level physics, this described by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a> in differential form:\n                    \\[ \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = \\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0} \\]\n                    This equation relates the local \"spread\" of the field to the local <strong>Charge Density</strong> (\\( \\rho \\)), proving that the electric field is not circulatory but rather emanates from its sources.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Flux and the Inverse-Square Law</h3>\n                    <p>A field is divergent only if it possesses sources. The <strong>Inverse-Square Dependence</strong> of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/coulombs-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Coulomb\'s Law</strong></a> is specifically required for the flux to be constant across any surface enclosing a charge. If the field decayed at any other rate, the total flux would depend on the distance, implying that field lines were being created or destroyed in empty space. Thus, the divergent nature of the E-field is a direct macroscopic signature of the 3D geometry of space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Sources and Sinks: The Physical Topology</h3>\n                    <p>In the  of electrodynamics, charges act as the topological \"Punctures\" where field lines enter or leave the vacuum.\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Positive Source (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} \\gt 0 \\)):</strong> Field lines move outward (e.g., a proton).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Negative Sink (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} \\lt 0 \\)):</strong> Field lines move inward (e.g., an electron).</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    This topology ensures that the electric field is <strong>Irrotational</strong> but not solenoidal, contrasting sharply with the magnetic field which is always solenoidal (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0 \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Divergence and Poisson\'s Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The divergent nature of the field allows it to be represented by a <strong>Scalar Potential</strong> (\\( \\Phi \\)). By substituting \\( \\mathbf{E} = -\\nabla \\Phi \\) into the divergence equation, we obtain <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/poisson-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Poisson\'s Equation</strong></a>: \\( \\nabla^2 \\Phi = -\\rho/\\varepsilon_0 \\). This second-order PDE describes the \"curvature\" of the potential landscape, where the divergence of the force determines the local \"depth\" of the energy well.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Divergence in Matter: Bound Charge</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Dielectric</strong> materials, the divergence of the total electric field must account for \"Bound Charges\" resulting from <strong>Permittivity</strong>. This leads to the definition of the <strong>Electric Displacement</strong> field (\\( \\mathbf{D} \\)), where \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{D} = \\rho_{free} \\). This formulation allows physicists to isolate the effects of the fundamental source from the \"Screening\" effects of the medium, a core concept in multi-body <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Cosmological Divergence and Dark Energy</h3>\n                    <p>On the largest scales, the concept of divergence is applied to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Expansion</strong></a>. In <strong>Physical Cosmology</strong>, the divergence of the velocity field of galaxies is proportional to the <strong>Hubble Parameter</strong>. Just as charges act as sources for the electric field, <strong>Dark Energy</strong> acts as a source for the expansion of the manifold, pushing the universe apart in a global, divergent flow that determines the ultimate fate of the cosmos.</p>','In vector calculus and electromagnetism, a Divergent Field is one for which the net flux through a closed surface is non-zero, indicating the presence of sources or sinks. For the electric field, the property of being divergent is the defining physical consequence of Coulomb\'s Law, formalized in Gauss\'s Law. It states that electric field lines originate on positive charges and terminate on negative charges, reflecting the \"Point-Source\" nature of the electrostatic force.','In vector calculus and electromagnetism, a Divergent Field is one for which the net flux through a closed surface is non-zero, indicating the presence of sources or sinks. For the electric field, the property of being divergent is the defining physical consequence of Coulomb\'s Law, formalized in Gauss\'s Law. It states that electric field lines originate on positive charges and terminate on negative charges, reflecting the \"Point-Source\" nature of the electrostatic force.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.927ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"11.284ex\" height=\"4.456ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1118 4987.6 1969.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\" d=\"M78 250Q78 274 95 292T138 310Q162 310 180 294T199 251Q199 226 182 208T139 190T96 207T78 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\" d=\"M723 286Q721 284 700 145T677 3V0H39V62H147V618H39V680H660V676Q662 670 675 552T691 428V424H629V428Q629 429 627 448T618 494T601 541Q574 593 527 605T382 618H374H304V384H336Q338 384 347 384T361 384T376 386T392 390T407 397T421 407T432 423Q442 444 443 482V501H505V205H443V224Q442 258 435 278T411 307T380 318T336 322H304V62H375H394Q429 62 449 62T497 66T541 76T577 95T609 126T632 170T651 232Q661 287 661 289H723V286Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D700\" d=\"M190 -22Q124 -22 76 11T27 107Q27 174 97 232L107 239L99 248Q76 273 76 304Q76 364 144 408T290 452H302Q360 452 405 421Q428 405 428 392Q428 381 417 369T391 356Q382 356 371 365T338 383T283 392Q217 392 167 368T116 308Q116 289 13