7Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1055.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1555.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1944.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2999.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3999.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D400\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D400\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4868.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5535.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"2261\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2261\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(6591.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"math-origin-5105fe83\",\"no-sink-law-89896bc1\",\"continuity-link-fa023ba5\",\"symmetry-source-35356b5e\"]'),
(2009747,'cylindrical-coordinates','curl','Curl in Cylindrical Coordinates','<p><strong>Cylindrical Coordinates</strong> (\\( s, \\phi, z \\)) are the natural geometric framework for physical systems with axial symmetry, such as the <strong>Infinite Straight Wire</strong>, solenoids, and rotating fluids. In university-level electromagnetism, expressing the <strong>curl</strong> in cylindrical form is essential for solving <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maxwells-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maxwell\'s Equations</strong></a> in environments where the  curvature follows a central axis, allowing for the direct calculation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/magnetostatic-circulation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Magnetostatic Circulation</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Unit Vector Basis</h3>\n                    <p>Cylindrical coordinates utilize a set of <strong>Orthonormal Basis Vectors</strong>: \\( \\hat{s} \\) (radial distance from the z-axis), \\( \\hat{\\phi} \\) (azimuthal angle), and \\( \\hat{z} \\) (axial height). Unlike Cartesian unit vectors, \\( \\hat{s} \\) and \\( \\hat{\\phi} \\) depend on position. This means that when taking derivatives, one must account for the change in direction of the basis itself, leading to the characteristic \\( 1/s \\) terms in the differential operators.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Cylindrical Curl Formula</h3>\n                    <p>The curl of a vector field \\( \\mathbf{A} \\) in cylindrical coordinates is given by:\n                    \\[ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{A} = \\left( \\frac{1}{s} \\frac{\\partial A_z}{\\partial \\phi} - \\frac{\\partial A_\\phi}{\\partial z} \\right) \\hat{s} + \\left( \\frac{\\partial A_s}{\\partial z} - \\frac{\\partial A_z}{\\partial s} \\right) \\hat{\\phi} + \\frac{1}{s} \\left( \\frac{\\partial (s A_\\phi)}{\\partial s} - \\frac{\\partial A_s}{\\partial \\phi} \\right) \\hat{z} \\]\n                    This formula reveals the \"Mixed\" nature of rotation in a curved space, where the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vorticity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vorticity</strong></a> along the z-axis determined by the radial rate of change of the azimuthal field.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Application: Field around a Wire</h3>\n                    <p>For a steady current flowing along the z-axis, the magnetic field is purely azimuthal (\\( \\mathbf{B} = B_\\phi \\hat{\\phi} \\)). According to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ampere-maxwell-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ampère-Maxwell Law</strong></a>, the z-component of the curl must equal \\( \\mu_0 J_z \\). Solving the cylindrical curl gives \\( \\frac{1}{s} \\frac{d(s B_\\phi)}{ds} = 0 \\) in vacuum, which integrates to the famous \\( 1/s \\) dependence of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/biot-savart-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Biot-Savart Law</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Angular Velocity and Rigid Rotation</h3>\n                    <p>If the vector field represents the velocity of a rotating fluid (\\( \\mathbf{v} = \\omega s \\hat{\\phi} \\)), the z-component of the curl is exactly \\( 2\\omega \\). This proves that the curl is a measure of twice the local <strong>Angular Velocity</strong>. This relationship is foundational for <strong>Astrophysical Fluid Dynamics</strong>, where it is used to track the rotation of accretion disks and planetary atmospheres.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Gauge Symmetry and the Vector Potential</h3>\n                    <p>In cylindrical symmetry, the <strong>Magnetic Vector Potential</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{A} \\)) often has only a z-component or an azimuthal component. Choosing the right cylindrical gauge simplifies the <strong>Poisson Equation</strong> for the potential. This allows for a clean <strong>Geometric Bridge</strong> between the 4-current density and the resulting field geometry, essential for the design of particle accelerators and fusion reactors.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Relativistic Frame Dragging</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, a rotating massive object (like a <strong>Kerr Black Hole</strong>) distorts the cylindrical geometry of the surrounding space. This leads to <strong>Frame-Dragging</strong>, where the spacetime itself \"Curls\" around the object. Expressing this distortion in cylindrical-like coordinates (Boyer-Lindquist) is the standard method for studying the orbits of matter in the strong-field relativistic regime.</p>','Cylindrical Coordinates are the natural geometric framework for physical systems with axial symmetry, such as the Infinite Straight Wire, solenoids, and rotating fluids. In university-level electromagnetism, expressing the curl in cylindrical form is essential for solving Maxwell\'s Equations in environments where the curvature follows a central axis, allowing for the direct calculation of Magnetostatic Circulation. Cylindrical coordinates utilize a set of Orthonormal Basis Vectors: (radial distance from the z-axis), (azimuthal angle), and (axial height).','Cylindrical Coordinates (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.474ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 2419.3 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\" d=\"M131 289Q131 321 147 354T203 415T300 442Q362 442 390 415T419 355Q419 323 402 308T364 292Q351 292 340 300T328 326Q328 342 337 354T354 372T367 378Q368 378 368 379Q368 382 361 388T336 399T297 405Q249 405 227 379T204 326Q204 301 223 291T278 274T330 259Q396 230 396 163Q396 135 385 107T352 51T289 7T195 -10Q118 -10 86 19T53 87Q53 126 74 143T118 160Q133 160 146 151T160 120Q160 94 142 76T111 58Q109 57 108 57T107 55Q108 52 115 47T146 34T201 27Q237 27 263 38T301 66T318 97T323 122Q323 150 302 164T254 181T195 196T148 231Q131 256 131 289Z\">