sform=\"translate(479,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-709.5)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(278,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(974.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(1974.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(507,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2775.4,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"3253.4\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-simple-sum-e1532491\",\"global-volume-3cffe761\",\"resolution-scaling-cda22a13\",\"impulse-response-4fcc7d95\"]'),
(2009763,'potential-difference','electric-potential','Potential Difference','<p>Potential Difference (\\( \\Delta \\Phi \\)) is the spatial difference in <strong>Electric Potential</strong> between two points in a field. In university-level physics, it is the primary driver of <strong>Physical Causality</strong> for charged matter. While \"Potential\" is a property of a single point relative to a reference, \"Potential Difference\" is the physically observable quantity that determines the work done on a charge, serving as the basis for <strong>Voltage</strong> and circuit dynamics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Measure of Available Work</h3>\n                    <p>Potential difference is defined as the work per unit charge required to move a particle from point A to point B:\n                    \\[ \\Delta \\Phi = \\Phi_B - \\Phi_A = \\frac{W_{ext}}{q} \\]\n                    It measures the \"Energy Gap\" available in the . A positive charge will naturally \"fall\" through a potential difference from high to low potential, converting its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/potential-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Potential Energy</strong></a> into kinetic energy or heat.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/path-independence\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Path Independence</strong></a> in Electrostatics</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Conservative Fields</strong>, the potential difference between two points depends only on the endpoints, not on the trajectory taken. This guaranteed by the <strong>irrotational</strong> condition (\\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{E} = 0 \\)). This path independence is what allowed for the development of modern electrical grids, as it ensures that the energy delivered to a home depends only on the voltage at the socket, not on the path the electrons took from the power plant.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Potential Difference vs. Electric Field</h3>\n                    <p>The potential difference is the integral of the <strong>Electric Field</strong> over distance. If the field is uniform, the relationship is simple: \\( \\Delta \\Phi = -Ed \\). This shows that a large potential difference can be achieved with a weak field over a long distance, or a strong field over a short distance. This trade-off is essential for the design of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/particle-accelerators\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Particle Accelerators</strong></a> and high-voltage power lines.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Potential Difference in Non-Conservative Fields</h3>\n                    <p>According to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a>, a changing magnetic flux induces a field with a non-zero <strong>curl</strong>. In such cases, the potential difference is no longer path-independent. The concept of \"Voltage\" between two points becomes ambiguous. Instead, we use the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromotive-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromotive Force (EMF)</strong></a>, which is the potential difference calculated around a specific <strong>closed path</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Potential Difference in the Standard Model</h3>\n                    <p>At the subatomic level, potential differences across the <strong>Potential Barrier</strong> of a nucleus or a semiconductor junction dictate the rate of <strong>Quantum Tunneling</strong>. The energy gap must be overcome by the <strong>Wave Function</strong> phase. This links macroscopic potential difference to the <strong>Born Interpretation</strong> of probability, where \\( \\Delta \\Phi \\) sets the odds of a particle crossing a forbidden region.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Relative Observable</h3>\n                    <p>Potential difference is the only part of potential theory that is truly observable. We cannot measure the absolute potential of a point without comparing it to another (the <strong>Reference Point</strong>). This <strong>Relational</strong> nature of potential difference suggests that the universe is not made of absolute values, but of differences and gradients, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the relationships between parts of the .</p>','Potential Difference is the spatial difference in Electric Potential between two points in a field. In university-level physics, it is the primary driver of Physical Causality for charged matter. While \"Potential\" is a property of a single point relative to a reference, \"Potential Difference\" is the physically observable quantity that determines the work done on a charge, serving as the basis for Voltage and circuit dynamics.','Potential Difference (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.518ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1555 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-394\" d=\"M51 0Q46 4 46 7Q46 9 215 357T388 709Q391 716 416 716Q439 716 444 709Q447 705 616 357T786 7Q786 4 781 0H51ZM507 344L384 596L137 92L383 91H630Q630 93 507 344Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\" d=\"M312 622Q310 623 307 625T303 629T297 631T286 634T270 635T246 636T211 637H184V683H196Q220 680 361 680T526 683H538V637H511Q468 637 447 635T422 631T411 622V533L425 531Q525 519 595 466T665 342Q665 301 642 267T583 209T506 172T425 152L411 150V61Q417 55 421 53T447 48T511 46H538V0H526Q502 3 361 3T196 0H184V46H211Q231 46 245 46T270 47T286 48T297 51T303 54T307 57T312 61V150H310Q309 151 289 153T232 166T160 195Q149 201 136 210T103 238T69 284T56 342Q56 414 128 467T294 530Q309 532 310 533H312V622ZM170 342Q170 207 307 188H312V495H309Q301 495 282 491T231 469T186 423Q170 389 170 342ZM415 188Q487 199 519 236T551 342Q551 384 539 414T507 459T470 481T434 491T415 495H410V188H415Z\"></path></defs><g strok