,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D714\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D714\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3493,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3854,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(14546.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(553,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(15503,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D424\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D424\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"construction-rule-5c04ca8c\",\"the-spectrum-6f5e3c9c\",\"localized-light-bb07c2fd\",\"group-vs-phase-47d36168\"]'),
(2009780,'transverse-nature','electromagnetic-wave-equation','Transverse Nature of EM Waves','<p>The <strong>Transverse Nature</strong> of electromagnetic waves is the physical property that the oscillations of the <strong>electric field</strong> and <strong>magnetic field</strong> are perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation. In university-level physics, this not an assumption but a rigorous requirement of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maxwells-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maxwell\'s Equations</strong></a> in a vacuum. It distinguishes light from longitudinal waves (like sound) and provides the physical basis for <strong>Polarization</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Constraint from Gauss\'s Law</h3>\n                    <p>In a region free of charges, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a> states \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = 0 \\). For a <strong>Monochromatic Plane Wave</strong>, the divergence operator (\\( \\nabla \\)) acts as a dot product with the <strong>Propagation Vector</strong> (\\( i\\mathbf{k} \\)). \n                    \\[ \\mathbf{k} \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = 0 \\]\n                    This dot product being zero mathematically proves that the electric field vector has no component in the direction of motion. The field can only \"wiggle\" in the 2D plane perpendicular to its path.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Constraint from Gauss\'s Law for Magnetism</h3>\n                    <p>Similarly, \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0 \\) implies \\( \\mathbf{k} \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0 \\). The magnetic field is also purely transverse. Furthermore, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a> dictates that \\( \\mathbf{B} = \\frac{1}{c} (\\hat{\\mathbf{k}} \\times \\mathbf{E}) \\). This ensures that the B-field is perpendicular to both the propagation direction and the E-field, forming a rigid, orthogonal 4D \"Structure\" of light.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Polarization: The Result of Transversality</h3>\n                    <p>Because the field is restricted to two perpendicular dimensions, it can have different orientations. This <strong>Polarization</strong>. If light were longitudinal, it would have only one possible state of vibration (forward/backward). The transverse nature allows for linear, circular, and elliptical polarization, providing the \"hidden\" degrees of freedom used in <strong>LHC Searches</strong> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Entanglement</strong></a> experiments.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Transverse nature in Relativity</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Special Relativity</strong>, the transverse nature is preserved under <strong>Lorentz Transformations</strong>. While the longitudinal components of a 4-vector are contracted, the transverse components of the <strong>Electromagnetic Field Tensor</strong> remain unchanged in magnitude. This ensures that the <strong>Velocity of Light</strong> remains constant and the wave remains transverse for all observers, anchoring the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Longitudinal Waves in Matter?</h3>\n                    <p>In most media, light remains transverse. However, in <strong>Plasmas</strong> or near 4D <strong>Boundary Conditions</strong>, \"Longitudinal-like\" waves (Plasmons) can exist. These are collective oscillations of the <strong>4-Current Density</strong>. Determining where the transverse-only rule breaks down is essential for <strong>Astrophysical Fluid Dynamics</strong> and the study of the <strong>Early Universe</strong> plasma.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Why Transverse?</h3>\n                    <p>The transverse nature of light is the reason why the <strong>Photon</strong> must be perfectly massless. In <strong>Quantum Field Theory</strong>, a massive particle (like the Z-boson) can have a longitudinal polarization state. The absolute masslessness of the photon (\\( m_\\gamma = 0 \\)) \"kills\" the longitudinal degree of freedom, forcing light to be purely transverse and ensuring the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Invariance</strong></a> of the universe.</p>','The Transverse Nature of electromagnetic waves is the physical property that the oscillations of the electric field and magnetic field are perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation. In university-level physics, this not an assumption but a rigorous requirement of Maxwell\'s Equations in a vacuum. It distinguishes light from longitudinal waves (like sound) and provides the physical basis for Polarization.','The Transverse Nature of electromagnetic waves is the physical property that the oscillations of the electric field and magnetic field are perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation. In university-level physics, this not an assumption but a rigorous requirement of Maxwell\'s Equations in a vacuum. It distinguishes light from longitudinal waves (like sound) and provides the physical basis for Polarization.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.867ex\" height=\"1.756ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 3919 776\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D424\" d=\"M32 686L123 690Q214 694 215 694H221V255L377 382H346V444H355Q370 441 476 441Q544 441 556 444H562V382H476L347 277L515 62H587V0H579Q564 3 476 3Q370 3 352 0H343V62H358L373 63L260 206L237 189L216 172V62H285V0H277Q259 3 157 3Q46 3 37 0H29V62H98V332Q98 387 98 453T99 534Q99 593 97 605T83 620Q69 624 42 624H29V686H32Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\" d=\"M78 250Q78 274 95 292T138 310Q162 310 180 294T199 251Q199 226 182 208T139 190T96 207T78 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\" d=\"M723 286Q721 284 700 145T677 3V0H39V62H147V618H39V680H660V676Q662 670 675 552T691 428V424H629V428Q629 429 627 448T618 494T601 541Q574 593 527 605T382 618H374H304V384H336Q338 384 347 384T361 384T376 386T392 390T407 397T421 407T432 423Q442 444 443 482V501H505V205H443V224Q442 258 435 278T411 307T380 318T336 322H304V62H375H394Q429 62 449 62T497 66T541 76T577 95T609 126T632 170T651 232Q661 287 661 289H723V286Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D424\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D424\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(829.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1329.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D404\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2363.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3419,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"crosswise-wiggle-5876494d\",\"divergence-proof-ee095497\",\"orthogonal-triplet-d90b32e7\",\"photon-rule-056fdbc5\"]'),
(2009781,'polarization','electromagnetic-wave-equation','Polarization of Electromagnetic Waves','<p><strong>Polarization</strong> is the physical property of <strong>transverse</strong> waves that describes the geometric orientation of the oscillations. For <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-radiation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromagnetic Radiation</strong></a>, polarization refers specifically to the direction of the <strong>electric field</strong> vector in the 2D plane perpendicular to the direction of propagation. In university-level optics, polarization is the definitive evidence of light\'s transverse nature and is essential for understanding 4D subatomic interactions and modern telecommunications.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Transverse Constraint</h3>\n                    <p>According to Maxwell\'s equations in a vacuum, the electric field \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) must be perpendicular to the <strong>propagation vector</strong> \\( \\mathbf{k} \\). This leaves two independent degrees of freedom for the vibration of the field. If we choose the z-axis as the direction of motion, the electric field can oscillate in any direction within the x-y plane. The specific trajectory traced by the tip of the \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) vector over time is what defines the state of polarization.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Linear, Circular, and Elliptical Polarization</h3>\n                    <p>Polarization is categorized by the phase relationship between the x and y components of the field:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Linear:</strong> The components are in phase, and the vector traces a straight line.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Circular:</strong> The components have equal magnitude but are 90 degrees out of phase, tracing a circle.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Elliptical:</strong> The general case where magnitudes and phases result in an elliptical path.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    Circular polarization corresponds to the <strong>Spin</strong> of individual photons, linking macroscopic optics to <strong>Quantum Physics</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Jones Vectors and Matrices</h3>\n                    <p>In university physics, the state of polarization is mathematically represented by <strong>Jones Vectors</strong>. A Jones vector is a 2-component complex vector \\( \\mathbf{J} = (E_x e^{i\\phi_x}, E_y e^{i\\phi_y}) \\). The transformation of polarization by optical elements (like waveplates or polarizers) is described by \\( 2 \\times 2 \\) <strong>Jones Matrices</strong>. This matrix-based approach is essential for modeling the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of light in complex systems like LCD screens.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Polarization by Scattering and Reflection</h3>\n                    <p>Unpolarized light (like sunlight) can become polarized through interaction with matter. <strong>Rayleigh Scattering</strong> in the atmosphere polarizes light perpendicular to the scattering plane, which is why the sky appears polarized. Similarly, <strong>Brewster\'s Angle</strong> is the specific angle of incidence where reflected light is 100% linearly polarized. These phenomena are used by astronomers to map the <strong>Magnetic Fields</strong> of distant nebulae and stars.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Stokes Parameters and Poincare Sphere</h3>\n                    <p>To describe partially polarized or unpolarized light, physicists use <strong>Stokes Parameters</strong> (\\( S_0, S_1, S_2, S_3 \\)). These four values represent the total intensity and the various polarization components. They are often mapped onto the <strong>Poincaré Sphere</strong>, a 3D visualization tool where every point on the surface represents a pure polarization state and the interior represents mixed states, providing a <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for the study of coherence.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Relativistic Polarization and Spin</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Special Relativity</strong>, the polarization vector is a part of the <strong>Electromagnetic Field Tensor</strong>. Under a <strong>Lorentz Transformation</strong>, the polarization of a light beam remains transverse but can appear rotated (the <strong>Wigner Rotation</strong>). In the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, the two polarization states of light correspond to the two <strong>Helicity</strong> states of the massless photon, proving that polarization is a fundamental subatomic label of 4D spacetime reality.</p>','Polarization is the physical property of transverse waves that describes the geometric orientation of the oscillations. For Electromagnetic Radiation, polarization refers specifically to the direction of the electric field vector in the 2D plane perpendicular to the direction of propagation. In university-level optics, polarization is the definitive evidence of light\'s transverse nature and is essential for understanding 4D subatomic interactions and modern telecommunications.','Polarization is the physical property of transverse waves that describes the geometric orientation of the oscillations. For Electromagnetic Radiation, polarization refers specifically to the direction of the electric field vector in the 2D plane perpendicular to the direction of propagation. In university-level optics, polarization is the definitive evidence of light\'s transverse nature and is essential for understanding 4D subatomic interactions and modern telecommunications.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.71ex\" height=\"1.538ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 756 680\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\" d=\"M723 286Q721 284 700 145T677 3V0H39V62H147V618H39V680H660V676Q662 670 675 552T691 428V424H629V428Q629 429 627 448T618 494T601 541Q574 593 527 605T382 618H374H304V384H336Q338 384 347 384T361 384T376 386T392 390T407 397T421 407T432 423Q442 444 443 482V501H505V205H443V224Q442 258 435 278T411 307T380 318T336 322H304V62H375H394Q429 62 449 62T497 66T541 76T577 95T609 126T632 170T651 232Q661 287 661 289H723V286Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D404\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"field-orientation-52a3ae4d\",\"circular-rule-89f5a59e\",\"matrix-math-8b8a3af3\",\"quantum-spin-e62a6b62\"]'),
(2009782,'dalembertian-operator','electromagnetic-wave-equation','The d\'Alembertian Operator (\\(\\Box\\))','<p>The <strong>d\'Alembertian Operator</strong> (\\( \\Box \\)) is the four-dimensional generalization of the Laplacian operator, combining spatial and temporal second derivatives into a single relativistic operator. In university-level physics, the d\'Alembertian is the fundamental operator of wave mechanics in 4D spacetime. It is the \"engine\" of the <strong>Electromagnetic Wave Equation</strong>, ensuring that the propagation of fields respects <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lorentz-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lorentz Invariance</strong></a> and the <strong>Cosmic Speed Limit</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Defining the 4D Laplacian</h3>\n                    <p>In standard Cartesian coordinates with the <strong>Minkowski Metric</strong> signature (\\( + - - - \\)), the d\'Alembertian is defined as:\n                    \\[ \\Box = \\frac{1}{c^2} \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial t^2} - \\nabla^2 \\]\n                    It represents the difference between the \"temporal curvature\" and the \"spatial curvature\" of a field. For any physical quantity that travels at the <strong>Velocity of Light</strong>, the d\'Alembertian must be zero, identifying the quantity as a <strong>Null</strong> propagation in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Wave Equation as \\(\\Box \\Psi = 0\\)</h3>\n                    <p>The vacuum <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maxwells-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maxwell\'s Equations</strong></a> can be condensed into the simple statement that the <strong>Electromagnetic Potential 4-Vector</strong> (\\( A^\\mu \\)) satisfies:\n                    \\[ \\Box A^\\mu = 0 \\]\n                    this is <strong>Relativistic Wave Equation</strong>. It unifies the wave behavior of the scalar potential (\\( \\Phi \\)) and the vector potential (\\( \\mathbf{A} \\)), proving that electricity and magnetism are governed by a single 4D geometric rule.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Inhomogeneous d\'Alembertian and Sourcing</h3>\n                    <p>In the presence of sources, the operator relates the field to the <strong>4-Current Density</strong> (\\( J^\\mu \\)):\n                    \\[ \\Box A^\\mu = \\mu_0 J^\\mu \\]\n                    this is \"Source-Response\" equation for electromagnetism. It shows that charges and currents act as the \"driving terms\" for spacetime ripples. The solution to this equation leads directly to <strong>Retarded Potentials</strong>, linking the d\'Alembertian to <strong>Physical Causality</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Lorentz Invariance of the Operator</h3>\n                    <p>The primary theoretical advantage of the d\'Alembertian is that it is a <strong>Lorentz Invariant</strong> scalar operator. This means that if the d\'Alembertian of a field is zero in one <strong>Inertial Frame</strong>, it is zero in all frames. This property is what makes the speed of light \\( c \\) a universal constant, providing the <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for the second postulate of special relativity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. d\'Alembertian in Quantum Field Theory</h3>\n                    <p>In the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, the d\'Alembertian appears in the <strong>Klein-Gordon Equation</strong> for massive particles: \\( (\\Box + m^2) \\phi = 0 \\). This shows that mass acts as a \"restorative\" term that prevents waves from traveling at \\( c \\). The d\'Alembertian is therefore the \"Massless Baseline\" from which all subatomic dynamics are measured, linking wave-math to the <strong>Big Bang</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. General Relativistic Generalization</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the d\'Alembertian is generalized using <strong>Covariant Divergence</strong>: \\( \\Box = \\nabla_\\mu \\nabla^\\mu \\). This \"Beltrami-Laplace\" operator accounts for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/curvature-of-spacetime\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Curvature of Spacetime</strong></a>. In this context, the d\'Alembertian describes the propagation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-waves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Waves</strong></a>, proving that ripples in the <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> itself follow the same wave logic as light.</p>','The d\'Alembertian Operator is the four-dimensional generalization of the Laplacian operator, combining spatial and temporal second derivatives into a single relativistic operator. In university-level physics, the d\'Alembertian is the fundamental operator of wave mechanics in 4D spacetime. It is the \"engine\" of the Electromagnetic Wave Equation, ensuring that the propagation of fields respects Lorentz Invariance and the Cosmic Speed Limit.','The d\'Alembertian Operator (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.559ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -689 778 689\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-25FB\" d=\"M71 0Q59 4 55 16V346L56 676Q64 686 70 689H709Q719 681 722 674V15Q719 10 709 1L390 0H71ZM682 40V649H95V40H682Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"25FB\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-25FB\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) is the four-dimensional generalization of the Laplacian operator, combining spatial and temporal second derivatives into a single relativistic operator. In university-level physics, the d\'Alembertian is the fundamental operator of wave mechanics in 4D spacetime. It is the \"engine\" of the Electromagnetic Wave Equation, ensuring that the propagation of fields respects Lorentz Invariance and the Cosmic Speed Limit.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.679ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.459ex\" height=\"5.095ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1509.9 7716.7 2251.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-25FB\" d=\"M71 0Q59 4 55 16V346L56 676Q64 686 70 689H709Q719 681 722 674V15Q719 10 709 1L390 0H71ZM682 40V649H95V40H682Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\" d=\"M202 508Q179 508 169 520T158 547Q158 557 164 577T185 624T230 675T301 710L333 715H345Q378 715 384 714Q447 703 489 661T549 568T566 457Q566 362 519 