(2009792,'interface-condition','faradays-law','Interface Conditions induction','<p><strong>Interface Conditions</strong> (or <strong>Boundary Conditions</strong>) induction describe how the <strong>non-conservative electric field</strong> and magnetic flux behave at the junction between two materials. In university-level physics, these conditions are derived from the <strong>Maxwell-Faraday Law</strong>. They dictate how time-varying fields \"refract\" at boundaries and are essential for understanding the <strong>Skin Effect</strong> and the efficiency of electromagnetic shielding.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Continuity of the Tangential E-Field</h3>\n                    <p>The primary interface condition for induction is that the tangential component of the electric field (\\( E_{\\parallel} \\)) must be continuous across any boundary:\n                    \\[ E_{1\\parallel} = E_{2\\parallel} \\]\n                    This arises because the line integral of \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) around a tiny loop spanning the interface must vanish as the loop area goes to zero. Even in a <strong>non-conservative</strong> field, the \"Swirl\" cannot be infinite at a point, ensuring that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the field remains smooth.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Flux Continuity and Normal B-Fields</h3>\n                    <p>Similarly, the normal component of the magnetic field (\\( B_{\\perp} \\)) must be continuous (\\( B_{1\\perp} = B_{2\\perp} \\)). This a consequence of the <strong>Solenoidal Nature</strong> of magnetism (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0 \\)). In the context of <strong>Induction</strong>, this means that a changing flux in one medium must \"leak\" perfectly into the next, providing the 4D linkage for <strong>Circular Loops</strong> in transformer cores.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Skin Effect and Eddy Currents</h3>\n                    <p>At the surface of a conductor, the interface conditions combined with <strong>Ohm\'s Law</strong> create the <strong>Skin Effect</strong>. A high-frequency alternating magnetic field induces <strong>Eddy Currents</strong> that \"Push\" the electric field to the very outer layer of the material. The \"Skin Depth\" (\\( \\delta \\)) is the distance where the field decays by \\( 1/e \\), a critical parameter in high-frequency engineering and <strong>Astrophysical Dynamics</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Perfect Conductors and Shielding</h3>\n                    <p>For an ideal conductor, the internal electric and magnetic fields must be zero. This forces the external magnetic field to be <strong>tangential</strong> to the surface at the interface. Any flux change is canceled by surface currents, creating an \"Electromagnetic Mirror.\" this is principle of the <strong>Faraday Cage</strong>, where the interface condition provides perfect isolation from <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-radiation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromagnetic Radiation</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Relativistic Interface Transformations</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Special Relativity</strong>, the interface conditions must be <strong>Lorentz Invariant</strong>. The jump in the <strong>Electromagnetic Field Tensor</strong> components at the boundary is related to the <strong>4-Current Density</strong> on the surface. This ensures that the \"Shielding\" effect of a material is the same for all observers, anchoring the behavior of matter in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Sharp Boundary Limit</h3>\n                    <p>Technically, no physical boundary is \"infinitesimally sharp\" at the subatomic level. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> treats these interface conditions as an <strong>Epistemic</strong> approximation of the rapid but continuous transition of <strong>Permittivity</strong> and conductivity. However, for all macroscopic applications in <strong>Astrophysical Fluid Dynamics</strong> and circuit design, the \"Interface Jump\" is an exact and necessary law of 4D geometry.</p>','Interface Conditions (or Boundary Conditions) induction describe how the non-conservative electric field and magnetic flux behave at the junction between two materials. In university-level physics, these conditions are derived from the Maxwell-Faraday Law. They dictate how time-varying fields \"refract\" at boundaries and are essential for understanding the Skin Effect and the efficiency of electromagnetic shielding.','Interface Conditions (or Boundary Conditions) induction describe how the non-conservative electric field and magnetic flux behave at the junction between two materials. In university-level physics, these conditions are derived from the Maxwell-Faraday Law. They dictate how time-varying fields \"refract\" at boundaries and are essential for understanding the Skin Effect and the efficiency of electromagnetic shielding.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.8ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"9.932ex\" height=\"2.338ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 4389.8 1033.5\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H757Q764 676 764 669Q764 664 751 557T737 447Q735 440 717 440H705Q698 445 698 453L701 476Q704 500 704 528Q704 558 697 578T678 609T643 625T596 632T532 634H485Q397 633 392 631Q388 629 386 622Q385 619 355 499T324 377Q347 376 372 376H398Q464 376 489 391T534 472Q538 488 540 490T557 493Q562 493 565 493T570 492T572 491T574 487T577 483L544 351Q511 218 508 216Q505 213 492 213Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2225\" d=\"M133 736Q138 750 153 750Q164 750 170 739Q172 735 172 250T170 -239Q164 -250 152 -250Q144 -250 138 -244L137 -243Q133 -241 133 -179T132 250Q132 731 133 736ZM329 739Q334 750 346 750Q353 750 361 744L362 743Q366 741 366 679T367 250T367 -178T362 -243L361 -244Q355 -250 347 -250Q335 -250 329 -239Q327 -235 327 250T329 739Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(771,-176.7) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2225\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2225\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1805.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(2861.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(771,-176.7) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2225\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2225\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"e-field-continuity-de3735c8\",\"b-field-continuity-1379bb7a\",\"surface-squeeze-24304b3e\",\"perfect-shield-8fe5c1fd\"]'),
(2009793,'conservation-of-energy-induction','faradays-law','Conservation of Energy induction','<p><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-mechanical-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Energy</strong></a> is the fundamental law stating that energy can neither be created nor destroyed. In the context of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-induction\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromagnetic Induction</strong></a>, this law is physically manifested as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lenzs-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lenz\'s Law</strong></a>. It ensures that the work done to move a magnet or change a field is perfectly accounted for by the electrical energy generated in the circuit and the <strong>Thermal Energy</strong> dissipated as heat.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Lenz\'s Law as an Energy Guard</h3>\n                    <p>Lenz\'s Law dictates that an induced current always opposes the change that created it. If it assisted the change (a positive sign in the <strong>Flux Rule</strong>), a tiny magnetic pulse would grow exponentially, creating infinite energy from \"nothing.\" This would violate the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamics-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Second Law of Thermodynamics</strong></a>. The \"Opposition\" is the \'s way of enforcing the energy balance of the <strong>Standard Model</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Work-EMF Balance</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/motional-emf\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Motional EMF</strong></a>, the energy is provided by the external mechanical force pushing a conductor. The <strong>Lorentz Force</strong> on the electrons creates a current, which in turn experiences a magnetic \"Back-Force\" (\\( I \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{B} \\)). The mechanical work done against this back-force (\\( P_{mech} = F v \\)) is exactly equal to the electrical power delivered (\\( P_{elec} = \\mathcal{E} I \\)). This proves that induction is a 100% efficient \"Transducer\" of energy types.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Poynting Vector and Energy Flux</h3>\n                    <p>Energy induction travels through the fields, not just the wires. The <strong>Poynting Vector</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{S} = \\mathbf{E} \\times \\mathbf{H} \\)) describes the flow of <strong>Energy Density</strong> from the magnetic source to the electrical sink. In a transformer, energy is not \"Conducted\" through the iron core but flows through the 4D vacuum surrounding it, guided by the magnetic flux geometry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Energy Dissipation: Joule Heating</h3>\n                    <p>In real conductors, some energy is lost to <strong>Resistance</strong> as \\( P = I^2 R \\). This \"Joule Heating\" converts electromagnetic energy into <strong>Thermal Energy</strong> (atomic vibrations). According to the first law of thermodynamics, the total energy (\\( W_{input} = U_{stored} + Q_{heat} \\)) is always conserved, linking subatomic collisions to the 4D <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Magnetic Energy Storage: Inductance</h3>\n                    <p>Induction allows for energy to be stored in the magnetic field itself. A <strong>Solenoid</strong> or inductor has a self-inductance \\( L \\), and its energy is \\( U_B = \\frac{1}{2} L I^2 \\). This energy represents the \"Inertia\" of the current. Changing the current requires doing work against the self-induced <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromotive-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromotive Force (EMF)</strong></a>, proving that the  acts as a reservoir of potential.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Global Balance</h3>\n                    <p>The conservation of energy induction suggests that the  is a closed, \"Zero-Sum\" system. Every watt of power in our electrical grid is a result of a <strong>Physical Causality</strong> chain that respects the 4D geometric constraints of the <strong>Maxwell-Faraday Law</strong>. This anchors <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the belief that the energy budget of the <strong>observable universe</strong> is a fundamental invariant of time.</p>','Conservation of Energy is the fundamental law stating that energy can neither be created nor destroyed. In the context of Electromagnetic Induction, this law is physically manifested as Lenz\'s Law. It ensures that the work done to move a magnet or change a field is perfectly accounted for by the electrical energy generated in the circuit and the Thermal Energy dissipated as heat.','Conservation of Energy is the fundamental law stating that energy can neither be created nor destroyed. In the context of Electromagnetic Induction, this law is physically manifested as Lenz\'s Law. It ensures that the work done to move a magnet or change a field is perfectly accounted for by the electrical energy generated in the circuit and the Thermal Energy dissipated as heat.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.478ex\" height=\"1.57ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 2863.4 694\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43C\" d=\"M43 1Q26 1 26 10Q26 12 29 24Q34 43 39 45Q42 46 54 46H60Q120 46 136 53Q137 53 138 54Q143 56 149 77T198 273Q210 318 216 344Q286 624 286 626Q284 630 284 631Q274 637 213 637H193Q184 643 189 662Q193 677 195 680T209 683H213Q285 681 359 681Q481 681 487 683H497Q504 676 504 672T501 655T494 639Q491 637 471 637Q440 637 407 634Q393 631 388 623Q381 609 337 432Q326 385 315 341Q245 65 245 59Q245 52 255 50T307 46H339Q345 38 345 37T342 19Q338 6 332 0H316Q279 2 179 2Q143 2 113 2T65 2T43 1Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D425\" d=\"M43 686L134 690Q225 694 226 694H232V62H301V0H292Q274 3 170 3Q67 3 49 0H40V62H109V332Q109 387 109 453T110 534Q110 593 108 605T94 620Q80 624 53 624H40V686H43Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\" d=\"M630 29Q630 9 609 9Q604 9 587 25T493 118L389 222L284 117Q178 13 175 11Q171 9 168 9Q160 9 154 15T147 29Q147 36 161 51T255 146L359 250L255 354Q174 435 161 449T147 471Q147 480 153 485T168 490Q173 490 175 489Q178 487 284 383L389 278L493 382Q570 459 587 475T609 491Q630 491 630 471Q630 464 620 453T522 355L418 250L522 145Q606 61 618 48T630 29Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\" d=\"M720 510Q720 476 704 448T665 404T619 377T580 362L564 359L583 356Q602 353 632 342T690 312Q712 292 725 276Q752 235 752 189V183Q752 160 741 125Q698 18 547 2Q543 1 288 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H264H409Q502 686 542 681T624 655Q720 607 720 510ZM563 513Q563 553 548 578T518 611T486 622Q479 624 385 624H293V382H375Q458 383 467 385Q563 405 563 513ZM590 192Q590 307 505 329Q504 330 503 330L398 331H293V62H391H400H444Q496 62 528 75T580 131Q590 155 590 192Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(504,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D425\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D425\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1045.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(2045.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D401\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"stability-rule-ae866695\",\"mechanical-to-electric-21c65467\",\"inductance-553147dd\",\"poynting-s-theorem-42e9b75f\"]');
INSERT INTO `subtopics` VALUES
(2009794,'gauge-transformation','gauge-invariance','Gauge Transformation','<p>A Gauge Transformation is a local redefinition of the <strong>Electromagnetic 4-Potential</strong> that leaves the physical <strong>Field Strength Tensor</strong> unchanged. In university-level physics, gauge transformations represent a <strong>Symmetry</strong> of the , showing that the underlying potential landscape has redundant \"Degrees of Freedom\" that do not affect the observable <strong>Physical Causality</strong> of the universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Defining the Local Shift</h3>\n                    <p>For the electromagnetic potential \\( A_\\mu \\), a gauge transformation is defined by:\n                    \\[ A_\\mu \\to A_\\mu + \\partial_\\mu \\Lambda \\]\n                    Where \\( \\Lambda(x) \\) is any smooth scalar function of spacetime. Because the <strong>Field Strength Tensor</strong> is the difference of derivatives (\\( \\partial_\\mu A_\\nu - \\dots \\)), the second derivatives of \\( \\Lambda \\) cancel out perfectly. This proves that there are infinitely many \"potential maps\" that describe the same physical E and B fields.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Invariance</strong></a> and Observables</h3>\n                    <p>Any quantity that remains unchanged under a gauge transformation is \"Gauge Invariant.\" In the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, only gauge-invariant quantities (like energy, charge, and the field tensor) are considered <strong>Ontologically</strong> real. This <strong>Epistemic</strong> limit ensures that our physics is not dependent on the arbitrary way we choose to label the phase of the subatomic 4D vacuum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Fixing the Gauge: Lorenz and Coulomb</h3>\n                    <p>To simplify the <strong>Equations of Motion</strong>, physicists \"Fix the Gauge\" by imposing a constraint on the potential.\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Lorenz Gauge:</strong> \\( \\partial_\\mu A^\\mu = 0 \\)—Preserves <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/manifest-covariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Manifest Covariance</strong></a> and leads to the wave equation.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Coulomb Gauge:</strong> \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = 0 \\)—Used in non-relativistic <strong>Quantum Physics</strong> to isolate the static interaction.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    The choice of gauge is a mathematical convenience, not a physical change, similar to choosing a coordinate system in 3D space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Gauge Transformation in Quantum Mechanics</h3>\n                    <p>In the <strong>Born Interpretation</strong>, a gauge transformation of the potential must be accompanied by a local phase shift of the <strong>Wave Function</strong> (\\( \\Psi \\)):\n                    \\[ \\Psi \\to e^{iq\\Lambda/\\hbar} \\Psi \\]\n                    This reveals that the electromagnetic potential is the \"Connection\" that keeps the quantum phase consistent across the . This link is the origin of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/aharonov-bohm-effect\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Aharonov-Bohm Effect</strong></a>, where the choice of gauge path affects the particle\'s interference pattern.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Geometric Origin: Fiber Bundles</h3>\n                    <p>In advanced <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>, a gauge transformation is interpreted as a \"Rotation\" within the internal fiber of a fiber bundle over spacetime. The 4-potential acts as a map for parallel-transporting the quantum state. The fact that the physical force (curvature) is invariant under these rotations is the defining characteristic of a <strong>Gauge Theory</strong>, linking 4D geometry to subatomic symmetry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Redundancy and the Standard Model</h3>\n                    <p>Gauge transformations are not just \"tricks\"; they are required for the mathematical consistency of <strong>Relativistic Quantum Field Theory</strong>. They ensure that the <strong>Gauge Bosons</strong> (photons) only have two physical polarizations, despite being described by a 4-component vector. This redundancy is what makes the <strong>Standard Model</strong> renormalizable, providing the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stability-threshold\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Stability Threshold</strong></a> for our understanding of the universe.</p>','A Gauge Transformation is a local redefinition of the Electromagnetic 4-Potential that leaves the physical Field Strength Tensor unchanged. In university-level physics, gauge transformations represent a Symmetry of the, showing that the underlying potential landscape has redundant \"Degrees of Freedom\" that do not affect the observable Physical Causality of the universe. For the electromagnetic potential, a gauge transformation is defined by: Where is any smooth scalar function of spacetime.','A Gauge Transformation is a local redefinition of the Electromagnetic 4-Potential that leaves the physical Field Strength Tensor unchanged. In university-level physics, gauge transformations represent a Symmetry of the , showing that the underlying potential landscape has redundant \"Degrees of Freedom\" that do not affect the observable Physical Causality of the universe. For the electromagnetic potential <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.849ex\" height=\"2.305ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1259.4 1018.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\" d=\"M208 74Q208 50 254 46Q272 46 272 35Q272 34 270 22Q267 8 264 4T251 0Q249 0 239 0T205 1T141 2Q70 2 50 0H42Q35 7 35 11Q37 38 48 46H62Q132 49 164 96Q170 102 345 401T523 704Q530 716 547 716H555H572Q578 707 578 706L606 383Q634 60 636 57Q641 46 701 46Q726 46 726 36Q726 34 723 22Q720 7 718 4T704 0Q701 0 690 0T651 1T578 2Q484 2 455 0H443Q437 6 437 9T439 27Q443 40 445 43L449 46H469Q523 49 533 63L521 213H283L249 155Q208 86 208 74ZM516 260Q516 271 504 416T490 562L463 519Q447 492 400 412L310 260L413 259Q516 259 516 260Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D434\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(783,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>, a gauge transformation is defined by:\n                    <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.908ex\" height=\"2.305ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 7031.2 1018.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\" d=\"M208 74Q208 50 254 46Q272 46 272 35Q272 34 270 22Q267 8 264 4T251 0Q249 0 239 0T205 1T141 2Q70 2 50 0H42Q35 7 35 11Q37 38 48 46H62Q132 49 164 96Q170 102 345 401T523 704Q530 716 547 716H555H572Q578 707 578 706L606 383Q634 60 636 57Q641 46 701 46Q726 46 726 36Q726 34 723 22Q720 7 718