485.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(438.3,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(650.8,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1054.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D467\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D467\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1031,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1597,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D466\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D466\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"2287\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7012.4,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(12006.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D422\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D422\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(159.5,269) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"5E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(12547.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(13547.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"2026\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2026\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"curl-free-rule-9c2a2687\",\"clairaut-link-0001a7d9\",\"conservative-link-a1d89e28\",\"induction-limit-6e5298a5\"]'),
(2009843,'lagrangian-lorentz-force','lorentz-force','Lagrangian Formalism of the Lorentz Force','<p>The <strong>Lagrangian Formalism</strong> provides the most profound theoretical basis for the <strong>Lorentz Force</strong>. Unlike simple mechanical systems where the potential depends only on position, the electromagnetic interaction requires a <strong>Generalized Potential</strong> that depends on the particle\'s velocity. This formulation is the gateway to <strong>Quantum Electrodynamics</strong> and the principle of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Invariance</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Velocity-Dependent Potential</h3>\n                  <p>In standard Newtonian mechanics, we assume a conservative force can be derived from a scalar potential \\( V(\\mathbf{r}) \\). However, the magnetic part of the Lorentz force (\\( q\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B} \\)) is velocity-dependent and always perpendicular to motion. To incorporate this into the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a>, we define a generalized potential function \\( U(\\mathbf{r}, \\mathbf{v}, t) \\) such that:\n                  \\[ U = q\\Phi(\\mathbf{r}, t) - q\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}(\\mathbf{r}, t) \\]\n                  where \\( \\Phi \\) is the <strong>Scalar Potential</strong> and \\( \\mathbf{A} \\) is the <strong>Magnetic Vector Potential</strong>. This interaction term \\( q\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} \\) is the mathematical signature of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/minimal-coupling\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Minimal Coupling</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. The Particle Lagrangian</h3>\n                  <p>The Lagrangian for a charged particle in an electromagnetic field is defined as the difference between kinetic energy (\\( T \\)) and the generalized potential (\\( U \\)):\n                  \\[ L = T - U = \\frac{1}{2}m\\mathbf{v}^2 - q\\Phi + q\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} \\]\n                  In this form, the Lagrangian is no longer just \"Energy,\" but a functional that encodes the entire interaction geometry. Note that the sign of the vector potential term is positive because it is subtracted (\\( -(-q\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}) \\)).</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Canonical Momentum and Minimal Coupling</h3>\n                  <p>A crucial consequence of this Lagrangian is the definition of momentum. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/canonical-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Canonical Momentum</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{p} \\)), defined as the partial derivative of \\( L \\) with respect to velocity, is:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{p} = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial \\mathbf{v}} = m\\mathbf{v} + q\\mathbf{A} \\]\n                  This reveals that the \"momentum\" tracked by the Hamiltonian is not just the <strong>Kinetic Momentum</strong> (\\( m\\mathbf{v} \\)), but also includes a contribution from the electromagnetic field itself. This shifting of momentum is essential for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> formulation: \\( H = \\frac{1}{2m}(\\mathbf{p} - q\\mathbf{A})^2 + q\\Phi \\).</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Deriving the Lorentz Force</h3>\n                  <p>Applying the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/euler-lagrange-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Euler-Lagrange Equations</strong></a> (\\( \\frac{d}{dt}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial \\dot{x}_i} = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial x_i} \\)) to our Lagrangian yields the equations of motion. The left side gives the rate of change of canonical momentum, while the right side involves gradients of the potentials. Using the vector identity for the gradient of a dot product:\n                  \\[ m\\mathbf{a} = q\\left( -\\nabla \\Phi - \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{A}}{\\partial t} + \\mathbf{v} \\times (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{A}) \\right) \\]\n                  Recognizing the definitions of the fields (\\( \\mathbf{E} = -\\nabla \\Phi - \\partial \\mathbf{A}/\\partial t \\) and \\( \\mathbf{B} = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{A} \\)), we recover the classic <strong>Lorentz Force Law</strong>: \\( \\mathbf{F} = q(\\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}) \\).</p>\n\n                  <h3>5. Gauge Invariance of the Action</h3>\n                  <p>The potentials \\( \\Phi \\) and \\( \\mathbf{A} \\) are not unique; they can be transformed via a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-transformation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Transformation</strong></a>: \\( \\mathbf{A} \\to \\mathbf{A} + \\nabla \\chi \\) and \\( \\Phi \\to \\Phi - \\partial \\chi / \\partial t \\). Under such a change, the Lagrangian transforms as \\( L \\to L + q\\frac{d\\chi}{dt} \\). Since the Lagrangian changes only by a <strong>Total Time Derivative</strong>, the <strong>Action</strong> (\\( S = \\int L dt \\)) remains stationary for the same paths, ensuring that the physics is independent of the choice of gauge—a fundamental principle of modern <strong>Field Theory</strong>.</p>','The Lagrangian Formalism provides the most profound theoretical basis for the Lorentz Force. Unlike simple mechanical systems where the potential depends only on position, the electromagnetic interaction requires a Generalized Potential that depends on the particle\'s velocity. This formulation is the gateway to Quantum Electrodynamics and the principle of Gauge Invariance.','The Lagrangian Formalism provides the most profound theoretical basis for the Lorentz Force. Unlike simple mechanical systems where the potential depends only on position, the electromagnetic interaction requires a Generalized Potential that depends on the particle\'s velocity. This formulation is the gateway to Quantum Electrodynamics and the principle of Gauge Invariance.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg