363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(518,-289.3) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"22A5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22A5\"></use></g></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1235.3,-686)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><rect width=\"2989.7\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5444,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(6499.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\" transform=\"translate(720.4,749.3)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(536,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(536,-289.3) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"22A5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22A5\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1378,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"2337\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"escape-region-52eb4ca0\",\"action-invariance-b5329a3a\"]'),
(2009848,'magnetic-moment','lorentz-force','Magnetic Moment and Dipole Dynamics','<p>The <strong>Magnetic Moment</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{m} \\)) is the fundamental quantity that describes the strength and orientation of a magnetic source. For a charged particle moving in a loop, the magnetic moment represents its \"Magnetic Identity,\" determining how it interacts with external fields through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/torque\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Torque</strong></a> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/potential-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Potential Energy</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Classical Definition: The Current Loop</h3>\n                  <p>Classically, the magnetic moment of a planar current loop is the product of the current (\\( I \\)) and the vector area (\\( \\mathbf{A} \\)):\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{m} = I\\mathbf{A} \\]\n                  For a single particle of charge \\( q \\) in a circular orbit, this relates directly to its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/orbital-angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Orbital Angular Momentum</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{L} \\)) via the <strong>Gyromagnetic Ratio</strong>:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{m} = \\frac{q}{2m}\\mathbf{L} \\]\n                  This link proves that all orbital magnetism is a direct consequence of the transport of charge in the .</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Interaction with External Fields</h3>\n                  <p>A magnetic moment in an external field \\( \\mathbf{B} \\) experiences two primary effects:\n                  <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Torque:</strong> \\( \\boldsymbol{\\tau} = \\mathbf{m} \\times \\mathbf{B} \\), which attempts to align the moment with the field lines.</li>\n                    <li><strong>Potential Energy:</strong> \\( U = -\\mathbf{m} \\cdot \\mathbf{B} \\), which is minimized when the moment is parallel to the field.</li>\n                  </ul>\n                  This energy landscape is what drives the alignment of spins in <strong>Ferromagnetism</strong> and the precession of nuclei in <strong>MRI</strong> scans.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Intrinsic Spin and Quantum Reality</h3>\n                  <p>In <strong>Quantum Mechanics</strong>, particles possess an intrinsic magnetic moment due to <strong>Spin</strong>. For an electron, the moment is \\( \\mathbf{m}_s = -g_e \\frac{e}{2m} \\mathbf{S} \\), where \\( g_e \\approx 2 \\) is the electron g-factor. The small correction from 2 (the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/anomalous-magnetic-moment\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Anomalous Magnetic Moment</strong></a>) is a triumph of <strong>Quantum Electrodynamics</strong>, matching experiment to 12 decimal places and anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the precision of the vacuum.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. The Magnetic Moment as an Invariant</h3>\n                  <p>In plasma physics, the magnitude of the orbital magnetic moment \\( \\mu = mv_{\\perp}^2 / 2B \\) is the first <strong>Adiabatic Invariant</strong>. This connects the subatomic property of the particle to the global <strong>Geometric Constraints</strong> of the field, allowing for the stable trapping of charges in \"magnetic bottles\" and planetary fields.</p>','The Magnetic Moment is the fundamental quantity that describes the strength and orientation of a magnetic source. For a charged particle moving in a loop, the magnetic moment represents its \"Magnetic Identity,\" determining how it interacts with external fields through Torque and Potential Energy. Classically, the magnetic moment of a planar current loop is the product of the current and the vector area : For a single particle of charge in a circular orbit, this relates directly to its Orbital Angular Momentum via the Gyromagnetic Ratio: This link proves that all orbital magnetism is a direct consequence of the transport of charge in the.','The Magnetic Moment (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.167ex\" height=\"1.018ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -450 958 450\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D426\" d=\"M40 442Q217 450 218 450H224V365Q226 367 235 378T254 397T278 416T314 435T362 448Q376 450 400 450H406Q503 450 534 393Q545 376 545 370Q545 368 555 379Q611 450 716 450Q774 450 809 434Q850 414 861 379T873 276V213V198V62H942V0H933Q915 3 809 3Q702 3 684 0H675V62H744V194V275Q744 348 735 373T690 399Q645 399 607 370T557 290Q555 281 554 171V62H623V0H614Q596 3 489 3Q374 3 365 0H356V62H425V194V275Q425 348 416 373T371 399Q326 399 288 370T238 290Q236 281 235 171V62H304V0H295Q277 3 171 3Q64 3 46 0H37V62H106V210V303Q106 353 104 363T91 376Q77 380 50 380H37V442H40Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D426\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D426\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is the fundamental quantity that describes the strength and orientation of a magnetic source. For a charged particle moving in a loop, the magnetic moment represents its \"Magnetic Identity,\" determining how it interacts with external fields through Torque and Potential Energy. Classically, the magnetic moment of a planar current loop is the product of the current (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.14ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 504 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43C\" d=\"M43 1Q26 1 26 10Q26 12 29 24Q34 43 39 45Q42 46 54 46H60Q120 46 136 53Q137 53 138 54Q143 56 149 77T198 273Q210 318 216 344Q286 624 286 626Q284 630 284 631Q274 637 213 637H193Q184 643 189 662Q193 677 