 transform=\"translate(1357.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2413.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3191.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(3794.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(866,-176.7) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2225\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2225\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5063.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"mirror-force-4783d415\",\"escape-region-52eb4ca0\"]'),
(2009850,'bound-currents','magnetic-matter','Bound Currents and Magnetization','<p><strong>Bound Currents</strong> are the macroscopic representation of the microscopic atomic current loops in a magnetized material. In university-level electrodynamics, these currents are the mathematical source of the magnetic field generated by the material itself, allowing us to treat magnetized matter as an equivalent distribution of vacuum currents.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Volume Bound Currents (\\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\))</h3>\n                  <p>In a region where the <strong>Magnetization (\\(\\mathbf{M}\\))</strong> is non-uniform, the internal atomic loops do not perfectly cancel each other out. The resulting net current density is given by the curl of the magnetization:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{J}_b = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M} \\]\n                  This \"circulation\" of magnetization effectively creates a macroscopic flow of charge, even though individual electrons remain bound to their respective atoms.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Surface Bound Currents (\\(\\mathbf{K}_b\\))</h3>\n                  <p>At the boundary of a magnetized material, the cancellation of internal loops is incomplete because there are no loops outside to balance them. This results in a <strong>Surface Current Density</strong>:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{K}_b = \\mathbf{M} \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\]\n                  where \\(\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}\\) is the unit normal to the surface. This surface sheet of current is responsible for the exterior magnetic field of permanent magnets, acting like the windings of a solenoid.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Physical Origin: The Bohr Atom</h3>\n                  <p>Microscopically, these currents represent the <strong>Orbital Motion</strong> of electrons. By treating every atom as a tiny Ampèrian loop, we can bridge the gap between quantum mechanics and classical field theory. Bound currents prove that the magnetic properties of bulk matter are a collective, emergent property of the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of atomic orbits.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Integration with Maxwell\'s Equations</h3>\n                  <p>The total magnetic field \\(\\mathbf{B}\\) is generated by the sum of <strong>Free Currents</strong> (\\(\\mathbf{J}_f\\)) and bound currents. This lead to the introduction of the auxiliary field \\(\\mathbf{H}\\), which streamlines the calculation of fields in matter by sequestering the complex bound current distributions into the definition of the medium\'s properties.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Bound Currents and Magnetization is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bound-volume-current\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Bound Volume Current ()</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bound-surface-current\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Bound Surface Current ()</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','Bound Currents are the macroscopic representation of the microscopic atomic current loops in a magnetized material. In university-level electrodynamics, these currents are the mathematical source of the magnetic field generated by the material itself, allowing us to treat magnetized matter as an equivalent distribution of vacuum currents. In a region where the Magnetization is non-uniform, the internal atomic loops do not perfectly cancel each other out.','Bound Currents are the macroscopic representation of the microscopic atomic current loops in a magnetized material. In university-level electrodynamics, these currents are the mathematical source of the magnetic field generated by the material itself, allowing us to treat magnetized matter as an equivalent distribution of vacuum currents. In a region where the Magnetization (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.471ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 1092 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\" d=\"M314 0Q296 3 181 3T48 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H305Q316 679 323 667Q330 653 434 414L546 157L658 414Q766 662 773 674Q778 681 788 686H1052V624H944V62H1052V0H1040Q1016 3 874 3T708 0H696V62H804V341L803 618L786 580Q770 543 735 462T671 315Q540 13 536 9Q528 1 507 1Q485 1 477 9Q472 14 408 162T281 457T217 603Q215 603 215 334V62H323V0H314Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is non-uniform, the internal atomic loops do not perfectly cancel each other out.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.356ex\" height=\"1.909ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 5461.3 843.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\" d=\"M174 114Q174 96 169 82T159 63T144 47L155 45Q183 40 203 40Q271 40 290 104Q294 118 294 150T295 380V624H154V686H169Q196 683 365 683Q499 683 517 686H527V624H446V379Q446 183 446 153T441 108Q413 32 315 2Q266 -11 208 -11Q160 -11 118 -2T42 37T8 114V122Q8 150 30 174T91 198T152 174T174 122V114Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\" d=\"M73 647Q73 657 77 670T89 683Q90 683 161 688T234 694Q246 694 246 685T212 542Q204 508 195 472T180 418L176 399Q176 396 182 402Q231 442 283 442Q345 442 383 396T422 280Q422 169 343 79T173 -11Q123 -11 82 27T40 150V159Q40 180 48 217T97 414Q147 611 147 623T109 637Q104 637 101 637H96Q86 637 83 637T76 640T73 647ZM336 325V331Q336 405 275 405Q258 405 240 397T207 376T181 352T163 330L157 322L136 236Q114 150 114 114Q114 66 138 42Q154 26 178 26Q211 26 245 58Q270 81 285 114T318 219Q336 291 336 325Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\" d=\"M630 29Q630 9 609 9Q604 9 587 25T493 118L389 222L284 117Q178 13 175 11Q171 9 168 9Q160 9 154 15T147 29Q147 36 161 51T255 146L359 250L255 354Q174 435 161 449T147 471Q147 480 153 485T168 490Q173 490 175 489Q178 487 284 383L389 278L493 382Q570 459 587 475T609 491Q630 491 630 471Q630 464 620 453T522 355L418 250L522 145Q606 61 618 48T630 29Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\" d=\"M314 0Q296 3 181 3T48 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H305Q316 679 323 667Q330 653 434 414L546 157L658 414Q766 662 773 674Q778 681 788 686H1052V624H944V62H1052V0H1040Q1016 3 874 3T708 0H696V62H804V341L803 618L786 580Q770 543 735 462T671 315Q540 13 536 9Q528 1 507 1Q485 1 477 9Q472 14 408 162T281 457T217 603Q215 603 215 334V62H323V0H314Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D409\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(627,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1258.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2313.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3369.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4369.3,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"volume-bound-current-5986e0ce\",\"surface-bound-current-3b15de46\"]'),
(2009851,'magnetic-susceptibility','magnetic-matter','Magnetic Susceptibility and Linear Media','<p><strong>Magnetic Susceptibility (\\(\\chi_m\\))</strong> is the dimensionless quantity that measures the degree to which a material becomes magnetized in response to an applied magnetic field. In university-level physics, it defines the <strong>Linear Response</strong> of a medium, providing the classification system for the fundamental types of magnetic matter.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Linear Constitutive Relation</h3>\n                  <p>For many materials, especially at low field strengths, the <strong>Magnetization (\\(\\mathbf{M}\\))</strong> is directly proportional to the auxiliary field <strong>Magnetic Intensity (\\(\\mathbf{H}\\))</strong>:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{M} = \\chi_m \\mathbf{H} \\]\n                  This proportionality constant \\(\\chi_m\\) captures the internal \"stiffness\" or \"flexibility\" of the material\'s magnetic dipoles. In this regime, the total induction \\(\\mathbf{B}\\) is also linear: \\(\\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0(1 + \\chi_m)\\mathbf{H} = \\mu \\mathbf{H}\\).</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Physical Interpretation of \\(\\chi_m\\)</h3>\n                  <p>The sign and magnitude of \\(\\chi_m\\) reveal the underlying quantum mechanics:\n                  <ul>\n                    <li>If \\(\\chi_m \\lt 0\\), the material is <strong>Diamagnetic</strong>, meaning it creates an internal field that opposes the external one.</li>\n                    <li>If \\(\\chi_m \\gt 0\\), the material is <strong>Paramagnetic</strong>, meaning it strengthens the external field through dipole alignment.</li>\n                  </ul>\n                  The value of susceptibility is a critical parameter in <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong>, where it is calculated as the second derivative of the free energy with respect to the field.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Tensor Susceptibility</h3>\n                  <p>In anisotropic materials (like certain crystals), \\(\\chi_m\\) is not a single number but a <strong>Rank-2 Tensor</strong>. This means the magnetization vector does not necessarily point in the same direction as the \\(\\mathbf{H}\\) field. This directional dependence is essential for understanding <strong>Birefringence</strong> and the behavior of advanced magnetic recording media.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Limits of Linearity</h3>\n                  <p>Magnetic susceptibility is often a function of temperature and field strength. In <strong>Ferromagnets</strong>, the linear relationship breaks down entirely due to spontaneous alignment and hysteresis. Understanding the limits of \\(\\chi_m\\) is the key to transitioning from simple linear models to the complex, non-linear dynamics of permanent magnets and superconductors.</p>','Magnetic Susceptibility is the dimensionless quantity that measures the degree to which a material becomes magnetized in response to an applied magnetic field. In university-level physics, it defines the Linear Response of a medium, providing the classification system for the fundamental types of magnetic matter. For many materials, especially at low field strengths, the Magnetization is directly proportional to the auxiliary field Magnetic Intensity : This proportionality constant captures the internal \"stiffness\" or \"flexibility\" of the material\'s magnetic dipoles.','Magnetic Susceptibility (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.462ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.009ex\" height=\"1.462ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1329.8 646\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D712\" d=\"M576 -125Q576 -147 547 -175T487 -204H476Q394 -204 363 -157Q334 -114 293 26L284 59Q283 58 248 19T170 -66T92 -151T53 -191Q49 -194 43 -194Q36 -194 31 -189T25 -177T38 -154T151 -30L272 102L265 131Q189 405 135 405Q104 405 87 358Q86 351 68 351Q48 351 48 361Q48 369 56 386T89 423T148 442Q22