lor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1369.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(2425.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D712\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D712\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(659,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3755.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D407\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"magnetic-susceptibility-06839f82\",\"permeability-892ad545\"]'),
(2009852,'diamagnetism','magnetic-matter','Diamagnetism: The Universal Response','<p><strong>Diamagnetism</strong> is a fundamental quantum mechanical property of all matter characterized by a weak, negative response to an external magnetic field. In university-level electrodynamics, it is described as the atomic-scale manifestation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lenzs-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lenz\'s Law</strong></a>, where induced currents always oppose the change that created them.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Negative Susceptibility</h3>\n                  <p>A diamagnetic material has a susceptibility \\(\\chi_m \\lt 0\\). When placed in a magnetic field, it develops a magnetization \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) in the <strong>opposite direction</strong> of \\(\\mathbf{B}\\). This causes the material to be slightly repelled from regions of high field strength. Because it arises from the universal behavior of orbiting electrons, diamagnetism is present in all materials, though it is often masked by stronger magnetic effects.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Classical Mechanism: Larmor Precession</h3>\n                  <p>Classically, diamagnetism can be understood by considering the change in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/orbital-angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Orbital Angular Momentum</strong></a> of an electron. An external field exerts a torque that causes the electron\'s orbit to precess (<strong>Larmor Precession</strong>). This precession generates an additional magnetic moment that, according to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a>, must oppose the applied field. This a purely kinetic effect that does not require permanent dipoles.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/superconductivity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Superconductivity</strong></a>: Perfect Diamagnetism</h3>\n                  <p>The most extreme case of diamagnetism is found in <strong>Superconductors</strong>. Through the <strong>Meissner Effect</strong>, a superconductor expels all magnetic flux from its interior, acting as a \"Perfect Diamagnet\" with \\(\\chi_m = -1\\). This not just induced current but a macroscopic quantum phase transition that proves the field-expulsion property is an <strong>Ontological</strong> feature of the superconducting state.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Landau Diamagnetism</h3>\n                  <p>In a formal quantum treatment, diamagnetism is explained by the quantization of electron orbits into <strong>Landau Levels</strong>. This <strong>Landau Diamagnetism</strong> is essential for understanding the magnetic properties of metals and the <strong>Quantum Hall Effect</strong>. It demonstrates that the macroscopic rejection of magnetic fields is rooted in the discrete, non-classical structure of the 4D <strong>Phase Space</strong>.</p>','Diamagnetism is a fundamental quantum mechanical property of all matter characterized by a weak, negative response to an external magnetic field. In university-level electrodynamics, it is described as the atomic-scale manifestation of Lenz\'s Law, where induced currents always oppose the change that created them. A diamagnetic material has a susceptibility.','Diamagnetism is a fundamental quantum mechanical property of all matter characterized by a weak, negative response to an external magnetic field. In university-level electrodynamics, it is described as the atomic-scale manifestation of Lenz\'s Law, where induced currents always oppose the change that created them. A diamagnetic material has a susceptibility <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.462ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.157ex\" height=\"1.968ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -666 3163.4 870\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D712\" d=\"M576 -125Q576 -147 547 -175T487 -204H476Q394 -204 363 -157Q334 -114 293 26