,'[]','[]','[\"domain-wall-width-2ada1bb5\",\"heisenberg-exchange-hamiltonian-54392c5a\",\"total-magnetic-free-energy-99e4b5fe\"]'),
(2009858,'magnetic-hysteresis','ferromagnetism','Magnetic Hysteresis and Memory','<p><strong>Magnetic Hysteresis</strong> is the phenomenon where the state of a ferromagnetic material depends on its magnetic history. In university-level physics, this represented by the <strong>B-H Curve</strong>, a non-linear loop that reveals how energy is dissipated through the movement of <strong>Magnetic Domains</strong> and the \"pinning\" of domain walls.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Hysteresis Loop</h3>\n                  <p>As the auxiliary field \\(\\mathbf{H}\\) is cycled, the induction \\(\\mathbf{B}\\) follows a closed path. Key points on this loop include:\n                  <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Saturation (\\(B_s\\)):</strong> The point where all domains are aligned and \\(B\\) no longer increases significantly.</li>\n                    <li><strong>Retentivity (\\(B_r\\)):</strong> The residual induction remaining when the external field is removed (the basis for permanent magnets).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Coercivity (\\(H_c\\)):</strong> The reverse field required to reduce the induction back to zero.</li>\n                  </ul></p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Energy Dissipation</h3>\n                  <p>The area enclosed by the hysteresis loop is proportional to the energy lost as heat during one cycle. This loss occurs because the movement of domain walls is hindered by crystal defects and impurities (pinning sites). Overcoming these barriers requires work, which is converted into thermal energy. Minimizing this area is critical for the efficiency of <strong>Transformers</strong> and electric motors.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Soft vs. Hard Magnets</h3>\n                  <p>Materials are classified by the \"Fatness\" of their loops:\n                  <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Soft Magnets:</strong> Narrow loops with low coercivity (e.g., iron), used where easy reversal is needed.</li>\n                    <li><strong>Hard Magnets:</strong> Wide loops with high coercivity (e.g., Alnico), used for stable <strong>Permanent Magnets</strong>.</li>\n                  </ul>\n                  This distinction is an <strong>Ontological</strong> property of the material\'s microscopic structure and its interaction with the .</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Application: Magnetic Memory</h3>\n                  <p>Hysteresis the physical basis for all non-volatile <strong>Magnetic Memory</strong> (HDD, MRAM). The two stable states of remanence (+B_r and -B_r) correspond to binary 1 and 0. The irreversibility of the path ensures that the information is preserved even without power, anchoring our digital world in the non-linear dynamics of magnetic domains.</p>','Magnetic Hysteresis is the phenomenon where the state of a ferromagnetic material depends on its magnetic history. In university-level physics, this represented by the B-H Curve, a non-linear loop that reveals how energy is dissipated through the movement of Magnetic Domains and the \"pinning\" of domain walls. As the auxiliary field is cycled, the induction follows a closed path.','Magnetic Hysteresis is the phenomenon where the state of a ferromagnetic material depends on its magnetic history. In university-level physics, this represented by the B-H Curve, a non-linear loop that reveals how energy is dissipated through the movement of Magnetic Domains and the \"pinning\" of domain walls. As the auxiliary field <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.036ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 900 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\" d=\"M400 0Q376 3 226 3Q75 3 51 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 226 683Q376 683 400 686H412V624H304V388H595V624H487V686H499Q523 683 673 683Q824 683 848 686H860V624H752V62H860V0H848Q824 3 674 3Q523 3 499 0H487V62H595V326H304V62H412V0H400Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D407\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg> is cycled, the induction <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.851ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 818 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\" d=\"M720 510Q720 476 704 448T665 404T619 377T580 362L564 359L583 356Q602 353 632 342T690 312Q712 292 725 276Q752 235 752 189V183Q752 160 741 125Q698 18 547 2Q543 1 288 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H264H409Q502 686 542 681T624 655Q720 607 720 510ZM563 513Q563 553 548 578T518 611T486 622Q479 624 385 624H293V382H375Q458 383 467 385Q563 405 563 513ZM590 192Q590 307 505 329Q504 330 503 330L398 331H293V62H391H400H444Q496 62 528 75T580 131Q590 155 590 192Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D401\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg> follows a closed path.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.036ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 900 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\" d=\"M400 0Q376 3 226 3Q75 3 51 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 226 683Q376 683 400 686H412V624H304V388H595V624H487V686H499Q523 683 673 683Q824 683 848 686H860V624H752V62H860V0H848Q824 3 674 3Q523 3 499 0H487V62H595V326H304V62H412V0H400Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D407\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"magnetic-hardness-a56d10cd\"]'),
(2009859,'intrinsic-spin','magnetic-matter','Intrinsic Spin and Quantum Magnetism','<p><strong>Intrinsic Spin</strong> is a fundamental form of angular momentum carried by subatomic particles. Unlike classical spin, which arises from physical rotation, intrinsic spin is an \"Internal\" property of the , quantized in units of \\( \\hbar/2 \\). In university-level physics, spin is the defining property that categorizes matter into <strong>Fermions</strong> (half-integer spin) and <strong>Bosons</strong> (integer spin), dictating the <strong>Statistical Distribution</strong> of all systems.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Stern-Gerlach Discovery</h3>\n                  <p>The existence of intrinsic spin was first demonstrated by the <strong>Stern-Gerlach Experiment</strong>, where a beam of silver atoms was split into two discrete paths by an inhomogeneous magnetic field. This showed that the magnetic moment of the electron is <strong>quantized</strong> into two states (Up and Down). It proved that spin is not a continuous vector but a <strong>Discrete</strong> 4D variable, shattering the <strong>Classical Mechanics</strong> view of the atom.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Spin and the Lorentz Group</h3>\n                  <p>In <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>, spin arises from the representation of the <strong>Lorentz Group</strong>. Just as position is related to translations and momentum to motion, spin is the property that determines how a particle behaves under 4D rotations. <strong>Fermions</strong> possess a spin-1/2 representation, which requires a rotation of 720 degrees to return to the original state—a topological \"Twist\" inherent to the 4D vacuum.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The Spin-Statistics Theorem</h3>\n                  <p>A fundamental law of <strong>Relativistic Quantum Field Theory</strong> is the <strong>Spin-Statistics Theorem</strong>. It proves that particles with half-integer spin must obey <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fermi-dirac-statistics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fermi-Dirac Statistics</strong></a> and the <strong>Pauli Exclusion Principle</strong>. This connection ensures that the  remains stable; without this link, the <strong>Exclusion Principle</strong> would not hold, and matter would collapse into a zero-volume state.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. The g-Factor and QED</h3>\n                  <p>Intrinsic spin generates a <strong>Magnetic Dipole</strong>. The relationship between the spin and the magnetic moment is the <strong>g-Factor</strong>. For a Dirac fermion, the g-factor is predicted to be exactly 2. Small deviations from this value (the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/anomalous-magnetic-moment\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Anomalous Magnetic Moment</strong></a>) are predicted by <strong>Quantum Electrodynamics</strong> and provide the most precise <strong>Precision Tests</strong> of the <strong>Standard Model</strong> in history.</p>','Intrinsic Spin is a fundamental form of angular momentum carried by subatomic particles. Unlike classical spin, which arises from physical rotation, intrinsic spin is an \"Internal\" property of the, quantized in units of. In university-level physics, spin is the defining property that categorizes matter into Fermions (half-integer spin) and Bosons (integer spin), dictating the Statistical Distribution of all systems.','Intrinsic Spin is a fundamental form of angular momentum carried by subatomic particles. Unlike classical spin, which arises from physical rotation, intrinsic spin is an \"Internal\" property of the , quantized in units of <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.534ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1562 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\" d=\"M182 599Q182 611 174 615T133 619Q118 619 114 621T109 630Q109 636 114 656T122 681Q125 685 202 688Q272 695 286 695Q304 695 