ating the Statistical Distribution of all systems.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.534ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1562 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\" d=\"M182 599Q182 611 174 615T133 619Q118 619 114 621T109 630Q109 636 114 656T122 681Q125 685 202 688Q272 695 286 695Q304 695 304 684Q304 682 295 644T282 597Q282 592 360 592H399Q430 592 445 587T460 563Q460 552 451 541L442 535H266L251 468Q247 453 243 436T236 409T233 399Q233 395 244 404Q295 441 357 441Q405 441 445 417T485 333Q485 284 449 178T412 58T426 44Q447 44 466 68Q485 87 500 130L509 152H531H543Q562 152 562 144Q562 128 546 93T494 23T415 -13Q385 -13 359 3T322 44Q318 52 318 77Q318 99 352 196T386 337Q386 386 346 386Q318 386 286 370Q267 361 245 338T211 292Q207 287 193 235T162 113T138 21Q128 7 122 4Q105 -12 83 -12Q66 -12 54 -2T42 26L166 530Q166 534 161 534T129 535Q127 535 122 535T112 534Q74 534 74 562Q74 570 77 576T84 585T96 589T109 591T124 592T138 592L182 595V599Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\" d=\"M423 750Q432 750 438 744T444 730Q444 725 271 248T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q68 -250 62 -245T56 -231Q56 -221 230 257T407 740Q411 750 423 750Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(562,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1062,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"symmetry-group-6037ccfc\",\"magnetic-link-374570f4\"]'),
(2009860,'bohr-magneton','magnetic-matter','The Bohr Magneton (\\(\\mu_B\\))','<p>The <strong>Bohr Magneton (\\(\\mu_B\\))</strong> is a physical constant that serves as the natural unit for expressing the magnetic dipole moments of electrons. In university-level quantum mechanics, it represents the magnetic moment of an electron in the ground state of the Bohr atom, arising from its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/orbital-angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Orbital Angular Momentum</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Physical Definition</h3>\n                  <p>The Bohr magneton is defined in terms of fundamental constants:\n                  \\[ \\mu_B = \\frac{e\\hbar}{2m_e} \\]\n                  where \\(e\\) is the elementary charge, \\(\\hbar\\) is the reduced Planck constant, and \\(m_e\\) is the electron mass. Its value is approximately \\(9.274 \\times 10^{-24} \\text{ J/T}\\). This unit provides the scale for all atomic-level magnetism, showing that magnetism is intrinsically linked to the <strong>Quantization of Action</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Relation to Spin</h3>\n                  <p>While originally derived for orbital motion, the Bohr magneton also scales the magnetic moment due to <strong>Intrinsic Spin</strong>. For an electron, the spin magnetic moment is \\(\\mathbf{m}_s \\approx -2\\mu_B (\\mathbf{S}/\\hbar)\\). The factor of 2 (the g-factor) means that spin is \"twice as effective\" at generating magnetism as orbital motion, a profound result of the 4D relativistic <strong>Dirac Equation</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. The Zeeman Effect</h3>\n                  <p>In a magnetic field, the energy of an atomic state is shifted by an amount proportional to the Bohr magneton: \\(\\Delta E = -\\mathbf{m} \\cdot \\mathbf{B}\\). This leads to the splitting of spectral lines known as the <strong>Zeeman Effect</strong>. By measuring this splitting, physicists can determine the angular momentum and spin states of electrons, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the spectroscopic mapping of the atom.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Effective Moments in Materials</h3>\n                  <p>In bulk materials, the magnetic moment of an ion is often expressed as an \"Effective number of Bohr magnetons.\" This allows physicists to compare different materials and understand the effects of the <strong>Crystal Field</strong> and <strong>Orbital Quenching</strong>. The Bohr magneton remains the fundamental \"Yardstick\" for the 4D magnetic landscape of the subatomic world.</p>','The Bohr Magneton is a physical constant that serves as the natural unit for expressing the magnetic dipole moments of electrons. In university-level quantum mechanics, it represents the magnetic moment of an electron in the ground state of the Bohr atom, arising from its Orbital Angular Momentum. The Bohr magneton is defined in terms of fundamental constants: where is the elementary charge, is the reduced Planck constant, and is the electron mass.','The Bohr Magneton (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.766ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1222.7 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q19