(2009863,'bound-volume-current','bound-currents','Bound Volume Current (\\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\))','<p>The <strong>Bound Volume Current</strong> (\\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\)) is the effective current density that arises within the interior of a magnetized material whenever the <strong>Magnetization (\\(\\mathbf{M}\\))</strong> is non-uniform. In university-level electrodynamics, this vector field represents the macroscopic result of atomic-scale \"circulation\" that does not perfectly cancel out between adjacent points in the medium.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Mathematical Origin: The Vector Potential</h3>\n                  <p>A magnetized volume can be modeled as a collection of magnetic dipoles. By integrating the contribution of these dipoles to the <strong>Vector Potential</strong> (\\(\\mathbf{A}\\)), we arrive at a term that looks mathematically identical to the potential produced by a current density:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{J}_b = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M} \\]\n                  This identity proves that any spatial variation in the alignment or strength of magnetic moments creates a net flow of charge density, even though every electron remains attached to its parent atom.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Physical Interpretation: The Bohr Orbit</h3>\n                  <p>Microscopically, electrons in an atom move in closed <strong>Bohr Orbits</strong>, creating tiny current loops. If the magnetization is uniform, the loops of neighboring atoms cancel each other out everywhere inside the material. However, if \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) varies, one atom\'s loop is stronger or differently oriented than the next, leaving a non-zero residual current flow. The curl operation precisely captures this \"Lack of Cancellation\" in the .</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Role in Maxwell\'s Equations</h3>\n                  <p>The bound volume current acts as a source for the magnetic induction (\\(\\mathbf{B}\\)) just like free currents do. In <strong>Magnetostatics</strong>, the total curl of the B-field is:\n                  \\[ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0 (\\mathbf{J}_f + \\mathbf{J}_b) \\]\n                  By substituting the definition of \\(\\mathbf{J}_b\\), we can derive the auxiliary field \\(\\mathbf{H}\\), providing theoretical foundation for the study of <strong>Magnetic Matter</strong>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Solenoidal Property</h3>\n                  <p>Like all current densities, the bound volume current must satisfy the <strong>Continuity Equation</strong>. For static fields, \\(\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{J}_b = 0\\). This automatically satisfied because the divergence of a curl is always zero (\\(\\nabla \\cdot (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M}) = 0\\)). This topological requirement ensures that bound currents always form closed loops, maintaining the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the electromagnetic field.</p>','The Bound Volume Current is the effective current density that arises within the interior of a magnetized material whenever the Magnetization is non-uniform. In university-level electrodynamics, this vector field represents the macroscopic result of atomic-scale \"circulation\" that does not perfectly cancel out between adjacent points in the medium. A magnetized volume can be modeled as a collection of magnetic dipoles.','The Bound Volume Current (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.218ex\" height=\"1.909ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 980.3 843.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\" d=\"M174 114Q174 96 169 82T159 63T144 47L155 45Q183 40 203 40Q271 40 290 104Q294 118 294 150T295 380V624H154V686H169Q196 683 365 683Q499 683 517 686H527V624H446V379Q446 183 446 153T441 108Q413 32 315 2Q266 -11 208 -11Q160 -11 118 -2T42 37T8 114V122Q8 150 30 174T91 198T152 174T174 122V114Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\" d=\"M73 647Q73 657 77 670T89 683Q90 683 161 688T234 694Q246 694 246 685T212 542Q204 508 195 472T180 418L176 399Q176 396 182 402Q231 442 283 442Q345 442 383 396T422 280Q422 169 343 79T173 -11Q123 -11 82 27T40 150V159Q40 180 48 217T97 414Q147 611 147 623T109 637Q104 637 101 637H96Q86 637 83 637T76 640T73 647ZM336 325V331Q336 405 275 405Q258 405 240 397T207 376T181 352T163 330L157 322L136 236Q114 150 114 114Q114 66 138 42Q154 26 178 26Q211 26 245 58Q270 81 285 114T318 219Q336 291 336 325Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D409\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(627,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is the effective current density that arises within the interior of a magnetized material whenever the Magnetization (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.471ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 1092 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\" d=\"M314 0Q296 3 181 3T48 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H305Q316 679 323 667Q330 653 434 414L546 157L658 414Q766 662 773 674Q778 681 788 686H1052V624H944V62H1052V0H1040Q1016 3 874 3T708 0H696V62H804V341L803 618L786 580Q770 543 735 462T671 315Q540 13 536 9Q528 1 507 1Q485 1 477 9Q472 14 408 162T281 457T217 603Q215 603 215 334V62H323V0H314Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is non-uniform. In university-level electrodynamics, this vector field represents the macroscopic result of atomic-scale \"circulation\" that does not perfectly cancel out between adjacent points in the medium. A magnetized volume can be modeled as a collection of magnetic dipoles.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.356ex\" height=\"1.909ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 5461.3 843.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\" d=\"M174 114Q174 96 169 82T159 63T144 47L155 45Q183 40 203 40Q271 40 290 104Q294 118 294 150T295 380V624H154V686H169Q196 683 365 683Q499 683 517 686H527V624H446V379Q446 183 446 153T441 108Q413 32 315 2Q266 -11 208 -11Q160 -11 118 -2T42 37T8 114V122Q8 150 30 174T91 198T152 174T174 122V114Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\" d=\"M73 647Q73 657 77 670T89 683Q90 683 161 688T234 694Q246 694 246 685T212 542Q204 508 195 472T180 418L176 399Q176 396 182 402Q231 442 283 442Q345 442 383 396T422 280Q422 169 343 79T173 -11Q123 -11 82 27T40 150V159Q40 180 48 217T97 414Q147 611 147 623T109 637Q104 637 101 637H96Q86 637 83 637T76 640T73 647ZM336 325V331Q336 405 275 405Q258 405 240 397T207 376T181 352T163 330L157 322L136 236Q114 150 114 114Q114 66 138 42Q154 26 178 26Q211 26 245 58Q270 81 285 114T318 219Q336 291 336 325Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\" d=\"M630 29Q630 9 609 9Q604 9 587 25T493 118L389 222L284 117Q178 13 175 11Q171 9 168 9Q160 9 154 15T147 29Q147 36 161 51T255 146L359 250L255 354Q1