(2009864,'bound-surface-current','bound-currents','Bound Surface Current (\\(\\mathbf{K}_b\\))','<p>The <strong>Bound Surface Current</strong> (\\(\\mathbf{K}_b\\)) is the net current per unit length that flows on the boundary of a magnetized material. In university-level physics, it represents the macroscopic result of atomic current loops at the material interface that are not cancelled by neighboring loops, effectively acting as the \"Solenoidal Windings\" of a permanent magnet.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Boundary Discontinuity</h3>\n                  <p>At the edge of a material, the atomic loops of the surface atoms have neighbors one side but not the other. This creates a \"Sheath\" of uncancelled current. The density of this surface current is determined by the cross product of the <strong>Magnetization</strong> and the surface normal:\n                  \\[ \\mathbf{K}_b = \\mathbf{M} \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\]\n                  This vector is always tangential to the surface, showing that the effective charge flow \"wraps around\" the magnetized object.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Equivalence to a Solenoid</h3>\n                  <p>A cylinder with uniform longitudinal magnetization (\\(\\mathbf{M} = M \\hat{\\mathbf{z}}\\)) has a surface current \\(\\mathbf{K}_b = M \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\phi}}\\). This distribution is identical to the current in a <strong>Solenoid</strong>. This equivalence allows us to calculate the exterior field of magnets using simple coil formulas, bridging the gap between <strong>Classical Electrodynamics</strong> and material science.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Boundary Conditions</h3>\n                  <p>The bound surface current is responsible for the discontinuity in the tangential component of the \\(\\mathbf{B}\\) field at the surface. Specifically, the \"Jump\" in the B-field is given by \\(\\mathbf{B}_{out} - \\mathbf{B}_{in} = \\mu_0 (\\mathbf{K}_b \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{n}})\\). These <strong>Boundary Conditions</strong> are the primary tool for solving  problems in complex geometries.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Physical Reality of \\(\\mathbf{K}_b\\)</h3>\n                  <p>Although electrons are not flowing from one atom to another, the <em>effect</em> of the collective orbital motion is a real magnetic field source. If you were to measure the magnetic field just outside a permanent magnet, the result is exactly what you would expect from the bound surface currents. This confirms that these currents are an <strong>Ontological</strong> feature of the material\'s boundary in the .</p>','The Bound Surface Current is the net current per unit length that flows on the boundary of a magnetized material. In university-level physics, it represents the macroscopic result of atomic current loops at the material interface that are not cancelled by neighboring loops, effectively acting as the \"Solenoidal Windings\" of a permanent magnet. At the edge of a material, the atomic loops of the surface atoms have neighbors one side but not the other.','The Bound Surface Current (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.913ex\" height=\"1.909ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 1287.3 843.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40A\" d=\"M400 0Q376 3 226 3Q75 3 51 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 226 683Q376 683 400 686H412V624H304V338L472 483L634 624H565V686H576Q597 683 728 683Q814 683 829 686H836V624H730L614 524Q507 432 497 422Q496 422 498 418T514 395T553 342T627 241L759 63L805 62H852V0H842Q830 3 701 3Q550 3 526 0H513V62H549Q584 62 584 63Q583 65 486 196T388 328L304 256V62H412V0H400Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\" d=\"M73 647Q73 657 77 670T89 683Q90 683 161 688T234 694Q246 694 246 685T212 542Q204 508 195 472T180 418L176 399Q176 396 182 402Q231 442 283 442Q345 442 383 396T422 280Q422 169 343 79T173 -11Q123 -11 82 27T40 150V159Q40 180 48 217T97 414Q147 611 147 623T109 637Q104 637 101 637H96Q86 637 83 637T76 640T73 647ZM336 325V331Q336 405 275 405Q258 405 240 397T207 376T181 352T163 330L157 322L136 236Q114 150 114 114Q114 66 138 42Q154 26 178 26Q211 26 245 58Q270 81 285 114T318 219Q336 291 336 325Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40A\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(934,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is the net current per unit length that flows on the boundary of a magnetized material. In university-level physics, it represents the macroscopic result of atomic current loops at the material interface that are not cancelled by neighboring loops, effectively acting as the \"Solenoidal Windings\" of a permanent magnet. At the edge of a material, the atomic loops of the surface atoms have neighbors one side but not the other.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.612ex\" height=\"2.208ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -818 5574.3 975.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40A\" d=\"M400 0Q376 3 226 3Q75 3 51 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 226 683Q376 683 400 686H412V624H304V338L472 483L634 624H565V686H576Q597 683 728 683Q814 683 829 686H836V624H730L614 524Q507 432 497 422Q496 422 498 418T514 395T553 342T627 241L759 63L805 62H852V0H842Q830 3 701 3Q550 3 526 0H513V62H549Q584 62 584 63Q583 65 486 196T388 328L304 256V62H412V0H400Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\" d=\"M73 647Q73 657 77 670T89 683Q90 683 161 688T234 694Q246 694 246 685T212 542Q204 508 195 472T180 418L176 399Q176 396 182 402Q231 442 283 442Q345 442 383 396T422 280Q422 169 343 79T173 -11Q123 -11 82 27T40 150V159Q40 180 48 217T97 414Q147 611 147 623T109 637Q104 637 101 637H96Q86 637 83 637T76 640T73 647ZM336 325V331Q336 405 275 405Q258 405 240 397T207 376T181 352T163 330L157 322L136 236Q114 150 114 114Q114 66 138 42Q154 26 178 26Q211 26 245 58Q270 81 285 114T318 219Q336 291 336 325Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\" d=\"M314 0Q296 3 181 3T48 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H305Q316 679 323 667Q330 653 434 414L546 157L658 414Q766 662 773 674Q778 681 788 686H1052V624H944V62H1052V0H1040Q1016 3 874 3T708 0H696V62H804V341L803 618L786 580Q770 543 735 462T671 315Q540 13 536 9Q528 1 507 1Q485 1 477 9Q472 14 408 162T281 457T217 603Q215 603 215 334V62H323V0H314Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\" d=\"M630 29Q630 9 609 9Q604 9 587 25T493 118L389 222L284 117Q178 13 175 11Q171 9 168 9Q160 9 154 15T147 29Q147 36 161 51T255 146L359 250L255 354Q174 435 161 449T147 471Q147 480 153 485T168 490Q173 490 175 489Q178 487 284 383L389 278L493 382Q570 459 587 475T609 491Q630 491 630 471Q630 464 620 453T522 355L418 250L522 145Q606 61 618 48T630 29Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D427\" d=\"M40 442Q217 450 218 450H224V407L225 365Q233 378 245 391T289 422T362 448Q374 450 398 450Q428 450 448 447T491 434T529 402T551 346Q553 335 554 198V62H623V0H614Q596 3 489 3Q374 3 365 0H356V62H425V194V275Q425 348 416 373T371 399Q326 399 288 370T238 290Q236 281 235 171V62H304V0H295Q277 3 171 3Q64 3 46 0H37V62H106V210V303Q106 353 104 363T91 376Q77 380 50 380H37V442H40Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\" d=\"M112 560L249 694L257 686Q387 562 387 560L361 531Q359 532 303 581L250 627L195 580Q182 569 169 557T148 538L140 532Q138 530 125 546L112 560Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40A\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(934,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1565.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(2620.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3935.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4935.3,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D427\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D427\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(319.5,24) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"5E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"surface-bound-current-3b15de46\",\"field-source-98e8a6ff\"]'),
(2009865,'free-currents-magnetism','magnetic-matter','Free Currents and the Macroscopic Field','<p>In the study of <strong>Magnetic Matter</strong>, currents are divided into two categories: <strong>Bound Currents</strong> (internal to atoms) and <strong>Free Currents</strong> (\\(\\mathbf{J}_f\\)). Free currents are the charges that we can directly control and measure—such as the electrons flowing through a wire or the ions in a beam—and they serve as the primary external source for the magnetic field.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Defining \"Free\"</h3>\n                  <p>A current is \"free\" if it consists of charge carriers that are not locally bound to a specific atom or molecule in a neutral way. In university-level physics, we distinguish free currents because they are the variables we manipulate in electrical circuits. The total current is the sum of free and bound parts: \\(\\mathbf{J}_{tot} = \\mathbf{J}_f + \\mathbf{J}_b\\).</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. The Auxiliary Field (\\(\\mathbf{H}\\))</h3>\n                  <p>The <strong>Magnetic Intensity (\\(\\mathbf{H}\\))</strong> was introduced specifically to isolate the effect of free currents. In <strong>Magnetostatics</strong>, the curl of H is equal to the free current density only:\n                  \\[ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{J}_f \\]\n                  This allows for the design of electromagnets where we know the current in the coil (free current) but not necessarily the complex magnetization of the iron core.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Interaction with Bound Charges</h3>\n                  <p>Free currents generate an initial B-field that penetrates the material. This field then exerts torques on the atomic dipoles, creating <strong>Magnetization</strong> and, consequently, <strong>Bound Currents</strong>. The total field is a feedback loop between the free sources and the material response, a dynamic encoded in the <strong>Constitutive Relation</strong> of the medium.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Precision Measurement</h3>\n                  <p>Because free currents are measured with ammeters and governed by <strong>Ohm\'s Law</strong>, they provide the empirical anchor for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in electrodynamics. Our ability to predict the  distribution based on measurable free currents is the foundation of all electrical power technology, from the simple transformer to the <strong>Tokamak</strong> fusion reactor.</p>','In the study of Magnetic Matter, currents are divided into two categories: Bound Currents (internal to atoms) and Free Currents. Free currents are the charges that we can directly control and measure—such as the electrons flowing through a wire or the ions in a beam—and they serve as the primary external source for the magnetic field. A current is \"free\" if it consists of charge carriers that are not locally bound to a specific atom or molecule in a neutral way.','In the study of Magnetic Matter, currents are divided into two categories: Bound Currents (internal to atoms) and Free Currents (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.667ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.412ex\" height=\"2.219ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 1065.9 981\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\" d=\"M174 114Q174 96 169 82T159 63T144 47L155 45Q183 40 203 40Q271 40 290 104Q294 118 294 150T295 380V624H154V686H169Q196 683 365 683Q499 683 517 686H527V624H446V379Q446 183 446 153T441 108Q413 32 315 2Q266 -11 208 -11Q160 -11 118 -2T42 37T8 114V122Q8 150 30 174T91 198T152 174T174 122V114Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\" d=\"M118 -162Q120 -162 124 -164T135 -167T147 -168Q160 -168 171 -155T187 -126Q197 -99 221 27T267 267T289 382V385H242Q195 385 192 387Q188 390 188 397L195 425Q197 430 203 430T250 431Q298 431 298 432Q298 434 307 482T319 540Q356 705 465 705