\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(824,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D459\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D459\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(220,-739.7)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(484,289) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D459\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D459\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(298,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1076,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g></g></g><rect width=\"1848.4\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"far-field-model-388eb3da\",\"angular-basis-de903d3a\",\"technical-relation-d4069326\"]'),
(2009887,'mean-value-property','potential-theory','The Mean Value Property','<p>The <strong>Mean Value Property</strong> is a fundamental mathematical characteristic of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/harmonic-functions-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Harmonic Functions</strong></a>—solutions to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/laplace-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Laplace\'s Equation</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla^2 \\Phi = 0 \\)). In university-level physics, it asserts that the value of the potential at any point in a source-free region is exactly equal to the average of its values over any sphere (or spherical volume) centered at that point.</p>\n\n                <h3>1. Mathematical Statement</h3>\n                <p>For a scalar potential \\( \\Phi(\\mathbf{r}) \\) that is harmonic within a domain, the potential at a point \\( \\mathbf{r}_0 \\) can be found by integrating over a surrounding sphere of radius \\( R \\):\n                \\[ \\Phi(\\mathbf{r}_0) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi R^2} \\oint_{S_R} \\Phi(\\mathbf{r}) \\, dS \\]\n                This shows that harmonic functions are \"perfectly smooth\" and have no local jumps or discontinuities, anchoring 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the continuous nature of the 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong>.</p>\n\n                <h3>2. Electrostatic Implications</h3>\n                <p>In electrostatics, the region between charges has no charge density (\\( \\rho = 0 \\)). The mean value property guarantees that the \"Voltage\" in empty space is simply a smeared-out average of the surrounding boundaries. This 4D <strong>Logical Basis</strong> dictates how 4D <strong>Equipotentials</strong> must arrange themselves between conductors without any \"kinks.\"</p>\n\n                <h3>3. Connection to the Maximum Principle</h3>\n                <p>The immediate consequence of the mean value property is the <strong>Maximum Principle</strong>. If a point\'s value is strictly an average of its neighbors, it cannot be strictly greater or strictly less than all of them. This leads to the conclusion that local extrema cannot exist in empty space, forming the physical basis for <strong>Earnshaw\'s Theorem</strong>.</p>','The Mean Value Property is a fundamental mathematical characteristic of Harmonic Functions—solutions to Laplace\'s Equation. In university-level physics, it asserts that the value of the potential at any point in a source-free region is exactly equal to the average of its values over any sphere (or spherical volume) centered at that point. For a scalar potential that is harmonic within a domain, the potential at a point can be found by integrating over a surrounding sphere of radius : This shows that harmonic functions are \"perfectly smooth\" and have no local jumps or discontinuities, anchoring 4D Scientific Realism in the continuous nature of the 4D Subatomic Vacuum.','The Mean Value Property is a fundamental mathematical characteristic of Harmonic Functions—solutions to Laplace\'s Equation (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.654ex\" height=\"2.072ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 3825.1 915.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549