\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\" d=\"M56 237T56 250T70 270H369V420L370 570Q380 583 389 583Q402 583 409 568V270H707Q722 262 722 250T707 230H409V-68Q401 -82 391 -82H389H387Q375 -82 369 -68V230H70Q56 237 56 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q340 441 372 389Q373 390 377 395T388 406T404 418Q438 442 450 442Q454 442 457 439T460 434Q460 425 391 149Q320 -135 320 -139Q320 -147 365 -148H390Q396 -156 396 -157T393 -175Q389 -188 383 -194H370Q339 -192 262 -192Q234 -192 211 -192T174 -192T157 -193Q143 -193 143 -185Q143 -182 145 -170Q149 -154 152 -151T172 -148Q220 -148 230 -141Q238 -136 258 -53T279 32Q279 33 272 29Q224 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM352 326Q329 405 277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q233 26 290 98L298 109L352 326Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"mathematical-proxy-4e628999\",\"surface-density-0b7c0fb1\",\"technical-relation-1c6725f1\"]'),
(2009893,'separation-of-variables-physics','potential-theory','Separation of Variables','<p>Separation of Variables is the primary analytical technique for solving 4D <strong>Partial Differential Equations</strong> (PDEs) like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/laplace-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Laplace\'s Equation</strong></a>. In university-level 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/potential-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Potential Theory</strong></a>, this method breaks a complex 3D field problem into three separate 1D problems, providing exact 4D <strong>Mathematical Structures</strong> for potentials in highly symmetric geometries.</p>\n\n                <h3>1. The Product Ansatz</h3>\n                <p>The core assumption is that the potential \\( V(x,y,z) \\) can be written as a product of single-variable functions: \\( V(x,y,z) = X(x)Y(y)Z(z) \\). When substituted into Laplace\'s equation (\\( \\nabla^2 V = 0 \\)) and divided by \\( V \\), the equation separates into independent terms. Since \\( x, y, \\) and \\( z \\) can vary independently, each term must equal a constant (the <strong>Separation Constants</strong>), which sum to zero.</p>\n\n                <h3>2. Eigenfunctions and Boundary Conditions</h3>\n                <p>This transforms the PDE into Ordinary Differential Equations (ODEs). The solutions to these ODEs are <strong>Eigenfunctions</strong>—like sines, cosines, or exponentials in Cartesian coordinates, and <strong>Legendre Polynomials</strong> or <strong>Bessel Functions</strong> in spherical/cylindrical coordinates. The 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the field is found by taking a linear combination (a Fourier series) of these eigenfunctions that perfectly matches the 4D <strong>Dirichlet</strong> or <strong>Neumann</strong> boundary conditions.</p>\n\n                <h3>3. Orthogonality and Fourier\'s Trick</h3>\n                <p>The coefficients of the series are determined using the <strong>Orthogonality</strong> of the eigenfunctions. By integrating the boundary condition against a specific basis function (Fourier\'s trick), we can isolate and calculate each coefficient. This anchors 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the fact that any complex 4D <strong>Subatomic Vacuum</strong> boundary state can be decomposed into an infinite sum of simple, fundamental 4D <strong>Harmonic</strong> shapes.</p>','Separation of Variables is the primary analytical technique for solving 4D Partial Differential Equations (PDEs) like Laplace\'s Equation. In university-level 4D Potential Theory, this method breaks a complex 3D field problem into three separate 1D problems, providing exact 4D Mathematical Structures for potentials in highly symmetric geometries. The core assumption is that the potential can be written as a product of single-variable functions:.','Separation of Variables is the primary analytical technique for solving 4D Partial Differential Equations (PDEs) like Laplace\'s Equation. In university-level 4D Potential Theory, this method breaks a complex 3D field problem into three separate 1D problems, providing exact 4D Mathematical Structures for potentials in highly symmetric geometries. The core assumption is that the potential <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.967ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3963.3 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D449\" d=\"M52 648Q52 670 65 683H76Q118 680 181 680Q299 680 320 683H330Q336 677 336 674T334 656Q329 641 325 637H304Q282 635 274 635Q245 630 242 620Q242 618 271 369T301 118L374 235Q447 352 520 471T595 594Q599 601 599 609Q599 633 555 637Q537 637 537 648Q537 649 539 661Q542 675 545 679T558 683Q560 683 570 683T604 682T668 681Q737 681 75