(2009909,'coulombs-law','biot-savart-law','Coulomb\'s Law','<p>Coulomb\'s Law is the empirical foundation of electrostatics, describing the fundamental force between two stationary point charges. At a university level, it is viewed as the electrostatic limit of the more general electromagnetic interaction, strictly valid for point charges at rest in a vacuum. It provides the basis for understanding how charges generate electric fields and store potential energy in the vacuum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Inverse Square Interaction and Gauss\'s Law</h3>\n                    <p>The law states that the electrostatic force between two point charges is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: \\( \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2} \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} \\). This specific \\( 1/r^2 \\) dependence is what allows for the derivation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a>. By integrating the flux of the Coulomb field over a closed surface, we find that the total flux is proportional to the enclosed charge (\\( \\oint \\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{a} = Q_{enc}/\\varepsilon_0 \\)). This implies that the electric field is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/divergent-fields\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>divergent</strong></a>, with point charges acting as the sources and sinks of the field lines.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Point Charges and the Dirac Delta Function</h3>\n                    <p>In classical field theory, a \"point charge\" is a mathematical idealization. To represent a point charge within the framework of continuous charge distributions (\\( \\rho(\\mathbf{r}) \\)), we use the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dirac-delta\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dirac Delta Function</strong></a> (\\( \\delta^3(\\mathbf{r}) \\)). For a charge \\( q \\) at position \\( \\mathbf{r}\' \\), the density is \\( \\rho(\\mathbf{r}) = q \\delta^3(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{r}\') \\). This representation is essential for solving <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/poisson-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Poisson\'s Equation</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla^2 \\Phi = -\\rho/\\varepsilon_0 \\)), where the Coulomb potential emerges as the <strong>Green\'s Function</strong> for the Laplacian operator in three-dimensional Euclidean space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/potential-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Potential Energy</strong></a> and Field Energy Density</h3>\n                    <p>The work required to assemble a configuration of charges is stored as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electrostatic-potential-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electrostatic Potential Energy</strong></a>. For a system of point charges, this is sum of the pairwise interactions: \\( W = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\sum \\sum \\frac{q_i q_j}{r_{ij}} \\). In a continuous field representation, this energy is stored in the electric field itself, with an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/energy-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Energy Density</strong></a> given by \\( u_E = \\frac{1}{2}\\varepsilon_0 E^2 \\). This \"Field-Centric\" view is critical for modern physics, as it shows that the vacuum itself possesses energy when a field is present.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Precision Tests of the Inverse Square Law</h3>\n                    <p>The precision of the \\( 1/r^2 \\) dependence has been tested to extraordinary levels. If the law were slightly different (\\( r^{-(2+\\epsilon)} \\)), Gauss\'s Law would fail, and a hollow conductor would have a non-zero internal field. Modern experiments (Cavendish-type) have established that \\( |\\epsilon| \\lt 10^{-16} \\). Any violation of this law would imply a non-zero <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/photon-mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Photon Mass</strong></a> (\\( m_\\gamma \\)), leading to a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/yukawa-potential\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Yukawa Potential</strong></a> (\\( \\Phi \\propto e^{-\\mu r}/r \\)) and the breakdown of the standard model of electrodynamics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Electrostatic Limit and Relativity</h3>\n                    <p>Coulomb\'s Law is strictly a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/static-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Static Law</strong></a>. It fails when charges are in motion because it does not account for the finite speed of light (\\( c \\)). When charges move, the \"information\" about their position travels at speed \\( c \\), necessitating the use of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/retarded-potentials\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Retarded Potentials</strong></a>. From a relativistic perspective, the magnetic force is simply the result of applying Lorentz transformations to the electrostatic Coulomb force. Thus, Coulomb\'s Law plus Special Relativity leads directly to the full suite of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maxwells-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maxwell\'s Equations</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n\n                    <h3>6. Coulomb\'s Law in Dielectrics and Screened Potentials</h3>\n                    <p>In material media, the effective force between charges is reduced by the presence of surrounding molecules. This accounted for by the <strong>Permittivity</strong> (\\( \\varepsilon \\)) of the medium, yielding \\( \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon} \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2} \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} \\). In plasmas or electrolytes, the law is further modified by <strong>Debye Screening</strong>. The mobile charges in the medium cluster around a source charge, \"shielding\" its influence. This results in the <strong>Screened Coulomb Potential</strong>: \\( \\Phi(r) \\propto \\frac{1}{r} e^{-r/\\lambda_D} \\), where \\( \\lambda_D \\) is the Debye length. This transition from long-range to short-range interaction is the defining characteristic of charged-particle dynamics in multi-body systems.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Coulomb\'s Law is intrinsically linked to the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/greens-function\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Green\'s Functions in Electrodynamics</a></strong>, which provides the necessary analytical depth for understanding the underlying physical manifold.</p>','Coulomb\'s Law is the empirical foundation of electrostatics, describing the fundamental force between two stationary point charges. At a university level, it is viewed as the electrostatic limit of the more general electromagnetic interaction, strictly valid for point charges at rest in a vacuum. It provides the basis for understanding how charges generate electric fields and store potential energy in the vacuum.','Coulomb\'s Law is the empirical foundation of electrostatics, describing the fundamental force between two stationary point charges. At a university level, it is viewed as the electrostatic limit of the more general electromagnetic interaction, strictly valid for point charges at rest in a vacuum. It provides the basis for understanding how charges generate electric fields and store potential energy in the vacuum.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.045ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13.698ex\" height=\"3.002ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864.9 6054.5 1327\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D405\" d=\"M425 0L228 3Q63 3 51 0H39V62H147V618H39V680H644V676Q647 670 659 552T675 428V424H613Q613 433 605 477Q599 511 589 535T562 574T530 599T488 612T441 617T387 618H368H304V371H333Q389 373 411 390T437 468V488H499V192H437V212Q436 244 430 263T408 292T378 305T333 309H304V62H439V0H425Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"