" transform=\"translate(530.3,-377.4) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(484,289) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1448.1\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(5580.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(237,24) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"5E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-coulomb-force-class-subtopic-link-coulomb-force-a-4cfc70aa\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-poissons-and-laplaces-equations-class-subtopic-link-poisson-equation-a-3d29b9ae\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-field-energy-class-subtopic-link-field-energy-a-df9eac1d\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-yukawa-potential-class-subtopic-link-yukawa-potential-a-f9e4388b\"]'),
(2009910,'electric-potential','electromagnetism','Electric Potential (\\( \\Phi \\))','<p><strong>Electric Potential</strong> (\\( \\Phi \\)) is a scalar field that represents the potential energy per unit charge at any point in space due to an arrangement of electric charges. In university-level electromagnetism, it is the fundamental tool for analyzing <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservative-force-field\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservative Fields</strong></a>, allowing physicists to describe the electromagnetic environment using a single scalar value instead of complex force vectors.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Work, Energy, and the Scalar Field</h3>\n                    <p>The electric potential at a point \\( \\mathbf{r} \\) is defined as the work done per unit charge by an external agent in moving a test charge from a reference point (usually infinity) to \\( \\mathbf{r} \\):\n                    \\[ \\Phi(\\mathbf{r}) = -\\int_{\\infty}^{\\mathbf{r}} \\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} \\]\n                    Because the electrostatic field is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/irrotational-fields\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>irrotational</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{E} = 0 \\)), this integral is independent of the path taken. This path independence is what guarantees the existence of a unique potential value at every point, making \\( \\Phi \\) a well-defined scalar field.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Gradient Relation</h3>\n                    <p>The relationship between the electric field \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) and the potential \\( \\Phi \\) is given by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/negative-gradient\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>negative gradient</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\mathbf{E} = -\\nabla \\Phi \\]\n                    This equation means that the electric field points in the direction of the steepest <em>decrease</em> in potential. In physical terms, positive charges are naturally \"pushed\" from regions of high potential to regions of low potential, similar to a ball rolling down a hill. By finding the potential distribution, the entire vector field can be derived through simple differentiation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Equipotential Surfaces</h3>\n                    <p>An <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/equipotential-surface\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Equipotential Surface</strong></a> is a locus of points where the potential is constant (\\( \\Phi = \\text{constant} \\)). A vital property of these surfaces is that they are <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/always-perpendicular\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>always perpendicular</strong></a> to the electric field lines at every point. Since no work is required to move a charge along an equipotential surface (\\( d\\Phi = -\\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = 0 \\)), these surfaces provide a powerful way to visualize the \"energy landscape\" of a system of charges.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Potential of Discrete and Continuous Sources</h3>\n                    <p>The potential generated by a single point charge \\( q \\) is given by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/coulomb-potential\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Coulomb Potential</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\Phi(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\frac{q}{r} \\]\n                    For a system of many charges, the total potential is the algebraic sum of individual potentials (the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/principle-of-superposition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-superposition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of Superposition</strong></a></a>). For a continuous charge distribution \\( \\rho \\), the potential is found by integrating over the entire volume:\n                    \\[ \\Phi(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\int \\frac{\\rho(\\mathbf{r}\')}{|\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{r}\'|} d\\tau\' \\]\n                    This integral formulation is the starting point for solving <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/poisson-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Poisson\'s Equation</strong></a> in complex geometries.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Electromotive Force (EMF) and Voltage</h3>\n                    <p>In the context of circuits, the difference in electric potential between two points is known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/voltage\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Voltage</strong></a> or potential difference (\\( \\Delta\\Phi \\)). While potential is a property of a point in space, voltage is a measure of the energy transferred per unit charge between two locations. In electrodynamics, the concept is generalized to include the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromotive-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromotive Force (EMF)</strong></a>, which accounts for non-conservative influences like changing magnetic fields through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a>.</p>','Electric Potential is a scalar field that represents the potential energy per unit charge at any point in space due to an arrangement of electric charges. In university-level electromagnetism, it is the fundamental tool for analyzing Conservative Fields, allowing physicists to describe the electromagnetic environment using a single scalar value instead of complex force vectors. The electric potential at a point is defined as the work done per unit charge by an external agent in moving a test charge from a reference point (usually infinity) to : Because the electrostatic field is irrotational, this integral is independent of the path taken.','Electric Potential (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.633ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 722 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\" d=\"M312 622Q310 623 307 625T303 629T297 631T286 634T270 635T2