dient-link-class-subtopic-link-the-gradient-link-a-f978a3ac\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-source-summation-class-subtopic-link-source-summation-a-96387421\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-potential-difference-class-subtopic-link-potential-difference-a-59b71f56\"]'),
(2009911,'electric-field','electromagnetism','Electric Field (\\( \\mathbf{E} \\))','<p>The electric field (\\( \\mathbf{E} \\)) is a vector field that permeates space, representing the force per unit charge exerted on a stationary test charge. In modern field theory, the field is considered a dynamical physical entity in its own right, capable of storing energy and momentum and propagating information through the vacuum at the speed of light.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Sources and Topology (Gauss\'s and Faraday\'s Laws)</h3>\n                    <p>In vacuum, the electric field is governed by two of Maxwell\'s equations. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = \\rho/\\varepsilon_0 \\)) identifies electric charges as the \"sources\" and \"sinks\" of the field lines. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{E} = -\\partial \\mathbf{B}/\\partial t \\)) reveals that a changing magnetic field induces a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-conservative-fields\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>non-conservative</strong></a> electric field. This means that while electrostatic fields are irrotational, time-varying fields can possess circulation, a principle fundamental to electrical generators and transformers.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Potential Formulation and Gauge Invariance</h3>\n                    <p>To ensure consistency with Maxwell\'s equations, the electric field is expressed in terms of the scalar potential (\\( \\Phi \\)) and the magnetic vector potential (\\( \\mathbf{A} \\)):\n                    \\[ \\mathbf{E} = -\\nabla \\Phi - \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{A}}{\\partial t} \\]\n                    This representation highlights that the field arises from both static charge distributions (the gradient of the potential) and dynamic current distributions (the time derivative of the vector potential). In university-level electrodynamics, this formulation is the starting point for analyzing radiation and the propagation of electromagnetic waves.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Energy and Momentum Flux (The Poynting Theorem)</h3>\n                    <p>One of the most profound realizations of field theory is that the electric field itself carries energy and momentum. The energy density is proportional to the square of the field strength: \\( u_E = \\frac{1}{2}\\varepsilon_0 E^2 \\). Furthermore, when combined with a magnetic field, the electric field generates a directional energy flux described by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/poynting-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Poynting Vector</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{S} = \\mathbf{E} \\times \\mathbf{B}/\\mu_0 \\)) and a corresponding field momentum density (\\( \\mathbf{g} = \\mathbf{S}/c^2 \\)). This demonstrates that the vacuum possesses a mechanical structure when fields are present.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Relativistic Covariance and Transformations</h3>\n                    <p>In Special Relativity, the electric and magnetic fields are not independent vectors but components of the antisymmetric <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-field-tensor\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electromagnetic Field Tensor</strong></a> (\\( F^{\\mu\\nu} \\)). Under a Lorentz boost, what one observer perceives as a pure electric field, another may perceive as a combination of electric and magnetic fields. This <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/relativistic-unification\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Relativistic Unification</strong></a> proves that the distinction between \"electric\" and \"magnetic\" is frame-dependent, reflecting the deeper symmetry of the underlying four-dimensional spacetime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Boundary Conditions and Interface Physics</h3>\n                    <p>At the boundary between two media, the electric field must satisfy specific <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/boundary-conditions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Boundary Conditions</strong></a> derived from Maxwell\'s equations. The tangential component (\\( E_{||} \\)) must be continuous to ensure that the potential is well-defined, while the normal component (\\( E_{\\perp} \\)) jumps by an amount proportional to the surface charge density \\( \\sigma \\). These conditions are essential for the study of capacitors, wave reflection, and the behavior of semiconductors.</p>','The electric field is a vector field that permeates space, representing the force per unit charge exerted on a stationary test charge. In modern field theory, the field is considered a dynamical physical entity in its own right, capable of storing energy and momentum and propagating information through the vacuum at the speed of light. In vacuum, the electric field is governed by two of Maxwell\'s equations.','The electric field (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.71ex\" height=\"1.538ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 756 680\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\" d=\"M723 286Q721 284 700 145T677 3V0H39V62H147V618H39V680H660V676Q662 670 675 552T691 428V424H629V428Q629 429 627 448T618 494T601 541Q574 593 527 605T382 618H374H304V384H336Q338 384 347 384T361 384T376 386T392 390T407 397T421 407T432 423Q442 444 443 482V501H505V205H443V224Q442 258 435 278T411 307T380 318T336 322H304V62H375H394Q429 62 449 62T497 66T541 76T577 95T609 126T632 170T651 232Q661 287 661 289H723V286Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D404\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is a vector field that permeates space, representing the force per unit charge exerted on a stationary test charge. In modern field theory, the field is considered a dynamical physical entity in its own right, capable of storing energy and momentum and propagating information through the vacuum at the speed of light. In vacuum, the electric field is governed by two of Maxwell\'s equations.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.602ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.014ex\" height=\"4.749ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1391 7520 2099\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\" d=\"M723 286Q721 284 700 145T677 3V0H39V62H147V618H39V680H660V676Q662 670 675 552T691 428V424H629V428Q629 429 627 448T618 494T601 541Q574 593 527 605T382 618H374H304V384H336Q338 384 347 384T361 384T376 386T392 390T407 397T421 407T432 423Q442 444 443 482V501H505V205H443V224Q442 258 435 278T411 307T380 318T336 322H304V62H375H394Q429 62 449 62T497 66T541 76T577 95T609 126T632 170T651 232Q661 287 661 289H723V286Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\" d=\"M84 237T84 250T98 270H679Q694 262 694 250T679 230H98Q84 237 84 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\" d=\"M312 622Q310 623 307 625T303 629T297 631T286 634T270 635T246 636T211 637H184V683H196Q220 680 361 680T526 683H538V637H511Q468 637 447 635T422 631T411 622V533L425 531Q525 519 595 466T665 342Q665 301 642 267T583 209T506 172T425 152L411 150V61Q417 55 421 53T447 48T511 46H538V0H526Q502 3 361 3T196 0H184V46H211Q231 46 245 46T270 47T286 48T297 51T303 54T307 57T312 61V150H310Q309 151 289 153T232 166T160 195Q149 201 136 210T103 238T69