\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3499.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4499.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D404\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D404\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5255.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5922.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6978.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8033.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(9033.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(736,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(1514,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D401\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(448.5,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1584\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3338,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-wave-equation-physics-class-subtopic-link-the-wave-equation-a-816dc899\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-velocity-of-light-class-subtopic-link-velocity-of-light-a-62f1acef\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-wave-phase-class-subtopic-link-wave-phase-a-d515798a\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-relativistic-wave-equation-class-subtopic-link-relativistic-wave-equation-a-1209a836\"]'),
(2009922,'electromagnetic-radiation','electromagnetism','Electromagnetic Radiation','<p>Electromagnetic Radiation is the process by which accelerating electric charges emit energy in the form of self-propagating waves of electric and magnetic fields. In university-level electrodynamics, radiation is the ultimate dynamical consequence of Maxwell\'s equations, revealing how matter and fields interact to transport information and energy across the universe at the speed of light.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Larmor Formula: Power from Acceleration</h3>\n                    <p>The most fundamental result in radiation theory is that a stationary or constant-velocity charge does not radiate. Radiation requires <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/acceleration-radiation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Acceleration</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{a} \\)). The total power (\\( P \\)) radiated by a non-relativistic point charge \\( q \\) is given by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/larmor-formula\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Larmor Formula</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ P = \\frac{\\mu_0 q^2 a^2}{6\\pi c} \\]\n                    This cubic dependence on acceleration explains why electrons in atomic orbits would classically radiate away all their energy (leading to the Bohr model) and why synchrotron radiation is a dominant energy loss mechanism in particle accelerators.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Retarded Potentials and Causality</h3>\n                    <p>Because electromagnetic influence propagates at a finite speed \\( c \\), the fields at a point \\( \\mathbf{r} \\) and time \\( t \\) depend on the state of the source at an earlier <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/retarded-time\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Retarded Time</strong></a>: \\( t_{ret} = t - |\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{r}\'|/c \\). This formalized through the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/retarded-potentials\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Retarded Potentials</strong></a> (\\( \\Phi, \\mathbf{A} \\)), which satisfy the inhomogeneous wave equations. This causal delay is the physical reason why fields can \"detach\" from their sources and travel independently as radiation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Liénard-Wiechert Potentials</h3>\n                    <p>For a point charge in arbitrary motion, the retarded potentials are known as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lienard-wiechert\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Liénard-Wiechert Potentials</strong></a>. Unlike static potentials, these include a \"velocity factor\" that accounts for the fact that the charge may be moving toward or away from the observer while its signal is in transit. These potentials lead to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/heaviside-feynman\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Heaviside-Feynman fields</strong></a>, which decompose the total field into a \"velocity field\" (which falls off as \\( 1/r^2 \\)) and an \"acceleration field\" (which falls off as \\( 1/r \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Near-Field and Far-Field (Radiation) Zones</h3>\n                    <p>The fields of an accelerating charge are divided into two distinct regions. In the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/near-field-zone\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Near-Field Zone</strong></a> (\\( r \\ll \\lambda \\)), the static and induction terms dominate, and energy cycles back and forth between the source and the field. In the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/far-field-zone\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Far-Field or Radiation Zone</strong></a> (\\( r \\gg \\lambda \\)), the acceleration fields (\\( 1/r \\)) become dominant. Because the Poynting vector (\\( \\mathbf{S} \\propto E^2 \\)) falls off as \\( 1/r^2 \\), the total power integrated over a large sphere remains constant, signifying the permanent escape of energy from the source.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Electric Dipole Radiation</h3>\n                    <p>The simplest radiating system is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/oscillating-electric-dipole\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Oscillating Electric Dipole</strong></a>. By analyzing a charge distribution that varies sinusoidally in time, we find that the radiation is not isotropic but follows a \"donut-shaped\" angular distribution proportional to \\( \\sin^2\\theta \\). Most of the energy is radiated perpendicular to the axis of oscillation, with zero radiation along the axis itself. This principle governs the design of radio antennas and the scattering of light in the atmosphere (Rayleigh scattering).</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Relativistic Radiation: Bremsstrahlung and Synchrotrons</h3>\n                    <p>When charges reach relativistic velocities (\\( v \\approx c \\)), the Larmor formula must be generalized using the 4-acceleration. The radiation becomes highly \"beamed\" in the direction of motion, a phenomenon known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/relativistic-beaming\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Relativistic Beaming</strong></a>. High-energy electrons undergoing rapid deceleration produce <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bremsstrahlung\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bremsstrahlung</strong></a> (braking radiation), while those forced into circular paths by magnetic fields emit <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/synchrotron-radiation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Synchrotron Radiation</strong></a>, providing a vital diagnostic tool in astrophysics for studying supernovae and active galactic nuclei.</p>','Electromagnetic Radiation is the process by which accelerating electric charges emit energy in the form of self-propagating waves of electric and magnetic fields. In university-level electrodynamics, radiation is the ultimate dynamical consequence of Maxwell\'s equations, revealing how matter and fields interact to transport information and energy across the universe at the speed of light. The most fundamental result in radiation theory is that a stationary or constant-velocity charge does not radiate.','Electromagnetic Radiation is the process by which accelerating electric charges emit energy in the form of self-propagating waves of electric and magnetic fields. In university-level electrodynamics, radiation is the ultimate dynamical consequence of Maxwell\'s equations, revealing how matter and fields interact to transport information and energy across the universe at the speed of light. The most fundamental result in radiation theory is that a stationary or constant-velocity charge does not radiate.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.602ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.391ex\" height=\"5.018ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1509.9 5476.9 2217.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\" d=\"M287 628Q287 635 230 637Q206 637 199 638T192 648Q192 649 194 659Q200 679 203 681T397 683Q587 682 600 680Q664 669 707 631T751 530Q751 453 685 389Q616 321 507 303Q500 302 402 301H307L277 182Q247 66 247 59Q247 55 248 54T255 50T272 48T305 46H336Q342 37 342 35Q342 19 335 5Q330 0 319 0Q316 0 282 1T182 2Q120 2 87 2T51 1Q33 1 33 11Q33 13 36 25Q40 41 44 43T67 46Q94 46 127 49Q141 52 146 61Q149 65 218 339T287 628ZM645 554Q645 567 643 575T634 597T609 619T560 635Q553 636 480 637Q463 637 445 637T416 636T404 636Q391 635 386 627Q384 621 367 550T332 412T314 344Q314 342 395 342H407H430Q542 342 590 392Q617 419 631 471T645 554Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 13