06 144Q506 138 501 117T481 63T449 13Q436 0 417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM351 328Q351 334 346 350T323 385T277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q217 26 254 59T298 110Q300 114 325 217T351 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-36\" d=\"M42 313Q42 476 123 571T303 666Q372 666 402 630T432 550Q432 525 418 510T379 495Q356 495 341 509T326 548Q326 592 373 601Q351 623 311 626Q240 626 194 566Q147 500 147 364L148 360Q153 366 156 373Q197 433 263 433H267Q313 433 348 414Q372 400 396 374T435 317Q456 268 456 210V192Q456 169 451 149Q440 90 387 34T253 -22Q225 -22 199 -14T143 16T92 75T56 172T42 313ZM257 397Q227 397 205 380T171 335T154 278T148 216Q148 133 160 97T198 39Q222 21 251 21Q302 21 329 59Q342 77 347 104T352 209Q352 289 347 316T329 361Q302 397 257 397Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70B\" d=\"M132 -11Q98 -11 98 22V33L111 61Q186 219 220 334L228 358H196Q158 358 142 355T103 336Q92 329 81 318T62 297T53 285Q51 284 38 284Q19 284 19 294Q19 300 38 329T93 391T164 429Q171 431 389 431Q549 431 553 430Q573 423 573 402Q573 371 541 360Q535 358 472 358H408L405 341Q393 269 393 222Q393 170 402 129T421 65T431 37Q431 20 417 5T381 -10Q370 -10 363 -7T347 17T331 77Q330 86 330 121Q330 170 339 226T357 318T367 358H269L268 354Q268 351 249 275T206 114T175 17Q164 -11 132 -11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\" d=\"M34 159Q34 268 120 355T306 442Q362 442 394 418T427 355Q427 326 408 306T360 285Q341 285 330 295T319 325T330 359T352 380T366 386H367Q367 388 361 392T340 400T306 404Q276 404 249 390Q228 381 206 359Q162 315 142 235T121 119Q121 73 147 50Q169 26 205 26H209Q321 26 394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 83T391 55T346 25T282 0T202 -11Q127 -11 81 37T34 159Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1028.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(2084.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(636,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1039.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(543.7,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1986.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(562,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(944.7,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"36\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-36\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1070,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"3152.4\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-larmor-formula-class-subtopic-link-larmor-formula-a-aa594928\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-retarded-time-class-subtopic-link-retarded-time-a-6386640b\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-lienard-wiechert-class-subtopic-link-li-nard-wiechert-potential-a-0b518e8a\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-relativistic-larmor-class-subtopic-link-relativistic-larmor-a-7cffa0c5\"]'),
(2009923,'magnetization','amperes-law','Magnetization and Magnetic Materials','<p>Magnetization (\\( \\mathbf{M} \\)) is a vector field that describes the density of permanent or induced magnetic dipole moments in a material. In university-level electrodynamics, it provides the bridge between the microscopic world of atomic currents and the macroscopic behavior of magnetic fields in matter, revealing how materials respond to and modify their electromagnetic environment.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Macroscopic Definition and Units</h3>\n                    <p>The magnetization is defined as the magnetic dipole moment per unit volume. For a collection of microscopic dipoles \\( \\mathbf{m}_i \\), the magnetization is given by the limit \\( \\mathbf{M} = \\lim_{\\Delta V \\to 0} \\frac{1}{\\Delta V} \\sum \\mathbf{m}_i \\). The units of \\( \\mathbf{M} \\) are Amperes per meter (\\( \\text{A/m} \\)), the same as the auxiliary field \\( \\mathbf{H} \\). This vector field captures the \"aligned state\" of the material\'s internal magnetic structure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Microscopic Origins: Spin and Orbital Motion</h3>\n                    <p>At the atomic scale, magnetization arises from two primary sources: the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/orbital-angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Orbital Angular Momentum</strong></a> of electrons as they move around the nucleus, and the <strong>Intrinsic Spin</strong> of the electrons. While each electron acts as a tiny current loop, in most materials, these moments cancel out. Magnetization occurs when an external field or exchange interactions force these atomic dipoles into a state of partial or total alignment.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Bound Currents and the Vector Potential</h3>\n                    <p>A magnetized object can be replaced by an equivalent distribution of <strong>Bound Currents</strong>. Using the vector potential \\( \\mathbf{A} \\), it can be shown that a non-uniform magnetization produces a <strong>Bound Volume Current</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{J}_b = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{M} \\)), while a discontinuity at the surface produces a <strong>Bound Surface Current</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{K}_b = \\mathbf{M} \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\)). These currents are not due to the flow of free electrons but represent the cumulative effect of the internal atomic \"loops\" that do not cancel at the boundaries.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Auxiliary Field (\\( \\mathbf{H} \\))</h3>\n                    <p>To simplify the analysis of fields in matter, we define the auxiliary field \\( \\mathbf{H} \\), which relates to the fundamental induction \\( \\mathbf{B} \\) and the magnetization \\( \\mathbf{M} \\) via:\n                    \\[ \\mathbf{H} \\equiv \\frac{\\mathbf{B}}{\\mu_0} - \\mathbf{M} \\]\n                    <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/amperes-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ampère\'s Law</strong></a> in matter is then expressed solely in terms of <strong>Free Currents</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{J}_f \\)): \\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{J}_f \\). This allows physicists to solve for the field by considering only the currents they can directly control, with the material\'s response encapsulated in the relationship between \\( \\mathbf{B} \\) and \\( \\mathbf{H} \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Linear Media and Susceptibility</h3>\n                    <p>In many materials, the magnetization is proportional to the applied field: \\( \\mathbf{M} = \\chi_m \\mathbf{H} \\), where \\( \\chi_m \\) is the <strong>Magnetic Susceptibility</strong>. For such linear media, the relation between \\( \\mathbf{B} \\) and \\( \\mathbf{H} \\) is \\( \\mathbf{B} = \\mu \\mathbf{H} \\), where \\( \\mu = \\mu_0(1 + \\chi_m) \\) is the <strong>Permeability</strong>. This linearity allows for the straightforward application of boundary conditions at interfaces between different magnetic materials.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Classification of Magnetism: Hysteresis and Domains</h3>\n                    <p>Magnetic materials are classified by their susceptibility:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Diamagnetism:</strong> Weak, negative \\( \\chi_m \\); the material creates a field opposing the external one (a universal quantum effect).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Paramagnetism:</strong> Weak, positive \\( \\chi_m \\); atomic dipoles align with the external field.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Ferromagnetism:</strong> Large, non-linear response where dipoles align in <strong>Magnetic Domains</strong>. These materials exhibit <strong>Hysteresis</strong>, where the state of the material depends on its magnetic history, allowing for the creation of permanent magnets.</li>\n                        </ul></p>','Magnetization is a vector field that describes the density of permanent or induced magnetic dipole moments in a material. In university-level electrodynamics, it provides the bridge between the microscopic world of atomic currents and the macroscopic behavior of magnetic fields in matter, revealing how materials respond to and modify their electromagnetic environment. The magnetization is defined as the magnetic dipole moment per unit volume.','Magnetization (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.471ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 1092 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\" d=\"M314 0Q296 3 181 3T48 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H305Q316 679 323 667Q330 653 434 414L546 157L658 414Q766 662 773 674Q778 681 788 686H1052V624H944V62H1052V0H1040Q1016 3 874 3T708 0H696V62H804V341L803 618L786 580Q770 543 735 462T671 315Q540 13 536 9Q528 1 507 1Q485 1 477 9Q472 14 408 162T281 457T217 603Q215 603 215 334V62H323V0H314Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is a vector field that describes the density of permanent or induced magnetic dipole moments in a material. In university-level electrodynamics, it provides the bridge between the microscopic world of atomic currents and the macroscopic behavior of magnetic fields in matter, revealing how materials respond to and modify their electromagnetic environment. The magnetization is defined as the magnetic dipole moment per unit volume.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.041ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13.637ex\" height=\"5.122ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1362 6027.6 2264\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\" d=\"M400 0Q376 3 226 3Q75 3 51 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 226 683Q376 683 400 686H412V624H304V388H595V624H487V686H499Q523 683 673 683Q824 683 848 686H860V624H752V62H860V0H848Q824 3 674 3Q523 3 499 0H487V62H595V326H304V62H412V0H400Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2261\" d=\"M56 444Q56 457 70 464H707Q722 456 722 444Q722 430 706 424H72Q56 429 56 444ZM56 237T56 250T70 270H707Q722 262 722 250T707 230H70Q56 237 56 250ZM56 56Q56 71 72 76H706Q722 70 722 56Q722 44 707 36H70Q56 43 56 56Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\" d=\"M720 510Q720 476 704 448T665 404T619 377T580 362L564 359L583 356Q602 353 632 342T690 312Q712 292 725 276Q752 235 752 189V183Q752 160 741 125Q698 18 547 2Q543 1 288 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H264H409Q502 686 542 681T624 655Q720 607 720 510ZM563 513Q563 553 548 578T518 611T486 622Q479 624 385 624H293V382H375Q458 383 467 385Q563 405 563 513ZM590 192Q590 307 505 329Q504 330 503 330L398 331H293V62H391H400H444Q496 62 528 75T580 131Q590 155 590 192Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\" d=\"M84 237T84 250T98 270H679Q694 262 694 250T679 230H98Q84 237 84 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\" d=\"M314 0Q296 3 181 3T48 0H39V62H147V624H39V686H305Q316 679 323 667Q330 653 434 414L546 157L658 414Q766 662 773 674Q778 681 788 686H1052V624H944V62H1052V0H1040Q1016 3 874 3T708 0H696V62H804V341L803 618L786 580Q770 543 735 462T671 315Q540 13 536 9Q528 1 507 1Q485 1 477 9Q472 14 408 162T281 457T217 603Q215 603 215 334V62H323V0H314Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D407\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D407\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1177.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2261\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2261\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(2233.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(330.8,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D401\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(636,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1239.6\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3935.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4935.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"magnetization-8d225957\",\"volume-bound-current-5986e0ce\",\"auxiliary-field-1a047c8d\",\"constitutive-relation-7fbd8e08\"]'),
(2009924,'maxwells-correction','electromagnetism','Maxwell\'s Correction and Displacement Current','<p>Maxwell\'s Correction, formally known as the addition of the **Displacement Current** to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/amperes-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ampère\'s Law</strong></a>, is theoretical breakthrough that unified electricity and magnetism into a single, consistent field theory. By identifying a critical mathematical inconsistency in the laws of magnetostatics, James Clerk Maxwell proved that a changing electric field must produce a magnetic field, just as moving charges do, a realization that predicted the existence of light as an electromagnetic wave.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Inconsistency of Ampère\'s Law</h3>\n                    <p>In its original form, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/amperes-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ampère\'s Law</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0 \\mathbf{J} \\)) was only valid for steady currents. A fundamental principle of vector calculus is that the divergence of a curl is always zero (\\( \\nabla \\cdot (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B}) = 0 \\)). This implies that the divergence of the current density must also be zero (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{J} = 0 \\)). However, the <strong>Continuity Equation</strong> for charge conservation states that \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{J} = -\\partial \\rho / \\partial t \\). For time-varying charge distributions, Ampère\'s original law was mathematically impossible.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Capacitor Paradox</h3>\n                    <p>The contradiction is best illustrated by a charging capacitor. During the charging process, a conduction current \\( I \\) flows through the wires, creating a magnetic field. However, in the gap between the plates, no physical charges move (\\( \\mathbf{J} = 0 \\)). According to Ampère\'s original law, there should be no magnetic field in the gap. Yet, experiments show a magnetic field continuous with the one outside. This \"gap\" in theory required a new form of \"current\" to bridge the space between the plates.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Defining the Displacement Current</h3>\n                    <p>Maxwell resolved the paradox by introducing the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/displacement-current\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Displacement Current Density</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{J}_d \\)). Using <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = \\rho / \\varepsilon_0 \\)), he substituted the charge density into the continuity equation and found that the quantity \\( \\varepsilon_0 \\partial \\mathbf{E} / \\partial t \\) has the same divergence as the conduction current. He defined this term as the displacement current, representing the magnetic effect of a time-varying electric flux.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Ampère-Maxwell Law</h3>\n                    <p>By adding the displacement current to the source term, Maxwell arrived at the generalized equation:\n                    \\[ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0 \\left( \\mathbf{J} + \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial t} \\right) \\]\n                    This complete law asserts that there are two ways to generate a magnetic field: by the movement of physical charges (conduction current) or by the variation of an electric field (displacement current). This unification ensures that the magnetic field is well-defined and continuous in all physical scenarios, including vacuum propagation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Theoretical Symmetry and EM Waves</h3>\n                    <p>Maxwell\'s correction introduced a profound symmetry to electrodynamics. Just as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a> shows that a changing magnetic field induces an electric field (\\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{E} = -\\partial \\mathbf{B}/\\partial t \\)), Maxwell showed the reciprocal: a changing electric field induces a magnetic field. This mutual induction allows fields to self-propagate through empty space, even in the absence of charges or currents, revealing light to be an <strong>Electromagnetic Wave</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Relativistic Completion and the 4-Current</h3>\n                    <p>In the language of Special Relativity, Maxwell\'s correction is not an \"addition\" but a natural consequence of the 4-dimensional geometry of spacetime. The conduction current and charge density are unified into the <strong>Charge-Current 4-Vector</strong> (\\( J^\\mu \\)). The Ampère-Maxwell law and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law</strong></a> then collapse into a single covariant expression: \\( \\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu} = \\mu_0 J^\\nu \\). This demonstrates that the displacement current is essential for the laws of electromagnetism to remainvariant across all inertial reference frames.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Maxwell\'s Correction and Displacement Current is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/displacement-current-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Displacement Current Density ()</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/charge-current-4-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Charge-Current 4-Vector ()</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/covariant-inhomogeneous-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Covariant Inhomogeneous Maxwell Equation</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-current-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Electromagnetic Current</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','Maxwell\'s Correction, formally known as the addition of the **Displacement Current** to Ampère\'s Law, is theoretical breakthrough that unified electricity and magnetism into a single, consistent field theory. By identifying a critical mathematical inconsistency in the laws of magnetostatics, James Clerk Maxwell proved that a changing electric field must produce a magnetic field, just as moving charges do, a realization that predicted the existence of light as an electromagnetic wave. In its original form, Ampère\'s Law was only valid for steady currents.','Maxwell\'s Correction, formally known as the addition of the **Displacement Current** to Ampère\'s Law, is theoretical breakthrough that unified electricity and magnetism into a single, consistent field theory. By identifying a critical mathematical inconsistency in the laws of magnetostatics, James Clerk Maxwell proved that a changing electric field must produce a magnetic field, just as moving charges do, a realization that predicted the existence of light as an electromagnetic wave. In its original form, Ampère\'s Law (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13.214ex\" height=\"2.041ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 5840.6 902\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-D7\" d=\"M630 29Q630 9 609 9Q604 9 587 25T493 118L389 222L284 117Q178 13 175 11Q171 9 168 9Q160 9 154 15T147 29Q147 36 161 51T255 146L359 250L255 354Q174 435 161 449T147 471Q147 480 153 485T168 490Q173 490 175 489Q178 487 284 383L389 278L493 382Q570 459 587 475T609 491Q630 491 630 471Q630 464 620 453T522 355L418 250L522 145Q606 61 618 48T630 29Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D401\" d=\"M720 510Q720 476 704 448T665 404T619 377T580 362L564 359L583 356Q602 353 632 342T690 312Q712 292 725 276Q752 235 752 189