lor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(683.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(736,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(343,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1373\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2571.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3571.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4400.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4900.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(5733.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6340.9,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8038.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9094.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9872.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(10705.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11430.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(12430.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(13033.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(866,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(14303.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(15132.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(16132.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D41F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D41F\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"friction-source-f3ff6185\",\"internal-force-map-f07e58d8\",\"flow-regime-4e7283f1\",\"heat-source-b5448197\"]'),
(2009945,'astrophysical-fluid-dynamics','fluids-nonlinear','Fluid Dynamics in the Cosmos','<p>Fluid Dynamics is the study of the motion of liquids and gases, providing the mathematical framework for understanding the behavior of stellar interiors, interstellar clouds, and the large-scale structure of the universe. In university-level astrophysics, most celestial matter is treated as a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/astrophysical-fluids\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fluid</strong></a>—a continuous medium where the collective interactions of particles are described by macroscopic variables like pressure, density, and velocity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Governing Equations: Euler and Navier-Stokes</h3>\n                    <p>The dynamics of astrophysical fluids are governed by the conservation of mass, momentum, and energy. For an ideal (non-viscous) fluid, these are the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/euler-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Euler Equations</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{v}}{\\partial t} + (\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\nabla) \\mathbf{v} = -\\frac{1}{\\rho} \\nabla P - \\nabla \\Phi \\]\n                    Where \\( \\Phi \\) is the gravitational potential. In environments where viscosity or thermal conduction is significant (such as the boundaries of accretion disks), the more complex <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/navier-stokes-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Navier-Stokes Equations</strong></a> are required to account for the internal friction of the gas.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Hydrostatic Equilibrium and Stability</h3>\n                    <p>The most fundamental application of fluid dynamics in astrophysics is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hydrostatic-equilibrium\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hydrostatic Equilibrium</strong></a>. This state occurs when the inward pull of gravity is perfectly balanced by the outward pressure gradient:\n                    \\[ \\frac{dP}{dr} = -\\rho(r) g(r) \\]\n                    If this balance is disturbed, the fluid will undergo <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dynamical-collapse\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Dynamical Collapse</strong></a> (as in star formation) or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/explosive-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Explosive Expansion</strong></a> (as in a supernova). Fluid stability is analyzed using the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schwarzschild-criterion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schwarzschild Criterion</strong></a>, which determines if a fluid element will oscillate (stable) or rise/sink (unstable convection) when displaced.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Accretion Disks and Angular Momentum</h3>\n                    <p>When gas falls toward a compact object (like a black hole or protostar), its conservation of angular momentum forces it into a flat, rotating <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/accretion-disk\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Accretion Disk</strong></a>. In these disks, fluid friction (viscosity) converts gravitational energy into heat and allows matter to spiral inward while transporting angular momentum outward. This process is the most efficient energy conversion mechanism in the universe, powering active galactic nuclei and X-ray binaries.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Magnetohydrodynamics (MHD)</h3>\n                    <p>Astrophysical fluids are often ionized (plasmas), meaning they conduct electricity. The study of these fluids in the presence of magnetic fields is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mhd-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Magnetohydrodynamics (MHD)</strong></a>. Magnetic fields can be \"frozen\" into the fluid, moving with the gas, or they can exert <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lorentz-forces\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lorentz Forces</strong></a> that drive powerful phenomena like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stellar-jets\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Stellar Jets</strong></a> and the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/solar-dynamo\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Solar Dynamo</strong></a>. MHD is essential for explaining how galaxies maintain their magnetic fields over billions of years.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Supersonic Flows and Shock Waves</h3>\n                    <p>Many astrophysical events involve velocities exceeding the local speed of sound. When a supersonic flow encounters an obstacle or another fluid, it forms a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/shock-wave\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Shock Wave</strong></a>—a thin region of extreme pressure and temperature increase. Supernova remnants are the primary examples of these shocks, where the kinetic energy of the explosion is transferred to the interstellar medium, heating it to millions of degrees and triggering new rounds of star formation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Jeans Instability and Structure Formation</h3>\n                    <p>The large-scale structure of the universe began with the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/jeans-instability\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Jeans Instability</strong></a>. If a fluid cloud is massive enough, its internal pressure cannot support it against its own gravity. The critical mass required for collapse is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/jeans-mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Jeans Mass</strong></a> (\\( M_J \\)):\n                    \\[ M_J \\propto \\left( \\frac{T}{\\rho^{1/3}} \\right)^{3/2} \\]\n                    This fluid-dynamical threshold determines the size of the first stars and the minimum scale of galaxy clusters, linking the microscopic thermodynamics of gas to the global architecture of the cosmos.</p>','Fluid Dynamics is the study of the motion of liquids and gases, providing the mathematical framework for understanding the behavior of stellar interiors, interstellar clouds, and the large-scale structure of the universe. In university-level astrophysics, most celestial matter is treated as a Fluid—a continuous medium where the collective interactions of particles are described by macroscopic variables like pressure, density, and velocity. The dynamics of astrophysical fluids are governed by the conservation of mass, momentum, and energy.','Fluid Dynamics is the study of the motion of liquids and gases, providing the mathematical framework for understanding the behavior of stellar interiors, interstellar clouds, and the large-scale structure of the universe. In university-level astrophysics, most celestial matter is treated as a Fluid—a continuous medium where the collective interactions of particles are described by macroscopic variables like pressure, density, and velocity. The dynamics of astrophysical fluids are governed by the conservation of mass, momentum, and energy.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.041ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"31.251ex\" height=\"5.188ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1391 13812.9 2293\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\" d=\"M202 508Q179 508 169 520T158 547Q158 557 164 577T185 624T230 675T301 710L333 715H345Q378 715 384 714Q447 703 489 661T549 568T566 457Q566 362 519 240T402 53Q321 -22 223 -22Q123 -22 73 56Q42 102 42 148V159Q42 276 129 370T322 465Q383 465 414 434T455 367L458 378Q478 461 478 515Q478 603 437 639T344 676Q266 676 223 612Q264 606 264 572Q264 547 246 528T202 508ZM430 306Q430 372 401 400T333 428Q270 428 222 382Q197 354 183 323T150 221Q132 149 132 116Q132 21 232 21Q244 21 250 22Q327 35 374 112Q389 137 409 196T430 306Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\" d=\"M401 444Q413 441 495 441Q568 441 574 444H580V382H510L409 156Q348 18 339 6Q331 -4 320 -4Q318 -4 313 -4T303 -3H288Q273 -3 264 12T221 102Q206 135 197 156L96 382H26V444H34Q49 441 145 441Q252 441 270 444H279V382H231L284 264Q335 149 338 149Q338 150 389 264T442 381Q442 382 418 382H394V444H401Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522