7 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\" d=\"M34 1438Q34 1446 37 1448T50 1450H56H71Q73 1448 99 1423T144 1380T198 1319T260 1238T323 1137T385 1013T440 864T485 688T514 485T526 251Q526 134 519 53Q472 -519 162 -860Q139 -885 119 -904T86 -936T71 -949H56Q43 -949 39 -947T34 -937Q88 -883 140 -813Q428 -430 428 251Q428 453 402 628T338 922T245 1146T145 1309T46 1425Q44 1427 42 1429T39 1433T36 1436L34 1438Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(884.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(1940.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D410\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D410\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(471.5,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1584\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4042.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(5098.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(378,676)\"><use data-c=\"210F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><rect width=\"1078\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(6416.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"49\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-49\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(361,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(7776.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(736,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(636.5,-686)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><rect width=\"1684\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2660,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"bohmian-velocity-26a97dcb\",\"von-neumann-process-e6d8db90\",\"decoherence-link-f31a1794\",\"qbism-update-db83ac46\"]'),
(2009962,'structure-of-spacetime','philosophy-of-physics','The Structure and Ontology of Spacetime','<p>The structure of spacetime examines whether space and time are fundamental \"things\" that exist independently of matter (Substantivalism), or whether they are simply a collection of relationships between physical objects (Relationism). In university-level philosophy of physics, this discourse explores how the mathematical formalisms of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, Quantum Field Theory, and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> constrain our metaphysical commitments to the stage upon which the universe performs.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Classical Debate: Substantivalism vs. Relationism</h3>\n                    <p>The conflict between <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/isaac-newton-philosophy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Isaac Newton</strong></a> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gottfried-leibniz\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gottfried Leibniz</strong></a> established the two primary ontological poles. Newton argued for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/absolute-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Absolute Space</strong></a> as a \"container,\" using his <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/rotating-bucket\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Rotating Bucket</strong></a> experiment to show that centrifugal forces depend on rotation relative to space itself, not just the bucket. Leibniz countered with the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/identity-of-indiscernibles\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of the Identity of Indiscernibles</strong></a>, arguing that if the entire universe were shifted by a constant distance, the resulting state would be physically identical to the original; therefore, absolute position is a mathematical fiction and space is merely an \"order of coexistence.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hole-argument-philosophy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Hole Argument</strong></a> and Diffeomorphism Invariance</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the debate was revitalized by the <strong>Hole Argument</strong>. Because theory is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/diffeomorphism-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Diffeomorphism Invariant</strong></a>, one can mathematically remap the metric field (\\( g_{\\mu\\nu} \\)) within a coordinate \"hole\" without changing the physical observations outside. If one assumes <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/manifold-substantivalism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Manifold Substantivalism</strong></a>—that spacetime points have a primitive identity independent of the field—theory becomes non-deterministic. To preserve physical determinism, one must accept <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/leibniz-equivalence\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Leibniz Equivalence</strong></a>: mathematically distinct coordinate representations of the same field must represent the same physical state.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/structural-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Structural Realism</strong></a> and Background Independence</h3>\n                    <p>To resolve the paradoxes of the manifold, many modern philosophers adopt <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-structural-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spacetime Structural Realism</strong></a>. This view posits that neither the points nor the objects are fundamental; rather, reality resides in the <strong>Structure</strong> of the metric field and its symmetries. This aligns with the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/background-independence\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Background Independence</strong></a> of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, where spacetime is not a fixed stage but a dynamic player that interacts with matter, evolving according to the Einstein field equations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Emergent Spacetime: \"It from Qubit\"</h3>\n                    <p>The most radical modern shift is the hypothesis that spacetime is not fundamental but <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/emergent-spacetime\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Emergent</strong></a>, similar to how fluid dynamics emerges from molecular motion. Research in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ads-cft\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>AdS/CFT Correspondence</strong></a> suggests that geometry is constructed from <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Entanglement</strong></a>. This quantified by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ryu-takayanagi-formula\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ryu-Takayanagi Formula</strong></a>, which relates entanglement entropy in a boundary quantum system to the area of a minimal surface in the higher-dimensional bulk spacetime. In this view, if the entanglement were removed, the spatial manifold would literally pull apart.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Causal Structure and the Light Cone</h3>\n                    <p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-structure\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Structure</strong></a> of the universe is defined by the set of all possible paths that information can take. In university-level theory, this encoded in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conformal-structure\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conformal Structure</strong></a> of the metric, which determines the orientation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/light-cones\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Light Cones</strong></a> at every point. This structure is more fundamental than distance or time measurements; it defines the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the universe, ensuring that \"cause\" always precedes \"effect\" and providing a rigid framework for an otherwise relative and flexible spacetime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Discrete Structure: Causal Sets</h3>\n                    <p>A leading candidate for quantum gravity, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/causal-set-theory-cst\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Set Theory</strong></a>, proposes that spacetime is not a continuous manifold but a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/discrete-set\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Discrete Set</strong></a> of elementary events. In this view, the \"atoms of space\" are organized by a partial ordering that represents the causal relationship between events. This approach resolves the mathematical infinities of the continuum and suggests that the smooth spacetime we perceive is a macroscopic approximation of a deeper, finite, and fundamentally interconnected structure.</p>','The structure of spacetime examines whether space and time are fundamental \"things\" that exist independently of matter (Substantivalism), or whether they are simply a collection of relationships between physical objects (Relationism). In university-level philosophy of physics, this discourse explores how the mathematical formalisms of General Relativity, Quantum Field Theory, and Quantum Gravity constrain our metaphysical commitments to the stage upon which the universe performs. The conflict between Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz established the two primary ontological poles.','The structure of spacetime examines whether space and time are fundamental \"things\" that exist independently of matter (Substantivalism), or whether they are simply a collection of relationships between physical objects (Relationism). In university-level philosophy of physics, this discourse explores how the mathematical formalisms of General Relativity, Quantum Field Theory, and Quantum Gravity constrain our metaphysical commitments to the stage upon which the universe performs. The conflict between Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz established the two primary ontological poles.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.08ex\" height=\"1.685ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1361.2 744.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D454\" d=\"M311 43Q296 30 267 15T206 0Q143 0 105 45T66 160Q66 265 143 353T314 442Q361 442 401 394L404 398Q406 401 409 404T418 412T431 419T447 422Q461 422 470 413T480 394Q480 379 423 152T363 -80Q345 -134 286 -169T151 -205Q10 -205 10 -137Q10 -111 28 -91T74 -71Q89 -71 102 -80T116 -111Q116 -121 114 -130T107 -144T99 -154T92 -162L90 -164H91Q101 -167 151 -167Q189 -167 211 -155Q234 -144 254 -122T282 -75Q288 -56 298 -13Q311 35 311 43ZM384 328L380 339Q377 350 375 354T369 368T359 382T346 393T328 402T306 405Q262 405 221 352Q191 313 171 233T151 117Q151 38 213 38Q269 38 323 108L331 118L384 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\" d=\"M74 431Q75 431 146 436T219 442Q231 442 231 434Q231 428 185 241L137 51H140L150 55Q161 59 177 67T214 86T261 119T312 165Q410 264 445 394Q458 442 496 442Q509 442 519 434T530 411Q530 390 516 352T469 262T388 162T267 70T106 5Q81 -2 71 -2Q66 -2 59 -1T51 1Q45 5 45 11Q45 13 88 188L132 364Q133 377 125 380T86 385H65Q59 391 59 393T61 412Q65 431 74 431Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D454\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D454\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(510,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(603,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"dynamical-spacetime-18abdd17\",\"holographic-link-6a4a4f2e\",\"causal-metric-f3ec654e\",\"variational-foundation-549ad2f4\"]'),
(2009963,'arrow-of-time','statistical-mechanics-philosophy','The Arrow of Time','<p>The Arrow of Time is the philosophical and physical concept describing the \'one-way\' direction or asymmetry of time. In university-level philosophy of physics, the central problem is why time appears to flow from the past to the future when the underlying microscopic laws of nature are almost entirely <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/time-symmetric-laws\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Time-Symmetric</strong></a>, with the notable exception of <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/weak-interaction-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Weak Interaction and T-Violation</a></strong>. The resolution of this paradox is the primary goal of <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong>, which links the macroscopic flow of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> to the unique initial conditions of the universe.</p><h3>1. The Thermodynamic Arrow</h3><p>The most prominent arrow is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamic-arrow\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Thermodynamic Arrow of Time</strong></a>, defined by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamics-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Second Law of Thermodynamics</strong></a>. It states that in an isolated system, entropy always increases. In university-level theory, this is not a law of force, but a law of probability: there are simply many more disordered microstates than ordered ones. This ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the manifold always trend toward <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/equilibrium\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Equilibrium</strong></a>, creating a distinguished direction for all physical processes.</p><h3>2. The Past Hypothesis and Cosmology</h3><p>The ultimate source of the arrow of time is found in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/past-hypothesis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Past Hypothesis</strong></a>. It posits that the universe began in an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/extremely-low-entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Extremely Low-Entropy</strong></a> state. This <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-fact\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Fact</strong></a> provides the necessary \'fuel\' for the universe to run \'downhill\' toward higher entropy. Without this ordered beginning, the manifold would be time-symmetric at all scales, and the concept of an irreversible \'History\' would be physically impossible, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the Big Bang\'s unique geometry and the specific <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/initial-conditions-cosmology\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Initial Conditions of the Universe</a></strong>.</p><h3>3. The Informational Arrow</h3><p>Modern physics increasingly links the arrow of time to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a>. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/irreversibility-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Irreversibility</strong></a> arises because information about the initial microstate becomes scrambled into complex, unobservable multi-particle correlations. According to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/landauers-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Landauer\'s Principle</strong></a>, the act of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/erasing-information\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Erasing Information</strong></a> is the only way to reset the system, which necessarily generates heat and entropy. This proves that the \'flow\' of time is fundamentally bound to the causal structure of information processing within the vacuum.</p><h3>4. Quantum Decoherence and Subjective Time</h3><p>In quantum mechanics, the arrow of time is reinforced by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-interference\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Decoherence</strong></a>. When a quantum system interacts with its environment, its internal phase information leaks out, making the process practically irreversible. This transition from \'Possible\' to \'Actual\' defines our subjective experience of time, defining <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/psychological-arrow\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Psychological Arrow of Time</a></strong>. This investigation pushes the boundaries of our understanding of <strong>Physical Causality</strong>, asking whether the arrow is a fundamental property of the <strong>Block Universe</strong> or an effect of our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/classical-ignorance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Classical Ignorance</strong></a>.</p><h3>5. The Radiative Arrow</h3><p>Beyond thermodynamics, the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electromagnetic-arrow\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Electromagnetic Arrow of Time</a></strong> is observed in the exclusive prevalence of <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/retarded-waves\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Retarded Waves and Potentials</a></strong>, ensuring that radiation always follows causation from the past to the future.</p>','The Arrow of Time is the philosophical and physical concept describing the \'one-way\' direction or asymmetry of time. In university-level philosophy of physics, the central problem is why time appears to flow from the past to the future when the underlying microscopic laws of nature are almost entirely Time-Symmetric, with the notable exception of Weak Interaction and T-Violation. The resolution of this paradox is the primary goal of Statistical Mechanics, which links the macroscopic flow of Entropy to the unique initial conditions of the universe.','The Arrow of Time is the philosophical and physical concept describing the \'one-way\' direction or asymmetry of time. In university-level philosophy of physics, the central problem is why time appears to flow from the past to the future when the underlying microscopic laws of nature are almost entirely Time-Symmetric, with the notable exception of Weak Interaction and T-Violation. The resolution of this paradox is the primary goal of Statistical Mechanics, which links the macroscopic flow of Entropy to the unique initial conditions of the universe.','','[]','[]','[\"arrow-of-time-e01dc7cd\",\"statistical-improbability-bf6fba95\"]'),
(2009964,'physical-causality','philosophy-of-physics','Physical Causality','<p>Physical Causality is the foundational principle asserting that every event in the universe is determined by preceding events and conditions according to consistent physical laws. In university-level philosophy of physics, this concept transcends the simple \'cause-and-effect\' intuition, exploring the rigorous mathematical requirements for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/determinism-and-locality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Determinism</strong></a> and the propagation of information within the constraints of the spacetime manifold. It anchors our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> by positing that the causal structure of the world is a real, discoverable feature of the physical state.</p><h3>1. The Principle of Causal Determinism</h3><p>The classical view of causality, epitomized by Newtonian mechanics, is strictly deterministic. If the positions and momenta of all particles are known at an instant, the laws of physics uniquely determine all future states. This \'Clockwork Universe\' implies a rigid causal chain where the state of the manifold at time \\( t \\) is the sufficient and necessary reason for its state at \\( t + dt \\). Philosophers analyze this through the lens of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/laplaces-demon\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Laplace\'s Demon</strong></a>, questioning whether the deterministic nature of differential equations leaves room for objective chance or if all randomness is merely epistemic.</p><h3>2. Relativistic Causality and Light Cones</h3><p>General and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/special-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Special Relativity</strong></a> introduce a geometric constraint on causality: the <strong>Speed of Light (\\( c \\))</strong>. For one event to causally influence another, information must travel between them at or below \\( c \\). This defines the <strong>Light-Cone Structure</strong> of spacetime. An event can only be caused by events in its past light cone and can only influence events in its future light cone. This \'<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a>\' ensures that <strong>Physical Causality</strong> respects the order of events for all inertial observers, preventing temporal paradoxes.</p><h3>3. Quantum Mechanics and Non-Locality</h3><p>Quantum theory challenges classical causal intuition through the phenomenon of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Entanglement</strong></a>. The violation of <strong>Bell Inequalities</strong> suggests that certain correlations between particles cannot be explained by local \'hidden variables\' or sub-luminal signals. This leads to the debate over <strong>Non-Local Causality</strong>. While information cannot be sent faster than light (the No-Communication Theorem), the \'Influences\' in a quantum measurement seem to transcend classical relativistic boundaries, forcing a re-evaluation of the <strong>Physical Link</strong> between distant regions of space.</p><h3>4. Causal Realism and Structural Realism</h3><p>A central debate in the philosophy of science is whether causality is a fundamental constituent of the world (<strong>Causal Realism</strong>) or if it is an emergent property of mathematical structures (<strong>Structural Realism</strong>). Realists argue that \'Causal Powers\' are intrinsic to entities like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quarks\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quarks</strong></a> and fields. Conversely, structural realists, following <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/structural-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Structural Realism</strong></a>, assert that what we call \'Causality\' is simply the persistence of mathematical symmetries and invariants across the evolution of the system.</p><h3>5. The Interventionist Account and Force</h3><p>Modern functional accounts of causality often use the <strong>Interventionist Framework</strong>. Here, \\( X \\) causes \\( Y \\) if an intervention on \\( X \\) (a change in its state) results in a change in \\( Y \\). In physics, this is formalized through the concept of a <strong>Physical Force</strong> or interaction. By modifying the source terms in a field equation, we can observe the propagation of a \'Causal Wave\' through the vacuum. This allows for a rigorous definition of <strong>Physical Causality</strong> that is compatible with both classical field theory and the path-integral formulation of quantum mechanics.</p><h3>6. The Arrow of Causality and Entropy</h3><p>While fundamental physical laws are often time-reversible, causality appears strictly one-way: causes always precede effects. This <strong>Arrow of Causality</strong> is deeply linked to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/arrow-of-time\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Arrow of Time</strong></a> and the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamics-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Second Law of Thermodynamics</strong></a></a>. The increase of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> provides a macroscopic directionality that aligns our perception of causal flow with the statistical evolution of the universe from a low-entropy \'Past Hypothesis\' to a high-entropy future.</p>','Physical Causality is the foundational principle asserting that every event in the universe is determined by preceding events and conditions according to consistent physical laws. In university-level philosophy of physics, this concept transcends the simple \'cause-and-effect\' intuition, exploring the rigorous mathematical requirements for Determinism and the propagation of information within the constraints of the spacetime manifold. It anchors our Scientific Realism by positing that the causal structure of the world is a real, discoverable feature of the physical state.','Physical Causality is the foundational principle asserting that every event in the universe is determined by preceding events and conditions according to consistent physical laws. In university-level philosophy of physics, this concept transcends the simple \'cause-and-effect\' intuition, exploring the rigorous mathematical requirements for Determinism and the propagation of information within the constraints of the spacetime manifold. It anchors our Scientific Realism by positing that the causal structure of the world is a real, discoverable feature of the physical state.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.817ex\" height=\"1.441ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -626 361 637\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522 166 540T173 566T179 586T187 603T197 615T211 624T229 626Q247 625 254 615T261 596Q261 589 252 549T232 470L222 433Q222 431 272 431H323Q330 424 330 420Q330 398 317 385H210L174 240Q135 80 135 68Q135 26 162 26Q197 26 230 60T283 144Q285 150 288 151T303 153H307Q322 153 322 145Q322 142 319 133Q314 117 301 95T267 48T216 6T155 -11Q125 -11 98 4T59 56Q57 64 57 83V101L92 241Q127 382 128 383Q128 385 77 385H26Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"physical-force-552de685\",\"physical-link-92b6ba2c\",\"physical-reality-eb0265b0\"]'),
(2009965,'big-bang-philosophy','philosophy-of-physics','The Philosophy of the Big Bang','<p>The Big Bang is not merely a physical event in the past, but the foundational concept that defines the origin, structure, and causal topology of the entire universe. In university-level philosophy of physics, the Big Bang raises profound questions about the nature of time, the status of physical laws at extreme scales, and the metaphysical boundary between a \"beginning of time\" and a \"beginning in time.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Origin of Spacetime and the Augustine Paradox</h3>\n                    <p>A central debate in cosmology is whether the Big Bang represents a beginning <em>of</em> time or a beginning <em>in</em> time. Following <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/st-augustine\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>St. Augustine</strong></a> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/immanuel-kant\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Immanuel Kant</strong></a>, modern theorists generally accept that spacetime itself originated at the Big Bang. In this view, asking \"what happened before the Big Bang\" is as meaningless asking \"what is north of the North Pole.\" Mathematically, this expressed through the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/singularity-theorems\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Singularity Theorems</strong></a>, which show that all world-lines in an expanding universe must terminate at a finite point in the past.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Past Hypothesis: The Source of Order</h3>\n                    <p>The universe currently exists in a state of relatively low entropy, allowing for the existence of stars and life. Philosophers like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/david-albert\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>David Albert</strong></a> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/roger-penrose\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Roger Penrose</strong></a> argue for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/past-hypothesis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Past Hypothesis</strong></a>: the requirement that the Big Bang occurred in a state of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/extremely-low-entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Extremely Low Entropy</strong></a>. This initial condition is the ultimate source of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamic-arrow\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Thermodynamic Arrow of Time</strong></a>. Without this \"extraordinarily special\" beginning, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamics-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Second Law of Thermodynamics</strong></a> would have no grip, and the universe would be a featureless, equilibrium void.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Creation ex Nihilo and Quantum Fluctuations</h3>\n                    <p>The Big Bang challenges the principle of <strong>Leibniz\'s Sufficient Reason</strong>: why is there something rather than nothing? Modern physics attempts to resolve this through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-cosmology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Cosmology</strong></a>, where the universe is viewed as a large-scale <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-fluctuation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Fluctuation</strong></a>. In models like the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/no-boundary-proposal\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hartle-Hawking No-Boundary Proposal</strong></a>, the singularity is replaced by a smooth, \"capped\" geometry where time behaves like space at the earliest moments, effectively removing the need for a discrete \"first cause.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Fine-Tuning and the Anthropic Principle</h3>\n                    <p>The physical constants of the universe (like the strength of gravity or the mass of the electron) appear to be \"fine-tuned\" to values that allow for the formation of complex structures. This leads to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/anthropic-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Anthropic Principle</strong></a>: the idea that we observe these specific values because only such a universe could produce observers. Philosophers debate whether this a deep <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/selection-effect\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Selection Effect</strong></a> or evidence for a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/multiverse\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Multiverse</strong></a>, where our Big Bang is just one of many \"bubbles\" in a vast landscape of differing physical laws.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Problem of the Initial Singularity</h3>\n                    <p>Classical <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> predicts that the Big Bang started with infinite density and curvature—a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/singularity-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Singularity</strong></a>. Philosophers and physicists view this as an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/epistemic-limit\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Epistemic Limit</strong></a>, indicating that theory is incomplete. The quest for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (such as String Theory or Loop Quantum Cosmology) aims to \"resolve\" the singularity, replacing the \"Big Bang\" with a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/big-bounce\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>\"Big Bounce\"</strong></a> or a discrete transition, thereby preserving the continuity and predictability of physical laws.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Laws vs. Contingency</h3>\n                    <p>Is the Big Bang a necessary consequence of the laws of physics, or a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/brute-fact\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Brute Fact</strong></a> of our history? This debate concerns the status of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/initial-conditions-philosophy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Initial Conditions</strong></a>. If the laws of physics are <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/humean-laws\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Humean</strong></a> (merely summaries of events), the Big Bang is just the first event. If laws are <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/necessitarian-laws\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Necessitarian</strong></a>, physicists must find a \"Master Law\" that dictates why the universe <em>had</em> to start the way it did. This investigation links the largest scales of the cosmos to the deepest questions of logical and physical necessity.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of The Philosophy of the Big Bang is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sufficient-reason\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Leibniz\'s Sufficient Reason and the Big Bang</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/geometric-origin\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Geometric Origin of Spacetime</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','The Big Bang is not merely a physical event in the past, but the foundational concept that defines the origin, structure, and causal topology of the entire universe. In university-level philosophy of physics, the Big Bang raises profound questions about the nature of time, the status of physical laws at extreme scales, and the metaphysical boundary between a \"beginning of time\" and a \"beginning in time.\" A central debate in cosmology is whether the Big Bang represents a beginning of time or a beginning in time. Following St.','The Big Bang is not merely a physical event in the past, but the foundational concept that defines the origin, structure, and causal topology of the entire universe. In university-level philosophy of physics, the Big Bang raises profound questions about the nature of time, the status of physical laws at extreme scales, and the metaphysical boundary between a \"beginning of time\" and a \"beginning in time.\"\n\n                    \n                    A central debate in cosmology is whether the Big Bang represents a beginning of time or a beginning in time. Following St.','','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-einstein-field-equations-class-subtopic-link-einstein-field-equations-a-861eb6a5\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-cosmic-expansion-class-subtopic-link-cosmic-expansion-a-1a35fb37\",\"initial-condition-08606240\",\"quantum-cosmology-291b3c03\"]'),
(2009966,'mathematical-realism','philosophy-of-physics','The Effectiveness of Mathematics','<p>The effectiveness of mathematics in physics concerns the profound and often mysterious way in which abstract mathematical structures—developed for their own sake—are found to be the exact descriptions needed for physical reality. This investigation sits at the boundary of <strong>Mathematics</strong>, <strong>Physics</strong>, and <strong>Metaphysics</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Wigner\'s \"Unreasonable Effectiveness\"</h3>\n                    <p>In 1960, physicist Eugene Wigner highlighted the \"unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics\" in the natural sciences. He noted that there is no obvious reason why the \"Book of Nature\" should be written in mathematics. The fact that a mathematician working on abstract <strong>Group Theory</strong> or <strong>Non-Euclidean Geometry</strong> can predict the physical behavior of subatomic particles or the curvature of the cosmos suggests a deep, perhaps fundamental, connection between the two realms.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-platonism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mathematical Platonism</strong></a> vs. Nominalism</h3>\n                    <p>This effectiveness fuels the debate between <strong>Platonism</strong> and <strong>Nominalism</strong>. Platonists argue that mathematical objects (like numbers and manifolds) exist in an independent, non-physical realm, and that physicists \"discover\" rather than \"invent\" them. Nominalists, by contrast, maintain that mathematics is a sophisticated human language or a shorthand for describing regularities—a tool we evolved to categorize the patterns of the natural world.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (MUH)</h3>\n                    <p>A radical modern view is the <strong>Mathematical Universe Hypothesis</strong>, proposed by Max Tegmark. This hypothesis suggests that our physical reality is not just <em>described</em> by mathematics, but <em>is</em> a mathematical structure. In this view, there is no \"stuff\"—only the relationships and symmetries defined by the math. If true, it implies that every consistent mathematical structure corresponds to a physical universe, forming the <strong>Level IV Multiverse</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Symmetry as the Final Language</h3>\n                    <p>Modern theoretical physics increasingly uses <strong>Symmetry</strong> as its primary guide. Through the study of <strong>Invariance</strong>, physicists identify what is fundamental. This has led some to propose that the \"laws of physics\" are actually the only possible ways to satisfy certain mathematical symmetries, suggesting that the structure of the universe is a logical necessity rather than a physical coincidence.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of The Effectiveness of Mathematics is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematics-ontology\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Ontology of Mathematics</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/physics-mathematization\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Mathematization of Physics</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/metaphysics-mathematical\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Metaphysics of Mathematical Reality</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/group-theory-structure\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Group Theory and Physical Structure</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-euclidean-geometry-philosophy\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Philosophy of Non-Euclidean Geometry</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-nominalism\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Mathematical Nominalism</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-universe-hypothesis\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (MUH)</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/symmetry-fundamental\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Symmetry as a Fundamental Principle</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/invariance-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Principle of Invariance</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-beauty\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Mathematical Beauty and Theoretical Truth</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/formal-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Formal Symmetry and Automorphism Groups</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/micro-macro-link\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Micro-Macro Link: Spectral Trace and Reality</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','The effectiveness of mathematics in physics concerns the profound and often mysterious way in which abstract mathematical structures—developed for their own sake—are found to be the exact descriptions needed for physical reality. This investigation sits at the boundary of Mathematics, Physics, and Metaphysics. In 1960, physicist Eugene Wigner highlighted the \"unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics\" in the natural sciences.','The effectiveness of mathematics in physics concerns the profound and often mysterious way in which abstract mathematical structures—developed for their own sake—are found to be the exact descriptions needed for physical reality. This investigation sits at the boundary of Mathematics, Physics, and Metaphysics. In 1960, physicist Eugene Wigner highlighted the \"unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics\" in the natural sciences.','','[]','[]','[\"mathematical-beauty-f2d2e607\",\"formal-symmetry-4b2c0cf9\",\"micro-macro-link-48c70830\"]'),
(2009967,'standard-model-theory','philosophy-of-physics','The Standard Model','<p>The Standard Model of particle physics is the highly successful mathematical framework that describes the fundamental particles and three of the four fundamental forces. In university-level philosophy of physics, the Standard Model is the premier evidence for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/structural-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Structural Realism</strong></a>. It treats particles not as independent substances, but as <strong>Representations</strong> of an underlying symmetry group, anchoring our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the rigorous algebraic relations of the manifold.</p><h3>1. The Gauge Group Structure</h3><p>The Standard Model is defined by the product Lie group \\( SU(3) \\times SU(2) \\times U(1) \\). In university-level theory, these groups dictate the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the strong, weak, and electromagnetic interactions. Particles are defined entirely by their charges under these groups. This ensures that <strong>Physical Causality</strong> is a result of the manifold\'s causal structure, providing the definitive map for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/universal-calculus\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Universal Calculus</strong></a>.</p><h3>2. Particle Identity as Symmetry representation</h3><p>For a structural realist, an \'Electron\' is not a tiny ball, but a specific representation of the <strong>Poincaré Group</strong> and the internal gauge groups. In university-level mechanics, this view resolves the problem of indististinguishability. Particles lack individual identity because they are nothing more than nodes in the structural network, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the algebraic vacuum.</p><h3>3. Symmetry Breaking and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Generation</h3><p>The Standard Model explains the origin of mass through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spontaneous-symmetry-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking</strong></a> and the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/higgs-mechanism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Higgs Mechanism</strong></a>. In university-level astrophysics, this process reveals that the manifold can transition between different structural states. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/higgs-boson\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Higgs Boson</strong></a> is the manifestation of this structural shift, ensuring that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the vacuum remain stable.</p><h3>4. Predictive Power and Limitations</h3><p>The extraordinary accuracy of the Standard Model is the primary driver of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/no-miracles-argument\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>No-Miracles Argument</strong></a>. However, the theory\'s inability to include gravity suggests it is an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/effective-ontology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Effective Ontology</strong></a>. This investigation pushes the boundaries of our understanding, asking whether the Standard Model is a subset of a larger 11D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/m-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>M-Theory</strong></a> structure.</p><h3>5. Beyond the Standard Model: Neutrino Mass</h3><p>The discovery of neutrino oscillations proves that neutrinos have non-zero mass, suggesting they may be <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/majorana-fermions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/majorana-fermions\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Majorana Fermions</a></strong></a></strong>. This possibility is currently being tested through searches for <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutrinoless-double-beta\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Neutrinoless Double Beta Decay</a></strong>, which would violate lepton number conservation.</p><h3>6. CP Violation and EDMs</h3><p>Searching for new sources of CP violation involves measuring <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/edm-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\">EDM Physics</a></strong> (Electric Dipole Moments) of fundamental particles, where a non-zero value would signal physics beyond the Standard Model.</p>','The Standard Model of particle physics is the highly successful mathematical framework that describes the fundamental particles and three of the four fundamental forces. In university-level philosophy of physics, the Standard Model is the premier evidence for Structural Realism. It treats particles not as independent substances, but as Representations of an underlying symmetry group, anchoring our Scientific Realism in the rigorous algebraic relations of the manifold.','The Standard Model of particle physics is the highly successful mathematical framework that describes the fundamental particles and three of the four fundamental forces. In university-level philosophy of physics, the Standard Model is the premier evidence for Structural Realism. It treats particles not as independent substances, but as Representations of an underlying symmetry group, anchoring our Scientific Realism in the rigorous algebraic relations of the manifold.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"22.33ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 9869.9 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554 544 579T526 616T501 641Q465 662 419 662Q362 662 313 616T263 510Q263 480 278 458T319 427Q323 425 389 408T456 390Q490 379 522 342T554 242Q554 216 546 186Q541 164 528 137T492 78T426 18T332 -20Q320 -22 298 -22Q199 -22 144 33L134 44L106 13Q83 -14 78 -18T65 -22Q52 -22 52 -14Q52 -11 110 221Q112 227 130 227H143Q149 221 149 216Q149 214 148 207T144 186T142 153Q144 114 160 87T203 47T255 29T308 24Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D448\" d=\"M107 637Q73 637 71 641Q70 643 70 649Q70 673 81 682Q83 683 98 683Q139 681 234 681Q268 681 297 681T342 682T362 682Q378 682 378 672Q378 670 376 658Q371 641 366 638H364Q362 638 359 638T352 638T343 637T334 637Q295 636 284 634T266 623Q265 621 238 518T184 302T154 169Q152 155 152 140Q152 86 183 55T269 24Q336 24 403 69T501 205L552 406Q599 598 599 606Q599 633 535 637Q511 637 511 648Q511 650 513 660Q517 676 519 679T529 683Q532 683 561 682T645 680Q696 680 723 681T752 682Q767 682 767 672Q767 650 759 642Q756 637 737 637Q666 633 648 597Q646 592 598 404Q557 235 548 205Q515 105 433 42T263 -22Q171 -22 116 34T60 167V183Q60 201 115 421Q164 622 164 628Q164 635 107 637Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-33\" d=\"M127 463Q100 463 85 480T69 524Q69 579 117 622T233 665Q268 665 277 664Q351 652 390 611T430 522Q430 470 396 421T302 350L299 348Q299 347 308 345T337 336T375 315Q457 262 457 175Q457 96 395 37T238 -22Q158 -22 100 21T42 130Q42 158 60 175T105 193Q133 193 151 175T169 130Q169 119 166 110T159 94T148 82T136 74T126 70T118 67L114 66Q165 21 238 21Q293 21 321 74Q338 107 338 175V195Q338 290 274 322Q259 328 213 329L171 330L168 332Q166 335 166 348Q166 366 174 366Q202 366 232 371Q266 376 294 413T322 525V533Q322 590 287 612Q265 626 240 626Q208 626 181 615T143 592T132 580H135Q138 579 1