 data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2334.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3093.7,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3371.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D706\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D706\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3954.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4621.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2260\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2260\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5677.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6428.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6817.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D434\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7567.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7956.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8707.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9096.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9855.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"pre-written-world-34894e7e\",\"independence-fail-9ab412bf\",\"the-cosmic-fake-6a24a3f4\",\"big-bang-start-4da62fa5\"]'),
(2010029,'locality-bound','determinism-and-locality','The Locality Bound','<p>The <strong>Locality Bound</strong> is the mathematical limit on the correlations between measurement outcomes that must be satisfied by any physical theory adhering to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/local-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Local Realism</strong></a>. In university-level physics, this bound is quantified by the <strong>Bell Inequality</strong>, most notably the value of 2 in the CHSH inequality. It represents the \"Classical Ceiling\" for statistical agreement, distinguishing a universe of independent parts from a <strong>Non-Separable</strong> quantum universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Statistical Independence and Locality</h3>\n                    <p>The locality bound is derived from the assumption that the probability distribution of outcomes for two distant observers, Alice and Bob, must factorize: \\( P(a,b|A,B,\\lambda) = P(a|A,\\lambda) P(b|B,\\lambda) \\). Here, \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are measurement settings and \\( \\lambda \\) represents any pre-existing \"Hidden Variables.\" This 4D <strong>Deterministic</strong> constraint ensures that Alice\'s choice cannot instantly change Bob\'s results, grounding theory in <strong>Locality</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/chsh-inequality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>CHSH Inequality</strong></a> (\\( |S| \\le 2 \\))</h3>\n                    <p>The most widely used locality bound is the CHSH value of 2. By defining a correlation function \\( S \\), John Bell showed that for any local realist model, the sum of correlations cannot exceed 2.\n                    \\[ S = E(a,b) - E(a,b\') + E(a\',b) + E(a\',b\') \\le 2 \\]\n                    This bound is not a law of physics but a logical consequence of the <strong>Classical Postulate</strong>. Any experimental result exceeding 2 is a direct violation of local realism.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Geometric Origin of the Bound</h3>\n                    <p>The value of 2 arises from the geometry of 3D unit vectors in Euclidean space. It reflects the \"Linearity\" of classical logic. In contrast, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-non-locality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Non-Locality</strong></a> utilizes the higher-dimensional geometry of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>, where vectors can overlap in ways that produce \"Stronger-than-Classical\" agreement, illustrating the difference between a 3D world and a  manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Tsirelson\'s Bound: The Quantum Limit</h3>\n                    <p>Even though quantum mechanics violates the locality bound of 2, it does not allow for infinite correlation. Boris Tsirelson proved that quantum mechanics has its own <strong>Locality Bound</strong> of \\( 2\\sqrt{2} \\approx 2.82 \\). This limit, known as the <strong>Tsirelson Bound</strong>, prevents the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-violation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Violation</strong></a> from enabling faster-than-light communication, preserving the <strong>No-Communication Theorem</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Popescu-Rohrlich (PR) Boxes</h3>\n                    <p>Theoretical physicists explore hypothetical \"PR Boxes\" that could reach a correlation score of 4 (the absolute logical maximum). Such boxes would still satisfy the <strong>No-Communication Theorem</strong> but would violate the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stability-threshold\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Stability Threshold</strong></a> of standard quantum mechanics. The search for why nature chooses the Tsirelson bound over the PR bound is a major goal of <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Reality at the Limit</h3>\n                    <p>The locality bound marks the <strong>Epistemic Limit</strong> of classical physics. It identifies the point where the \"Separability\" of objects breaks down. Observing a violation of the bound is a \"Smoking Gun\" for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/holistic-reality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Holistic Reality</strong></a>, proving that the  is more interconnected than a collection of independent local facts, anchoring our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in quantum holism.</p>','The Locality Bound is the mathematical limit on the correlations between measurement outcomes that must be satisfied by any physical theory adhering to Local Realism. In university-level physics, this bound is quantified by the Bell Inequality, most notably the value of 2 in the CHSH inequality. It represents the \"Classical Ceiling\" for statistical agreement, distinguishing a universe of independent parts from a Non-Separable quantum universe.','The Locality Bound is the mathematical limit on the correlations between measurement outcomes that must be satisfied by any physical theory adhering to Local Realism. In university-level physics, this bound is quantified by the Bell Inequality, most notably the value of 2 in the CHSH inequality. It represents the \"Classical Ceiling\" for statistical agreement, distinguishing a universe of independent parts from a Non-Separable quantum universe.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"46.081ex\" height=\"2.396ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -809 20367.9 1059\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554