X-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"lambda-dominance-556c055a\",\"causal-wall-06ae3cc3\",\"horizon-heat-a2039d8b\",\"symmetry-of-time-423b8286\"]'),
(2010038,'radiation-only-universe','friedmann-equations','The Radiation-Only Universe','<p>The <strong>Radiation-Only Universe</strong> is a cosmological model representing the first \\( \\sim 50,000 \\) years of the <strong>History of the Universe</strong>. In university-level physics, this era is characterized by an <strong>Energy Density</strong> dominated by relativistic particles (photons and neutrinos). Because radiation pressure is high, the  undergoes a unique <strong>Expansion History</strong> where space stretches as \\( t^{1/2} \\), setting the stage for <strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Radiation Equation of State</h3>\n                    <p>Relativistic particles exert a pressure \\( P = \\rho c^2/3 \\). In the <strong>Fluid Equation</strong>, this leads to a density evolution \\( \\rho_r \\propto a^{-4} \\). This one factor of \\( a \\) steeper than matter, as photons not only dilute by volume (\\( a^{-3} \\)) but also lose energy through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a> (\\( a^{-1} \\)). This rapid dilution ensures that the radiation-dominated era was relatively brief in  time.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The t^(1/2) Expansion Law</h3>\n                    <p>By substituting the radiation density into the <strong>First Friedmann Equation</strong>, we derive the scale factor growth for a flat radiation universe:\n                    \\[ a(t) \\propto t^{1/2} \\]\n                    This expansion is slower than the matter-dominated \\( t^{2/3} \\). The high <strong>Cosmic Gravity</strong> provided by the radiation pressure acts as a powerful brake, causing the expansion to decelerate rapidly in the first minutes after the <strong>Big Bang</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Dominance in the Early Universe</h3>\n                    <p>In the primal , everything was relativistic. The <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> was 100% radiation. This era was essential for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bbn-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN)</strong></a>, as the expansion rate determined the \"Freezing out\" of neutrons and protons, and the eventual <strong>Cosmic Abundance</strong> of light elements like Helium and Deuterium.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Horizon Growth and Silk Damping</h3>\n                    <p>The particle horizon in a radiation universe grows as \\( 2ct \\). Because of the high photon density, baryonic matter was \"locked\" to the radiation via Thomson scattering. Small-scale density fluctuations were washed out by the rapid movement of photons—a process known as <strong>Silk Damping</strong>. This 4D smoothing is why the <strong>CMB Theory</strong> peaks have a specific damping tail at high multipoles.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Transition to Matter Dominance</h3>\n                    <p>As the universe expanded, the radiation density (\\( a^{-4} \\)) dropped faster than the matter density (\\( a^{-3} \\)). At a redshift of \\( z \\approx 3400 \\), the two became equal. This \"Matter-Radiation Equality\" epoch is a major turning point: it allowed <strong>Dark Matter</strong> to start clustering, leading to the <strong>Hierarchical Structure Formation</strong> of the modern .</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Reconstructing the Fire</h3>\n                    <p>We cannot \"see\" the radiation-only universe directly, as it was opaque to light before <strong>Recombination</strong>. However, our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> about this era is anchored in the <strong>Cosmic Neutrino Background</strong> and the <strong>BBN</strong> results. The radiation equations provide the 4D \"Initial Conditions\" for the <strong>Expansion History</strong>, proving that the hottest, smallest beginnings dictate the largest structures we see today.</p>','The Radiation-Only Universe is a cosmological model representing the first years of the History of the Universe. In university-level physics, this era is characterized by an Energy Density dominated by relativistic particles (photons and neutrinos). Because radiation pressure is high, the undergoes a unique Expansion History where space stretches as, setting the stage for Big Bang Nucleosynthesis.','The Radiation-Only Universe is a cosmological model representing the first <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"9.051ex\" height=\"1.946ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -666 4000.4 860\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\" d=\"M55 166Q55 241 101 304T222 367Q260 367 296 349T362 304T421 252T484 208T554 189Q616 189 655 236T694 338Q694 350 698 358T708 367Q722 367 722 334Q722 260 677 197T562 134H554Q517 134 481 152T414 196T355 248T292 293T223 311Q179 311 145 286Q109 257 96 218T80 156T69 133Q55 133 55 166Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-35\" d=\"M164 157Q164 133 148 117T109 101H102Q148 22 224 22Q294 22 326 82Q345 115 345 210Q345 313 318 349Q292 382 260 382H254Q176 382 136 314Q132 307 129 306T114 304Q97 304 95 310Q93 314 93 485V614Q93 664 98 664Q100 666 102 666Q103 666 123 658T178 642T253 634Q324 634 389 662Q397 666 402 666Q410 666 410 648V635Q328 538 205 538Q174 538 149 544L139 546V374Q158 388 169 396T205 412T256 420Q337 420 393 355T449 201Q449 109 385 44T229 -22Q148 -22 99 32T50 154Q50 178 61 192T84 210T107 214Q132 214 148 197T164 157Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-