m=\"translate(1246.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"221D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-221D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2302.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3080.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3469.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4208.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(5209,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5709,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6460,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(6960,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(466,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7829.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"total-source-9849c0a3\",\"push-pull-factor-99281eff\",\"deceleration-rule-d55c5775\",\"metric-link-57c43190\"]'),
(2010041,'conservation-cosmology','friedmann-equations','Conservation in Cosmology','<p>Conservation in Cosmology refers to the requirement that the energy and momentum of the cosmic fluid follow the local conservation laws of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> (\\( \\nabla_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\)). In university-level physics, this leads to the <strong>Fluid Equation</strong>, which describes how the <strong>Energy Density</strong> of matter, radiation, and dark energy must change as the universe expands to maintain a consistent 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/continuity-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Continuity Equation</strong></a> of Space</h3>\n                    <p>The law of conservation implies that for a homogeneous universe, any change in the energy of a volume must be balanced by the work done by pressure on the boundaries.\n                    \\[ \\frac{d}{dt}(\\rho c^2 a^3) = -P \\frac{d}{dt}(a^3) \\]\n                    This equation ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the Big Bang respect the <strong>First Law of Thermodynamics</strong>, showing that \"New Space\" does not create \"New Energy\" from nothing (except for the case of the <strong>Cosmological Constant</strong>).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Conservation of Baryon Number</h3>\n                    <p>While energy density can change form (e.g., matter annihilating into light), the total number of baryons (protons and neutrons) is conserved in the standard 4D <strong>Expansion History</strong>. This \"Baryon Conservation\" is essential for <strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis</strong>, as it dictates the constant number of nuclei available to build the elements of the <strong>Standard Model</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Photon Energy Loss</h3>\n                    <p>A university-level nuance of conservation is that individual photons <em>do</em> lose energy as they travel through an expanding manifold (<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a>). Where does this energy go? It is transferred to the gravitational potential energy of the . This \"Non-local\" conservation is a feature of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, where energy and 4D geometry are in a constant state of <strong>Relational</strong> exchange.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Dark Energy and Constant Density</h3>\n                    <p>Dark energy (if \\( w=-1 \\)) appears to violate simple conservation because its density stays constant as volume grows. However, the <strong>Fluid Equation</strong> shows that this perfectly consistent with <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> if the <strong>Negative Pressure</strong> does exactly enough work to \"Fill\" the new volume with vacuum energy. This ensures that the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> remains mathematically closed.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Conservation during Phase Transitions</h3>\n                    <p>During events like <strong>Recombination</strong> or the <strong>QCD Transition</strong>, energy is transferred between different species of <strong>Fermions</strong> and bosons. The total <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> of the universe is conserved during these \"Adiabatic\" processes, providing a strict timeline for the <strong>History of the Universe</strong> and the cooling of the <strong>CMB Theory</strong> peaks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Global Balance</h3>\n                    <p>Is energy conserved in the whole universe? Strictly speaking, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> does not have a global definition of energy for an expanding  because there is no <strong>Time-Translation Symmetry</strong>. However, the local conservation law (\\( \\nabla \\cdot T = 0 \\)) is an absolute requirement of 4D logic, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the local consistency of the <strong>Friedmann Equations</strong>.</p>','Conservation in Cosmology refers to the requirement that the energy and momentum of the cosmic fluid follow the local conservation laws of General Relativity. In university-level physics, this leads to the Fluid Equation, which describes how the Energy Density of matter, radiation, and dark energy must change as the universe expands to maintain a consistent 4D Causal Topology. The law of conservation implies that for a homogeneous universe, any change in the energy of a volume must be balanced by the work done by pressure on the boundaries.','Conservation in Cosmology refers to the requirement that the energy and momentum of the cosmic fluid follow the local conservation laws of General Relativity (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.905ex\" height=\"2.23ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 4820.1 985.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 