26Q168 26 194 71Q203 87 217 139T245 247T261 313Q266 340 266 352Q266 380 251 392T217 404Q177 404 142 372T93 290Q91 281 88 280T72 278H58Q52 284 52 289Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(502,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1703.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(2759.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(466,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3628.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(4148.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(394,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5168.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6168.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(6688.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(605,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"geometric-curvature-03efa3c7\",\"causal-metric-f3ec654e\"]'),
(2010050,'mathematical-platonism','mathematical-realism','Mathematical Platonism','<p>Mathematical Platonism is the metaphysical view that mathematical objects—such as numbers, sets, and functions—exist as abstract, non-physical entities in a realm independent of human thought and the physical universe. In university-level philosophy of physics, Platonism is the most common justification for why the \"Laws of Nature\" appear to be discoverable rather than merely invented.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Existence and Independence</h3>\n                    <p>Platonists assert that mathematical structures possess <strong>Ontological Reality</strong>. They argue that theorem \\( e^{i\\pi} + 1 = 0 \\) was true long before humans existed to conceive of it. This independence implies that the  of spacetime is \"Shadowing\" a deeper mathematical reality, where the properties of physical systems are constrained by the immutable truths of the Platonic realm.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Mathematics as Discovery</h3>\n                    <p>Under Platonism, the work of a physicist is a form of <strong>Archeology</strong>. By using mathematical tools, they uncover the pre-existing patterns of the universe. This explains the \"Unreasonable Effectiveness\" of math: if the universe is a physical instantiation of Platonic forms, then mathematics must necessarily be the correct language for describing its 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Access Problem</h3>\n                    <p>A major <strong>Epistemic</strong> challenge for Platonism is explaining how physical brains can access a non-physical realm. Critics argue that if math is outside of spacetime, there can be no causal link to our senses. Platonists respond by suggesting that human reason is a unique faculty capable of perceiving \"Logical Necessity,\" a property that connects our internal 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>State Vector</strong></a> of thought to the external mathematical absolute.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Platonism and Quantum Reality</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Quantum Mechanics</strong>, the wave function (\\( \\Psi \\)) is an abstract mathematical object in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>. Platonists argue that the wave function is more \"Real\" than the particles it describes. This view anchors <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the abstract mathematical state rather than the \"Appearances\" of the laboratory, suggesting that the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> is essentially a collection of evolving Platonic structures.</p>','Mathematical Platonism is the metaphysical view that mathematical objects—such as numbers, sets, and functions—exist as abstract, non-physical entities in a realm independent of human thought and the physical universe. In university-level philosophy of physics, Platonism is the most common justification for why the \"Laws of Nature\" appear to be discoverable rather than merely invented. Platonists assert that mathematical structures possess Ontological Reality.','Mathematical Platonism is the metaphysical view that mathematical objects—such as numbers, sets, and functions—exist as abstract, non-physical entities in a realm independent of human thought and the physical universe. In university-level philosophy of physics, Platonism is the most common justification for why the \"Laws of Nature\" appear to be discoverable rather than merely invented. Platonists assert that mathematical structures possess Ontological Reality.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.751ex\" height=\"2.064ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -830.4 4752 912.4\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D452\" d=\"M39 168Q39 225 58 272T107 350T174 402T244 433T307 442H310Q355 442 388 420T421 355Q421 265 310 237Q261 224 176 223Q139 223 138 221Q138 219 132 186T125 128Q125 81 146 54T209 26T302 45T394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 82T390 55T344 24T281 -1T205 -11Q126 -11 83 42T39 168ZM373 353Q367 405 305 405Q272 405 244 391T199 357T170 316T154 280T149 261Q149 260 169 260Q282 260 327 284T373 353Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\" d=\"M184 600Q184 624 203 642T247 661Q265 661 277 649T290 619Q290 596 270 577T226 557Q211 557 198 567T184 600ZM21 287Q21 295 30 318T54 369T98 420T158 442Q197 442 223 419T250 357Q250 340 236 301T196 196T154 83Q149 61 149 51Q149 26 166 26Q175 26 185 29T208 43T235 78T260 137Q263 149 265 151T282 153Q302 153 302 143Q302 135 293 112T268 61T223 11T161 -11Q129 -11 102 10T74 74Q74 91 79 106T122 220Q160 321 166 341T173 380Q173 404 156 404H154Q124 404 99 371T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70B\" d=\"M132 -11Q98 -11 98 22V33L111 61Q186 219 220 334L228 358H196Q158 358 142 355T103 336Q92 329 81 318T62 297T53 285Q51 284 38 284Q19 284 19 294Q19 300 38 329T93 391T164 429Q171 431 389 431Q549 431 553 430Q573 423 573 402Q573 371 541 360Q535 358 472 358H408L405 341Q393 269 393 222Q393 170 402 129T421 65T431 37Q431 20 417 5T381 -10Q370 -10 363 -7T347 17T331 77Q330 86 330 121Q330 170 339 226T357 318T367 358H269L268 354Q268 351 249 275T206 114T175 17Q164 -11 132 -11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\" d=\"M56 237T56 250T70 270H369V420L370 570Q380 583 389 583Q402 583 409 568V270H707Q722 262 722 250T707 230H409V-68Q401 -82 391 -82H389H387Q375 -82 369 -68V230H70Q56 237 56 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(499,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(345,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1418.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2418.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3196.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(4252,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"ontological-claim-8ff8fe70\"]'),
(2010051,'mathematical-nominalism','mathematical-realism','Mathematical Nominalism','<p>Mathematical Nominalism is the philosophical position that mathematical objects do not exist as independent, abstract entities. Instead, nominalists argue that mathematics is a sophisticated <strong>Human Language</strong> or a shorthand for describing regularities in the physical world. In university-level physics, this view treats the effectiveness of math as a result of our evolution rather than a fundamental cosmic mystery.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Math as a Shorthand</h3>\n                    <p>For a nominalist, the \"Law\" \\( \\mathbf{F}=m\\mathbf{a} \\) is not a pre-existing Platonic truth. It is a concise summary of billions of observations of moving matter. Mathematics is seen as a <strong>Heuristic</strong> tool that helps us categorize and predict the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of our environment without needing to commit to the existence of unseeable mathematical \"Things.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Evolution of the Language</h3>\n                    <p>Nominalists explain the effectiveness of mathematics through <strong>Natural Selection</strong>. Humans evolved in a 4D universe with stable patterns. Those who developed logical and quantitative languages to describe those patterns were more likely to survive. In this view, mathematics is \"Effective\" for the same reason a hammer is effective at hitting nails: we built the tool specifically to solve the problems we encounter in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Rejection of Abstract Realism</h3>\n                    <p>Nominalism avoids the \"Access Problem\" of Platonism by denying that there is a non-physical realm to access. All physics is <strong>Empirical</strong>. When we say a system has \"Rotational Symmetry,\" we are merely describing the fact that we can turn our laboratory without seeing a change in measurements. This \"Instrumentalist\" view anchors <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the data of observations rather than the beauty of equations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Limits of the Linguistic View</h3>\n                    <p>A challenge for nominalism is explaining why math often predicts <em>new</em> physical entities (like <strong>Quarks</strong> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-waves\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Waves</strong></a>) before they are seen. Realists argue that a \"mere language\" should not have such creative power. Nominalists respond by suggesting that the language of math is so structurally rich that its internal logic naturally explores the 4D <strong>Phase Space</strong> of physical possibilities, leading us to new discoveries by accident.</p>','Mathematical Nominalism is the philosophical position that mathematical objects do not exist as independent, abstract entities. Instead, nominalists argue that mathematics is a sophisticated Human Language or a shorthand for describing regularities in the physical world. In university-level physics, this view treats the effectiveness of math as a result of our evolution rather than a fundamental cosmic mystery.','Mathematical Nominalism is the philosophical position that mathematical objects do not exist as independent, abstract entities. Instead, nominalists argue that mathematics is a sophisticated Human Language or a shorthand for describing regularities in the physical world. In university-level physics, this view treats the effectiveness of math as a result of our evolution rather than a fundamental cosmic mystery.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.906ex\" height=\"1.724ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 3494.6 762\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D405\" d=\"M425 0L228 3Q63 3 51 0H39V62H147V618H39V680H644V676Q647 670 659 552T675 428V424H613Q613 433 605 477Q599 511 589 535T562 574T530 599T488 612T441 617T387 618H368H304V371H333Q389 373 411 390T437 468V488H499V192H437V212Q436 244 430 263T408 292T378 305T333 309H304V62H439V0H425Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\" d=\"M21 287Q22 293 24 303T36 341T56 388T88 425T132 442T175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q303 442 384 442Q401 442 415 440T441 433T460 423T475 411T485 398T493 385T497 373T500 364T502 357L510 367Q573 442 659 442Q713 442 746 415T780 336Q780 285 742 178T704 50Q705 36 709 31T724 26Q752 26 776 56T815 138Q818 149 821 151T837 153Q857 153 857 145Q857 144 853 130Q845 101 831 73T785 17T716 -10Q669 -10 648 17T627 73Q627 92 663 193T700 345Q700 404 656 404H651Q565 404 506 303L499 291L466 157Q433 26 428 16Q415 -11 385 -11Q372 -11 364 -4T353 8T350 18Q350 29 384 161L420 307Q423 322 423 345Q423 404 379 404H374Q288 404 229 303L222 291L189 157Q156 26 151 16Q138 -11 108 -11Q95 -11 87 -5T76 7T74 17Q74 30 112 181Q151 335 151 342Q154 357 154 369Q154 405 129 405Q107 405 92 377T69 316T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D41A\" d=\"M64 349Q64 399 107 426T255 453Q346 453 402 423T473 341Q478 327 478 310T479 196V77Q493 63 529 62Q549 62 553 57T558 31Q558 9 552 5T514 0H497H481Q375 0 367 56L356 46Q300 -6 210 -6Q130 -6 81 30T32 121Q32 188 111 226T332 272H350V292Q350 313 348 327T337 361T306 391T248 402T194 399H189Q204 376 204 354Q204 327 187 306T134 284Q97 284 81 305T64 349ZM164 121Q164 89 186 67T238 45Q274 45 307 63T346 108L350 117V226H347Q248 218 206 189T164 121Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D405\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D405\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1001.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2057.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(2935.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D41A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D41A\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"the-middle-ground-826d3ad1\"]'),
(2010052,'mathematical-universe-hypothesis','mathematical-realism','The Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (MUH)','<p>The <strong>Mathematical Universe Hypothesis</strong> (proposed by Max Tegmark) is the radical claim that physical reality is not just <em>described</em> by mathematics, but <em>is</em> a mathematical structure. In university-level theoretical physics, this represents the ultimate logical conclusion of <strong>Mathematical Realism</strong>, where the distinction between \"Math\" and \"Stuff\" is completely abolished.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. External Reality as Math</h3>\n                    <p>Tegmark argues that if there exists an <strong>External Reality</strong> independent of humans, it must be something that can be described without human language. The only thing that fits this description is a mathematical structure—a set of abstract entities and relations. If our universe is such a structure, then every physical property (mass, charge, 4D position) is simply a mathematical property of a specific point in that structure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The \"Bag of Stuff\" Illusion</h3>\n                    <p>According to MUH, our perception of \"Physical Matter\" is a byproduct of being part of the structure. We are **Self-Aware Substructures (SASs)** that perceive the mathematical relations around us as \"Solid Objects\" or \"Force Fields.\" This removes the need for <strong>Ontological</strong> dualism: there is no \"Matter\" that the \"Math\" acts upon; there are only the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the structure itself.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Computability Constraint</h3>\n                    <p>MUH implies that the universe must be <strong>Computable</strong>. If reality is math, then the laws of physics must be expressible as algorithms. This links the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the cosmos to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a>, suggesting that the \"Beginning\" of the universe (the Big Bang) is equivalent to the initialization of a formal system, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the logic of Turing machines.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Prediction of the Level IV Multiverse</h3>\n                    <p>The most profound consequence of MUH is the <strong>Level IV Multiverse</strong>. If a mathematical structure is \"Real,\" then <em>every</em> consistent mathematical structure must be equally real. This means that all possible universes (with different dimensions, constants, and symmetries) exist somewhere in the infinite landscape of formal systems, proving that our particular 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> is merely one coordinate in the absolute space of logic.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of The Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (MUH) is intrinsically linked to the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/level-iv-multiverse\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Level IV Multiverse: The Ultimate Ensemble</a></strong>, which provides the necessary analytical depth for understanding the underlying physical manifold.</p>','The Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (proposed by Max Tegmark) is the radical claim that physical reality is not just described by mathematics, but is a mathematical structure. In university-level theoretical physics, this represents the ultimate logical conclusion of Mathematical Realism, where the distinction between \"Math\" and \"Stuff\" is completely abolished. Tegmark argues that if there exists an External Reality independent of humans, it must be something that can be described without human language.','The Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (proposed by Max Tegmark) is the radical claim that physical reality is not just described by mathematics, but is a mathematical structure. In university-level theoretical physics, this represents the ultimate logical conclusion of Mathematical Realism, where the distinction between \"Math\" and \"Stuff\" is completely abolished. Tegmark argues that if there exists an External Reality independent of humans, it must be something that can be described without human language.','','[]','[]','[\"total-identity-e63a6c7d\"]'),
(2010053,'level-iv-multiverse','mathematical-universe-hypothesis','The Level IV Multiverse: The Ultimate Ensemble','<p>The <strong>Level IV Multiverse</strong> is the final and most expansive level of the multiverse hierarchy. It consists of all possible universes governed by different mathematical structures. While Level I, II, and III multiverses share the same fundamental laws but different initial conditions or branches, Level IV asserts that <strong>Every Consistent Mathematical Structure</strong> is a physically real universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Beyond Physical Laws</h3>\n                    <p>In university-level cosmology, this multiverse addresses the \"Fine-Tuning\" problem by removing the need for a special designer or a lucky coincidence. If all math is real, then there are universes where gravity is repulsive, space has 10 dimensions, or time is two-dimensional. We simply find ourselves in a  where the math allows for stable atoms and biological complexity—an application of the <strong>Anthropic Principle</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Equivalence of Math and Reality</h3>\n                    <p>The core of Level IV is the <strong>Mathematical Universe Hypothesis</strong>. It denies the existence of a \"Preferential\" mathematical structure. There is no reason why the <strong>Group Theory</strong> of the Standard Model should be \"More Real\" than the non-Euclidean geometry of a 5D cube. By asserting the reality of the <strong>Ultimate Ensemble</strong>, Level IV provides a final answer to \"Why these laws?\": all laws exist, and we inhabit one possible branch of the infinite tree of logic.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Causal Isolation</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike the other levels of the multiverse, Level IV universes are <strong>Causally Isolated</strong>. There is no physical \"Space\" between them; they are separated by their differing logical foundations. Each universe is a closed 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> (or n-dimensional structure) that cannot communicate with any other. Their only link is through the <strong>Formal Symmetry</strong> of the overarching mathematical landscape.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Epistemic Verification</h3>\n                    <p>Level IV is often criticized as being <strong>Untestable</strong>. However, proponents argue that it is the only way to avoid the \"Brute Fact\" problem—where the laws of physics are simply \"There\" without explanation. By deriving our 4D reality from a principle of <strong>Mathematical Realism</strong>, Level IV provides a coherent <strong>Ontology</strong> that eliminates the mysterious \"Beginning\" of the universe, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the totality of mathematical possibility.</p>','The Level IV Multiverse is the final and most expansive level of the multiverse hierarchy. It consists of all possible universes governed by different mathematical structures. While Level I, II, and III multiverses share the same fundamental laws but different initial conditions or branches, Level IV asserts that Every Consistent Mathematical Structure is a physically real universe.','The Level IV Multiverse is the final and most expansive level of the multiverse hierarchy. It consists of all possible universes governed by different mathematical structures. While Level I, II, and III multiverses share the same fundamental laws but different initial conditions or branches, Level IV asserts that Every Consistent Mathematical Structure is a physically real universe.','','[]','[]','[\"totality-343b2946\"]'),
(2010054,'symmetry-fundamental','mathematical-realism','Symmetry as a Fundamental Principle','<p>In modern <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>, <strong>Symmetry</strong> is viewed not just as a property of a system, but as the generative principle of physical laws. In university-level physics, identifying the symmetries of the universe is the first step toward discovering its fundamental interactions, suggesting that the \"Stuff\" of reality is secondary to the mathematical patterns that govern it.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Generative Power of Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>A symmetry is a transformation that leaves the <strong>Action</strong> of a system invariant. For example, <strong>Gauge Symmetry</strong> in quantum field theory dictates the form of the electromagnetic, weak, and strong forces. This \"Symmetry Dictates Interaction\" paradigm implies that the laws of physics are not arbitrary but are the only possible ways to maintain certain mathematical consistencies in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Symmetry and Noether\'s Theorem</h3>\n                    <p><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/noethers-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Noether\'s Theorem</strong></a> provides the mathematical bridge between symmetry and conservation. It proves that every continuous symmetry corresponds to a conserved quantity (e.g., rotational symmetry leads to the <strong>Conservation of Angular Momentum</strong>). This theorem anchors <strong>Mathematical Realism</strong> in the physical world, showing that the most stable features of our 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> are direct consequences of abstract mathematical invariants.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Broken Symmetry and Emergence</h3>\n                    <p>While the fundamental laws are highly symmetrical, the universe we observe often is not. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spontaneous-symmetry-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking</strong></a> (like the <strong>Higgs Mechanism</strong>) explains how variety and complexity arise from a single, unified state. This proves that the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> can possess hidden mathematical order that only becomes apparent through the lens of group-theoretic analysis.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Symmetry as Beauty</h3>\n                    <p>The quest for symmetry is often driven by the concept of <strong>Mathematical Beauty</strong>. Many physicists believe that the most fundamental theory must be the most symmetrical. This belief is a form of <strong>Ontological</strong> commitment to the idea that the universe is a perfect mathematical structure, and that our role as physicists is to uncover the <strong>Formal Symmetry</strong> that was present at the Beginning.</p>','In modern Theoretical Physics, Symmetry is viewed not just as a property of a system, but as the generative principle of physical laws. In university-level physics, identifying the symmetries of the universe is the first step toward discovering its fundamental interactions, suggesting that the \"Stuff\" of reality is secondary to the mathematical patterns that govern it. A symmetry is a transformation that leaves the Action of a system invariant.','In modern Theoretical Physics, Symmetry is viewed not just as a property of a system, but as the generative principle of physical laws. In university-level physics, identifying the symmetries of the universe is the first step toward discovering its fundamental interactions, suggesting that the \"Stuff\" of reality is secondary to the mathematical patterns that govern it. A symmetry is a transformation that leaves the Action of a system invariant.','','[]','[]','[\"stationarity-condition-90ce0d70\",\"noether-current-identity-606a86e8\"]'),
(2010055,'invariance-principle','mathematical-realism','The Principle of Invariance','<p>The <strong>Principle of Invariance</strong> states that the fundamental laws of nature must remain the same for all observers, regardless of their position, orientation, or state of uniform motion. In university-level physics, invariance is the \"Criterion of Reality\": only those properties that remain unchanged under <strong>Lorentz Transformations</strong> or other group actions are considered objective features of the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Invariance vs. Covariance</h3>\n                    <p>Physicists distinguish between <strong>Invariant</strong> quantities (like rest mass or the spacetime interval), which have the same numerical value for everyone, and <strong>Covariant</strong> equations, which maintain the same mathematical form. This distinction is essential for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>, as it allows us to separate the \"Appearances\" (coordinate-dependent values) from the \"Reality\" (the absolute 4D geometric relationships).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Role of the Metric</h3>\n                    <p>Invariance is mathematically encoded in the <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> (\\( g_{\\mu\\nu} \\)). The metric defines the \"Rules of the Game\" for a , specifying how coordinates must transform to keep the physical laws invariant. In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the requirement of <strong>General Covariance</strong> (invariance under any smooth coordinate change) led Einstein to the realization that gravity is a manifestation of spacetime geometry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Global vs. Local Invariance</h3>\n                    <p>A symmetry is <strong>Global</strong> if the transformation is the same everywhere. A <strong>Local Invariance</strong> (or gauge invariance) requires that the laws remain the same even if the transformation varies from point to point in the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>. Requiring local invariance is what necessitates the existence of <strong>Force Fields</strong> (like photons or gluons) to \"Correct\" for the local variations, linking the geometry of the manifold to the interactions of matter.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Invariance as Truth</h3>\n                    <p>From the perspective of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/structural-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Structural Realism</strong></a>, invariance is the ultimate map of truth. If a property (like the \"Up\" direction) changes when you rotate your head, it is not a fundamental property of the universe. Only those structures that are invariant under the full <strong>Poincaré Group</strong> are deemed physically real. This anchors our <strong>Ontology</strong> in the absolute, unchanging mathematical skeleton of the cosmos.</p>','The Principle of Invariance states that the fundamental laws of nature must remain the same for all observers, regardless of their position, orientation, or state of uniform motion. In university-level physics, invariance is the \"Criterion of Reality\": only those properties that remain unchanged under Lorentz Transformations or other group actions are considered objective features of the. Physicists distinguish between Invariant quantities (like rest mass or the spacetime interval), which have the same numerical value for everyone, and Covariant equations, which maintain the same mathematical form.','The Principle of Invariance states that the fundamental laws of nature must remain the same for all observers, regardless of their position, orientation, or state of uniform motion. In university-level physics, invariance is the \"Criterion of Reality\": only those properties that remain unchanged under Lorentz Transformations or other group actions are considered objective features of the . Physicists distinguish between Invariant quantities (like rest mass or the spacetime interval), which have the same numerical value for everyone, and Covariant equations, which maintain the same mathematical form.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.08ex\" height=\"1.685ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1361.2 744.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D454\" d=\"M311 43Q296 30 267 15T206 0Q143 0 105 45T66 160Q66 265 143 353T314 442Q361 442 401 394L404 398Q406 401 409 404T418 412T431 419T447 422Q461 422 470 413T480 394Q480 379 423 152T363 -80Q345 -134 286 -169T151 -205Q10 -205 10 -137Q10 -111 28 -91T74 -71Q89 -71 102 -80T116 -111Q116 -121 114 -130T107 -144T99 -154T92 -162L90 -164H91Q101 -167 151 -167Q189 -167 211 -155Q234 -144 254 -122T282 -75Q288 -56 298 -13Q311 35 311 43ZM384 328L380 339Q377 350 375 354T369 368T359 382T346 393T328 402T306 405Q262 405 221 352Q191 313 171 233T151 117Q151 38 213 38Q269 38 323 108L331 118L384 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\" d=\"M74 431Q75 431 146 436T219 442Q231 442 231 434Q231 428 185 241L137 51H140L150 55Q161 59 177 67T214 86T261 119T312 165Q410 264 445 394Q458 442 496 442Q509 442 519 434T530 411Q530 390 516 352T469 262T388 162T267 70T106 5Q81 -2 71 -2Q66 -2 59 -1T51 1Q45 5 45 11Q45 13 88 188L132 364Q133 377 125 380T86 385H65Q59 391 59 393T61 412Q65 431 74 431Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D454\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D454\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(510,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(603,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"absolute-measure-ef818fe7\",\"technical-relation-29a517d7\"]'),
(2010056,'mathematical-beauty','mathematical-realism','Mathematical Beauty and Theoretical Truth','<p><strong>Mathematical Beauty</strong> is the aesthetic quality of simplicity, elegance, and profound connection found in certain equations and theories. In university-level physics, beauty is often used as a <strong>Heuristic</strong> guide, with many great scientists (like Dirac and Einstein) asserting that a beautiful equation is more likely to be true than an ugly one.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Simplicity and Elegance</h3>\n                    <p>Beauty in physics is often equated with <strong>Simplicity</strong>—the ability to describe a vast range of phenomena with a single, compact formula. <strong>Euler\'s Identity</strong> (\\( e^{i\\pi} + 1 = 0 \\)) is the classic example, linking five fundamental constants in a single line. In 4D physics, the compact form of the <strong>Maxwell Equations</strong> in tensor notation is considered beautiful because it reveals the unified nature of electricity and magnetism.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Unexpected Connections</h3>\n                    <p>A theory is deemed beautiful when it reveals deep, unexpected links between seemingly unrelated parts of nature. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/noethers-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Noether\'s Theorem</strong></a> is considered a masterpiece of mathematical beauty because it proves that \"Motion\" (conservation laws) and \"Pattern\" (symmetry) are two sides of the same coin. These connections suggest that the universe is not a collection of random facts but a coherent, unified <strong>Logical Structure</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Risk of Aesthetic Bias</h3>\n                    <p>While beauty has led to many successes (like the prediction of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/antimatter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Antimatter</strong></a>), it also carries risks. Critics argue that <strong>Mathematical Beauty</strong> is a human projection and that nature is under no obligation to be \"Pretty.\" The long, difficult search for <strong>Supersymmetry</strong> or certain <strong>Grand Unified Theories</strong> has led some to question whether our aesthetic preference for high symmetry is blinding us to the messy reality of the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Beauty as an Ontological Guide</h3>\n                    <p>For a mathematical realist, beauty is an <strong>Epistemic</strong> signal that we are close to the truth. If the universe <em>is</em> a mathematical structure, then its most fundamental level should be characterized by the highest degree of formal elegance. In this view, our aesthetic sense is a biological \"Antenna\" that allows us to sense the perfection of the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a>, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the harmony of the vacuum.</p>','Mathematical Beauty is the aesthetic quality of simplicity, elegance, and profound connection found in certain equations and theories. In university-level physics, beauty is often used as a Heuristic guide, with many great scientists (like Dirac and Einstein) asserting that a beautiful equation is more likely to be true than an ugly one. Beauty in physics is often equated with Simplicity—the ability to describe a vast range of phenomena with a single, compact formula.','Mathematical Beauty is the aesthetic quality of simplicity, elegance, and profound connection found in certain equations and theories. In university-level physics, beauty is often used as a Heuristic guide, with many great scientists (like Dirac and Einstein) asserting that a beautiful equation is more likely to be true than an ugly one. Beauty in physics is often equated with Simplicity—the ability to describe a vast range of phenomena with a single, compact formula.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.751ex\" height=\"2.064ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -830.4 4752 912.4\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D452\" d=\"M39 168Q39 225 58 272T107 350T174 402T244 433T307 442H310Q355 442 388 420T421 355Q421 265 310 237Q261 224 176 223Q139 223 138 221Q138 219 132 186T125 128Q125 81 146 54T209 26T302 45T394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 82T390 55T344 24T281 -1T205 -11Q126 -11 83 42T39 168ZM373 353Q367 405 305 405Q272 405 244 391T199 357T170 316T154 280T149 261Q149 260 169 260Q282 260 327 284T373 353Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\" d=\"M184 600Q184 624 203 642T247 661Q265 661 277 649T290 619Q290 596 270 577T226 557Q211 557 198 567T184 600ZM21 287Q21 295 30 318T54 369T98 420T158 442Q197 442 223 419T250 357Q250 340 236 301T196 196T154 83Q149 61 149 51Q149 26 166 26Q175 26 185 29T208 43T235 78T260 137Q263 149 265 151T282 153Q302 153 302 143Q302 135 293 112T268 61T223 11T161 -11Q129 -11 102 10T74 74Q74 91 79 106T122 220Q160 321 166 341T173 380Q173 404 156 404H154Q124 404 99 371T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70B\" d=\"M132 -11Q98 -11 98 22V33L111 61Q186 219 220 334L228 358H196Q158 358 142 355T103 336Q92 329 81 318T62 297T53 285Q51 284 38 284Q19 284 19 294Q19 300 38 329T93 391T164 429Q171 431 389 431Q549 431 553 430Q573 423 573 402Q573 371 541 360Q535 358 472 358H408L405 341Q393 269 393 222Q393 170 402 129T421 65T431 37Q431 20 417 5T381 -10Q370 -10 363 -7T347 17T331 77Q330 86 330 121Q330 170 339 226T357 318T367 358H269L268 354Q268 351 249 275T206 114T175 17Q164 -11 132 -11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\" d=\"M56 237T56 250T70 270H369V420L370 570Q380 583 389 583Q402 583 409 568V270H707Q722 262 722 250T707 230H409V-68Q401 -82 391 -82H389H387Q375 -82 369 -68V230H70Q56 237 56 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(499,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(345,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D70B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70B\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1418.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2418.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3196.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(4252,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"mathematical-beauty-f2d2e607\",\"geometric-beauty-bcee7467\"]'),
(2010057,'formal-symmetry','mathematical-realism','Formal Symmetry and Automorphism Groups','<p><strong>Formal Symmetry</strong> is the rigorous mathematical definition of symmetry as an <strong>Automorphism</strong>—a mapping of a structure onto itself that preserves all its internal relationships. In university-level physics, this concept provides the precise language for identifying the fundamental \"Identity\" of physical systems and the group of transformations that leave their structure unchanged.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Automorphism Group (Aut)</h3>\n                    <p>Every mathematical structure \\( \\mathcal{S} \\) has an associated group of automorphisms, denoted \\( \\text{Aut}(\\mathcal{S}) \\). This group contains all the ways the  can be shifted or rotated without altering its <strong>Metric</strong> or field relations. In <strong>Mathematical Realism</strong>, these groups are seen as the \"Skeleton\" of reality, defining what is physically possible by specifying what is mathematically preserved.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Structure-Preserving Maps</h3>\n                    <p>When a physicist says that spacetime has <strong>Lorentz Symmetry</strong>, they are formally stating that the Lorentz transformations are the automorphisms of <strong>Minkowski Space</strong>. These maps preserve the <strong>Invariant Inner Product</strong> and the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a>. This formal approach removes human intuition from the definition of symmetry, replacing it with the objective criteria of abstract algebra.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Symmetries of the Lagrangian</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Quantum Field Theory</strong>, we analyze the symmetries of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian Density</strong></a>. If a transformation belongs to the automorphism group of the action, the resulting physics is unchanged. This allows theorists to classify all possible interactions by looking at the available symmetry groups, such as the <strong>SU(3) x SU(2) x U(1)</strong> structure of the Standard Model, which dictates the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the vacuum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Symmetry and Objectivity</h3>\n                    <p>Formal symmetry is the ultimate guarantor of <strong>Objectivity</strong>. If a property is not part of the automorphism group—meaning it can be changed by a structure-preserving map—it is not an essential feature of the system. This provides a rigorous <strong>Epistemic</strong> filter, allowing us to discard \"Gaugy\" or coordinate-dependent artifacts and focus on the truly real, invariant structure of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p>','Formal Symmetry is the rigorous mathematical definition of symmetry as an Automorphism—a mapping of a structure onto itself that preserves all its internal relationships. In university-level physics, this concept provides the precise language for identifying the fundamental \"Identity\" of physical systems and the group of transformations that leave their structure unchanged. Every mathematical structure has an associated group of automorphisms, denoted.','Formal Symmetry is the rigorous mathematical definition of symmetry as an Automorphism—a mapping of a structure onto itself that preserves all its internal relationships. In university-level physics, this concept provides the precise language for identifying the fundamental \"Identity\" of physical systems and the group of transformations that leave their structure unchanged. Every mathematical structure <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.452ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 642 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http:/