(2010060,'von-neumann-chain','measurement-problem','The Von Neumann Chain','<p>The <strong>Von Neumann Chain</strong> is a theoretical concept illustrating the \"Infinite Regress\" problem in quantum measurement. Proposed by John von Neumann in 1932, it demonstrates that if quantum mechanics is universal, then the measuring device, the observer\'s eyes, and even the observer\'s brain should all be part of a single, ever-expanding <strong>Entangled State</strong> rather than producing a definite result.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Interaction Chain</h3>\n                    <p>When a quantum system (\\( S \\)) interacts with a measuring device (\\( D \\)), they become entangled: \\( |\\psi\\rangle = \\sum c_i |s_i\\rangle |d_i\\rangle \\). If a second device (\\( D_2 \\)) measures the first, the entanglement spreads: \\( |\\psi\\rangle = \\sum c_i |s_i\\rangle |d_i\\rangle |d_2,i\\rangle \\). This chain continues through the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the experiment, from the detector to the computer screen to the retina of the physicist.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Requirement of a \"Cut\"</h3>\n                    <p>The existence of the chain implies that unitary evolution alone never produces a single outcome; it only produces more complex superpositions. To account for our experience of definite results, there must be a point where the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/unitary-evolution-quantum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Unitary Evolution</strong></a> is broken—the <strong>Heisenberg Cut</strong>. Von Neumann proved that for all practical purposes, the \"Cut\" can be placed anywhere in the chain (the <strong>Psychophysical Parallelism</strong>), but theory does not specify <em>where</em> it actually occurs.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Consciousness Argument</h3>\n                    <p>Because the chain can be extended all the way into the brain, some interpretations (notably Wigner and Wheeler) suggested that <strong>Consciousness</strong> is the only entity capable of \"Collapsing\" the wave function. This view suggests that the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong> remains in a superposition until a mind perceives it. While controversial, this highlights the extreme <strong>Epistemic</strong> difficulty of defining what constitutes a \"Final\" measurement in a quantum world.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Modern View: Decoherence</h3>\n                    <p>Most modern physicists view <strong>Decoherence</strong> as the mechanism that \"Stops\" the chain for all practical purposes. By interacting with the trillions of degrees of freedom in the environment, the phase information of the superposition is lost, making the system <em>behave</em> as if it has collapsed. This \"Apparent Collapse\" prevents the Von Neumann chain from reaching macroscopic scales, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the statistical noise of the .</p>','The Von Neumann Chain is a theoretical concept illustrating the \"Infinite Regress\" problem in quantum measurement. Proposed by John von Neumann in 1932, it demonstrates that if quantum mechanics is universal, then the measuring device, the observer\'s eyes, and even the observer\'s brain should all be part of a single, ever-expanding Entangled State rather than producing a definite result. When a quantum system interacts with a measuring device, they become entangled:.','The Von Neumann Chain is a theoretical concept illustrating the \"Infinite Regress\" problem in quantum measurement. Proposed by John von Neumann in 1932, it demonstrates that if quantum mechanics is universal, then the measuring device, the observer\'s eyes, and even the observer\'s brain should all be part of a single, ever-expanding Entangled State rather than producing a definite result. When a quantum system (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.459ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 645 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554 544 579T526 616T501 641Q465 662 419 662Q362 662 313 616T263 510Q263 480 278 458T319 427Q323 425 389 408T456 390Q490 379 522 342T554 242Q554 216 546 186Q541 164 528 137T492 78T426 18T332 -20Q320 -22 298 -22Q199 -22 144 33L134 44L106 13Q83 -14 78 -18T65 -22Q52 -22 52 -14Q52 -11 110 221Q112 227 130 227H143Q149 221 149 216Q149 214 148 207T144 186T142 153Q144 114 160 87T203 47T255 29T308 24Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) interacts with a measuring device (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.873ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 828 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D437\" d=\"M287 628Q287 635 230 637Q207 637 200 638T193 647Q193 655 197 667T204 682Q206 683 403 683Q570 682 590 682T630 676Q702 659 752 597T803 431Q803 275 696 151T444 3L430 1L236 0H125H72Q48 0 41 2T33 11Q33 13 36 25Q40 41 44 43T67 46Q94 46 127 49Q141 52 146 61Q149 65 218 339T287 628ZM703 469Q703 507 692 537T666 584T629 613T590 629T555 636Q553 636 541 636T512 636T479 637H436Q392 637 386 627Q384 623 313 339T242 52Q242 48 253 48T330 47Q335 47 349 47T373 46Q499 46 581 128Q617 164 64