(2010121,'schmidt-decomposition','quantum-entanglement','Schmidt Decomposition','<p>Schmidt Decomposition is a fundamental theorem in quantum information theory that provides a canonical representation for any pure state of a bipartite system. It asserts that for any state \\( |\\Psi\\rangle_{AB} \\) in a composite Hilbert space, there exist orthonormal bases \\( \\{|u_i\\rangle_A\\} \\) and \\( \\{|v_i\\rangle_B\\} \\) such that the state can be expressed as a single sum over a diagonal form.</p>\n                    <h3>1. The Mathematical Theorem</h3>\n                    <p>The decomposition is written as:\n                    \\[ |\\Psi\\rangle_{AB} = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sqrt{\\lambda_i} |u_i\\rangle_A |v_i\\rangle_B \\]\n                    where \\( \\lambda_i \\ge 0 \\) are the <strong>Schmidt Coefficients</strong> and satisfy the normalization condition \\( \\sum \\lambda_i = 1 \\). This theorem is powerful because it simplifies the study of bipartite entanglement by reducing a double sum over all basis combinations to a single sum, effectively \"pairing up\" the states of the two subsystems.</p>\n                    <h3>2. Physical Significance</h3>\n                    <p>The Schmidt Decomposition is unique (up to phase factors) if the coefficients are distinct. It reveals the shared structure between two particles: if you measure particle A in the Schmidt basis and obtain result \\( |u_i\\rangle \\), you know with 100% certainty that particle B is in the state \\( |v_i\\rangle \\). This perfect correlation is the essence of entanglement and is used to define the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schmidt-rank\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schmidt Rank</strong></a>.</p>','Schmidt Decomposition is a fundamental theorem in quantum information theory that provides a canonical representation for any pure state of a bipartite system. It asserts that for any state in a composite Hilbert space, there exist orthonormal bases and such that the state can be expressed as a single sum over a diagonal form. The decomposition is written as: where are the Schmidt Coefficients and satisfy the normalization condition.','Schmidt Decomposition is a fundamental theorem in quantum information theory that provides a canonical representation for any pure state of a bipartite system. It asserts that for any state <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.871ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 2595 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\" d=\"M340 622Q338 623 335 625T331 629T325 631T314 634T298 635T274 636T239 637H212V683H224Q248 680 389 680T554 683H566V637H539Q479 637 464 635T439 622L438 407Q438 192 439 192Q443 193 449 195T474 207T507 232T536 276T557 344Q560 365 562 417T573 493Q587 536 620 544Q627 546 671 546H715L722 540V515Q714 509 708 509Q680 505 671 476T658 392T644 307Q599 177 451 153L438 151V106L439 61Q446 54 451 52T476 48T539 46H566V0H554Q530 3 389 3T224 0H212V46H239Q259 46 273 46T298 47T314 48T325 51T331 54T335 57T340 61V151Q126 178 117 406Q115 503 69 509Q55 509 55 526Q55 541 59 543T86 546H107H120Q150 546 161 543T184 528Q198 514 204 493Q212 472 213 420T226 316T272 230Q287 216 303 207T330 194L339 192Q340 192 340 407V622Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\" d=\"M55 732Q56 739 61 744T75 750Q85 750 92 740Q95 733 186 494T278 250T187 6T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q67 -250 62 -245T55 -232Q55 -227 145 11Q236 248 236 250T145 489Q55 727 55 732Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\" d=\"M208 74Q208 50 254 46Q272 46 272 35Q272 34 270 22Q267 8 264 4T251 0Q249 0 239 0T205 1T141 2Q70 2 50 0H42Q35 7 35 11Q37 38 48 46H62Q132 49 164 96Q170 102 345 401T523 704Q530 716 547 716H555H572Q578 707 578 706L606 383Q634 60 636 57Q641 46 701 46Q726 46 726 36Q726 34 723 22Q720 7 718