(2010122,'schmidt-rank','quantum-entanglement','Schmidt Rank','<p>The Schmidt Rank (or Schmidt Number) is the number of non-zero coefficients in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schmidt-decomposition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schmidt Decomposition</strong></a> of a bipartite pure state. It serves as the most basic qualitative measure of entanglement: a state is entangled if and only if its Schmidt rank is greater than one.</p>\n                    <h3>1. Characterizing Entanglement</h3>\n                    <p>For a pure state \\( |\\Psi\\rangle_{AB} \\), the Schmidt rank \\( k \\) tells us how many degrees of freedom are \"involved\" in the entanglement. If \\( k=1 \\), the state is a product state (separable), meaning the particles are completely independent. If \\( k \\gt 1 \\), the state is entangled. A <strong>Maximally Entangled State</strong> (like a Bell state) is one where the Schmidt rank is equal to the dimension of the smaller Hilbert space and all coefficients are equal (\\( \\lambda_i = 1/k \\)).</p>\n                    <h3>2. Invariance under Local Operations</h3>\n                    <p>The Schmidt rank is a \"Local Unitary Invariant,\" meaning it cannot be changed by performing operations only one of the particles. No matter how much you rotate or manipulate particle A, the number of correlated states in the decomposition remains constant. This makes the Schmidt rank a fundamental property of the entanglement itself, independent of the specific basis chosen by an observer.</p>','The Schmidt Rank (or Schmidt Number) is the number of non-zero coefficients in the Schmidt Decomposition of a bipartite pure state. It serves as the most basic qualitative measure of entanglement: a state is entangled if and only if its Schmidt rank is greater than one. For a pure state, the Schmidt rank tells us how many degrees of freedom are \"involved\" in the entanglement.','The Schmidt Rank (or Schmidt Number) is the number of non-zero coefficients in the Schmi