(2010124,'chsh-inequality','quantum-entanglement','CHSH Inequality','<p>The CHSH Inequality (Clauser, Horne, Shimony, and Holt) is the most common experimental formulation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bells-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bell\'s Theorem</strong></a>. It provides a definitive mathematical bound that any theory based on local hidden variables must satisfy, allowing for a direct experimental test of the fundamental nature of reality.</p>\n                    <h3>1. The Classical Bound</h3>\n                    <p>Consider two observers, Alice and Bob, performing measurements on entangled particles using two different settings each (\\( a, a\' \\) for Alice and \\( b, b\' \\) for Bob). In any local realistic theory, the correlation function \\( S \\) must satisfy the inequality:\n                    \\[ |S| = |E(a,b) - E(a,b\') + E(a\',b) + E(a\',b\')| \\le 2 \\]\n                    This limit of 2 is a consequence of the fact that in a local world, Alice\'s choice of setting cannot influence Bob\'s outcome, and the values are determined by pre-existing variables.</p>\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-violation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Violation</strong></a>: Tsirelson\'s Bound</h3>\n                    <p>Quantum mechanics predicts that for certain entangled states (like the Bell states) and specific measurement angles, the value of \\( S \\) can reach \\( 2\\sqrt{2} \\approx 2.828 \\). This value, known as <strong>Tsirelson\'s Bound</strong>, is the maximum possible violation allowed by quantum theory. Experimental violations of the CHSH inequality have definitively ruled out local realism, proving that the universe is fundamentally <strong>Non-Local</strong>.</p>','The CHSH Inequality (Clauser, Horne, Shimony, and Holt) is the most common experimental formulation of Bell\'s Theorem. It provides a definitive mathematical bound that any theory based on local hidden variables must satisfy, allowing for a direct experimental test of the fundamental nature of reality. Consider two observers, Alice and Bob, performing measurements on entangled particles using two different settings each ( for Alice and for Bob).','The CHSH Inequality (Clauser, Horne, Shimony, and Holt) is the most common experimental formulation of Bell\'s Theorem. It provides a definitive mathematical bound that any theory based on local hidden variables must satisfy, allowing for a direct experimental test of the fundamental nature of reality. Consider two observers, Alice and Bob, performing measurements on entangled particles using two different settings each (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.027ex\" height=\"2.156ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -759 1780.1 953\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q331 441 370 392Q386 422 416 422Q429 422 439 414T449 394Q449 381 412 234T374 68Q374 43 381 35T402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487Q506 153 506 144Q506 138 501 117T481 63T449 13Q436 0 417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM351 328Q351 334 346 350T323 385T277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q217 26 254 59T298 110Q300 114 325 217T351 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\" d=\"M78 35T78 60T94 103T137 121Q165 121 187 96T210 8Q210 -27 201 -60T180 -117T154 -158T130 -185T117 -194Q113 -194 104 -185T95 -172Q95 -168 106 -156T131 -126T157 -76T173 -3V9L172 8Q170 7 167 6T161 3T152 1T140 0Q113 0 96 17Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-V-2032\" d=\"M79 43Q73 43 52 49T30 61Q30 68 85 293T146 528Q161 560 198 560Q218 560 240 545T262 501Q262 496 260 486Q259 479 173 263T84 45T79 43Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale