(2010126,'mixed-state','quantum-entanglement','Mixed State','<p>A Mixed State represents a quantum system where our knowledge is incomplete, not because of the uncertainty principle, but because the system is part of a statistical ensemble or is entangled with an unobserved environment. Unlike a pure state, which is a single vector in Hilbert space, a mixed state must be described by a <strong>Density Operator (\\( \\rho \\))</strong>.</p>\n                    <h3>1. Statistical Ensembles</h3>\n                    <p>A mixed state is a classical probability distribution over several pure states:\n                    \\[ \\rho = \\sum_i p_i |\\psi_i\\rangle \\langle \\psi_i| \\]\n                    where \\( p_i \\) is the probability of the system being in state \\( |\\psi_i\\rangle \\). It is vital to understand that a mixture of states (e.g., 50% spin-up and 50% spin-down) is physically different from a <strong>Superposition</strong> (spin-up + spin-down). In a superposition, the system can still exhibit interference; in a mixed state, the phase information between the states has been lost.</p>\n                    <h3>2. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Density Matrix</strong></a> Criterion</h3>\n                    <p>A state is pure if and only if \\( \\rho^2 = \\rho \\) (it is a projection operator). For a mixed state, \\( \\text{Tr}(\\rho^2) \\lt 1 \\). Mixed states are the rule rather than the exception in the real world, as any interaction with the environment (decoherence) or any measurement of a subsystem of an entangled pair will result in a mixed state description for the remaining system.</p>','A Mixed State represents a quantum system where our knowledge is incomplete, not because of the uncertainty principle, but because the system is part of a statistical ensemble or is entangled with an unobserved environment. Unlike a pure state, which is a single vector in Hilbert space, a mixed state must be described by a Density Operator. A mixed state is a classical probability distribution over several pure states: where is the probability of the system being in state.','A Mixed State represents a quantum system where our knowledge is incomplete, not because of the uncertainty principle, but because the system is part of a statistical ensemble or is entangled with an unobserved environment. Unlike a pure state, which is a single vector in Hilbert space, a mixed state must be described by a Density Operator (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.17ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 517 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g></g></g></svg>). A mixed state is a classical probability distribution over several pure states:\n                    <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.697ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.152ex\" height=\"4.847ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -950 7581.1 2142.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71