(2010127,'monogamy-of-entanglement','quantum-entanglement','Monogamy of Entanglement','<p>The Monogamy of Entanglement is a fundamental principle in quantum information theory that restricts the distribution of quantum correlations among multiple parties. Unlike classical correlations, which can be shared indefinitely (e.g., multiple people can have identical copies of a digital file), quantum entanglement is a \"limited resource\" that cannot be freely distributed without degrading existing links.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Exclusivity of Quantum Bonds</h3>\n                    <p>In its simplest form, monogamy states that if two qubits A and B are maximally entangled (in a <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bell State</strong></a>), they cannot be entangled with any third qubit C. Any attempt to entangle A with C necessarily reduces the degree of entanglement between A and B. This principle ensures the security of <strong>Quantum Key Distribution (QKD)</strong>, as an eavesdropper (Eve) attempting to entangle herself with the signal will inevitably leave a detectable trace by weakening the entanglement between the legitimate parties.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Coffman-Kundu-Wootters (CKW) Inequality</h3>\n                    <p>For three-qubit systems, monogamy is mathematically formalized by the <strong>CKW Inequality</strong> (2000). It uses the <strong>Tangle</strong> (\\( \\tau \\)), defined as the square of the <strong>Concurrence</strong>, to quantify entanglement. The inequality states that the sum of the entanglements between A-B and A-C cannot exceed the total entanglement between A and the joint system BC:\n                    \\[ \\tau(A|B) + \\tau(A|C) \\le \\tau(A|BC) \\]\n                    This bound proves that quantum correlations are fundamentally \"private\" and obey strict conservation-like laws within multipartite systems.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Global vs. Local Correlations</h3>\n                    <p>Monogamy highlights the trade-off between local and global information. In a highly entangled multipartite state like the <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>GHZ State</strong></a>, the particles share strong global correlations, but any two individual particles viewed in isolation (the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-representation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/reduced-density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Reduced Density Matrix</strong></a></a>) show zero entanglement. This \"all-or-nothing\" entanglement structure is a direct consequence of the monogamy constraints imposed by the geometry of Hilbert space.</p>','The Monogamy of Entanglement is a fundamental principle in quantum information theory that restricts the distribution of quantum correlations among multiple parties. Unlike classical correlations, which can be shared indefinitely (e.g., multiple people can have identical copies of a digital file), quantum entanglement is a \"limited resource\" that cannot be freely distributed without degrading existing links. In its simplest form, monogamy states that if two qubits A and B are maximally entangled (in a Bell State), they cannot be entangled with any third qubit C.','The Monogamy of Entanglement is a fundamental principle in quantum information theory that restricts the distribution of quantum correlations among multiple parties. Unlike classical correlations, which can be shared indefinitely (e.g., multiple people can have identical copies of a digital file), quantum entanglement is a \"limited resource\" that cannot be freely distributed without degrading existing links. In its simplest form, monogamy states that if two qubits A and B are maximally entangled (in a Bell State), they cannot be entangled with any third qubit C.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"28.423ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 12563 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70F\" d=\"M39 284Q18 284 18 294Q18 301 45 338T99 398Q134 425 164 429Q170 431 332 431Q492 431 497 429Q517 424 517 402Q517 388 508 376T485 360Q479 358 389 358T299 356Q298 355 283 274T251 109T233 20Q228 5 215 -4T186 -13Q153 -13 153 20V30L203 192Q214 228 227 272T248 336L254 357Q254 358 208 358Q206 358 197 358T183 359Q105 359 61 295Q56 287 53 286T39 284Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\" d=\"M208 74Q208 50 254 46Q272 46 272 35Q272 34 270 22Q267 8 264 4T251 0Q249 0 239 0T205 1T141 2Q70 2 50 0H42Q35 7 35 11Q37 38 48 46H62Q132 49 164 96Q170 102 345 401T523 704Q530 716 547 716H555H572Q578 707 578 706L606 383Q634 60 636 57Q641 46 701 46Q726 46 726 36Q726 34 723 22Q720 7 718 4T704 0Q701 0 690 0T651 1T578 2Q484 2 455 0H443Q437 6 437 9T439 27Q443 40 445 43L449 46H469Q523 49 533 63L521 213H283L249 155Q208 86 208 74ZM516 260Q516 271 504 416T490 562L463 519Q447 492 400 412L310 260L413 259Q516 259 516 260Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\" d=\"M231 637Q204 637 199 638T194 649Q194 676 205 682Q206 683 335 683Q594 683 608 681Q671 671 713 636T756 544Q756 480 698 429T565 360L555 357Q619 348 660 311T702 219Q702 146 630 78T453 1Q446 0 242 0Q42 0 39 2Q35 5 35 10Q35 17 37 24Q42 43 47 45Q51 46 62 46H68Q95 46 128 49Q142 52 147 61Q150 65 219 339T288 628Q288 635 231 637ZM649 544Q649 574 634 600T585 634Q578 636 493 637Q473 637 451 637T416 636H403Q388 635 384 626Q382 622 352 506Q352 503 351 500L320 374H401Q482 374 4