(2010128,'quantum-teleportation','quantum-entanglement','Quantum Teleportation','<p>Quantum Teleportation is a protocol that allows the transfer of an unknown quantum state (\\( |\\psi\\rangle \\)) from one location to another using pre-shared <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entanglement</strong></a> and classical communication. It is important to note that teleportation does not transport matter or energy, nor does it allow for faster-than-light communication, as the receiver requires classical information to \"reconstruct\" the state.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Protocol Setup</h3>\n                    <p>The process involves three qubits: the state to be teleported (C) held by Alice, and a <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bell Pair</strong></a> shared between Alice (A) and Bob (B). The initial state of the tripartite system is:\n                    \\[ |\\Psi\\rangle_{total} = |\\psi\\rangle_C \\otimes |\\Phi^+\\rangle_{AB} \\]\n                    Alice performs a <strong>Bell State Measurement (BSM)</strong> on qubits C and A, which projects them onto one of the four Bell states. This measurement entangles Alice\'s local qubits but, due to the initial entanglement between A and B, it also affects Bob\'s distant qubit.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Classical Communication and Unitary Correction</h3>\n                    <p>Alice\'s measurement yields two classical bits of information, representing which of the four Bell states she obtained. She transmits these bits to Bob via a classical channel. Depending on Alice\'s result, Bob\'s qubit (B) will be in one of four states that are <strong>Unitarily Equivalent</strong> to the original state \\( |\\psi\\rangle \\):\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Result 00: Bob applies \\( I \\) (No change)</li>\n                        <li>Result 01: Bob applies \\( X \\) (Bit flip)</li>\n                        <li>Result 10: Bob applies \\( Z \\) (Phase flip)</li>\n                        <li>Result 11: Bob applies \\( XZ \\) (Both)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    After Bob applies the corresponding <strong>Pauli Correction</strong>, his qubit becomes an exact replica of Alice\'s original state \\( |\\psi\\rangle \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. No-Cloning Theorem and State Destruction</h3>\n                    <p>The teleportation process adheres to the <strong>No-Cloning Theorem</strong> because Alice\'s original qubit is destroyed by her measurement. The information is not \"copied\" but \"moved.\" This protocol is the backbone of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-teleportation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Networks</strong></a> and the concept of <strong>Entanglement Swapping</strong>, where entanglement is generated between parties who have never interacted directly.</p>','Quantum Teleportation is a protocol that allows the transfer of an unknown quantum state from one location to another using pre-shared Entanglement and classical communication. It is important to note that teleportation does not transport matter or energy, nor does it allow for faster-than-light communication, as the receiver requires classical information to \"reconstruct\" the state. The process involves three qubits: the state to be teleported (C) held by Alice, and a Bell Pair shared between Alice (A) and Bob (B).','Quantum Teleportation is a protocol that allows the transfer of an unknown quantum state (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.982ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1318 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 41