g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S4-2192\" transform=\"translate(372.5,0)\"></use><svg width=\"472.5\" height=\"865\" x=\"0\" y=\"-182\" viewBox=\"118.1 -182 472.5 865\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S4-2212\" transform=\"scale(0.911,1)\"></use></svg></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mpadded\" transform=\"translate(0,870.8) scale(0.707)\"><g transform=\"translate(278,-200)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(345,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1108,0)\"></g></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(25431.4,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(25709.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(811,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(27120.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"local-unitary-520af705\",\"joint-measurement-ad197438\",\"entanglement-usage-5d99bc03\"]'),
(2010130,'multipartite-entanglement','quantum-entanglement','Multipartite Entanglement','<p>Multipartite Entanglement refers to quantum correlations existing between more than two parties. Unlike bipartite systems, which can be fully described by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schmidt-decomposition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schmidt Decomposition</strong></a>, multipartite systems exhibit a rich and complex structure that cannot be categorized by a single entanglement measure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Classification: SLOCC and Entanglement Classes</h3>\n                    <p>In multipartite systems, states are classified based on whether they can be transformed into each other using <strong>Stochastic Local Operations and Classical Communication (SLOCC)</strong>. For a three-qubit system, there are two distinct, non-equivalent classes of genuine tripartite entanglement:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>GHZ-Class</strong></a>: States like \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|000\\rangle + |111\\rangle) \\). These represent \"all-or-nothing\" entanglement.</li>\n                        <li><strong>W-Class</strong>: States like \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(|100\\rangle + |010\\rangle + |001\\rangle) \\). These represent \"distributed\" entanglement.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    Because these classes cannot be interconverted via SLOCC, they represent fundamentally different types of quantum resources.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Robustness vs. Fragility</h3>\n                    <p>The difference between GHZ and W states is most evident in their robustness to particle loss. If one qubit is measured or lost from a <strong>GHZ State</strong>, the remaining two qubits become completely unentangled (a mixed state with zero correlation). In contrast, if one qubit is lost from a <strong>W State</strong>, the remaining two qubits retain a high degree of bipartite entanglement. This makes W-states more suitable for robust quantum storage, while GHZ-states are better for precise sensing and non-locality proofs.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Entanglement Measures and Complexity</h3>\n                    <p>Quantifying multipartite entanglement requires complex invariants like the <strong>3-Tangle</strong> or the <strong>Geometric Measure of Entanglement</strong>. As the number of particles (N) increases, the number of SLOCC classes grows exponentially, leading to the study of <strong>Cluster States</strong> and <strong>Graph States</strong>, which are the foundations of <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Measurement-Based Quantum Computation</strong></a>.</p>','Multipartite Entanglement refers to quantum correlations existing between more than two parties. Unlike bipartite systems, which can be fully described by Schmidt Decomposition, multipartite systems exhibit a rich and complex structure that cannot be categorized by a single entanglement measure. In multipartite systems, states are classified based on whether they can be transformed into each other using Stochastic Local Operations and Classical Communication (SLOCC).','Multipartite Entanglement refers to quantum correlations existing between more than two parties. Unlike bipartite systems, which can be fully described by Schmidt Decomposition, multipartite systems exhibit a rich and complex structure that cannot be categorized by a single entanglement measure. In multipartite systems, states are classified based on whether they can be transformed into each other using Stochastic Local Operations and Classical Communication (SLOCC).','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.334ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.491ex\" height=\"3.291ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864.9 7731.2 1454.5\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 