f=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4174.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5175.2,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(5453.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(1000,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6953.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7342.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"equivalence-classes-e0511f0b\",\"robust-entanglement-6c416d89\",\"non-bipartite-56cf55a1\"]'),
(2010131,'ghz-state','quantum-entanglement','The GHZ State','<p>The <strong>GHZ State</strong> (Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger) is a specific type of <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/multipartite-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Multipartite Entanglement</strong></a></a> involving three or more qubits. It is often referred to as a \"Schrödinger\'s Cat state\" for multiple particles because it represents a macroscopic superposition of two distinct states across the entire system.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Definition and Maximality</h3>\n                    <p>A 3-qubit GHZ state is defined as:\n                    \\[ |GHZ\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|000\\rangle + |111\\rangle) \\]\n                    It is a <strong>Maximally Entangled</strong> state in the sense that it maximizes certain multipartite entanglement measures. However, it is also the most \"fragile\" form of entanglement; measuring a single qubit in the computational basis collapses the entire state into a purely classical configuration (either 000 or 111), destroying all quantum correlations between the remaining parties.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-locality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Non-Locality</strong></a> without Inequalities</h3>\n                    <p>The GHZ state is historically significant because it provides a \"non-statistical\" proof the failure of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/local-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Local Realism</strong></a>. While <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bells-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bell\'s Theorem</strong></a></a> relies on statistical averages over many trials, the GHZ paradox shows that for a single measurement, local hidden variable theories predict an outcome (e.g., +1) that is the exact opposite of the quantum prediction (-1). This \"all-or-nothing\" contradiction makes the GHZ state the ultimate tool for demonstrating the non-local nature of reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Eigenstates of Stabilizers</h3>\n                    <p>In the <strong>Stabilizer Formalism</strong>, the GHZ state is uniquely defined as the simultaneous eigenstate of the operators \\( X_1 X_2 X_3 \\), \\( Z_1 Z_2 \\), and \\( Z_2 Z_3 \\). This representation is crucial for <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Error Correction</strong></a>, where the GHZ state serves as a resource for detecting and correcting bit-flip and phase-flip errors across a distributed network.</p>','The GHZ State (Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger) is a specific type of Multipartite Entanglement involving three or more qubits. It is often referred to as a \"Schrödinger\'s Cat state\" for multiple particles because it represents a macroscopic superposition of two distinct states across the entire system. A 3-qubit GHZ state is defined as: It is a Maximally Entangled state in the sense that it maximizes certain multipartite entanglement measures.','The GHZ State (Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger) is a specific type of Multipartite Entanglement involving three or more qubits. It is often referred to as a \"Schrödinger\'s Cat state\" for multiple particles because it represents a macroscopic superposition of two distinct states across the entire system. A 3-qubit GHZ state is defined as:\n                    <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.308ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"28.337ex\" height=\"5.344ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1342 12525 2362\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43A\" d=\"M50 252Q50 367 117 473T286 641T490 704Q580 704 633 653Q642 643 648 636T656 626L657 62