(2010132,'state-representation','quantum-entanglement','State Representation of Entanglement','<p>The mathematical representation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entanglement</strong></a> requires the formal language of <strong>Tensor Product Spaces</strong> and <strong>Density Operators</strong>. For a bipartite system composed of subsystems A and B, the total <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a> is defined as \\( \\mathcal{H}_{total} = \\mathcal{H}_A \\otimes \\mathcal{H}_B \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Pure States and Factorizability</h3>\n                    <p>A system is in a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/pure-state\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Pure State</strong></a> if it can be described by a single state vector \\( |\\Psi\\rangle \\). The definitive test for entanglement in pure states is <strong>Factorizability</strong>. If \\( |\\Psi\\rangle \\) can be written as a simple tensor product \\( |\\phi\\rangle_A \\otimes |\\chi\\rangle_B \\), it is <strong>Separable</strong> (unentangled). If no such factorization exists, the state is entangled. In coordinate basis, this means the coefficients of the state \\( c_{ij} \\) in \\( \\sum c_{ij} |i\\rangle |j\\rangle \\) cannot be written as a product \\( a_i b_j \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Mixed States and the Density Matrix</h3>\n                    <p>For systems interacting with an environment, we use the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Density Matrix</strong></a> \\( \\rho = \\sum p_k |\\psi_k\\rangle\\langle\\psi_k| \\). A mixed state is entangled if it cannot be written as a convex sum of product states:\n                    \\[ \\rho_{sep} = \\sum p_k (\\rho_A^k \\otimes \\rho_B^k) \\]\n                    Determining entanglement for mixed states is notoriously difficult and often requires the <strong>Peres-Horodecki Criterion</strong> (Partial Transpose), which states that if the partial transpose of \\( \\rho \\) has negative eigenvalues, the state is entangled.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Partial Trace</h3>\n                    <p>To find the local state of a subsystem (e.g., Alice\'s qubit), we perform a <strong>Partial Trace</strong> over the other degrees of freedom: \\( \\rho_A = \\text{Tr}_B(\\rho_{AB}) \\). A hallmark of entanglement is that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/reduced-density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Reduced Density Matrix</strong></a> of a pure entangled state is always mixed. This \"local ignorance\" is not due to a lack of knowledge, but is a fundamental property of the global entanglement: information about the individual particles is literally encoded in the <em>relationships</em> between them, not in the particles themselves.</p>','The mathematical representation of Entanglement requires the formal language of Tensor Product Spaces and Density Operators. For a bipartite system composed of subsystems A and B, the total Hilbert Space is defined as. A system is in a Pure State if it can be described by a single state vector.','The mathematical representation of Entanglement requires the formal language of Tensor Product Spaces and Density Operators. For a bipartite system composed of subsystems A and B, the total Hilbert Space is defined as <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.75ex\" height=\"1.902ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 7845.3 840.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-48\" d=\"M18 487Q18 496 29 517T67 566T127 621T216 665T330 683Q359 683 376 669T397 643T400 622Q400 584 382 488T348 343Q348 342 467 342H587L594 366Q615 440 648 534T690 641Q701 656 723 669T764 683Q783 683 783 672L750 578Q716 485 677 346T625 101Q624 92 623 82T622 65T621 56Q621 20 658 20Q666 20 701 25Q709 52 736 69T785 87Q803 87 803 75