/g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(550,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(550,-281.1) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D434\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7257,0)\"><use data-c=\"2297\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2297\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\" transform=\"translate(8257.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(550,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(550,-257.7) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9393.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"space-composition-963581ee\",\"subsystem-analysis-04859648\",\"entanglement-test-9312ee27\"]'),
(2010133,'bell-state','quantum-entanglement','Bell States','<p>The Bell States (or EPR pairs) are the four specific quantum states that represent the <strong>Maximum Possible Entanglement</strong> between two qubits. They form a complete, orthonormal basis for the 4-dimensional Hilbert space of a 2-qubit system, and serve as the fundamental \"currency\" for almost all <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Communication Protocols</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Four Maximally Entangled States</h3>\n                    <p>The Bell basis consists of two \"Phi\" states (correlated) and two \"Psi\" states (anti-correlated):\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>\\( |\\Phi^+\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle) \\)</li>\n                        <li>\\( |\\Phi^-\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|00\\rangle - |11\\rangle) \\)</li>\n                        <li>\\( |\\Psi^+\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|01\\rangle + |10\\rangle) \\)</li>\n                        <li>\\( |\\Psi^-\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|01\\rangle - |10\\rangle) \\)</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    The state \\( |\\Psi^-\\rangle \\) is particularly notable as the <strong>Singlet State</strong>, which is invariant under any simultaneous rotation of both qubits (it has total angular momentum \\( J=0 \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Mathematical Properties</h3>\n                    <p>Bell states are characterized by <strong>Maximum Uncertainty</strong> for local measurements. If you measure Alice\'s qubit in a Bell pair, you have a 50/50 chance of getting 0 or 1, regardless of the measurement basis. However, Bob\'s outcome is then 100% determined relative to Alice\'s. This reflects that in a Bell state, the <strong>Joint Information</strong> is perfectly known, while the <strong>Individual Information</strong> is non-existent—the defining trait of maximal <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entanglement</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Applications information Theory</h3>\n                    <p>Bell states are the essential resource for:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-teleportation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Teleportation</strong></a>: Used as the \"bridge\" to move states.</li>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/superdense-coding\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Superdense Coding</strong></a>: Used to double classical capacity.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Entanglement Swapping</strong>: The process of creating Bell states between non-interacting particles.</li>\n                    </ul></p>','The Bell States (or EPR pairs) are the four specific quantum states that represent the Maximum Possible Entanglement between two qubits. They form a complete, orthonormal basis for the 4-dimensional Hilbert space of a 2-qubit system, and serve as the fundamental \"currency\" for almost all Quantum Communication Protocols. The Bell basis consists of two \"Phi\" states (correlated) and two \"Psi\" states (anti-correlated): The state is particularly notable as the Singlet State, which is invariant under any simultaneous rotation of both qubits (it has total angular momentum ).','The Bell States (or EPR pairs) are the four specific quantum states that represent the Maximum Possible Entanglement between two qubits. They form a complete, orthonormal basis for the 4-dimensional Hilbert space of a 2-qubit system, and serve as the fundamental \"currency\" for almost all Quantum Communication Protocols. The Bell basis consists of two \"Phi\" states (correlated) and two \"Psi\" states (anti-correlated):\n                    \n                        <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.334ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"22.821ex\" height=\"3.291ex\" role=\"