=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(8308.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9308.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9697.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"hilbert-foundation-99e4905b\",\"rotational-invariance-66be389b\",\"entropy-peak-371df26a\"]'),
(2010134,'ontological-interpretation','quantum-interpretations','Ontological Interpretations of Quantum Mechanics','<p>The <strong>Ontological Interpretation</strong> of quantum mechanics seeks to provide a realist description of quantum phenomena. Unlike the standard Copenhagen view, which treats the wave function as a tool for calculating probabilities, ontological interpretations posit that quantum systems possess definite, objective properties independent of measurement. This approach aims to resolve the <strong>Measurement Problem</strong> by eliminating the need for a subjective observer or a non-physical collapse of the wave function.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Reality of the Quantum Potential</h3>\n                    <p>At the heart of the ontological framework is the introduction of the <strong>Quantum Potential</strong> (\\( Q \\)). By expressing the Schrödinger equation in polar form (\\( \\psi = R e^{iS/\\hbar} \\)), the dynamics of a particle can be separated into a classical-like Hamilton-Jacobi equation and a continuity equation. The quantum potential emerges as a term that influences the particle\'s trajectory, representing a form of \"active information\" that depends on the shape of the wave function.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-locality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Non-Locality</strong></a> and Holism</h3>\n                    <p>Ontological interpretations are inherently <strong>Non-Local</strong>. In an entangled multi-particle state, the motion of one particle is instantaneously influenced by the positions of all others, regardless of their spatial separation. This suggests a deeply <strong>Holistic</strong> structure to reality, where the parts cannot be fully understood without reference to the whole, aligning with the violations of Bell\'s inequalities.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Beables vs. Observables</h3>\n                    <p>To distinguish between real physical entities and the results of measurement, John Bell introduced the concept of <strong>Beables</strong>. In an ontological theory, the position of a particle and the wave function itself are beables—they exist objectively. <strong>Observables</strong>, on the other hand, are viewed as secondary properties that emerge during the interaction between a system and a measuring apparatus.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Implicate and Explicate Order</h3>\n                    <p>David Bohm extended the ontological interpretation into a broader framework known as the <strong>Implicate Order</strong>. In this view, the manifest world (the explicate order) is a projection of a deeper, undivided wholeness where everything is enfolded into everything else. Quantum mechanics provides a mathematical window into this underlying enfolded reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Causal Determinism in the Quantum Realm</h3>\n                    <p>By ascribing definite trajectories to particles, the ontological interpretation restores <strong>Causal Determinism</strong>. If the initial positions and the initial wave function are known exactly, the future evolution of the system is perfectly predictable. The apparent randomness observed in experiments is attributed to our ignorance of the precise initial conditions.</p>','The Ontological Interpretation of quantum mechanics seeks to provide a realist description of quantum phenomena. Unlike the standard Copenhagen view, which treats the wave function as a tool for calculating probabilities, ontological interpretations posit that quantum systems possess definite, objective properties independent of measurement. This approach aims to resolve the Measurement Problem by eliminating the need for a subjective observer or a non-physical collapse of the wave function.','The Ontological Interpretation of quantum mechanics seeks to provide a realist description of quantum phenomena. Unlike the standard Copenhagen view, which treats the wave function as a tool for calculating probabilities, ontological interpretations posit that quantum systems possess definite, objective properties independent of measurement. This approach aims to resolve the Measurement Problem by eliminating the need for a subjective observer or a non-physical collapse of the wave function.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.79ex\" height=\"2.032ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -704 791 898\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D444\" d=\"M399 -80Q399 -47 400 -30T402 -11V-7L387 -11Q341 -22 303 -22Q208 -22 138 35T51 201Q50 209 50 244Q50 346 98 438T227 601Q351 704 476 704Q514 704 524 703Q621 689 680 617T740 435Q740 255 592 107Q529 47 461 16L444 8V3Q444 2 449 -24T470 -66T516 -82Q551 -82 583 -60T625 -3Q631 11 638 11Q647 11 649 2Q649 -6 639 -34T611 -100T557 -165T481 -194Q399 -194 399 -87V-80ZM636 468Q636 523 621 564T580 625T530 655T477 665Q429 665 379 640Q277 591 215 464T153 216Q153 110 207 59Q231 38 236 38V46Q236 86 269 120T347 155Q372 155 390 144T417 114T429 82T435 55L448 64Q512 108 557 185T619 334T636 468ZM314 18Q362 18 404 39L403 49Q399 104 366 115Q354 117 347 117Q344 117 341 117T337 118Q317 118 296 98T274 52Q274 18 314 18Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"non-classical-force-96e8c227\",\"dynamic-evolution-33038d72\",\"origin-of-chance-f54dd2b4\"]'),
(2010135,'john-von-neumann','quantum-interpretations','John von Neumann and the Measurement Problem','<p><strong>John von Neumann</strong> provided the first rigorous mathematical axiomatization of quantum theory. By situating theory within <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>, he clarified the relationship between physical states and linear operators, while exposing the conceptual rift known as the measurement problem.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Hilbert Space and the Density Matrix</h3>\n                    <p>Von Neumann established that quantum states are vectors in a complex <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>. He introduced the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Density Matrix</strong></a> (\\( \\rho \\)) to describe both pure and mixed states, enabling a unified treatment of quantum statistical mechanics and linking information theory with thermodynamics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Process 1 and Process 2</h3>\n                    <p>Von Neumann distinguished between two types of evolution. <strong>Process 2</strong> is the continuous, unitary, and deterministic evolution governed by the Schrödinger equation. <strong>Process 1</strong> is the discontinuous, non-unitary \"collapse\" that occurs upon measurement. The incompatibility of these two constitutes the <strong>Measurement Problem</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/von-neumann-chain\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Von Neumann Chain</strong></a> and the Heisenberg Cut</h3>\n                    <p>If the measuring device is also quantum, it should be subject to Process 2. This leads to the <strong>Von Neumann Chain</strong>: the system becomes entangled with the apparatus, then the observer\'s brain, etc. Von Neumann argued that the \"cut\" (the <strong>Heisenberg Cut</strong>) must be placed somewhere for a result to emerge.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Consciousness as the Ultimate Observer</h3>\n                    <p>In exploring the limits of the chain, von Neumann speculated that the only entity capable of terminating the chain and causing collapse is <strong>Consciousness</strong>. While controversial, this \"Von Neumann-Wigner interpretation\" remains a historically significant attempt to resolve the measurement circularity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The No-Hidden-Variables Theorem</h3>\n                    <p>Von Neumann produced a theorem asserting that no <strong>Hidden Variable</strong> theory could reproduce quantum results. While later shown to have overly restrictive assumptions, it stood for decades as a major hurdle for realist interpretations like Bohmian mechanics.</p>','John von Neumann provided the first rigorous mathematical axiomatization of quantum theory. By situating theory within Hilbert Space, he clarified the relationship between physical states and linear operators, while exposing the conceptual rift known as the measurement problem. Von Neumann established that quantum states are vectors in a complex Hilbert Space.','John von Neumann provided the first rigorous mathematical axiomatization of quantum theory. By situating theory within Hilbert Space, he clarified the relationship between physical states and linear operators, while exposing the conceptual rift known as the measurement problem. Von Neumann established that quantum states are vectors in a complex Hilbert Space.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.17ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 517 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\" d=\"M58 -216Q25 -216 23 -186Q23 -176 73 26T127 234Q143 289 182 341Q252 427 341 441Q343 441 349 441T359 442Q432 442 471 394T510 276Q510 219 486 165T425 74T345 13T266 -10H255H248Q197 -10 165 35L160 41L133 -71Q108 -168 104 -181T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216ZM424 322Q424 359 407 382T357 405Q322 405 287 376T231 300Q217 269 193 170L176 102Q193 26 260 26Q298 26 334 62Q367 92 389 158T418 266T424 322Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"wave-function-collapse-03266b1a\",\"unitary-evolution-8b94ea18\",\"epistemic-boundary-8f3c20ee\"]'),
(2010136,'psi-ontic','quantum-interpretations','Psi-Ontic: The Wave Function as Reality','<p>A <strong>\\(\\psi\\)-Ontic</strong> model is one where the quantum wave function represents a real, objective physical entity. In this view, the wave function is a fundamental constituent of the universe, much like the electromagnetic field in classical physics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Wave Function Realism</h3>\n                    <p><strong>Wave Function Realism</strong> posits that the world is fundamentally composed of the wave function evolving in a high-dimensional configuration space. Our 3D reality is seen as an emergent phenomenon. Interpretations like <strong>Many-Worlds</strong> and <strong>GRW</strong> are primary examples of ontic models.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Direct vs. Indirect Onticity</h3>\n                    <p>Ontic models can be subdivided into those where the wave function is the <em>only</em> thing that exists (pure realism) and those where it is a real field guiding other entities. In <strong>Bohmian Mechanics</strong>, the wave function is ontic because it is a physical field that exerts a real force on particles.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Robustness Against Measurement</h3>\n                    <p>A key characteristic of ontic models is that system properties are determined by the state itself, not by measurement. If the wave function is real, different physical states must be represented by different wave functions, preserving <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Implications for Non-Locality</h3>\n                    <p>If the wave function is a real object, its instantaneous collapse (or branching) across distances implies a genuine non-local connection. For an onticist, Bell correlations are the result of a single, extended physical entity being affected globally.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Challenge of Configuration Space</h3>\n                    <p>The primary objection is the <strong>Problem of Dimensionality</strong>. For \\( N \\) particles, the wave function lives in a \\( 3N \\)-dimensional configuration space. Onticists must explain how this relates to our perceived three-dimensional world.</p>','A -Ontic model is one where the quantum wave function represents a real, objective physical entity. In this view, the wave function is a fundamental constituent of the universe, much like the electromagnetic field in classical physics. Wave Function Realism posits that the world is fundamentally composed of the wave function evolving in a high-dimensional configuration space.','A <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>-Ontic model is one where the quantum wave function represents a real, objective physical entity. In this view, the wave function is a fundamental constituent of the universe, much like the electromagnetic field in classical physics. Wave Function Realism posits that the world is fundamentally composed of the wave function evolving in a high-dimensional configuration space.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"real-state-fe1623e8\",\"objective-collapse-e464bedb\",\"stationary-state-8963d27d\"]'),
(2010137,'psi-epistemic','quantum-interpretations','Psi-Epistemic: The Wave Function as Information','<p>A <strong>\\(\\psi\\)-Epistemic</strong> model interprets the wave function not as a physical object, but as a representation of an observer\'s <strong>Knowledge</strong> or information. Collapse is seen as a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/bayesian-updating\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bayesian update</strong></a> of probabilities, not a physical event.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Statistical Interpretation</h3>\n                    <p>The roots of epistemic thinking lie in Einstein\'s <strong>Ensemble Interpretation</strong>, which argued that the wave function describes a statistical collective of systems rather than a single particle. Quantum mechanics is seen as an incomplete theory providing a \"blurred\" picture of reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. QBism and Subjective Probability</h3>\n                    <p><strong>Quantum Bayesianism (QBism)</strong> takes the epistemic view further, arguing that the wave function represents the personal degrees of belief of an \"agent.\" There is no objective wave function; it is a tool agents use to make better predictions about future experiences.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Resolving the Measurement Problem</h3>\n                    <p>The epistemic view offers a clean solution to the measurement problem. If the wave function is just information, its \"collapse\" is no more mysterious than updating a probability when seeing a coin toss result. This removes the need for physical \"hidden cuts.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Locality Argument</h3>\n                    <p>Proponents are often motivated by a desire to preserve <strong>Local Causality</strong>. If the wave function is not a real object, its \"instantaneous\" change at a distance is not a physical violation of the speed of light, but merely an update in what the observer knows.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Challenges from the PBR Theorem</h3>\n                    <p>The primary challenge is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/pbr-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>PBR Theorem</strong></a>, which suggests that any model reproducing quantum predictions must treat the wave function as ontic. Epistemicists must either find flaws in theorem\'s assumptions or accept that the \"information\" behaves as an ontic entity.</p>','A -Epistemic model interprets the wave function not as a physical object, but as a representation of an observer\'s Knowledge or information. Collapse is seen as a Bayesian update of probabilities, not a physical event. The roots of epistemic thinking lie in Einstein\'s Ensemble Interpretation, which argued that the wave function describes a statistical collective of systems rather than a single particle.','A <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>-Epistemic model interprets the wave function not as a physical object, but as a representation of an observer\'s Knowledge or information. Collapse is seen as a Bayesian update of probabilities, not a physical event. The roots of epistemic thinking lie in Einstein\'s Ensemble Interpretation, which argued that the wave function describes a statistical collective of systems rather than a single particle.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"informational-shift-08e704d5\",\"personalist-interpretation-8fc8f1f4\",\"non-physical-collapse-efcafda8\"]'),
(2010138,'pilot-wave-theory','quantum-interpretations','Pilot Wave Theory: De Broglie-Bohm Mechanics','<p><strong>Pilot Wave Theory</strong>, originated by Louis de Broglie and rediscovered by David Bohm, is a deterministic, non-local hidden variable interpretation. It posits that particles have definite trajectories guided by a physical \"pilot wave\" (the wave function).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Dual Nature of Reality</h3>\n                    <p>In this framework, reality consists of both <strong>Particles</strong> and a <strong>Wave</strong>. Unlike the Copenhagen \"wave-particle duality,\" where an object is one or the other depending on measurement, Pilot Wave theory asserts that both exist simultaneously at all times.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Deterministic Trajectories</h3>\n                    <p>The motion of a particle is governed by its initial position and the <strong>Guidance Equation</strong>. If the initial configuration of the universe were known, all future positions would be determined, restoring the classical ideal of causality to the quantum realm.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Quantum Equilibrium</h3>\n                    <p>The statistical predictions of quantum mechanics are recovered through the <strong>Quantum Equilibrium Hypothesis</strong>, which states that particle distributions follow \\( P = |\\psi|^2 \\). Randomness arises purely from our ignorance of the precise initial positions of particles.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-locality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Non-Locality</strong></a> and Entanglement</h3>\n                    <p>Theory is explicitly <strong>Non-Local</strong>. The velocity of a particle depends on the position of all other entangled particles via the common wave function. This provides a direct physical mechanism for the correlations observed in EPR experiments.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Lack of Wave Function Collapse</h3>\n                    <p>There is no physical <strong>Collapse</strong>. All branches of the wave function continue to exist, but the particle only enters one of them (the \"effective\" wave function). The other branches become \"empty\" but remain the configuration space.</p>','Pilot Wave Theory, originated by Louis de Broglie and rediscovered by David Bohm, is a deterministic, non-local hidden variable interpretation. It posits that particles have definite trajectories guided by a physical \"pilot wave\" (the wave function). In this framework, reality consists of both Particles and a Wave.','Pilot Wave Theory, originated by Louis de Broglie and rediscovered by David Bohm, is a deterministic, non-local hidden variable interpretation. It posits that particles have definite trajectories guided by a physical \"pilot wave\" (the wave function). In this framework, reality consists of both Particles and a Wave.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.564ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.435ex\" height=\"2.451ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 3728.1 1083.4\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\" d=\"M287 628Q287 635 230 637Q206 637 199 638T192 648Q192 649 194 659Q200 679 203 681T397 683Q587 682 600 680Q664 669 707 631T751 530Q751 453 685 389Q616 321 507 303Q500 302 402 301H307L277 182Q247 66 247 59Q247 55 248 54T255 50T272 48T305 46H336Q342 37 342 35Q342 19 335 5Q330 0 319 0Q316 0 282 1T182 2Q120 2 87 2T51 1Q33 1 33 11Q33 13 36 25Q40 41 44 43T67 46Q94 46 127 49Q141 52 146 61Q149 65 218 339T287 628ZM645 554Q645 567 643 575T634 597T609 619T560 635Q553 636 480 637Q463 637 445 637T416 636T404 636Q391 635 386 627Q384 621 367 550T332 412T314 344Q314 342 395 342H407H430Q542 342 590 392Q617 419 631 471T645 554Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1028.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2084.6,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2362.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(3013.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"guiding-field-75b0aed5\",\"inactive-branches-3595a12b\",\"particle-positions-987fc885\"]'),
(2010139,'guidance-equation','quantum-interpretations','The Guidance Equation: Laws of Motion in Bohmian Mechanics','<p>The <strong>Guidance Equation</strong> is the fundamental equation of motion in de Broglie-Bohm mechanics. It defines the velocity of a particle as a function of the wave function, providing the link between the quantum state and the particle\'s trajectory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Derivation from the Phase</h3>\n                    <p>The equation is derived by writing the wave function in polar form (\\( \\psi = R e^{iS/\\hbar} \\)). The velocity is then proportional to the gradient of the phase \\( S \\). This reveals that the \"push\" the particle receives is determined by the local structure of the wave function\'s phase.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. First-Order Dynamics</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike Newton\'s Second Law, which is second-order (acceleration), the Guidance Equation is <strong>First-Order</strong> (velocity). This means that a particle\'s velocity is entirely determined by its current position and the wave function, with no need for an independent \"momentum\" variable.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Many-Body Configuration</h3>\n                    <p>For a system of \\( N \\) particles, the velocity of the \\( k \\)-th particle depends on the positions of <em>all</em> particles. this is source of <strong>Bohmian Non-Locality</strong>, as any change in the wave function (caused by moving one particle) instantly affects the velocities of all others.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Singularities and Nodes</h3>\n                    <p>The equation predicts that particles cannot cross the <strong>Nodes</strong> of a wave function (where \\( \\psi = 0 \\)). This explains the interference patterns in the double-slit experiment: the pilot wave creates regions where the guidance equation forbids particles from entering.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Compatibility with Schrödinger</h3>\n                    <p>The Guidance Equation is perfectly consistent with the Schrödinger equation. If the initial distribution of particles is \\( |\\psi|^2 \\), the Guidance Equation ensures that the distribution will remain \\( |\\psi|^2 \\) for all time, a property known as <strong>Equivariance</strong>.</p>','The Guidance Equation is the fundamental equation of motion in de Broglie-Bohm mechanics. It defines the velocity of a particle as a function of the wave function, providing the link between the quantum state and the particle\'s trajectory. The equation is derived by writing the wave function in polar form.','The Guidance Equation is the fundamental equation of motion in de Broglie-Bohm mechanics. It defines the velocity of a particle as a function of the wave function, providing the link between the quantum state and the particle\'s trajectory. The equation is derived by writing the wave function in polar form (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.732ex\" height=\"2.485ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -893.3 4743.5 1098.3\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H