"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1984.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(2743.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(499,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(345,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(990,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1490,0)\"><use data-c=\"210F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"probability-persistence-f3960497\",\"vector-guidance-4a9a2179\",\"origin-of-chance-f54dd2b4\"]'),
(2010140,'many-worlds-interpretation','quantum-interpretations','Many-Worlds Interpretation: Universal Wave Function','<p>The <strong>Many-Worlds Interpretation (MWI)</strong>, proposed by Hugh Everett III, asserts that the wave function never collapses. Instead, every possible outcome of a quantum measurement is realized in a different, branching branch of reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/unitary-evolution-quantum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Unitary Evolution</strong></a> Only</h3>\n                    <p>MWI is the ultimate <strong>\\(\\psi\\)-Ontic</strong> theory. It claims that the Schrödinger equation (Process 2) is the <em>only</em> law of physics. There is no Process 1 (collapse). The \"Measurement Problem\" is solved by denying that collapse ever happens.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Decoherence and Branching</h3>\n                    <p>The appearance of \"worlds\" arises through <strong>Decoherence</strong>. When a quantum system interacts with its environment, the wave function becomes entangled with countless degrees of freedom, causing different components of the superposition to become effectively isolated.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Universal Wave Function</h3>\n                    <p>In MWI, there is only one wave function: the <strong>Universal Wave Function</strong>. Every observer, measuring device, and galaxy is part of this single state. What we perceive as \"the world\" is just one relatively small, decoherent branch of this massive entity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Problem of Probability</h3>\n                    <p>Since every outcome happens with certainty in some world, MWI struggles to explain why we observe <strong>Probabilities</strong> (the Born Rule). Proponents often use Decision Theory or the \"Symmetry\" of the wave function to justify why agents should act as if probabilities exist.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Relative States</h3>\n                    <p>Everett originally called it the <strong>Relative State Formulation</strong>. An observer does not see a collapse; rather, the state of the observer becomes correlated with the state of the system. In one branch, the observer sees \"spin up,\" and in another, they see \"spin down.\"</p>','The Many-Worlds Interpretation (MWI), proposed by Hugh Everett III, asserts that the wave function never collapses. Instead, every possible outcome of a quantum measurement is realized in a different, branching branch of reality. MWI is the ultimate -Ontic theory.','The Many-Worlds Interpretation (MWI), proposed by Hugh Everett III, asserts that the wave function never collapses. Instead, every possible outcome of a quantum measurement is realized in a different, branching branch of reality. 1.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"world-splitting-a3e1ec07\",\"total-reality-eddbcf33\",\"observer-correlation-b68071d6\"]'),
(2010141,'decoherence','quantum-interpretations','Decoherence: The Emergence of the Classical World','<p><strong>Quantum Decoherence</strong> explains how the \"weirdness\" of the quantum world (superpositions and interference) disappears at macroscopic scales through interactions with the environment, resulting in a classical-looking reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Interaction with the Environment</h3>\n                    <p>No quantum system is perfectly isolated. It is constantly bombarded by photons, air molecules, and other particles. These interactions cause the system\'s phase information to leak into the <strong>Environment</strong>, effectively destroying the interference terms.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Reduced Density Matrix</h3>\n                    <p>Mathematically, decoherence is described by tracing over the degrees of freedom of the environment. This leaves the system in a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mixed-state\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mixed State</strong></a>, represented by a density matrix where the off-diagonal \"interference\" terms have decayed to zero.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Pointer States</h3>\n                    <p>Environment interactions don\'t destroy all information. Certain states, called <strong>Pointer States</strong>, are more robust and survive decoherence. These typically correspond to classical properties like position, explaining why we see objects in definite places.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Solving the \"Apparent\" Collapse</h3>\n                    <p>Decoherence explains <em>why</em> we don\'t see superpositions, but it does not technically solve the measurement problem. It explains the transition from a pure state to a mixed state, but it doesn\'t explain why only <em>one</em> outcome is actually experienced by an observer.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Timescales of Decoherence</h3>\n                    <p>The speed of decoherence is incredibly fast for macroscopic objects. A dust grain the atmosphere decoheres into a position basis in roughly \\( 10^{-13} \\) seconds, making quantum effects effectively invisible in our daily lives.</p>','Quantum Decoherence explains how the \"weirdness\" of the quantum world (superpositions and interference) disappears at macroscopic scales through interactions with the environment, resulting in a classical-looking reality. No quantum system is perfectly isolated. It is constantly bombarded by photons, air molecules, and other particles.','Quantum Decoherence explains how the \"weirdness\" of the quantum world (superpositions and interference) disappears at macroscopic scales through interactions with the environment, resulting in a classical-looking reality. No quantum system is perfectly isolated. It is constantly bombarded by photons, air molecules, and other particles.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.295ex\" height=\"2.005ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864 2340.2 886\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\" d=\"M84 237T84 250T98 270H679Q694 262 694 250T679 230H98Q84 237 84 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-33\" d=\"M127 463Q100 463 85 480T69 524Q69 579 117 622T233 665Q268 665 277 664Q351 652 390 611T430 