(2010142,'transactional-interpretation','quantum-interpretations','Transactional Interpretation: Quantum Handshakes','<p>The <strong>Transactional Interpretation (TI)</strong>, proposed by John Cramer, views quantum interactions as \"handshakes\" between <strong>Offer Waves</strong> moving forward in time and <strong>Confirmation Waves</strong> moving backward in time.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Time-Symmetric Waves</h3>\n                    <p>TI is based on the time-symmetry of the wave equation. It posits that every emitter sends an offer wave (\\( \\psi \\)) into the future, and every potential absorber sends a confirmation wave (\\( \\psi^* \\)) into the past.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Transaction</h3>\n                    <p>A \"transaction\" occurs when an offer wave and a confirmation wave interfere constructively. This handshake completes the exchange of energy and momentum, resulting in what we observe as a quantum event or measurement.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Resolving Non-Locality</h3>\n                    <p>TI explains <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-locality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Non-Locality</strong></a> through these advanced (backward-in-time) waves. Because information travels both ways, the emitter \"knows\" about the absorber\'s state instantaneously through a four-dimensional connection in spacetime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Probability as Intensity</h3>\n                    <p>The Born Rule (\\( P = \\psi^*\\psi \\)) arises naturally in TI as the echo or \"beat\" between the offer and confirmation waves. The probability is literally the strength of the handshake established between the source and the detector.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Ontological Status</h3>\n                    <p>In TI, the waves are real physical entities. However, they exist in a sort of \"pseudo-time\" or as a 4D structure, where the \"now\" is the completed transaction. It offers a unique visualization of quantum processes that avoids the need for collapse.</p>','The Transactional Interpretation (TI), proposed by John Cramer, views quantum interactions as \"handshakes\" between Offer Waves moving forward in time and Confirmation Waves moving backward in time. TI is based on the time-symmetry of the wave equation. It posits that every emitter sends an offer wave into the future, and every potential absorber sends a confirmation wave into the past.','The Transactional Interpretation (TI), proposed by John Cramer, views quantum interactions as \"handshakes\" between Offer Waves moving forward in time and Confirmation Waves moving backward in time. TI is based on the time-symmetry of the wave equation. It posits that every emitter sends an offer wave (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) into the future, and every potential absorber sends a confirmation wave (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.461ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1087.6 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\" d=\"M229 286Q216 420 216 436Q216 454 240 464Q241 464 245 464T251 465Q263 464 273 456T283 436Q283 419 277 356T270 286L328 328Q384 369 389 372T399 375Q412 375 423 365T435 338Q435 325 425 315Q420 312 357 282T289 250L355 219L425 184Q434 175 434 161Q434 146 425 136T401 125Q393 125 383 131T328 171L270 213Q283 79 283 63Q283 53 276 44T250 35Q231 35 224 44T216 63Q216 80 222 143T229 213L171 171Q115 130 110 127Q106 124 100 124Q87 124 76 134T64 161Q64 166 64 169T67 175T72 181T81 188T94 195T113 204T138 215T170 230T210 250L74 315Q65 324 65 338Q65 353 74 363T98 374Q106 374 116 368T171 328L229 286Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(684,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) into the past.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"backward-signaling-9b79e1fb\",\"completed-event-205113cb\",\"causal-foundation-f5681823\"]'),
(2010143,'offer-wave','quantum-interpretations','Offer Waves: Probing Future Possibilities','<p>In the Transactional Interpretation, an <strong>Offer Wave</strong> is the retarded wave function (\\( \\psi \\)) emitted by a source that propagates forward in time, representing all possible paths or outcomes for a quantum system.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Retarded Component</h3>\n                    <p>The offer wave corresponds exactly to the standard Schrödinger wave function. It carries information about the energy, momentum, and spin of the particle, and it expands through space following the standard laws of wave propagation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Probing the Environment</h3>\n                    <p>The offer wave \"scouts\" the future. It reaches all possible absorbers in its path. Until a confirmation wave is received, the offer wave exists in a state of potentiality, not yet representing a completed physical event.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Multiple Absorbers</h3>\n                    <p>A single offer wave can reach many potential absorbers. Each absorber responds by sending back its own confirmation wave. The final transaction is a competitive process where only one handshake is usually completed, similar to a \"selection\" from a menu.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Linearity and Superposition</h3>\n                    <p>Offer waves obey the principle of superposition. They can interfere with themselves or other offer waves, creating the complex probability patterns (like the double-slit) that the confirmation waves then \"lock into.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Transition to Actuality</h3>\n                    <p>An offer wave alone is not a particle. It is only half of the story. Only when it is met by a confirmation wave does it \"precipitate\" into a real, localized event, effectively explaining why we only see particles at the end of experiments.</p>','In the Transactional Interpretation, an Offer Wave is the retarded wave function emitted by a source that propagates forward in time, representing all possible paths or outcomes for a quantum system. The offer wave corresponds exactly to the standard Schrödinger wave function. It carries information about the energy, momentum, and spin of the particle, and it expands through space following the standard laws of wave propagation.','In the Transactional Interpretation, an Offer Wave is the retarded wave function (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) emitted by a source that propagates forward in time, representing all possible paths or outcomes for a quantum system. The offer wave corresponds exactly to the standard Schrödinger wave function. It carries information about the energy, momentum, and spin of the particle, and it expands through space following the standard laws of wave propagation.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"forward-propagation-5707a251\",\"unrealized-state-f36b7205\",\"environmental-probe-77996316\"]'),
(2010144,'confirmation-wave','quantum-interpretations','Confirmation Waves: Retrocausal Feedback','<p>The <strong>Confirmation Wave</strong> is the advanced wave function (\\( \\psi^* \\)) in the Transactional Interpretation. It is sent backward in time by a potential absorber upon receiving an offer wave.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Advanced Component</h3>\n                    <p>In the time-symmetric Maxwell or Schrödinger equations, there are solutions that move toward the past. These \"advanced waves\" are usually discarded as unphysical, but in TI, they are essential for the <strong>Quantum Handshake</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Identifying the Source</h3>\n                    <p>The confirmation wave travels back along the exact same path that the offer wave took, but in reverse. It reaches the source at the exact moment the offer wave was emitted, completing the circuit across time.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Born Rule Origin</h3>\n                    <p>Because the confirmation wave is the complex conjugate (\\( \\psi^* \\)), its interference with the offer wave (\\( \\psi \\)) at the source produces the intensity \\( \\psi^*\\psi \\). This explains the <strong>Born Rule</strong> as a physical echo rather than a mathematical postulate.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Retrocausality</h3>\n                    <p>Confirmation waves introduce a form of <strong>Retrocausality</strong>. The future (the absorber) influences the past (the emitter). This solves the EPR paradox: the \"instantaneous\" correlation is achieved through a signal that went forward to the detector and back to the source.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Response of the Universe</h3>\n                    <p>According to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wheeler-feynman-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wheeler-Feynman Absorber Theory</strong></a>, these waves are not just theoretical. The total confirmation wave is the sum of responses from all matter in the future, meaning the present is constantly being \"confirmed\" by the future state of the universe.</p>','The Confirmation Wave is the advanced wave function in the Transactional Interpretation. It is sent backward in time by a potential absorber upon receiving an offer wave. In the time-symmetric Maxwell or Schrödinger equations, there are solutions that move toward the past.','The Confirmation Wave is the advanced wave function (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.461ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1087.6 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\" d=\"M229 286Q216 420 216 436Q216 454 240 464Q241 464 245 464T251 465Q263 464 273 456T283 436Q283 419 277 356T270 286L328 328Q384 369 389 372T399 375Q412 375 423 365T435 338Q435 325 425 315Q420 312 357 282T289 250L355 219L425 184Q434 175 434 161Q434 146 425 136T401 125Q393 125 383 131T328 171L270 213Q283 79 283 63Q283 53 276 44T250 35Q231 35 224 44T216 63Q216 80 222 143T229 213L171 171Q115 130 110 127Q106 124 100 124Q87 124 76 134T64 161Q64 166 64 169T67 175T72 181T81 188T94 195T113 204T138 215T170 230T210 250L74 315Q65 324 65 338Q65 353 74 363T98 374Q106 374 116 368T171 328L229 286Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(684,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) in the Transactional Interpretation. It is sent backward in time by a potential absorber upon receiving an offer wave. In the time-symmetric Maxwell or Schrödinger equations, there are solutions that move toward the past.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.461ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1087.6 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\" d=\"M229 286Q216 420 216 436Q216 454 240 464Q241 464 245 464T251 465Q263 464 273 456T283 436Q283 419 277 356T270 286L328 328Q384 369 389 372T399 375Q412 375 423 365T435 338Q435 325 425 315Q420 312 357 282T289 250L355 219L425 184Q434 175 434 161Q434 146 425 136T401 125Q393 125 383 131T328 171L270 213Q283 79 283 63Q283 53 276 44T250 35Q231 35 224 44T216 63Q216 80 222 143T229 213L171 171Q115 130 110 127Q106 124 100 124Q87 124 76 134T64 161Q64 166 64 169T67 175T72 181T81 188T94 195T113 204T138 215T170 230T210 250L74 315Q65 324 65 338Q65 353 74 363T98 374Q106 374 116 368T171 328L229 286Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(684,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"backward-propagation-ce1b6df3\",\"mathematical-mirror-01910fe9\",\"temporal-bridge-94a6028e\"]'),
(2010145,'bohmian-velocity','quantum-interpretations','Bohmian Velocity: Deterministic Particle Flow','<p><strong>Bohmian Velocity</strong> is the well-defined instantaneous velocity attributed to a particle in the Ontological Interpretation. It is the core concept that differentiates Bohmian mechanics from standard quantum theory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Velocity Formula</h3>\n                    <p>The velocity is defined by the <strong>Guidance Equation</strong>: \\( \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\hbar}{m} \\text{Im}(\\frac{\\nabla \\psi}{\\psi}) \\). This shows that the particle\'s speed and direction are determined by the local structure of the wave function\'s phase.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Streamlines in Configuration Space</h3>\n                    <p>If you map out the velocity at every point, you get <strong>Streamlines</strong>. Particles follow these streamlines like corks floating in a river. They never cross each other in configuration space, leading to a highly organized, \"laminar\" flow of reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Non-Local Dependency</h3>\n                    <p>A particle\'s velocity is not just a local property. In a multi-particle system, the velocity of particle A is a function of the positions of particles B, C, and D. If particle B is moved, the velocity of particle A changes <strong>Instantaneously</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Vanishing Velocity at Extrema</h3>\n                    <p>At points where the wave function is a standing wave (stationary state), the velocity is often zero. In the ground state of a Hydrogen atom, for instance, the Bohmian particle is actually <strong>Stationary</strong>, with its \"motion\" being represented by the wave function\'s energy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Overcoming the Uncertainty Principle</h3>\n                    <p>Bohmian velocity exists even though the <strong>Uncertainty Principle</strong> says we cannot <em>measure</em> it and position simultaneously. Theory distinguishes between the actual value (the beable) and what can be retrieved through a disturbing measurement process.</p>','Bohmian Velocity is the well-defined instantaneous velocity attributed to a particle in the Ontological Interpretation. It is the core concept that differentiates Bohmian mechanics from standard quantum theory. The velocity is defined by the Guidance Equation:.','Bohmian Velocity is the well-defined instantaneous velocity attributed to a particle in the Ontological Interpretation. It is the core concept that differentiates Bohmian mechanics from standard quantum theory. The velocity is defined by the Guidance Equation: <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.108ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"14.622ex\" height=\"3.316ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -975.7 6462.7 1465.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\" d=\"M401 444Q413 441 495 441Q568 441 574 444H580V382H510L409 156Q348 18 339 6Q331 -4 320 -4Q318 -4 313 -4T303 -3H288Q273 -3 264 12T221 102Q206 135 197 156L96 382H26V444H34Q49 441 145 441Q252 441 270 444H279V382H231L284 264Q335 149 338 149Q338 150 389 264T442 381Q442 382 418 382H394V444H401Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\" d=\"M182 599Q182 611 174 615T133 619Q118 619 114 621T109 630Q109 636 114 656T122 681Q125 685 202 688Q272 695 286 695Q304 695 304 684Q304 682 295 644T282 597Q282 592 360 592H399Q430 592 445 587T460 563Q460 552 451 541L442 535H266L251 468Q247 453 243 436T236 409T233 399Q233 395 244 404Q295 441 357 441Q405 441 445 417T485 333Q485 284 449 178T412 58T426 44Q447 44 466 68Q485 87 500 130L509 152H531H543Q562 152 562 144Q562 128 546 93T494 23T415 -13Q385 -13 359 3T322 44Q318 52 318 77Q318 99 352 196T386 337Q386 386 346 3