JX-1-TEX-N-49\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(361,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4195.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(4584.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,485) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(514.5,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><rect width=\"1249.3\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6073.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"probability-flux-42718ed6\",\"non-crossing-rule-d7588799\",\"zero-velocity-limit-feef7d7a\"]'),
(2010146,'von-neumann-process','quantum-interpretations','The Von Neumann Process: Collapse vs. Continuity','<p>The <strong>Von Neumann Process</strong> refers to the two distinct ways a quantum system can change over time: the smooth, deterministic evolution of the wave function and its abrupt, stochastic collapse.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Process 2: Unitary Evolution</h3>\n                    <p><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/process-2-unitary\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Process 2 (Unitary)</strong></a> is the standard time-evolution described by the Schrödinger equation. It is linear, deterministic, and reversible. Under Process 2, a system in a superposition stays in a superposition, and entropy remains constant.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Process 1: The Measurement Event</h3>\n                    <p><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/process-1-nonunitary\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Process 1 (Non-Unitary)</strong></a> is the \"intervention\" of an observer. It is non-linear, stochastic (random), and irreversible. It forces the system into an eigenstate of the observable being measured, effectively \"collapsing\" the wave function.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Mathematical Incompatibility</h3>\n                    <p>The central paradox of quantum foundations is that there is no mathematical rule within Process 2 that can derive Process 1. If everything is made of atoms, and atoms follow Process 2, then Process 1 should technically never happen.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Problem of the \"Cut\"</h3>\n                    <p>Von Neumann showed that you can apply Process 2 to the measuring device as well, but this just pushes the problem further back. Eventually, you must invoke Process 1 to get a result. The location of this <strong>Heisenberg Cut</strong> is the subject of much debate.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Entropy Production</h3>\n                    <p>Process 1 is where <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> enters quantum mechanics. Process 2 preserves the \"information\" in the wave function, while Process 1 discards the unobserved branches, leading to a measurable increase in the system\'s entropy (decoherence).</p>','The Von Neumann Process refers to the two distinct ways a quantum system can change over time: the smooth, deterministic evolution of the wave function and its abrupt, stochastic collapse. Process 2 (Unitary) is the standard time-evolution described by the Schrödinger equation. It is linear, deterministic, and reversible.','The Von Neumann Process refers to the two distinct ways a quantum system can change over time: the smooth, deterministic evolution of the wave function and its abrupt, stochastic collapse. Process 2 (Unitary) is the standard time-evolution described by the Schrödinger equation. It is linear, deterministic, and reversible.','','[]','[]','[\"information-preserving-8170a9cc\",\"probabilistic-2f24c297\",\"definite-value-0f7483ed\"]'),
(2010147,'quantum-agent','quantum-interpretations','The Quantum Agent: Decision-Making in QBism','<p>In <strong>Quantum Bayesianism (QBism)</strong>, the <strong>Quantum Agent</strong> is the central figure. An agent is any entity that uses quantum mechanics to manage its expectations and make decisions about its future interactions with the world.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Subjective Probability</h3>\n                    <p>For a quantum agent, the wave function is not a property of the world, but a <strong>Subjective Degree of Belief</strong>. When an agent assigns a state \\( |\\psi\\rangle \\), they are quantifying their personal betting odds for the outcomes of possible measurements.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Agent-Environment Boundary</h3>\n                    <p>QBism rejects the idea of an objective \"cut.\" Instead, the boundary is between the <strong>Agent</strong> (the user of theory) and the <strong>External World</strong> (the system). Measurement is an action the agent takes, and the result is an experience the agent has.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Born Rule as a Normative Link</h3>\n                    <p>In this view, the <strong>Born Rule</strong> is not a law of nature, but a <strong>Normative Rule</strong> for consistency. It tells the agent how to relate their beliefs about different, non-commuting measurements so they don\'t make irrational bets.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Participatory Realism</h3>\n                    <p>QBism advocates for <strong>Participatory Realism</strong>. The world is not a finished product; it is constantly being \"created\" or modified by the actions of agents. Every measurement is a new \"stroke\" on the canvas of reality that wasn\'t there before.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Multi-Agent Consistency</h3>\n                    <p>Two different agents can assign two different wave functions to the same system and both be \"right,\" as long as they are consistent with their own information. The physical world is the \"meeting ground\" where their experiences eventually align.</p>','In Quantum Bayesianism (QBism), the Quantum Agent is the central figure. An agent is any entity that uses quantum mechanics to manage its expectations and make decisions about its future interactions with the world. For a quantum agent, the wave function is not a property of the world, but a Subjective Degree of Belief.','In Quantum Bayesianism (QBism), the Quantum Agent is the central figure. An agent is any entity that uses quantum mechanics to manage its expectations and make decisions about its future interactions with the world. For a quantum agent, the wave function is not a property of the world, but a Subjective Degree of Belief.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.982ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1318 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\" d=\"M55 732Q56 739 61 744T75 750Q85 750 92 740Q95 733 186 494T278 250T187 6T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q67 -250 62 -245T55 -232Q55 -227 145 11Q236 248 236 250T145 489Q55 727 55 732Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(278,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(929,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"consistency-guide-04209475\",\"measurement-result-68b99bd2\",\"active-universe-58151142\"]'),
(2010148,'pessimistic-meta-induction','philosophy-of-physics','Pessimistic Meta-Induction','<p>Pessimistic Meta-Induction (PMI) is the primary historical challenge to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>, asserting that the frequent ontological failures of past theories provide strong inductive evidence that our current theories are also likely false. Formulated most forcefully by Larry Laudan in 1981, PMI notes that the history of physics is a \'Graveyard\' of once-successful but now-discarded entities. In university-level philosophy of physics, PMI is the \'Great Skeptic\' that realists must answer to justify their belief in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/unobservable-entities\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Unobservable Entities</strong></a>.</p><h3>1. The Induction from Theory Change</h3><p>Realists argue that the predictive success of a theory is a reliable indicator of its truth. PMI counters this with a \'Meta-Induction\': many past theories (e.g., Aristotelian Mechanics, the Aether) were empirically successful and made <strong>Novel Predictions</strong>, yet their underlying ontologies were proven to be fictional. In university-level theory, this implies that <strong>Predictive Success</strong> and <strong>Truth</strong> are not linked by physical causality.</p><h3>2. The Case of the Luminiferous Aether</h3><p>The primary example used for PMI is the 19th-century <strong>Luminiferous Aether</strong>. It was highly predictive, correctly describing the wave nature of light. However, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/special-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Special Relativity</strong></a> proved that the aether does not exist. PMI argues that if a theory as successful as the aether theory can be ontologically false, we have no reason to believe the current <a href=\"/physics/topic/standard-model\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Standard Model</strong></a> is any different.</p><h3>3. The Realist\'s Response: Structural Realism</h3><p>Structural realists, such as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/john-worrall\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>John Worrall</strong></a>, resolve PMI by pointing out that while the \'Entities\' were discarded, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-structure\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mathematical Structure</strong></a> was preserved. Fresnel\'s equations for the aether were identical to Maxwell\'s equations for the vacuum. This ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the manifold are cumulative, providing <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/the-bridge\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Bridge</strong></a> between successive frameworks.</p><h3>4. PMI and Information Theory</h3><p>From the perspective of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a>, PMI is a statement about the \'Redundancy\' of ontological descriptions. Multiple inconsistent manifolds can yield the same observational data. This investigation pushes the boundaries of our understanding, asking whether \'Reality\' is a stable causal structure or an <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/effective-ontology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Effective Ontology</strong></a> that is periodically rewritten.</p>','Pessimistic Meta-Induction (PMI) is the primary historical challenge to Scientific Realism, asserting that the frequent ontological failures of past theories provide strong inductive evidence that our current theories are also likely false. Formulated most forcefully by Larry Laudan in 1981, PMI notes that the history of physics is a \'Graveyard\' of once-successful but now-discarded entities. In university-level philosophy of physics, PMI is the \'Great Skeptic\' that realists must answer to justify their belief in Unobservable Entities.','Pessimistic Meta-Induction (PMI) is the primary historical challenge to Scientific Realism, asserting that the frequent ontological failures of past theories provide strong inductive evidence that our current theories are also likely false. Formulated most forcefully by Larry Laudan in 1981, PMI notes that the history of physics is a \'Graveyard\' of once-successful but now-discarded entities. In university-level philosophy of physics, PMI is the \'Great Skeptic\' that realists must answer to justify their belief in Unobservable Entities.','','[]','[]','[\"pmi-inductive-bound-3360f877\",\"success-truth-independence-d452daad\"]'),
(2010149,'caloric-fluid','scientific-realism','Caloric Fluid','<p>The <strong>Caloric Theory</strong> was the dominant 18th and early 19th-century scientific theory of heat. It posited that heat is a self-repellent, weightless fluid called <strong>caloric</strong> that flows from hotter bodies to colder ones. While theory was eventually superseded by the <strong>Kinetic Theory of Heat</strong> (which defines heat as the random motion of particles), it remains a classic example of an empirically successful but ontologically false theory, serving as a pillar for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/pessimistic-meta-induction\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Pessimistic Meta-Induction</strong></a> challenge to scientific realism.</p><h3>1. The Logic of Caloric</h3><p>Caloric theory was not merely speculative; it provided a robust quantitative framework for thermodynamics. It successfully explained phenomena such as thermal expansion (as caloric particles occupied more space), the melting of ice (as matter absorbed caloric), and the behavior of gases. Theory was so successful that it allowed pioneers like <strong>Sadi Carnot</strong> to derive the laws of the heat engine, which remain fundamentally correct even though his underlying assumption—that heat was a conserved fluid—was later proven wrong.</p><h3>2. The Refutation: Work and Heat</h3><p>The death of caloric theory was driven by the realization that heat is a form of energy that can be converted into mechanical work. <strong>Benjamin Thompson (Count Rumford)</strong> observed the heat generated during the boring of cannons, noting that an inexhaustible amount of heat could be produced by friction. This contradicted the idea of caloric as a conserved fluid. Later, <strong>James Prescott Joule</strong> performed definitive experiments demonstrating a precise conversion factor between mechanical work and heat, showing that heat could be \'produced\' and \'destroyed\' by doing work on a system, rendering the conserved fluid model untenable.</p><h3>3. From Fluid to Kinetic Energy</h3><p>The replacement of caloric theory by the <strong>Kinetic Theory</strong> was a paradigm shift. Heat was no longer seen as a substance *in* a body, but as the *energy* of the motion of the body\'s constituent particles. This shift also resolved the issue of temperature: rather than being a measure of the density of \'caloric fluid\', temperature became a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles. This unified thermodynamics with classical mechanics, creating the foundation for <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong>.</p><h3>4. Philosophical Lessons for Realism</h3><p>The Caloric Theory is often used by anti-realists to argue that the success of a theory\'s predictions is not a reliable indicator of the truth of its theoretical entities. Because caloric theory predicted phenomena such as the laws of the Carnot cycle with great precision, realists are forced to explain why it was so successful despite its underlying entities being purely fictitious. Realists generally counter that while the <em>entity</em> (caloric) was false, theory was <strong>approximately true</strong> in its functional description of heat-transfer relations, which were preserved in the later kinetic model.</p>','The Caloric Theory was the dominant 18th and early 19th-century scientific theory of heat. It posited that heat is a self-repellent, weightless fluid called caloric that flows from hotter bodies to colder ones. While theory was eventually superseded by the Kinetic Theory of Heat (which defines heat as the random motion of particles), it remains a classic example of an empirically successful but ontologically false theory, serving as a pillar for the Pessimistic Meta-Induction challenge to scientific realism.','The Caloric Theory was the dominant 18th and early 19th-century scientific theory of heat. It posited that heat is a self-repellent, weightless fluid called caloric that flows from hotter bodies to colder ones. While theory was eventually superseded by the Kinetic Theory of Heat (which defines heat as the random motion of particles), it remains a classic example of an empirically successful but ontologically false theory, serving as a pillar for the Pessimistic Meta-Induction challenge to scientific realism.','','[]','[]','[]'),
(2010150,'underdetermination','scientific-realism','Underdetermination of Theory by Data','<p>The Underdetermination of Theory by Data (often called the <strong>Underdetermination Thesis</strong> or <strong>Quine-Duhem Thesis</strong>) is the philosophical doctrine that for any given set of empirical evidence, there are multiple, mutually incompatible scientific theories that can account for that data equally well. This poses a fundamental epistemic challenge to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>: if the data does not uniquely point to a single theory, on what rational basis can a realist claim that one of them is the \'true\' description of the world?</p><h3>1. The Duhem-Quine Thesis</h3><p>Thesis has two primary formulations. The <strong>Duhem-Quine Thesis</strong> suggests that theories cannot be tested in isolation. Any experiment involves a \'web of belief\'—including background assumptions, auxiliary hypotheses, and instrument calibrations. If an experiment fails, we have no logical way to isolate *which* part of the web is responsible; we can always \'save\' the main theory by making adjustments elsewhere. This means that data is never sufficient to definitively falsify a theory.</p><h3>2. Global Underdetermination</h3><p>A stronger form is <strong>Global Underdetermination</strong>, which suggests that for any total set of observational evidence, one can always construct an infinite number of alternative theories that are empirically equivalent but ontologically distinct. This a severe threat to realism, as it implies that the \'truth\' of a theory is not a consequence of the evidence, but rather an extra-empirical choice based on convenience or personal bias.</p><h3>3. Theoretical Virtues as Resolvers</h3><p>Realists often respond by arguing that while data may be insufficient, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/theoretical-virtues\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Theoretical Virtues</strong></a> (or \'super-empirical\' virtues) provide the rational grounds for theory choice. These virtues include <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/simplicity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Simplicity (Occam\'s Razor)</strong></a>, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/consistency\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Consistency</strong></a>, and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/elegance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Elegance</strong></a>. Realists assert that these are not mere subjective preferences but are objective indicators of a theory\'s truth-likeness. Anti-realists, however, contend that these virtues are purely pragmatic and have no intrinsic connection to whether a theory is \'true\' of the mind-independent world.</p><h3>4. The Case of Quantum Interpretations</h3><p>A prime example of underdetermination in modern physics is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-interpretations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Interpretations of Quantum Mechanics</strong></a>. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/copenhagen\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Copenhagen</strong></a>, <strong>Many-Worlds</strong>, and <strong>Pilot Wave</strong> interpretations are empirically equivalent—they all predict the exact same experimental results for all possible measurements. Yet, they provide wildly different ontological commitments (e.g., whether the wave function collapses, whether multiple worlds branch, or whether hidden variables exist). The existence of these competing interpretations illustrates that even our most successful theories are deeply underdetermined at the ontological level.</p>','The Underdetermination of Theory by Data (often called the Underdetermination Thesis or Quine-Duhem Thesis) is the philosophical doctrine that for any given set of empirical evidence, there are multiple, mutually incompatible scientific theories that can account for that data equally well. This poses a fundamental epistemic challenge to Scientific Realism: if the data does not uniquely point to a single theory, on what rational basis can a realist claim that one of them is the \'true\' description of the world?','The Underdetermination of Theory by Data (often called the Underdetermination Thesis or Quine-Duhem Thesis) is the philosophical doctrine that for any given set of empirical evidence, there are multiple, mutually incompatible scientific theories that can account for that data equally well. This poses a fundamental epistemic challenge to Scientific Realism: if the data does not uniquely point to a single theory, on what rational basis can a realist claim that one of them is the \'true\' description of the world?','','[]','[]','[\"duhem-quine-holism-56f947ca\",\"underdetermination-4a62609d\"]'),
(2010151,'copenhagen','scientific-realism','Copenhagen Interpretation','<p>An instrumentalist-leaning interpretation of quantum mechanics that avoids ontological commitment to the state vector. Realists often reject it, viewing it as an incomplete description of reality or a denial of mind-independent physical states.</p>','An instrumentalist-leaning interpretation of quantum mechanics that avoids ontological commitment to the state vector. Realists often reject it, viewing it as an incomplete description of reality or a denial of mind-independent physical states.','An instrumentalist-leaning interpretation of quantum mechanics that avoids ontological commitment to the state vector. Realists often reject it, viewing it as an incomplete description of reality or a denial of mind-independent physical states.','','[]','[]','[]'),
(2010152,'many-worlds','philosophy-of-physics','Many-Worlds Interpretation','<p>The Many-Worlds Interpretation (MWI) of quantum mechanics proposes that the <strong>Universal Wavefunction</strong> is objectively real and that no <strong>wavefunction collapse</strong> occurs. Instead, every quantum event branches the universe into a <strong>Multiverse</strong> of parallel histories.</p><h3>1. The Universal Wavefunction</h3><p>In MWI, the entire universe is described by a single <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>State Vector</strong></a> (\\( |\\Psi\\rangle \\)) that evolves unitarily according to the <strong>Schr\\u00f6dinger Equation</strong>: \\[ |\\Psi(t)\\rangle = e^{-i \\hat{H} t / \\hbar} |\\Psi(0)\\rangle \\] There are no observers outside the system; the observer is simply <strong>entangled</strong> with the environment.</p><h3>2. Decoherence and Branching</h3><p>Branching occurs via <strong>Decoherence</strong>. When a system interacts with the environment, the off-diagonal elements of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Density Matrix</strong></a> vanish, making different outcomes unable to interfere. This slices the Hilbert space into orthogonal \"worlds\" within the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p><h3>3. The Probability Problem</h3><p>Critics ask how probability (the <strong>Born Rule</strong>) arises in a deterministic theory where everything happens. Proponents argue that the branching structure naturally leads to the standard probabilities for an observer inside a branch, linking <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong> to unitary evolution.</p><h3>4. Relative State Formulation</h3><p>Hugh Everett III originally called MWI the \"Relative State\" formulation. He argued that the state of an observer is relative to the state of the object: \\[ |\\Psi\\rangle = \\sum_i c_i |\\phi_i\\rangle_{system} \\otimes |\\text{\"I saw } i\\text{\"}\\rangle_{observer} \\] This preserves <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> without the need for a \"mysterious\" collapse.</p><h3>5. Preferred Basis Problem</h3><p>How does nature choose the basis for branching? Decoherence provides the answer: the interaction with the environment selects the \"pointer states\" (like position) that are robust against noise, defining the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of our macroscopic world.</p><h3>6. Ontological Parsimony</h3><p>MWI is often considered the most parsimonious interpretation because it adds no new axioms (like collapse) to the <strong>Schr\\u00f6dinger Equation</strong>. The \"worlds\" are not added; they are a direct consequence of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of linear quantum mechanics.</p>','The Many-Worlds Interpretation (MWI) of quantum mechanics proposes that the Universal Wavefunction is objectively real and that no wavefunction collapse occurs. Instead, every quantum event branches the universe into a Multiverse of parallel histories.','The Many-Worlds Interpretation (MWI) of quantum mechanics proposes that the Universal Wavefunction is objectively real and that no wavefunction collapse occurs. Instead, every quantum event branches the universe into a Multiverse of parallel histories.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"21.76ex\" height=\"3.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1156.2 9617.8 1406.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\" d=\"M340 622Q338 623 335 625T331 629T325 631T314 634T298 635T274 636T239 637H212V683H224Q248 680 38