448\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D448\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(854.2,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(17997.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D448\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D448\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(854.2,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"definition-4efeafdf\",\"focusing-theorem-b325b5e0\",\"sec-tensor-definition-bce8e1f3\",\"covariant-expansion-definition-7e6feeaa\"]'),
(2010168,'friedmann-equations','big-bang-theory','The Friedmann Equations and Cosmic Dynamics','<p>The Friedmann Equations are the fundamental set of ordinary differential equations that describe the expansion and evolution of the universe as a whole. Derived by Alexander Friedmann in 1922 from the Einstein <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/field-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Field Equations</strong></a> under the assumptions of homogeneity and isotropy, they link the global geometry of spacetime to the total density and pressure of the cosmic fluid, providing the mathematical \"engine\" for the Big Bang model.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The First Friedmann Equation: Expansion Rate</h3>\n                    <p>The first Friedmann equation relates the expansion rate—expressed through the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hubble-parameter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hubble Parameter</strong></a> (\\( H = \\dot{a}/a \\))—to the energy density (\\( \\rho \\)), the spatial curvature (\\( k \\)), and the cosmological constant (\\( \\Lambda \\)):\n                    \\[ H^2 = \\left(\\frac{\\dot{a}}{a}\\right)^2 = \\frac{8\\pi G}{3} \\rho - \\frac{kc^2}{a^2} + \\frac{\\Lambda c^2}{3} \\]\n                    This equation proves that the expansion of the universe is not an arbitrary motion but a direct dynamical consequence of its material content. It determines the instantaneous \"velocity\" of the scale factor \\( a(t) \\), showing how the universe responds to the presence of matter, radiation, and dark energy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Fluid Equation: Energy Conservation</h3>\n                    <p>For the universe to follow the laws of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, it must obey local conservation of energy and momentum (\\( \\nabla_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\)). Applied to the homogeneous cosmic fluid, this yields the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fluid-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fluid Equation</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\dot{\\rho} + 3H \\left( \\rho + \\frac{P}{c^2} \\right) = 0 \\]\n                    This equation describes how the density of different components changes as the universe expands. For matter (\\( P=0 \\)), density falls as \\( a^{-3} \\) (dilution); for radiation (\\( P = \\rho c^2/3 \\)), it falls as \\( a^{-4} \\) due to both dilution and redshift, explaining why the early universe was radiation-dominated.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Second Friedmann Equation: Acceleration</h3>\n                    <p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/acceleration-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Acceleration Equation</strong></a> (or Raychaudhuri equation for the scale factor) describes the \"force\" driving the expansion:\n                    \\[ \\frac{\\ddot{a}}{a} = -\\frac{4\\pi G}{3} \\left( \\rho + \\frac{3P}{c^2} \\right) + \\frac{\\Lambda c^2}{3} \\]\n                    Because gravity is naturally attractive (\\( \\rho \\gt 0 \\)), ordinary matter and radiation always act to slow down (decelerate) the expansion. Accelerated expansion requires a component with <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/negative-pressure\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Negative Pressure</strong></a> (\\( P \\lt -\\rho c^2 / 3 \\)), which is the defining physical property of Dark Energy.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Critical Density and Geometry</h3>\n                    <p>By setting the curvature \\( k=0 \\) and \\( \\Lambda=0 \\) in the first Friedmann equation, we define the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/critical-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Critical Density</strong></a> (\\( \\rho_c \\)):\n                    \\[ \\rho_c = \\frac{3H^2}{8\\pi G} \\]\n                    The ratio of the actual density to this value, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-parameter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Density Parameter</strong></a> (\\( \\Omega = \\rho / \\rho_c \\)), determines the geometry of the universe. If \\( \\Omega \\gt 1 \\), space has positive curvature (\\( k=+1 \\), a \"Closed\" universe); if \\( \\Omega \\lt 1 \\), it has negative curvature (\\( k=-1 \\), an \"Open\" universe); and if \\( \\Omega = 1 \\), the universe is spatially \"Flat\" (\\( k=0 \\)).</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Fate of the Universe</h3>\n                    <p>The Friedmann equations reveal that the ultimate fate of the cosmos is tied to its composition. In a universe dominated by matter, the expansion would eventually stop or slow to zero. However, in the presence of a non-zero \\( \\Lambda \\) (Dark Energy), the second equation allows for a transition to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/exponential-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Exponential Expansion</strong></a>. Modern measurements show that our universe is flat and dominated by dark energy, implying it will expand forever in a \"Big Freeze,\" where galaxies eventually become causally isolated from one another.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The De Sitter and Einstein-de Sitter Limits</h3>\n                    <p>Cosmologists use specific solutions to the Friedmann equations to model different eras:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Einstein-de Sitter (\\( \\Omega_m = 1 \\)):</strong> A flat, matter-only universe where \\( a(t) \\propto t^{2/3} \\).</li>\n                            <li><strong>De Sitter (\\( \\Omega_\\Lambda = 1 \\)):</strong> A flat universe dominated entirely by the cosmological constant, where \\( a(t) \\propto e^{Ht} \\).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Radiation-Only:</strong> Describes the very early universe where \\( a(t) \\propto t^{1/2} \\).</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    The transition from the matter-dominated to the \\( \\Lambda \\)-dominated era occurred approximately 6 billion years ago, a milestone precisely captured by the non-linear coupling of these equations.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of The Friedmann Equations and Cosmic Dynamics is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/einstein-de-sitter\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Einstein-de Sitter Universe ()</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/de-sitter-universe\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The De Sitter Universe ()</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/radiation-only-universe\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Radiation-Only Universe</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','The Friedmann Equations are the fundamental set of ordinary differential equations that describe the expansion and evolution of the universe as a whole. Derived by Alexander Friedmann in 1922 from the Einstein Field Equations under the assumptions of homogeneity and isotropy, they link the global geometry of spacetime to the total density and pressure of the cosmic fluid, providing the mathematical \"engine\" for the Big Bang model. The first Friedmann equation relates the expansion rate—expressed through the Hubble Parameter —to the energy density, the spatial curvature, and the cosmological constant : This equation proves that the expansion of the universe is not an arbitrary motion but a direct dynamical consequence of its material content.','The Friedmann Equations are the fundamental set of ordinary differential equations that describe the expansion and evolution of the universe as a whole. Derived by Alexander Friedmann in 1922 from the Einstein Field Equations under the assumptions of homogeneity and isotropy, they link the global geometry of spacetime to the total density and pressure of the cosmic fluid, providing the mathematical \"engine\" for the Big Bang model. The first Friedmann equation relates the expansion rate—expressed through the Hubble Parameter (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-