</use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2783.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3355.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(3744.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4459,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4979,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5828.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"3C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6884.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"221E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-221E\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"state-limit-b164f83a\",\"hilbert-logic-57a28e1d\",\"localization-4607c831\",\"idealization-7e362e97\"]'),
(2010239,'momentum-representation','born-interpretation','The Momentum Representation','<p>The <strong>Momentum Representation</strong> is a formulation of quantum mechanics where the state of a system is expressed as a function of momentum (\\( \\mathbf{p} \\)) rather than position (\\( \\mathbf{r} \\)). In university-level physics, this achieved through the <strong>Fourier Transform</strong> of the position wave function. According to the <strong>Born Interpretation</strong>, the squared magnitude of the momentum wave function (\\( |\\phi(\\mathbf{p})|^2 \\)) defines the probability density for measuring the particle\'s momentum, illustrating the deep symmetry between space and motion in the quantum world.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fourier-transform\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Fourier Transform</strong></a> Link</h3>\n                    <p>The transition between position space \\( \\psi(\\mathbf{r}) \\) and momentum space \\( \\phi(\\mathbf{p}) \\) is given by the integral transform:\n                    \\[ \\phi(\\mathbf{p}, t) = \\frac{1}{(2\\pi\\hbar)^{3/2}} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\psi(\\mathbf{r}, t) e^{-i\\mathbf{p}\\cdot\\mathbf{r}/\\hbar} d^3\\mathbf{r} \\]\n                    This relation proves that position and momentum are <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conjugate-variables\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conjugate Variables</strong></a>. A localized state in position space (small \\( \\Delta x \\)) necessarily corresponds to a spread-out state in momentum space (large \\( \\Delta p \\)), providing the mathematical foundation for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/uncertainty-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Probability in k-space</h3>\n                    <p>Just as \\( |\\psi(\\mathbf{r})|^2 \\) is the probability density for position, \\( |\\phi(\\mathbf{p})|^2 \\) is the <strong>Probability Density</strong> for momentum. If you measure the momentum of an ensemble of particles, the results will follow the statistical distribution defined by the \"shape\" of \\( \\phi(\\mathbf{p}) \\). This ensures that the <strong>Born Rule</strong> is universal, applying to any set of observables that form a complete basis in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Operators in Momentum Space</h3>\n                    <p>In the momentum representation, the operators for position and momentum swap their roles. The momentum operator becomes a simple multiplier (\\( \\hat{\\mathbf{p}} \\to \\mathbf{p} \\)), while the position operator becomes a differential operator (\\( \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} \\to i\\hbar \\nabla_p \\)). This representation is particularly powerful for solving problems involving <strong>Free Particles</strong> or periodic potentials (like crystals), where the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> is diagonal or simplified in terms of momentum eigenvalues.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Parseval\'s Theorem and Normalization</h3>\n                    <p>A critical requirement for the consistency of the Born Interpretation is that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/normalization-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Normalization</strong></a> must be preserved across representations. <strong>Parseval\'s Theorem</strong> guarantees that if the position wave function is normalized to 1, the momentum wave function is also normalized to 1:\n                    \\[ \\int |\\psi(\\mathbf{r})|^2 d^3\\mathbf{r} = \\int |\\phi(\\mathbf{p})|^2 d^3\\mathbf{p} = 1 \\]\n                    This confirms that the total \"Existence\" of the particle is a representation-independent invariant of the quantum state.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Wave Packets and Group Velocity</h3>\n                    <p>The momentum representation allows for the construction of <strong>Wave Packets</strong>, which are superpositions of different momentum states. The \"Center\" of the momentum distribution determines the <strong>Group Velocity</strong> of the particle, while the width determines its rate of dispersion. By analyzing the momentum representation, physicists can predict how a quantum particle will \"Spread\" over time, linking subatomic probability to macroscopic motion.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Momentum Representation in QFT</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Quantum Field Theory</strong>, the momentum representation is the standard language for calculating particle interactions (Feynman diagrams). Particles are viewed as discrete excitations of field modes with definite momentum. The transition from the \"spatial\" view of waves to the \"momentum\" view of particles is the key step in unifying <strong>Special Relativity</strong> with quantum mechanics, proving that the Born Interpretation holds even at the highest energy scales.</p>','The Momentum Representation is a formulation of quantum mechanics where the state of a system is expressed as a function of momentum rather than position. In university-level physics, this achieved through the Fourier Transform of the position wave function. According to the Born Interpretation, the squared magnitude of the momentum wave function defines the probability density for measuring the particle\'s momentum, illustrating the deep symmetry between space and motion in the quantum world.','The Momentum Representation is a formulation of quantum mechanics where the state of a system is expressed as a function of momentum (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.446ex\" height=\"1.457ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -450 639 644\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D429\" d=\"M32 442L123 446Q214 450 215 450H221V409Q222 409 229 413T251 423T284 436T328 446T382 450Q480 450 540 388T600 223Q600 128 539 61T361 -6H354Q292 -6 236 28L227 34V-132H296V-194H287Q269 -191 163 -191Q56 -191 38 -194H29V-132H98V113V284Q98 330 97 348T93 370T83 376Q69 380 42 380H29V442H32ZM457 224Q457 303 427 349T350 395Q282 395 235 352L227 345V104L233 97Q274 45 337 45Q383 45 420 86T457 224Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D429\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D429\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) rather than position (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.072ex\" height=\"1.018ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -450 474 450\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\" d=\"M405 293T374 293T324 312T305 361Q305 378 312 394Q315 397 315 399Q305 399 294 394T266 375T238 329T222 249Q221 241 221 149V62H308V0H298Q280 3 161 3Q47 3 38 0H29V62H98V210V303Q98 353 96 363T83 376Q69 380 42 380H29V442H32L118 446Q204 450 205 450H210V414L211 378Q247 449 315 449H321Q384 449 413 422T442 360Q442 332 424 313Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>). In university-level physics, this achieved through the Fourier Transform of the position wave function. According to the Born Interpretation, the squared magnitude of the momentum wave function (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.8ex\" height=\"2.452ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 3005.6 1083.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\