:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3636.6,0) translate(0 1)\"><use data-c=\"222B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-LO-222B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(4747.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(629,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5779.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6168.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6740.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7129.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7780.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(8169.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8741.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9130.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9650.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"bra-ket-math-bc7fbd01\",\"probability-amplitude-514e5641\"]'),
(2010250,'quantization-of-light','photoelectric-effect','The Quantization of Light','<p>The <strong>Quantization of Light</strong> is the revolutionary concept that electromagnetic radiation consists of discrete, localized packets of energy rather than continuous waves. This idea, first proposed by Max Planck and extended by Albert Einstein, provides the university-level solution to the ultraviolet catastrophe and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/photoelectric-effect\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>the photoelectric effect</strong></a>, marking the definitive birth of quantum mechanics.</p>\n\n                <h3>1. Planck\'s Quanta</h3>\n                <p>In 1900, Max Planck discovered that the <strong>Blackbody Radiation</strong> spectrum could only be explained if energy was exchanged in discrete units. He proposed that the energy of these oscillators is quantized according to the relation \\( E = nhf \\). While Planck initially viewed this as a mathematical trick, Einstein realized it was a fundamental property of the 4D electromagnetic field itself.</p>\n\n                <h3>2. The Photon Hypothesis</h3>\n                <p>Einstein proposed that light itself is spatially localized into \"Light Quanta\" (photons). Unlike a wave that spreads its energy across a 4D wavefront, a photon carries its energy in a single 4D \"point-like\" interaction. This explains why the 4D <strong>Photoelectric Effect</strong> happens instantly: an electron doesn\'t need to \"soak up\" energy over time; it either absorbs a full photon or nothing at all.</p>\n\n                <h3>3. Wave-Particle Duality</h3>\n                <p>The quantization of light introduces <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-particle-duality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wave-Particle Duality</strong></a>. While light propagates as a wave (obeying Maxwell\'s equations and exhibiting interference), it interacts with matter as a particle. The 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of light is therefore dualistic, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the discrete nature of the subatomic vacuum while maintaining the continuous description of classical fields.</p>','The Quantization of Light is the revolutionary concept that electromagnetic radiation consists of discrete, localized packets of energy rather than continuous waves. This idea, first proposed by Max Planck and extended by Albert Einstein, provides the university-level solution to the ultraviolet catastrophe and the photoelectric effect, marking the definitive birth of quantum mechanics. In 1900, Max Planck discovered that the Blackbody Radiation spectrum could only be explained if energy was exchanged in discrete units.','The Quantization of Light is the revolutionary concept that electromagnetic radiation consists of discrete, localized packets of energy rather than continuous waves. This idea, first proposed by Max Planck and extended by Albert Einstein, provides the university-level solution to the ultraviolet catastrophe and the photoelectric effect, marking the definitive birth of quantum mechanics. In 1900, Max Planck discovered that the Blackbody Radiation spectrum could only be explained if energy was exchanged in discrete units.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.651ex\" height=\"2.059ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 3823.6 910\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 