45Q555 144 551 130Q535 71 500 33Q466 -10 419 -10H414Q367 -10 346 17T325 74Q325 90 361 192T398 345Q398 404 354 404H349Q266 404 205 306L198 293L164 158Q132 28 127 16Q114 -11 83 -11Q69 -11 59 -2T48 16Q48 30 121 320L195 616Q195 629 188 632T149 637H128Q122 643 122 645T124 664Q129 683 137 683Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\" d=\"M118 -162Q120 -162 124 -164T135 -167T147 -168Q160 -168 171 -155T187 -126Q197 -99 221 27T267 267T289 382V385H242Q195 385 192 387Q188 390 188 397L195 425Q197 430 203 430T250 431Q298 431 298 432Q298 434 307 482T319 540Q356 705 465 705Q502 703 526 683T550 630Q550 594 529 578T487 561Q443 561 443 603Q443 622 454 636T478 657L487 662Q471 668 457 668Q445 668 434 658T419 630Q412 601 403 552T387 469T380 433Q380 431 435 431Q480 431 487 430T498 424Q499 420 496 407T491 391Q489 386 482 386T428 385H372L349 263Q301 15 282 -47Q255 -132 212 -173Q175 -205 139 -205Q107 -205 81 -186T55 -132Q55 -95 76 -78T118 -61Q162 -61 162 -103Q162 -122 151 -136T127 -157L118 -162Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1041.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2097.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2673.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"energy-quantum-424cc97a\",\"frequency-domain-58e162b9\",\"technical-relation-30a07912\"]'),
(2010258,'photoelectric-effect','quantum-physics','The Photoelectric Effect','<p>The Photoelectric Effect is the emission of electrons from a surface (typically a metal) when exposed to electromagnetic radiation. Its successful explanation by Albert Einstein 1905 was the first conclusive evidence for the <strong>Quantization of Light</strong>, proving that the electromagnetic field consists of discrete packets of energy called photons.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Failure of Classical Electrodynamics</h3>\n                    <p>Classical wave theory predicted that the energy of ejected electrons (photoelectrons) should be proportional to the <strong>Intensity</strong> of the light wave, and that emission should occur at any frequency given enough time. Experimental observations contradicted this: the maximum kinetic energy of electrons depended only on the <strong>Frequency</strong> of the light, and below a certain \"threshold frequency\" (\\( f_0 \\)), no electrons were emitted regardless of intensity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Einstein\'s Photon Hypothesis and the Work Function</h3>\n                    <p>Einstein resolved the paradox by treating light as a stream of particles, each with energy \\( E = hf \\). For an electron to escape the metal, it must absorb a single photon with enough energy to overcome the <strong>Work Function</strong> (\\( \\Phi \\))—the binding energy of the surface. Any energy remaining after the electron \"pays\" the work function becomes its maximum kinetic energy: \\( K_{max} = hf - \\Phi \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Millikan\'s Validation and the Planck Constant</h3>\n                    <p>While initially controversial, the effect was rigorously verified by Robert Millikan, whose measurements of the \"stopping potential\" (\\( V_{stop} \\)) provided a precise value for Planck\'s constant (\\( h \\)). This interaction is a perfect example of a <strong>Quantized Interaction</strong>, where energy is exchanged in discrete \"all-or-nothing\" units rather than continuous waves, serving as the experimental foundation for the Old Quantum Theory.</p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of The Photoelectric Effect is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantization-of-light\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Quantization of Light</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/light-intensity-quantum\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Intensity and Photon Flux</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/threshold-frequency-photoelectric\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Frequency and the Threshold Limit</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/work-function-photoelectric\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Work Function ()</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantized-interaction-quantum\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Quantized Interaction: The One-to-One Exchange</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/photoelectric-equation-einstein\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Einstein\'s Photoelectric Equation</a></strong>, <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stopping-potential-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Stopping Potential and Retarding Fields</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/photon-energy-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Photon Energy and Spectral Resolution</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','The Photoelectric Effect is the emission of electrons from a surface (typically a metal) when exposed to electromagnetic radiation. Its successful explanation by Albert Einstein 1905 was the first conclusive evidence for the Quantization of Light, proving that the electromagnetic field consists of discrete packets of energy called photons. Classical wave theory predicted that the energy of ejected electrons (photoelectrons) should be proportional to the Intensity of the light wave, and that emission should occur at any frequency given enough time.','The Photoelectric Effect is the emission of electrons from a surface (typically a metal) when exposed to electromagnetic radiation. Its successful explanation by Albert Einstein 1905 was the first conclusive evidence for the Quantization of Light, proving that the electromagnetic field consists of discrete packets of energy called photons. Classical wave theory predicted that the energy of ejected electrons (photoelectrons) should be proportional to the Intensity of the light wave, and that emission should occur at any frequency given enough time.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.096ex\" height=\"2.059ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 926.6 910\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\" d=\"M118 -162Q120 -162 124 -164T135 -167T147 -168Q160 -168 171 -155T187 -126Q197 -99 221 27T267 267T289 382V385H242Q195 385 192 387Q188 390 188 397L195 425Q197 430 203 430T250 431Q298 431 298 432Q298 434 307 482T319 540Q356 705 465 705Q502 703 526 683T550 630Q550 594 529 578T487 561Q443 561 443 603Q443 622 454 636T478 657L487 662Q471 668 457 668Q445 668 434 658T419 630Q412 601 403 552T387 469T380 433Q380 431 435 431Q480 431 487 430T498 424Q499 420 496 407T491 391Q489 386 482 386T428 385H372L349 263Q301 15 282 -47Q255 -132 212 -173Q175 -205 139 -205Q107 -205 81 -186T55 -132Q55 -95 76 -78T118 -61Q162 -61 162 -103Q162 -122 151 -136T127 -157L118 -162Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(523,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"photoelectric-equation-5d8baeb6\",\"stopping-potential-f07c2547\",\"photon-energy-978c251c\"]'),
(2010259,'de-broglie-hypothesis','quantum-physics','de Broglie\'s Hypothesis','<p>The de Broglie hypothesis, proposed in 1924, is the foundational postulate of wave mechanics, asserting that all matter possesses an associated wavelength inversely proportional to its momentum. This symmetry between radiation and matter is the conceptual bridge that led to the development of the Schrödinger equation.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Matter Waves and Wave-Particle Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>Building on Einstein\'s photon theory, Louis de Broglie hypothesized that the relations \\( E = hf \\) and \\( p = h/\\lambda \\) should apply to all physical entities. This implies that a particle with mass \\( m \\) and velocity \\( v \\) behaves as a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/matter-wave\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Matter Wave</strong></a> with a wavelength \\( \\lambda = h/p \\). For macroscopic objects, these wavelengths are infinitesimally small, but for subatomic particles, the wave nature becomes the dominant physical characteristic.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Quantization and Standing Waves</h3>\n                    <p>The hypothesis provided a physical justification for the \"quantized\" orbits in the Bohr model of the atom. De Broglie proposed that an electron can only occupy an orbit if its path length is an integer multiple of its wavelength, creating a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/standing-wave\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Standing Wave</strong></a> around the nucleus. This condition (\\( 2\\pi r = n\\lambda \\)) naturally restricts the allowed energy levels of the atom, transforming quantization from a mysterious rule into a geometric requirement of wave stability.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Diffraction and Experimental Proof</h3>\n                    <p>The wave nature of matter was unequivocally confirmed by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/davisson-germer-experiment\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Davisson-Germer Experiment</strong></a>, where electrons were scattered off a nickel crystal. The resulting interference pattern matched the Bragg diffraction observed with X-rays, proving that electrons—particles with mass—undergo diffraction. This discovery established <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-particle-duality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wave-Particle Duality</strong></a> as a universal principle of the physical world, applicable to electrons, neutrons, and even complex molecules.</p>','The de Broglie hypothesis, proposed in 1924, is the foundational postulate of wave mechanics, asserting that all matter possesses an associated wavelength inversely proportional to its momentum. This symmetry between radiation and matter is the conceptual bridge that led to the development of the Schrödinger equation. Building on Einstein\'s photon theory, Louis de Broglie hypothesized that the relations and should apply to all physical entities.','The de Broglie hypothesis, proposed in 1924, is the foundational postulate of wave mechanics, asserting that all matter possesses an associated wavelength inversely proportional to its momentum. This symmetry between radiation and matter is the conceptual bridge that led to the development of the Schrödinger equation. Building on Einstein\'s photon theory, Louis de Broglie hypothesized that the relations <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.293ex\" height=\"2.059ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 3223.6 910\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 