tions derived from the state are unambiguous, anchoring Scientific Realism in a consistent logical framework.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.128ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 2708.7 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\" d=\"M405 293T374 293T324 312T305 361Q305 378 312 394Q315 397 315 399Q305 399 294 394T266 375T238 329T222 249Q221 241 221 149V62H308V0H298Q280 3 161 3Q47 3 38 0H29V62H98V210V303Q98 353 96 363T83 376Q69 380 42 380H29V442H32L118 446Q204 450 205 450H210V414L211 378Q247 449 315 449H321Q384 449 413 422T442 360Q442 332 424 313Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\" d=\"M78 35T78 60T94 103T137 121Q165 121 187 96T210 8Q210 -27 201 -60T180 -117T154 -158T130 -185T117 -194Q113 -194 104 -185T95 -172Q95 -168 106 -156T131 -126T157 -76T173 -3V9L172 8Q170 7 167 6T161 3T152 1T140 0Q113 0 96 17Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522 166 540T173 566T179 586T187 603T197 615T211 624T229 626Q247 625 254 615T261 596Q261 589 252 549T232 470L222 433Q222 431 272 431H323Q330 424 330 420Q330 398 317 385H210L174 240Q135 80 135 68Q135 26 162 26Q197 26 230 60T283 144Q285 150 288 151T303 153H307Q322 153 322 145Q322 142 319 133Q314 117 301 95T267 48T216 6T155 -11Q125 -11 98 4T59 56Q57 64 57 83V101L92 241Q127 382 128 383Q128 385 77 385H26Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(651,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1040,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1514,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1958.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2319.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"periodic-boundary-condition-527f0fd1\",\"probability-uniqueness-df884d2f\"]'),
(2010264,'continuous-wave-function','wave-function','Continuous Wave Function','<p>A core mathematical requirement in quantum mechanics is that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-function\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wave Function</strong></a> (\\( \\psi \\)) must be a <strong>Continuous</strong> function of spatial coordinates. In university-level physics, this implies there can be no sudden \'jumps\' or breaks in the probability amplitude. This continuity ensures that the physical properties derived from the state, particularly momentum and kinetic energy, are well-behaved, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in a smooth and predictable subatomic manifold.</p><h3>1. Probability Flow and Conservation</h3><p>The requirement of continuity is deeply linked to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conservation-of-probability\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conservation of Probability</strong></a>. If a wave function were discontinuous, the probability density \\( |\\psi|^2 \\) would exhibit instantaneous spatial jumps, implying that a particle could \'teleport\' from one location to another without traversing the space in between. Continuous wave functions guarantee a smooth <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Probability Current</strong></a>, ensuring that the total probability within the universe remains locally conserved over time.</p><h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/kinetic-energy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Kinetic Energy</strong></a> and the Hamiltonian</h3><p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a> contains the kinetic energy term, which is proportional to the second spatial derivative of the wave function (\\( \\nabla^2 \\psi \\)). If \\( \\psi \\) were discontinuous, its first derivative would be infinite, and its second derivative would be undefined (or involve pathological singularities like the derivative of a Dirac delta function). This would result in an infinite expected kinetic energy, which is physically impossible for any real system.</p><h3>3. Boundary Conditions at Potential Steps</h3><p>The continuity requirement is crucial when solving the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a> across different regions of space, such as potential steps or barriers. In university-level mechanics, when a particle encounters a finite potential boundary, the wave function must remain continuous across the boundary: \\( \\psi_{left}(x_0) = \\psi_{right}(x_0) \\). This matching condition is the mathematical foundation for phenomena like <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Tunneling</strong></a>, where the wave smoothly penetrates classically forbidden regions.</p><h3>4. Exceptions: Infinite Potentials</h3><p>The only time a derivative of the wave function may be discontinuous is at a boundary where the potential energy is infinite (e.g., an infinite square well or a Dirac delta potential). However, even in these extreme, idealized mathematical models, the wave function \\( \\psi \\) itself remains strictly <strong>Continuous</strong>, ensuring the integrity of the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> and preserving the logical structure of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a>.</p>','A core mathematical requirement in quantum mechanics is that the Wave Function must be a Continuous function of spatial coordinates. In university-level physics, this implies there can be no sudden \'jumps\' or breaks in the probability amplitude. This continuity ensures that the physical properties derived from the state, particularly momentum and kinetic energy, are well-behaved, anchoring Scientific Realism in a smooth and predictable subatomic manifold.','A core mathematical requirement in quantum mechanics is that the Wave Function (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) must be a Continuous function of spatial coordinates. In university-level physics, this implies there can be no sudden \'jumps\' or breaks in the probability amplitude. This continuity ensures that the physical properties derived from the state, particularly momentum and kinetic energy, are well-behaved, anchoring Scientific Realism in a smooth and predictable subatomic manifold.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"wave-function-continuity-fddbd9b8\",\"probability-current-density-c8663458\"]'),
(2010265,'continuously-differentiable','wave-function','Continuously Differentiable Wave Function','<p>A strict mathematical condition in quantum mechanics is that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-function\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wave Function</strong></a> (\\( \\psi \\)) must be <strong>Continuously Differentiable</strong>. In university-level physics, this means that not only must the function itself be <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/continuous-wave-function\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Continuous</strong></a>, but its first spatial derivative (\\( \\nabla \\psi \\)) must also have no instantaneous jumps. This requirement is fundamentally linked to the physical reality of momentum and the logical consistency of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a>, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in a smooth momentum space.</p><h3>1. The Momentum Operator</h3><p>In the position representation, the <strong>Momentum Operator</strong> is defined as \\( \\hat{p} = -i\\hbar \\nabla \\). If the first derivative of the wave function were discontinuous, applying the momentum operator would yield a distribution with infinite spikes (Dirac delta functions) at the points of discontinuity. A continuously differentiable wave function ensures that the expected value of momentum is always a well-behaved, finite quantity, preserving the stability of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a>.</p><h3>2. Finite Kinetic Energy</h3><p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a> determines the total energy of the system, with the kinetic energy term depending on the second derivative of the wave function (\\( \\nabla^2 \\psi \\)). If the first derivative is discontinuous, the second derivative becomes infinite at that point. Since infinite kinetic energy is physically impossible, the first derivative must be continuous. This reveals that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the subatomic manifold strictly forbids infinitely sharp \"kinks\" in the probability amplitude.</p><h3>3. Boundary Conditions and Wave Matching</h3><p>When analyzing piecewise potentials, such as a finite potential well, physicists must \"match\" the solutions across regional boundaries. The requirement that \\( \\psi \\) is continuously differentiable provides the second essential matching condition: \\( \\frac{d\\psi_{left}}{dx}(x_0) = \\frac{d\\psi_{right}}{dx}(x_0) \\). This \"smoothness\" condition is the mathematical mechanism that allows waves to \"leak\" into classically forbidden zones, providing the foundation for <strong>Quantum Tunneling</strong>.</p><h3>4. The Exception: Infinite Potential Boundaries</h3><p>The only physical scenario where the continuously differentiable condition is relaxed is at a boundary where the potential energy \\( V(x) \\) goes to infinity (such as the walls of an infinite square well or a delta-function potential). In these mathematically idealized cases, the infinite force causes an instantaneous change in momentum, allowing for a \"kink\" in \\( \\psi \\). However, for all realistic, finite potentials in the universe, the derivative remains perfectly continuous.</p>','A strict mathematical condition in quantum mechanics is that the Wave Function must be Continuously Differentiable. In university-level physics, this means that not only must the function itself be Continuous, but its first spatial derivative must also have no instantaneous jumps. This requirement is fundamentally linked to the physical reality of momentum and the logical consistency of the Schrödinger Equation, anchoring Scientific Realism in a smooth momentum space.','A strict mathematical condition in quantum mechanics is that the Wave Function (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) must be Continuously Differentiable. In university-level physics, this means that not only must the function itself be Continuous, but its first spatial derivative (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.357ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1484 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\" d=\"M46 676Q46 679 51 683H781Q786 679 786 676Q786 674 617 326T444 -26Q439 -33 416 -33T388 -26Q385 -22 216 326T46 676ZM697 596Q697 597 445 597T193 596Q195 591 319 336T445 80L697 596Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"2207\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2207\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) must also have no instantaneous jumps. This requirement is fundamentally linked to the physical reality of momentum and the logical consistency of the Schrödinger Equation, anchoring Scientific Realism in a smooth momentum space.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"derivative-continuity-63922544\",\"finite-kinetic-energy-da5f08a4\"]'),
(2010266,'conservation-of-probability','wave-function','Conservation of Probability','<p>The Conservation of Probability is a fundamental requirement in quantum mechanics stating that the total probability of finding a particle anywhere in the universe must always remain exactly 1. In university-level physics, this means that while the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/probability-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Probability Density</strong></a> may evolve and flow through space over time, particles cannot simply vanish or be created out of nothing in standard non-relativistic theory, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the continuity of existence.</p><h3>1. Unitary Time Evolution</h3><p>The conservation law is a direct mathematical consequence of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a>. Because the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> is a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hermitian-operators\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hermitian Operator</strong></a>, the time evolution operator \\( \\hat{U}(t) = e^{-i\\hat{H}t/\\hbar} \\) is unitary. Unitary operators preserve the norm (length) of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>State Vector</strong></a>. Therefore, if a wave function satisfies the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/normalization-condition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Normalization Condition</strong></a> at an initial time, it is guaranteed to remain normalized for all future times.</p><h3>2. The Probability Current</h3><p>Locally, the conservation of probability is expressed through the continuity equation, which relates the change in probability density to the divergence of the <strong>Probability Current</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{J} \\)). If probability decreases in one region of space, it must flow outward into adjacent regions. This local conservation implies that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the manifold prevents instantaneous \'teleportation\' of probability amplitude, requiring a smooth, continuous wave propagation.</p><h3>3. Optical Theorem and Unitarity</h3><p>In scattering theory, the conservation of probability leads directly to the <strong>Optical Theorem</strong>, which relates the total cross-section of a scattering event to the imaginary part of the forward scattering amplitude. This ensures that the total probability of all possible scattering outcomes (transmission, reflection, deflection) sums identically to 1, providing a rigorous consistency check for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of particle interactions.</p><h3>4. Loss of Conservation: Open Systems and Decay</h3><p>There are physical models where probability appears not to be conserved, such as phenomenological models of radioactive decay using a complex optical potential. In these cases, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> is non-Hermitian, leading to a decreasing total probability. However, in the fundamental, closed-system perspective of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>, probability is always conserved; the \'loss\' simply represents the particle transitioning into different channels or fields not explicitly tracked in the simplified model.</p>','The Conservation of Probability is a fundamental requirement in quantum mechanics stating that the total probability of finding a particle anywhere in the universe must always remain exactly','The Conservation of Probability is a fundamental requirement in quantum mechanics stating that the total probability of finding a particle anywhere in the universe must always remain exactly','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"14.065ex\" height=\"3.068ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1106.2 6216.8 1356.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D448\" d=\"M107 637Q73 637 71 641Q70 643 70 649Q70 673 81 682Q83 683 98 683Q139 681 234 681Q268 681 297 681T342 682T362 682Q378 682 378 672Q378 670 376 658Q371 641 366 638H364Q362 638 359 638T352 638T343 637T334 637Q295 636 284 634T266 623Q265 621 238 518T184 302T154 169Q152 155 152 140Q152 86 183 55T269 24Q336 24 403 69T501 205L552 406Q599 598 599 606Q599 633 535 637Q511 637 511 648Q511 650 513 660Q517 676 519 679T529 683Q532 683 561 682T645 680Q696 680 723 681T752 682Q767 682 767 672Q767 650 759 642Q756 637 737 637Q666 633 648 597Q646 592 598 404Q557 235 548 205Q515 105 433 42T263 -22Q171 -22 116 34T60 167V183Q60 201 115 421Q164 622 164 628Q164 635 107 637Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\" d=\"M112 560L249 694L257 686Q387 562 387 560L361 531Q359 532 303 581L250 627L195 580Q182 569 169 557T148 538L140 532Q138 530 125 546L112 560Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522 166 540T173 566T179 586T187 603T197 615T211 624T229 626Q247 625 254 615T261 596Q261 589 252 549T232 470L222 433Q222 431 272 431H323Q330 424 330 420Q330 398 317 385H210L174 240Q135 80 135 68Q135 26 162 26Q197 26 230 60T283 144Q285 150 288 151T303 153H307Q322 153 322 145Q322 142 319 133Q314 117 301 95T267 48T216 6T155 -11Q125 -11 98 4T59 56Q57 64 57 83V101L92 241Q127 382 128 383Q128 385 77 385H26Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86