14 86T261 119T312 165Q410 264 445 394Q458 442 496 442Q509 442 519 434T530 411Q530 390 516 352T469 262T388 162T267 70T106 5Q81 -2 71 -2Q66 -2 59 -1T51 1Q45 5 45 11Q45 13 88 188L132 364Q133 377 125 380T86 385H65Q59 391 59 393T61 412Q65 431 74 431Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(792,506.3) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D70C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(792,-138.9) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D70E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(571,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1174,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"parallel-transport-a252f306\",\"independent-parts-8c405ca6\",\"strain-law-a4253679\",\"field-vs-matter-bc17e4a5\"]'),
(2010292,'gravitational-redshift-theory','equivalence-principle','Gravitational Redshift','<p>Gravitational Redshift is the phenomenon where electromagnetic radiation originating from a source in a gravitational field is shifted to longer wavelengths (lower frequencies) when measured by a distant observer. In university-level physics, this not a result of \"light losing energy,\" but a direct consequence of the <strong>Einstein Equivalence Principle</strong> and the 4D warping of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/proper-time\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Proper Time</strong></a> within the manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Redshift from Acceleration</h3>\n                    <p>Gravitational redshift can be derived using the <strong>Equivalence Principle</strong>. Imagine a photon traveling from the floor to the ceiling of an accelerating rocket. By the time the photon reaches the top, the ceiling has speeded up, and the observer measures a <strong>Relativistic Doppler Effect</strong>. Because acceleration is locally indistinguishable from gravity, light must also be redshifted when climbing \"up\" from a massive source like a star.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Metric Origin: Time Dilation</h3>\n                    <p>At a deeper level, the redshift is caused by the <strong>Time Curvature</strong> of the . Clocks in a deep gravitational potential (\\(\\Phi\\)) tick slower than clocks at infinity.\n                    \\[\\frac{\\nu_{obs}}{\\nu_{emit}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{g_{00}(emit)}{g_{00}(obs)}}\\]\n                    This implies that \"Redshift\" is literally the observer seeing the source\'s internal clock running at a slower rate. It confirms that gravity is a <strong>Metric Phenomenon</strong> that affects the very flow of time.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Pound-Rebka Experiment</h3>\n                    <p>The first high-precision laboratory test of gravitational redshift was the <strong>Pound-Rebka Experiment</strong> (1959). Using the <strong>Mössbauer Effect</strong> to detect tiny changes in gamma-ray frequency, they measured the redshift over a height of only 22 meters. The result matched the <strong>Einstein Equivalence Principle</strong> prediction to within 1%, providing the first definitive proof that gravity affects the time-component of the 4D metric.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Redshift near Black Holes</h3>\n                    <p>Near a <strong>Black Hole</strong>, the gravitational redshift becomes extreme. As a source approaches the <strong>Schwarzschild Radius</strong> (\\(R_s\\)), the redshift \\(z\\) approaches infinity (\\(\\nu_{obs} \\to 0\\)). This \"Infinite Redshift\" is why black holes are black: light cannot escape from the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/event-horizon\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Event Horizon</strong></a> because its frequency is stretched to zero, marking the  of <strong>Physical Causality</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Cosmological vs. Gravitational Redshift</h3>\n                    <p>A university-level distinction must be made between Gravitational Redshift (caused by potential) and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-redshift\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Redshift</strong></a> (caused by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Expansion</strong></a>). While both stretch light, they have different <strong>Geometric Origins</strong>. Gravitational redshift depends on the local <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> at two points, whereas cosmological redshift depends on the global <strong>Scale Factor</strong> history since the Big Bang.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Is Energy Conserved?</h3>\n                    <p>Gravitational redshift raises the question of <strong>Conservation</strong> of energy. If a photon loses frequency, where does its energy go? In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the answer is that energy is not a scalar constant across the  but a component of the <strong>Four-Momentum</strong>. The energy is \"exchanged\" with the gravitational field geometry, proving that our 3D concept of conservation is a local limit of a more complex .</p>','Gravitational Redshift is the phenomenon where electromagnetic radiation originating from a source in a gravitational field is shifted to longer wavelengths (lower frequencies) when measured by a distant observer. In university-level physics, this not a result of \"light losing energy,\" but a direct consequence of the Einstein Equivalence Principle and the 4D warping of Proper Time within the manifold. Gravitational redshift can be derived using the Equivalence Principle.','Gravitational Redshift is the phenomenon where electromagnetic radiation originating from a source in a gravitational field is shifted to longer wavelengths (lower frequencies) when measured by a distant observer. In university-level physics, this not a result of \"light losing energy,\" but a direct consequence of the Einstein Equivalence Principle and the 4D warping of Proper Time within the manifold. Gravitational redshift can be derived using the Equivalence Principle.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.567ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"21.166ex\" height=\"6.923ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1925.3 9355.2 3060\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\" d=\"M74 431Q75 431 146 436T219 442Q231 442 231 434Q231 428 185 241L137 51H140L150 55Q161 59 177 67T214 86T261 119T312 165Q410 264 445 394Q458 442 496 442Q509 442 519 434T530 411Q530 390 516 352T469 262T388 162T267 70T106 5Q81 -2 71 -2Q66 -2 59 -1T51 1Q45 5 45 11Q45 13 88 188L132 364Q133 377 125 380T86 385H65Q59 391 59 393T61 412Q65 431 74 431Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45C\" d=\"M201 -11Q126 -11 80 38T34 156Q34 221 64 279T146 380Q222 441 301 441Q333 441 