O-222B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3333.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4162.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4662.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(5182.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D429\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D429\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6099.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7155.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7544.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8309.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(9309.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9809.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(10198.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(11076.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(466,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"total-energy-2baab323\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-energy-momentum-relation-class-subtopic-link-energy-momentum-relation-a-07bc2f9f\",\"relativistic-kinetic-energy-86bf4ce1\",\"de-broglie-momentum-681a504a\"]'),
(2010379,'equivalence-principle','inertial-frames','The Equivalence Principle','<p>The Equivalence Principle (EP) is the foundational postulate of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, asserting that the effects of gravitation are locally indistinguishable from the effects of acceleration. In university-level physics, this principle is the mechanism by which gravity is transformed from a force into a geometric property of the spacetime manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Hierarchy of Equivalence Principles</h3>\n                    <p>Advanced relativity distinguishes between three increasingly restrictive formulations:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Weak Equivalence Principle (WEP):</strong> The trajectory of a point mass in a gravitational field depends only on its initial state, not its composition. This implies the numerical equality of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/inertial-mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Inertial Mass</strong></a> (\\(m_i\\)) and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Mass</strong></a> (\\(m_g\\)).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Einstein Equivalence Principle (EEP):</strong> Adds that the results of any local non-gravitational experiment are independent of the velocity and spacetime location of the laboratory. This necessitates that gravity is a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/metric-phenomenon\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Metric Phenomenon</strong></a>.</li>\n                            <li><strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/strong-equivalence-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Strong Equivalence Principle (SEP)</strong></a>:</strong> Extends EEP to include self-gravitating bodies (like stars) and gravitational experiments themselves. Only <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> is known to satisfy SEP strictly.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schiffs-conjecture\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schiff\'s Conjecture</strong></a> posits that any consistent theory of gravity that embodies the WEP must necessarily satisfy the EEP, linking composition-independence to the metric nature of gravity.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Tidal Forces and Geodesic Deviation</h3>\n                    <p>The Equivalence Principle holds only <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/local-inertial-frame\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>locally</strong></a>—in an infinitesimal neighborhood of a point. Over larger regions, the non-uniformity of the gravitational field produces <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/tidal-forces\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Tidal Forces</strong></a>. Mathematically, these are described by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/geodesic-deviation-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Equation of Geodesic Deviation</strong></a>:\n                    \\[\\frac{D^2 \\xi^\\mu}{d\\tau^2} + R^\\mu_{\\nu\\rho\\sigma} u^\\nu \\xi^\\rho u^\\sigma = 0\\]\n                    Where \\(R^\\mu_{\\nu\\rho\\sigma}\\) is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/riemann-curvature-tensor\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Riemann Curvature Tensor</strong></a>. The relative acceleration of two nearby test particles (\\(\\xi\\)) is a direct measure of spacetime curvature, representing the \"failure\" of the global equivalence principle in finite volumes.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Gravitational Redshift from Acceleration</h3>\n                    <p>A profound consequence of the EEP is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gravitational-redshift-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gravitational Redshift</strong></a>. By considering a light signal sent from the base to the top of an accelerating rocket, one can show via the Doppler shift that the frequency must change. By the principle of equivalence, this same effect must occur in a gravitational field:\n                    \\[z = \\frac{\\Delta \\nu}{\\nu} \\approx \\frac{\\Delta \\Phi}{c^2}\\]\n                    This demonstrates that gravity affects the very \"rate\" of time itself, requiring clocks at different potentials to tick at different speeds.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Precision Tests: Eötvös to MICROSCOPE</h3>\n                    <p>The validity of the WEP is quantified by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/eotvos-parameter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Eötvös Parameter</strong></a> (\\(\\eta\\)), measuring the fractional difference in acceleration between different materials. While 19th-century torsion balance experiments reached a precision of \\(10^{-9}\\), the modern <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/microscope-satellite\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>MICROSCOPE Satellite</strong></a> (2022) confirmed the principle to a staggering \\(\\eta \\lt 10^{-15}\\). These null results place severe constraints on \"Fifth Force\" theories and modifications to the standard model of physics.</p>','The Equivalence Principle (EP) is the foundational postulate of General Relativity, asserting that the effects of gravitation are locally indistinguishable from the effects of acceleration. In university-level physics, this principle is the mechanism by which gravity is transformed from a force into a geometric property of the spacetime manifold. Advanced relativity distinguishes between three increasingly restrictive formulations: Weak Equivalence Principle (WEP): The trajectory of a point mass in a gravitational field depends only on its initial state, not its composition.','The Equivalence Principle (EP) is the foundational postulate of General Relativity, asserting that the effects of gravitation are locally indistinguishable from the effects of acceleration. In university-level physics, this principle is the mechanism by which gravity is transformed from a force into a geometric property of the spacetime manifold. Advanced relativity distinguishes between three increasingly restrictive formulations:\n                        \n                            Weak Equivalence Principle (WEP): The trajectory of a point mass in a gravitational field depends only on its initial state, not its composition.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.658ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24.138ex\" height=\"5.074ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1509.9 10669.1 2242.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D437\" d=\"M287 628Q287 635 230 637Q207 637 200 638T193 647Q193 655 197 667T204 682Q206 683 403 683Q570 682 590 682T630 676Q702 659 752 597T803 431Q803 275 696 151T444 3L430 1L236 0H125H72Q48 0 41 2T33 11Q33 13 36 25Q40 41 44 43T67 46Q94 46 127 49Q141 52 146 61Q149 65 218 339T287 628ZM703 469Q703 507 692 537T666 584T629 613T590 629T555 636Q553 636 541 636T512 636T479 637H436Q392 637 386 627Q384 623 313 339T242 52Q242 48 253 48T330 47Q335 47 349 47T373 46Q499 46 581 128Q617 164 640 212T683 339T703 469Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D709\" d=\"M268 632Q268 704 296 704Q314 704 314 687Q314 682 311 664T308 635T309 620V616H315Q342 619 360 619Q443 619 443 586Q439 548 358 546H344Q326 546 317 549T290 566Q257 550 226 505T195 405Q195 381 201 364T211 342T218 337Q266 347 298 347Q375 347 375 314Q374 297 359 288T327 277T280 275Q234 275 208 283L195 286Q149 260 119 214T88 130Q88 116 90 108Q101 79 129 63T229 20Q238 17 243 1