(2010402,'electroweak-baryogenesis','baryogenesis','Electroweak Baryogenesis','<p><strong>Electroweak Baryogenesis (EWBG)</strong> is a theoretical mechanism that seeks to explain the <strong>Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry</strong> of the universe using the physics of the Electroweak phase transition. Unlike models that rely on the distant <strong>GUT Scale</strong>, EWBG occurs at a much lower energy (\\( \\sim 100 \\text{ GeV} \\)), making it potentially testable at the <strong>Large Hadron Collider (LHC)</strong>. It relies on the rapid movement of Higgs field \"bubbles\" to create a non-equilibrium environment where <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sphalerons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sphalerons</strong></a> can forge matter from leptons.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Electroweak Phase Transition</h3>\n                    <p>When the universe was \\( 10^{-11} \\) seconds old, it cooled enough for the Higgs field to acquire a non-zero value, giving mass to particles. For EWBG to work, this transition must be <strong>Strongly First-Order</strong>. This means the universe does not change smoothly; instead, bubbles of the \"New Vacuum\" (where particles have mass) nucleate and expand into the \"Old Vacuum\" (where they are massless), creating the <strong>Departure from Equilibrium</strong> required by Sakharov.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Interaction with the Bubble Wall</h3>\n                    <p>As the bubble walls sweep through the primordial plasma, quarks and leptons reflect off or pass through them. Because the Standard Model contains <strong>CP Violation</strong> (via the CKM matrix), the reflection and transmission probabilities are slightly different for particles and antiparticles. This creates a \"Chiral Charge\" density in front of the wall, where a small surplus of left-handed fermions is generated.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Sphalerons and the Conversion</h3>\n                    <p>In the region of high temperature in front of the bubble wall, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sphalerons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sphalerons</strong></a> are extremely active. These non-perturbative configurations \"sense\" the chiral surplus and, through an <strong>Anomaly</strong>, convert the left-handed leptons into baryons. As the bubble expands, these newly created baryons are \"swallowed\" into the bubble interior. Inside the bubble, the Higgs field is high, the sphalerons are suppressed, and the <strong>Baryon Number</strong> is safely frozen in.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Higgs Boson Mass Problem</h3>\n                    <p>A major challenge for EWBG within the <strong>Standard Model</strong> is that the Higgs boson discovered at the LHC is too heavy (\\( 125 \\text{ GeV} \\)). A heavy Higgs leads to a \"Crossover\" transition rather than a strong first-order one. For EWBG to remain viable, <strong>\"New Physics\"</strong> such as additional Higgs bosons or <strong>Supersymmetry</strong> must be added to \"stiffen\" the transition and provide the necessary bubble dynamics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Search for New CP Violation</h3>\n                    <p>In addition to the bubble problem, the Standard Model\'s CKM phase is too weak to generate the observed <strong>Baryon-to-Photon Ratio</strong>. EWBG requires a new, much stronger source of CP violation. Physicists look for this by measuring the <strong>Electric Dipole Moments</strong> of electrons and neutrons; a detection of a non-zero EDM would be the \"Smoking Gun\" that the electroweak era had the CP-violating power required to build our world.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Probing the Era with Gravitational Waves</h3>\n                    <p>If the electroweak phase transition was indeed strongly first-order, the collision of vacuum bubbles would have produced a stochastic background of <strong>Gravitational Waves</strong>. These waves would have a characteristic frequency reachable by future detectors like <strong>LISA</strong>. Observing this signal would allow us to \"hear\" the moment when the Higgs field turned on and the matter-antimatter asymmetry was finalized, linking particle physics to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the cosmos.</p>','Electroweak Baryogenesis (EWBG) is a theoretical mechanism that seeks to explain the Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry of the universe using the physics of the Electroweak phase transition. Unlike models that rely on the distant GUT Scale, EWBG occurs at a much lower energy, making it potentially testable at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC). It relies on the rapid movement of Higgs field \"bubbles\" to create a non-equilibrium environment where Sphalerons can forge matter from leptons.','Electroweak Baryogenesis (EWBG) is a theoretical mechanism that seeks to explain the Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry of the universe using the physics of the Electroweak phase transition. Unlike models that rely on the distant GUT Scale, EWBG occurs at a much lower energy (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.825ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 4784.8 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\" d=\"M55 166Q55 241 101 304T222 367Q260 367 296 349T362 304T421 252T484 208T554 189Q616 189 655 236T694 338Q694 350 698 358T708 367Q722 367 722 334Q722 260 677 197T562 134H554Q517 134 481 152T414 196T355 248T292 293T223 311Q179 311 145 286Q109 257 96 218T80 156T69 133Q55 133 55 166Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 64