 transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"223C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-223C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1055.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use><use data-c=\"38\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-38\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(3396,0)\"><use data-c=\"A0\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-A0\"></use><use data-c=\"65\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-65\" transform=\"translate(250,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4312.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" transform=\"translate(4812.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"63\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-63\"></use><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-6D\" transform=\"translate(444,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"dipole-definition-520ec8ba\",\"symmetry-link-ec0ec72c\",\"standard-model-floor-5ad14b52\",\"detection-method-3f2891f8\"]'),
(2010407,'lhc-searches','higgs-mechanism','LHC Searches and New Physics','<p><strong>LHC Searches</strong> refer to the experimental programs at the Large Hadron Collider designed to find evidence for particles and interactions beyond the Standard Model. In university-level particle physics, the LHC is the primary tool for investigating the high-energy processes that drove <strong>Baryogenesis</strong>. By colliding protons at nearly the speed of light, physicists recreate the conditions of the universe when it was only \\( 10^{-12} \\) seconds old, searching for the \"extra\" CP violation needed to explain the matter-antimatter imbalance.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Search for Supersymmetry (SUSY)</h3>\n                    <p>A major focus of the LHC (specifically the <strong>ATLAS</strong> and CMS experiments) is the hunt for <strong>Supersymmetry</strong>. SUSY particles, if they exist, would have been abundant in the early universe. Their decays could provide the necessary <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/baryon-number-violation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Baryon Number Violation</strong></a> and CP violation for successful baryogenesis. While no SUSY particles have been detected yet, the LHC results have significantly constrained the possible mass scales for these particles, forcing theorists to refine their models of the early universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Exotic Higgs Bosons and Phase Transitions</h3>\n                    <p>For <strong>Electroweak Baryogenesis</strong> to work, the phase transition must be strongly first-order. This requires the existence of additional Higgs-like particles. The LHC searches for these \"Exotic Higgses\" in various decay channels. Detecting a second Higgs boson would provide the \"Thermal Kick\" required to push the universe out of equilibrium, providing the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermal-disequilibrium\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Departure from Thermal Equilibrium</strong></a> needed to freeze in a matter surplus.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Precision Measurements of the CKM Matrix</h3>\n                    <p>The <strong>LHCb</strong> experiment is dedicated to the study of B-mesons and the <strong>CKM Matrix</strong>. By measuring CP violation in rare decays with unprecedented precision, LHCb looks for subtle deviations from the Standard Model predictions. Any \"anomaly\" in the B-sector would signal <strong>\"New Physics\"</strong>—specifically new sources of CP violation that could be the missing ingredient in the recipe for a matter-dominated universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Searching for Leptoquarks and Z\' Bosons</h3>\n                    <p>Grand Unified Theories predict new force-carrying particles like <strong>Leptoquarks</strong> (which turn quarks into leptons) and heavy Z\' bosons. These particles would directly mediate <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/baryon-number-violation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Baryon Number Violation</strong></a>. The LHC searches for these particles through \"High-Mass Resonance\" searches. A discovery here would link the physics of the smallest particles directly to the <strong>Grand Unification</strong> scale of the Big Bang.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Dark Matter and Missing Energy</h3>\n                    <p>Baryogenesis and <strong>Dark Matter</strong> are often linked in \"Co-genesis\" models. The LHC searches for dark matter by looking for events with \"Missing Transverse Energy\"—where a particle is produced but escapes detection. If the LHC produces dark matter particles, it would reveal how the 85% of the universe\'s mass was created alongside the visible baryons, providing a unified <strong>Total Theory</strong> of the cosmic census.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Future: High-Luminosity LHC (HL-LHC)</h3>\n                    <p>The upcoming <strong>High-Luminosity LHC</strong> upgrade will increase the number of collisions by a factor of ten. This will allow for the measurement of extremely rare processes that were previously invisible, such as the self-coupling of the Higgs boson. These measurements are essential for determining the <strong>Symmetry Breaking</strong> potential of the early universe, potentially revealing the final secret of how the \"vacuum\" chose matter over antimatter.</p>','LHC Searches refer to the experimental programs at the Large Hadron Collider designed to find evidence for particles and interactions beyond the Standard Model. In university-level particle physics, the LHC is the primary tool for investigating the high-energy processes that drove Baryogenesis. By colliding protons at nearly the speed of light, physicists recreate the conditions of the universe when it was only seconds old, searching for the \"extra\" CP violation needed to explain the matter-antimatter imbalance.','LHC Searches refer to the experimental programs at the Large Hadron Collider designed to find evidence for particles and interactions beyond the Standard Model. In university-level particle physics, the LHC is the primary tool for investigating the high-energy processes that drove Baryogenesis. By colliding protons at nearly the speed of light, physicists recreate the conditions of the universe when it was only <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.295ex\" height=\"2.005ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864 2340.2 886\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\" d=\"M84 237T84 250T98 270H679Q694 262 694 250T679 230H98Q84 237 84 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg> seconds old, searching for the \"extra\" CP violation needed to explain the matter-antimatter imbalance.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.295ex\" height=\"2.005ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864 2340.2 886\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275