-search-0f48d63d\"]'),
(2010408,'asymmetry-parameter','baryogenesis','The Asymmetry Parameter (\\(\\eta\\))','<p>The <strong>Asymmetry Parameter</strong>, denoted by the Greek letter <strong>eta (\\( \\eta \\))</strong>, is the fundamental cosmological constant that represents the excess of baryons (matter) over antibaryons in the early universe. It is defined as the net baryon number density divided by the photon density. In university-level astrophysics, \\( \\eta \\) is the definitive \"Success Metric\" for any theory of <strong>Baryogenesis</strong>, describing the tiny subatomic residue that survived the massive annihilation events of the Big Bang.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Mathematical Definition</h3>\n                    <p>The parameter is defined as \\( \\eta = \\frac{n_B - n_{\\bar{B}}}{n_\\gamma} \\). Because today \\( n_{\\bar{B}} \\approx 0 \\), we measure \\( \\eta \\approx n_B / n_\\gamma \\). The current value, determined by the <strong>Planck Satellite</strong> and observations of <strong>Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN)</strong>, is approximately \\( 6.1 \\times 10^{-10} \\). This implies that for every one billion pairs of matter and antimatter, only one single matter particle was \"extra\" and avoided destruction.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Role of Entropy Density</h3>\n                    <p>Technically, \\( \\eta \\) can change slightly when particles annihilate and heat up the photon sea. To avoid this, cosmologists often use the <strong>Baryon-to-Entropy Ratio</strong> (\\( Y_B = n_B / s \\)). Since the entropy density \\( s \\) remains constant in a comoving volume during <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmic-expansion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmic Expansion</strong></a>, \\( Y_B \\) is a truly conserved quantity of the universe\'s evolution, serving as an absolute record of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the baryogenesis era.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Sensitivity of the Chemical Universe</h3>\n                    <p>The value of \\( \\eta \\) dictates the entire chemistry of the cosmos. If \\( \\eta \\) were much smaller (\\( \\sim 10^{-12} \\)), the density of matter would be too low for gravity to form galaxies. If it were larger (\\( \\sim 10^{-7} \\)), the <strong>Deuterium Bottleneck</strong> would have been bypassed so efficiently that almost all hydrogen would have fused into helium, resulting in a universe with no water and no main-sequence stars like our Sun.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Observation via BBN: The Deuterium Probe</h3>\n                    <p>The most precise way to measure \\( \\eta \\) locally is through <strong>Deuterium Abundance</strong>. Deuterium is \"burnt\" into helium during BBN, and the efficiency of this burning depends exclusively on the baryon density. By observing pristine gas clouds at high <strong>Redshift</strong>, astronomers can \"count\" the deuterium atoms and directly solve for the value of \\( \\eta \\) when the universe was only a few minutes old.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Observation via the CMB: Acoustic Oscillations</h3>\n                    <p>The <strong>Cosmic Microwave Background</strong> provides a completely independent measurement of \\( \\eta \\). The relative heights of the <strong>Acoustic Peaks</strong> in the CMB power spectrum depend on the \"Inertia\" of the baryon-photon fluid. A higher baryon density (\\( \\eta \\)) makes the compression peaks taller. The fact that the BBN and CMB values of \\( \\eta \\) agree perfectly is one of the strongest proofs of the <strong>Standard Model</strong> of cosmology.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Challenge for Particle Physics</h3>\n                    <p>The primary task of <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong> is to explain why \\( \\eta \\) has the specific value it does. Standard Model CP violation (the <strong>CKM Matrix</strong>) predicts an \\( \\eta \\) that is ten orders of magnitude too small. This \"Baryon Asymmetry of the Universe\" (BAU) is the definitive proof that <strong>\"New Physics\"</strong>—such as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/leptogenesis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Leptogenesis</strong></a> or Supersymmetry—must have existed at high energies to generate the world we see today.</p>','The Asymmetry Parameter, denoted by the Greek letter eta, is the fundamental cosmological constant that represents the excess of baryons (matter) over antibaryons in the early universe. It is defined as the net baryon number density divided by the photon density. In university-level astrophysics, is the definitive \"Success Metric\" for any theory of Baryogenesis, describing the tiny subatomic residue that survived the massive annihilation events of the Big Bang.','The Asymmetry Parameter, denoted by the Greek letter eta (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.124ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 497 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D702\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295T28 317T38 348T53 381T73 411T99 433T132 442Q156 442 175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q304 442 382 442Q436 442 469 415T503 336V326Q503 302 439 53Q381 -182 377 -189Q364 -216 332 -216Q319 -216 310 -208T299 -186Q299 -177 358 57L420 307Q423 322 423 345Q423 404 379 404H374Q288 404 229 303L222 291L189 157Q156 26 151 16Q138 -11 108 -11Q95 -11 87 -5T76 7T74 17Q74 30 114 189T154 366Q154 405 128 405Q107 405 92 377T68 316T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D702\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D702\"></use></g></g></g></svg>), is the fundamental cosmological constant that represents the excess of baryons (matter) over antibaryons in the early universe. It is defined as the net baryon number density divided by the photon density. In university-level astrophysics, <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.124ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 497 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D702\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295T28 317T38 348T53 381T73 411T99 433T132 442Q156 442 175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q304 442 382 442Q436 442 469 415T503 336V326Q503 302 439 53Q381 -182 377 -189Q364 -216 332 -216Q319 -216 310 -208T299 -186Q299 -177 358 57L420 307Q423 322 423 345Q423 404 379 404H374Q288 404 229 303L222 291L189 157Q156 26 151 16Q138 -11 108 -11Q95 -11 87 -5T76 7T74 17Q74 30 114 189T154 366Q154 405 128 405Q107 405 92 377T68 316T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D702\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D702\"></use></g></g></g></svg> is the definitive \"Success Metric\" for any theory of Baryogenesis, describing the tiny subatomic residue that survived the massive annihilation events of the Big Bang.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.124ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 497 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D702\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295T28 317T38 348T53 381T73 411T99 433T132 442Q156 442 175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q304 442 382 442Q436 442 469 415T503 336V326Q503 302 439 53Q381 -182 377 -189Q364 -216 332 -216Q319 -216 310 -208T299 -186Q299 -177 358 57L420 307Q423 322 423 345Q423 404 379 404H374Q288 404 229 303L222 291L189 157Q156 26 151 16Q138 -11 108 -11Q95 -11 87 -5T76 7T74 17Q74 30 114 189T154 366Q154 405 128 405Q107 405 92 377T68 316T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D702\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D702\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"success-rate-0c8c402e\",\"conserved-ratio-1d09af87\",\"cosmic-thermostat-b4427dc4\",\"dual-verification-46bce102\"]'),
(2010409,'statistical-distribution','fermions','Statistical Distribution of Quantum Outcomes','<p>The <strong>Statistical Distribution</strong> in quantum mechanics is the collection of all possible measurement outcomes and their associated probabilities for a given state. Unlike classical mechanics, which predicts a single, certain outcome, the <strong>Born Interpretation</strong> asserts that the state of a system (\\( \\psi \\)) determines only the <strong>Frequency</strong> with which different results will occur. This transforms physics from a science of trajectories into a science of populations and probabilities.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. From Wave Function to Histogram</h3>\n                    <p>If a measurement is repeated many times on an <strong>Ensemble</strong> of identically prepared systems, the resulting data forms a <strong>Statistical Distribution</strong>. The shape of this distribution is dictated by the magnitude squared of the wave function: \\( P(x) = |\\psi(x)|^2 \\). For a particle in an excited state of a 1D box, the distribution will show \"peaks\" and \"valleys,\" representing the \"Wavy\" nature of the particle\'s presence.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Expectation Values and Variance</h3>\n                    <p>A distribution is characterized by its \"Moments\":\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Expectation Value (\\( \\langle A \\rangle \\)):</strong> The \"Mean\" or average result of many measurements.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Variance (\\( \\sigma^2 \\)):</strong> The \"Spread\" or uncertainty of the distribution.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    These values provide the macroscopic connection to classical physics, as the average behavior of a quantum distribution often mimics the deterministic path of a classical particle (<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ehrenfests-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ehrenfest\'s Theorem</strong></a>).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Discrete vs. Continuous Distributions</h3>\n                    <p>In university-level physics, the nature of the distribution depends on the operator:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Energy (Bound States):</strong> Produces a <strong>Discrete Distribution</strong>, where only specific \"spikes\" (eigenvalues) are possible.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Position/Momentum:</strong> Produces a <strong>Continuous Distribution</strong>, described by a density function.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    This distinction is the origin of the \"Quantization\" of matter, showing that some physical properties are restricted to specific \"packets\" of probability.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/probability-conservation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Probability Conservation</strong></a> and Flux</h3>\n                    <p>The statistical distribution is not static; it evolves according to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a>. The <strong>Probability Current</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{j} \\)) describes how the \"weight\" of the distribution moves through space. For a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-function-collapse-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wave Function Collapse</strong></a>, the distribution changes instantly from a wide spread to a single delta-function peak, representing the 100% certainty of the <strong>Measurement Postulate</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Role of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> in Distributions</h3>\n                    <p>Each quantum distribution has an associated <strong>Quantum Entropy</strong> (von Neumann entropy). A \"Pure State\" has zero entropy because its distribution is entirely determined by the wave function. A \"Mixed State\" (like a thermal bath) has higher entropy, indicating a lack of information about the underlying quantum amplitudes, linking <strong>Born Rule</strong> statistics to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/thermodynamics-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Second Law of Thermodynamics</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Testing the Distribution: Bell\'s Theorem</h3>\n                    <p>Quantum distributions are unique because they can exhibit <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Entanglement</strong></a>. Measurements on two entangled particles produce a \"Joint Distribution\" that violates the limits of classical probability (<strong>Bell\'s Inequalities</strong>). This proves that the statistical nature of quantum mechanics is not due to \"hidden variables\" but is a fundamental, non-local feature of the .</p>','The Statistical Distribution in quantum mechanics is the collection of all possible measurement outcomes and their associated probabilities for a given state. Unlike classical mechanics, which predicts a single, certain outcome, the Born Interpretation asserts that the state of a system determines only the Frequency with which different results will occur. This transforms physics from a science of trajectories into a science of populations and probabilities.','The Statistical Distribution in quantum mechanics is the collection of all possible measurement outcomes and their associated probabilities for a given state. Unlike classical mechanics, which predicts a single, certain outcome, the Born Interpretation asserts that the state of a system (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) determines only the Frequency with which different results will occur. This transforms physics from a science of trajectories into a science of populations and probabilities.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"statistical-average-b4be5460\",\"uncertainty-ede33f7f\",\"dynamic-stats-9f51a17e\",\"non-local-links-6fef8adc\"]'),
(2010410,'bianchi-identity','field-strength-tensor','The Bianchi Identity in Electromagnetism','<p>The <strong>Bianchi Identity</strong> is a fundamental geometric relationship in tensor calculus that describes the \"Closure\" of a field. In university-level electromagnetism, the Bianchi identity is the covariant expression of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/homogeneous-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Homogeneous Maxwell Equations</strong></a> (<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faradays-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faraday\'s Law</strong></a> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gausss-law-for-magnetism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauss\'s Law for Magnetism</strong></a>). It asserts that the electromagnetic field is a consistent geometric object with no \"edges\" or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/magnetic-monopoles\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Magnetic Monopoles</strong></a>, providing the topological foundation for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Differential Form: dF = 0</h3>\n                    <p>In the language of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/differential-forms\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Differential Forms</strong></a>, the Bianchi identity is written as \\( dF = 0 \\), where \\( F \\) is the Faraday 2-form. Index notation, this corresponds to:\n                    \\[ \\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda} + \\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu} + \\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\]\n                    This \"Cyclic Sum\" of derivatives must vanish identically for any field derived from an <strong>Electromagnetic 4-Potential</strong>. It proves that the field components are not independent but are constrained by the 4D geometry of the manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Absence of Monopoles</h3>\n                    <p>Physically, the Bianchi identity is the statement that the 4-divergence of the dual field strength tensor is zero: \\( \\partial_\\mu \\tilde{F}^{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\). this is relativistic version of \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0 \\). It implies that there are no \"Magnetic Charges\" (monopoles) in the <strong>Standard Model</strong>. If a magnetic monopole were discovered, the Bianchi identity would be violated, signaling the existence of <strong>\"New Physics\"</strong> and a breakdown of the standard geometric interpretation of electromagnetism.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Link to Potential Theory</h3>\n                    <p>The Bianchi identity is a mathematical <strong>Tautology</strong> if the fields are defined in terms of a potential: \\( F_{\\mu\\nu} = \\partial_\\mu A_\\nu - \\partial_\\nu A_\\mu \\). Because partial derivatives commute (\\( \\partial_\\mu \\partial_\\nu = \\partial_\\nu \\partial_\\mu \\)), the cyclic sum of second derivatives cancels out to zero. This confirms that the existence of a 4-potential \\( A^\\mu \\) is the physical \"Reason\" why the homogeneous equations hold, linking <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Invariance</strong></a> topological consistency.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Dual Tensor (\\(\\tilde{F}^{\\mu\\nu}\\))</h3>\n                    <p>To express the Bianchi identity more compactly, physicists use the <strong>Levi-Civita Symbol</strong> (\\( \\epsilon^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} \\)) to define the dual tensor: \\( \\tilde{F}^{\\mu\\nu} = \\frac{1}{2} \\epsilon^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} F_{\\rho\\sigma} \\). In this representation, the identity becomes \\( \\partial_\\mu \\tilde{F}^{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\). This \"Dual\" view is essential for <strong>Relativistic Quantum Field Theory</strong>, where it is used to study the <strong>Symmetries</strong> of the vacuum and the behavior of fields under parity transformations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Bianchi Identity in General Relativity</h3>\n                    <p>The concept of the Bianchi identity is even more famous in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, where it applies to the <strong>Riemann Curvature Tensor</strong>. The \"Geometric Bianchi Identity\" (\\( \\nabla_\\lambda R_{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} + \\dots = 0 \\)) is what necessitates the <strong>Conservation</strong> of energy and momentum (\\( \\nabla_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\)). This proves that the laws of conservation are not added to physics but emerge naturally from the <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> of curved spacetime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Topological Invariants and Chern-Simons</h3>\n                    <p>In advanced <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>, the integral of the Bianchi identity relates to <strong>Topological Invariants</strong>. In <strong>Gauge Theories</strong>, certain configurations of the field (like <strong>Instantons</strong> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sphalerons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sphalerons</strong></a>) have non-zero integrals that measure the \"Winding Number\" of the vacuum. This links the local differential rule of the Bianchi identity to the global <strong>Baryogenesis</strong> processes that created the matter in our universe.</p>','The Bianchi Identity is a fundamental geometric relationship in tensor calculus that describes the \"Closure\" of a field. In university-level electromagnetism, the Bianchi identity is the covariant expression of the Homogeneous Maxwell Equations (Faraday\'s Law and Gauss\'s Law for Magnetism). It asserts that the electromagnetic field is a consistent geometric object with no \"edges\" or Magnetic Monopoles, providing the topological foundation for the Total Dynamics of the manifold.','The Bianchi Identity is a fundamental geometric relationship in tensor calculus that describes the \"Closure\" of a field. In university-level electromagnetism, the Bianchi identity is the covariant expression of the Homogeneous Maxwell Equations (Faraday\'s Law and Gauss\'s Law for Magnetism). It asserts that the electromagnetic field is a consistent geometric object with no \"edges\" or Magnetic Monopoles, providing the topological foundation for the Total Dynamics of the manifold.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"27.01ex\" height=\"2.303ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -715 11938.6 1017.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\" d=\"M202 508Q179 508 169 520T158 547Q158 557 164 577T185 624T230 675T301 710L333 715H345Q378 715 384 714Q447 703 489 661T549 568T566 457Q566 362 519 240T402 53Q321 -22 223 -22Q123 -22 73 56Q42 102 42 148V159Q42 276 129 370T322 465Q383 465 414 434T455 367L458 378Q478 461 478 515Q478 603 437 639T344 676Q266 676 223 612Q264 606 264 572Q264 547 246 528T202 508ZM430 306Q430 372 401 400T333 428Q270 428 222 382Q197 354 183 323T150 221Q132 149 132 116Q132 21 232 21Q244 21 250 22Q327 35 374 112Q389 137 409 196T430 306Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\" d=\"M58 -216Q44 -216 34 -208T23 -186Q23 -176 96 116T173 414Q186 442 219 442Q231 441 239 435T249 423T251 413Q251 401 220 279T187 142Q185 131 185 107V99Q185 26 252 26Q261 26 270 27T287 31T302 38T315 45T327 55T338 65T348 77T356 88T365 100L372 110L408 253Q444 395 448 404Q461 431 491 431Q504 431 512 424T523 412T525 402L449 84Q448 79 448 68Q448 43 455 35T476 26Q485 27 496 35Q517 55 537 131Q543 151 547 152Q549 153 557 153H561Q580 153 580 144Q580 138 575 117T555 63T523 13Q510 0 491 -8Q483 -10 467 -10Q446 -10 429 -4T402 11T385 29T376 44T374 51L368 45Q362 39 350 30T324 12T288 -4T246 -11Q199 -11 153 12L129 -85Q108 -167 104 -180T92 -202Q76 -216 58 -216Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\" d=\"M48 1Q31 1 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H742Q749 676 749 669Q749 664 736 557T722