(2010422,'exclusion-principle','fermions','The Exclusion Principle','<p>The <strong>Exclusion Principle</strong> (or Pauli Exclusion Principle) is the fundamental constraint of the  that prevents two identical <strong>Fermions</strong> from occupying the same quantum state. In university-level physics, it is the primary reason why matter has volume and structure. It arises from the required <strong>Antisymmetry</strong> of the 4-spinor <strong>Wave Function</strong>, serving as the \"Logic Gate\" that builds the periodic table and stabilizes <strong>Compact Objects</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Anti-Symmetry Axiom</h3>\n                    <p>The exclusion principle is a mathematical consequence of the <strong>Spin-Statistics Theorem</strong>. For a system of two fermions, the joint wave function must satisfy:\n                    \\[ \\Psi(1, 2) = -\\Psi(2, 1) \\]\n                    If the two particles were in identical states (\\( 1 = 2 \\)), the equation would imply \\( \\Psi = -\\Psi \\), which is only possible if \\( \\Psi = 0 \\). This proves that the probability of two fermions being in the same spot with the same <strong>Intrinsic Spin</strong> is identically zero.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Electron Shells and Chemistry</h3>\n                    <p>In atoms, the exclusion principle forces electrons to fill higher energy shells once the lower ones are full. Each \"State\" in the  is defined by four quantum numbers. Because only two electrons (spin up/down) can occupy each spatial orbital, atoms grow in size and complexity. This \"Geometric Stacking\" is the source of all chemical <strong>Symmetries</strong> and the 4D structure of <strong>Magnetic Matter</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Bulk Modulus and the Hardness of Matter</h3>\n                    <p>The exclusion principle is the physical \"Reason\" why solid objects are hard. When you squeeze a material, you are forcing fermions to overlap. To avoid violating the principle, the particles must jump to higher momentum states, requiring massive amounts of work. This generates <strong>Degeneracy Pressure</strong>, which provides the <strong>Bulk Modulus</strong> of metals and the \"Hardness\" of everything from diamonds to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutron-stars\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Neutron Stars</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. White Dwarfs and the Chandrasekhar Limit</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Astrophysics</strong>, the exclusion principle provides the final line of defense against gravitational collapse. In a <strong>White Dwarf</strong>, the gravity of a sun-sized mass is balanced purely by the electron exclusion pressure. This balance holds up to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/chandrasekhar-limit\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Chandrasekhar Limit</strong></a>, beyond which gravity overcomes the subatomic \"logic\" of the , leading to a <strong>Supernova</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Exclusion in the Standard Model</h3>\n                    <p>The principle also applies to <strong>Quarks</strong> and <strong>Leptons</strong>. It was the exclusion principle that led to the discovery of <strong>Color Charge</strong>. To explain how three \"Up\" quarks could exist in the same state inside a proton, physicists had to introduce a new \"Color\" degree of freedom, proving that the exclusion principle is a \"Detector\" for new subatomic 4D structures.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: Information Limit</h3>\n                    <p>The exclusion principle suggests that the  has a finite <strong>Information Density</strong>. You cannot pack more than one \"bit\" of matter into a single quantum cell of <strong>Phase Space</strong>. This <strong>Ontological</strong> limit is what makes a world of distinct \"things\" possible, anchoring our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the mathematical necessity of subatomic separation.</p>','The Exclusion Principle (or Pauli Exclusion Principle) is the fundamental constraint of the that prevents two identical Fermions from occupying the same quantum state. In university-level physics, it is the primary reason why matter has volume and structure. It arises from the required Antisymmetry of the 4-spinor Wave Function, serving as the \"Logic Gate\" that builds the periodic table and stabilizes Compact Objects.','The Exclusion Principle (or Pauli Exclusion Principle) is the fundamental constraint of the  that prevents two identical Fermions from occupying the same quantum state. In university-level physics, it is the primary reason why matter has volume and structure. It arises from the required Antisymmetry of the 4-spinor Wave Function, serving as the \"Logic Gate\" that builds the periodic table and stabilizes Compact Objects.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"18.355ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 8112.9 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\" d=\"M340 622Q338 623 335 625T331 629T325 631T314 634T298 635T274 636T239 637H212V683H224Q248 680 389 680T554 683H566V637H539Q479 637 464 635T439 622L438 407Q438 192 439 192Q443 193 449 195T474 207T507 232T536 276T557 344Q560 365 562 417T573 493Q587 536 620 544Q627 546 671 546H715L722 540V515Q714 509 708 509Q680 505 671 476T658 392T644 307Q599 177 451 153L438 151V106L439 61Q446 54 451 52T476 48T539 46H566V0H554Q530 3 389 3T224 0H212V46H239Q259 46 273 46T298 47T314 48T325 51T331 54T335 57T340 61V151Q126 178 117 406Q115 503 69 509Q55 509 55 526Q55 541 59 543T86 546H107H120Q150 546 161 543T184 528Q198 514 204 493Q212 472 213 420T226 316T272 230Q287 216 303 207T330 194L339 192Q340 192 340 407V622Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-31\" d=\"M213 578L200 573Q186 568 160 563T102 556H83V602H102Q149 604 189 617T245 641T273 663Q275 666 285 666Q294 666 302 660V361L303 61Q310 54 315 52T339 48T401 46H427V0H416Q395 3 257 3Q121 3 100 0H88V46H114Q136 46 152 46T177 47T193 50T201 52T207 57T213 61V578Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\" d=\"M78 35T78 60T94 103T137 121Q165 121 187 96T210 8Q210 -27 201 -60T180 -117T154 -158T130 -185T117 -194Q113 -194 104 -185T95 -172Q95 -168 106 -156T131 -126T157 -76T173 -3V9L172 8Q170 7 167 6T161 3T152 1T140 0Q113 0 96 17Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M109 429Q82 429 66 447T50 491Q50 562 103 614T235 666Q326 666 387 610T449 465Q449 422 429 383T381 315T301 241Q265 210 201 149L142 93L218 92Q375 92 385 97Q392 99 409 186V189H449V186Q448 183 436 95T421 3V0H50V19V31Q50 38 56 46T86 81Q115 113 136 137Q145 147 170 174T204 211T233 244T261 278T284 308T305 340T320 369T333 401T340 431T343 464Q343 527 309 573T212 619Q179 619 154 602T119 569T109 550Q109 549 114 549Q132 549 151 535T170 489Q170 464 154 447T109 429Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\" d=\"M84 237T84 250T98 270H679Q694 262 694 250T679 230H98Q84 237 84 250Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1167,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1667,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2111.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2611.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3278.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4334.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5112.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5890.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(6279.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6779.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(7223.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7723.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"slater-rule-64b396ba\",\"packing-limit-af9a3e65\",\"stability-threshold-dc3ce05b\",\"charge-discovery-e681093a\"]'),
(2010423,'mass-origin','fermions','The Origin of Mass','<p>The <strong>Mass Origin</strong> of <strong>Fermions</strong> is one of the most fundamental questions in modern science. In university-level physics, mass is not a primitive property but a dynamic quantity generated through the interaction of matter with various 4D fields. It arises through two primary mechanisms: <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/yukawa-coupling\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Yukawa Coupling</strong></a> with the Higgs field for fundamental particles, and <strong>Chiral Symmetry</strong> breaking for composite matter (like protons).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Higgs Contribution (Intrinsic <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>)</h3>\n                    <p>According to the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, fundamental fermions (quarks and leptons) are born massless in the <strong>Big Bang</strong>. They acquire \"Rest <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>\" by interacting with the <strong>Higgs Field</strong> (\\( \\phi \\)). This interaction, known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/yukawa-coupling\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Yukawa Coupling</strong></a>, \"drags\" on the particles as they move through the vacuum, effectively slowing them down and giving them <strong>Inertial Mass</strong> (\\( m \\propto y_f v \\)). This accounts for the mass of the electron but only a fraction of the mass of the proton.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The QCD Contribution (Binding <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>)</h3>\n                    <p>The vast majority (\\( \\sim 99\\% \\)) of the mass of everyday objects does not come from the Higgs field. It comes from the <strong>Strong Interaction</strong> energy inside nuclei. Because \\( E=mc^2 \\), the kinetic energy and field energy of the gluons and <strong>Quarks</strong> are perceived as mass. This \"Dynamic <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>\" is the result of <strong>Energy Density</strong> being trapped in small 4D volumes, proving that matter is mostly \"captured energy.\"</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Chirality and Masslessness</h3>\n                    <p>Mass is linked to <strong>Chirality</strong>. A mass term in the 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> necessarily mixes left-handed and right-handed states. For a <strong>Chiral Theory</strong> like the weak interaction, this mixing is initially forbidden by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Invariance</strong></a>. The \"Origin\" of mass is therefore synonymous with the \"Breaking\" of these symmetries, linking the <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> of weight to the 4D structure of forces.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Neutrino <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>: The Majorana vs. Dirac Mystery</h3>\n                    <p>The mass origin of neutrinos is a major <strong>Theoretical Crisis</strong>. Because they are so light (\\( \\sim 10^6 \\) times lighter than electrons), they may acquire mass through a different process, such as the <strong>Seesaw Mechanism</strong>. If neutrinos are their own antiparticles (<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/majorana-fermions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Majorana Fermions</strong></a>), their mass origin would reveal a new 4D symmetry of the vacuum, potentially explaining the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> of dark matter.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a>-Energy Equivalence and gravity</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the origin of mass is irrelevant; only the total <strong>Stress-Energy Tensor</strong> sources gravity. Whether mass comes from the Higgs field or nuclear energy, it curves the  in the same way. This <strong>Relativistic Unification</strong> ensures that the <strong>Equivalence Principle</strong> holds, as all forms of mass-energy respond to the 4D <strong>Metric Tensor</strong> identically.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> as an Interaction</h3>\n                    <p>Modern physics has moved away from an <strong>Ontological</strong> view of mass as an \"amount of stuff.\" Instead, mass is an \"Interaction Strength.\" It tells us how strongly a particle couples to the Higgs field or the 4D geometry of the vacuum. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> is a <strong>Relational</strong> property of the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>, proving that the solidity of the world is a dynamic effect of subatomic field interactions.</p>','The Mass Origin of Fermions is one of the most fundamental questions in modern science. In university-level physics, mass is not a primitive property but a dynamic quantity generated through the interaction of matter with various 4D fields. It arises through two primary mechanisms: Yukawa Coupling with the Higgs field for fundamental particles, and Chiral Symmetry breaking for composite matter (like protons).','The Mass Origin of Fermions is one of the most fundamental questions in modern science. In university-level physics, mass is not a primitive property but a dynamic quantity generated through the interaction of matter with various 4D fields. It arises through two primary mechanisms: Yukawa Coupling with the Higgs field for fundamental particles, and Chiral Symmetry breaking for composite matter (like protons).','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.348ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 596 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D719\" d=\"M409 688Q413 694 421 694H429H442Q448 688 448 686Q448 679 418 563Q411 535 404 504T392 458L388 442Q388 441 397 441T429 435T477 418Q521 397 550 357T579 260T548 151T471 65T374 11T279 -10H275L251 -105Q245 -128 238 -160Q230 -192 227 -198T215 -205H209Q189 -205 189 -198Q189 -193 211 -103L234 -11Q234 -10 226 -10Q221 -10 206 -8T161 6T107 36T62 89T43 171Q43 231 76 284T157 370T254 422T342 441Q347 441 348 445L378 567Q409 686 409 688ZM122 150Q122 116 134 91T167 53T203 35T237 27H244L337 404Q333 404 326 403T297 395T255 379T211 350T170 304Q152 276 137 237Q122 191 122 150ZM500 282Q500 320 484 347T444 385T405 400T381 404H378L332 217L284 29Q284 27 285 27Q293 27 317 33T357 47Q400 66 431 100T475 170T494 234T500 282Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"intrinsic-weight-5e1302fa\",\"energy-as-mass-c5bc1ab4\",\"symmetry-origin-f4e914c7\",\"neutrino-mystery-1391b8f6\"]'),
(2010424,'antisymmetric-tensor','field-strength-tensor','Antisymmetric Tensor Properties','<p>An <strong>Antisymmetric Tensor</strong> is a mathematical object whose components flip sign when its indices are swapped (\\( F^{\\mu\\nu} = -F^{\\nu\\mu} \\)). In university-level electrodynamics, the <strong>Field Strength Tensor</strong> is the primary example of antisymmetric rank-2 tensor. This property is not a coincidence but is a direct reflection of the fact that electric and magnetic fields represent rotations and \"twists\" in the  of <strong>Minkowski Spacetime</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Zero-Diagonal Requirement</h3>\n                    <p>A fundamental property of any antisymmetric tensor \\( F^{\\mu\\nu} \\) is that its diagonal components must be zero (\\( F^{\\mu\\mu} = -F^{\\mu\\mu} \\implies F^{\\mu\\mu} = 0 \\)). In the <strong>Field Strength Tensor</strong>, these four zeroes occupy the time-time and space-space diagonal positions. This reflects the physical fact that there is no \"self-force\" or self-interaction in the primary  map, providing the <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for the linearity of Maxwell\'s equations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Degrees of Freedom and Components</h3>\n                    <p>A general \\( 4 \\times 4 \\) tensor has 16 components. However, antisymmetry imposes strict constraints: \\( F^{\\mu\\nu} \\) has only \\( (4^2 - 4)/2 = 6 \\) independent components. In university physics, we identify these 6 components as the 3 components of the <strong>electric field</strong> (\\( E_x, E_y, E_z \\)) and the 3 components of the <strong>magnetic field</strong> (\\( B_x, B_y, B_z \\)). This proves that E and B are not separate vectors, but parts of a single antisymmetric 4D structure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Contracting with Symmetric Tensors</h3>\n                    <p>A powerful mathematical identity is that the contraction of antisymmetric tensor with a symmetric tensor is always zero: \\( F^{\\mu\\nu} S_{\\mu\\nu} = 0 \\). This property is used in the derivation of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/inhomogeneous-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Inhomogeneous Maxwell Equations</strong></a> and ensures that terms like \\( \\partial_\\mu \\partial_\\nu F^{\\mu\\nu} \\) vanish identically, enforcing the <strong>Conservation</strong> of charge in the .</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Antisymmetry and Exterior Calculus</h3>\n                    <p>In the language of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/differential-forms\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Differential Forms</strong></a>, antisymmetric tensor is a 2-form (\\( F = \\frac{1}{2} F_{\\mu\\nu} dx^\\mu \\wedge dx^\\nu \\)). The wedge product (\\( \\wedge \\)) is inherently antisymmetric, ensuring that the fields are related to the <strong>Topology</strong> of space. This perspective interprets the <strong>Field Strength Tensor</strong> as a measure of the \"flux\" through 4D surfaces, linking the 19th-century flux laws to modern subatomic geometry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Dual Tensor (\\(\\tilde{F}^{\\mu\\nu}\\))</h3>\n                    <p>Antisymmetry allows for the definition of the <strong>Dual Tensor</strong> using the <strong>Levi-Civita Symbol</strong> (\\( \\epsilon^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} \\)). This operation (\\( \\tilde{F}^{\\mu\\nu} = \\frac{1}{2} \\epsilon^{\\mu\\nu\\rho\\sigma} F_{\\rho\\sigma} \\)) effectively swaps the E and B components. This \"Dual Mapping\" is essential for expressing the <strong>Homogeneous Equations</strong> and is the basis for exploring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/magnetic-monopoles\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Magnetic Monopoles</strong></a> in <strong>Grand Unification</strong> theories.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Nature of Rotation</h3>\n                    <p>Why is the field tensor antisymmetric? Because forces in a  are related to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> and spin. Swapping two directions in a rotation reverses the direction of the spin. The antisymmetry of \\( F^{\\mu\\nu} \\) is the <strong>Ontological</strong> fingerprint of the universe\'s \"twistiness,\" anchoring our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the rigid algebraic rules of 4D space.</p>','An Antisymmetric Tensor is a mathematical object whose components flip sign when its indices are swapped. In university-level electrodynamics, the Field Strength Tensor is the primary example of antisymmetric rank-2 tensor. This property is not a coincidence but is a direct reflection of the fact that electric and magnetic fields represent rotations and \"twists\" in the of Minkowski Spacetime.','An Antisymmetric Tensor is a mathematical object whose components flip sign when its indices are swapped (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.417ex\" height=\"1.724ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 5488.5 762\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\" d=\"M48 1Q31 1 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H742Q749 676 749 669Q749 664 736 557T722 447Q720 440 702 440H690Q683 445 683 453Q683 454 686 477T689 530Q689 560 682 579T663 610T626 626T575 633T503 634H480Q398 633 393 631Q388 629 386 623Q385 622 352 492L320 363H375Q378 363 398 363T426 364T448 367T472 374T489 386Q502 398 511 419T524 457T529 475Q532 480 548 480H560Q567 475 567 470Q567 467 536 339T502 207Q500 200 482 200H470Q463 206 463 212Q463 215 468 234T473 274Q473 303 453 310T364 317H309L277 190Q245 66 245 60Q245 46 334 46H359Q365 40 365 39T363 19