(2010456,'flavor-eigenstates','standard-model','Flavor Eigenstates','<p>Flavor Eigenstates are the states of elementary particles, specifically <strong>leptons</strong> and quarks, that participate in the <strong>Weak Interaction</strong>. In the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, these states are not identical to the <strong>mass eigenstates</strong>, leading to the phenomena of mixing and oscillation.</p><h3>1. The Weak Basis vs. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Basis</h3><p>Flavor is defined by coupling to the \\( W \\) and \\( Z \\) bosons. For neutrinos, the flavor eigenstates (\\( \\nu_e, \\nu_\\mu, \\nu_\\tau \\)) are related to the mass eigenstates (\\( \\nu_1, \\nu_2, \\nu_3 \\)) by a unitary transformation: \\[ |\\nu_\\alpha\\rangle = \\sum_i U_{\\alpha i}^* |\\nu_i\\rangle \\] where \\( U \\) is the mixing matrix. This misalignment ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the particle include flavor change during propagation.</p><h3>2. Neutrino Oscillations</h3><p>As a neutrino propagates, the different mass components accumulate different phases. The probability of measuring a flavor \\( \\beta \\) from a source of flavor \\( \\alpha \\) is: \\[ P_{\\alpha \\to \\beta} = \\delta_{\\alpha \\beta} - 4 \\sum_{i>j} \\text{Re}(U_{\\alpha i}^* U_{\\beta i} U_{\\alpha j} U_{\\beta j}^*) \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\Delta m_{ij}^2 L}{4E}\\right) \\] This proves neutrinos have non-zero mass, a deviation from the original <strong>Standard Model Theory</strong>.</p><h3>3. The PMNS Matrix</h3><p>The <strong>Pontecorvo-Maki-Nakagawa-Sakata (PMNS)</strong> matrix parameterizes lepton mixing. It is characterized by three mixing angles and one CP-violating phase. The non-trivial values of these angles indicate <strong>Lepton Flavor Violation</strong> in the neutral sector.</p><h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ckm-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The CKM Matrix</strong></a> for Quarks</h3><p>In the quark sector, the <strong>Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa (CKM)</strong> matrix describes the mixing between down-type quarks (d, s, b) and up-type quarks (u, c, t). The off-diagonal elements determine the rate of flavor-changing weak decays, anchoring <strong>Nuclear Physics</strong> in the fundamental couplings of the <strong>Standard Model</strong>.</p><h3>5. The GIM Mechanism</h3><p>The <strong>Glashow-Iliopoulos-Maiani (GIM)</strong> mechanism explains the suppression of flavor-changing neutral currents (FCNCs). It relies on the unitarity of the mixing matrix, ensuring that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a> of flavor is preserved in tree-level neutral interactions.</p><h3>6. Seesaw Mechanism</h3><p>The smallness of neutrino masses is often explained by the <strong>Seesaw Mechanism</strong>, where the existence of very heavy right-handed neutrinos suppresses the masses of the observed left-handed neutrinos. This links <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong> at the TeV scale to the low-energy observations of flavor eigenstates.</p>','Flavor Eigenstates are the states of elementary particles, specifically leptons and quarks, that participate in the Weak Interaction. In the Standard Model, these states are not identical to the mass eigenstates, leading to the phenomena of mixing and oscillation.','Flavor Eigenstates are the states of elementary particles, specifically leptons and quarks, that participate in the Weak Interaction. In the Standard Model, these states are not identical to the mass eigenstates, leading to the phenomena of mixing and oscillation.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.697ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.175ex\" height=\"4.847ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -950 7591.2 2142.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N