3106.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(4162.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,492.7) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"B1\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-B1\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(627.3,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-35\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(859.5,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><rect width=\"1832.6\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" fill=\"red\" stroke=\"red\" transform=\"translate(6234.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"5C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-5C\"></use><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"left-handed-projection-operator-1a19d44d\",\"electroweak-lepton-doublet-b128fa24\"]'),
(2010464,'dirac-fields','fermions','Dirac Fields and the Lagrangian Formalism','<p><strong>Dirac Fields</strong> are the mathematical framework in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-field-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Field Theory (QFT)</strong></a> used to describe particles with spin-1/2, such as electrons and quarks. Unlike scalar fields (bosons), Dirac fields are represented by <strong>Spinors</strong>—multi-component objects that transform specifically under the <strong>Lorentz Group</strong>. This formalism provides the bridge between special relativity and quantum mechanics, naturally predicting the existence of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/antimatter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Antimatter</strong></a> and the intrinsic magnetic properties of matter.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Dirac <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> Density</h3>\n                    <p>The dynamics of a free fermion field (\\( \\psi \\)) are derived from the <strong>Dirac Lagrangian</strong>:\n                    \\[ \\mathcal{L} = \\bar{\\psi}(i\\gamma^\\mu \\partial_\\mu - m)\\psi \\]\n                    Where \\( \\bar{\\psi} = \\psi^\\dagger \\gamma^0 \\) is the adjoint spinor and \\( \\gamma^\\mu \\) are the <strong>Dirac Matrices</strong>. Applying the <strong>Euler-Lagrange Equation</strong> to this density yields the Dirac equation. The complexity of the spinor reflects the fact that it must describe both the particle\\s spin orientation and its charge state (particle vs. antiparticle) simultaneously.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Spinors and Lorentz Representations</h3>\n                    <p>At the university level, a Dirac field is understood as a \\( (1/2, 0) \\oplus (0, 1/2) \\) representation of the Lorentz group. This means that under a <strong>Lorentz Boost</strong> or rotation, the components of the field mix in a way that preserves the relativistic invariant of the field. A full Dirac spinor consists of four complex components, which can be decomposed into two-component <strong>Weyl Spinors</strong> (left-handed and right-handed) in the massless limit, forming the basis for <strong>Chiral Symmetry</strong> in particle physics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/canonical-quantization\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Canonical Quantization</strong></a> and Anti-Commutation</h3>\n                    <p>To transition from a classical field to a quantum field, we must <strong>Quantize</strong> the Dirac field. Unlike bosons, which use commutators, fermions must satisfy <strong>Anti-Commutation Relations</strong>:\n                    \\[ \\{ \\psi_\\alpha(\\mathbf{x}, t), \\psi_\\beta^\\dagger(\\mathbf{y}, t) \\} = \\delta_{\\alpha\\beta} \\delta^{(3)}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y}) \\]\n                    This algebraic rule is the mathematical manifestation of the <strong>Pauli Exclusion Principle</strong>. It ensures that create-and-annihilation operators obey the logic of fermions, where creating two particles in the same state is impossible.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Symmetries and the Noether Current</h3>\n                    <p>Dirac fields possess a global <strong>U(1) Phase Symmetry</strong>: the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> is invariant under the transformation \\( \\psi \\to e^{i\\alpha} \\psi \\). According to <strong>Noether\\s Theorem</strong>, this symmetry leads to a conserved current:\n                    \\[ j^\\mu = \\bar{\\psi}\\gamma^\\mu\\psi \\]\n                    The corresponding conserved charge is the <strong>Electric Charge</strong> (or fermion number). This deep link between the symmetry of the complex field and the conservation of charge is the foundation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-electrodynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Electrodynamics (QED)</strong></a> and the gauge structure of the Standard Model.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Plane-Wave Expansion and Vacuum Energy</h3>\n                    <p>The general solution to the Dirac field is a superposition of <strong>Plane Waves</strong> corresponding to positive energy (particles) and negative energy (antiparticles) solutions. When calculating the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a>, the anti-commutation relations lead to a negative infinite <strong>Zero-Point Energy</strong>. This is interpreted as the \"Dirac Sea\" or, in modern terms, the requirement of <strong>Normal Ordering</strong>, where we measure all energies relative to the physical vacuum state.</p>','Dirac Fields are the mathematical framework in Quantum Field Theory (QFT) used to describe particles with spin-1/2, such as electrons and quarks. Unlike scalar fields (bosons), Dirac fields are represented by Spinors—multi-component objects that transform specifically under the Lorentz Group. This formalism provides the bridge between special relativity and quantum mechanics, naturally predicting the existence of Antimatter and the intrinsic magnetic properties of matter.','Dirac Fields are the mathematical framework in Quantum Field Theory (QFT) used to describe particles with spin-1/2, such as electrons and quarks. Unlike scalar fields (bosons), Dirac fields are represented by Spinors—multi-component objects that transform specifically under the Lorentz Group. This formalism provides the bridge between special relativity and quantum mechanics, naturally predicting the existence of Antimatter and the intrinsic magnetic properties of matter.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.685ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"19.667ex\" height=\"2.823ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -945 8693 1247.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\" d=\"M62 -22T47 -22T32 -11Q32