(2010468,'beta-function','quarks','The QCD Beta Function','<p>The Beta function, \\( \\beta(g) \\), describes how a coupling constant changes under the renormalization group flow. For a gauge theory, the sign of the beta function determines whether theory is asymptotically free (negative) or infrared stable (positive, like QED).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Renormalization Group Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The dependence of the coupling \\( g \\) on the renormalization scale \\( \\mu \\) is given by the Callan-Symanzik equation. In QCD, the first-order calculation of the beta function yields:\n                    \\[ \\beta(g) = \\mu \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial \\mu} = -\\frac{g^3}{16\\pi^2} \\left( \\frac{11}{3} C_A - \\frac{4}{3} n_f T_F \\right) \\]\n                    where \\( C_A = 3 \\) for SU(3) and \\( n_f \\) is the number of active quark flavors.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Competition between Gluons and Quarks</h3>\n                    <p>The negative term (proportional to 11) comes from gluon loops and represents anti-screening, while the positive term (proportional to \\( n_f \\)) comes from quark loops and represents screening. As long as the number of flavors is less than 17, the gluon term dominates, the beta function remains negative, and theory remains asymptotically free.</p>','The Beta function,, describes how a coupling constant changes under the renormalization group flow. For a gauge theory, the sign of the beta function determines whether theory is asymptotically free (negative) or infrared stable (positive, like QED). The dependence of the coupling on the renormalization scale is given by the Callan-Symanzik equation.','The Beta function, <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.12ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1821 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FD\" d=\"M29 -