(2010472,'parton-distribution-functions','quarks','Parton Distribution Functions (PDFs)','<p>Parton Distribution Functions (PDFs) describe the internal structure of the nucleon (proton/neutron) in terms of its constituent partons (quarks and gluons). They provide the probability density for finding a parton carrying a specific fraction \\( x \\) of the nucleon\'s total momentum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Parton Model</h3>\n                    <p>In high-energy collisions, the nucleon behaves not as a single particle but as a collection of \"partons.\" While we often think of a proton as \"up-up-down,\" this only describes the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/valence-quarks\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Valence Quarks</strong></a>. At high resolution, we also see a vast \"sea\" of gluons and virtual <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sea-quarks\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sea Quarks</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Bjorken Scaling and Evolution</h3>\n                    <p>PDFs (\\( f_i(x, Q^2) \\)) depend on both the momentum fraction \\( x \\) and the energy scale \\( Q^2 \\). Their evolution with energy is governed by the <strong>DGLAP equations</strong>, which describe how gluons radiate and split into quark pairs as the resolution of the \"probe\" increases.</p>','Parton Distribution Functions (PDFs) describe the internal structure of the nucleon (proton/neutron) in terms of its constituent partons (quarks and gluons). They provide the probability density for finding a parton carrying a specific fraction of the nucleon\'s total momentum. In high-energy collisions, the nucleon behaves not as a single particle but as a collection of \"partons.\" While we often think of a proton as \"up-up-down,\" this only describes the Valence Quarks.','Parton Distribution Functions (PDFs) describe the internal structure of the nucleon (proton/neutron) in terms of its constituent partons (quarks and gluons). They provide the probability density for finding a parton carrying a specific fraction <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.294ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 572 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\" d=\"M52 289Q59 331 106 386T222 442Q257 442 286 424T329 379Q371 442 430 442Q467 442 494 420T522 361Q522 332 508 314T481 292T458 288Q439 288 427 299T415 328Q415 374 465 391Q454 404 425 404Q412 404 406 402Q368 386 350 336Q290 115 290 78Q290 50 306 38T341 26Q378 26 414 59T463 140Q466 150 469 151T485 153H489Q504 153 504 145Q504 144 502 134Q486 77 440 33T333 -11Q263 -11 227 52Q186 -10 133 -10H127Q78 -10 57 16T35 71Q35 103 54 123T99 143Q142 143 142 101Q142 81 130 66T107 46T94 41L91 40Q91 39 97 36T113 29T132 26Q168 26 194 71Q203 87 217 139T245 247T261 313Q266 340 266 352Q266 380 251 392T217 404Q177 404 142 372T93 290Q91 281 88 280T72 278H58Q52 284 52 289Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g></g></g></svg> of the nucleon\'s total momentum. In high-energy collisions, the nucleon behaves not as a single particle but as a collection of \"partons.\" While we often think of a proton as \"up-up-down,\" this only describes the Valence Quarks.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.294ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 572 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\" d=\"M52 289Q59 331 106 386T222 442Q257 442 286 424T329 379Q371 442 430 442Q467 442 494 420T522 361Q522 332 508 314T481 292T458 288Q439 288 427 299T415 328Q415 374 465 391Q454 404 425 404Q412 404 406 402Q368 386 350 336Q290 115 290 78Q290 50 306 38T341 26Q378 26 414 59T463 140Q466 150 469 151T485 153H489Q504 153 504 145Q504 144 502 134Q486 77 440 33T333 -11Q263 -11 227 52Q186 -10 133 -10H127Q78 -10 57 16T35 71Q35 103 54 123T99 143Q142 143 142 101Q142 81 130 66T107 46T94 41L91 40Q91 39 97 36T113 29T132 26Q168 26 194 71Q203 87 217 139T245 247T261 313Q266 340 266 352Q266 380 251 392T217 404Q177 404 142 372T93 290Q91 281 88 280T72 278H58Q52 284 52 289Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"conservation-000d0850\",\"evolution-627059e1\"]'),
(2010473,'valence-quarks','quarks','Valence Quarks','<p>Valence quarks are the permanent quarks within a hadron that determine its quantum numbers, such as electric charge, baryon number, and strangeness. For example, the valence structure of a proton is <strong>uud</strong> (two up quarks and one down quark).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Quantum Number Conservation</h3>\n                    <p>While a hadron contains an infinite number of virtual particles, the net flavor and charge are always determined by the valence quarks. If you subtract the number of anti-quarks from the number of quarks for a specific flavor, the result is the valence count. In the proton: \\( \\int (u(x) - \\bar{u}(x)) dx = 2 \\) and \\( \\int (d(x) - \\bar{d}(x)) dx = 1 \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Momentum Contribution</h3>\n                    <p>Interestingly, valence quarks only carry about 30-40% of the proton\'s total momentum at high energies; the rest is carried by the gluons and the sea, demonstrating the highly dynamic nature of the nucleon interior.</p>','Valence quarks are the permanent quarks within a hadron that determine its quantum numbers, such as electric charge, baryon number, and strangeness. For example, the valence structure of a proton is uud (two up quarks and one down quark). While a hadron contains an infinite number of virtual particles, the net flavor and charge are always determined by the valence quarks.','Valence quarks are the permanent quarks within a hadron that determine its quantum numbers, such as electric charge, baryon number, and strangeness. For example, the valence structure of a proton is uud (two up quarks and one down quark). While a hadron contains an infinite number of virtual particles, the net flavor and charge are always determined by the valence quarks.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.691ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"21.222ex\" height=\"2.514ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox