(2010474,'sea-quarks','quarks','Sea Quarks','<p>Sea quarks are virtual quark-antiquark pairs (\\( q\\bar{q} \\)) that are constantly being created and annihilated within the vacuum of a hadron due to gluon fluctuations. They represent the \"ocean\" of particles that surround the valence quarks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Origin and Flavor</h3>\n                    <p>Sea quarks arise from the process \\( g \\to q\\bar{q} \\). Because gluons are flavor-blind, the sea contains not only light quarks (up, down) but also heavier quarks like strange (s) and even charm (c), though the probability decreases with the mass of the quark flavor.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Observability</h3>\n                    <p>The presence of sea quarks is essential for explaining the results of deep inelastic scattering experiments. At low momentum fractions (small \\( x \\)), the sea quark and gluon densities dominate over the valence quarks, characterizing the \"high-density\" regime of QCD explored at the Large Hadron Collider.</p>','Sea quarks are virtual quark-antiquark pairs that are constantly being created and annihilated within the vacuum of a hadron due to gluon fluctuations. They represent the \"ocean\" of particles that surround the valence quarks. Sea quarks arise from the process.','Sea quarks are virtual quark-antiquark pairs (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.081ex\" height=\"2.007ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -693 920 887\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q340 441 372 389Q373 390 377 395T388 406T404 418Q438 442 450 442Q454 442 457 439T460 434Q460 425 391 149Q320 -135 320 -139Q320 -147 365 -148H390Q396 -156 396 -157T393 -175Q389 -188 383 -194H370Q339 -192 262 -192Q234 -192 211 -192T174 -192T157 -193Q143 -193 143 -185Q143 -182 145 -170Q149 -154 152 -151T172 -148Q220 -148 230 -141Q238 -136 2