93 920 887\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q340 441 372 389Q373 390 377 395T388 406T404 418Q438 442 450 442Q454 442 457 439T460 434Q460 425 391 149Q320 -135 320 -139Q320 -147 365 -148H390Q396 -156 396 -157T393 -175Q389 -188 383 -194H370Q339 -192 262 -192Q234 -192 211 -192T174 -192T157 -193Q143 -193 143 -185Q143 -182 145 -170Q149 -154 152 -151T172 -148Q220 -148 230 -141Q238 -136 258 -53T279 32Q279 33 272 29Q224 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM352 326Q329 405 277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q233 26 290 98L298 109L352 326Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-AF\" d=\"M69 544V590H430V544H69Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(460,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(316.8,3) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"AF\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-AF\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"equality-c0d25a00\"]'),
(2010475,'strong-lagrangian','quarks','The Strong Lagrangian','<p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> of QCD is the mathematical starting point for all calculations in theory of the strong interaction. It is constructed to be invariant under local SU(3) color transformations and Lorentz transformations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Matter and Gauge Terms</h3>\n                    <p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> consists of two main parts. The <strong>Matter Term</strong> describes the kinetic energy of the quarks and their interaction with gluons via the covariant derivative. The <strong>Gauge Term</strong> (or Yang-Mills term) describes the dynamics of the gluon fields themselves, including their self-interactions.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Gauge Fixing and Ghosts</h3>\n                    <p>To perform calculations (especially in perturbation theory), one must add a \"gauge-fixing\" term to remove redundant degrees of freedom. In non-Abelian theories like QCD, this requires the introduction of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/faddeev-popov-ghosts\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Faddeev-Popov Ghosts</strong></a>—mathematical \"particles\" that violate the spin-statistics theorem and only appear internal loops to cancel unphysical states of the gluons.</p>','The Lagrangian of QCD is the mathematical starting point for all calculations in theory of the strong interaction. It is constructed to be invariant under local SU(3) color transformations and Lorentz transformations. The Lagrangian consists of two main parts.','The Lagrangian of QCD is the mathematical starting point for all calculations in theory of the strong interaction. It is constructed to be invariant under local SU(3) color transformations and Lorentz transformations. The Lagrangian consists of two main parts.','','[]','[]','[\"unification-63a2b564\"]'),
(2010476,'running-coupling','quarks','The Running Coupling','<p>The \"Running\" of the coupling constant (\\( \\alpha_s \\)) is the effect where the strength of the strong force changes depending on the energy scale of the interaction being probed. This a fundamental feature of quantum field theories arising from renormalization.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Screening vs. Anti-screening</h3>\n                    <p>In vacuum, virtual particles \"polarize\" the space. In QED, virtual electrons screen the charge. In QCD, while quarks screen the color charge, gluons (which also carry color) provide a much stronger <strong>anti-screening</strong> effect. This means as you get closer to a quark, the \"cloud\" of anti-screening gluons is removed, and the charge appears weaker.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Scale \\( \\Lambda_{QCD} \\)</h3>\n                    <p>The running is so dramatic that at low energies (long distances), the coupling formally diverges. The scale at which this happens is \\( \\Lambda_{QCD} \\approx 200 \\text{ MeV} \\). This defines the boundary between the high-energy world of free quarks and the low-energy world of confined hadrons.</p>','The \"Running\" of the coupling constant is the effect where the strength of the strong force changes depending on the energy scale of the interaction being probed. This a fundamental feature of quantum field theories arising from renormalization. In vacuum, virtual particles \"polarize\" the space.','The \"Running\" of the coupling constant (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.355ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.386ex\" height=\"1.355ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1054.6 599.1\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FC\" d=\"M34 156Q34 270 120 356T309 442Q379 442 421 402T478 304Q484 275 485 237V208Q534 282 560 374Q564 388 566 390T582 393Q603 393 603 385Q603 376 594 346T558 261T497 161L486 147L487 123Q489 67 495 47T514 26Q528 28 540 37T557 60Q559 67 562 68T577 70Q597 70 597 62Q597 56 591 43Q579 19 556 5T512 -10H505Q438 -10 414 62L411 69L400 61Q390 53 370 41T325 18T267 -2T203 -11Q124 -11 79 39T34 156ZM208 26Q257 26 306 47T379 90L403 112Q401 255 396 290Q382 405 304 405Q235 405 183 332Q156 292 139 224T121 120Q121 71 146 49T208 26Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\" d=\"M131 289Q131 321 147 354T203 415T300 442Q362 442 390 415T419 355Q419 323 402 308T364 292Q351 292 340 300T328 326Q328 342 337 354T354 372T367 378Q368 378 368 379Q368 382 361 388T336 399T297 405Q249 405 227 379T204 326Q204 301 223 291T278 274T330 259Q396 230 396 163Q396 135 385 107T352 51T289 7T195 -10Q118 -10 86 19T53 87Q53 126 74 143T118 160Q133 160 146 151T160 120Q160 94 142 76T111 58Q109 57 108 57T107 55Q108 52 115 47T146 34T201 27Q237 27 263 38T301 66T318 97T323 122Q323 150 302 164T254 181T195 196T148 231Q131 256 131 289Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FC\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FC\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(673,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is the effect where the strength of the strong force changes depending on the energy scale of the interaction being probed. This a fundamental feature of quantum field theories arising from renormalization. In vacuum, virtual particles \"polarize\" the space.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.355ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.386ex\" height=\"1.355ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 1054.6 599.1\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FC\" d=\"M34 156Q34 270 120 356T309 442Q379 442 421 402T478 304Q484 275 485 237V208Q534 282 560 374Q564 388 566 390T582 393Q603 393 603 385Q603 376 594 3