(2010477,'confinement-potential','quarks','The Confinement Potential','<p>The confinement potential is a phenomenological description of the static potential energy between a heavy quark and antiquark. It encapsulates both the short-range and long-range behavior of QCD.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Cornell Potential</h3>\n                    <p>A widely used form is the <strong>Cornell Potential</strong>:\n                    \\[ V(r) = -\\frac{a}{r} + \\sigma r \\]\n                    The first term is a \"Coulomb-like\" term arising from single-gluon exchange, which dominance at short distances (high energy). The second term is the <strong>linear confinement term</strong>, which dominates at large distances.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. String Tension and Regge Trajectories</h3>\n                    <p>The parameter \\( \\sigma \\) is known as the <strong>String Tension</strong>, approximately \\( 1 \\text{ GeV/fm} \\). This linear potential explains why the mass-squared of hadrons is proportional to their angular momentum (Regge Trajectories), as a rotating relativistic string naturally exhibits this relationship.</p>','The confinement potential is a phenomenological description of the static potential energy between a heavy quark and antiquark. It encapsulates both the short-range and long-range behavior of QCD. A widely used form is the Cornell Potential: The first term is a \"Coulomb-like\" term arising from single-gluon exchange, which dominance at short distances (high energy).','The confinement potential is a phenomenological description of the static potential energy between a heavy quark and antiquark. It encapsulates both the short-range and long-range behavior of QCD. A widely used form is the Cornell Potential:\n                    <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -1.577ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"16.568ex\" height=\"4.104ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1117 7323 1814\" xmlns:xlink=\"