(2010478,'quantum-mechanics','relativistic-quantum-field-theory','Quantum Foundations for QFT','<p>Non-relativistic Quantum Mechanics provided the first glimpse into the wave-like nature of matter. While QFT generalizes these concepts to fields, the underlying principles of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>, operators, and probability amplitudes remain the bedrock of theory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. States in Hilbert Space</h3>\n                    <p>A quantum system is described by a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>State Vector</strong></a> (\\( |\\psi\\rangle \\)) in a complex <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>. The total state of the universe is a superposition of all possible configurations, with the probability of any outcome determined by the <strong>Born Rule</strong> (\\( P = |\\langle \\phi | \\psi \\rangle|^2 \\)). In QFT, this space is upgraded to a <strong>Fock Space</strong> to handle multiple particles.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Operators and Observables</h3>\n                    <p>Physical quantities are represented by <strong>Linear Operators</strong> acting on the Hilbert space. To ensure real-valued measurement outcomes, these operators must be <strong>Hermitian</strong>. The dynamics of the system are governed by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a> (\\( \\hat{H} \\)), which represents the total energy and generates the time-evolution of the state.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Schrödinger Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The time-evolution of a quantum state is given by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a>: \\( i\\hbar \\partial_t |\\psi\\rangle = \\hat{H} |\\psi\\rangle \\). This equation is deterministic and unitary, ensuring that the total probability is conserved. However, in its original form, it treats space and time differently, a limitation that QFT resolves by switching to a 4D-symmetric <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> approach.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Uncertainty and Commutation</h3>\n                    <p>The fundamental \"weirdness\" of the quantum world arises from <strong>Non-Commutative Algebra</strong>. Operators for conjugate variables, like position and momentum, do not commute: \\( [x, p] = i\\hbar \\). This leads to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/uncertainty-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle</strong></a>, which in QFT manifests as vacuum fluctuations and the unavoidable noise present in all fields.</p>','Non-relativistic Quantum Mechanics provided the first glimpse into the wave-like nature of matter. While QFT generalizes these concepts to fields, the underlying principles of Hilbert Space, operators, and probability amplitudes remain the bedrock of theory. A quantum system is described by a State Vector in a complex Hilbert Space.','Non-relativistic Quantum Mechanics provided the first glimpse into the wave-like nature of matter. While QFT generalizes these concepts to fields, the underlying principles of Hilbert Space, operators, and probability amplitudes remain the bedrock of theory. A quantum system is described by a State Vector (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.982ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1318 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 