tColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(734,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(627.3,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(1837.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(564,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3100.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4100.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4978.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5367.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6296.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(7352,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-relativistic-dynamics-class-subtopic-link-relativistic-dynamics-a-2ff9a2f4\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-exclusion-principle-class-subtopic-link-exclusion-principle-a-b4790b37\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-mass-origin-class-subtopic-link-mass-origin-a-839d1809\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-statistical-distribution-class-subtopic-link-statistical-distribution-a-544875c9\"]'),
(2010489,'quarks','fermions','Quarks and the Strong Interaction','<p>Quarks are the fundamental spin-1/2 fermions that constitute the building blocks of hadronic matter. In university-level particle physics, their dynamics are described by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-chromodynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD)</strong></a>, a non-Abelian gauge theory based on the \\( SU(3)_c \\) symmetry group. Unlike leptons, quarks carry <strong>Color Charge</strong>, subjecting them to the strong interaction—the most powerful force in the Standard Model.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The QCD <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> and Gluon Self-Interaction</h3>\n                    <p>The dynamics of quarks and gluons are encoded in the QCD <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\mathcal{L}_{QCD} = \\sum_q \\bar{\\psi}_q (i \\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu - m_q) \\psi_q - \\frac{1}{4} G^a_{\\mu\\nu} G^{\\mu\\nu}_a \\]\n                    A defining feature of QCD is that gluons, the force carriers, also carry color charge. This leads to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gluon-self-interaction\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gluon Self-Interaction</strong></a> (the cubic and quartic terms in the field strength tensor \\( G^a_{\\mu\\nu} \\)). This non-Abelian nature is responsible for the unique phenomena of color confinement and asymptotic freedom, which have no counterparts in electromagnetism.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Asymptotic Freedom and the Beta Function</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike the electromagnetic coupling, the strong coupling constant \\( \\alpha_s \\) decreases at short distances (high energy scales). This behavior, known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/asymptotic-freedom\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Asymptotic Freedom</strong></a>, is mathematically determined by the negative sign of the QCD <strong>Beta Function</strong>:\n                    \\[ \\beta(g_s) = \\mu \\frac{\\partial g_s}{\\partial \\mu} = -\\frac{g_s^3}{16\\pi^2} \\left( 11 - \\frac{2}{3} n_f \\right) \\]\n                    At high energies (small \\( \\mu \\)), quarks behave as quasi-free particles, allowing for the use of perturbation theory. This was confirmed by deep inelastic scattering experiments, which revealed the \"parton\" structure of protons.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Color Confinement and Hadronization</h3>\n                    <p>At low energy scales (\\( \\sim 200 \\text{ MeV} \\)), \\( \\alpha_s \\) becomes large, leading to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/color-confinement\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Color Confinement</strong></a>. The color field lines between quarks form narrow \"flux tubes\" with a constant tension, resulting in a potential that grows linearly with distance (\\( V(r) \\approx \\sigma r \\)). Consequently, quarks can never be observed in isolation; any attempt to separate them provides enough energy to create new quark-antiquark pairs from the vacuum, a process called <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hadronization\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hadronization</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> Generation and Chiral Symmetry Breaking</h3>\n                    <p>Quark masses arise from two distinct physical processes:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Higgs Mechanism:</strong> Generates the \"current\" masses (\\( m_u \\approx 2 \\text{ MeV} \\)), which are parameters in the electroweak sector and mixed via the <strong>CKM Matrix</strong>.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Spontaneous <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/physical-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/chiral-symmetry-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Chiral Symmetry Breaking</strong></a></a> (\\( \\chi \\)SB):</strong> In the low-energy regime, the QCD vacuum develops a non-zero quark-condensate (\\( \\langle 0 | \\bar{\\psi} \\psi | 0 \\rangle \\neq 0 \\)). This process generates the \"constituent\" masses (\\( \\sim 300 \\text{ MeV} \\)), accounting for 99% of the visible mass of the universe.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Internal Structure: Valence vs. Sea Quarks</h3>\n                    <p>A hadron is not a simple collection of three quarks, but a dynamic system described by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/parton-distribution-functions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Parton Distribution Functions (PDFs)</strong></a>.\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Valence Quarks:</strong> The \"fixed\" quarks that define the hadron\'s quantum numbers (e.g., \\( uud \\) for a proton).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Sea Quarks:</strong> A virtual \"sea\" of quark-antiquark pairs and gluons constantly being created and annihilated via quantum fluctuations. At high energies, these sea partons dominate the total momentum fraction of the hadron.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Quarks and the Strong Interaction is intrinsically linked to several specialized concepts, including the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/strong-lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Strong Lagrangian</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/confinement-potential\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Confinement Potential</a></strong>. These topics provide the necessary analytical depth for a comprehensive understanding of the manifold\'s dynamics.</p>','Quarks are the fundamental spin-1/2 fermions that constitute the building blocks of hadronic matter. In university-level particle physics, their dynamics are described by Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD), a non-Abelian gauge theory based on the symmetry group. Unlike leptons, quarks carry Color Charge, subjecting them to the strong interaction—the most powerful force in the Standard Model.','Quarks are the fundamental spin-1/2 fermions that constitute the building blocks of hadronic matter. In university-level particle physics, their dynamics are described by Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD), a non-Abelian gauge theory based on the <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.966ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3079.2 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554 544 579T526 616T501 641Q465 662 419 662Q362 662 313 616T263 510Q263 480 278 458T319 427Q323 425 389 408T456 390Q490 379 522 342T554 242Q554 216 546 186Q541 164 528 137T492 78T426 18T332 -20Q320 -22 298 -22Q199 -22 144 33L134 44L106 13Q83 -14 78 -18T65 -22Q52 -22 52 -14Q52 -11 110 221Q112 227 130 227H143Q149 221 149 216Q149 214 148 207T144 186T142 153Q144 114 160 87T203 47T255 29T308 24Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D448\" d=\"M107 637Q73 637 71 641Q70 643 70 649Q70 673 81 682Q83 683 98 683Q139 681 234 681Q268 681 297 681T342 682T362 682Q378 682 378 672Q378 670 376 658Q371 641 366 638H364Q362 638 359 638T352 638T343 637T334 637Q295 636 284 634T266 623Q265 621 238 518T184 302T154 169Q152 155 152 140Q152 86 183 55T269 24Q336 24 403 69T501 205L552 406Q599 598 599 606Q599 633 535 637Q511 637 511 648Q511 650 513 660Q517 676 519 679T529 683Q532 683 561 682T645 680Q696 680 723 681T752 682Q767 682 767 672Q767 650 759 642Q756 637 737 637Q666 633 648 597Q646 592 598 404Q557 235 548 205Q515 105 433 4