-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(603,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(819,-247) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"strong-lagrangian-b745ff48\",\"running-coupling-4222f3c3\",\"confinement-potential-78d4fbbf\",\"flavor-rotation-7e227c98\"]'),
(2010490,'leptons','fermions','Leptons and the Weak Interaction','<p>Leptons are the fundamental spin-1/2 fermions that do not participate in the strong nuclear interaction. In university-level particle physics, they are described within the framework of a chiral gauge theory, where their properties are dictated by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electroweak-symmetry-group\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electroweak Symmetry Group</strong></a> \\( SU(2)_L \\times U(1)_Y \\). Unlike quarks, leptons are exempt from color charge, allowing them to exist as isolated particles.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Chiral Structure and the V-A Interaction</h3>\n                    <p>A defining characteristic of the Standard Model is its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/chirality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Chirality</strong></a>. Left-handed (LH) leptons form <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/left-handed-doublet\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>\\( SU(2)_L \\) doublets</strong></a>: \\( L_L = \\binom{\\nu_\\ell}{\\ell}_L \\), while right-handed (RH) charged leptons exist as singlets (\\( \\ell_R \\)). This asymmetry is responsible for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/v-a-interaction\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>V-A (Vector minus Axial-vector)</strong></a> nature of the weak force, which couples \\( W^\\pm \\) bosons exclusively to left-handed currents. This maximal violation of parity ensures that the universe distinguishes between left and right at the most fundamental level.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Neutrino Oscillations and the PMNS Matrix</h3>\n                    <p>The discovery that neutrinos possess non-zero mass led to the realization that their <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/flavor-eigenstates\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Flavor Eigenstates</strong></a> (\\( \\nu_e, \\nu_\\mu, \\nu_\\tau \\)) are not identical to their <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass-eigenstates\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass Eigenstates</strong></a> (\\( \\nu_1, \\nu_2, \\nu_3 \\)). This mismatch is described by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/pmns-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>PMNS Matrix</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ |\\nu_\\ell\\rangle = \\sum_{i} U_{\\ell i} |\\nu_i\\rangle \\]\n                    As a neutrino propagates, the interference between these states causes the flavor to oscillate over distance (see <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Oscillation Probability</strong></a>). This process is sensitive to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cp-violating-phase\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>CP-Violating Phase</strong></a> (\\( \\delta_{CP} \\)), which may provide the key to understanding the matter-antimatter asymmetry of the universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Majorana Neutrinos and the Seesaw Mechanism</h3>\n                    <p>Because neutrinos carry no electric charge, they may be <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/majorana-fermions\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Majorana Fermions</strong></a>—particles that are their own antiparticles. This possibility allows for a unique mass term that violates lepton number conservation by two units (\\( \\Delta L = 2 \\ )). The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/seesaw-mechanism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Seesaw Mechanism</strong></a> posits that the observed lightness of neutrinos is due to their coupling with extremely heavy right-handed Majorana states, providing a bridge to high-energy physics beyond the Standard Model.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Precision Tests: Anomalous Magnetic Moment</h3>\n                    <p>Leptons provide some of the most rigorous tests of Quantum Electrodynamics (QED). The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/anomalous-magnetic-moment\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Anomalous Magnetic Moment</strong></a> (\\( g-2 \\)) measures the deviation of a lepton\'s gyromagnetic ratio from the Dirac value of 2. While the electron \\( g-2 \\) is the most precisely measured quantity in science, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/muon-g-2\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Muon g-2</strong></a> persistent discrepancy (\\( \\sim 4.2\\sigma \\)) suggests the presence of virtual contributions from yet-to-be-discovered particles, such as supersymmetric partners or leptoquarks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Lepton Flavor Universality</h3>\n                    <p>The Standard Model assumes <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lepton-flavor-universality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lepton Flavor Universality (LFU)</strong></a>, meaning that the fundamental gauge couplings are identical for all three generations. Any differences in behavior must arise solely from phase space and Higgs-mediated masses. Recent anomalies in B-meson decays have challenged this principle, sparking intense research into \"New Physics\" that might preferentially couple to third-generation leptons (tau particles).</p>','Leptons are the fundamental spin-1/2 fermions that do not participate in the strong nuclear interaction. In university-level particle physics, they are described within the framework of a chiral gauge theory, where their properties are dictated by the Electroweak Symmetry Group. Unlike quarks, leptons are exempt from color charge, allowing them to exist as isolated particles.','Leptons are the fundamental spin-1/2 fermions that do not participate in the strong nuclear interaction. In university-level particle physics, they are described within the framework of a chiral gauge theory, where their properties are dictated by the Electroweak Symmetry Group <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"16.164ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 7144.5 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q5