g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(769,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1158,0)\"><use data-c=\"3A6\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1880,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2546.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3602.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(4380.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(636,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(5420.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A6\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(755,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2020\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2020\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6539.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"3A6\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7483.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(8483.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D706\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D706\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9066.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(9455.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A6\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(755,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2020\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2020\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(10574.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"3A6\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A6\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(11296.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(422,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-higgs-potential-class-subtopic-link-higgs-potential-a-0da7a123\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-vacuum-expectation-value-class-subtopic-link-vacuum-expectation-value-a-aa795af5\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-mass-generation-class-subtopic-link-mass-generation-a-c49b69ef\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-fermion-mass-origin-class-subtopic-link-fermion-mass-origin-a-1aba5583\"]'),
(2010493,'spontaneous-symmetry-breaking','higgs-mechanism','Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking','<p>Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking (SSB) is a foundational concept in modern physics where a system governed by symmetric physical laws settles into an asymmetric state. In university-level particle physics, SSB is the primary mechanism for generating mass and distinguishing between fundamental forces, proving that the vacuum state of the universe can \"hide\" the underlying symmetries of the Lagrangian.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Symmetry in Physics: From Global to Local</h3>\n                    <p>In the language of field theory, a symmetry exists if the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> (\\( \\mathcal{L} \\)) remains invariant under a transformation of the fields. While we require our fundamental equations to be highly symmetric (e.g., \\( SU(2) \\times U(1) \\)), nature often appears asymmetric. SSB resolves this paradox by demonstrating that while the <em>equations</em> are symmetric, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vacuum-state\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vacuum State</strong></a> (the state of lowest energy) does not necessarily share that symmetry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Explicit vs. Spontaneous Breaking</h3>\n                    <p>It is crucial to distinguish between <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/explicit-symmetry-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Explicit Symmetry Breaking</strong></a>, where a non-symmetric term is added to the equations, and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spontaneous-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spontaneous Breaking</strong></a>. In SSB, the underlying laws remain perfectly symmetric. A classic analogy is a pencil balanced on its tip: the system is rotationally symmetric, but it must eventually fall in <em>some</em> direction, choosing a specific orientation and thus \"breaking\" the symmetry of its final state.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Mexican Hat Potential</h3>\n                    <p>The mathematical heart of SSB in the Standard Model is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/higgs-potential\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Higgs Potential</strong></a>, often called the \"Mexican Hat\" potential due to its shape:\n                    \\[ V(\\phi) = -\\mu^2 |\\phi|^2 + \\lambda |\\phi|^4 \\]\n                    When \\( \\mu^2 \\gt 0 \\), the minimum of the potential is not at \\( \\phi = 0 \\), but along a circle of radius \\( v = \\sqrt{\\mu^2/\\lambda} \\). The system must choose one point on this circle as its vacuum, breaking the phase symmetry of the field and establishing a non-zero <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vacuum-expectation-value\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vacuum Expectation Value</strong></a> (VEV).</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Goldstone\'s Theorem</h3>\n                    <p>According to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/goldstones-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Goldstone\'s Theorem</strong></a>, whenever a continuous global symmetry is spontaneously broken, there must appear new massless particles called <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/nambu-goldstone-bosons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Nambu-Goldstone Bosons</strong></a>. These particles represent the \"cost-free\" oscillations of the field along the direction of the symmetry (the rim of the Mexican hat). While these are observed in solid-state physics (as phonons or magnons), in particle physics, they play a more subtle role when combined with gauge theories.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Higgs-Kibble Mechanism</h3>\n                    <p>When SSB occurs in a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Theory</strong></a> (a theory with local symmetry), the Goldstone bosons are not observed as independent particles. Instead, they are \"eaten\" by the gauge fields. This process, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/higgs-kibble-mechanism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Higgs-Kibble Mechanism</strong></a>, provides the gauge bosons with a third longitudinal polarization state, effectively giving them <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/origin-of-mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a></a>. This explains why the \\( W \\) and \\( Z \\) bosons are heavy while the underlying electroweak symmetry remains a valid description of the high-energy universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. SSB in Superconductivity and Ferromagnetism</h3>\n                    <p>The concept of SSB transcends particle physics. In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ferromagnetism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Ferromagnetism</strong></a>, the rotational symmetry of electron spins is broken as they align below the Curie temperature. In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/superconductivity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Superconductivity</strong></a>, the \\( U(1) \\) gauge symmetry of electromagnetism is broken within the material by the formation of Cooper pairs. This results in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/meissner-effect\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Meissner Effect</strong></a>—the expulsion of magnetic fields—which is the condensed matter analog of gauge bosons acquiring mass.</p>','Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking (SSB) is a foundational concept in modern physics where a system governed by symmetric physical laws settles into an asymmetric state. In university-level particle physics, SSB is the primary mechanism for generating mass and distinguishing between fundamental forces, proving that the vacuum state of the universe can \"hide\" the underlying symmetries of the Lagrangian. In the language of field theory, a symmetry exists if the Lagrangian remains invariant under a transformation of the fields.','Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking (SSB) is a foundational concept in modern physics where a system governed by symmetric physical laws settles into an asymmetric state. In university-level particle physics, SSB is the primary mechanism for generating mass and distinguishing between fundamental forces, proving that the vacuum state of the universe can \"hide\" the underlying symmetries of the Lagrangian. In the language of field theory, a symmetry exists if the Lagrangian (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.561ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 690 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\" d=\"M62 -22T47 -22T32 -11Q32 -1 56 24T83 55Q113 96 138 172T180 320T234 473T323 609Q364 649 419 677T531 705Q559 705 578 696T604 671T615 645T618 623V611Q618 582 615 571T598 548Q581 531 558 520T518 509Q503 509 503 520Q503 523 505 536T507 560Q507 590 494 610T452 630Q423 630 410 617Q367 578 333 492T271 301T233 170Q211 123 204 112L198 103L224 102Q281 102 369 79T509 52H523Q535 64 544 87T579 128Q616 152 641 152Q656 152 656 142Q656 101 588 40T433 -22Q381 -22 289 1T156 28L141 29L131 20Q111 0 87 -11Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) remains invariant under a transformation of the fields.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"23.25ex\" height=\"2.583ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -891.7 10276.7 1141.7\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D449\" d=\"M52 648Q52 670 65 683H76Q118 680 181 680Q299 680 320 683H330Q336 677 336 674T334 656Q329 641 325 637H304Q282 635 274 635Q245 630 242 620Q242 618 271 369T301 118L374 235Q447 352 520 471T595 594Q599 601 599 609Q599 633 555 637Q537 637 537 648Q537 649 539 661Q542 675 545 679T558 683Q560 683 570 683T604 682T668 681Q7