k:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D706\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8688.1,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(8966.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(9562.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(311,413) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-34\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-higgs-potential-class-subtopic-link-higgs-potential-a-0da7a123\",\"vacuum-expectation-value-551e56d6\",\"massless-excitation-a8b8c52b\",\"spontaneous-breaking-ea761291\"]'),
(2010494,'baryogenesis','standard-model','Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry and Baryogenesis','<p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/matter-antimatter-asymmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Matter-<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/antimatter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Antimatter</strong></a> Asymmetry</strong></a> is the profound cosmological puzzle of why the observable universe is composed almost entirely of matter rather than an equal mixture of matter and antimatter. According to the Standard Model, matter and antimatter were created in equal amounts during the Big Bang, yet today we see a universe where antimatter is extremely rare. <strong>Baryogenesis</strong> is theoretical process in the early universe that generated this small but critical excess of matter over antimatter.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Sakharov Conditions</h3>\n                    <p>In 1967, Andrei Sakharov identified three necessary conditions for a physical theory to generate a baryon asymmetry from an initially symmetric state:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Baryon Number (\\(B\\)) Violation:</strong> Processes must exist that can change the total number of quarks minus antiquarks.</li>\n                            <li><strong>C and CP Violation:</strong> The laws of physics must distinguish between particles and antiparticles, and between their mirror-image reflections.</li>\n                            <li><strong>Departure from Thermal Equilibrium:</strong> The asymmetry must be \"frozen in\" during a rapid transition; otherwise, forward and backward reactions would cancel each other out.</li>\n                        </ul></p>\n\n                    <h3>2. CP Violation in the Standard Model</h3>\n                    <p>The Standard Model contains a source of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cp-violation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>CP Violation</strong></a> in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/ckm-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>CKM Matrix</strong></a> (quark sector) and the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/pmns-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>PMNS Matrix</strong></a> (neutrino sector). This arises from a single complex phase in the Yukawa couplings. While this effect has been measured in Kaon and B-meson decays, the magnitude of CP violation within the Standard Model is several orders of magnitude too small to explain the observed matter density of the universe, implying that <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/new-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>\"New Physics\"</strong></a> must exist.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Baryon-to-Photon Ratio</h3>\n                    <p>The extent of the asymmetry is quantified by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/baryon-photon-ratio\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Baryon-to-Photon Ratio</strong></a> (\\( \\eta \\)), measured to be approximately \\( 6 \\times 10^{-10} \\). This means that for every billion pairs of matter and antimatter particles created in the early universe, one extra matter particle survived the massive annihilation that occurred as the universe cooled. This tiny \"leftover\" is what constitutes all the stars, galaxies, and life we see today.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Electroweak Baryogenesis</h3>\n                    <p>One candidate for generating the asymmetry is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/electroweak-baryogenesis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Electroweak Baryogenesis</strong></a>, which could have occurred during the Electroweak Phase Transition when particles first acquired mass. This process relies on <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sphalerons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sphalerons</strong></a>—complex non-perturbative fluctuations in the \\( SU(2) \\) gauge fields that can convert leptons into baryons. However, for this to work, the Higgs phase transition must be \"strongly first-order,\" a condition that requires a lighter Higgs boson than the one discovered at the LHC.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Leptogenesis and Neutrinos</h3>\n                    <p>A leading alternative theory is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/leptogenesis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Leptogenesis</strong></a>. In this model, an asymmetry is first generated in the lepton sector through the CP-violating decays of extremely heavy right-handed neutrinos (part of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/seesaw-mechanism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Seesaw Mechanism</strong></a>). This lepton asymmetry is then partially converted into a baryon asymmetry by sphaleron processes. Leptogenesis links the matter-antimatter puzzle directly to the mysterious properties of neutrinos and the high-energy scale of Grand Unification.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Experimental Frontiers</h3>\n                    <p>Physicists are searching for the origin of the asymmetry through several avenues:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Neutrinoless Double Beta Decay:</strong> Searching for evidence that neutrinos are their own antiparticles (Majorana fermions).</li>\n                            <li><strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/edm-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Electric Dipole Moments (EDMs)</a>:</strong> Measuring the subatomic \"roundness\" of electrons and neutrons, where a non-zero value would signal new sources of CP violation.</li>\n                            <li><strong>LHC Searches:</strong> Hunting for new heavy particles or symmetries (like Supersymmetry) that could provide the extra \"kick\" needed for successful baryogenesis.</li>\n                        </ul></p>\n\n<h3>Related Theoretical Frameworks</h3><p>The study of Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry and Baryogenesis is intrinsically linked to the <strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/asymmetry-parameter\" class=\"subtopic-link\">The Asymmetry Parameter ()</a></strong>, which provides the necessary analytical depth for understanding the underlying physical manifold.</p>','The Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry is the profound cosmological puzzle of why the observable universe is composed almost entirely of matter rather than an equal mixture of matter and antimatter. According to the Standard Model, matter and antimatter were created in equal amounts during the Big Bang, yet today we see a universe where antimatter is extremely rare. Baryogenesis is theoretical process in the early universe that generated this small but critical excess of matter over antimatter.','The Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry is the profound cosmological puzzle of why the observable universe is composed almost entirely of matter rather than an equal mixture of matter and antimatter. According to the Standard Model, matter and antimatter were created in equal amounts during the Big Bang, yet today we see a universe where antimatter is extremely rare. Baryogenesis is theoretical process in the early universe that generated this small but critical excess of matter over antimatter.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.717ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 759 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\" d=\"M231 637Q204 637 199 638T194 649Q194 676 205 682Q206 683 335 683Q594 683 608 681Q671 671 713 636T756 544Q756 480 698 429T565 360L555 357Q619 348 660 311T702 219Q702 146 630 78T453 1Q446 0 242 0Q42 0 39 2Q35 5 35 10Q35 17 37 24Q42 43 47 45Q51 46 62 46H68Q95 46 128 49Q142 52 147 61Q150 65 219 339T288 628Q288 635 231 637ZM649 544Q649 574 634 600T585 634Q578 636 493 637Q473 637 451 637T416 636H403Q388 635 384 626Q382 622 352 506Q352 503 351 500L320 374H401Q482 374 494 376Q554 386 601 434T649 544ZM595 229Q595 273 572 302T512 336Q506 337 429 337Q311 337 310 336Q310 334 293 263T258 122L240 52Q240 48 252 48T333 46Q422 46 429 47Q491 54 543 105T595 229Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"asymmetry-parameter-3fd62e75\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-sakharov-conditions-class-subtopic-link-theoretical-requirements-a-745816e3\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-sphalerons-class-subtopic-link-tunneling-process-a-4ddc7f13\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-cp-violation-class-subtopic-link-symmetry-breaking-a-e4cf623d\"]'),
(2010495,'vacuum-state','spontaneous-symmetry-breaking','The Vacuum State','<p>In quantum field theory, the Vacuum State (\\( |0\\rangle \\)) is defined as the state of absolute minimum energy in a physical system. While classical physics views the vacuum as a simple void, university-level physics recognizes it as a dynamic medium characterized by zero-point fluctuations. In the context of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spontaneous-symmetry-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking</strong></a>, the vacuum state is the \"stage\" upon which symmetries are hidden; it is possible for the underlying laws of nature to be symmetric while the specific vacuum state chosen by the universe is not, leading to the emergence of mass and fundamental forces.</p><h3>1. The Lowest Energy Eigenstate</h3><p>The vacuum is the <strong>Ground State</strong> of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a>. In a symmetric theory, one might expect the vacuum to be unique and to possess the full symmetry of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a>. However, in systems where spontaneous breaking occurs, the state of lowest energy is <strong>Degenerate</strong>. This means there is an infinite set of possible vacuum states, all with the same minimum energy. The universe must \"choose\" one of these states, an event that defines a preferred orientation in the 4D manifold and breaks the symmetry of the resulting <strong>Physical Causality</strong>.</p><h3>2. Vacuum Expectation Value (VEV)</h3><p>When a symmetry is broken, the vacuum state acquires a non-zero <strong>Vacuum Expectation Value</strong> (\\( v \\)). For a scalar field \\( \\phi \\), this is expressed as \\( \\langle 0|\\phi|0\\rangle = v \\). This VEV represents a constant \"background field\" that permeates all of space. Particles moving through this non-empty vacuum interact with the VEV, effectively gaining inertia. This is the mathematical basis for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/higgs-mechanism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Higgs Mechanism</strong></a>, where the vacuum itself acts as the source of mass for the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/standard-model-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Standard Model</strong></a> particles.</p><h3>3. Vacuum Stability and Phase Transitions</h3><p>The nature of the vacuum can change depending on the energy scales involved. At extremely high temperatures (like the early <strong>Big Bang</strong>), the vacuum is symmetric and the VEV is zero. As the universe cools, it undergoes a <strong>Vacuum Phase Transition</strong>, where the symmetric state becomes unstable and the system \"falls\" into a new, asymmetric vacuum. The study of <strong>False Vacuum</strong> decay investigates whether our current state is truly the absolute minimum or a metastable configuration that could theoretically collapse into a lower-energy 4D <strong>Ontology</strong>.</p><h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> of the Vacuum</h3><p>The vacuum state dictates the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-structure\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Structure</strong></a> of the universe. By hiding certain symmetries, the vacuum determines which fields are long-range (massless) and which are short-range (massive). This ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the cosmos are consistent across the <strong>Block Universe</strong>. Any local fluctuation in the vacuum properties would violate the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/cosmological-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Cosmological Principle</strong></a>, making the stability and homogeneity of the vacuum a cornerstone of modern <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>.</p>','In quantum field theory, the Vacuum State is defined as the state of absolute minimum energy in a physical system. While classical physics views the vacuum as a simple void, university-level physics recognizes it as a dynamic medium characterized by zero-point fluctuations. In the context of Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking, the vacuum state is the \"stage\" upon which symmetries are hidden; it is possible for the underlying laws of nature to be symmetric while the specific vacuum state chosen by the universe is not, leading to the emergence of mass and fundamental forces.','In quantum field theory, the Vacuum State (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.64ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1167 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\" d=\"M55 732Q56 739 61 744T75 750Q85 750 92 740Q95 733 186 494T278 250T187 6T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q67 -250 62 -245T55 -232Q55 -227 145 11Q236 248 236 250T145 489Q55 727 55 732Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(278,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) is defined as the state of absolute minimum energy in a physical system. While classical physics views the vacuum as a simple void, university-level physics recognizes it as a dynamic medium characterized by zero-point fluctuations. In the context of Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking, the vacuum state is the \"stage\" upon which symmetries are hidden; it is possible for the underlying laws of nature to be symmetric while the specific vacuum state chosen by the universe is not, leading to the emergence of mass and fundamental forces.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.64ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1167 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\" d=\"M55 732Q56 739 61 744T75 750Q85 750 92 740Q95 733 186 494T278 250T187 6T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q67 -250 62 -245T55 -232Q55 -227 145 11Q236 248 236 250T145 489Q55 727 55 732Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(278,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"vacuum-expectation-value-120df58b\",\"ground-state-energy-ca8dfdfd\"]'),
(2010496,'explicit-symmetry-breaking','spontaneous-symmetry-breaking','Explicit Symmetry Breaking','<p>Explicit Symmetry Breaking occurs when the fundamental laws of motion for a physical system—specifically the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a></a>—are not invariant under a particular symmetry group. Unlike <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spontaneous-symmetry-breaking\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking</strong></a>, where the underlying equations remain symmetric while the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vacuum-state\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vacuum State</strong></a> does not, explicit breaking happens because a non-symmetric term is added directly to the equations. This distinguishes between a symmetry that is \"hidden\" and one that is fundamentally absent at a given energy scale.</p><h3>1. The Asymmetric Lagrangian</h3><p>In university-level field theory, a system is described as explicitly broken if the variation of its Lagrangian (\\( \\delta \\mathcal{L} \\)) does not vanish under a symmetry transformation. This is usually modeled as a symmetric piece plus a small perturbation: \\[ \\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{sym} + \\mathcal{L}_{breaking} \\] The \"breaking term\" identifies a preferred direction or scale in the 4D manifold, effectively defining the limits of the system\'s <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a> and ensuring that the resulting <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> do not possess the conservation laws associated with the original symmetry.</p><h3>2. Comparison with Spontaneous Breaking</h3><p>The distinction between explicit and spontaneous breaking is a cornerstone of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>. In spontaneous breaking, the symmetry is still present in the dynamics but \"realized\" in a way that obscures it (like a balanced pencil falling). In explicit breaking, the \"pencil\" is weighted on one side from the start. This has profound implications for <strong>Particle Physics</strong>; for instance, the masses of quarks explicitly break <strong>Chiral Symmetry</strong>, which is a different physical process than the Higgs mechanism providing mass through the vacuum.</p><h3>3. External Fields as Breaking Agents</h3><p>One of the most common sources of explicit breaking is the presence of an <strong>External Field</strong>. A system of particles that is normally invariant under <strong>Spatial Rotations</strong> (isotropic) will have its symmetry explicitly broken if placed in a uniform magnetic field. The field defines a unique axis, forcing the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-vector\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>State Vector</strong></a> to align with it. This is not a choice made by the system, but a requirement imposed by the environment, anchoring 4D <strong>Physical Causality</strong> in the interaction between the system and its background geometry.</p><h3>4. Pseudo-Goldstone Bosons</h3><p>When a symmetry that would normally be spontaneously broken is also slightly broken explicitly, the resulting particles are not massless <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/nambu-goldstone-bosons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Nambu-Goldstone Bosons</strong></a>, but instead become <strong>Pseudo-Goldstone Bosons</strong>. These particles, such as the <strong>Pion</strong> in nuclear physics, possess a small mass proportional to the strength of the explicit breaking term. Understanding this \"Soft Breaking\" is essential for describing the <strong>Standard Model</strong> and the approximate symmetries that govern the subatomic vacuum.</p>','Explicit Symmetry Breaking occurs when the fundamental laws of motion for a physical system—specifically the Lagrangian or Hamiltonian Operator—are not invariant under a particular symmetry group. Unlike Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking, where the underlying equations remain symmetric while the Vacuum State does not, explicit breaking happens because a non-symmetric term is added directly to the equations. This distinguishes between a symmetry that is \"hidden\" and one that is fundamentally absent at a given energy scale.','Explicit Symmetry Breaking occurs when the fundamental laws of motion for a physical system—specifically the Lagrangian or Hamiltonian Operator—are not invariant under a particular symmetry group. Unlike Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking, where the underlying equations remain symmetric while the Vacuum State does not, explicit breaking happens because a non-symmetric term is added directly to the equations. This distinguishes between a symmetry that is \"hidden\" and one that is fundamentally absent at a given energy scale.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.667ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"19.888ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 8790.7 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\" d=\"M62 -22T47 -22T32 -11Q32 -1 56 24T83 55Q113 96 138 172T180 320T234 473T323 609Q364 649 419 677T531 705Q559 705 578 696T604 671T615 645T618 623V611Q618 582 615 571T598 548Q581 531 558 520T518 509Q503 509 503 520Q503 523 505 536T507 560Q507 590 494 610T452 630Q423 630 410 617Q367 578 333 492T271 301T233 170Q211 123 204 112L198 103L224 102Q281 102 369 79T509 52H523Q535 64 544 87T579 128Q616 152 641 152Q656 152 656 142Q656 101 588 40T433 -22Q381 -22 289 1T156 28L141 29L131 20Q111 0 87 -11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 17