181 615T143 592T132 580H135Q138 579 143 578T153 573T165 566T175 555T183 540T186 520Q186 498 172 481T127 463Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(645,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D442\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D442\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1408,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1797,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2297,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"scalar-laws-01d54036\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-orbital-angular-momentum-class-subtopic-link-orbital-momentum-a-afe05d2a\",\"non-commutativity-6c599f6c\",\"field-symmetry-af6a4d8f\"]'),
(2010528,'fields-theory','action-principle','Physical Fields','<p>A Physical Field is a mathematical entity that assigns a value to every point in the manifold. In university-level philosophy of physics, fields are the primary <strong>Ontologies</strong> of the universe. They anchor our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the realization that the vacuum is not empty but is university-level permeated by these evolving structures.</p><h3>1. Scalar and Vector Field Foundations</h3><p>Fields are categorized by their transformation properties. A <strong>Scalar Field</strong> is invariant, while a <strong>Vector Field</strong> assigns a magnitude and direction. In university-level theory, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vector-calculus\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vector Calculus</strong></a> provides the tools to analyze the <strong>Gradients</strong> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fluxes\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fluxes</strong></a> of these fields. This ensured that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> are coordinate-independent, providing the definitive map for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/classical-field-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Classical Field Theory</strong></a>.</p><h3>2. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/field-action\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Field Action</strong></a> and Locality</h3><p>The behavior of fields is governed by the <strong>Action Functional</strong>. In university-level mechanics, the system identically extremizes the integral of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian Density</strong></a>. A critical requirement is <strong>Locality</strong>, ensuring that changes at one point only affect their immediate neighbors. This reveals that the causal structure of the field respects the speed of light, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the geometric consistency of the vacuum.</p><h3>3. The Bridge to Interaction</h3><p>Fields interact through the coupling of their equations. In university-level astrophysics, the <strong>Electromagnetic Field</strong> is coupled to matter through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maxwells-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maxwell\'s Equations</strong></a>. This link provides <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/the-bridge\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Bridge</strong></a> between local field bits and the global <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the Block Universe.</p><h3>4. Fields and Information Theory</h3><p>From the perspective of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a>, a field is a \'Distributed Data Register.\' The vacuum handles the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> by ensuring that the information bits at each point are university-level linked. This investigation pushes the boundaries of our understanding, providing the ultimate map for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mathematical Realism</strong></a>.</p>','A Physical Field is a mathematical entity that assigns a value to every point in the manifold. In university-level philosophy of physics, fields are the primary Ontologies of the universe. They anchor our Scientific Realism in the realization that the vacuum is not empty but is university-level permeated by these evolving structures.','A Physical Field is a mathematical entity that assigns a value to every point in the manifold. In university-level philosophy of physics, fields are the primary Ontologies of the universe. They anchor our Scientific Realism in the realization that the vacuum is not empty but is university-level permeated by these evolving structures.','','[]','[]','[\"field-action-identity-a98aa2cf\",\"field-equation-law-149aafbb\"]'),
(2010529,'action-density','action-principle','Action Density','<p>The Action Density, almost universally referred to as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian Density</strong></a> (\\( \\mathcal{L} \\)), is the fundamental local scalar that determines the dynamics of continuous fields. In university-level physics, particularly in <strong>Quantum Field Theory</strong> and <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the global action is obtained by integrating this density over a 4D spacetime volume. The action density encodes all the local information about field energy, momentum, and interactions at a specific event in spacetime.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Transition to Local Physics</h3>\n                    <p>In classical particle mechanics, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> \\( L \\) is a function of time only. However, for fields that fill all of space, we must define the physics at each point \\( x^\\mu \\). The Action Density allows us to do this by expressing the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> as a spatial integral: \\( L = \\int \\mathcal{L} d^3x \\). This shift ensures that the laws of physics are <strong>Local</strong>, meaning that a perturbation at one point cannot affect another point faster than the speed of light.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Lorentz Scalar Property</h3>\n                    <p>For a physical theory to be consistent with <strong>Special Relativity</strong>, its action density must be a <strong>Lorentz Scalar</strong>. This means that \\( \\mathcal{L} \\) must have the same value for all inertial observers. This requirement severely restricts the possible terms in the density; they must be constructed from contracted tensors (like \\( F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu} \\)) or spinor bilinears, ensuring that the resulting <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/field-action\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Field Action</strong></a> is an invariant of nature.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Energy and Kinetic Terms</h3>\n                    <p>A typical action density consists of <strong>Kinetic Terms</strong>, which involve spacetime derivatives (\\( \\partial_\\mu \\phi \\)), and <strong>Potential Terms</strong>, which depend only on the field values. The kinetic terms describe how the field \"spreads\" or oscillates, while the potential terms (including mass terms) describe the energy cost of having a non-zero field value. In a vacuum, the action density is usually minimized at the field\'s <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vacuum-expectation-value\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vacuum Expectation Value (VEV)</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Source of Field Equations</h3>\n                    <p>Applying the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stationary-action-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of Stationary Action</strong></a> to the action density yields the <strong>Euler-Lagrange Field Equations</strong>. These are partial differential equations that dictate the local evolution of the field. Because the density depends on both space and time derivatives, the resulting equations typically take the form of <strong>Wave Equations</strong>, explaining why subatomic particles behave like waves as they propagate through the vacuum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Gauge Densities and Interactions</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Gauge Theory</strong>, the action density includes terms that couple different fields together. For example, the interaction between an electron and a photon is described by the density term \\( -e\\bar{\\psi}\\gamma^\\mu A_\\mu \\psi \\). This term ensures that the total action density remains invariant under local symmetry transformations. This approach reveals that <strong>Forces</strong> are simply the \"interaction densities\" required by the geometric symmetries of the universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Dimensional Analysis and Renormalizability</h3>\n                    <p>The Action Density has dimensions of <strong>Energy per Unit Volume</strong> (or Action per unit 4D volume). In 4D spacetime, this means its mass dimension is 4 (in units where \\( \\hbar = c = 1 \\)). This dimensional constraint is the basis for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/renormalization\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Renormalization Theory</strong></a>: only terms in the action density with dimensions less than or equal to 4 lead to \"sensible\" quantum theories that don\'t produce unphysical infinities at high energies.</p>','The Action Density, almost universally referred to as the Lagrangian Density, is the fundamental local scalar that determines the dynamics of continuous fields. In university-level physics, particularly in Quantum Field Theory and General Relativity, the global action is obtained by integrating this density over a 4D spacetime volume. The action density encodes all the local information about field energy, momentum, and interactions at a specific event in spacetime.','The Action Density, almost universally referred to as the Lagrangian Density (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.561ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 690 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\" d=\"M62 -22T47 -22T32 -11Q32 -1 56 24T83 55Q113 96 138 172T180 320T234 473T323 609Q364 649 419 677T531 705Q559 705 578 696T604 671T615 645T618 623V611Q618 582 615 571T598 548Q581 531 558 520T518 509Q503 509 503 520Q503 523 505 536T507 560Q507 590 494 610T452 630Q423 630 410 617Q367 578 333 492T271 301T233 170Q211 123 204 112L198 103L224 102Q281 102 369 79T509 52H523Q535 64 544 87T579 128Q616 152 641 152Q656 152 656 142Q656 101 588 40T433 -22Q381 -22 289 1T156 28L141 29L131 20Q111 0 87 -11Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>), is the fundamental local scalar that determines the dynamics of continuous fields. In university-level physics, particularly in Quantum Field Theory and General Relativity, the global action is obtained by integrating this density over a 4D spacetime volume. The action density encodes all the local information about field energy, momentum, and interactions at a specific event in spacetime.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.561ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 690 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\" d=\"M62 -22T47 -22T32 -11Q32 -1 56 24T83 55Q113 96 138 172T180 320T234 473T323 609Q364 649 419 677T531 705Q559 705 578 696T604 671T615 645T618 623V611Q618 582 615 571T598 548Q581 531 558 520T518 509Q503 509 503 520Q503 523 505 536T507 560Q507 590 494 610T452 630Q423 630 410 617Q367 578 333 492T271 301T233 170Q211 123 204 112L198 103L224 102Q281 102 369 79T509 52H523Q535 64 544 87T579 128Q616 152 641 152Q656 152 656 142Q656 101 588 40T433 -22Q381 -22 289 1T156 28L141 29L131 20Q111 0 87 -11Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-C-4C\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"spacetime-integral-9d420441\",\"scalar-field-density-6bba9017\",\"propagation-energy-f223c593\",\"vacuum-state-c2e0999d\"]'),
(2010530,'quantum-interference','action-principle','Quantum Interference and the Action','<p><strong>Quantum Interference</strong> is the phenomenon where the probability amplitudes of different paths add together to determine the total probability of an event. In university-level physics, interference is the mechanism that bridges the gap between the quantum and classical worlds. While classical particles follow a single path of stationary action, quantum entities explore all possible paths, with their <strong>Quantum Phases</strong> interfering constructively or destructively based on the value of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/action-integral\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Action Integral</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Superposition of Amplitudes</h3>\n                    <p>The core of interference lies in the <strong>Superposition Principle</strong>. If an event can occur via multiple alternative paths, the total probability amplitude \\( \\Psi \\) is the sum of the individual amplitudes: \\( \\Psi = \\sum \\psi_i \\). Because these amplitudes are <strong>Complex Numbers</strong>, they can reinforce each other (Constructive Interference) or cancel each other out (Destructive Interference). The observable probability is the squared magnitude of this sum: \\( P = |\\Psi|^2 \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Role of the Action Integral</h3>\n                    <p>Richard Feynman\'s <strong>Path Integral</strong> formulation identifies the <strong>Action</strong> (\\( S \\)) as the source of the quantum phase. Each path contributes a phase factor \\( e^{iS/\\hbar} \\). Interference occurs because different paths have different actions, causing their phase \"clocks\" to point in different directions. The total amplitude is a massive interference pattern over the space of all possible histories, where the action acts as the fundamental phase-keeper.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Constructive Interference: The Classical Path</h3>\n                    <p>The <strong>Method of Stationary Phase</strong> explains why we don\'t see interference in our daily lives. For macroscopic objects, the action \\( S \\) is much larger than Planck\'s constant \\( \\hbar \\). Even a tiny variation from the classical path causes the phase to oscillate wildly, leading to <strong>Destructive Interference</strong>. Only in the immediate neighborhood of the <strong>Stationary Path</strong> (\\( \\delta S = 0 \\)) do the phases align constructively, creating the \"one true path\" of classical mechanics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Double-Slit Experiment</h3>\n                    <p>The most iconic demonstration of quantum interference is the <strong>Double-Slit Experiment</strong>. Even when particles are sent one at a time, they build up an interference pattern on the screen. This proves that the particle \"interferes with itself\" by exploring the paths through both slits simultaneously. The total action for each path determines the relative phase at the detector, resulting in the characteristic fringe pattern that defies classical intuition.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Coherence and Decoherence</h3>\n                    <p>Interference is only possible if the system maintains <strong>Quantum Coherence</strong>. If the environment \"measures\" or interacts with the system, it gains information about which path was taken. This interaction randomizes the phases, a process known as <strong>Decoherence</strong>. When decoherence occurs, the interference terms vanish, and the quantum probabilities collapse into classical \"or\" logic, explaining why the macroscopic world appears deterministic and non-interfering.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Applications: Mach-Zehnder and Computing</h3>\n                    <p>Understanding interference is critical for modern technology. In a <strong>Mach-Zehnder Interferometer</strong>, physicists use the relative phase between two paths to measure infinitesimal shifts in distance or refractive index. In <strong>Quantum Computing</strong>, algorithms are designed to use interference to amplify the probability amplitude of the correct answer while canceling out the amplitudes of incorrect ones, allowing for exponential speedups in solving complex problems.</p>','Quantum Interference is the phenomenon where the probability amplitudes of different paths add together to determine the total probability of an event. In university-level physics, interference is the mechanism that bridges the gap between the quantum and classical worlds. While classical particles follow a single path of stationary action, quantum entities explore all possible paths, with their Quantum Phases interfering constructively or destructively based on the value of the Action Integral.','Quantum Interference is the phenomenon where the probability amplitudes of different paths add together to determine the total probability of an event. In university-level physics, interference is the mechanism that bridges the gap between the quantum and classical worlds. While classical particles follow a single path of stationary action, quantum entities explore all possible paths, with their Quantum Phases interfering constructively or destructively based on the value of the Action Integral.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 778 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\" d=\"M340 622Q338 623 335 625T331 629T325 631T314 634T298 635T274 636T239 637H212V683H224Q248 680 389 680T554 683H566V637H539Q479 637 464 635T439 622L438 407Q438 192 439 192Q443 193 449 195T474 207T507 232T536 276T557 344Q560 365 562 417T573 493Q587 536 620 544Q627 546 671 546H715L722 540V515Q714 509 708 509Q680 505 671 476T658 392T644 307Q599 177 451 153L438 151V106L439 61Q446 54 451 52T476 48T539 46H566V0H554Q530 3 389 3T224 0H212V46H239Q259 46 273 46T298 47T314 48T325 51T331 54T335 57T340 61V151Q126 178 117 406Q115 503 69 509Q55 509 55 526Q55 541 59 543T86 546H107H120Q150 546 161 543T184 528Q198 514 204 493Q212 472 213 420T226 316T272 230Q287 216 303 207T330 194L339 192Q340 192 340 407V622Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"superposition-1963907b\",\"cross-product-7df7b6fd\",\"path-comparison-77e1d7d9\",\"classical-limit-30306b28\"]'),
(2010531,'master-equation-physics','action-principle','The Master Equation of Physics','<p>In the context of variational principles, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stationary-action-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of Stationary Action</strong></a> is often referred to as the <strong>Master Equation of Physics</strong>. While most physical laws are written as specific differential equations (like \\( F=ma \\) or Maxwell\'s equations), the action principle provides a single, overarching meta-law from which all others descend. In university-level theory, this \"Master Equation\" represents the highest level of unification, showing that the diverse phenomena of the universe are all manifestations of a single requirement for dynamical optimality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Unification through Extremization</h3>\n                    <p>The power of the \"Master Equation\" (\\( \\delta S = 0 \\)) lies in its universality. By changing only the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian Density</strong></a> (\\( \\mathcal{L} \\)), a physicist can switch from describing a vibrating string to the behavior of light or the expansion of the universe. This scalar-based approach bypasses the complexities of vector forces, revealing that the fundamental character of nature is not one of push-and-pull, but one of <strong>Stationarity</strong> in a functional landscape.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. From Classical to Quantum: The Path Integral</h3>\n                    <p>The action principle remains the \"Master Equation\" even in the quantum regime. In the <strong>Path Integral</strong> formulation, the classical condition \\( \\delta S = 0 \\) is replaced by the integral \\( \\int \\mathcal{D}\\phi \\, e^{iS/\\hbar} \\). This shows that classical laws are not separate from quantum laws; they are simply the <strong>Stationary Phase Approximation</strong> of a deeper, more comprehensive quantum totality. The action integral thus serves as the essential mathematical link between all scales of reality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Einstein-Hilbert Action: Gravity as Geometry</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the \"Master Equation\" takes the form of the <strong>Einstein-Hilbert Action</strong>: \\( S = \\int R \\sqrt{-g} d^4x \\). Here, the scalar being extremized is the <strong>Ricci Curvature</strong> of spacetime itself. This reveals that gravity is not a force in the Newtonian sense, but is the requirement that the geometry of the universe must be stationary. This geometric master equation unifies space, time, and matter into a single dynamical system.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/standard-model-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Standard Model</strong></a> Action</h3>\n                    <p>The entire known subatomic world is summarized by the <strong>Standard Model Action</strong>. This incredibly complex <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> density includes terms for electromagnetism, the weak and strong nuclear forces, and the Higgs field. By applying the Master Equation to this specific density, physicists derive the interactions of every known elementary particle, proving that the diversity of the <strong>Standard Model</strong> arises from a single variational principle.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Symmetries as the Source Code</h3>\n                    <p>The \"Master Equation\" approach reveals that the laws of physics are dictated by <strong>Symmetries</strong>. Instead of guessing equations, modern physicists guess the symmetries of the universe (like <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Invariance</strong></a> or <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lorentz-covariant\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lorentz Covariance</strong></a>). They then construct the most general action density that respects these symmetries. This \"Symmetry-First\" methodology proves that the Master Equation is actually a statement about the <strong>Geometric Harmony</strong> of the universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. The Search for the Ultimate Action</h3>\n                    <p>Theoretical physics is currently engaged in a search for the <strong>Ultimate Master Equation</strong>—a single action principle that incorporates both Quantum Field Theory and General Relativity. Candidates like <strong>String Theory</strong> or <strong>Loop Quantum Gravity</strong> propose new, higher-dimensional actions. The goal is to find a single scalar functional that, when varied, yields the \"Theory of Everything,\" completing the journey that began with the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/least-action-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of Least Action</strong></a>.</p>','In the context of variational principles, the Principle of Stationary Action is often referred to as the Master Equation of Physics. While most physical laws are written as specific differential equations (like or Maxwell\'s equations), the action principle provides a single, overarching meta-law from which all others descend. In university-level theory, this \"Master Equation\" represents the highest level of unification, showing that the diverse phenomena of the universe are all manifestations of a single requirement for dynamical optimality.','In the context of variational principles, the Principle of Stationary Action is often referred to as the Master Equation of Physics. While most physical laws are written as specific differential equations (like <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.895ex\" height=\"1.724ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 3489.6 762\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\" d=\"M48 1Q31 1 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H742Q749 676 749 669Q749 664 736 557T722 447Q720 440 702 440H690Q683 445 683 453Q683 454 686 477T689 530Q689 560 682 579T663 610T626 626T575 633T503 634H480Q398 633 393 631Q388 629 386 623Q385 622 352 492L320 363H375Q378 363 398 363T426 364T448 367T472 374T489 386Q502 398 511 419T524 457T529 475Q532 480 548 480H560Q567 475 567 470Q567 467 536 339T502 207Q500 200 482 200H470Q463 206 463 212Q463 215 468 234T473 274Q473 303 453 310T364 317H309L277 190Q245 66 245 60Q245 46 334 46H359Q365 40 365 39T363 19Q359 6 353 0H336Q295 2 185 2Q120 2 86 2T48 1Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45A\" d=\"M21 287Q22 293 24 303T36 341T56 388T88 425T132 442T175 435T205 417T221 395T229 376L231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q303 442 384 442Q401 442 415 440T441 433T460 423T475 411T485 398T493 385T497 373T500 364T502 357L510 367Q573 442 659 442Q713 442 746 415T780 336Q780 285 742 178T704 50Q705 36 709 31T724 26Q752 26 776 56T815 138Q818 149 821 151T837 153Q857 153 857 145Q857 144 853 130Q845 101 831 73T785 17T716 -10Q669 -10 648 17T627 73Q627 92 663 193T700 345Q700 404 656 404H651Q565 404 506 303L499 291L466 157Q433 26 428 16Q415 -11 385 -11Q372 -11 364 -4T353 8T350 18Q350 29 384 161L420 307Q423 322 423 345Q423 404 379 404H374Q288 404 229 303L222 291L189 157Q156 26 151 16Q138 -11 108 -11Q95 -11 87 -5T76 7T74 17Q74 30 112 181Q151 335 151 342Q154 357 154 369Q154 405 129 405Q107 405 92 377T69 316T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q331 441 370 392Q386 422 416 422Q429 422 439 414T449 394Q449 381 412 234T374 68Q374 43 381 