195 656 227 686T302 717Q319 716 351 709T407 697T433 690Q451 682 451 662Q451 644 438 628T403 612Q382 612 348 641T288 671T249 657T235 628Q235 584 334 463Q401 379 401 292Q401 169 340 80T205 -10H198Q127 -10 83 36T36 153Q36 286 151 382Q191 413 252 434Q252 435 245 449T230 481T214 521T201 566T195 609ZM112 130Q112 83 136 55T204 27Q233 27 256 51T291 111T309 178T316 232Q316 267 309 298T295 344T269 400L259 396Q215 381 183 342T137 256T118 179T112 130Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"7B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(479,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1273,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(1717.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(536,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2594.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"7D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3372.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(4428.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FF\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FF\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(477,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(345,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"poisson-bracket-337b833b\",\"algebraic-harmony-05b8b290\",\"driver-of-symmetry-6746f980\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-generalized-momentum-class-subtopic-link-generalized-momentum-a-6c138f34\"]'),
(2010544,'generator-of-rotations','angular-momentum','Generator of Rotations','<p>In the mathematical language of modern physics, the Generator of Rotations is the operator or physical quantity that produces an infinitesimal rotation of a system\'s state. In university-level mechanics, this role is filled by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{J} \\)). This perspective, rooted in <strong>Lie Group Theory</strong>, unifies classical and quantum dynamics by showing that rotational motion is not just a type of travel, but a geometric transformation of the underlying physical manifold.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Infinitesimal Rotations and Lie Algebra</h3>\n                    <p>A rotation by a finite angle \\( \\theta \\) is a complex transformation, but an <strong>Infinitesimal Rotation</strong> \\( \\delta \\theta \\) is much simpler. It can be expressed as a linear perturbation of the identity: \\( R(\\delta \\theta) = I + \\delta \\theta \\times \\dots \\). The operator that defines this first-order change is the <strong>Generator</strong>. This leads to the <strong>Lie Algebra</strong> of the rotation group \\( SO(3) \\), where the generators satisfy non-commutative commutation relations, reflecting the fact that the order of rotations matters in three-dimensional space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Classical Generators and Poisson Brackets</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> mechanics, physical quantities act as generators of transformations in <strong>Phase Space</strong>. Angular momentum \\( L_i \\) is the generator of rotations about the \\( i \\)-th axis. The change in any observable \\( f \\) under a rotation is given by its <strong>Poisson Bracket</strong> with the generator: \\( \\delta f = \\{f, \\mathbf{L} \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}\\} \\delta \\theta \\). This reveals that the \"momentum\" we measure is actually the mathematical \"charge\" that pushes the system through its orientation states.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Quantum Generators and Unitary Operators</h3>\n                    <p>In quantum mechanics, a finite rotation is produced by a <strong>Unitary Operator</strong> \\( \\hat{U} \\). According to Stone\'s Theorem, any continuous symmetry operator can be written as an exponential of its generator: \\( \\hat{U}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) = \\exp(-i \\boldsymbol{\\theta} \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{J}} / \\hbar) \\). Here, \\( \\hat{\\mathbf{J}} \\) is the angular momentum operator. This formulation proves that the total dynamics of a quantum state under rotation are completely determined by the algebraic properties of its generators.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Non-Abelian Structure: [Ji, Jj]</h3>\n                    <p>The most critical feature of the rotation generators is their <strong>Non-Abelian</strong> nature. Unlike translations, rotations about different axes do not commute. This captured by the fundamental commutation relation: \\( [J_i, J_j] = i\\hbar \\epsilon_{ijk} J_k \\). This algebraic requirement is what leads to <strong>Quantized Angular Momentum</strong> and restricts the possible values of spin and orbital states to discrete integers and half-integers, defining the structure of atoms.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Generators of Internal Symmetries (Spin)</h3>\n                    <p>Advanced physics recognizes that generators can act on more than just spatial coordinates. <strong>Intrinsic Spin</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{S} \\)) is the generator of rotations in the \"Internal Space\" of a particle. For fermions, these generators act on <strong>Spinors</strong> and satisfy the \\( SU(2) \\) algebra. This reveals that spin is not a \"spinning ball\" effect but is the fundamental requirement that subatomic fields must transform correctly under the rotational group of the universe.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Conservation and Invariance</h3>\n                    <p>The status of angular momentum as a generator is deeply linked to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/rotational-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Rotational Invariance</strong></a> (isotropy). According to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/noethers-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Noether\'s Theorem</strong></a>, if the action integral is invariant under the transformation produced by a generator, that generator must be a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conserved-quantity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conserved Quantity</strong></a>. Thus, the conservation of angular momentum is not an independent law, but a mathematical necessity arising from the fact that the generators of rotations preserve the symmetry of the physical <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a>.</p>','In the mathematical language of modern physics, the Generator of Rotations is the operator or physical quantity that produces an infinitesimal rotation of a system\'s state. In university-level mechanics, this role is filled by Angular Momentum. This perspective, rooted in Lie Group Theory, unifies classical and quantum dynamics by showing that rotational motion is not just a type of travel, but a geometric transformation of the underlying physical manifold.','In the mathematical language of modern physics, the Generator of Rotations is the operator or physical quantity that produces an infinitesimal rotation of a system\'s state. In university-level mechanics, this role is filled by Angular Momentum (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.344ex\" height=\"1.577ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 594 697\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\" d=\"M174 114Q174 96 169 82T159 63T144 47L155 45Q183 40 203 40Q271 40 290 104Q294 118 294 150T295 380V624H154V686H169Q196 683 365 683Q499 683 517 686H527V624H446V379Q446 183 446 153T441 108Q413 32 315 2Q266 -11 208 -11Q160 -11 118 -2T42 37T8 114V122Q8 150 30 174T91 198T152 174T174 122V114Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D409\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>). This perspective, rooted in Lie Group Theory, unifies classical and quantum dynamics by showing that rotational motion is not just a type of travel, but a geometric transformation of the underlying physical manifold.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.344ex\" height=\"1.577ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 594 697\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\" d=\"M174 114Q174 96 169 82T159 63T144 47L155 45Q183 40 203 40Q271 40 290 104Q294 118 294 150T295 380V624H154V686H169Q196 683 365 683Q499 683 517 686H527V624H446V379Q446 183 446 153T441 108Q413 32 315 2Q266 -11 208 -11Q160 -11 118 -2T42 37T8 114V122Q8 150 30 174T91 198T152 174T174 122V114Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D409\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D409\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"linear-mapping-52afec42\",\"finite-rotation-ca0d648a\",\"group-algebra-721a2d6d\",\"conserved-charge-1e792fb7\"]'),
(2010545,'orbital-angular-momentum','angular-momentum','Orbital Angular Momentum','<p>Orbital <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{L} \\)) is the vector quantity that describes the rotational motion of a particle or system of particles about a specific origin. In university-level mechanics, it is defined as the cross product of the position vector and the linear momentum: \\( \\mathbf{L} = \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{p} \\). This quantity represents the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generator-of-rotations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Generator of Rotations</strong></a> through space and is the key to understanding central-force motion, including planetary orbits and atomic electronic states.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Cross Product Definition and Geometry</h3>\n                    <p>The vector \\( \\mathbf{L} \\) is perpendicular to the plane formed by the position (\\( \\mathbf{r} \\)) and momentum (\\( \\mathbf{p} \\)) vectors. Its magnitude is given by \\( L = rp \\sin \\phi \\), where \\( \\phi \\) is the angle between the two. This geometric structure ensures that for a <strong>Central Force</strong>, where the force is always directed toward the origin, the angular momentum vector remains constant in both magnitude and direction, forcing the motion to remain a fixed 2D plane.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Relation to Areal Velocity</h3>\n                    <p>For a particle in an orbit, the rate at which the position vector sweeps out area is proportional to the orbital angular momentum: \\( dA/dt = L / (2m) \\). This provides the mathematical proof for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/keplers-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Kepler\'s Second Law</strong></a>. In any system where angular momentum is conserved, the particle must sweep out equal areas in equal times, a rule that holds for any attractive or repulsive force that depends only on the distance from the source.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generalized-coordinates\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Generalized Coordinates</strong></a> and Conjugate Momenta</h3>\n                    <p>In the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> framework, if a system is described in spherical coordinates (\\( r, \\theta, \\phi \\)), the orbital angular momentum is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/canonical-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Canonical Momentum</strong></a> conjugate to the angular coordinates. For example, \\( p_\\phi = \\partial L / \\partial \\dot{\\phi} = mr^2 \\sin^2 \\theta \\dot{\\phi} \\). This identification allows physicists to treat rotations with the same algebraic rigor as linear translations, linking conservation laws directly to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/isotropy-of-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Isotropy of Space</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Multi-Particle Systems and Center of Mass</h3>\n                    <p>For a system of particles, the total orbital angular momentum is the sum of the angular momentum of the <strong>Center of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mass\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mass</strong></a> (CM)</strong> and the internal angular momentum relative to the CM. \n                    \\[ \\mathbf{L}_{total} = \\mathbf{R} \\times \\mathbf{P} + \\sum \\mathbf{r}_i\' \\times \\mathbf{p}_i\' \\]\n                    This decomposition allows for the separation of the global trajectory (e.g., a planet orbiting a star) from the internal rotational dynamics of the body itself.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Quantum Orbital Momentum and Spherical Harmonics</h3>\n                    <p>In quantum mechanics, orbital angular momentum is quantized. The operator \\( \\hat{\\mathbf{L}} = -i\\hbar (\\mathbf{r} \\times \\nabla) \\) has eigenvalues restricted to \\( L^2 = l(l+1)\\hbar^2 \\), where \\( l \\) is an integer. The stationary states of the system are described by <strong>Spherical Harmonics</strong> (\\( Y_{lm} \\)), which dictate the 3D shapes of electron probability clouds in atoms, providing the physical basis for chemical bonding.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Relativistic <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> Tensor</h3>\n                    <p>In <strong>Special Relativity</strong>, orbital angular momentum is not a 3-vector but part of antisymmetric rank-2 <strong>Angular Momentum Tensor</strong> (\\( M^{\\mu\\nu} = x^\\mu p^\\nu - x^\\nu p^\\mu \\)). The spatial components of this tensor correspond to the classical \\( \\mathbf{L} \\), while the mixed time-space components describe the motion of the center of mass. This formulation ensures that angular momentum conservation is a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/manifestly-covariant\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Manifestly Covariant</strong></a> law, consistent across all Lorentz frames.</p>','Orbital Angular Momentum is the vector quantity that describes the rotational motion of a particle or system of particles about a specific origin. In university-level mechanics, it is defined as the cross product of the position vector and the linear momentum:. This quantity represents the Generator of Rotations through space and is the key to understanding central-force motion, including planetary orbits and atomic electronic states.','Orbital Angular Momentum (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.566ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 692 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40B\" d=\"M643 285Q641 280 629 148T612 4V0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 228 683Q415 685 425 686H439V624H304V62H352H378Q492 62 539 138Q551 156 558 178T569 214T576 255T581 289H643V285Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColo