)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5622,0)\"><use data-c=\"7B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6122,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6672,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7116.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D43B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8004.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"7D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(8782.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(9838.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"phase-stability-cab852e5\",\"noether-charge-2bb016dd\",\"lagrangian-rule-e96cc5a1\",\"hidden-invariant-54cd8ee9\"]'),
(2010552,'keplers-second-law','angular-momentum','Kepler\'s Second Law','<p>Kepler\'s Second Law, also known as the <strong>Law of Equal Areas</strong>, states that a line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time. In university-level astrophysics, this law is recognized as the direct macroscopic manifestation of the <strong>Conservation of Angular Momentum</strong>. It proves that the dynamics of planetary motion are governed by a <strong>Central Force</strong>, where the \"rotational speed\" of a body must increase as its distance from the center decreases.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Geometric Postulate</h3>\n                    <p>Johannes Kepler deduced this law from Tycho Brahe\'s observational data of Mars. He realized that planets do not move at a constant speed along their orbits; they move faster at <strong>Perihelion</strong> (closest approach) and slower at <strong>Aphelion</strong> (farthest point). The \"Equal Area\" rule provided the first precise mathematical description of this non-uniform motion, replacing the Greek ideal of uniform circular motion with a more accurate dynamical principle.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Derivation from Angular Momentum</h3>\n                    <p>The mathematical proof the Second Law is a staple of analytical mechanics. The infinitesimal area \\( dA \\) swept out by the radius vector \\( \\mathbf{r} \\) in time \\( dt \\) is given by \\( dA = \\frac{1}{2} |\\mathbf{r} \\times d\\mathbf{r}| \\). Since \\( \\mathbf{v} = d\\mathbf{r}/dt \\), the rate of area sweeping is \\( dA/dt = \\frac{1}{2m} |\\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{p}| \\). Because \\( \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{p} \\) is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{L} \\)), the law reduces to the statement that \\( dA/dt = L / (2m) \\), which is constant if \\( \\mathbf{L} \\) is conserved.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Implications for Central Forces</h3>\n                    <p>A critical insight is that Kepler\'s Second Law is <strong>independent of the inverse-square law</strong>. It holds for <em>any</em> central force, regardless of whether the force follows \\( 1/r^2 \\), \\( 1/r^3 \\), or a harmonic potential. Its validity only requires that the force be directed toward the origin (zero torque). This makes the Second Law a powerful diagnostic tool: if a system obeys the equal-area rule, physicists can immediately conclude that the interaction is purely radial.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Areal Velocity and Orbital Speed</h3>\n                    <p>The quantity \\( dA/dt \\) is known as the <strong>Areal Velocity</strong>. The conservation of areal velocity implies that the transverse velocity \\( v_\\theta \\) is inversely proportional to the radius: \\( v_\\theta = L / (mr) \\). This relationship ensures that total energy is conserved throughout the orbit, as the increase in kinetic energy near the source is exactly balanced by the decrease in potential energy, providing a stable, repeating cycle of motion.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Relativistic Corrections</h3>\n                    <p>In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the equal-area law is slightly modified due to the curvature of spacetime. In the Schwarzschild geometry around a massive star, the \"proper\" area swept out depends on the local metric. While Kepler\'s law remains an excellent approximation for the solar system, the high-speed orbits of stars around <strong>Supermassive Black Holes</strong> exhibit deviations that allow astronomers to test the limits of Einstein\'s theory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Application in Galactic Dynamics</h3>\n                    <p>Kepler\'s Second Law is also used to study the <strong>Rotation Curves</strong> of galaxies. By comparing the observed areal velocities of stars to the predictions based on visible matter, astronomers identified a discrepancy: stars far from the galactic center move much faster than expected. This failure of the \"standard\" Keplerian prediction was the first line of evidence for the existence of <strong>Dark Matter</strong>, proving that the law remains a vital tool for probing the unseen mass of the universe.</p>','Kepler\'s Second Law, also known as the Law of Equal Areas, states that a line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time. In university-level astrophysics, this law is recognized as the direct macroscopic manifestation of the Conservation of Angular Momentum. It proves that the dynamics of planetary motion are governed by a Central Force, where the \"rotational speed\" of a body must increase as its distance from the center decreases.','Kepler\'s Second Law, also known as the Law of Equal Areas, states that a line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time. In university-level astrophysics, this law is recognized as the direct macroscopic manifestation of the Conservation of Angular Momentum. It proves that the dynamics of planetary motion are governed by a Central Force, where the \"rotational speed\" of a body must increase as its distance from the center decreases.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.023ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.873ex\" height=\"1.643ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1270 726\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\" d=\"M366 683Q367 683 438 688T511 694Q523 694 523 686Q523 679 450 384T375 83T374 68Q374 26 402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487H491Q506 153 506 145Q506 140 503 129Q490 79 473 48T445 8T417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157Q33 205 53 255T101 341Q148 398 195 420T280 442Q336 442 364 400Q369 394 369 396Q370 400 396 505T424 616Q424 629 417 632T378 637H357Q351 643 351 645T353 664Q358 683 366 683ZM352 326Q329 405 277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q233 26 290 98L298 109L352 326Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\" d=\"M208 74Q208 50 254 46Q272 46 272 35Q272 34 270 22Q267 8 264 4T251 0Q249 0 239 0T205 1T141 2Q70 2 50 0H42Q35 7 35 11Q37 38 48 46H62Q132 49 164 96Q170 102 345 401T523 704Q530 716 547 716H555H572Q578 707 578 706L606 383Q634 60 636 57Q641 46 701 46Q726 46 726 36Q726 34 723 22Q720 7 718 4T704 0Q701 0 690 0T651 1T578 2Q484 2 455 0H443Q437 6 437 9T439 27Q443 40 445 43L449 46H469Q523 49 533 63L521 213H283L249 155Q208 86 208 74ZM516 260Q516 271 504 416T490 562L463 519Q447 492 400 412L310 260L413 259Q516 259 516 260Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D434\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"equal-areas-92c56b86\",\"variational-proof-58150822\",\"speed-relation-67756662\",\"galactic-scaling-4f6f001b\"]'),
(2010553,'tensor-mapping','angular-momentum','Tensor Mapping (L = I omega)','<p>The <strong>Tensor Mapping</strong> relationship \\( \\mathbf{L} = \\mathbf{I} \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\) defines the fundamental connection between a rigid body\'s rotation and its total angular momentum. In university-level mechanics, this equation represents a <strong>Linear Map</strong> from the vector space of angular velocities (\\( \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\)) to the vector space of angular momenta (\\( \\mathbf{L} \\)). The mediator of this map is the <strong>Inertia Tensor</strong> (\\( \\mathbf{I} \\)), which encodes the body\'s mass distribution and geometric symmetry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Geometry of the Mapping</h3>\n                    <p>In elementary physics, rotation is often treated as a scalar relationship (\\( L = I\\omega \\)). However, for a generic rigid body, the angular momentum vector \\( \\mathbf{L} \\) is not necessarily parallel to the rotation axis \\( \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\). The tensor mapping acts as a <strong>Shear and Scale</strong> transformation: it \"tilts\" the momentum vector toward the directions of greater mass distribution. This misalignment is the physical origin of <strong>Rotational Precession</strong> and the \"wobble\" observed in asymmetrical spinning objects.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Eigenvalues and Principal Axes</h3>\n                    <p>The tensor mapping is most clearly understood through its <strong>Eigenvectors</strong>, which are the <strong>Principal Axes</strong> of the body. For rotation about these specific axes, the tensor mapping simplifies to a scalar multiplication: \\( \\mathbf{I} \\boldsymbol{\\omega}_k = I_k \\boldsymbol{\\omega}_k \\). In this case, and only in this case, \\( \\mathbf{L} \\) and \\( \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\) are parallel. Finding these axes is an <strong>Eigenvalue Problem</strong> that reveals the natural \"symmetry coordinates\" of the dynamical system.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Energy and the Quadratic Form</h3>\n                    <p>The tensor mapping also determines the <strong>Rotational Kinetic Energy</strong> (\\( T \\)). Using the dot product of the two vectors, we find \\( T = \\frac{1}{2} \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\cdot \\mathbf{L} = \\frac{1}{2} \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\cdot \\mathbf{I} \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\). This <strong>Quadratic Form</strong> defines an <strong>Inertia Ellipsoid</strong> in velocity space. The physical motion of a torque-free body can be visualized as the angular velocity vector \"rolling\" on the surface of this ellipsoid while keeping the angular momentum vector fixed in space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Changing Basis and Covariance</h3>\n                    <p>Because \\( \\mathbf{I} \\) is a rank-2 tensor, the mapping \\( \\mathbf{L} = \\mathbf{I} \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\) is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/manifestly-covariant\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Manifestly Covariant</strong></a>. When the coordinate system is rotated, the components of \\( \\mathbf{L} \\), \\( \\mathbf{I} \\), and \\( \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\) all change according to specific tensor transformation rules such that the physical relationship remains identical. This allows physicists to solve dynamics in the <strong>Body Frame</strong> (where \\( \\mathbf{I} \\) is constant) and then transform the results back to the <strong>Laboratory Frame</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/time-evolution\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Time Evolution</strong></a>: Euler\'s Equations</h3>\n                    <p>By taking the time derivative of the tensor mapping in the rotating body frame, we derive <strong>Euler\'s Equations</strong>. Because the inertia tensor is fixed relative to the body, the rate of change of angular momentum is purely due to the \"turning\" of the velocity vector: \\( d\\mathbf{L}/dt = \\mathbf{I} \\dot{\\boldsymbol{\\omega}} + \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\times (\\mathbf{I} \\boldsymbol{\\omega}) \\). This explains why a body can accelerate rotationally even in the absence of external torque, as seen in the <strong>Dzhanibekov Effect</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Quantum Extension: The Rigid Rotor</h3>\n                    <p>In quantum mechanics, the tensor mapping is used to define the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> for a rotating molecule (the Rigid Rotor). The energy levels are determined by the eigenvalues of the angular momentum operator acting through the inverse inertia tensor: \\( \\hat{H} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum \\hat{L}_i I_i^{-1} \\hat{L}_i \\). This mapping dictates the discrete <strong>Rotational Spectrum</strong> of molecules, which is used in <strong>Radio Astronomy</strong> to identify the chemical composition of interstellar gas clouds.</p>','The Tensor Mapping relationship defines the fundamental connection between a rigid body\'s rotation and its total angular momentum. In university-level mechanics, this equation represents a Linear Map from the vector space of angular velocities to the vector space of angular momenta. The mediator of this map is the Inertia Tensor, which encodes the body\'s mass distribution and geometric symmetry.','The Tensor Mapping relationship <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.194ex\" height=\"1.738ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 3179.6 768\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40B\" d=\"M643 285Q641 280 629 148T612 4V0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 228 683Q415 685 425 686H439V624H304V62H352H378Q492 62 539 138Q551 156 558 178T569 214T576 255T581 289H643V285Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"