21 238 21Q293 21 321 74Q338 107 338 175V195Q338 290 274 322Q259 328 213 329L171 330L168 332Q166 335 166 348Q166 366 174 366Q202 366 232 371Q266 376 294 413T322 525V533Q322 590 287 612Q265 626 240 626Q208 626 181 615T143 592T132 580H135Q138 579 143 578T153 573T165 566T175 555T183 540T186 520Q186 498 172 481T127 463Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(824,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1754.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2810,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-34\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3310,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3810,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"force-bridges-fd3919a6\",\"proton-killers-96a22c94\",\"primary-matter-880dc441\",\"unification-proof-5760cf1c\"]'),
(2010590,'baryon-number-gut','grand-unification','Baryon Number (B)','<p><strong>Baryon Number (\\(B\\))</strong> is a conserved additive quantum number in the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, representing the number of <strong>Quarks</strong> minus antiquarks in a system (divided by three). In university-level physics, while \\( B \\) is a symmetry of the Standard Model at the classical level, <strong>Grand Unified Theories</strong> predict that it is violated at high energies, providing the 4D mechanism for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/proton-decay\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Proton Decay</strong></a> and the origin of matter in the <strong>Early Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Defining the Baryon Count</h3>\n                    <p>Every quark is assigned a baryon number of \\( +1/3 \\), and every anti-quark \\( -1/3 \\). Protons and neutrons, being triplets of quarks, have \\( B = 1 \\). This <strong>Ontological</strong> assignment ensures that in most nuclear reactions (like <strong>Astrophysical Nuclear Physics</strong>), the total number of protons and neutrons remains stable, dictating the  of atoms.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. B-Violation in GUTs</h3>\n                    <p>The primary prediction of GUTs is that baryon number is not a fundamental 4D symmetry. Because <strong>X and Y Bosons</strong> couple to both quarks and leptons, they can catalyze reactions like \\( q + q \\to \\bar{q} + \\bar{l} \\). This results in \\( \\Delta B = -1 \\), turning matter into <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/antimatter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Antimatter</strong></a> leptons and mesons. This 4D \"Leaking\" of matter is what leads to the eventual death of stars through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/proton-decay\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Proton Decay</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The B-L Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>A university-level insight is that while \\( B \\) and \\( L \\) (Lepton number) are individually violated in GUTs, the combination <strong>B-L</strong> is often preserved. In the <strong>SO(10) Model</strong>, B-L is a local <strong>Gauge Symmetry</strong>. This ensures that for every baryon that vanishes, a lepton must be created, maintaining a 4D balance in the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> that prevents the universe from losing all its subatomic information.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Sphalerons and SM Violation</h3>\n                    <p>Even within the <strong>Standard Model</strong>, baryon number is violated at extremely high temperatures by non-perturbative 4D effects called <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/sphalerons\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Sphalerons</strong></a>. These field configurations \"Tunnel\" between different 4D vacuum states, converting baryon number into lepton number. This process was active in the <strong>Early Universe</strong> primordial plasma, linking the subatomic scale to the <strong>History of the Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Sakharov\'s B-Violation Requirement</h3>\n                    <p>Baryon number violation is the first of the <strong>Sakharov Conditions</strong> for <strong>Baryogenesis</strong>. To explain the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/matter-antimatter-asymmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Matter-Antimatter Asymmetry</strong></a>, the 4D laws of nature <em>must</em> distinguish between \"Matter\" and \"Nothing.\" If \\( B \\) were perfectly conserved, the equal amounts of matter and antimatter created in the <strong>Big Bang</strong> would have totally annihilated, leaving an empty 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Impermanent Law</h3>\n                    <p>Baryon number conservation is an <strong>Epistemic</strong> law—it holds true at our low-energy scales but fails at the <strong>Unification Energy</strong>. It teaches us that the \"Solidarity\" of matter is a 4D illusion of the <strong>Expansion History</strong>. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> must accept that the fundamental constituents of our bodies are 4D resonances that will one day return to the vacuum through the slow decay of the \\( B \\) symmetry.</p>','Baryon Number is a conserved additive quantum number in the Standard Model, representing the number of Quarks minus antiquarks in a system (divided by three). In university-level physics, while is a symmetry of the Standard Model at the classical level, Grand Unified Theories predict that it is violated at high energies, providing the 4D mechanism for Proton Decay and the origin of matter in the Early Universe. Every quark is assigned a baryon number of, and every anti-quark.','Baryon Number (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.717ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 759 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\" d=\"M231 637Q204 637 199 638T194 649Q194 676 205 682Q206 683 335 683Q594 683 608 681Q671 671 713 636T756 544Q756 480 698 429T565 360L555 357Q619 348 660 311T702 219Q702 146 630 78T453 1Q446 0 242 0Q42 0 39 2Q35 5 35 10Q35 17 37 24Q42 43 47 45Q51 46 62 46H68Q95 46 128 49Q142 52 147 61Q150 65 219 339T288 628Q288 635 231 637ZM649 544Q649 574 634 600T585 634Q578 636 493 637Q473 637 451 637T416 636H403Q388 635 384 626Q382 622 352 506Q352 503 351 500L320 374H401Q482 374 494 376Q554 386 601 434T649 544ZM595 229Q595 273 572 302T512 336Q506 337 429 337Q311 337 310 336Q310 334 293 263T258 122L240 52Q240 48 252 48T333 46Q422 46 429 47Q491 54 543 105T595 229Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) is a conserved additive quantum number in the Standard Model, representing the number of Quarks minus antiquarks in a system (divided by three). In university-level physics, while <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.717ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 759 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\" d=\"M231 637Q204 637 199 638T194 649Q194 676 205 682Q206 683 335 683Q594 683 608 681Q671 671 713 636T756 544Q756 480 698 429T565 360L555 357Q619 348 660 311T702 219Q702 146 630 78T453 1Q446 0 242 0Q42 0 39 2Q35 5 35 10Q35 17 37 24Q42 43 47 45Q51 46 62 46H68Q95 46 128 49Q142 52 147 61Q150 65 219 339T288 628Q288 635 231 637ZM649 544Q649 574 634 600T585 634Q578 636 493 637Q473 637 451 637T416 636H403Q388 635 384 626Q382 622 352 506Q352 503 351 500L320 374H401Q482 374 494 376Q554 386 601 434T649 544ZM595 229Q595 273 572 302T512 336Q506 337 429 337Q311 337 310 336Q310 334 293 263T258 122L240 52Q240 48 252 48T333 46Q422 46 429 47Q491 54 543 105T595 229Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg> is a symmetry of the Standard Model at the classical level, Grand Unified Theories predict that it is violated at high energies, providing the 4D mechanism for Proton Decay and the origin of matter in the Early Universe. Every quark is assigned a baryon number of <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.154ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 2278 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\" d=\"M56 237T56 250T70 270H369V420L370 570Q380 583 389 583Q402 583 409 568V270H707Q722 262 722 250T707 230H409V-68Q401 -82 391 -82H389H387Q375 -82 369 -68V230H70Q56