 2T48 1Q31 1 31 10Q31 12 34 24Q39 43 44 45Q48 46 59 46H65Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q147 65 216 339T285 628Q285 635 228 637Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\" d=\"M112 560L249 694L257 686Q387 562 387 560L361 531Q359 532 303 581L250 627L195 580Q182 569 169 557T148 538L140 532Q138 530 125 546L112 560Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1167,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1750.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2750.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3270.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3631.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4298.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5354,0)\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6132,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6521,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6882,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7493.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(8493.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(328.5,676)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><use data-c=\"210F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-V-210F\"></use></g><rect width=\"762\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(9495.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(513.8,257) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"5E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(10383.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3A8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3A8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11161.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(11550.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11911.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(12300.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(12820.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"wave-logic-9db34d39\",\"linear-fate-6c9d4bec\",\"unitarity-lock-f083b391\",\"action-link-24c18559\"]'),
(2010617,'energy-eigenvalues','hamiltonian-operator','Energy Eigenvalues','<p>Energy Eigenvalues (\\( E_n \\)) are the discrete, allowed energy values that a quantum system can possess. In university-level physics, they are the scalars obtained by solving the <strong>Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation</strong> eigenvalue problem: \\( \\hat{H}\\psi = E\\psi \\). They represent the \"Tuned Frequencies\" of the , defining the <strong>Ontological</strong> structure of all matter in the <strong>Standard Model</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Quantization and the Bound State</h3>\n                    <p>The most profound feature of energy eigenvalues is their <strong>Quantization</strong>. For a particle trapped in a potential well, the eigenvalues form a discrete \"Ladder\" of values (\\( E_1, E_2, \\dots \\)). This a 4D geometric requirement: the <strong>Wave Function</strong> must vanish at infinity, which only happens for specific energy values. This \"Ladder of Being\" is what allows for stable atoms and the chemical <strong>Symmetries</strong> of the periodic table.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Ground State and Zero-Point Energy</h3>\n                    <p>The lowest energy eigenvalue is the <strong>Ground State</strong> (\\( E_0 \\)). According to the <strong>Uncertainty Principle</strong>, the ground state energy is never zero; a particle must always have some \"Zero-Point Energy\" due to its 4D confinement. This 4D <strong>Geometric Origin</strong> for vacuum energy is what prevents matter from reaching absolute rest, dictating the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong> at low temperatures.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Continuous vs. Discrete Spectrums</h3>\n                    <p>A university-level distinction must be made between bound and unbound states.\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Bound States:</strong> Discrete eigenvalues (like an electron in an atom).</li>\n                            <li><strong>Scattering States:</strong> A continuous range of eigenvalues (like a free electron).</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    The transition between these two—the \"Ionization Threshold\"—is a major landmark in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/potential-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Potential Theory</strong></a>, determining how light interacts with matter.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Degeneracy and Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>If multiple different wave functions (\\( \\psi_a, \\psi_b \\)) share the same energy eigenvalue \\( E \\), the system is said to be <strong>Degenerate</strong>. This occurs whenever the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> possesses high spatial <strong>Symmetries</strong> (like spherical symmetry). Breaking these symmetries (e.g., via an external <strong>Magnetic Field</strong>) \"Splits\" the eigenvalues, a phenomenon known as the <strong>Zeeman Effect</strong>, providing 4D proof for subatomic structure.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Eigenvalues in the Early Universe</h3>\n                    <p>During the <strong>Early Universe</strong>, the energy eigenvalues of fundamental fields dictated the production of particles. As the temperature fell below specific eigenvalues, the  \"Froze out\" certain species, setting the <strong>Cosmic Budget</strong> and the <strong>History of the Universe</strong> atomic ratios, from Hydrogen to Helium.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Epistemic Status: The Measurable Real</h3>\n                    <p>Energy eigenvalues are the only \"Real\" energies in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a>. When we look at the universe through a spectroscope, we are looking at the <em>differences</em> between energy eigenvalues (\\( \\Delta E = hf \\)). They are the <strong>Epistemic</strong> signature of the , proving that the subatomic world is not a chaotic mess but a highly tuned musical instrument governed by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a>.</p>','Energy Eigenvalues are the discrete, allowed energy values that a quantum system can possess. In university-level physics, they are the scalars obtained by solving the Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation eigenvalue problem:. They represent the \"Tuned Frequencies\" of the, defining the Ontological structure of all matter in the Standard Model.','Energy Eigenvalues (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.817ex\" height=\"1.895ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 1245.3 837.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H757Q764 676 764 669Q764 664 751 557T737 447Q735 440 717 440H705Q698 445 698 453L701 476Q704 500 704 528Q704 558 697 578T678 609T643 625T596 632T532 634H485Q397 633 392 631Q388 629 386 622Q385 619 355 499T324 377Q347 376 372 376H398Q464 376 489 391T534 472Q538 488 540 490T557 493Q562 493 565 493T570 492T572 491T574 487T577 483L544 351Q511 218 508 216Q505 213 492 213Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45B\" d=\"M21 287Q22 293 24 303T36 341T56 388T89 425T135 442Q171 442 195 424T225 390T231 369Q231 367 232 367L243 378Q304 442 382 442Q436 442 469 415T503 336T465 179T427 52Q427 26 444 26Q450 26 453 27Q482 32 505 65T540 