4T161 441Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\" d=\"M139 -249H137Q125 -249 119 -235V251L120 737Q130 750 139 750Q152 750 159 735V-235Q151 -249 141 -249H139Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\" d=\"M55 732Q56 739 61 744T75 750Q85 750 92 740Q95 733 186 494T278 250T187 6T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q67 -250 62 -245T55 -232Q55 -227 145 11Q236 248 236 250T145 489Q55 727 55 732Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-30\" d=\"M96 585Q152 666 249 666Q297 666 345 640T423 548Q460 465 460 320Q460 165 417 83Q397 41 362 16T301 -15T250 -22Q224 -22 198 -16T137 16T82 83Q39 165 39 320Q39 494 96 585ZM321 597Q291 629 250 629Q208 629 178 597Q153 571 145 525T137 333Q137 175 145 125T181 46Q209 16 250 16Q290 16 318 46Q347 76 354 130T362 333Q362 478 354 524T321 597Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(347.6,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"823\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1063,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E8\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E8\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1452,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2103,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2492,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2853,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3242,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"7C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-7C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3520,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4171,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4560,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4921,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5310,0)\"><use data-c=\"27E9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-27E9\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5976.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(7032.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"total-100-46c9477d\",\"the-u-operator-398c3925\",\"memory-link-cac7e699\",\"collapse-rule-56b2e657\"]'),
(2010636,'phase-space-flow','phase-space','Phase Space Flow and Liouville\'s Theorem','<p>In <strong>Hamiltonian Mechanics</strong>, the evolution of a system is visualized as a <strong>flow</strong> within the 2n-dimensional <strong>Phase Space</strong>. This flow represents the motion of a cloud of possible system states over time. In university-level physics, understanding the properties of this flow is essential for <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong> and the study of chaotic systems in the .</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> Vector Field</h3>\n                  <p>At any point in phase space, <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltons-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamilton\'s Canonical Equations</strong></a> define a unique vector \\( \\mathbf{v} = (\\dot{q}, \\dot{p}) \\). The collection of these vectors forms a field that \"Pushes\" states along their trajectories. This flow is <strong>Incompressible</strong>, meaning that as the states move, they may change shape, but the total volume they occupy remains constant—a property known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/liouvilles-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Liouville\'s Theorem</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Conservation of Density</h3>\n                  <p>Liouville\'s Theorem states that the phase-space density \\( \\rho(q, p, t) \\) is constant along the trajectories of the flow:\n                  \\[ \\frac{d\\rho}{dt} = \\frac{\\partial \\rho}{\\partial t} + \\{\\rho, H\\} = 0 \\]\n                  This implies that information in <strong>Classical Mechanics</strong> is never created or destroyed. In the 4D <strong>Block Universe</strong>, the distribution of possible histories is a rigid geometric structure that simply \"slides\" through the manifold according to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> law.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Ergodicity and Chaos</h3>\n                  <p>The nature of the flow determines whether a system is predictable or chaotic. In an <strong>Integrable System</strong>, the flow is confined to \"Tori,\" leading to stable, periodic motion. In a <strong>Chaotic System</strong>, the flow is \"Ergodic,\" eventually visiting every part of the available phase space. This transition is the key to understanding how macroscopic <strong>Thermodynamics</strong> emerges from the deterministic 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of individual particles.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Poincare Recurrence</h3>\n                  <p>A profound consequence of the incompressible flow is the <strong>Poincare Recurrence Theorem</strong>: in a bounded system, the flow will eventually return arbitrarily close to its initial state. This suggests that in a finite 4D <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spacetime-causal-topology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Causal Topology</strong></a>, even the most complex states (like a broken glass) will eventually \"un-break,\" though the timescale for this return is often longer than the <strong>History of the Universe</strong>.</p>','In Hamiltonian Mechanics, the evolution of a system is visualized as a flow within the 2n-dimensional Phase Space. This flow represents the motion of a cloud of possible system states over time. In university-level physics, understanding the properties of this flow is essential for Statistical Mechanics and the study of chaotic systems in the.','In Hamiltonian Mechanics, the evolution of a system is visualized as a flow within the 2n-dimensional Phase Space. This flow represents the motion of a cloud of possible system states over time. In university-level physics, understanding the properties of this flow is essential for Statistical Mechanics and the study of chaotic systems in the .','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; verti