9Q23 284 23 287ZM178 102Q200 26 252 26Q282 26 310 49T356 107Q374 141 392 215T411 325V331Q411 405 350 405Q339 405 328 402T306 393T286 380T269 365T254 350T243 336T235 326L232 322Q232 321 229 308T218 264T204 212Q178 106 178 102Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D439\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(749,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1138,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1598,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2042.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2545.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"infinitesimal-shift-law-8b3350dd\",\"conserved-generator-dc888ae2\",\"technical-relation-c2454e85\"]'),
(2010641,'hamiltonian-vector-field','phase-space','The Hamiltonian Vector Field (\\( X_H \\))','<p>A <strong>Hamiltonian Vector Field</strong> is the mathematical object that assigns a specific velocity vector to every point in <strong>Phase Space</strong>. In university-level theoretical physics, the vector field \\( X_H \\) is the geometric manifestation of <strong>Hamilton\'s Equations</strong>, defining the \"currents\" through which the state of a system must flow.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Geometric Construction</h3>\n                    <p>Given a <strong>Symplectic Manifold</strong> (\\( M, \\omega \\)) and a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> function \\( H \\), the vector field \\( X_H \\) is uniquely defined by the condition:\n                    \\[ \\omega(X_H, Y) = dH(Y) \\]\n                    for any other vector field \\( Y \\). This means the vector field is always \"orthogonal\" to the gradient of the energy with respect to the symplectic structure. Consequently, the flow of \\( X_H \\) is always tangent to surfaces of constant energy (isoelectronic surfaces).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Divergence-Free Property</h3>\n                    <p>Hamiltonian vector fields are naturally <strong>Divergence-Free</strong> with respect to the <strong>Liouville Measure</strong>. this is geometric origin of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/liouvilles-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Liouville\'s Theorem</strong></a>. Because the divergence of \\( X_H \\) vanishes, the \"phase fluid\" is incompressible; a volume of states may change its shape (stretching or shearing), but its total magnitude remains constant throughout the evolution.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Commutation and Brackets</h3>\n                    <p>The relationship between different physical generators is expressed through the <strong>Lie Bracket</strong> of their associated vector fields. The bracket of two <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> vector fields is itself a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> vector field, corresponding to the <strong>Poisson Bracket</strong> of their generators: \\( [X_F, X_G] = -X_{\\{F, G\\}} \\). This demonstrates the deep isomorphism between the geometry of the manifold and the algebra of the physical observables.</p>','A Hamiltonian Vector Field is the mathematical object that assigns a specific velocity vector to every point in Phase Space. In university-level theoretical physics, the vector field is the geometric manifestation of Hamilton\'s Equations, defining the \"currents\" through which the state of a system must flow. Given a Symplectic Manifold and a Hamiltonian function, the vector field is uniquely defined by the condition: for any other vector field.','A Hamiltonian Vector Field is the mathematical object that assigns a specific velocity vector to every point in Phase Space. In university-level theoretical physics, the vector field <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.482ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1538.9 833\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44B\" d=\"M42 0H40Q26 0 26 11Q26 15 29 27Q33 41 36 43T55 46Q141 49 190 98Q200 108 306 224T411 342Q302 620 297 625Q288 636 234 637H206Q200 643 200 645T202 664Q206 677 212 683H226Q260 681 347 681Q380 681 408 681T453 682T473 682Q490 682 490 671Q490 670 488 658Q484 643 481 640T465 637Q434 634 411 620L488 426L541 485Q646 598 646 610Q646 628 622 635Q617 635 609 637Q594 637 594 648Q594 650 596 664Q600 677 606 683H618Q619 683 643 683T697 681T738 680Q828 680 837 683H845Q852 676 852 672Q850 647 840 637H824Q790 636 763 628T722 611T698 593L687 584Q687 585 592 480L505 384Q505 383 536 304T601 142T638 56Q648 47 699 46Q734