11Q297 -11 267 66Q266 68 260 61Q201 -11 125 -11Q15 -11 15 139Q15 230 56 325T123 434Q135 441 147 436Q160 429 160 418Q160 406 140 379T94 306T62 208Q61 202 61 187Q61 124 85 100T143 76Q201 76 245 129L253 137V156Q258 297 317 297Q348 297 348 261Q348 243 338 213T318 158L308 135Q309 133 310 129T318 115T334 97T358 83T393 76Q456 76 501 148T546 274Q546 305 533 325T508 357T495 384Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(629,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2217\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2217\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1032.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D714\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D714\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1932.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2988.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D714\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D714\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"geometric-consistency-394c42b4\",\"dynamical-symmetry-1a3d6b19\",\"technical-relation-fd65e33c\"]'),
(2010644,'old-quantum-theory','phase-space','The Old Quantum Theory and Action Quantization','<p>The <strong>Old Quantum Theory</strong> (1900–1925) represents the bridge between classical <strong>Hamiltonian Mechanics</strong> and modern wave mechanics. It introduced the radical idea that <strong>Phase Space</strong> is not continuous but is composed of discrete \"cells.\" In university-level history of physics, this theory—centered on the <strong>Bohr-Sommerfeld Quantization Rules</strong>—provided the first geometric interpretation of quantum numbers.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Bohr-Sommerfeld Rule</h3>\n                    <p>The central postulate of the Old Quantum Theory is that for any periodic system, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/action-integral\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Action Integral</strong></a> over one complete cycle in phase space must be an integer multiple of <strong>Planck\'s Constant</strong> (\\( h \\)):\n                    \\[ \\oint p \\, dq = n h \\]\n                    This rule restricts the allowed <strong>Orbits</strong> of a system (such as an electron in an atom) to a discrete set of paths, explaining why atoms are stable and why they emit light at specific 4D <strong>Spectral Frequencies</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Action-Angle Variables</h3>\n                    <p>The most sophisticated version of theory used <strong>Action-Angle Variables</strong> (\\( J, \\theta \\)). In this representation, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> depends only on the action \\( J \\), making the equations of motion trivial. The quantization rule simply becomes \\( J = n\\hbar \\). This demonstrated that 4D <strong>Quantum Numbers</strong> are actually <strong>Topological Invariants</strong> of the classical motion, providing a deep geometric link between the two regimes.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Failure and Transition to Wave Mechanics</h3>\n                    <p>While the Old Quantum Theory successfully explained the <strong>Hydrogen Atom</strong> and the <strong>Zeeman Effect</strong>, it failed for multi-electron systems (like Helium) and could not account for <strong>Superposition</strong> or <strong>Tunneling</strong>. It viewed the \"Cell\" of phase space as a hard boundary rather than a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/probability-amplitude\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Probability Amplitude</strong></a> distribution. This eventually led to the development of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a> and <strong>Phase Space Quantization</strong> using the <strong>Wigner Function</strong>.</p>','The Old Quantum Theory (1900–1925) represents the bridge between classical Hamiltonian Mechanics and modern wave mechanics. It introduced the radical idea that Phase Space is not continuous but is composed of discrete \"cells.\" In university-level history of physics, this theory—centered on the Bohr-Sommerfeld Quantization Rules—provided the first geometric interpretation of quantum numbers. The central postulate of the Old Quantum Theory is that for any periodic system, the Action Integral over one complete cycle in phase space must be an integer multiple of Planck\'s Constant : This rule restricts the allowed Orbits of a system (such as an electron in an atom) to a discrete set of paths, explaining why atoms are stable and why they emit light at specific 4D Spectral Frequencies.','The Old Quantum Theory (1900–1925) represents the bridge between classical Hamiltonian Mechanics and modern wave mechanics. It introduced the radical idea that Phase Space is not continuous but is composed of discrete \"cells.\" In university-level history of physics, this theory—centered on the Bohr-Sommerfeld Quantization Rules—provided the first geometric interpretation of quantum numbers. The central postulate of the Old Quantum Theory is tha