(2010646,'wigner-function','phase-space','The Wigner Quasi-Probability Distribution','<p>The <strong>Wigner Function</strong> (\\( W(q, p) \\)) is the primary tool for representing quantum states in <strong>Phase Space</strong>. Introduced by Eugene Wigner in 1932, it provides a bridge between the wave function \\( \\psi(q) \\) and the classical density \\( \\rho(q, p) \\). In university-level quantum optics and foundations, the Wigner function is used to visualize <strong>Superposition</strong>, <strong>Entanglement</strong>, and the transition to classicality.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Definition and Marginals</h3>\n                    <p>The Wigner function is a 4D Fourier transform of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Density Matrix</strong></a>. While it looks like a probability distribution, it is called a <strong>Quasi-Probability Distribution</strong> because it can take negative values. However, its <strong>Marginals</strong> are strictly positive: integrating \\( W(q, p) \\) over momentum gives the spatial probability density \\( |\\psi(q)|^2 \\), and integrating over position gives the momentum density \\( |\\phi(p)|^2 \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Negativity as a Signature of Quantumness</h3>\n                    <p>The most striking feature of the Wigner function is its <strong>Negativity</strong>. Regions where \\( W(q, p) \\lt 0 \\) represent areas of phase space that have no classical analog. These \"Negative Pockets\" are direct evidence of <strong>Quantum Interference</strong>. For example, the Wigner function of a <strong>Schrödinger Cat State</strong> (a superposition of two distinct locations) shows oscillatory negative regions between the two classical peaks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Evolution and the Quantum Liouville Equation</h3>\n                    <p>The time evolution of the Wigner function is governed by the <strong>Quantum Liouville Equation</strong> (or Moyal Equation). For a harmonic oscillator, the quantum evolution is identical to the classical 4D <strong>Phase Flow</strong>. However, for non-linear potentials, the quantum flow develops 4D <strong>Stretching and Squeezing</strong> that classical orbits do not possess, providing the geometric interpretation of 4D <strong>Quantum Chaos</strong> and decoherence.</p>','The Wigner Function is the primary tool for representing quantum states in Phase Space. Introduced by Eugene Wigner in 1932, it provides a bridge between the wave function and the classical density. In university-level quantum optics and foundations, the Wigner function is used to visualize Superposition, Entanglement, and the transition to classicality.','The Wigner Function (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.316ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3233.7 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44A\" d=\"M436 683Q450 683 486 682T553 680Q604 680 638 681T677 682Q695 682 695 674Q695 670 692 659Q687 641 683 639T661 637Q636 636 621 632T600 624T597 615Q597 603 613 377T629 138L631 141Q633 144 637 151T649 170T666 200T690 241T720 295T759 362Q863 546 877 572T892 604Q892 619 873 628T831 637Q817 637 817 647Q817 650 819 660Q823 676 825 679T839 682Q842 682 856 682T895 682T949 681Q1015 681 1034 683Q1048 683 1048 672Q1048 666 1045 655T1038 640T1028 637Q1006 637 988 631T958 617T939 600T927 584L923 578L754 282Q586 -14 585 -15Q579 -22 561 -22Q546 -22 542 -17Q539 -14 523 229T506 480L494 462Q472 425 366 239Q222 -13 220 -15T215 -19Q210 -22 197 -22Q178 -22 176 -15Q176 -12 154 304T131 622Q129 631 121 633T82 637H58Q51 644 51 648Q52 671 64 683H76Q118 680 176 680Q301 680 313 683H323Q329 677 329 674T327 656Q322 641 318 637H297Q236 634 232 620Q262 160 266 136L501 550L499 587Q496 629 489 632Q483 636 447 637Q428 637 422 639T416 648Q416 650 418 660Q419 664 420 669T421 676T424 680T428 682T436 6