 613 598 614H500L499 610Q499 598 467 486T428 367Q428 365 551 365H674Q683 360 684 355Q687 346 677 329Q666 312 642 299T598 285Q586 285 582 296H402L394 277Q386 258 373 229T346 167T315 102T286 51Q265 22 225 -5T133 -32Q108 -32 87 -25T54 -7T33 15T21 35T18 47Q18 60 44 80T98 103Q108 103 111 101T119 88Q130 66 150 54T179 39T195 37Q199 37 203 43Q217 67 245 125T318 300T391 532Q393 543 398 564T406 598T409 613T339 614H269Q229 579 199 579Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-C-46\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"total-state-space-2d2fcc74\",\"state-uniqueness-8ddbb05c\",\"completeness-relation-6a477cae\"]'),
(2010668,'classical-field-equations','quantum-field-theory','Classical Field Equations: The Quantum Limit','<p>In the framework of QFT, the <strong>Classical Field Equations</strong> (like Klein-Gordon, Dirac, and Maxwell) emerge as the equations of motion for the field operators. They represent the \"classical limit\" where the quantum fluctuations are averaged out or neglected.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/klein-gordon-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Klein-Gordon Equation</strong></a> (Spin-0)</h3>\n                    <p>The <strong>Klein-Gordon Equation</strong> is the simplest relativistic field equation, describing massive, spinless particles like the Higgs boson. It is a second-order wave equation that ensures the field follows the relativistic energy-momentum relation \\( E^2 = p^2 + m^2 \\). In QFT, it governs the propagation of scalar field operators in the vacuum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/dirac-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Dirac Equation</strong></a> (Spin-1/2)</h3>\n                    <p>To describe fermions like electrons and quarks, we use the <strong>Dirac Equation</strong>. This a first-order differential equation that utilizes <strong>Dirac Matrices</strong> (\\( \\gamma^\\mu \\)) and 4-component <strong>Spinors</strong>. It naturally predicts the existence of spin and antiparticles, and its successful quantization was the first major triumph of relativistic QFT.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maxwells-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maxwell\'s Equations</strong></a> (Spin-1)</h3>\n                    <p>Classical electromagnetism is a <strong>Vector Field Theory</strong>. Maxwell\'s equations can be written in a compact relativistic form using the <strong>Field Strength Tensor</strong> \\( F^{\\mu\\nu} \\). When quantized, these equations describe the behavior of the photon, the massless gauge boson that mediates the electromagnetic force.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Proca and Yang-Mills Equations</h3>\n                    <p>Beyond Maxwell, we have the <strong>Proca Equation</strong> for massive vector bosons (like the \\( W \\) and \\( Z \\)) and the <strong>Yang-Mills Equations</strong> for non-abelian gauge fields (like the gluons in QCD). These equations describe how fields interact with themselves, a key feature of the strong and weak forces that is absent in electromagnetism.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Principle of Minimal Coupling</h3>\n                    <p>To describe how matter fields interact with gauge fields, we use <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/minimal-coupling\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Minimal Coupling</strong></a>, replacing partial derivatives with covariant derivatives. This ensures that the field equations remainvariant under local symmetry transformations, linking the geometry of the field to the physical forces of nature.</p>','In the framework of QFT, the Classical Field Equations (like Klein-Gordon, Dirac, and Maxwell) emerge as the equations of motion for the field operators. They represent the \"classical limit\" where the quantum fluctuations are averaged out or neglected. The Klein-Gordon Equation is the simplest relativistic field equation, describing massive, spinless particles like the Higgs boson.','In the framework of QFT, the Classical Field Equations (like Klein-Gordon, Dirac, and Maxwell) emerge as the equations of motion for the field operators. They represent the \"classical limit\" where the quantum fluctuations are averaged out or neglected. 1.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"13.715ex\" height=\"2.326ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 6062 1027.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q3