(2010670,'generating-functional','quantum-field-theory','Generating Functional and Path Integrals','<p>The <strong>Generating Functional</strong> (\\( Z[J] \\)) is the central object of the path integral formulation of Quantum Field Theory. It serves as a master function from which all correlation functions and physical observables can be derived by functional differentiation with respect to an external source \\( J(x) \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Path Integral Formulation</h3>\n                    <p>In contrast to the operator-based canonical quantization, the path integral approach defines the transition amplitude as a weighted sum over all possible field configurations. The generating functional is defined as the vacuum-to-vacuum transition amplitude in the presence of a source:\n                    \\[ Z[J] = \\int \\mathcal{D}\\phi \\exp\\left( i \\int d^4x [\\mathcal{L}(\\phi, \\partial\\phi) + J(x)\\phi(x)] \\right) \\]\n                    This functional integral over the infinite-dimensional space of fields \\( \\phi(x) \\) captures the full quantum dynamics of the system, including all possible fluctuations.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Correlation Functions from <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/functional\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Functional</strong></a> Derivatives</h3>\n                    <p>The power of the generating functional lies in its ability to generate <strong>n-point Green\'s functions</strong>. By taking functional derivatives with respect to the source \\( J \\) and then setting \\( J = 0 \\), we obtain the time-ordered vacuum expectation values of field operators:\n                    \\[ \\langle 0 | T \\phi(x_1) \\dots \\phi(x_n) | 0 \\rangle = \\left. \\frac{1}{Z[0]} \\frac{\\delta^n Z[J]}{i\\delta J(x_1) \\dots i\\delta J(x_n)} \\right|_{J=0} \\]\n                    This relationship provides a direct bridge between the abstract path integral and experimental observables like scattering cross-sections.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Euclidean Path Integral and Wick Rotation</h3>\n                    <p>To ensure the mathematical convergence of the path integral, we often perform a <strong>Wick Rotation</strong> to imaginary time (\\( t \\to -i\\tau \\)). This transforms the oscillatory Minkowski path integral into a damped Euclidean path integral:\n                    \\[ Z_E[J] = \\int \\mathcal{D}\\phi \\exp\\left( - \\int d^4x_E [\\mathcal{L}_E + J\\phi] \\right) \\]\n                    In this form, the QFT problem becomes mathematically equivalent to a system in <strong>Statistical Mechanics</strong> in four spatial dimensions, where the action plays the role of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> and \\( \\hbar \\) acts like temperature.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Connected Functions and the W[J] Functional</h3>\n                    <p>While \\( Z[J] \\) generates all Green\'s functions, it is often more useful to focus on the <strong>Connected Green\'s Functions</strong>. These are generated by the functional \\( W[J] \\), defined as the logarithm of the generating functional:\n                    \\[ W[J] = -i \\ln Z[J] \\]\n                    The connected functions exclude diagrams that are composed of independent, non-interacting sub-processes, simplifying the analysis of the fundamental interactions of theory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. The Effective Action and Vertex Functions</h3>\n                    <p>By performing a functional <strong>Legendre Transformation</strong> on \\( W[J] \\), we define the <strong>Effective Action</strong> \\( \\Gamma[\\phi_{cl}] \\). This functional is the generator of <strong>One-Particle Irreducible (1PI)</strong> vertex functions. The effective action encapsulates all quantum corrections to the classical equations of motion, leading to the concept of the <strong>Quantum Equations of Motion</strong> and the study of spontaneous symmetry breaking and vacuum stability.</p>','The Generating Functional is the central object of the path integral formulation of Quantum Field Theory. It serves as a master function from which all correlation functions and physical observables can be derived by functional differentiation with respect to an external source. In contrast to the operator-based canonical quantization, the path integral approach defines the transition amplitude as a weighted sum over all possible field configurations.','The Generating Functional (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.326ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1912 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44D\" d=\"M58 8Q58 23 64 35Q64 36 329 334T596 635L586 637Q575 637 512 637H500H476Q442 637 420 635T365 624T311 598T266 548T228 469Q227 466 226 463T224 458T223 453T222 450L221 448Q218 443 202 443Q185 443 182 453L214 561Q228 606 241 651Q249 679 253 681Q256 683 487 683H718Q723 678 723 675Q723 673 717 649Q189 54 188 52L185 49H274Q369 50 377 51Q452 60 500 100T579 247Q587 272 590 277T603 282H607Q628 282 628 271Q547 5 541 2Q538 0 300 0H124Q58 0 58 8Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5B\" d=\"M118 -250V750H255V710H158V-210H255V-250H118Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43D\" d=\"M447 625Q447 637 354 637H329Q323 642 323 645T325 664Q329 677 335 683H352Q393 681 498 681Q541 681 568 681T605 682T619 682Q633 682 633 672Q633 670 630 658Q626 642 623 640T604 637Q552 637 545 623Q541 610 483 376Q420 128 419 127Q397 64 333 21T195 -22Q137 -22 97 8T57 88Q57 130 80 152T132 174Q177 174 182 130Q182 98 164 80T123 56Q115 54 115 53T122 44Q148 15 197 15Q235 15 271 47T324 130Q328 142 387 380T447 625Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5D\" d=\"M22 710V750H159V-250H22V-210H119V710H22Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-