(2010671,'correlation-functions','quantum-field-theory','Correlation Functions and Wick\'s Theorem','<p><strong>Correlation Functions</strong> (or Green\'s functions) represent the fundamental probability amplitudes for a set of particles to propagate between spacetime points. They are the primary link between the mathematical theory of QFT and the physical reality of particle physics experiments.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. n-Point Green\'s Functions</h3>\n                    <p>An n-point correlation function is defined as the vacuum expectation value (VEV) of the time-ordered product of field operators at different spacetime points:\n                    \\[ G^{(n)}(x_1, \\dots, x_n) = \\langle 0 | T\\phi(x_1) \\phi(x_2) \\dots \\phi(x_n) | 0 \\rangle \\]\n                    These functions describe how fields at different locations are correlated. For example, the 2-point function is the <strong>Propagator</strong>, describing the amplitude for a particle to travel from one point to another.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Time-Ordering and the T-Operator</h3>\n                    <p>The <strong>Time-Ordering Operator</strong> (\\( T \\)) is essential in QFT because field operators do not commute at timelike separations. The \\( T \\) operator rearranges the operators such that those with later times appear to the left. This ensures that the resulting correlation functions are causal and consistent with the path integral formulation, where time evolution is naturally ordered.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Wick\'s Theorem</h3>\n                    <p>In free field theories, <strong>Wick\'s Theorem</strong> provides a powerful algebraic method to calculate n-point functions by reducing them to products of 2-point functions. It states that a time-ordered product can be expressed as a sum of <strong>Normal-Ordered</strong> products and all possible <strong>Contractions</strong> (propagators):\n                    \\[ T(\\phi_1 \\dots \\phi_n) = :\\phi_1 \\dots \\phi_n: + \\sum \\text{contractions} \\]\n                    This theorem is the mathematical foundation for the expansion of interaction theories in terms of Feynman diagrams.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Vacuum Expectation Values (VEVs)</h3>\n                    <p>When applying Wick\'s theorem to a vacuum expectation value, all normal-ordered terms vanish (since annihilation operators acting on the vacuum give zero). Therefore, the Green\'s function of a free theory is simply the sum of all possible full contractions between pairs of fields. For a 4-point function, this yields three distinct ways to pair the four spacetime points, representing the different ways particles can propagate.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Interpretation and LSZ Reduction</h3>\n                    <p>Correlation functions are not directly measurable, but they contain the information needed to calculate <strong>S-Matrix</strong> elements through the <strong>LSZ Reduction Formula</strong>. By analyzing the \"poles\" of the Green\'s functions in momentum space, we extract the masses and scattering amplitudes of physical particles, bridging the gap between field fluctuations and particle detections.</p>','Correlation Functions (or Green\'s functions) represent the fundamental probability amplitudes for a set of particles to propagate between spacetime points. They are the primary link between the mathematical theory of QFT and the physical reality of particle physics experiments. 1.','Correlation Functions (or Green\'s functions) represent the fundamental probability amplitudes for a set of particles to propagate between spacetime points. They are the primary link between the mathematical theory of QFT and the physical reality of particle physics experiments. 1.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"45.322ex\" height=\"2.7ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -943.3 20032.5 1193.3\" xmlns:xlink=\