(2010674,'feynman-diagrams','quantum-field-theory','Feynman Diagrams and Rules','<p><strong>Feynman Diagrams</strong> are a revolutionary graphical language used to organize and calculate complex mathematical expressions in Quantum Field Theory. They represent the paths and interactions of particles as they scatter through spacetime, providing a direct mapping between physical processes and the terms of the <strong>Dyson Series</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Graphical Language of QFT</h3>\n                    <p>In a Feynman diagram, different components of theory are represented by specific geometric elements:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Internal Lines:</strong> Represent <strong>Propagators</strong> (the virtual particles that mediate forces).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Vertices:</strong> Represent <strong>Interactions</strong> where lines meet (the coupling strength).</li>\n                        <li><strong>External Lines:</strong> Represent the real incoming and outgoing particles that are detected in an experiment.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    The topology of the diagram tells us how many interactions occurred and how many virtual particles were exchanged.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Momentum Space Feynman Rules</h3>\n                    <p>To calculate a scattering amplitude, we apply the <strong>Feynman Rules</strong> in momentum space:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Each line contributes a factor of the propagator (e.g., \\( i/(p^2 - m^2) \\)).</li>\n                        <li>Each vertex contributes a coupling factor (e.g., \\( -i\\lambda \\) or \\( ie\\gamma^\\mu \\)).</li>\n                        <li>Energy and momentum are conserved at every vertex.</li>\n                        <li>Integrate over all internal \"loop\" momenta that are not fixed by conservation laws.</li>\n                    </ul></p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Tree-Level vs. Loop Diagrams</h3>\n                    <p>Diagrams are classified by their <strong>Loop Order</strong>. <strong>Tree-level</strong> diagrams (no loops) represent the classical limit of theory. <strong>Loop diagrams</strong> represent the quantum corrections, where a particle can \"emit and reabsorb\" itself. The number of loops corresponds to the power of \\( \\hbar \\), showing that loops are fundamentally quantum mechanical effects.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Virtual Particles and Off-Shell Motion</h3>\n                    <p>Internal lines in Feynman diagrams represent <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/virtual-particles\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Virtual Particles</strong></a>. Unlike real particles, virtual particles are not restricted to the \"mass shell\"—their energy and momentum do not have to satisfy \\( E^2 = p^2 + m^2 \\). They can have any mass, but their contribution is suppressed as they move further away from their physical mass, a consequence of the <strong>Uncertainty Principle</strong>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Divergences and Renormalization</h3>\n                    <p>A major challenge in diagrammatic QFT is that loop diagrams often result infinite integrals. These \"ultraviolet divergences\" occur because virtual particles can have infinitely high momenta. Resolving these infinities leads to theory of <strong>Renormalization</strong>, where we realize that the parameters in our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> (mass and charge) are not constants but must be \"rescaled\" to match experimental values.</p>','Feynman Diagrams are a revolutionary graphical language used to organize and calculate complex mathematical expressions in Quantum Field Theory. They represent the paths and interactions of particles as they scatter through spacetime, providing a direct mapping between physical processes and the terms of the Dyson Series. In a Feynman diagram, different components of theory are represented by specific geometric elements: Internal Lines: Represent Propagators (the virtual particles that mediate forces).','Feynman Diagrams are a revolutionary graphical language used to organize and calculate complex mathematical expressions in Quantum Field Theory. They represent the paths and interactions of particles as they scatter through spacetime, providing a direct mapping between physical processes and the terms of the Dyson Series. In a Feynman diagram, different components of theory are represented by specific geometric elements:\n                    \n                        Internal Lines: Represent Propagators (the virtual particles that mediate forces).','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"11.537ex\" height=\"2.452ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 5099.6 1083.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\" d=\"M184 600Q184 624 203 642T247 661Q265 661 277 649T290 619Q290 596 270 577T226 557Q211 557 198 567T184 600ZM21 287Q21 295 30 318T54 369T98 420T158 442Q197 442 223 419T250 357Q250 340 236 301T196 196T154 83Q149 61 149 51Q149 26 166 26Q175 26 185 29T208 43T235 78T260 137Q263 149 265 151T282 153Q302 153 302 143Q302 135 293 112T268 61T223 11T161 -11Q129 -11 102 10T74 74Q74 91 79 106T122 220Q160 321 166 341T173 380Q173 404 156 404H154Q124 404 99 371T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-2F\" d=\"M423 750Q432 750 438 744T444 730Q444 725 271 248T92 -240Q85 -250 75 -250Q68 -250 62 -245T56 -231Q56 -221 230 257T407 740Q411 750 423 750Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><