(2010675,'propagators-qft','quantum-field-theory','Propagators and Green\'s Functions','<p>The <strong>Propagator</strong> (or 2-point Green\'s function) is the fundamental quantity in QFT that describes the probability amplitude for a particle to travel between two points in spacetime. It is the \"inverse\" of the differential operator in the field equations and serves as the building block for all interaction diagrams.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Feynman Propagator (\\( \\Delta_F \\))</h3>\n                    <p>The Feynman propagator is defined as the vacuum expectation value of the time-ordered product of two fields:\n                    \\[ D_F(x-y) = \\langle 0 | T\\phi(x)\\phi(y) | 0 \\rangle \\]\n                    It satisfies the inhomogeneous field equation with a delta-function source: \\( (\\Box + m^2) D_F(x-y) = -i\\delta^4(x-y) \\). This identifies the propagator as the <strong>Green\'s Function</strong> for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/relativistic-wave-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>the relativistic wave equation</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The iε Prescription and Causality</h3>\n                    <p>In momentum space, the propagator has poles at \\( p^2 = m^2 \\). To make the integral over these poles well-defined, we shift them slightly off the real axis using the <strong>iε prescription</strong>:\n                    \\[ D_F(p) = \\frac{i}{p^2 - m^2 + i\\epsilon} \\]\n                    This specific choice of contour ensures <strong>Causality</strong>: it describes positive-energy particles moving forward in time and negative-energy antiparticles moving backward in time (the Feynman-Stueckelberg interpretation).</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Position Space vs. Momentum Space</h3>\n                    <p>While interactions are easier to calculate in momentum space (where they are algebraic), the position-space propagator reveals the physical behavior of the field. For massive particles, the propagator decays exponentially at large spacelike separations, consistent with the finite range of forces like the weak nuclear force. For massless particles (photons), it follows an inverse-square law, giving rise to long-range Coulomb-like interactions.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Fermion and Vector Propagators</h3>\n                    <p>Each type of field has a unique propagator determined by its spin:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Fermions (Dirac):</strong> \\( S_F(p) = i/(\\cancel{p} - m) \\), incorporating the spinor structure.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Massless Vectors (Photon):</strong> \\( D_{\\mu\\nu}(p) = -i g_{\\mu\\nu} / p^2 \\) (in Feynman gauge).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Massive Vectors (W/Z):</strong> Includes terms proportional to \\( p_\\mu p_\\nu / m^2 \\), reflecting the longitudinal polarization mode.</li>\n                    </ul></p>\n\n                    <h3>5. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/virtual-particles\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Virtual Particles</strong></a> and Field Correlations</h3>\n                    <p>The propagator represents a <strong>Virtual Particle</strong>. Because the propagator is non-zero even for spacelike separations, it shows that quantum fields are correlated across all of spacetime. However, information cannot be transmitted faster than light because the <strong>Commutator</strong> (the difference between the retarded and advanced propagators) vanishes for spacelike intervals, preserving <strong>Microcausality</strong>.</p>','The Propagator (or 2-point Green\'s function) is the fundamental quantity in QFT that describes the probability amplitude for a particle to travel between two points in spacetime. It is the \"inverse\" of the differential operator in the field equations and serves as the building block for all interaction diagrams. The Feynman propagator is defined as the vacuum expectation value of the time-ordered product of two fields: It satisfies the inhomogeneous field equation with a delta-function source:.','The Propagator (or 2-point Green\'s function) is the fundamental quantity in QFT that describes the probability amplitude for a particle to travel between two points in spacetime. It is the \"inverse\" of the differential operator in the field equations and serves as the building block for all interaction diagrams. The Feynman propagator is defined as the vacuum expectation value of the time-ordered product of two fields:\n                    <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"28.698ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 12684.6 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D437\" d=\"M287 628Q287 635 230 637Q207 637 200 638T193 647Q193 655 197 667T204 682Q206 683 403 683Q570 682 590 682T630 676Q702 659 752 597T803 431Q803 275 696 151T444 3L430 1L236 0H125H72Q48 0 41 2T33 11Q33 13 36 25Q40 41 44 43T67 46Q94 46 127 49Q141 52 146 61Q149 65 218 339T287 628ZM703 469Q703 507 692 537T666 584T629 613T590 629T555 636Q553 636 541 636T512 636T479 637H436Q392 637 386 627Q384 623 313 339T242 52Q242 48 253 48T330 47Q335 47 349 47T373 46Q499 46 581 128Q617 164 640 212T683 339T703 469Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D439\" d=\"M48 1Q31 1 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H742Q749 676 749 669Q749 664 736 557T722 447Q720 440 702 440H690Q683 445 683 453Q683 454 686 477T689 530Q689 560 682 579T663 610T626 626T575 633T503 634H480Q398 633 393 631Q388 629 386 623Q385 622 352 492L320 363H375Q378 363 398 363T426 364T448 367T472 374T489 386Q502 398 511 419T524 457T529 475Q532 480 548 480H560Q567 475 567 470Q567 467 536 339T502 207Q500 200 482 200H470Q463 206 463 212Q463 215 468 234T473 274Q473 303 453 310T364 317H309L277 190Q245 66 245 60Q245 46 