(2010695,'immirzi-parameter','loop-quantum-gravity','Immirzi Parameter','<p>The Immirzi Parameter, denoted by the Greek letter \\(\\gamma\\), is a dimensionless, fundamental constant in Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG). It appears in the relation between the Ashtekar-Barbero connection and the extrinsic curvature, and it dictates the scale of the quantum geometry, specifically the units of area and volume. The value of \\(\\gamma\\) is not determined by theory itself and must be fixed by an external condition, typically by requiring that the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy formula for black holes holds in the LQG framework.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Setting the Scale of Quantum Geometry</h3>\n                    <p>In LQG, geometric observables like area and volume are quantized and have discrete spectra. The Immirzi parameter sets the size of these discrete quanta. For instance, the area of a minimal surface is proportional to \\(\\gamma\\). By choosing a specific value for \\(\\gamma\\), one can match the quantum area gap to the Planck area, which is a key step in recovering classical spacetime from the quantum description.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/black-hole-entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Black Hole Entropy</strong></a> and the Bekenstein-Hawking Formula</h3>\n                    <p>A significant achievement of LQG is its ability to reproduce the Bekenstein-Hawking formula for black hole entropy, \\(S_{BH} = A / (4 l_p^2)\\), where \\(A\\) is the black hole\'s horizon area and \\(l_p\\) is the Planck length. In LQG, the horizon area is quantized, and the entropy is calculated by counting the quantum states of the horizon geometry. The Immirzi parameter appears in the calculation of the area gap and must be carefully chosen to yield the correct \\(1/4\\) factor in the entropy formula.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Role in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> Constraint</h3>\n                    <p>The Immirzi parameter also appears in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> constraint equation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> in Ashtekar variables. This constraint is fundamental to the dynamics of the gravitational field. While its precise role in the dynamics is complex, \\(\\gamma\\) influences how the gravitational field evolves.</p>','The Immirzi Parameter, denoted by the Greek letter, is a dimensionless, fundamental constant in Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG). It appears in the relation between the Ashtekar-Barbero connection and the extrinsic curvature, and it dictates the scale of the quantum geometry, specifically the units of area and volume. The value of is not determined by theory itself and must be fixed by an external condition, typically by requiring that the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy formula for black holes holds in the LQG framework.','The Immirzi Parameter, denoted by the Greek letter <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.229ex\" height=\"1.486ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -441 543 657\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\" d=\"M31 249Q11 249 11 258Q11 275 26 304T66 365T129 418T206 441Q233 441 239 440Q287 429 318 386T371 255Q385 195 385 170Q385 166 386 166L398 193Q418 244 443 300T486 391T508 430Q510 431 524 431H537Q543 425 543 422Q543 418 522 378T463 251T391 71Q385 55 378 6T357 -100Q341 -165 330 -190T303 -216Q286 -216 286 -188Q286 -138 340 32L346 51L347 69Q348 79 348 100Q348 257 291 317Q251 355 196 355Q148 355 108 329T51 260Q49 251 47 251Q45 249 31 249Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g></g></g></svg>, is a dimensionless, fundamental constant in Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG). It appears in the relation between the Ashtekar-Barbero connection and the extrinsic curvature, and it dictates the scale of the quantum geometry, specifically the units of area and volume. The value of <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.229ex\" height=\"1.486ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -441 543 657\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\" d=\"M31 249Q11 249 11 258Q11 275 26 304T66 365T129 418T206 441Q233 441 239 440Q287 429 318 386T371 255Q385 195 385 170Q385 166 386 166L398 193Q418 244 443 300T486 391T508 430Q510 431 524 431H537Q543 425 543 422Q543 418 522 378T463 251T391 71Q385 55 378 6T357 -100Q341 -165 330 -190T303 -216Q286 -216 286 -188Q286 -138 340 32L346 51L347 69Q348 79 348 100Q348 257 291 317Q251 355 196 355Q148 355 108 329T51 260Q49 251 47 251Q45 249 31 249Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g></g></g></svg> is not determined by theory itself and must be fixed by an external condition, typically by requiring that the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy formula for black holes holds in the LQG framework.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.229ex\" height=\"1.486ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -441 543 657\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\" d=\"M31 249Q11 249 11 258Q11 275 26 304T66 365T129 418T206 441Q233 441 239 440Q287 429 318 386T371 255Q385 195 385 170Q385 166 386 166L398 193Q418 244 443 300T486 391T508 430Q510 431 524 431H537Q543 425 543 422Q543 418 522 378T463 251T391 71Q385 55 378 6T357 -100Q341 -165 330 -190T303 -216Q286 -216 286 -188Q286 -138 340 32L346 51L347 69Q348 79 348 100Q348 257 291 317Q251 355 196 355Q148 355 108 329T51 260Q49 251 47 251Q45 249 31 249Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"area-quantum-4d61f3aa\",\"entropy-matching-271d2c67\"]'),
(2010696,'holonomies','loop-quantum-gravity','Holonomies','<p>In Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG), Holonomies are fundamental to the definition of quantum states of geometry. They represent the parallel transport of the Ashtekar connection along paths or edges in space. Since the connection itself is not directly quantizable due to its non-polynomial nature, LQG uses its integrated form along curves (holonomies) as the basic observable. These holonomies effectively measure how the geometry of spacetime \"twists\" along these paths.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Parallel Transport in Gauge Theory</h3>\n                    <p>In gauge theories like electromagnetism and Yang-Mills theory, holonomies describe how a gauge field transforms a vector as it is parallel transported along a path. For a connection \\(A\\) and a path \\(\\gamma\\), the holonomy is given by the path-ordered exponential:\n                    \\[ h_\\gamma(A) = \\mathcal{P} \\exp \\left( -\\int_\\gamma A \\right) \\]\n                    In LQG, \\(A_a^i\\) is the Ashtekar-Barbero connection, and the holonomy along an edge \\(e\\) quantifies the effect of the gravitational field on a test vector moving along that edge.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Holonomies as Fundamental Variables</h3>\n                    <p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> formulation of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a> in terms of Ashtekar variables means that the phase space of theory is naturally described by functions of the connection \\(A_a^i\\). However, directly quantizing \\(A_a^i\\) is problematic. Instead, LQG defines a space of \"confining\" states based on holonomies. These holonomy functions form a dense set in the space of all possible connections, and they are used to define the physical Hilbert space of LQG. The spin network basis states are constructed from holonomies.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Relation to Spin Networks</h3>\n                    <p>Spin networks are graphs where edges are labeled by representations of SU(2) (spins \\(j\\)) and nodes are labeled by intertwiners. The spin \\(j\\) on an edge \\(e\\) can be thought of as selecting a particular representation of the group SU(2) that the holonomy along that edge is evaluated in. The nodes represent the flux of the densitized triad and are where these holonomies \"connect,\" defining the quantum states of geometry.</p>','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), Holonomies are fundamental to the definition of quantum states of geometry. They represent the parallel transport of the Ashtekar connection along paths or edges in space. Since the connection itself is not directly quantizable due to its non-polynomial nature, LQG uses its integrated form along curves (holonomies) as the basic observable.','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), Holonomies are fundamental to the definition of quantum states of geometry. They represent the parallel transport of the Ashtekar connection along paths or edges in space. Since the connection itself is not directly quantizable due to its non-polynomial nature, LQG uses its integrated form along curves (holonomies) as the basic observable.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.374ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24.559ex\" height=\"5.653ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1449.5 10855.1 2498.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-210E\" d=\"M137 683Q138 683 209 688T282 694Q294 694 294 685Q294 674 258 534Q220 386 220 383Q220 381 227 388Q288 442 357 442Q411 442 444 415T478 336Q478 285 440 178T402 50Q403 36 407 31T422 26Q450 26 474 56T513 138Q516 149 519 151T535 153Q555 153 555 145Q555 144 551 130Q535 71 500 33Q466 -10 419 -10H414Q367 -10 346 17T325 74Q325 90 361 192T398 345Q398 404 354 404H349Q266 404 205 306L198 293L164 158Q132 28 127 16Q114 -11 83 -11Q69 -11 59 -2T48 16Q48 30 121 320L195 616Q195 629 188 632T149 637H128Q122 643 122 645T124 664Q129 683 137 683Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D6FE\" d=\"M31 249Q11 249 11 258Q11 275 26 304T66 365T129 418T206 441Q233 441 239 440Q287 429 318 386T371 255Q385 195 385 170Q385 166 386 166L398 193Q418 244 443 300T486 391T508 430Q510 431 524 431H537Q543 425 543 422Q543 418 522 378T463 251T391 71Q385 55 378 6T357 -100Q341 -165 330 -190T303 -216Q286 -216 286 -188Q286 -138 340 32L346 51L347 69Q348 79 348 100Q348 257 291 317Q251 355 196 355Q148 355 108 329T51 260Q49 251 47 251Q45 249 31 249Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L274 -226Q180 -141 137 -14T94 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D434\" d=\"M208 74Q208 50 254 46Q272 46 272 35Q272 34 270 22Q267 8 264 4T251 0Q249 0 239 0T205 1T141 2Q70 2 50 0H42Q35 7 35 11Q37 38 48 46H62Q132 49 164 96Q170 102 345 401T523 704Q530 716 547 716H555H572Q578 707 578 706L606 383Q634 60 636 57Q641 46 701 46Q726 46 726 36Q726 34 723 22Q720 7 718 4T704 0Q701 0 690 0T651 1T578 2Q484 2 455 0H443Q437 6 437 9T439 27Q443 40 445 43L449 46H469Q523 49 533 63L521 213H283L249 155Q208 86 208 74ZM516 260Q516 271 504 416T490 562L463 519Q447 492 400 412L310 260L413 259Q516 259 516 260Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749L64 750Q69 750 74 750H86L114 726Q208 641 251 514T294 250Q294 182 284 119T261 12T224 -76T186 -143T145 -194T113 -227T90 -246Q87 -249 86 -250H74Q66 -250 63 -250T58 -247T55 -238Q56 -237 66 -225Q221 -64 221 250T66 725Q56 737 55 738Q55 746 60 749Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 