(2010697,'fluxes','loop-quantum-gravity','Fluxes','<p>In Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG), Fluxes are essential observables that, along with holonomies, form the fundamental variables of theory. They are defined as integrals of the densitized triad over surfaces. While holonomies measure how the Ashtekar connection changes along paths, fluxes measure the \"amount\" of gravitational field (represented by the densitized triad) passing through a given surface. They are conjugate to holonomies and play a crucial role in defining the quantum states of geometry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Geometric Interpretation of Fluxes</h3>\n                    <p>The densitized triad \\(E^a_i\\) is related to the spatial metric \\(q_{ab}\\) and determines the geometry of space. A flux of the densitized triad across a surface \\(\\Sigma\\) can be interpreted as measuring the \"volume\" or \"area\" content piercing that surface. Specifically, fluxes of the electric part of the triad are related to quantized areas.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Fluxes and Spin Networks</h3>\n                    <p>Fluxes are crucial for constructing the spin network basis. The nodes of a spin network represent points where the flux has been \"diagonalized.\" That is, at a node, the flux of the densitized triad through infinitesimal surfaces piercing the node is quantized, and the intertwiner at the node describes how these quantized fluxes are combined.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Commutation Relations</h3>\n                    <p>Fluxes and holonomies do not commute. This non-commutativity is a manifestation of the quantum nature of geometry. The algebra generated by these non-commuting observables forms the basis for the physical Hilbert space of LQG. The quantization of these variables leads to the discrete spectra of area and volume operators.</p>','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), Fluxes are essential observables that, along with holonomies, form the fundamental variables of theory. They are defined as integrals of the densitized triad over surfaces. While holonomies measure how the Ashtekar connection changes along paths, fluxes measure the \"amount\" of gravitational field (represented by the densitized triad) passing through a given surface.','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), Fluxes are essential observables that, along with holonomies, form the fundamental variables of theory. They are defined as integrals of the densitized triad over surfaces. While holonomies measure how the Ashtekar connection changes along paths, fluxes measure the \"amount\" of gravitational field (represented by the densitized triad) passing through a given surface.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.677ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.879ex\" height=\"2.215ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 1272.4 979.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\" d=\"M492 213Q472 213 472 226Q472 230 477 250T482 285Q482 316 461 323T364 330H312Q311 328 277 192T243 52Q243 48 254 48T334 46Q428 46 458 48T518 61Q567 77 599 117T670 248Q680 270 683 272Q690 274 698 274Q718 274 718 261Q613 7 608 2Q605 0 322 0H133Q31 0 31 11Q31 13 34 25Q38 41 42 43T65 46Q92 46 125 49Q139 52 144 61Q146 66 215 342T285 622Q285 629 281 629Q273 632 228 634H197Q191 640 191 642T193 659Q197 676 203 680H757Q764 676 764 669Q764 664 751 557T737 447Q735 440 717 440H705Q698 445 698 453L701 476Q704 500 704 528Q704 558 697 578T678 609T643 625T596 632T532 634H485Q397 633 392 631Q388 629 386 622Q385 619 355 499T324 377Q347 376 372 376H398Q464 376 489 391T534 472Q538 488 540 490T557 493Q562 493 565 493T570 492T572 491T574 487T577 483L544 351Q511 218 508 216Q505 213 492 213Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q331 441 370 392Q386 422 416 422Q429 422 439 414T449 394Q449 381 412 234T374 68Q374 43 381 35T402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487Q506 153 506 144Q506 138 501 117T481 63T449 13Q436 0 417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM351 328Q351 334 346 350T323 385T277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q217 26 254 59T298 110Q300 114 325 217T351 328Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\" d=\"M184 600Q184 624 203 642T247 661Q265 661 277 649T290 619Q290 596 270 577T226 557Q211 557 198 567T184 600ZM21 287Q21 295 30 318T54 369T98 420T158 442Q197 442 223 419T250 357Q250 340 236 301T196 196T154 83Q149 61 149 51Q149 26 166 26Q175 26 185 29T208 43T235 78T260 137Q263 149 265 151T282 153Q302 153 302 143Q302 135 293 112T268 61T223 11T161 -11Q129 -11 102 10T74 74Q74 91 79 106T122 220Q160 321 166 341T173 380Q173 404 156 404H154Q124 404 99 371T61 287Q60 286 59 284T58 281T56 279T53 278T49 278T41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(848.3,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(771,-291.5) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"flux-definition-9bfffcc1\"]'),
(2010698,'spin-networks','loop-quantum-gravity','Spin Networks','<p>Spin Networks are a key concept in Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG), forming the basis for the quantum states of space. They are graphs where edges are labeled by representations of SU(2) (spins \\(j\\)) and nodes are labeled by intertwiners. These networks provide a discrete representation of quantum geometry, where edges represent quanta of area and nodes represent quanta of volume. They are fundamental to understanding the granular structure of space predicted by LQG.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Graph Representation of Space</h3>\n                    <p>A spin network can be visualized as a network of interconnected lines. Each line (edge) is assigned a non-negative half-integer spin \\(j\\), which corresponds to a quantum of area. The nodes (vertices) are where these edges meet, and they are assigned intertwiners, which describe how the spins of the incoming and outgoing edges are combined. These intertwiners are crucial for ensuring that the total angular momentum is conserved at each node, which translates to geometric constraints in the quantum theory.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Dual to Quantum Volumes</h3>\n                    <p>The nodes of a spin network are particularly important as they are dual to quantum volumes. The intertwiner at a node encodes the quantum state of the region of space associated with that node. The volume operator in LQG acts on these nodes, yielding a discrete spectrum of eigenvalues. The discreteness of volume arises from the fact that intertwiners can only combine spins in specific ways, leading to quantized volume contributions.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Relation to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/holonomies\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Holonomies</strong></a> and Fluxes</h3>\n                    <p>Spin networks arise naturally from the quantization of the Ashtekar variables. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/holonomies\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Holonomies</strong></a> of the Ashtekar connection along the edges of a graph define the spin labels \\(j\\) on those edges, while fluxes of the densitized triad through surfaces intersecting the nodes define the intertwiners. Thus, spin networks provide a concrete representation of the quantum states of geometry, derived from the fundamental algebra of holonomies and fluxes.</p>','Spin Networks are a key concept in Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), forming the basis for the quantum states of space. They are graphs where edges are labeled by representations of SU(2) (spins ) and nodes are labeled by intertwiners. These networks provide a discrete representation of quantum geometry, where edges represent quanta of area and nodes represent quanta of volume.','Spin Networks are a key concept in Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), forming the basis for the quantum states of space. They are graphs where edges are labeled by representations of SU(2) (spins <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.462ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.932ex\" height=\"1.957ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -661 412 865\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\" d=\"M297 596Q297 627 318 644T361 661Q378 661 389 651T403 623Q403 595 384 576T340 557Q322 557 310 567T297 596ZM288 376Q288 405 262 405Q240 405 220 393T185 362T161 325T144 293L137 279Q135 278 121 278H107Q101 284 101 286T105 299Q126 348 164 391T252 441Q253 441 260 441T272 442Q296 441 316 432Q341 418 354 401T367 348V332L318 133Q267 -67 264 -75Q246 -125 194 -164T75 -204Q25 -204 7 -183T-12 -137Q-12 -110 7 -91T53 -71Q70 -71 82 -81T95 -112Q95 -148 63 -167Q69 -168 77 -168Q111 -168 139 -140T182 -74L193 -32Q204 11 219 72T251 197T278 308T289 365Q289 372 288 376Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) and nodes are labeled by intertwiners. These networks provide a discrete representation of quantum geometry, where edges represent quanta of area and nodes represent quanta of volume.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.462ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.932ex\" height=\"1.957ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -661 412 865\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\" d=\"M297 596Q297 627 318 644T361 661Q378 661 389 651T403 623Q403 595 384 576T340 557Q322 557 310 567T297 596ZM288 376Q288 405 262 405Q240 405 220 393T185 362T161 325T144 293L137 279Q135 278 121 278H107Q101 284 101 286T105 299Q126 348 164 391T252 441Q253 441 260 441T272 442Q296 441 316 432Q341 418 354 401T367 348V332L318 133Q267 -67 264 -75Q246 -125 194 -164T75 -204Q25 -204 7 -183T-12 -137Q-12 -110 7 -91T53 -71Q70 -71 82 -81T95 -112Q95 -148 63 -167Q69 -168 77 -168Q111 -168 139 -140T182 -74L193 -32Q204 11 219 72T251 197T278 308T289 365Q289 372 288 376Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"area-quantization-65e1db68\",\"volume-quantization-acb94e03\"]'),
(2010699,'intertwiners','loop-quantum-gravity','Intertwiners','<p>In Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG), Intertwiners are mathematical objects that label the nodes of spin networks. They play a crucial role in the quantization of geometry, as they describe how the spins (representations of SU(2)) associated with the edges meeting at a node are \"intertwined\" or combined. Geometrically, intertwiners are associated with quantum volumes, and they are responsible for the quantization of volume in LQG.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Algebraic Structure</h3>\n                    <p>An intertwiner is an operator that maps a tensor product of SU(2) representations to another SU(2) representation, preserving the group structure. In the context of spin networks, intertwiners connect the representations (spins \\(j\\)) of the incoming edges to the representation of the outgoing edge at a node. They ensure that the total angular momentum is conserved at each node, which is analogous to geometric constraints in classical geometry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Quantum Volumes</h3>\n                    <p>The nodes of a spin network are dual to quantum volumes. The intertwiner at a node encodes the quantum state of the region of space associated with that node. The volume operator in LQG acts on the intertwiners, and its spectrum is determined by the possible ways intertwiners can combine spins. This leads to the discreteness of volume in quantum geometry.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Spin Network Basis</h3>\n                    <p>Intertwiners, along with edge spins, form the basis states of the physical Hilbert space of LQG, known as spin networks. The choice of intertwiners determines the physical states of the gravitational field, and their algebraic properties are essential for deriving the dynamics of LQG through spin foams.</p>','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), Intertwiners are mathematical objects that label the nodes of spin networks. They play a crucial role in the quantization of geometry, as they describe how the spins (representations of SU(2)) associated with the edges meeting at a node are \"intertwined\" or combined. Geometrically, intertwiners are associated with quantum volumes, and they are responsible for the quantization of volume in LQG.','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), Intertwiners are mathematical objects that label the nodes of spin networks. They play a crucial role in the quantization of geometry, as they describe how the spins (representations of SU(2)) associated with the edges meeting at a node are \"intertwined\" or combined. Geometrically, intertwiners are associated with quantum volumes, and they are responsible for the quantization of volume in LQG.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.462ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.932ex\" height=\"1.957ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -661 412 865\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\" d=\"M297 596Q297 627 318 644T361 661Q378 661 389 651T403 623Q403 595 384 576T340 557Q322 557 310 567T297 596ZM288 376Q288 405 262 405Q240 405 220 393T185 362T161 325T144 293L137 279Q135 278 121 278H107Q101 284 101 286T105 299Q126 348 164 391T252 441Q253 441 260 441T272 442Q296 441 316 432Q341 418 354 401T367 348V332L318 133Q267 -67 264 -75Q246 -125 194 -164T75 -204Q25 -204 7 -183T-12 -137Q-12 -110 7 -91T53 -71Q70 -71 82 -81T95 -112Q95 -148 63 -167Q69 -168 77 -168Q111 -168 139 -140T182 -74L193 -32Q204 11 219 72T251 197T278 308T289 365Q289 372 288 376Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"intertwiner-notation-30b7e77a\"]'),
(2010700,'quantized','loop-quantum-gravity','Quantized Geometry','<p>In Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG), a revolutionary concept is that <strong>Geometry itself is Quantized</strong>. Unlike classical physics where space and time are continuous, LQG predicts that fundamental geometric quantities like area and volume are discrete, existing in distinct, indivisible units. This discreteness arises from the underlying quantum states of space, which are represented by spin networks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Discrete Spectra of Area and Volume</h3>\n                    <p>The operators corresponding to area and volume in LQG have been shown to possess discrete spectra. The area operator, when acting on spin network states, yields eigenvalues that are multiples of the Planck area. Similarly, the volume operator yields discrete values, meaning there is a minimum quantum of volume. This fundamental discreteness is a direct consequence of the spin network structure, where edges correspond to quanta of area and nodes to quanta of volume.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Planck Scale</h3>\n                    <p>The scale at which this quantization becomes significant is the Planck scale (Planck length \\(l_P \\approx 1.6 \\times 10^{-35}\\) meters, Planck area \\(l_P^2 \\approx 2.6 \\times 10^{-70}\\) m², Planck volume \\(l_P^3 \\approx 4.2 \\times 10^{-105}\\) m³). LQG suggests that below this scale, the classical notion of continuous spacetime breaks down, and a quantum, granular structure emerges. This prevents the infinities that plague attempts to quantize gravity in a continuous spacetime background.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Implications for Singularities</h3>\n                    <p>The quantization of geometry has profound implications for phenomena like black holes and the Big Bang. In classical <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, these involve singularities where curvature and density become infinite. LQG offers a potential resolution to these singularities, as the discrete nature of space would prevent curvature from becoming infinite. For example, in Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-cosmology\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Cosmology</strong></a> (LQC), a simplified model of LQG, the Big Bang singularity is replaced by a \"Big Bounce.\"</p>','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), a revolutionary concept is that Geometry itself is Quantized. Unlike classical physics where space and time are continuous, LQG predicts that fundamental geometric quantities like area and volume are discrete, existing in distinct, indivisible units. This discreteness arises from the underlying quantum states of space, which are represented by spin networks.','In Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG), a revolutionary concept is that Geometry itself is Quantized. Unlike classical physics where space and time are continuous, LQG predicts that fundamental geometric quantities like area and volume are discrete, existing in distinct, indivisible units. This discreteness arises from the underlying quantum states of space, which are represented by spin networks.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"16.032ex\" height=\"2.294ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864 7086.3 1014\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D459\" d=\"M117 59Q117 26 142 26Q179 26 205 131Q211 151 215 152Q217 153 225 153H229Q238 153 241 153T246 151T248 144Q247 138 245 128T234 90T214 43T183 6T137 -11Q101 -11 70 11T38 85Q38 97 39 102L104 360Q167 615 167 623Q167 626 166 628T162 632T157 634T149 635T141 636T132 637T122 637Q112 637 109 637T101 638T95 641T94 647Q94 649 96 661Q101 680 107 682T179 688Q194 689 213 690T243 693T254 694Q266 694 266 686Q266 675 193 386T118 83Q118 81 118 75T117 65V59Z