data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(445,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(739,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(1128,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D457\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D457\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(445,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2089.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3089.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3589.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0,115.3)\"><use data-c=\"221A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-LO-221A\"></use></g><rect width=\"3978.3\" height=\"60\" x=\"1020\" y=\"1205.3\"></rect></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"area-operator-action-0dcc507d\",\"area-spectrum-0b10478d\"]'),
(2010702,'volume-operator','loop-quantum-gravity','Volume Operator','<p>The Volume Operator is a fundamental observable in Loop <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-gravity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Gravity</strong></a> (LQG) that quantifies the volume of a region in quantum spacetime. Similar to the area operator, its spectrum is discrete, indicating that volume also comes in fundamental, indivisible units. This quantization of volume is intrinsically linked to the intertwiners at the nodes of spin networks.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Operator Definition and Nodes</h3>\n                    <p>The volume operator \\(\\hat{V}(R)\\) associated with a region \\(R\\) in space acts on the intertwiners at the nodes of a spin network that are contained within \\(R\\). Its definition is quite complex, involving the summation over contributions from each node. The operator is constructed such that it yields discrete eigenvalues, reflecting the granular nature of space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Discrete Spectrum</h3>\n                    <p>The volume operator has a discrete spectrum of eigenvalues. While the exact formula is complex and depends on the specific intertwiner at each node, it is known that the smallest possible non-zero volume is on the order of the Planck volume \\(l_P^3\\). This discreteness is a direct consequence of the fact that intertwiners can only combine spins in specific ways, leading to quantized volume contributions at the nodes.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Link to Intertwiners</h3>\n                    <p>The quantization of volume is intimately tied to the algebraic properties of intertwiners. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/intertwiners\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Intertwiners</strong></a> act as the \"building blocks\" of quantum volumes. Different intertwiners, corresponding to different ways of combining spins, lead to different quantized volume values. This connection highlights the role of intertwiners in defining the fundamental geometry of spacetime in LQG.</p>','The Volume Operator is a fundamental observable in Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG) that quantifies the volume of a region in quantum spacetime. Similar to the area operator, its spectrum is discrete, indicating that volume also comes in fundamental, indivisible units. This quantization of volume is intrinsically linked to the intertwiners at the nodes of spin networks.','The Volume Operator is a fundamental observable in Loop Quantum Gravity (LQG) that quantifies the volume of a region in quantum spacetime. Similar to the area operator, its spectrum is discrete, indicating that volume also comes in fundamental, indivisible units. This quantization of volume is intrinsically linked to the intertwiners at the nodes of spin networks.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.217ex\" height=\"2.943ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1051 2306 1301\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D449\" d=\"M52 648Q52 670 65 683H76Q118 680 181 680Q299 680 320 683H330Q336 677 336 674T334 656Q329 641 325 637H304Q282 635 274 635Q245 630 242 620Q242 618 271 369T301 118L374 235Q447 352 520 471T595 594Q599 601 599 609Q599 633 555 637Q537 637 537 648Q537 649 539 661Q542 675 545 679T558 683Q560 683 570 683T604 682T668 681Q737 681 755 683H762Q769 676 769 672Q769 655 760 640Q757 637 743 637Q730 636 719 635T698 630T682 623T670 615T660 608T652 599T645 592L452 282Q272 -9 266 -16Q263 -18 259 -21L241 -22H234Q216 -22 216 -15Q213 -9 177 305Q139 623 138 626Q133 637 76 637H59Q52 642 52 648Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5E\" d=\"M112 560L249 694L257 686Q387 562 387 560L361 531Q359 532 303 581L250 627L195 580Q182 569 169 557T148 538L140 532Q138 530 125 546L112 560Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 