-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2422.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"stationary-action-2d9f46c3\",\"second-variation-17c35e75\",\"equation-of-motion-92bda0da\"]'),
(2010711,'action-physics','action-principle','The Action Integral','<p>The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/action-integral\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Action Integral</strong></a> (\\( S \\)) is the central quantity in variational mechanics, representing the accumulated \"cost\" or dynamical history of a system as it evolves between two points in time. It is a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/functional\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Functional</strong></a> that evaluates an entire path through configuration space, assigning it a single scalar value. <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltons-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamilton\'s Principle</strong></a> states that the true physical path is the one that extremizes this integral.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. Definition as a Time Integral</h3>\n                  <p>For a discrete mechanical system, the action is defined as the definite integral of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> with respect to time:\n                  \\[ S[q(t)] = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} L(q, \\dot{q}, t) dt \\]\n                  By integrating over time, the action evaluates the global history of the path, shifting physics from a local differential view (<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/isaac-newton\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Isaac Newton</strong></a>\'s forces acting at an instant) to a global integral view (the system evaluating the entire trajectory at once).</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Units and Fundamental Constants</h3>\n                  <p>The dimensions of action are Energy \\( \\times \\) Time (e.g., Joule-seconds). These are the exact same dimensions as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Angular Momentum</strong></a>. In modern physics, the most fundamental unit of action is <strong>Planck\'s Constant</strong> (\\( h \\) or \\( \\hbar \\)). Classical mechanics emerges purely because macroscopic systems involve actions that are gigantically larger than \\( \\hbar \\).</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Hamilton\'s Principal Function</h3>\n                  <p>When evaluated strictly along the classical, stationary path, the action becomes a function of the endpoints and time, known as <strong>Hamilton\'s Principal Function</strong> (\\( S_{cl}(q_2, t_2; q_1, t_1) \\)). This function satisfies the <strong>Hamilton-Jacobi Equation</strong>, a non-linear partial differential equation that relates the action directly to the system\'s <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> and serves as the conceptual bridge to the Schrödinger equation.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Maupertuis\' Principle (Reduced Action)</h3>\n                  <p>An alternative formulation is <strong>Maupertuis\' Principle</strong>, which considers the <strong>Reduced Action</strong> (\\( S_0 = \\int p dq \\)) for systems where the total energy \\( E \\) is conserved. In this formulation, the time is not fixed, and the variation is taken over paths of constant energy. It demonstrates that particles follow the path of minimum \"effort\" through the spatial manifold, independently of how fast they travel along it.</p>\n\n                  <h3>5. Action in General Relativity</h3>\n                  <p>The action principle is the foundation of modern gravity. In <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/general-relativity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>General Relativity</strong></a>, the action is the <strong>Einstein-Hilbert Action</strong>, an integral of the Ricci scalar curvature (\\( R \\)) over spacetime. Setting the variation of this action to zero yields the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/albert-einstein\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Albert Einstein</strong></a> <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/field-equations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Field Equations</strong></a>, proving that the curvature of spacetime itself dynamically evolves to follow the path of least resistance.</p>','The Action Integral is the central quantity in variational mechanics, representing the accumulated \"cost\" or dynamical history of a system as it evolves between two points in time. It is a Functional that evaluates an entire path through configuration space, assigning it a single scalar value. Hamilton\'s Principle states that the true physical path is the one that extremizes this integral.','The Action Integral (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.459ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 645 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554 544 579T526 616T501 641Q465 662 419 662Q362 662 313 616T263 510Q263 480 278 458T319 427Q323 425 389 408T456 390Q490 379 522 342T554 242Q554 216 546 186Q541 164 528 137T492 78T426 18T332 -20Q320 -22 298 -22Q199 -22 144 33L134 44L106 13Q83 -14 78 -18T65 -22Q52 -22 52 -14Q52 -11 110 221Q112 227 130 227H143Q149 221 149 216Q149 214 148 207T144 186T142 153Q144 114 160 87T203 47T255 29T308 24Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g></g></g></svg>) is the central quantity in variational mechanics, representing the accumulated \"cost\" or dynamical history of a system as it evolves between two points in time. It is a Functional that evaluates an entire path through configuration space, assigning it a single scalar value. Hamilton\'s Principle states that the true physical path is the one that extremizes this integral.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.268ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"23.69ex\" height=\"5.731ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1530.7 10470.9 2533.1\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T569 456Q549 456 549 465Q549 471 550 475Q550 478 551 494T553 520Q553 554 544 579T526 616T501 641Q465 662 419 662Q362 662 313 616T263 510Q263 480 278 458T319 427Q323 425 389 408T456 390Q490 379 522 342T554 242Q554 216 546 186Q541 164 528 137T492 78T426 18T332 -20Q320 -22 298 -22Q199 -22 144 33L134 44L106 13Q83 -14 78 -18T65 -22Q52 -22 52 -14Q52 -11 110 221Q112 227 130 227H143Q149 221 149 216Q149 214 148 207T144 186T142 153Q144 114 160 87T203 47T255 29T308 24Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-5B\" d=\"M118 -250V750H255V710H158V-210H255V-250H118Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\" d=\"M33 157Q33 258 109 349T280 441Q340 441 372 389Q373 390 377 395T388 406T404 418Q438 442 450 442Q454 442 457 439T460 434Q460 425 391 149Q320 -135 320 -139Q320 -147 365 -148H390Q396 -156 396 -157T393 -175Q389 -188 383 -194H370Q339 -192 262 -192Q234 -192 211 -192T174 -192T157 -193Q143 -193 143 -185Q143 -182 145 -170Q149 -154 152 -151T172 -148Q220 -148 230 -141Q238 -136 258 -53T279 32Q279 33 272 29Q224 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157ZM352 326Q329 405 277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q233 26 290 98L298 109L352 326Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M94 250Q94 319 104 381T127 488T164 576T202 643T244 695T277 729T302 750H315H319Q333 750 333 741Q333 738 316 720T275 667T226 581T184 443T167 250T184 58T225 -81T274 -167T316 -220T333 -241Q333 -250 318 -250H315H302L2