ct width=\"1539\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7851.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(8906.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"form-invariance-0416c073\",\"space-geometry-58c188e8\"]'),
(2010713,'fermats-principle','action-principle','Fermat\'s Principle of Least Time','<p>Fermat\'s Principle of Least Time is the optical precursor to the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/least-action-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principle of Least Action</strong></a>. Formulated by Pierre de Fermat in 1662, it states that the path taken by a ray of light between two points is the path that can be traversed in the least amount of time. In university-level physics, it serves as the foundational variational principle linking geometric optics to the analytical mechanics of Hamilton and Lagrange.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Optical Path Length</h3>\n                  <p>The principle is formalized by defining the <strong>Optical Path Length (OPL)</strong>, which is the integral of the refractive index \\( n(\\mathbf{r}) \\) along the curve \\( C \\ of the light ray:\n                  \\[ \\text{OPL} = \\int_C n(\\mathbf{r}) ds = c \\int_C dt \\]\n                  Fermat\'s Principle asserts that the actual path taken by light makes this integral <strong>stationary</strong> (\\( \\delta (\\text{OPL}) = 0 \\)). In a vacuum where \\( n = 1 \\), the path of least time is the path of least distance—a straight Euclidean line.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. Derivation of Snell\'s Law</h3>\n                  <p>The power of Fermat\'s principle lies in its ability to derive all the laws of geometrical optics without analyzing electromagnetic wave fields. By minimizing the travel time across a planar boundary between two media with indices \\( n_1 \\) and \\( n_2 \\), one naturally derives <strong>Snell\'s Law of Refraction</strong>:\n                  \\[ n_1 \\sin \\theta_1 = n_2 \\sin \\theta_2 \\]\n                  Similarly, minimizing the time for a ray reflecting off a surface perfectly yields the <strong>Law of Reflection</strong> (angle of incidence equals angle of reflection).</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Analogy to Maupertuis\' Principle</h3>\n                  <p>In the 19th century, William Rowan Hamilton recognized a profound mathematical analogy between optics and mechanics. The refractive index \\( n(\\mathbf{r}) \\) in optics plays the exact same mathematical role as the momentum \\( p(\\mathbf{r}) \\) in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/maupertuiss-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Maupertuis\' Principle of Least Action</strong></a> (\\( \\delta \\int p ds = 0 \\)). This implies that particles in a potential field move exactly like light rays traversing a medium with a variable refractive index.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. The Wave-Particle Connection</h3>\n                  <p>This optic-mechanical analogy was the crucial insight that led Louis de Broglie and Erwin Schrödinger to develop <strong>Wave Mechanics</strong>. If particles move like light rays, then perhaps particles are actually the geometric \"ray approximation\" of underlying matter waves. This synthesis showed that Fermat\'s Principle of stationary time is just the high-frequency limit of the constructive interference of quantum probability waves.</p>\n\n                  <h3>5. Stationary vs. Minimum Time</h3>\n                  <p>Just like the action principle, Fermat\'s principle strictly requires the time to be <strong>Stationary</strong>, not necessarily a minimum. For example, light reflecting off the inside of a spherical mirror (a concave surface) takes a path that is a local maximum in time compared to adjacent reflected paths, yet it perfectly represents a valid physical trajectory.</p>','Fermat\'s Principle of Least Time is the optical precursor to the Principle of Least Action. Formulated by Pierre de Fermat in 1662, it states that the path taken by a ray of light between two points is the path that can be traversed in the least amount of time. In university-level physics, it serves as the foundational variational principle linking geometric optics to the analytical mechanics of Hamilton and Lagrange.','Fermat\'s Principle of Least Time is the optical precursor to the Principle of Least Action. Formulated by Pierre de Fermat in 1662, it states that the path taken by a ray of light between two points is the path that can be traversed in the least amount of time. In university-level physics, it serves as the foundational variational principle linking geometric optics to the analytical mechanics of Hamilton and Lagrange.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.063ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"26.604ex\" height=\"5.142ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1361 11758.9 2272.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-4F\" d=\"M56 340Q56 423 86 494T164 610T270 680T388 705Q521 705 621 601T722 341Q722 260 693 191T617 75T510 4T388 -22T267 3T160 74T85 189T56 340ZM467 647Q426 665 388 665Q360 665 331 654T269 620T213 549T179 439Q174 411 174 354Q174 144 277 61Q327 20 385 20H389H391Q474 20 537 99Q603 188 603 354Q603 411 598 439Q577 592 467 647Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-50\" d=\"M130 622Q123 629 119 631T103 634T60 637H27V683H214Q237 683 276 683T331 684Q419 684 471 671T567 616Q624 563 624 489Q624 421 573 372T451 307Q429 302 328 301H234V181Q234 62 237 58Q245 47 304 46H337V0H326Q305 3 182 3Q47 3 38 0H27V46H60Q102 47 111 49T130 61V622ZM507 488Q507 514 506 528T500 564T483 597T450 620T397 635Q385 637 307 637H286Q237 637 234 628Q231 624 231 483V342H302H339Q390 342 423 349T481 382Q507 411 507 488Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-4C\" d=\"M128 622Q121 629 117 631T101 634T58 637H25V683H36Q48 680 182 680Q324 680 348 683H360V637H333Q273 637 258 635T233 622L232 342V129Q232 57 237 52Q243 47 313 47Q384 47 410 53Q470 70 498 110T536 221Q536 226 537 238T540 261T542 272T562 273H582V268Q580 265 568 137T554 5V0H25V46H58Q100 47 109 49T128 61V622Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70