ata-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(5749.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D42B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D42B\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6223.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6612.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(7132.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7879.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(8935.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(9534.8,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 1)\"><use data-c=\"222B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-LO-222B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(589,-896.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D436\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D436\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(10877.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(11397.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"fermat-s-principle-7cc379e6\",\"refraction-74d97afe\",\"mechanical-analogy-2fa5cc20\"]'),
(2010714,'richard-feynman','action-principle','Feynman and the Action Principle','<p>While Hamilton and Lagrange established the Action Principle as the bedrock of classical mechanics, it was <strong>Richard Feynman</strong> in the 1940s who elevated it to the absolute foundation of quantum physics. Feynman\'s revolutionary insight was that the classical concept of a single \"path of least action\" is merely the macroscopic deterministic illusion generated by the quantum interference of infinite possible histories.</p>\n\n                  <h3>1. The Dirac Inspiration</h3>\n                  <p>Feynman\'s journey began with a speculative paper by Paul Dirac, which suggested a deep analogy between the classical action exponential \\( e^{iS/\\hbar} \\) and the quantum transformation amplitude between states. Feynman took this mathematical hint and built an entirely new formulation of quantum mechanics, abandoning the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a>-focused Schrödinger equation in favor of a purely <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> approach based directly on the action integral.</p>\n\n                  <h3>2. The \"Sum Over Histories\"</h3>\n                  <p>Feynman proposed that unlike a classical particle that follows a single deterministic trajectory, a quantum particle travels from point A to point B by taking <strong>every possible path simultaneously</strong> through spacetime. This \"Sum Over Histories\" requires calculating the action \\( S \\) for every conceivable trajectory—no matter how chaotic, loopy, or non-physical—and assigning a complex probability amplitude to each.</p>\n\n                  <h3>3. Recovering the Classical Limit</h3>\n                  <p>The brilliance of Feynman\'s approach lies in its natural, geometric explanation of classical physics. Each path contributes a phase \\( e^{iS/\\hbar} \\). For macroscopic objects, the action \\( S \\) is gigantically larger than Planck\'s constant \\( \\hbar \\). Consequently, paths that differ slightly have vastly different phases, leading to destructive interference that cancels their probabilities to zero. Only the paths near the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stationary-action\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Stationary Action</strong></a> (where \\( \\delta S = 0 \\)) have phases that align and constructively interfere, elegantly explaining why classical objects rigidly obey <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltons-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamilton\'s Principle</strong></a>.</p>\n\n                  <h3>4. Feynman Diagrams</h3>\n                  <p>Feynman extended this action-based path integral from single particles to relativistic fields, resulting in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-electrodynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Electrodynamics (QED)</strong></a>. To manage the extraordinarily complex perturbative calculations of the action integrals, he invented <strong>Feynman Diagrams</strong>. These visual topological tools map out the interactions of field trajectories in spacetime, transforming abstract analytical mechanics into the standard calculational language of modern particle physics.</p>\n\n                  <h3>5. A New Intuitive Ontology</h3>\n                  <p>Feynman\'s reformulation fundamentally shifted the ontology of physics. Rather than viewing the universe as a state evolving deterministically through time (the standard <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> view), the action perspective views the universe globally across all of spacetime. It portrays reality as a democratic superposition of all possibilities, where the classical laws of motion are simply emergent phenomena dictated by the geometry of phase interference.</p>','While Hamilton and Lagrange established the Action Principle as the bedrock of classical mechanics, it was Richard Feynman in the 1940s who elevated it to the absolute foundation of quantum physics. Feynman\'s revolutionary insight was that the classical concept of a single \"path of least action\" is merely the macroscopic deterministic illusion generated by the quantum interference of infinite possible histories. Feynman\'s journey began with a speculative paper by Paul Dirac, which suggested a deep analogy between the classical action exponential and the quantum transformation amplitude between states.','While Hamilton and Lagrange established the Action Principle as the bedrock of classical mechanics, it was Richard Feynman in the 1940s who elevated it to the absolute foundation of quantum physics. Feynman\'s revolutionary insight was that the classical concept of a single \"path of least action\" is merely the macroscopic deterministic illusion generated by the quantum interference of infinite possible histories. Feynman\'s journey began with a speculative paper by Paul Dirac, which suggested a deep analogy between the classical action exponential <svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.525ex\" height=\"2.046ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -893.3 2000 904.3\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D452\" d=\"M39 168Q39 225 58 272T107 350T174 402T244 433T307 442H310Q355 442 388 420T421 355Q421 265 310 237Q261 224 176 223Q139 223 138 221Q138 219 132 186T125 128Q125 81 146 54T209 26T302 45T394 111Q403 121 406 121Q410 121 419 112T429 98T420 82T390 55T344 24T281 -1T205 -11Q126 -11 83 42T39 168ZM373 353Q367 405 305 405Q272 405 244 391T199 357T170 316T154 280T149 261Q149 260 169 260Q282 260 327 284T373 353Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\" d=\"M184 600Q184 624 203 642T247 661Q265 661 277 649T290 619Q290 596 270 577T226 557Q211 557 198 567T184 600ZM21 287Q21 295 30 318T54 369T98 420T158 442Q197 442 223 419T250 357Q250 340 23