ef=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(520,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1081\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(1321,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(736,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(266,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D43F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-727)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(316.8,-2) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"2D9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2D9\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(479,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g><rect width=\"1539\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2515,0) translate(0 -0.5)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-S3-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4794.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(5794.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(266,676)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D43F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43F\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-686)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D715\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D715\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(566,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D45E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(479,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D456\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D456\"></use></g></g></g><rect width=\"1539\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7851.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(8906.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-stationary-action-class-subtopic-link-stationary-action-a-234d4f80\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-energy-operator-class-subtopic-link-energy-operator-a-2b2cf775\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-state-geometry-class-subtopic-link-state-geometry-a-5314f032\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-canonical-algebra-class-subtopic-link-canonical-algebra-a-2545fae5\"]'),
(2010721,'euler-lagrange-equations','analytical-mechanics','Euler-Lagrange Equations','<p>The Euler-Lagrange Equations are the fundamental second-order differential equations that govern the dynamics of a physical system in analytical mechanics. They are derived from <strong>Hamilton\'s Principle of Stationary Action</strong> and represent the condition that a system\'s trajectory must extremize the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/action-integral\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Action Integral</strong></a>. These equations provide a powerful, coordinate-independent alternative to Newton\'s laws, naturally accounting for constraints and focusing on the system\'s energy functions.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Derivation from the Calculus of Variations</h3>\n                    <p>The Euler-Lagrange equations are the result of applying the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/calculus-of-variations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Calculus of Variations</strong></a> to the action functional \\( S = \\int \\mathcal{L}(q, \\dot{q}, t) dt \\). By requiring that the first variation of the action vanish (\\( \\delta S = 0 \\)) for an arbitrary infinitesimal perturbation \\( \\delta q(t) \\) that vanishes at the endpoints, we obtain the master equations:\n                    \\[ \\frac{d}{dt} \\left( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{q}_i} \\right) - \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial q_i} = 0 \\]\n                    For a system with \\( n \\) degrees of freedom, this yields a set of \\( n \\) coupled second-order ordinary differential equations (ODEs). These equations describe how the system evolves over time in configuration space, ensuring that the \"cost\" of the trajectory is stationary.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Equivalence to Newtonian Dynamics</h3>\n                    <p>In a Cartesian coordinate system where the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> is given by \\( \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 - V(\\mathbf{r}) \\), the Euler-Lagrange equations reduce exactly to <strong>Newton\'s Second Law</strong>. Specifically:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The \"velocity term\" \\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{x}} = m\\dot{x} = p_x \\) (Momentum).</li>\n                        <li>The \"position term\" \\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x} = -\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial x} = F_x \\) (Force).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    Substituting these into the Euler-Lagrange equation gives \\( \\dot{p}_x - F_x = 0 \\), or \\( \\mathbf{F} = \\dot{\\mathbf{p}} \\). This demonstrates that Newtonian mechanics is a specific coordinate representation of a more universal variational principle.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Coordinate Independence and Constraints</h3>\n                    <p>The greatest strength of the Euler-Lagrange formulation is its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/manifest-covariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Manifest Covariance</strong></a> under coordinate transformations. Whether using Cartesian, spherical, or any other set of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generalized-coordinates\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Generalized Coordinates</strong></a>, the form of the equations remains identical. This allows physicists to solve systems with complex holonomic constraints (like a bead on a rotating wire) without needing to calculate the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/constraint-forces\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Constraint Forces</strong></a> (like tension or normal force). These workless forces are automatically eliminated from the analysis, as the generalized coordinates are chosen to satisfy the constraints by definition.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Generalized Momentum and Conjugate Forces</h3>\n                    <p>The terms within the Euler-Lagrange equations have profound physical interpretations:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Generalized Momentum (\\( p_i \\)):</strong> Defined as \\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\dot{q}_i} \\). this is momentum conjugate to the coordinate \\( q_i \\).</li>\n                        <li><strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generalized-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Generalized Force</strong></a> (\\( Q_i \\)):</strong> The term \\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial q_i} \\).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                    If a coordinate \\( q_i \\) is \"cyclic\" (meaning it does not appear in the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a>), then \\( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial q_i} = 0 \\), and the corresponding generalized momentum is strictly conserved (\\( \\dot{p}_i = 0 \\)). This provides a direct link between the geometric symmetries of the system and its dynamical invariants.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Extension to Fields and Relativistic Physics</h3>\n                    <p>The Euler-Lagrange equations extend beyond particle mechanics into <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/classical-field-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Classical Field Theory</strong></a>. In this context, the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> is replaced by a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian-density\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian Density</strong></a> (\\( \\mathcal{L} \\)), and the equations take a four-dimensional form:\n                    \\[ \\partial_\\mu \\left( \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial (\\partial_\\mu \\phi)} \\right) - \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\phi} = 0 \\]\n                    This generalization is the basis for describing electromagnetic fields (Maxwell\'s equations), gravitational fields (Einstein\'s field equations), and the fundamental interactions in the Standard Model of particle physics.</p>','The Euler-Lagrange Equations are the fundamental second-order differential equations that govern the dynamics of a physical system in analytical mechanics. They are derived from Hamilton\'s Principle of Stationary Action and represent the condition that a system\'s trajectory must extremize the Action Integral. These equations provide a powerful, coordinate-independent alternative to Newton\'s laws, naturally accounting for constraints and focusing on the system\'s energy functions.','The Euler-Lagrange Equations are the fundamental second-order differential equations that govern the dynamics of a physical system in analytical mechanics. They are derived from Hamilton\'s Principle of Stationary Action and represent the condition that a system\'s trajectory must extremize the Action Integral. These equations provide a powerful, coordinate-independent alternative to Newton\'s laws, naturally accounting for constraints and focusing on the system\'s energy functions.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -2.148ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"21.283ex\" height=\"5.428ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1449.5 9406.9 2399\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D451\" d=\"M366 683Q367 683 438 688T511 694Q523 694 523 686Q523 679 450 384T375 83T374 68Q374 26 402 26Q411 27 422 35Q443 55 463 131Q469 151 473 152Q475 153 483 153H487H491Q506 153 506 145Q506 140 503 129Q490 79 473 48T445 8T417 -8Q409 -10 393 -10Q359 -10 336 5T306 36L300 51Q299 52 296 50Q294 48 292 46Q233 -10 172 -10Q117 -10 75 30T33 157Q33 205 53 255T101 341Q148 398 195 420T280 442Q336 442 364 400Q369 394 369 396Q370 400 396 505T424 616Q424 629 417 632T378 637H357Q351 643 351 645T353 664Q358 683 366 683ZM352 326Q329 405 277 405Q242 405 210 374T160 293Q131 214 119 129Q119 126 119 118T118 106Q118 61 136 44T179 26Q233 26 290 98L298 109L352 326Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\" d=\"M26 385Q19 392 19 395Q19 399 22 411T27 425Q29 430 36 430T87 431H140L159 511Q162 522 166 540T