late(316.8,-2) translate(-250 0)\"><use data-c=\"2D9\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2D9\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(12083.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(12528.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D461\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D461\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(12889.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-hamiltonian-class-subtopic-link-the-hamiltonian-a-12d6c0d8\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-state-geometry-class-subtopic-link-state-geometry-a-5314f032\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-poisson-dynamics-class-subtopic-link-poisson-dynamics-a-c0dbfb0b\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-generating-function-class-subtopic-link-generating-function-a-bb74f7f8\"]'),
(2010725,'angular-momentum','action-integral','Angular Momentum','<p>Angular momentum (\\( \\mathbf{L} \\)) is the fundamental vector quantity associated with rotational motion and the isotropy of space. In the modern analytical view, it is the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/generator-of-rotations\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>generator of rotations</strong></a> in the same way that linear momentum is the generator of translations. Its conservation is not merely a consequence of Newton\'s laws, but a deep structural property of the universe itself.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Noether\'s Theorem and Rotational Symmetry</h3>\n                    <p>According to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/noethers-theorem\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Noether\'s Theorem</strong></a>, every continuous symmetry of a physical system corresponds to a conserved quantity. Angular momentum is the \"Noether charge\" associated with <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/rotational-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Rotational Invariance</strong></a> (isotropy). If the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/lagrangian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Lagrangian</strong></a> of a system is invariant under an infinitesimal rotation \\( \\delta \\theta \\) about an axis \\( \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} \\), the component of angular momentum along that axis strictly conserved. This implies that the laws of physics do not depend on the orientation of the observer in space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The Poisson Algebra of Rotations</h3>\n                    <p>In the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian</strong></a> framework, the components of angular momentum (\\( L_x, L_y, L_z \\)) do not commute under the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/poisson-bracket\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Poisson Bracket</strong></a> operation. Instead, they satisfy a closed algebra:\n                    \\[ \\{L_i, L_j\\} = \\epsilon_{ijk} L_k \\]\n                    This non-Abelian structure is the classical precursor to the commutation relations of quantum mechanical operators. It demonstrates that the rotation group \\( SO(3) \\) is non-commutative; the order in which rotations about different axes are performed fundamentally changes the final orientation of the system.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Orbital vs. Spin Angular Momentum</h3>\n                    <p>University-level physics distinguishes between two forms of angular momentum:\n                        <ul>\n                            <li><strong>Orbital Angular Momentum (\\( \\mathbf{L} \\)):</strong> Associated with the motion of a particle\'s center of mass through space, defined relative to an origin as \\( \\mathbf{L} = \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{p} \\).</li>\n                            <li><strong><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spin-angular-momentum\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Spin Angular Momentum</a> (\\( \\mathbf{S} \\)):</strong> An intrinsic property of particles or composite systems in their rest frame. While classically modeled as \"internal rotation,\" it is fundamentally a quantum mechanical property that obeys the same \\( SU(2) \\) symmetry as orbital momentum.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    The total angular momentum of a system is the vector sum \\( \\mathbf{J} = \\mathbf{L} + \\mathbf{S} \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Rigid Body Dynamics and the Inertia Tensor</h3>\n                    <p>For a rigid body, the relationship between angular momentum and angular velocity is mediated by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/inertia-tensor\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Inertia Tensor</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{I} \\)): \\( \\mathbf{L} = \\mathbf{I} \\boldsymbol{\\omega} \\). A critical insight in advanced mechanics is that <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/angular-momentum-parallelism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>( mathbf{L} ) and ( boldsymbol{omega} ) are not necessarily parallel</strong></a>. They only align if the body rotates about one of its <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/principal-axes\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Principal Axes</strong></a>. If the rotation occurs about any other axis, the angular momentum vector will \"wobble\" or precess in space even in the absence of external torque, a phenomenon central to the study of gyroscopes and spinning planets.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Conservation and Central Forces</h3>\n                    <p>In any system governed by a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/central-force\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Central Force</strong></a> (where \\( \\mathbf{F} \\parallel \\mathbf{r} \\)), the torque \\( \\boldsymbol{\\tau} = \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{F} \\) is identically zero. This guarantees that the angular momentum is a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/constant-of-motion\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Constant of the Motion</strong></a>. This conservation restricts the particle\'s motion to a fixed plane in space (the \"orbital plane\") and yields <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/keplers-second-law\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Kepler\'s Second Law</strong></a>, stating that the radius vector sweeps out equal areas in equal times.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Quantization and the Quantum of Action</h3>\n                    <p>The deepest link between angular momentum and the action integral is found in <strong>Quantum Mechanics</strong>. Because angular momentum has the same units as action (Energy \\(\\times\\) Time), it is naturally quantized in units of \\( \\hbar \\). According to the <strong>Bohr-Sommerfeld Quantization Rule</strong>, the action integral for a periodic coordinate must be an integer multiple of Planck\'s constant: \\( \\oint p dq = n h \\). This restriction explains the stability of atoms, as electrons are confined to discrete \"stationary states\" where their angular momentum is a multiple of \\( \\hbar \\), preventing the continuous loss of energy through radiation.</p>','Angular momentum is the fundamental vector quantity associated with rotational motion and the isotropy of space. In the modern analytical view, it is the generator of rotations in the same way that linear momentum is the generator of translations. Its conservation is not merely a consequence of Newton\'s laws, but a deep structural property of the universe itself.','Angular momentum (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.566ex\" height=\"1.552ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -686 692 686\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40B\" d=\"M643 285Q641 280 629 148T612 4V0H39V62H147V624H39V686H51Q75 683 228 683Q415 685 425 686H439V624H304V62H352H378Q492 62 539 138Q551 156 558 178T569 214T576 255T581 289H643V285Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D40B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-B-1D40B\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>) is the fundamental vector quantity associated with rotational motion and the isotropy of space. In the modern analytical view, it is the generator of rotations in the same way that linear momentum is the generator of translations. Its conservation is not merely a consequence of Newton\'s laws, but a deep structural property of the universe itself.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.666ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"16.666ex\" height=\"2.363ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 7366.6 1044.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-N-7B\" d=\"M434 -231Q434 -244 428 -250H410Q281 -250 230 -184Q225 -177 222 -172T217 -161T213 -148T211 -133T210 -111T209 -84T209 -47T209 0Q209 21 209 53Q208 142 204 153Q203 154 203 155Q189 191 153 211T82 231Q71 231 68 234T65 250T68 266T82 269Q116 269 152 289T203 345Q208 356 