ate(757,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(6234.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D43F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(714,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"a-href-physics-subtopic-orbital-angular-momentum-class-subtopic-link-orbital-momentum-a-afe05d2a\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-tensor-mapping-class-subtopic-link-tensor-mapping-a-abbdde5d\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-poisson-algebra-class-subtopic-link-poisson-algebra-a-f2ccbf47\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-conservation-law-class-subtopic-link-conservation-law-a-d617d3a0\"]'),
(2010726,'gauge-symmetry','grand-unification','Gauge Symmetry and Local Invariance','<p>Gauge Symmetry is a fundamental principle in modern theoretical physics that identifies the mathematical redundancies in the description of a physical system as a profound internal symmetry. In university-level electrodynamics, gauge symmetry is not merely a convenience of calculation but a generative principle: the requirement of **<a href=\"/physics/subtopic/local-gauge-invariance\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Local Gauge Invariance</strong></a>** for a matter field necessitates the existence of the electromagnetic field itself.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Symmetry and Internal Rotations</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike spatial symmetries (like rotation or translation), gauge symmetry is an **Internal Symmetry**. It relates to the phase of the complex wave function (\\( \\psi \\)) that describes a particle. If we multiply the wave function by a constant phase factor \\( e^{i\\theta} \\), the physical observables (probabilities) remain unchanged. this is known as a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/global-symmetry\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Global Symmetry</strong></a>, and it is the foundation for the conservation of electric charge.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. The \\( U(1) \\) Group Structure</h3>\n                    <p>The collection of all such phase transformations forms a mathematical group called **\\( U(1) \\)** (the unitary group of degree one). This group is represented as rotations in the complex plane. In the context of electromagnetism, the symmetry is **Abelian**, meaning that consecutive gauge transformations commute—the order in which you perform two phase shifts does not change the final result. This simplicity is what distinguishes electromagnetism from the more complex strong and weak forces.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. From Global to Local Invariance</h3>\n                    <p>A transformation is **Local** if the phase shift (\\( \\theta \\)) is allowed to vary from point to point in space and time: \\( \\theta(\\mathbf{r}, t) \\). However, the kinetic energy term in the Schrödinger or Dirac equation contains derivatives (\\( \\nabla \\psi \\)), which \"break\" the symmetry when the phase is local. To restore the symmetry, a new field must be introduced—the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/gauge-field\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Gauge Field</strong></a>—which transforms in a way that exactly cancels the unwanted terms arising from the gradient of the phase.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. The Invention of the Photon</h3>\n                    <p>This requirement for local invariance forces the introduction of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/vector-potential\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Vector Potential</strong></a> (\\( \\mathbf{A} \\)). By redefining the derivative as a **Covariant Derivative** (\\( D_\\mu = \\partial_\\mu + iqA_\\mu \\)), the equations of motion become locally invariant. In this profound view, the photon is not just a particle that happens to exist; it is a mathematical necessity required to preserve the local phase symmetry of the charged matter fields.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Geometric Interpretation: Fiber Bundles</h3>\n                    <p>In advanced theoretical physics, gauge symmetry is given a geometric interpretation. The phase of the wave function is viewed as a coordinate on a \"circle\" (fiber) attached to every point in spacetime. The gauge field (\\( A_\\mu \\)) is a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/connection-gauge\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Connection</strong></a> on this fiber bundle, prescribing how the phase must change as a particle moves through space. The Field Strength Tensor (\\( F_{\\mu\\nu} \\)) then represents the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/curvature-tensor-em\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Curvature</strong></a> of this connection—a measure of how the phase \"twists\" as it moves around a closed loop.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Foundation of the Standard Model</h3>\n                    <p>Gauge symmetry is the architectural blueprint for all known fundamental forces. Electromagnetism is based on the \\( U(1) \\) group, the weak force on \\( SU(2) \\), and the strong force on \\( SU(3) \\). These <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/non-abelian-yang-mills\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Non-Abelian Gauge Theories</strong></a> (Yang-Mills theories) extend the concept of phase rotation to more complex internal spaces. The entire Standard Model is, at its heart, a collection of gauge symmetries that dictate the allowed interactions between the particles of the universe.</p>','Gauge Symmetry is a fundamental principle in modern theoretical physics that identifies the mathematical redundancies in the description of a physical system as a profound internal symmetry. In university-level electrodynamics, gauge symmetry is not merely a convenience of calculation but a generative principle: the requirement of **Local Gauge Invariance** for a matter field necessitates the existence of the electromagnetic field itself. Unlike spatial symmetries (like rotation or translation), gauge symmetry is an **Internal Symmetry**.','Gauge Symmetry is a fundamental principle in modern theoretical physics that identifies the mathematical redundancies in the description of a physical system as a profound internal symmetry. In university-level electrodynamics, gauge symmetry is not merely a convenience of calculation but a generative principle: the requirement of **Local Gauge Invariance** for a matter field necessitates the existence of the electromagnetic field itself. Unlike spatial symmetries (like rotation or translation), gauge symmetry is an **Internal Symmetry**.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.473ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 651 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\" d=\"M161 441Q202 441 226 417T250 358Q250 338 218 252T187 127Q190 85 214 61Q235 43 257 37Q275 29 288 29H289L371 360Q455 691 456 692Q459 694 472 694Q492 694 492 687Q492 678 411 356Q329 28 329 27T335 26Q421 26 498 114T576 278Q576 302 568 319T550 343T532 361T524 384Q524 405 541 424T583 443Q602 443 618 425T634 366Q634 337 623 288T605 220Q573 125 492 57T329 -11H319L296 -104Q272 -198 272 -199Q270 -205 252 -205H239Q233 -199 233 -197Q233 -192 256 -102T279 -9Q272 -8 265 -8Q106 14 106 139Q106 174 139 264T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Q21 299 34 333T82 404T161 441Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D713\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-TEX-I-1D713\"></use></g></g></g></svg>','[]','[]','[\"local-gauge-transformation-4c13f03f\",\"covariant-derivative-fd0194f4\",\"curvature-relation-a8ff85bf\",\"a-href-physics-subtopic-gauge-invariant-lagrangian-class-subtopic-link-gauge-invariant-lagrangian-a-8449210f\"]'),
(2010727,'uncertainty-principle','theoretical-quantum-mechanics','Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle','<p>The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is a fundamental limit in <strong>Quantum Mechanics</strong> stating that certain pairs of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/conjugate-variables\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Conjugate Variables</strong></a> cannot be simultaneously known with infinite precision. In university-level philosophy of physics, this is not a result of experimental error but an intrinsic property of the vacuum. It anchors our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the realization that the subatomic causal structure is defined by geometric bounds rather than definite trajectories.</p><h3>1. Derivation from <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/commutation-relation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Commutation Relations</strong></a></h3><p>The uncertainty principle is a direct consequence of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/canonical-commutation-relation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Canonical Commutation Relation</strong></a> (CCR). Because the operators do not commute, they do not share a <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/eigenbasis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/simultaneous-eigenbasis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Simultaneous Eigenbasis</strong></a></a>. In university-level mechanics, this ensuring that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> are spread across the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a>, providing the definitive map for <strong>Quantum Kinematics</strong>.</p><h3>2. The Robertson Uncertainty Relation</h3><p>The general form of the principle is quantified by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/robertson-uncertainty\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Robertson Relation</strong></a>. For any two <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hermitian-operators\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hermitian Operators</strong></a>, the product of their standard deviations is identically bounded by the expectation value of their <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-commutators\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Quantum Commutator</strong></a>. This reveals that the causal structure of information flow is limited by the non-commutative geometry of the vacuum.</p><h3>3. Wave-Particle Duality and Fourier Limits</h3><p>From the perspective of wave mechanics, the uncertainty principle is a result of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/fourier-analysis\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Fourier Analysis</strong></a>. A localized <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-packet\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>wave packet</strong></a> requires a wide spectrum of momenta. This link provides <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/the-bridge\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Bridge</strong></a> to <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/wave-particle-duality\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Wave-Particle Duality</strong></a>, ensuring that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of matter are consistent with wave properties.</p><h3>4. Energy-Time Uncertainty and Virtual Particles</h3><p>A second fundamental form of the principle relates energy and time. In university-level theory, this allows for the existence of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/virtual-particles\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Virtual Particles</strong></a> that borrow energy from the vacuum for transient intervals. This reveal that the causal structure of the manifold handles the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of forces through these ephemeral fluctuations, providing the basis for <strong>Quantum Field Theory</strong>.</p><h3>5. Uncertainty and Information Theory</h3><p>From the perspective of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a>, the uncertainty principle is the \'Resolution Limit\' of the vacuum. It defines the minimum information density required to describe an event. This investigation pushes the boundaries of our understanding, asking whether \'Uncertainty\' is a result of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of quantum information, providing the ultimate map for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mathematical Realism</strong></a>.</p>','The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is a fundamental limit in Quantum Mechanics stating that certain pairs of Conjugate Variables cannot be simultaneously known with infinite precision. In university-level philosophy of physics, this is not a result of experimental error but an intrinsic property of the vacuum. It anchors our Scientific Realism in the realization that the subatomic causal structure is defined by geometric bounds rather than definite trajectories.','The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is a fundamental limit in Quantum Mechanics stating that certain pairs of Conjugate Variables cannot be simultaneously known with infinite precision. In university-level philosophy of physics, this is not a result of experimental error but an intrinsic property of the vacuum. It anchors our Scientific Realism in the realization that the subatomic causal structure is defined by geometric bounds rather than definite trajectories.','','[]','[]','[\"uncertainty-principle-3e9b6bde\",\"robertson-uncertainty-law-699c7364\"]'),
(2010728,'quantum-superposition','theoretical-quantum-mechanics','Principle of Superposition','<p>The Principle of Superposition is the fundamental axiom stating that any two or more valid quantum states can be added together to create another valid, physical quantum state. In university-level physics, it is the direct consequence of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a> vector geometry and the linearity of the operators that govern subatomic dynamics. It anchors our <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the realization that the vacuum is composed of linear superpositions of all possible configurations.</p><h3>1. The Vector Addition of States</h3><p>If a system can be found in two different states, then their linear combination is also a physically realizable state. In university-level theory, this linearity is a requirement of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/axiomatic-postulates\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Axiomatic Postulates</strong></a>. The complex coefficients are the probability amplitudes, encoding both the likelihood of outcomes and the relative phase that drives <strong>Quantum Interference</strong> across the manifold.</p><h3>2. Superposition vs. Mixed States</h3><p>A university-level distinction must be made between a coherent superposition and a classical mixed state. A superposition is a single, definitive vector in <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a> possessing exact interference properties. In contrast, a mixed state represents classical ignorance. The loss of phase coherence that degrades a superposition into a mixed state is called <strong>Quantum Decoherence</strong>, providing the definitive map for the transition to the macroscopic Block Universe.</p><h3>3. Linearity of the Schrödinger Equation</h3><p>The superposition principle holds strictly because the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/schrodinger-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Schrödinger Equation</strong></a> is a linear differential equation. In university-level mechanics, this ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/total-dynamics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Total Dynamics</strong></a> of the system are additive. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hamiltonian-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hamiltonian Operator</strong></a> does not \'mix\' the superimposed branches; they evolve independently in the subatomic vacuum, anchoring <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/scientific-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Scientific Realism</strong></a> in the stability of the wave function.</p><h3>4. Superposition and Information Theory</h3><p>From the perspective of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/information-theory\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Information Theory</strong></a>, the principle of superposition is the basis for \'Quantum Parallelism.\' It allows a quantum system to store information in multiple states simultaneously. This investigation pushes the boundaries of our understanding, asking whether the \'Superposition\' is the primary <strong>Ontology</strong>, providing the ultimate map for <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/mathematical-realism\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Mathematical Realism</strong></a>.</p>','The Principle of Superposition is the fundamental axiom stating that any two or more valid quantum states can be added together to create another valid, physical quantum state. In university-level physics, it is the direct consequence of the Hilbert Space vector geometry and the linearity of the operators that govern subatomic dynamics. It anchors our Scientific Realism in the realization that the vacuum is composed of linear superpositions of all possible configurations.','The Principle of Superposition is the fundamental axiom stating that any two or more valid quantum states can be added together to create another valid, physical quantum state. In university-level physics, it is the direct consequence of the Hilbert Space vector geometry and the linearity of the operators that govern subatomic dynamics. It anchors our Scientific Realism in the realization that the vacuum is composed of linear superpositions of all possible configurations.','','[]','[]','[\"linear-superposition-identity-50b2dad3\",\"quantum-interference-law-99264ac0\"]'),
(2010729,'linear-operators','hilbert-space','Linear Operators and Hilbert Space','<p>Linear Operators are the fundamental mathematical tools of quantum mechanics, acting as the bridge between abstract state vectors and measurable physical quantities. In the university-level formulation of theory, every physical observable is represented by a linear operator acting on a complex <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/linear-operator-hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hilbert-space\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hilbert Space</strong></a></a> (\\( \\mathcal{H} \\)), transforming the study of physics into the study of operator algebra and functional analysis.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. The Linearity Condition</h3>\n                    <p>An operator \\( \\hat{A} \\) is <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/linear-operator-linearity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Linear</strong></a> if it satisfies two conditions for any state vectors \\( |\\psi\\rangle, |\\phi\\rangle \\) and any complex numbers \\( a, b \\):\n                    \\[ \\hat{A}(a|\\psi\\rangle + b|\\phi\\rangle) = a\\hat{A}|\\psi\\rangle + b\\hat{A}|\\phi\\rangle \\]\n                    This property is essential because it ensures that the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-superposition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Superposition Principle</strong></a> is preserved. If the equations of motion were non-linear, the combined state of two quantum systems would not simply be the sum of their individual states, violating the core logic of quantum mechanics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Observables and Hermitian Operators</h3>\n                    <p>In quantum physics, not every operator represents a physical quantity. An operator must be <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hermiticity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hermitian</strong></a> (\\( \\hat{A} = \\hat{A}^\\dagger \\)) to be an observable. The adjoint operator \\( \\hat{A}^\\dagger \\) is defined by the inner product relation \\( \\langle \\phi | \\hat{A} \\psi \\rangle = \\langle \\hat{A}^\\dagger \\phi | \\psi \\rangle \\). Hermiticity ensures that the measurement outcomes (eigenvalues) are always real numbers, a necessary requirement for values like energy, position, or momentum.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. The Eigenvalue Problem and Measurement</h3>\n                    <p>The act of measurement is mathematically represented by the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/eigenvalue-equation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Eigenvalue Equation</strong></a>:\n                    \\[ \\hat{A} |\\psi_n\\rangle = a_n |\\psi_n\\rangle \\]\n                    Where \\( a_n \\) is the measured value and \\( |\\psi_n\\rangle \\) is the corresponding <strong>Eigenstate</strong>. When a measurement is performed, the system\'s state vector \"collapses\" onto one of the eigenvectors of the operator. The probability of measuring a specific value \\( a_n \\) is given by the <strong>Born Rule</strong>, using the squared magnitude of the projection onto that eigenvector.</p>\n\n                    <h3>4. Commutation Relations and Uncertainty</h3>\n                    <p>Unlike numbers in classical physics, quantum operators do not necessarily commute. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/commutators\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Commutator</strong></a> of two operators is defined as \\( [\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] = \\hat{A}\\hat{B} - \\hat{B}\\hat{A} \\). If the commutator is non-zero, the two quantities cannot be measured simultaneously with infinite precision. this is mathematical origin of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/uncertainty-principle\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle</strong></a>, proving that uncertainty is a fundamental property of non-commuting operators.</p>\n\n                    <h3>5. Unitary Operators and Time Evolution</h3>\n                    <p>While observables are Hermitian, the processes that change a quantum state over time are represented by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/energy-operator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Unitary Operators</strong></a> (\\( \\hat{U}^\\dagger \\hat{U} = I \\)). Unitary transformations are \"rotations\" in Hilbert space that preserve the total probability (the norm of the state vector). The time-evolution operator, derived from the Schrödinger equation, ensures that a normalized quantum state remains normalized as it evolves.</p>\n\n                    <h3>6. Spectral Decomposition and Completeness</h3>\n                    <p>A powerful theorem in operator theory states that any Hermitian operator can be decomposed into a sum of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/projection-operators\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Projection Operators</strong></a> weighted by its eigenvalues. This <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/spectral-decomposition\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Spectral Decomposition</strong></a> allows any quantum state to be written as a linear combination of basis states. The <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/completeness-relation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Completeness Relation</strong></a> (\\( \\sum_n |n\\rangle\\langle n| = I \\)) ensures that the basis formed by an operator\'s eigenvectors spans the entire Hilbert space.</p>\n\n                    <h3>7. Additional Topics</h3>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/eigenstate\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Eigenstate</strong></a></li>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/superposition-property\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Superposition Property</strong></a></li>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hermiticity\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hermiticity</strong></a></li>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/canonical-commutator\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Canonical Commutator</strong></a></li>\n                        <li><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/identity-resolution\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Identity Resolution</strong></a></li>\n                    </ul>','Linear Operators are the fundamental mathematical tools of quantum mechanics, acting as the bridge between abstract state vectors and measurable physical quantities. In the university-level formulation of theory, every physical observable is represented by a linear operator acting on a complex Hilbert Space, transforming the study of physics into the study of operator algebra and functional analysis. An operator is Linear if it satisfies two conditions for any state vectors and any complex numbers : This property is essential because it ensures that the Superposition Principle is preserved.','Linear Operators are the fundamental mathematical tools of quantum mechanics, acting as the bridge between abstract state vectors and measurable physical quantities. In the university-level formulation of theory, every physical observable is represented by a linear operator acting on a complex Hilbert Space (<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.109ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.912ex\" height=\"1.654ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 845 731\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-C-48\" d=\"M18 487Q18 496 29 517T67 566T127 621T216 665T330 683Q359 683 376 669T397 643T400 622Q400 584 382 488T348 343Q348 342 467 342H587L594 366Q615 440 648 534T690 641Q701 656 723 669T764 683Q783 683 783 672L750 578Q716 485 677 346T625 101Q624 92 623 82T622 65T621 56Q621 20 658 20Q666 20 701 25Q709 52 736 69T785 87Q803 87 803 75T791 44T754 3T685 -33T588 -48Q568 -48 562 -46Q522 -31 522 13V23Q531 129 562 250L569 281L565 280Q561 278 556 277T549 274L438 273H328L321 249Q307 202 275 107T232 0Q219 -16 196 -28T155 -41Q149 -41 145 -39T140 -34T139 -29Q139 -24 148 -3T181 86T233 247Q240 270 240 272Q240 273 194 273H169Q139 273 139 285Q139 295 153 308T187 332Q206 341 236 342L260 343L264 359Q278 414 289 482T300 578Q300 613 260 613H254Q198 613 169 592Q148 578 