(2010828,'entanglement-entropy','thermodynamics-statistical-mechanics','Entanglement Entropy','<p>Entanglement <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Entropy</strong></a> is the primary tool for quantifying the degree of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/quantum-entanglement\" class=\"subtopic-link\">Quantum Entanglement</a> in a bipartite system. It measures the \"missing information\" in a subsystem that arises from its correlation with the rest of the system. In modern theoretical physics, it has become a central concept in condensed matter, quantum gravity, and black hole thermodynamics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>1. Von Neumann Entropy</h3>\n                    <p>For a pure state \\( |\\Psi\\rangle_{AB} \\), the entanglement entropy is defined as the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/von-neumann-entropy\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Von Neumann Entropy</strong></a> of the <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/state-representation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><a href=\"/physics/subtopic/reduced-density-matrix\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Reduced Density Matrix</strong></a></a> \\( \\rho_A \\):\n                    \\[ S(\\rho_A) = -\\text{Tr}(\\rho_A \\ln \\rho_A) \\]\n                    If the state is separable, \\( \\rho_A \\) is pure and \\( S = 0 \\). For a maximally entangled <a href=\"/physics/topic/quantum-physics\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Bell State</strong></a>, \\( S = \\ln(2) \\), indicating one \"bit\" of entanglement. A key property is that for any pure state, the entropy of both subsystems is identical: \\( S(\\rho_A) = S(\\rho_B) \\).</p>\n\n                    <h3>2. Area Laws in Many-Body Systems</h3>\n                    <p>In classical systems, entropy is typically <strong>Extensive</strong> (proportional to volume). However, for ground states of \"local\" quantum many-body systems, the entanglement entropy typically follows an <strong>Area Law</strong>: it is proportional to the surface area of the boundary between the subsystem and the rest of the system. This implies that quantum correlations are primarily localized at the interface, a principle that mirrors the <strong>Holographic Principle</strong> in black hole physics.</p>\n\n                    <h3>3. Topological Entanglement Entropy</h3>\n                    <p>In certain exotic phases of matter, such as <strong>Fractional Quantum Hall</strong> states, the entropy includes a constant negative correction called the <strong>Topological Entanglement Entropy</strong> (\\( \\gamma \\)). This term is \"universal\" and depends only on the long-range topological order of the system, providing a robust way to identify new states of matter that cannot be described by local symmetries.</p>','Entanglement Entropy is the primary tool for quantifying the degree of Quantum Entanglement in a bipartite system. It measures the \"missing information\" in a subsystem that arises from its correlation with the rest of the system. In modern theoretical physics, it has become a central concept in condensed matter, quantum gravity, and black hole thermodynamics.','Entanglement Entropy is the primary tool for quantifying the degree of Quantum Entanglement in a bipartite system. It measures the \"missing information\" in a subsystem that arises from its correlation with the rest of the system. In modern theoretical physics, it has become a central concept in condensed matter, quantum gravity, and black hole thermodynamics.','<svg style=\"color: #FFD700; vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"22.59ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 9984.9 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-TEX-I-1D446\" d=\"M308 24Q367 24 416 76T466 197Q466 260 414 284Q308 311 278 321T236 341Q176 383 176 462Q176 523 208 573T273 648Q302 673 343 688T407 704H418H425Q521 704 564 640Q565 640 577 653T603 682T623 704Q624 704 627 704T632 705Q645 705 645 698T617 577T585 459T